Chapter 1. Designing in a Distributed World
Section: Chapter 1. Designing in a Distributed World
============================================================

Concept_Name: Distributed computing
Core_Definition: The art of building large systems that divide work over many machines to achieve size, scale, speed, and reliability.
Key_Actors: Hundreds or thousands of machines; millions of users; operations teams; system designers.
Operational_Workflow: Systems divide work among many machines to provide a large service; components generate copious logs and export metrics for collection and storage; systems appraise their own health and export status information to load balancers; operations are automated to handle software deployment and failure handling; work is often structured through composition patterns such as server trees or load-balanced replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Speed is a competitive advantage with a 200 ms threshold for user responsiveness; failure is treated as normal and expected; software must anticipate and work around hardware failures; operations must be automated due to system size; designs should remain as simple as possible to maintain an accurate mental model; systems must be loosely coupled through abstraction; the CAP Principle limits systems to providing at most two of consistency, availability, and partition tolerance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hardware failures multiplied by thousands of machines become common; network latency can consume the majority of the response time budget; congestion problems can occur during fan-in when many small queries result in large responses; split brain scenarios can occur if servers in a master-slave relationship lose their heartbeat connection; stale data may be read if replicas are not yet updated.

Concept_Name: Server
Core_Definition: Software that provides a function or application program interface (API).
Key_Actors: Software; application program interfaces; frontends; backends; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A server provides a specific function or API and communicates with other servers via protocols; it can function as a frontend by sending requests or a backend by replying with results; it exports internal state and health information via special URLs; it may be replicated as a clone to provide the same response as other replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: A server is explicitly software rather than a piece of hardware; components should be loosely coupled to hide implementation details; servers should count interesting events as metrics; they should generate negative replies when starting up or shutting down to notify load balancers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A server can become swamped if a naive least-loaded algorithm sends it all traffic after a reboot; servers may return negative health replies or no response at all when unhealthy; a server may produce slow replies that require the frontend to give up to meet a latency budget.

Concept_Name: Service
Core_Definition: A user-visible system or product composed of many servers.
Key_Actors: Users; multiple servers; machines; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Many servers work together to provide a product or system; services use distributed computing techniques to manage millions of users and billions of queries; the service is managed through visibility into internal state and log aggregation; services evolve over time through the replacement of subsystems using abstraction.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must be fast and responsive to avoid being considered sluggish by users; they are expected to be highly available and last a long time; services must be designed for visibility at scale to allow for debugging and repair; they should be built using simple designs to remain understandable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services can become essentially unavailable during sudden traffic surges if backends crash in a cycle; network outages can prevent even partition-tolerant services from working; services may sacrifice consistency for availability in globally distributed networks.

Concept_Name: Machine
Core_Definition: A virtual or physical machine.
Key_Actors: Physical hardware; virtual machines; CPUs; RAM; mechanical disk spindles; solid-state drives (SSDs).
Operational_Workflow: Machines provide the underlying processing power and storage for servers; they are organized into configurations such as root, parent, and leaf nodes in a server tree; machines store fractions or shards of large datasets; they communicate over local or long-distance networks which are limited by the speed of light.
Rules_and_Constraints: Individual machines have limited storage and processing power; hardware failures are assumed to be a normal part of the landscape; disk access is significantly slower than RAM access; solid-state drives provide faster access than mechanical platters but are more expensive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hardware failures are frequent when thousands of machines are involved; mechanical disk seeks take approximately 10 ms compared to 0.002 ms for RAM; machines can become partitioned from each other due to network link failures; naive load balancing algorithms can cause a single machine to become swamped and crash.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: QPS stands for queries per second and represents the measurement of how many web hits or API calls a system receives in a single second.
Key_Actors: Replica servers; distributed state systems; clients.
Operational_Workflow: Incoming requests are received by the system and distributed among shards and replicas; each individual replica processes a portion of the total queries; the system tracks the number of these queries occurring every second to measure load and capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems are designed to process specific numbers of simultaneous read and write requests by increasing the number of replicas; each replica has a limit to the number of queries per second it can handle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Sudden traffic surges or unpredictable bursts of large replies can lead to overloaded servers; if a machine receives more queries than it can process it may crash and reboot.

Concept_Name: Traffic
Core_Definition: Traffic is a generic term encompassing queries, API calls, or any other requests sent to a server.
Key_Actors: Servers; load balancers; backends; requestors.
Operational_Workflow: A requestor sends a query or API call to a service; a load balancer receives the incoming traffic and selects a backend replica to forward the request to; the backend processes the traffic and returns a response.
Rules_and_Constraints: Load balancers must monitor backends to ensure they are alive and ready to accept traffic; health checks are performed dozens of times per second; servers should not receive traffic when they are initializing or shutting down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Load balancers stop sending traffic to a backend if a health check fails; naive algorithms can cause problems by sending all traffic to a single backend during surges; network links can become congested if there is not enough bandwidth to sustain the traffic fanning in from multiple backends.

Concept_Name: Performant
Core_Definition: A performant system is one whose performance meets or exceeds its specific design requirements. The term is a neologism derived from the words performance and conformant.
Key_Actors: Design teams; users; interactive services; prototypes.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers create designs based on estimates of how quickly a system can process requests; prototypes are built to test these assumptions; measurements are taken during construction to adjust the design if estimates were inaccurate.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance is often measured against a latency budget such as 200 ms for web replies; the design process involves eliminating designs that are not fast enough; estimates must account for slow operations like disk access and network delays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems may be considered sluggish by users if they fail to meet response time requirements; small added delays can lead to sharp drops in revenue; if a design is not performant engineers must iterate or consider more complex asynchronous implementations.

Concept_Name: Application Programming Interface (API)
Core_Definition: An API is a protocol that governs the communication between different servers.
Key_Actors: Servers; software; distributed system components.
Operational_Workflow: Software provides an API to make functions available to other components; servers send requests to these APIs to facilitate inter-server communication; the system counts API calls as metrics and records them in logs for analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs should provide a level of abstraction that hides implementation details to keep systems loosely coupled; components should export counters to show how many times a particular API was called; high-level logs should record every API call to provide visibility at scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an API uses a bad level of abstraction it may require every frontend to be rewritten when the backend implementation changes; failures in API calls are monitored through logs to debug and repair the system.

Concept_Name: Failure is normal
Core_Definition: In distributed systems, hardware failures that are rare in isolation become common when multiplied by thousands of machines, making failure an expected part of the landscape.
Key_Actors: Hardware; thousands of machines; software; system designs.
Operational_Workflow: The system operates under the assumption that failures will happen; designs are created specifically to work around these failures; software is written to anticipate them rather than treating them as exceptions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Failures must be assumed as part of the normal operating environment; designs must actively work around hardware failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Operations must be automated
Core_Definition: Automation is the necessary practice of using programmed processes to manage systems because the sheer size of distributed systems makes manual tasks involving hundreds or thousands of machines inconceivable.
Key_Actors: Software systems; operational processes; hundreds or thousands of machines.
Operational_Workflow: Automation is integrated into the preparation and deployment of software; it is applied to the execution of regular system operations; it is utilized for the automatic handling of failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is considered inconceivable to manually perform tasks on systems of this scale; automation is critical for software deployment and regular operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Visibility
Core_Definition: Visibility is the actively created capability to see into a distributed system's internal state, which is necessary for management, debugging, tuning, and repair.
Key_Actors: Engineers; teams; system components; log aggregation systems; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are designed to draw out and expose information; components generate copious logs detailing system events at high and low levels; these logs are aggregated to a central location for collection, storage, and analysis; systems export metrics by counting and exposing interesting events; components appraise their own health and make this status visible.
Rules_and_Constraints: Visibility must be actively created by design because no person or team can manually monitor all parts of a large system; components should generate logs and export metrics.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a health check output is anything other than the specific bytes O and K, or if there is no response at all, the system is flagged as unhealthy or not ready to receive requests.

Concept_Name: Introspection
Core_Definition: Introspection is the ability of a system or component to examine and expose its own internal state and health.
Key_Actors: System components; load balancers; engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Components count interesting events like API calls and make these counters accessible; special URLs are provided to view internal state; components appraise their own health and output their readiness status; load balancers use this readiness information to determine if a server should receive traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: Introspection is required to operate, debug, tune, and repair large systems; systems should make their counters and health status visible to other components and engineers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A server will send negative health replies during initialization when it is starting up or during shutdown when it is finishing in-flight requests but not accepting new ones.

Concept_Name: Logs
Core_Definition: Copious data records generated by system components that detail the events and internal actions occurring within a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Distributed system components; central aggregation and storage systems; engineering and operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: System components generate high-level logs for events such as user purchases and API calls alongside low-level logs for function call parameters; these logs are transmitted from individual machines to a central location; the centralized system collects, stores, and organizes the data; engineers analyze the aggregated logs to perform system operations, debugging, tuning, and repairs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logs must be copious to ensure adequate visibility; information must be aggregated to a central location for effective analysis; visibility must be actively designed into the system because manual monitoring of all parts is impossible at scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Metrics
Core_Definition: Quantitative data exported by system components to provide visibility into their internal state and performance.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; system components; APIs; operational teams.
Operational_Workflow: Components track internal state and behavior; systems export these observations as formatted metrics; the metrics are made accessible to external observers or management tools for the purpose of introspection.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should export metrics as a standard practice; metrics must count interesting events; the data must be made accessible to enable the management and tuning of large-scale distributed systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Counters
Core_Definition: A specific category of metric used to track the frequency and occurrence of specific events within a system.
Key_Actors: Distributed system components; APIs.
Operational_Workflow: A system identifies an event of interest such as a specific API call; the system increments a numerical count each time that event occurs; the system makes the resulting counter accessible as an exported metric for introspection.
Rules_and_Constraints: Counters should be used for interesting events; they must be accessible to allow operators to examine the internal state of the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Server-status page
Core_Definition: A specialized URL or web page used to expose the internal state and health appraisal of a server component.
Key_Actors: Apache HTTP Web Server; distributed system components; load balancers; engineers.
Operational_Workflow: A system component monitors its own internal state and health; it makes this information visible through a dedicated URL; a load balancer or engineer accesses the URL to retrieve status information; the load balancer uses the output to determine if the server is healthy and ready to receive traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: Components must use these pages to make internal state visible for introspection; a specific output such as the byte O followed by the byte K signifies the system is ready to receive requests; any other response or no response at all indicates the system is not ready.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The server sends negative replies when it is starting up and still initializing; negative replies are sent when the server is shutting down and no longer accepting new requests while still processing requests in flight; failure to respond at all is treated as a negative health indication.

Concept_Name: Health
Core_Definition: The state of a component being ready and able to receive new requests as determined by its own self-appraisal.
Key_Actors: Components of distributed systems; load balancers; backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: Components appraise their own internal state and make this information visible; load balancers send health check queries to these components dozens of times each second; the component responds with a status indicating its readiness; the load balancer uses this response to decide whether to continue sending traffic to that specific backend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Health checks should be simple queries that execute quickly; a component is considered healthy only if it returns the specific bytes O and K; any other response or a lack of response indicates the system is not healthy and should not receive requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a health check fails, the load balancer stops sending traffic to that backend; servers may report they are not healthy during startup while they are still initializing or during shutdown when they are only processing remaining in-flight requests.

Concept_Name: OK
Core_Definition: A specific two-byte output string used by a component to signal to a load balancer that it is ready to receive requests.
Key_Actors: Distributed system components; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A component provides a specific URL for health checks; the load balancer accesses this URL to check readiness; the component outputs the byte O followed by the byte K if it is prepared for traffic; the load balancer interprets this exact sequence as a signal to route traffic to the server.
Rules_and_Constraints: The output must be exactly the byte O followed by the byte K; any deviation from this exact output is treated as a sign that the system does not want to receive new requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Receiving no response at all is treated the same as receiving an incorrect response, resulting in the server being marked as not ready for traffic.

Concept_Name: Negative replies
Core_Definition: Any response from a health check that is not the exact string OK, including no response at all, indicating a server is unavailable for new traffic.
Key_Actors: Servers; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A server monitors its own initialization or shutdown status; when the server is not ready for new queries, it provides an output other than OK or remains silent when queried by a load balancer; the load balancer receives this negative indicator and ceases sending traffic to that server.
Rules_and_Constraints: Any output other than the specific byte O followed by the byte K constitutes a negative reply.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Negative replies are intentionally used when a server is starting up and initializing; they are also used during shutdown sequences where a server is no longer accepting new requests but is still processing requests that are already in flight.

Concept_Name: Simplicity
Core_Definition: The practice of constructing software designs that are uncomplicated enough that they have obviously no deficiencies and can be understood by those operating them.
Key_Actors: System designers; engineers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Designers prioritize simple designs that meet service needs to avoid starting at a disadvantage; engineers develop a mental model of the system to track operations and perform debugging; every minute spent simplifying the design is intended to pay off during the operational life of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: A design should remain as simple as possible while still meeting service needs; simplicity is required because debugging is considered twice as hard as writing the code; the system must be simple enough for a person to hold a mental model of it in their head.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems naturally grow and become more complex over time; if a system is overly complex, no single person can understand the entire system at once, and if code is written too cleverly, it may be impossible for the creator to debug it.

Concept_Name: Mental model
Core_Definition: An internal visualization of how a system operates that an engineer maintains to track its function and identify problems.
Key_Actors: Engineers; persons; teams.
Operational_Workflow: An engineer holds an image of the system operating in their head; they use this model to track how the system works during normal operations; they apply the model to debug the system when it fails to function correctly.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should be designed to be as simple as possible to ensure the mental model can remain accurate; as systems grow and become more complex it becomes harder to maintain an accurate mental model.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Overly complex systems can reach a point where no single person is capable of understanding the entire system at any one time.

Concept_Name: Debugging is twice as hard as writing the code
Core_Definition: A principle suggesting that the intellectual effort required to find and fix errors is significantly greater than the effort required to create the code initially.
Key_Actors: Kernighan and Plauger; developers; engineers.
Operational_Workflow: A developer writes code using their available cleverness; the developer or an engineer later attempts to debug that code; because debugging is twice as difficult as writing the code if the original code was written at the limit of the developer's cleverness they will not be smart enough to fix it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers should avoid writing code as cleverly as possible; simplifying a design during the creation phase is necessary to ensure it can be operated and fixed later.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a design is not simplified the system may become impossible for the original creator or others to debug during operations.

Concept_Name: Composition patterns
Core_Definition: Fundamental architectural structures used to build large distributed systems by combining many smaller individual systems.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; sub-systems; load balancers; backend replicas; root servers; parent servers; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: Large services are constructed by dividing work among many smaller machines; these components are organized into specific structural arrangements to achieve scale and reliability; components work together to process queries and manage data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Three specific fundamental patterns are utilized including load balancer with multiple backend replicas, server with multiple backends, and server trees; designs should remain simple even when using these patterns.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load balancer with multiple backend replicas
Core_Definition: A composition pattern where a central server receives incoming requests and distributes them among several identical cloned servers.
Key_Actors: Load balancer server; backend replicas; original requester; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A requester sends a request to the load balancer; the load balancer selects a specific backend replica using an algorithm; the load balancer forwards the request to the chosen backend; the backend processes the request and sends a response back to the load balancer; the load balancer relays the final response to the original requester.
Rules_and_Constraints: Backend replicas must be clones that produce identical responses for any given request; the load balancer must perform health checks dozens of times each second; traffic must be stopped if a health check fails; selection methods can include round-robin, proportional round-robin, or least loaded schemes; slow start algorithms should be used to prevent swamping machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Naive least loaded algorithms can cause disasters by sending all traffic to a single new or rebooted machine that reports zero load; trailing averages used for load measurement can be blind to sudden spikes or the present state of the system; cascading failures can occur where backends crash one by one due to being swamped with shifted traffic.

Concept_Name: Server with multiple backends
Core_Definition: A composition pattern where a frontend server receives a request, sends queries to several different backend servers simultaneously, and constructs a final reply by combining the results. This approach is typically used when a query can be deconstructed into independent tasks that can be processed in parallel.
Key_Actors: Frontend server; backend servers such as spell checkers, web search, image search, and advertisement servers; web browser or original requester.
Operational_Workflow: The frontend receives a request from a user and relays it to multiple backend servers; the backends perform their specific functions in parallel and send their replies back to the frontend; the frontend uses these various replies to construct a complete response, such as an HTML page, and sends that final result to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system should be loosely coupled so that backends can fail or be replaced without breaking the frontend; backends must perform their work in parallel so the total reply time is not the sum of all backend processes; the frontend should manage a latency time budget to decide when to stop waiting for slow backends.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend is slow or fails, the frontend can fill in default information or leave that portion of the final page blank to meet latency requirements; the system may face fan-in congestion problems if small queries result in sudden, unpredictable bursts of large replies that exceed network or server capacity.

Concept_Name: Server tree
Core_Definition: A hierarchical composition pattern where a root server coordinates with parent servers, which in turn coordinate with leaf servers at the bottom of the tree to search a large dataset. This pattern allows a large corpus to be spread across many machines and searched in parallel.
Key_Actors: Root server; parent servers; leaf servers; the corpus or dataset being searched.
Operational_Workflow: The root server receives a query and forwards it to multiple parent servers; the parents forward the query to leaf servers which search their specific shard or fraction of the corpus; the leaves send their findings back up to the parents; the parents sort and filter these results before forwarding them to the root; finally, the root combines all parent responses into a full answer for the requester.
Rules_and_Constraints: The corpus is divided into shards so that each leaf stores only a fraction of the whole; parents and roots should perform sorting, ranking, and filtering in parallel; the tree can be made taller with more levels of parents to permit wider fanout for extremely large datasets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If fast answers are prioritized over perfect ones, parents and roots may stop waiting for slow replies as the latency deadline approaches; redundant servers and load balancing are often used to route around failed nodes within the tree; additional parent levels may be used to provide caching functions to relieve pressure on leaf servers.

Concept_Name: Load balancer
Core_Definition: A specialized server that sits in front of multiple backend replicas to distribute incoming requests among them and relay the responses back to the original requester. It ensures that traffic is handled by healthy servers and can help scale a service.
Key_Actors: Load balancer server; backend replicas; original requester; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives a request and selects a specific backend replica based on a defined algorithm; it forwards the request to that backend and waits for a response; once the response is received, the load balancer relays it to the requester; throughout this process, the load balancer sends health check queries to all backends multiple times per second to ensure they are ready to accept traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: The load balancer must always know which backends are alive and ready; health checks must be simple and execute quickly; selection algorithms can include round-robin, proportional round-robin, or least-loaded schemes; a slow start algorithm should be used with least-loaded schemes to prevent swamping newly added or rebooted machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend fails a health check, the load balancer immediately stops sending traffic to it; naive least-loaded algorithms can cause a cascade of failures if they send all traffic to a single machine that appears to have no load; trailing averages used for load measurement can be dangerous because they reflect past state rather than sudden present spikes in traffic.

Concept_Name: Replicas
Core_Definition: Multiple identical clones or replications of a server that are designed to produce the same response to a given request. They are used to increase the capacity of a system and provide resiliency against hardware failure.
Key_Actors: Backend servers; load balancers; root servers; database or state storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Replicas operate as a group where any individual unit can handle a request for the service; in stateful systems, state is sharded and each shard is stored on multiple replicas; when an update occurs, all replicas of that shard must be updated to maintain consistency; load balancers or roots distribute incoming queries across the available replicas to share the workload.
Rules_and_Constraints: A request sent to any replica should produce the same response; increasing the number of replicas allows the system to process a higher number of simultaneous read and write requests; each replica must be capable of processing a certain number of queries per second to meet design requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During an update, some clients might read from stale replicas that have not yet received the new data; in the event of a network partition, the system might have to sacrifice consistency for availability or vice versa; if a replica crashes, other replicas continue to serve traffic so that the service remains available to users.

Concept_Name: Health check queries
Core_Definition: A simple query sent by a load balancer that executes quickly to determine whether a backend system is healthy and ready to receive traffic.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend servers; components of distributed systems.
Operational_Workflow: Load balancers send these queries dozens of times each second to backends; if the health check fails, the load balancer stops sending traffic to that specific backend; healthy servers return a specific positive response while servers in the process of starting up or shutting down send negative replies.
Rules_and_Constraints: The query must execute quickly; an OK response is specifically defined as the byte O followed by the byte K; any other output or a lack of response indicates the system should not receive requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Servers send negative replies when they are still initializing during startup or when they are shutting down but still processing requests that are in flight.

Concept_Name: Round-robin
Core_Definition: A simple method for selecting which backend to send a query to by alternating among the available backends in a loop.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives a request; it selects a backend from a list in sequential order; it forwards the request to that backend; it moves to the next backend in the list for the subsequent request.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach is described as a reliable alternative to naive least loaded implementations which can lead to system instability.

Concept_Name: Proportional round-robin scheme
Core_Definition: A variation of the round-robin method where specific backends are selected more frequently than others based on their capacity.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; powerful backend machines; less powerful backend machines.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer alternates among backends in a loop but uses a weighting system to favor certain machines; backends with more power or resources are assigned a higher frequency of requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: This scheme is used when some backends in the replica set are more powerful than others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Least loaded scheme
Core_Definition: A complex selection approach where a load balancer monitors the current load of each backend and assigns new requests to the machine that is currently the least busy.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend replicas; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer tracks the load on each backend using metrics such as active connections or CPU utilization; when a request arrives the load balancer identifies the machine with the lowest current load; the request is forwarded to that least loaded backend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Accurate measurement of load is required; a slow start algorithm should be implemented to prevent sending too many requests to a single machine in a row.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Naive implementations can cause a disaster where a new or rebooted backend with zero load is immediately swamped with all traffic and crashes; trailing averages used for load measurement can be blind to sudden spikes or reflect the past rather than the present; I/O overload may be missed if the system only measures CPU utilization; if a machine crashes and reboots the cycle of being swamped may repeat.

Concept_Name: Trailing average
Core_Definition: A measurement used by load balancers to track backend load based on a specific period of recent history, such as the last five minutes.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer tracks how loaded each backend is by examining its performance over a set period of time; it calculates an average of this load to decide which server should receive the next query; this historical average informs the selection process in complex load balancing schemes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Trailing averages reflect the past rather than the present state of a system; they are a component of more complex load balancing solutions like the least loaded scheme.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A sharp or sudden increase in load is not reflected in the average for a significant amount of time; new machines added to a service may report an artificially low load while older machines continue to report an artificially high load; this discrepancy can lead the load balancer to believe a new machine is less loaded than others for a duration even after it has become swamped.

Concept_Name: Slow start algorithm
Core_Definition: A control mechanism used in load balancing to prevent a single machine from receiving too many consecutive requests.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend servers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The algorithm is implemented as part of a least loaded load balancing scheme; it monitors the flow of traffic to individual backends; it restricts the system so that it never sends more than a certain number of requests to the same machine in a row; this allows a new or rebooted machine to gradually take on traffic rather than being immediately overwhelmed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This algorithm is considered a less naive and more robust implementation of the least loaded balancing scheme; it is a best practice for managing how traffic is introduced to backends.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Without this algorithm, a load balancer may send all traffic to a single new backend, causing it to crash and reboot in a continuous cycle; the CNN.com service was essentially unavailable during a major traffic surge because this upgrade had not yet been installed.

Concept_Name: Naive least loaded algorithm
Core_Definition: A load balancing approach that selects the backend with the lowest measured load but lacks controls to prevent overwhelming new or recovered machines.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend servers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer monitors load metrics like connections or CPU utilization for all backends; it identifies which machine currently has the lowest load; it directs incoming traffic to that specific machine; because the machine appears to have the least load, the balancer continues to send it all traffic until the reported load increases.
Rules_and_Constraints: This algorithm can be blind to the fact that some connections are long-lasting or that a system is experiencing input/output overload; it often relies on trailing averages which reflect past rather than present conditions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A new backend with no load will receive all traffic and become absolutely swamped because it cannot process the volume previously handled by multiple other machines; machines may enter a cycle of crashing and rebooting as the algorithm repeatedly targets them for all incoming traffic upon their return to service; this failure mode caused significant issues for CNN.com during the September 11 terrorist attacks.

Concept_Name: Traffic surges
Core_Definition: Sudden and unpredictable increases in the volume of requests or large replies sent through a system.
Key_Actors: Users; load balancers; backend servers; frontends; network equipment.
Operational_Workflow: A massive influx of user requests hits the service load balancers; the queries are distributed to backend replicas; in fan-out architectures, one query results in many new queries to different backends; the resulting replies fan in to the frontend to be combined; if the replies are large, they can saturate network links or overload servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be engineered with sufficient network and server capacity to handle these bursts; backends can use rate-limiting to avoid creating congestion; frontends can manage surges by controlling the rate of new queries or notifying backends to slow down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Surges can cause a cascading failure where one backend crashes and then others crash in succession as they are targeted by load balancing algorithms; sudden bursts of large replies may exceed the available bandwidth of network links; system administrators may have to resort to emergency measures such as halting and rebooting all backends simultaneously to recover the service.

Concept_Name: Frontend
Core_Definition: A frontend is a server that receives requests from a user or client and relays those queries to one or more backend servers to gather information. It is responsible for using the received replies to construct a final response, such as an HTML page, for the original requester.
Key_Actors: Users; web browsers; frontend servers; backend servers; parent servers; root servers.
Operational_Workflow: The frontend receives a request from a client; it relays the query to multiple backend servers which may work in parallel; the frontend receives replies from these backends; it uses the information gathered to construct the final results page; the frontend sends the completed reply back to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: The terms frontend and backend are a matter of perspective because a server can be both a frontend and a backend depending on the relationship; the frontend manages fan out where one query results in many new queries to backends; frontends can manage congestion by controlling the queries they send out or by notifying backends to slow down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend is slow, the frontend may give up on it and generate a page with the information it has or fill in default information; the frontend may receive a negative reply or no response from a backend if that backend is unhealthy, initializing, or shutting down.

Concept_Name: Backend
Core_Definition: A backend is a server or software that provides a function, API, or data in response to queries from a frontend. Multiple backends often work in parallel as clones or specialized systems to compose a final answer for a service.
Key_Actors: Backend servers; replicas; frontend servers; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A backend receives a query or request forwarded from a frontend or load balancer; it performs its specific task such as a spell check, web search, or database retrieval; it sends the reply back to the frontend for final processing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Backends are often referred to as replicas when they are clones of each other and produce the same response for a given request; backends can rate-limit themselves to avoid creating network congestion; they must provide health check information to load balancers to show they are ready to receive traffic.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A backend may become overloaded and crash, leading a load balancer to stop sending traffic to it; a backend may be slow due to input/output overload or CPU utilization; backends can be updated or replaced while the system is running if they are loosely coupled and maintain the same abstract interface.

Concept_Name: Latency management
Core_Definition: Latency management is the practice of controlling the time it takes for a system to respond by setting deadlines for backend processes and making trade-offs between speed and completeness. This approach ensures that a service remains responsive even when individual components are slow.
Key_Actors: Frontend servers; backend servers; parent servers; root servers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The system defines a total time limit for a request to complete; as the request is processed, the system tracks the elapsed time against this limit; if the latency deadline is near, the frontend or parent server stops waiting for slow backends; the system then proceeds to generate the best possible answer using only the data received so far.
Rules_and_Constraints: Fast answers are often considered more important than perfect or complete answers when a deadline is approaching; network latency typically consumes a large portion of the available time, leaving very little for internal processing; the system must be designed to work around the speed of light and mechanical disk delays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend like an advertisement system is slow, the service may display search results without any ads to stay within the time limit; asynchronous designs can be used to return control to the user quickly while the actual work is still lagging in the background.

Concept_Name: Latency time budget
Core_Definition: A latency time budget is a calculated allocation of time for each step of a transaction, used during the design phase to ensure a service meets its overall performance goals. It involves breaking down a request into small operations and estimating the duration of each based on hardware and network constraints.
Key_Actors: Engineers; developers; authentication systems; storage systems; database indexes.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify a target response time, such as 300 ms; they subtract fixed delays that cannot be controlled, such as network travel time; the remaining time is divided among internal tasks like authentication, index lookups, and data retrieval; the design is then evaluated to see if it fits within these limits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users perceive a website as sluggish if replies do not return in 200 ms or less; disk access is estimated at 10 ms per seek while RAM access is estimated at 0.002 ms; the budget must account for the speed of light which causes significant delays over long distances.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the initial estimates exceed the budget, engineers may redesign the system, such as by moving an index from disk to RAM or using an asynchronous design; if the time budget for a specific operation is too small, developers may implement a scheme that returns a fast answer rather than a perfect one.

Concept_Name: Fan out
Core_Definition: A process in distributed systems where a single query results in the generation of multiple new queries sent to various backend servers.
Key_Actors: Frontend servers and backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: A frontend server receives an initial request; the frontend relays the query to many different backend servers simultaneously; the queries fan out to the individual backends for parallel processing.
Rules_and_Constraints: This pattern allows backends to perform work in parallel so that the system does not have to wait for one process to complete before the next begins; in server trees, adding more levels of parent servers permits a wider fanout which is necessary for searching extremely large datasets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Fan in
Core_Definition: The phase of a distributed operation where responses from multiple backend servers are sent back to a frontend server to be aggregated.
Key_Actors: Backend servers and frontend servers.
Operational_Workflow: Individual backend servers complete their assigned tasks and generate replies; these replies are sent back to the originating frontend; the frontend collects and combines these answers to construct the final response for the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Fan in situations must be monitored for potential congestion; frontends can manage the fan in process using a latency budget to give up on slow backends and generate a response with whatever information has been received before a deadline.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Any fan in situation is at risk of congestion problems, particularly when small initial queries result in large responses that exceed available network bandwidth.

Concept_Name: Congestion problems
Core_Definition: Situations where network links become saturated and servers become overloaded because the volume of data in a fan in process exceeds available capacity.
Key_Actors: Network links, network equipment, backend servers, and frontend servers.
Operational_Workflow: A small amount of bandwidth is used to fan out queries to multiple backends; these backends produce large responses or sudden unpredictable bursts of data; the resulting volume of data exceeds the network bandwidth or server capacity; network links become congested and servers are swamped.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should be engineered with sufficient capacity based on consistent query and reply sizes; certain network equipment can be engineered to dynamically provision more buffer space to handle bursts; frontends can manage congestion by controlling the rate of new queries or notifying backends to slow down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unpredictable bursts of large replies make engineering difficult; in these cases, frontends may need to implement emergency measures to handle the flood.

Concept_Name: Rate-limit
Core_Definition: A self-imposed restriction by a server to control the pace of its output to avoid overwhelming the network or other system components.
Key_Actors: Backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: A backend server processes requests and prepares responses; the server monitors its own output rate; the server restricts the volume or speed of outgoing data to prevent creating network congestion or overloading the frontend.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is used as a preventative measure to avoid creating congestion situations in the first place.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Emergency measures
Core_Definition: Strategies implemented by frontends to manage network congestion and handle a flood of responses more effectively.
Key_Actors: Frontends; backends.
Operational_Workflow: Frontends implement these measures to handle a flood of replies when managing congestion problems related to fan-in; they serve as an alternative to controlling new queries or notifying backends to slow down.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Root
Core_Definition: The server at the top of a server tree composition that receives the original query and coordinates the aggregation of results.
Key_Actors: Root server; parent servers; original requester.
Operational_Workflow: The root receives the original query from a requester and forwards it to the parent servers; after parents process the query, the root receives their responses; the root then combines these results and replies with the full answer.
Rules_and_Constraints: The root is at the top of an upside-down tree structure; it coordinates with parent servers below it to access datasets larger than one machine can hold; it may manage a latency budget by not waiting for slow replies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a latency deadline is near, the root does not have to wait for slow replies to provide an answer to the requester.

Concept_Name: Parent
Core_Definition: Intermediate servers in a tree configuration that distribute queries to lower levels and process findings from leaf nodes.
Key_Actors: Parent servers; root server; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: Parents receive a query from the root server and forward it to all leaf nodes below them; after leaves reply, the parents rank, sort, and filter the results; the refined findings are then forwarded up to the root.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sorting and ranking by parents are performed in parallel; expanding the number of parents at each level increases the capacity to sort and rank results; additional levels of parents permit a wider fanout for large datasets; parents may provide a caching function to reduce pressure on leaf servers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Parents do not have to wait for slow replies from leaf nodes if the latency deadline for the query is approaching.

Concept_Name: Leaf
Core_Definition: Servers at the bottom of a server tree that store a specific fraction of a dataset and perform the actual search or retrieval tasks.
Key_Actors: Leaf servers; parent servers.
Operational_Workflow: Leaf servers receive a query from parent servers and search their specific shard or fraction of the corpus; once the search is complete, they send their findings back up to the parents.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each leaf stores one fraction or shard of the total dataset; leaves search their share of the corpus in parallel; shards can be placed on multiple leaf servers to improve availability and resiliency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In systems that manage state, queries may be sent to a single leaf instead of all leaves; updates to state require all replicas of a shard stored on various leaf servers to be updated.

Concept_Name: Shard
Core_Definition: A fraction or portion of a whole dataset or corpus that is stored on an individual machine. This allows the total amount of state to be limited only by the number of machines acquired rather than the capacity of a single machine.
Key_Actors: Leaf servers; root servers; parent servers; physical or virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: A large dataset or corpus is divided into multiple fractions. Each fraction is assigned to a leaf server within a server tree. When a query is made, the root server and parent servers forward the request to the leaf servers, which search their specific shard. The findings from each shard are then sent back up the tree to be sorted, filtered, and combined into a final answer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each shard should be stored on multiple machines to ensure that a single machine failure does not result in the loss of access to that state. Expanding the number of leaf servers can give each leaf a smaller portion of the corpus to search, which can improve performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Updates to shards are more complex when state is replicated across multiple machines; all replicas must be updated to maintain consistency. If updates are not synchronized, some clients may read from stale replicas while others see the new data.

Concept_Name: Parallel searching
Core_Definition: A technique used to search a large corpus by dividing it among many machines and querying them simultaneously. This approach completes tasks faster by performing the search, sorting, and ranking processes across multiple nodes at the same time.
Key_Actors: Root server; parent servers; leaf servers; individual machines.
Operational_Workflow: The root server receives a search query and relays it to all parent servers. Each parent server repeats the query to all leaf nodes situated below it in the server tree. The leaf nodes search their specific shards of the corpus in parallel and return results to the parents. The parents perform sorting and ranking of these results in parallel before forwarding the top findings to the root server to construct the final response.
Rules_and_Constraints: This pattern is typically used for datasets larger than any one machine can hold. It allows developers to work within a latency budget by prioritizing fast answers; the root and parent servers do not have to wait for slow replies if a latency deadline is near.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In fan-in situations where many parallel replies converge on a single server, congestion problems can occur if the volume of replies exceeds the available network bandwidth or server capacity.

Concept_Name: Redundant servers
Core_Definition: Multiple clones or replications of a server that provide the same function to ensure capacity and availability. These servers allow the system to continue operating and route around hardware failures, which are considered a normal and expected part of large-scale distributed systems.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend replicas; master servers; slave servers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple replicas of a backend server are created so that any replica can produce the same response for a given request. A load balancer receives incoming traffic and selects one healthy replica to process the request based on algorithms like round-robin or least loaded. The load balancer continuously monitors the health of these redundant servers by sending health check queries dozens of times per second. If a server fails its health check, the load balancer stops sending it traffic until it is healthy again.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed to work around failures by anticipating them in software. Load balancers must always know which backends are alive and ready to accept requests. Redundant servers are used both for increasing capacity and for providing resiliency through sharding and replication.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In a master-slave configuration, a network partition can cause a split brain situation where both servers act as the master because they can no longer communicate. A naive least loaded algorithm can also cause a failure cycle if it sends an overwhelming surge of traffic to a single newly-rebooted redundant server.

Concept_Name: Caching function
Core_Definition: A performance optimization mechanism where servers store previously retrieved data to fulfill future requests more quickly. It is used to relieve pressure on leaf servers and mitigate network congestion.
Key_Actors: Parent servers; leaf servers; frontend servers; backend replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A server, often a parent in a server tree, retains a copy of data or query results that have been recently processed. When a new request for the same information arrives, the server provides the stored data directly rather than querying the backend or leaf servers again. This process reduces the number of queries that must fan out to the rest of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Caching can be implemented at various levels of a distributed system. Increasing the number of parent levels in a server tree can be used to improve the effectiveness of the cache.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Using cached data can lead to an inconsistent view of the system if the underlying state is updated but the cache still contains old information. During network partitions or outages, a system may be forced to serve outdated results from a cache to maintain availability.

Concept_Name: Cache effectiveness
Core_Definition: The degree to which a caching function reduces the workload or pressure on underlying leaf servers in a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Parent servers; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: Parent servers in a server tree provide a caching function to handle requests; the system can be designed with additional levels of parent servers to improve the effectiveness of this cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: Increasing the number of levels of parent servers is a technique used to improve cache effectiveness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Distributed State
Core_Definition: Data that is frequently updated and stored across multiple machines using replication and sharding to ensure capacity and accessibility.
Key_Actors: Root servers; leaf servers; replica servers; master servers; clients.
Operational_Workflow: A root server receives a request to store or retrieve data and determines which shard contains that part of the state; it forwards the request to the appropriate leaf server and the reply flows back up the tree; in cases involving large data transfers, a master server may reply with instructions on how to get the data rather than the data itself so the requester can contact the source directly.
Rules_and_Constraints: State must be sharded across individual machines to overcome storage and processing limits; each shard must be stored on multiple machines so that a single machine failure does not lose access to any state; updates to state require all replicas to be updated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Clients may read from stale replicas that have not yet been updated during a write operation; network partitioning can cause a split brain situation where a slave promotes itself to master while the original master is still running; according to the CAP Principle, systems cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition resistance.

Concept_Name: State
Core_Definition: Data that is in constant churn and frequently updated, such as a database.
Key_Actors: Databases; servers; machines.
Operational_Workflow: State is characterized by continuous activities including the arrival of new data, the updating of existing data such as moving messages between folders, and the deletion of data.
Rules_and_Constraints: State is contrasted with a corpus because it is not static and is updated much more frequently than once a week or periodically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If state is kept in only one location or on one machine, a single machine death results in the loss of access to 100 percent of that state.

Concept_Name: Corpus
Core_Definition: A large dataset that is relatively static or updated only periodically, such as a new edition of an encyclopedia or a weekly library update.
Key_Actors: Root servers; parent servers; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: Because a corpus is larger than any one machine can hold, it is divided into shards across many leaf machines; a root server receives a query and relays it through parents to all leaf nodes; leaf servers search their shards in parallel and send findings back to parents; parent servers sort and filter the results before the root combines them into a final answer.
Rules_and_Constraints: A corpus is distinguished from state by its relative lack of frequent updates; the server tree pattern is typically used to provide parallel searching across the corpus.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When working within a latency budget, parents and roots may not wait for slow replies from leaf servers if a deadline is near, prioritizing fast answers over perfect answers.

Concept_Name: Replication
Core_Definition: The practice of creating multiple clones or copies of a server or data shard to increase system capacity and ensure service continuity during hardware failures.
Key_Actors: Backend replicas; load balancers; master servers; slave servers; clients.
Operational_Workflow: A load balancer receives an incoming request and selects one backend replica to handle it using a method like round-robin or least loaded; the load balancer forwards the request and then relays the resulting response back to the requester; in systems that maintain state, any update must be propagated to all replicas of a shard to ensure they stay synchronized.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas must be clones of one another so that any replica can produce the same response for a given request; load balancers must send health check queries multiple times per second and stop traffic to any replica that fails; a slow start algorithm should be used to gradually increase traffic to new or rebooted replicas to prevent overloading.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Clients may read from stale replicas if an update has not yet propagated to all nodes; a naive least loaded algorithm can cause a cascade of failures by swamping a newly added or rebooted machine with all available traffic; a split brain situation occurs in master-slave configurations if a network partition breaks the heartbeat connection, causing the slave to incorrectly promote itself to master.

Concept_Name: Sharding
Core_Definition: A method of storing state or a large corpus by dividing the data into fractions called shards and distributing them across multiple individual machines.
Key_Actors: Leaf servers; root servers; parent servers; master servers; requesters.
Operational_Workflow: A large dataset or corpus is divided into chunks; each chunk is stored on a leaf server; when a request is made, a root or master server determines which shard contains the necessary data and either retrieves it from the leaf or directs the requester to contact the leaf machine directly; in a server tree, queries are fanned out to all leaves simultaneously to search the entire corpus in parallel.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sharding allows the system to store more data than can fit on a single machine; each shard can be replicated across multiple leaf servers to improve availability; parents and roots can be programmed to work within a latency budget by ignoring slow replies from specific shards when a deadline is near.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Fan-in congestion can occur when many shards send large replies back to a single parent or root server simultaneously, potentially overloading network links; sudden and unpredictable bursts of large replies may require specialized network equipment or rate-limiting to manage the flood.

Concept_Name: Consistency
Core_Definition: A guarantee in distributed systems that all nodes and users see the same data at the same time.
Key_Actors: Database replicas; relational databases like MySQL or Oracle; NoSQL systems; users.
Operational_Workflow: When an update is made to a piece of data, the system ensures the change is applied across all replicas; the system coordinates these updates so that any client reading from any replica receives the most recent and identical version of the information.
Rules_and_Constraints: Perfect consistency is required for financial applications to prevent simultaneous withdrawals from empty accounts; relational databases use ACID properties to ensure updates are atomic and result in a valid state; the CAP Principle dictates that a system cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition tolerance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some systems provide eventual consistency where updates propagate over time, meaning some users may see outdated answers until the deadline is reached; during a network partition, a system focused on consistency may refuse to respond to any requests rather than serve inconsistent or old data.

Concept_Name: Availability
Core_Definition: A guarantee that every request sent to a distributed system results in a response indicating whether the operation succeeded or failed.
Key_Actors: Replicas; load balancers; clients; system nodes.
Operational_Workflow: The system uses many replicas to ensure that users always have access to at least one working node; load balancers monitor the health of these nodes and route traffic away from failed components; if a system is too busy to process a request, it fulfills the availability guarantee by immediately sending an error message rather than timing out.
Rules_and_Constraints: Availability ensures the system is up and responsive to every query; it is a primary focus for systems like Cassandra or Dynamo that emphasize being able to serve requests even during network instability; the CAP Principle states that availability must sometimes be sacrificed to ensure data consistency during a partition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A complete network outage prevents even partition-tolerant systems from remaining available; in a partitioned state, a system might serve outdated results to maintain availability if it is configured to prioritize AP (availability and partition tolerance) over consistency.

Concept_Name: Partitioning
Core_Definition: A state where parts of a distributed system lose the ability to communicate with each other due to a network link failure or arbitrary message loss. It is a condition that a system must be designed to withstand to maintain partition tolerance.
Key_Actors: Nodes; servers; network links; heartbeat networks; master and slave servers.
Operational_Workflow: A distributed system operates with various nodes communicating over a network; a failure occurs such as a network link going down or packet loss; the nodes are separated into groups that can no longer exchange information; the system must then attempt to continue operating despite this lack of communication according to its design for partition tolerance.
Rules_and_Constraints: The CAP Principle states that a distributed system cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition resistance; any one or two can be achieved but not all three; packet loss is considered a temporary partitioning; if a system is read-only it is easier to make it partition tolerant.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Split brain occurs when a heartbeat network is partitioned and a slave promotes itself to master because it cannot see the original master which is still actually running; a complete network outage is a special case where even the most partition-tolerant system cannot work; some systems like Bigtable may become read-only or refuse to respond to requests during a partition to maintain consistency.

Concept_Name: Simultaneous reads and writes
Core_Definition: The processing of multiple data retrieval and data update requests at the same time within a distributed system. This is achieved by distributing the load across many machines to overcome the processing limits of a single machine.
Key_Actors: Client machines; replica servers; shards; root servers.
Operational_Workflow: State data is divided into fractions called shards; each shard is stored on multiple replica machines; incoming traffic is distributed among the various shards and their replicas; for a read request, the system accesses the specific shard containing the data; for a write or update request, all replicas of the relevant shard must be updated to ensure the state remains current.
Rules_and_Constraints: An individual machine can only process a limited number of simultaneous reads and writes; increasing the number of replicas allows a system to be designed to handle any number of simultaneous requests; all replicas must be updated when a write occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While an update process is happening, it is possible for some clients to read from stale replicas that have not yet been updated; updates can be confounded by reads to an out-of-date cache leading to an inconsistent view of the data.

Concept_Name: Stale replicas
Core_Definition: Replicas of a data shard that have not yet been updated with the most recent write requests. These nodes contain older versions of data during the time it takes for an update to propagate through the system.
Key_Actors: Replicas; leaf servers; users; root servers.
Operational_Workflow: A write request is initiated to update a piece of state; the update begins propagating to the various replicas of a shard; a client sends a read request to the system; the system directs the user to a replica that has not yet received the update; the replica serves the old data to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems that do not guarantee consistency may provide eventual consistency; they may guarantee that an update will propagate to all replicas within a certain amount of time; until that time is reached, some queries will receive out-of-date answers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In systems focusing on availability and partition tolerance, such as Cassandra or Dynamo, clients are expected to occasionally receive outdated results; network congestion or outages can cause the propagation of updates to take minutes or hours.

Concept_Name: Out-of-date cache
Core_Definition: A temporary storage mechanism containing data that is no longer consistent with the primary state. This occurs when the underlying data has been updated but the cached version has not been refreshed or invalidated.
Key_Actors: Cache storage; parent servers; leaf servers; clients.
Operational_Workflow: Data is stored in a cache, such as at the parent level of a server tree, to improve performance or relieve pressure on leaf servers; a write operation updates the primary state of the data; a subsequent read request is fulfilled using the cached data before it has been updated to reflect the write; the client receives a result that does not match the current state.
Rules_and_Constraints: Caching may be done at various levels of a distributed system; more levels of parents in a server tree may improve cache effectiveness; the ability to manage a latency budget often involves using such components to provide fast though potentially imperfect answers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: State updates using cached data can lead to an inconsistent view of the system; in some cases, such as the CAP Principle's sacrifice of consistency, systems may intentionally serve cached data even if it is out-of-date to ensure the system remains available.

Concept_Name: Distributed file system
Core_Definition: A system where petabytes of data are spread out over thousands of machines and files are split into gigabyte-sized chunks stored on multiple machines for redundancy.
Key_Actors: Master server; machines storing data blocks; requester or client system.
Operational_Workflow: File system operations go through a master server that uses inode-like information to determine which machines to involve in an operation; for large read requests the master determines the location of the data and replies to the requestor with a list of machines that have the data; the requestor then contacts those machines directly to retrieve the data rather than having the master relay it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Files must be split into chunks; each chunk must be stored on multiple machines to ensure redundancy; the system must permit the creation of files larger than those that would fit on a single machine; the master server should not act as a middle man for large data transfers to avoid becoming overloaded.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because each shard or chunk is stored on multiple machines a single machine failure does not result in a loss of access to the state or data.

Concept_Name: Master server
Core_Definition: A central server in a distributed file system that tracks the list of files and identifies where their constituent chunks are located.
Key_Actors: Master server; slave server; machines storing data blocks; inodes.
Operational_Workflow: The server receives requests for file operations and uses inode-like information to identify the specific machines involved; it manages the metadata of the file system rather than the raw data itself; in a master-slave relationship a slave maintains a copy of the state and takes over if a heartbeat check fails.
Rules_and_Constraints: The master server stores the inodes or per-file lists of data blocks; it must delegate large data transfers to other servers to avoid becoming a performance bottleneck.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a master server attempts to relay huge chunks of data directly it can quickly become overloaded; if the heartbeat network between a master and slave is partitioned the system may experience a split brain situation where two masters exist simultaneously.

Concept_Name: Chunks
Core_Definition: Gigabyte-sized segments of a file that are distributed across multiple machines in a distributed file system.
Key_Actors: Master server; machines storing data blocks.
Operational_Workflow: Large files are divided into these segments so they can be spread over thousands of machines; the master server maintains a list identifying which machines hold which specific segments.
Rules_and_Constraints: Chunks are typically gigabyte-sized; each chunk must be stored on multiple machines for redundancy purposes; this method allows for the storage of files that are larger than the capacity of any individual machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Inodes
Core_Definition: Per-file lists of data blocks used to determine which machines to involve in a file system operation.
Key_Actors: Master server; UNIX file system.
Operational_Workflow: In a distributed context the master server stores these lists to identify the location of file chunks; in a standard UNIX system they are used to look up data blocks after a directory search.
Rules_and_Constraints: Inodes provide the mapping necessary for a master server to manage file operations across distributed machines; they are considered a key part of the internal workings of the operating system used to design and operate distributed systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Delegate replies
Core_Definition: A communication pattern where a master server responds to a request by providing instructions on how to find the data rather than providing the data itself. This allows the requester to contact the source directly to avoid overloading the master server.
Key_Actors: Master server; Requestor; Source machines or servers holding data chunks.
Operational_Workflow: A requestor sends a large data request to a master server; the master server identifies which specific machines contain the required data chunks; the master server replies to the requestor with a list of those machines; the requestor then contacts the source machines directly to perform the data transfer.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method is used when large amounts of data are being transferred to prevent a central server from becoming a bottleneck; it is a common feature in distributed file systems where files are split into chunks across thousands of machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the master server acted as a middleman for large transfers instead of delegating the reply, it would quickly become overloaded while receiving and relaying huge chunks of data.

Concept_Name: CAP Principle
Core_Definition: A theorem stating that a distributed system can guarantee only two of the following three attributes simultaneously: consistency, availability, and partition resistance. Designers must choose which attributes to prioritize based on the needs of the service.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; Replicas; Nodes; Users; Relational databases; NoSQL storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: When designing a system, engineers select a combination of two attributes such as consistency and availability (CA), consistency and partition tolerance (CP), or availability and partition tolerance (AP); the system then operates according to these priorities, either sacrificing data uniformity, uptime, or communication resilience during failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is impossible to achieve all three attributes at once; relational databases like MySQL typically choose CA; newer storage systems like Bigtable choose CP; systems like Cassandra or Dynamo choose AP.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A complete network outage is a special case where even the most partition-tolerant system is unable to work; packet loss is considered a temporary partitioning of the system.

Concept_Name: Partition resistance
Core_Definition: The ability of a system to continue operating despite arbitrary message loss or the failure of part of the system's communication infrastructure. It ensures the system remains functional even when machines lose the ability to talk to each other.
Key_Actors: Network links; Nodes or machines; Replicas; Master and slave servers; Heartbeat networks.
Operational_Workflow: A network link failure or packet loss occurs between components of a distributed system; the system uses predefined logic to continue serving requests or managing state; nodes may continue to work independently or follow specific protocols to prevent errors like split brain.
Rules_and_Constraints: Partition tolerance is a requirement for systems that must operate across less reliable media such as the Internet; the system must decide whether to remain available or stay consistent when a partition occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A split brain situation can occur if a heartbeat network is partitioned, leading a slave server to promote itself to master while the original master is still active; a complete network outage prevents any level of partition resistance from maintaining service.

Concept_Name: Eventual consistency
Core_Definition: A consistency model where updates are guaranteed to propagate to all replicas in a certain amount of time, rather than happening simultaneously across the entire system. During the propagation delay, different users may see different versions of the data.
Key_Actors: Replicas; Clients or users; Update requests; Distributed databases.
Operational_Workflow: An update request is made to one replica in a distributed system; the system accepts the change and begins the process of copying that update to all other replicas; until the update is fully propagated, some queries receive the new data while others receive older answers; eventually, all replicas reflect the same updated state.
Rules_and_Constraints: This model is often used in systems that prioritize availability and partition tolerance over perfect consistency; it is frequently associated with the BASE (Basically Available Soft-state services with Eventual consistency) model.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large network delays or outages can extend the time it takes to reach consistency from seconds to hours; in specific applications like banking, eventual consistency can be exploited to withdraw more money than is available if updates do not propagate fast enough.

Concept_Name: Error code
Core_Definition: A specific reply sent by a system to a requester indicating the status of a request, such as a message to try again later.
Key_Actors: The system or component detecting an internal state; the client or requester receiving the reply.
Operational_Workflow: The system detects that it is overloaded or otherwise unable to process a request; it generates a specific code to communicate this status; it sends the code immediately to the requester so they do not have to wait for a timeout.
Rules_and_Constraints: Providing an immediate response via an error code is more favorable than forcing a client to wait for a long period before giving up.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: System is up
Core_Definition: A state of availability where a system guarantees that every request receives a response indicating whether it was successful or failed.
Key_Actors: The distributed system or service; the clients or users making requests.
Operational_Workflow: A user or client sends a request to the system; the system processes the request; the system provides a definitive response about the success or failure of that request.
Rules_and_Constraints: Availability requires that the system is able to report failure rather than leaving a request unanswered; a system that detects overload and sends an immediate error code is still considered to be providing availability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Partition tolerance
Core_Definition: The ability of a distributed system to continue operating despite arbitrary message loss or the failure of part of the system.
Key_Actors: Replicas; master and slave servers; communication networks.
Operational_Workflow: A failure occurs in the system such as message loss or a broken communication path; the system maintains operations; depending on the design the system may continue as read-only or refuse certain requests to maintain other properties like consistency.
Rules_and_Constraints: According to the CAP Principle, partition tolerance cannot be guaranteed simultaneously with both consistency and availability; systems that are read-only are easier to make partition tolerant because replicas do not need to communicate updates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A complete network outage is a special case where even the most partition-tolerant system is unable to work; a split brain situation can occur if a heartbeat network is partitioned and a slave promotes itself to master while the original master is still functioning.

Concept_Name: Network link going down
Core_Definition: A failure in the communication infrastructure that causes the machines involved in providing a service to lose the ability to talk to each other.
Key_Actors: The machines providing the service; the network infrastructure connecting them; master and slave servers.
Operational_Workflow: A communication link fails; the involved machines become partitioned from one another; the system must then use partition tolerance techniques to decide how to handle requests while communication is severed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This event is cited as the simplest example of a system partition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the failure occurs on a dedicated heartbeat network used for health checks, it can lead to a split brain where two servers both attempt to act as the master; a complete network outage is a related but more severe edge case where no communication is possible.

Concept_Name: Master–slave relationship
Core_Definition: A setup where two servers cooperate and maintain a complete copy of the state so that one can take over if the other fails.
Key_Actors: Master server; slave server.
Operational_Workflow: Both servers maintain a complete copy of the system state; the slave monitors the master’s status through a periodic health check; if the master fails, the slave promotes itself to the master role to maintain service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Both servers must maintain identical copies of the state; the slave must be able to detect the master's failure to take over.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the communication link between the two servers is lost but the master is still functioning, the slave may incorrectly promote itself, leading to a split brain scenario.

Concept_Name: Heartbeat
Core_Definition: A periodic health check conducted between two servers to monitor operational status.
Key_Actors: Master server; slave server.
Operational_Workflow: Servers exchange signals at regular intervals to confirm they are still functioning; the absence of these signals is used to determine that a server has failed.
Rules_and_Constraints: These checks are performed periodically; they are used to trigger a role promotion in a master-slave relationship.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A loss of heartbeat can occur due to a network partition rather than an actual server failure, leading to incorrect system states.

Concept_Name: Heartbeat network
Core_Definition: A dedicated network used for the periodic health checks transmitted between servers.
Key_Actors: Master server; slave server; specialized network infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: Health check signals travel across this dedicated network to allow servers to monitor each other's status independently of other traffic; if this network fails, the monitoring server assumes the other has died.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is often a dedicated network specifically for the purpose of maintaining heartbeats.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the heartbeat network is partitioned, the slave will not know the original master is still up and will promote itself.

Concept_Name: Split brain
Core_Definition: A system failure where two servers both assume the role of master at the same time.
Key_Actors: Master server; slave server.
Operational_Workflow: The heartbeat network between a master and slave is partitioned; the slave server stops receiving health checks and promotes itself to master; the original master remains functional and continues to act as master because it is unaware of the partition; the system breaks due to having two simultaneous masters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This occurs specifically when servers are up but unable to communicate on the heartbeat network.

Concept_Name: Packet loss
Core_Definition: A condition in a distributed system that is treated as a temporary partitioning of the system within the framework of the CAP Principle.
Key_Actors: Distributed system nodes; communication messages.
Operational_Workflow: Messages or data packets are sent between nodes in a distributed system; these messages fail to reach their intended destination; the system experiences a temporary break in communication that mimics a network partition; the system must then rely on its partition tolerance design to manage the loss.
Rules_and_Constraints: Packet loss must be handled as a form of partitioning when designing for the CAP Principle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the loss of communication is total or sustained rather than temporary, it transitions into a complete network outage.

Concept_Name: Complete network outage
Core_Definition: An extreme state of network failure where all communication between components in a distributed system is severed.
Key_Actors: Network infrastructure; all nodes and servers within the distributed system.
Operational_Workflow: All communication links between servers or nodes fail; nodes become entirely isolated from one another; the system loses its ability to coordinate or share state across any part of the network; the service ceases to function as a distributed entity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Even the most partition-tolerant systems are unable to work in this situation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Traditional relational databases
Core_Definition: Data management systems that prioritize consistency and availability by using transactions to ensure updates are atomic.
Key_Actors: Oracle; MySQL; PostgreSQL; database nodes; end users.
Operational_Workflow: The database receives an update or write request; it employs transactions to ensure the update propagates completely or not at all; the system ensures that after each transaction the database is in a valid state; all users reading from the system see the same data at the same time.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems must adhere to ACID principles including Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability; they are categorized as CA (Consistent and Available) systems in the CAP triangle; updates must be atomic and propagate to all nodes simultaneously to ensure a uniform view of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because these systems prioritize being consistent and available, they are typically unable to handle network partitions without sacrificing one of their primary guarantees.

Concept_Name: Consistent and available (CA)
Core_Definition: A classification of systems in the CAP Principle that guarantees all nodes see the same data simultaneously and every request receives a response.
Key_Actors: Traditional relational databases; Oracle; MySQL; PostgreSQL; system replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A request is sent to the system; the system ensures all replicas have the most recent data; updates are processed using database techniques like transactions to ensure they are atomic; the system provides a response to the user regarding the success or failure of the request.
Rules_and_Constraints: All users must see the same update go live at the same time even if they are reading from different replicas; the system must guarantee a response for every request; these systems generally sacrifice partition tolerance as it is not possible to achieve all three CAP attributes simultaneously.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the presence of a network partition, a CA system will fail to maintain its guarantees and may become unable to operate.

Concept_Name: Transactions
Core_Definition: A database technique used by traditional relational databases to ensure that updates are processed as a single unit that either completes entirely or not at all.
Key_Actors: Traditional relational databases such as Oracle, MySQL, and PostgreSQL; system users or clients performing data updates.
Operational_Workflow: The system applies a set of updates through a transaction process to ensure they propagate completely to all users; this allows all nodes to see the same state at the same time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Transactions must be atomic to ensure updates are all or nothing; they are a core part of the ACID model which includes atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an update cannot be fully propagated, the transaction ensures it does not go live at all to prevent inconsistent data views.

Concept_Name: Atomic
Core_Definition: A property of database updates where a transaction is treated as a single unit that succeeds completely or fails completely.
Key_Actors: Relational databases; transactions; update requests.
Operational_Workflow: When an update is initiated, the system ensures the change propagates fully across the system; if any part of the process fails, the entire update is discarded to maintain a valid state.
Rules_and_Constraints: Atomicity is defined as being all or nothing; it is a fundamental component of the ACID acronym used to describe the guarantees of relational databases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Newer storage systems
Core_Definition: Distributed data storage technologies that prioritize consistency and partition tolerance over constant availability.
Key_Actors: Systems such as Hbase, Redis, and Bigtable; NoSQL databases.
Operational_Workflow: These systems store state across distributed nodes and manage requests by focusing on the CP side of the CAP triangle; they ensure that data remains consistent across the network even if the system is partitioned.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems follow the CAP Principle which dictates that only two of three attributes can be achieved simultaneously; they are often classified as NoSQL.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a partition occurs, these systems may become read-only or refuse to respond to any requests to avoid permitting some users to see old data while others see fresh data.

Concept_Name: Consistency and partition tolerance (CP)
Core_Definition: A combination of two distributed system attributes where all nodes see the same data at the same time and the system remains functional despite message loss or network failure.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; storage systems like Hbase, Redis, and Bigtable.
Operational_Workflow: The system processes requests to ensure that every node maintains an identical view of the data; if a network partition occurs, the system maintains this consistency by restricting certain operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Based on the CAP Principle, a system that chooses CP must sacrifice availability; it must guarantee that it will not provide inconsistent or outdated data to different users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During a network partition, the system may stop responding to queries or transition to a read-only state to prevent the risk of inconsistency.

Concept_Name: Read-only
Core_Definition: A state where a system or its replicas allow data to be accessed but do not permit updates or changes to that data.
Key_Actors: Replicas; storage systems such as Hbase, Redis, and Bigtable.
Operational_Workflow: In a read-only configuration, replicas do not need to communicate with each other because there are no state updates to propagate. This makes it simpler to maintain the system during a network partition. Some systems are designed to automatically switch to a read-only mode when partitioned to prevent inconsistent data views.
Rules_and_Constraints: A system that is read-only is inherently easier to make partition tolerant. Systems that prioritize consistency and partition tolerance (CP) may choose to become read-only rather than risk serving inconsistent data during a failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a system is partitioned, it may refuse to respond to any requests at all instead of becoming read-only if that is how it manages the sacrifice of availability.

Concept_Name: Availability and partition tolerance (AP)
Core_Definition: A classification of distributed systems that prioritize staying operational and responsive during network failures over maintaining immediate data consistency across all nodes.
Key_Actors: Cassandra; Risk; Dynamo; globally distributed networks; clients.
Operational_Workflow: AP systems emphasize always being able to serve requests even if it means some clients receive outdated results. They are often deployed in globally distributed networks where replicas must communicate over less reliable media such as the Internet.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems sacrifice the consistency attribute of the CAP Principle to ensure the system remains available and partition tolerant. They are often described as BASE systems: Basically Available Soft-state services with Eventual consistency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the event of network congestion or outages, updates may take minutes or hours to propagate; during this time, different clients may receive different versions of the same data.

Concept_Name: Cassandra
Core_Definition: A newer storage system designed to prioritize availability and partition tolerance within the framework of the CAP Principle.
Key_Actors: Replicas; globally distributed networks; clients.
Operational_Workflow: Cassandra functions by emphasizing the ability to always serve requests even during network partitions or message loss. It is typically used in globally distributed environments where replicas talk to each other over the Internet.
Rules_and_Constraints: Cassandra is classified as an AP system, meaning it focuses on being available and partition tolerant rather than guaranteeing that all nodes see the same data at the same time. It follows a weaker consistency model often referred to as NoSQL or BASE.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Risk
Core_Definition: A storage system that focuses on the availability and partition tolerance (AP) aspects of the CAP Principle.
Key_Actors: Replicas; globally distributed networks.
Operational_Workflow: As an AP system, Risk is designed to emphasize always being able to serve requests even if some clients receive outdated results. It is often used in globally distributed networks where communication between replicas happens over less reliable media.
Rules_and_Constraints: Risk sacrifices perfect consistency to ensure that the system remains available and can tolerate partitions in the network.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dynamo
Core_Definition: A storage system that focuses on availability and partition tolerance (AP) as defined by the CAP Principle.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The system emphasizes always being able to serve requests even if it means some clients receive outdated results.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used in situations where availability and partition tolerance are prioritized over perfect consistency; it is often utilized in globally distributed networks where each replica communicates with others via less reliable media such as the Internet.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Clients may receive outdated results during the time it takes for updates to propagate across the system.

Concept_Name: SQL
Core_Definition: Traditional relational databases that are categorized as consistent and available (CA) within the CAP Principle.
Key_Actors: Oracle; MySQL; PostgreSQL.
Operational_Workflow: These databases use transactions and other specific database techniques to ensure that updates are atomic and propagate completely or not at all.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must guarantee that all users see the same state at the same time; it follows the ACID model to maintain database integrity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ACID
Core_Definition: An acronym representing Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability; used by relational databases to describe their operational guarantees.
Key_Actors: SQL and other relational databases.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Transactions must be all or nothing; the database must remain in a valid state after each transaction; concurrent transactions must produce the same results as if they were executed serially; a committed transaction's data must not be lost in the event of a crash or other problem.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Atomicity
Core_Definition: A component of the ACID model meaning that database transactions are treated as all or nothing.
Key_Actors: Traditional relational databases such as Oracle; MySQL; and PostgreSQL.
Operational_Workflow: Databases use transactions and other techniques to ensure that updates propagate completely across the system or do not occur at all.
Rules_and_Constraints: Updates must be atomic to ensure that all users see the same state at the same time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Isolation
Core_Definition: A property of database transactions where concurrent transactions yield the same results as if they were executed one after another in a serial order.
Key_Actors: Traditional relational databases such as Oracle, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
Operational_Workflow: The system manages multiple transactions occurring at the same time to ensure they do not interfere with each other; it processes these simultaneous requests so that the final state of the database remains consistent with a sequential execution of those same tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is one of the four components of the ACID model; it is used by relational databases to ensure that updates are atomic and users see a valid, consistent state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Durability
Core_Definition: A guarantee that once a transaction has been committed, the resulting data is permanently recorded and will not be lost even if the system crashes or encounters other problems.
Key_Actors: Traditional relational databases including Oracle, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
Operational_Workflow: After a transaction is successfully completed and committed, the system ensures the data is saved in a way that survives technical failures; this functions as a critical part of the ACID framework used to maintain data integrity in consistent and available systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a required attribute of the ACID model; it ensures that committed data remains intact regardless of subsequent system failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: NoSQL
Core_Definition: A category of modern storage systems that provide weaker consistency models compared to traditional relational databases.
Key_Actors: Storage systems such as Hbase, Redis, Bigtable, Cassandra, Risk, and Dynamo.
Operational_Workflow: These systems manage distributed state by prioritizing different sides of the CAP Principle; some systems focus on consistency and partition tolerance by becoming read-only during a network failure, while others focus on availability and partition tolerance by continuing to serve requests even if some results are outdated.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems often follow the BASE model instead of the ACID model; they are frequently used in globally distributed networks where components communicate over less reliable media like the Internet.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In systems focusing on consistency and partition tolerance, the service may refuse to respond to any requests during a partition to avoid serving inconsistent data; in systems focusing on availability, some clients may receive outdated answers until updates propagate.

Concept_Name: BASE
Core_Definition: An acronym used to describe the properties of NoSQL databases that prioritize availability over strict consistency. It stands for Basically Available Soft-state services with Eventual consistency.
Key_Actors: Storage systems and databases that provide weaker consistency models than traditional SQL systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system remains basically available to handle requests even during partial failures; the internal state is considered soft and is updated over time; the system ensures that updates eventually propagate to all replicas so that all nodes will eventually see the same data.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is the model used by systems that fall on the availability and partition tolerance side of the CAP Principle; it serves as an alternative to the ACID model for distributed storage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Until the eventual consistency deadline is reached or if there is network congestion or an outage, some queries may receive old or out-of-date answers while others see the new data.

Concept_Name: Basically Available Soft-state services with Eventual consistency
Core_Definition: A model referred to by the acronym BASE that describes databases with weaker consistency models than traditional relational databases.
Key_Actors: NoSQL databases such as Cassandra, Risk, and Dynamo; replicas; clients.
Operational_Workflow: Systems process updates to state and guarantee that these updates will propagate to all replicas within a certain amount of time; during this period, some queries may receive the new data while others receive older, out-of-date answers.
Rules_and_Constraints: This model is typically associated with systems that focus on availability and partition tolerance within the CAP Principle; it serves as an alternative to the ACID model used by relational databases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Updates may be delayed for minutes due to network congestion or hours due to network outages; in financial applications, this lack of consistency can lead to errors such as allowing simultaneous withdrawals from the same account at different locations.

Concept_Name: Loosely coupled system
Core_Definition: A distributed system design where each component has little or no knowledge of the internal workings of the other components.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; components; subsystems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Each component interacts with others through an abstract interface that hides implementation details; this allows a subsystem to be replaced by a new one with a completely different implementation without requiring changes to other parts of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should be able to evolve and change without disruption; the design should allow subsystems to be replaced while the system is up and running; new and old versions can be run in parallel to compare results for quality control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system is not loosely coupled, a change in one service might require every frontend using that service to be rewritten; some replacements may require bug-for-bug compatibility where the new system must reproduce the exact errors of the old system before deployment.

Concept_Name: Abstraction
Core_Definition: A design technique where each component provides an interface that is defined in a way that hides the implementation details from other components.
Key_Actors: Components; services; developers; frontends.
Operational_Workflow: A service defines a high-level way to interact with its functions, such as a spell checker taking in text and returning corrections, rather than exposing its internal data structures or specific technologies like lexicons or machine learning.
Rules_and_Constraints: A good level of abstraction is required to build a loosely coupled system; it must be designed to remain consistent even if the underlying technology or method of the service changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A bad level of abstraction provides too much access to internals, such as exposing a lexicon for spell checking, which forces all dependent systems to be rewritten if the internal implementation changes.

Concept_Name: Interface
Core_Definition: A protocol or defined method that governs how one server or component communicates with another.
Key_Actors: Servers; components; Application Programming Interfaces (APIs).
Operational_Workflow: A component or server provides an interface that other components use to send requests and receive replies; this interface acts as a boundary that enables composition of smaller systems into larger distributed services.
Rules_and_Constraints: The interface must be defined through abstraction to hide implementation details and maintain loose coupling; it allows for the parallel operation and testing of new system versions against old ones.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During the deployment of a new system, the interface may need to be tested to ensure it is bug-for-bug compatible with the previous version to maintain consistency in sensitive operations like financial transactions.

Concept_Name: Implementation details
Core_Definition: The specific internal code, technology, or methods used by a component to perform its function, which are hidden from other components via abstraction.
Key_Actors: Subsystems; components; frontends; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A component provides a defined abstract interface to the rest of the system; the implementation details perform the work required by the interface; because these details are hidden, the entire implementation can be replaced by a different one as long as it adheres to the same abstract interface.
Rules_and_Constraints: A system is loosely coupled if each component has little or no knowledge of the internals of other components; abstraction must be defined in a way that hides implementation details to allow the system to evolve.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a bad level of abstraction is used, such as providing access to a lexicon instead of a generalized spell check service, any change to the implementation details would require all frontend components to be rewritten.

Concept_Name: Quality control
Core_Definition: A process of comparing the output of a new system version against an old version while they are running in parallel to ensure the new version is ready for production.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; old system; new system; users.
Operational_Workflow: A load balancer is programmed to send all requests to both the old and new versions of a system; the results from the old system are sent to users while results from the new system are collected; these results are compared to ensure the new system is performing correctly; the new system is enhanced until its results are quantifiably better than the old system.
Rules_and_Constraints: The comparison of results is used to decide when a system is ready for production; as a caution, the new system may initially be given only one percent of traffic to check for user complaints before a full transition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Run in parallel
Core_Definition: The simultaneous execution of tasks across multiple machines or the simultaneous operation of multiple versions of a service.
Key_Actors: Backend replicas; leaf servers; parent servers; load balancers; old and new system versions.
Operational_Workflow: In a server tree, a query is sent to multiple leaf servers that search their specific shards of a corpus at the same time; in a server with multiple backends, different types of queries such as web search and advertisements are processed concurrently; during system upgrades, a load balancer may send the same requests to both an old and a new system version simultaneously.
Rules_and_Constraints: Work is divided over many machines to achieve size, scale, speed, and reliability; parallel processing allows a system to meet a latency budget by not waiting for one process to complete before the next begins.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Fan-in situations where parallel replies converge on a single frontend can lead to network congestion and overloaded servers if bandwidth is insufficient to handle sudden bursts.

Concept_Name: Bug-for-bug compatible
Core_Definition: A design requirement where a new system must replicate not only the intended features of an old system but also its specific errors or flaws.
Key_Actors: Developers; engineers; legacy systems; new system replacements.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify specific bugs or non-standard behaviors in a legacy system; developers reverse-engineer these behaviors and emulate them in the code of the new system; both systems are run in parallel to confirm that their outputs match exactly; once the new system is proven to be bug-for-bug compatible, it is activated as the primary system.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is used for systems requiring high precision and accuracy, such as financial systems; it ensures confidence in a new system before it offers new functionality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Once a company has gained confidence in the new system and the old system is disabled, the code used to emulate the legacy bugs is typically the first thing to be removed.

Concept_Name: Decommissioned
Core_Definition: The final stage in a system migration where an old system is completely shut down and removed from service after a replacement system has successfully taken over all its functions.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; old systems; new systems; users; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: A new version of a service is run in parallel with the old system while a load balancer sends requests to both. Engineers collect and compare results from both systems for quality control. Initially, the new system might handle a small fraction of traffic such as 1 percent. If no issues or user complaints arise, the new system takes a larger fraction of traffic until it handles all responses. Once the new system is fully operational and the old system is no longer receiving traffic, the old system is decommissioned.
Rules_and_Constraints: The new system must be proven to produce results that are as good as or quantifiably better than the old system before the old system is decommissioned. In some cases, the new system must be proven bug-for-bug compatible with the old system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Emulation
Core_Definition: The practice of designing a new system to replicate the exact behavior and flaws of an older system to ensure compatibility during a transition.
Key_Actors: Developers; engineers; new systems; old systems.
Operational_Workflow: When replacing a complex or calcified system, engineers may find that the new system needs to produce identical results to the old one to prove its reliability. Developers reverse-engineer specific behaviors or bugs from the old system and implement them into the new system. The two systems are then run in parallel and their outputs are compared. Once the new system is activated as the primary system, the emulation code can be removed as an improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: High-precision environments like financial systems may require a new system to be bug-for-bug compatible before it can be deployed or offer new functionality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Currency conversion bug
Core_Definition: A non-standard calculation flaw found in an obsolete financial system that produced slightly inaccurate monetary results.
Key_Actors: Tom Limoncelli; Cibernet engineers; developers; the old financial system; the new replacement system.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers discovered the bug while comparing results between a new system and an old pre-web technology system. To ensure the results matched down to the penny for comparison purposes, developers reverse-engineered the non-standard conversion method and emulated it in the new system. Once the new system gained company confidence and was activated as the primary system, the code emulating the bug was removed as the first improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: In the context of the case study, the new system had to prove it was bug-for-bug compatible with the old system before it could be fully deployed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Speed
Core_Definition: A competitive advantage and critical design factor for services where fast response times and high throughput are required to satisfy users and maintain revenue.
Key_Actors: Users; engineers; developers; hardware components including disks, RAM, and solid-state drives; network infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers design systems using best estimates of processing time and then build prototypes to test those assumptions. Common transactions are broken down into smaller steps, such as authentication, database index lookups, and data retrieval, to estimate the time each step takes. If estimates or prototypes show a design is too slow, engineers eliminate the design or adjust it by changing components, such as moving an index from disk to RAM or implementing an asynchronous design.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users perceive a website as sluggish if replies take more than 200 ms. Delays as small as 50 ms can lead to sharp drops in revenue. Systems must be designed to meet a latency budget. Calculations must account for physical constraints like the speed of light for network latency and mechanical limitations for disk seeks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When fast answers are more important than perfect answers in a server tree, parents and roots may give up on slow backends and generate a page with only the information available to meet a latency deadline. Asynchronous designs can be used to return control to a user quickly even if the actual work is still lagging in the background.

Concept_Name: Response times
Core_Definition: The amount of time it takes for a service to reply to a request, which is a critical factor in how users perceive the speed and responsiveness of a web site or interactive service.
Key_Actors: Users; interactive systems; web sites; frontends; backends; network routers; disk read arms and platters.
Operational_Workflow: A user sends a request through a web browser which travels across the network to a service; the request may undergo authentication and index lookups to determine data location; the service retrieves the required message or data from storage; the system formats the response and transmits it back across the network to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users perceive replies as instant if they return in 200 ms or less; network latency often consumes a significant portion of the time budget; systems should be designed based on estimates of processing speed; the fastest design is not always chosen if it is significantly more expensive than a sufficient design.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Delays as small as 50 ms can lead to sharp drops in revenue; if a backend is slow, a frontend might give up on it and generate a page using default information or leaving that area blank to stay within a latency budget; asynchronous designs can be used where the server queues a request and returns control to the user quickly even if the work is lagging.

Concept_Name: Throughput
Core_Definition: The total volume of work or requests that a system can process successfully over a period of time.
Key_Actors: Batch systems; non-interactive systems.
Operational_Workflow: Work enters the system at a certain rate; the system processes this work using available resources; the resulting volume of completed tasks is measured to ensure it keeps pace with the demands of the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: In batch and non-interactive systems, total throughput must meet or exceed the incoming flow of work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Incoming flow of work
Core_Definition: The rate or quantity of tasks, queries, or data arriving at a system to be processed.
Key_Actors: Users; batch systems; non-interactive systems; upstream services.
Operational_Workflow: Tasks or queries are generated by users or other systems; these requests flow into the processing system; the system must handle these arrivals by maintaining a high enough throughput to prevent a backlog.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must be designed to ensure that its processing capacity is sufficient to handle the arrival rate of work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Prototypes
Core_Definition: Experimental or preliminary versions of a system used to test design assumptions and verify performance estimates before full-scale implementation.
Key_Actors: Engineers; designers; business decision-makers.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers create multiple designs and use simple estimating exercises to eliminate those that are too slow; a prototype is built for a promising design to test specific assumptions; the system is remeasured during construction; the design is adjusted based on findings or the process restarts if estimates were wrong.
Rules_and_Constraints: Prototypes are time-consuming to build and should be preceded by estimation exercises involving common transactions; they provide empirical evidence to back up speed and cost estimates for business decisions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the prototype reveals that the initial design estimates were incorrect, the team must go back to the first step of the design process and iterate based on what was learned.

Concept_Name: Estimates
Core_Definition: Simple calculating exercises used at the beginning of the design process to determine if a design is worth pursuing by breaking transactions into smaller steps.
Key_Actors: Engineers; design teams; business decision-makers; prototypes.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers pick a few common transactions and break them down into smaller steps; each step is assigned a time estimate based on known approximations; designs that do not meet speed requirements are eliminated; remaining designs are backed by prototypes and compared against cost requirements to inform final business decisions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designers use best estimates of processing speed and build prototypes to test assumptions; knowledge of operating system internals is required to create accurate estimates; the fastest design is not automatically selected if a cheaper design is sufficient; computation time is often ignored compared to network and disk delays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If estimates or prototypes do not guide the process well, engineers must remeasure and adjust the design; if a design is found to be slow during estimation, it is discarded to avoid wasted effort.

Concept_Name: Disk access
Core_Definition: The process of reading or writing data to a disk which is slow due to the mechanical operations involved.
Key_Actors: Read arm; tracks; platters or spindles; read head; data blocks; solid-state drives.
Operational_Workflow: The read arm moves to the correct track; the platter spins until the required block is under the read head; the head reads the data blocks sequentially or individually.
Rules_and_Constraints: Typical mechanical disk access takes 10 ms; a spindle can process only one request at a time; reading sequential blocks is nearly as fast as reading one block if the blocks are adjacent; solid-state drives are faster than mechanical disks but more expensive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Reading adjacent blocks avoids the need for multiple slow seeks; solid-state drives avoid the mechanical delays associated with spinning platters.

Concept_Name: Network delays
Core_Definition: The time required for data packets to travel between systems which is primarily limited by the speed of light and hardware processing time.
Key_Actors: Packets; routers; fiber-optic electronics; users; datacenters; programs receiving data.
Operational_Workflow: Data is packaged into packets and sent across a network; routers process the packets and fiber-optic electronics convert signals between wired and light-based media; the receiving program waits for the full packet to arrive before processing it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Network speed is limited by the speed of light; a packet takes approximately 75 ms to travel between California and the Netherlands; designers must budget for round-trip times by accounting for both the request and the reply; local network communication is not instant due to the time required to assemble and disassemble packets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Additional delays occur due to router processing time and hardware conversion electronics; network equipment may use dynamic buffer space to handle bursts; backends may use rate-limiting to avoid congestion; frontends can manage congestion by slowing down queries or implementing emergency measures.

Concept_Name: RAM
Core_Definition: A high-speed electronic storage medium used to store frequently accessed data such as indices to improve system performance over mechanical alternatives.
Key_Actors: RAM storage; lookup trees; datacenter servers.
Operational_Workflow: Data or index lookups are stored in RAM rather than on disk; the system navigates lookup trees to find information; the process may involve sending packets between machines within a datacenter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reading from RAM takes 0.002 ms; RAM access is 5,000 times faster than mechanical disk access; holding a lookup tree in RAM can allow a system to span large amounts of data while maintaining a small latency budget such as 2.5 ms for a five-packet lookup.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Solid-state drives (SSDs)
Core_Definition: Storage devices that do not have mechanical spinning platters and provide significantly faster data access than traditional mechanical disks.
Key_Actors: Hardware components; storage systems; distributed services requiring high performance.
Operational_Workflow: SSDs retrieve data electronically without the delays associated with mechanical components like read arms or spinning platters; this allows them to avoid the 10 ms seek time typical of traditional disks, though the text notes they are more expensive.
Rules_and_Constraints: SSDs are used when high speed is a design requirement; they are faster than traditional spindles but more costly; hardware failures in these and other machines are considered normal and expected in large distributed systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Datacenter
Core_Definition: A facility housing the computers and networking equipment used to provide distributed services.
Key_Actors: Computers; routers; electronics for wired and fiber-optic communication; local network segments.
Operational_Workflow: Requests travel from a user to the datacenter over long distances; packets are processed by routers and electronics that convert between wired and fiber-optic signals; within the datacenter, computers communicate over local network segments where they experience small delays from packet assembly and disassembly.
Rules_and_Constraints: Transaction speed is heavily influenced by the distance between the user and the datacenter due to the speed of light; communication within the datacenter is not instant and is subject to network delays and processing time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A complete network outage is an exception where even a partition-tolerant system is unable to function.

Concept_Name: Index lookup
Core_Definition: The process of searching a database index to identify the specific location of requested data within a storage system or corpus.
Key_Actors: Database; storage system; disk read heads; RAM; lookup tree; UNIX file system.
Operational_Workflow: A system receives a request and consults an index; this may involve multiple mechanical disk seeks to navigate a directory and find an inode or traversing a multi-level lookup tree if the index is stored in RAM; the system then uses this location information to retrieve the actual data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Disk-based lookups are constrained by the 10 ms seek time of mechanical spindles; RAM-based lookups are faster but require a tree deep enough to span the data; lookups must be completed within the service's overall latency budget.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some designs mark items as deleted in the index rather than removing them immediately; this requires a background process to occasionally compact the index and remove the marked items.

Concept_Name: Authentication system
Core_Definition: A service component used to verify the identity of a request or user before allowing a transaction to proceed.
Key_Actors: Authentication system team; web browser; distributed service.
Operational_Workflow: A request arriving from a web browser is sent to the authentication system for verification; once identity is confirmed, the request moves to subsequent stages such as index lookups and data retrieval.
Rules_and_Constraints: Authentication systems are often assigned a specific latency budget by their operating teams, such as 3 ms, to ensure the total transaction remains fast and responsive for the user.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Budget
Core_Definition: A defined allocation of time or resources, such as a latency budget, within which a system or component must complete its operations to remain performant.
Key_Actors: Designers; frontends; backends; users; authentication systems.
Operational_Workflow: Designers break down a common transaction into smaller steps and estimate the time each step takes; they subtract time for factors they cannot control, such as network latency and the speed of light, from the total time allowed for the transaction; the remaining time is allocated to internal processes like database lookups or authentication; during operation, a frontend may give up on a backend that exceeds its allocated time to ensure the overall response meets the deadline.
Rules_and_Constraints: A service is considered sluggish if replies do not come back in 200 ms or less; designs that cannot meet the budget are eliminated early in the process; speed of light and network delays are treated as fixed constraints that consume a significant portion of the budget.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend is slow and the latency deadline is near, the frontend can give up on the slow reply and generate a response using default information or leaving specific areas blank.

Concept_Name: UNIX file system
Core_Definition: A system architecture where directories are used to look up inodes, which in turn serve as per-file lists that identify the location of specific data blocks.
Key_Actors: Master server; inodes; data blocks; directories.
Operational_Workflow: A file is looked up in a directory to find its corresponding inode; the inode is then used to look up the specific data blocks where the file contents are stored.
Rules_and_Constraints: Understanding these internals is described as key to being able to design and operate distributed systems; the documentation of these internals provides an advantage for those designing distributed services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Asynchronous design
Core_Definition: A design pattern where a client sends a request to a server and control is returned to the user immediately without waiting for the request to complete.
Key_Actors: Client; server; user; background process; queue; thread or subprocess.
Operational_Workflow: The client sends a request to the server; the server may place the request into a queue instead of performing the action immediately; the system returns control to the user to create the perception of speed; a separate process or thread reads the request from the queue and performs the action in the background; the client may check for the reply at a later time.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is used to achieve faster response times in interactive systems; it is noted as being more complex to implement than synchronous designs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The actual work being performed may lag behind the user's perception of the transaction being finished.

Concept_Name: Queue the request
Core_Definition: The act of placing an incoming service request into a waiting line to be processed later by a background process.
Key_Actors: Server; request; queue; background process.
Operational_Workflow: Upon receiving a request, the server stores the request in a queue rather than executing it immediately; this allows the server to return a response to the user quickly; subsequently, a separate process reads the requests from the queue and performs the required actions in the background.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is an implementation detail of asynchronous designs used to manage latency and throughput.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Background
Core_Definition: Distributed computing is the art of building large systems that divide work over many machines to achieve size, scale, speed, and reliability. It represents a shift from traditional computing where a single computer runs software or many machines access a centralized service.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; physical or virtual machines; large-scale web services such as search engines, social media timelines, and product catalogs; millions of users; and automated operational systems.
Operational_Workflow: Work is distributed across hundreds or thousands of machines to process billions or trillions of queries. Systems are designed to provide fast and responsive services, targeting response times of 200 ms or less to avoid appearing sluggish to users. Because the scale makes manual management impossible, operations are automated for software deployment, regular tasks, and failure handling. To maintain these systems, visibility is actively created by requiring components to generate copious logs and export metrics to a central location for analysis. Engineers maintain mental models of these systems to track operations and perform debugging.
Rules_and_Constraints: Failure is considered normal and must be an expected part of the design landscape; operations must be automated due to the sheer size of the system; designs must be kept as simple as possible to ensure they can be understood and debugged; and systems must provide introspection through logs and metrics to be manageable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hardware failures that are rare in traditional systems become common when multiplied by thousands of machines; network latency can consume most of the 200 ms response time budget; and overly complex designs can lead to situations where no single person understands the entire system, making debugging twice as hard as writing the code.

============================================================
Chapter 2. Designing for Operations
Section: Chapter 2. Designing for Operations
============================================================

Concept_Name: Design for Operations
Core_Definition: Designing for operations means ensuring that all normal operational functions can be performed effectively by building specific features into software that enhance the ability to perform and automate tasks. This approach acknowledges that the majority of a service lifecycle is spent in the operational phase rather than the feature-building phase.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations engineers; systems administrators; managers; business teams; legal departments; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Operational requirements are identified in the early stages of planning; developers build features such as configuration points and queue draining into the software; introspection points are created to allow for debugging and repairing large systems; operations staff and developers collaborate to create playbooks for service procedures; repetitive manual tasks are identified and replaced with automated systems and self-service APIs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational functions must be treated as essential requirements rather than non-functional after-thoughts; systems must be configurable by automated means using plain text files for auditing and archiving; all operational procedures must include a test suite to verify success or failure; priority for implementation should be given to high-impact projects that address system bottlenecks; high availability requires features that enhance the ability to automate operational tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems may be designed in ways that work against the ability to improvise solutions during emergencies; developers may refuse to fix problems if a configuration was not officially supported; code contributed by operations staff may be rejected if it does not meet development team standards; large-scale power-on events can result in a small percentage of hardware failing to boot correctly.

Concept_Name: Periodic maintenance
Core_Definition: Periodic maintenance refers to routine operational tasks such as backups, restores, configuration archiving, and hardware swaps required to maintain the long-term health of a service. These tasks are designed to be performed without causing service outages for the end user.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; load balancers; backend replicas; database teams; manager teams.
Operational_Workflow: Maintenance procedures are defined in a playbook created through collaboration between developers and operations; a drain mode is triggered to stop the service from accepting new requests while it finishes in-flight tasks; the service intentionally fails load balancer health checks to redirect traffic; the maintenance task such as a backup or hardware replacement is performed; the system is tested using a test suite and then undrained to resume normal service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Maintenance should be performed seamlessly using features like queue draining and toggles; the system must support live backups and restores while running; startup and shutdown times must be documented for disaster recovery and power planning; maintenance procedures must include notification steps and verification of dependencies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Legacy systems may require full downtime for maintenance if they lack live backup or draining capabilities; killing a service replica without draining it first will result in the loss of in-flight messages or requests; crash-only software may skip orderly shutdown procedures to ensure that the crash recovery system is frequently tested and reliable.

Concept_Name: Updates
Core_Definition: Updates involve implementing software upgrades or feature changes without taking down the service, typically by swapping out replicas behind a load balancer. This process separates the deployment of software binaries from the activation of specific features.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; load balancers; backend replicas; trusted testers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Software is deployed to backend replicas located behind a load balancer; replicas are removed from service and upgraded one at a time to maintain overall availability; feature toggles are used to include new code in the binary while keeping it disabled; new features are enabled via a flag flip at a specific time or for specific groups of testers; if a feature causes issues it is disabled via a toggle rather than performing a full binary rollback.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software upgrades must be possible without service downtime; roll-outs of new software versions should be independent of when new features are made available to users; non-replicated systems require risky cloning processes for upgrades; every software release must be tested to ensure configuration data is not omitted.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Clones of production systems used for updates may be imperfect if data changes occur during the cloning process; race conditions can occur between database masters and replicas during the update window; a bad software release may be detected by a sharp increase in collected exceptions; feature toggles can be used to mitigate problems without the need for a risky binary rollback.

Concept_Name: Monitoring
Core_Definition: Monitoring provides visibility into system performance and health by exposing and collecting metrics from all components to support availability tracking, capacity planning, and troubleshooting. It ensures that operations staff can observe the internal state and functioning of the infrastructure.
Key_Actors: System components; monitoring systems; operations staff; system administrators; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Every system component exposes metrics to a centralized monitoring system; metrics are used to track availability and performance in real-time; monitoring data is analyzed to predict future resource needs for capacity planning; dashboards display the system status including the use of manual or emergency configurations; automated alerts are generated when usage deviates from predicted models or when specific events occur.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each component of a system must expose metrics to the monitoring system; the monitoring system must detect and alert when manual or emergency configurations are active; advanced systems should set their own thresholds based on prediction models to reduce manual tuning; debug logging should be enabled on individual modules to prevent overwhelming information volume.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Severe software errors may go unnoticed if an automated exception collector is not used to centralize and analyze exceptions; monitoring large numbers of replicas manually is impossible and requires automated detection and alerting; temporary emergency configuration fixes may be forgotten if the monitoring system does not alert on their duration.

Concept_Name: Configuration
Core_Definition: The system must be configurable by automated means for both initial setup and changes made later, ideally using plain text files.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; automated systems; developers; monitoring systems; source code repositories.
Operational_Workflow: Configuration should take the form of a text file with a well-defined format that can be generated by automated systems and archived in a source code repository; text comparison tools like UNIX diff are used to analyze changes; for systems updated dynamically, automation reads and updates the state through an API or configuration file using a locking protocol; sanity checks are performed on configurations without activating them on live systems; in emergencies or for experimentation, configurations can be provided directly to the system to bypass the source code repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be possible to back up, restore, and archive the running configuration without taking the system down; history must be maintained to view differences between revisions; locking protocols must prevent update collisions or reading incomplete states; extracted configurations must be tested to ensure they do not omit data; manual overrides of source-controlled configurations must be exposed to monitoring systems and dashboards; alerts should be generated if manual overrides remain active for more than a certain amount of time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Opaque binary blobs are an inferior option because they are not human-readable and prevent history tracking; APIs may fail to represent the entire configuration, an omission often only found during disasters; source code repositories may be down during emergencies necessitating manual configuration; temporary fixes may be forgotten if they are not tracked via alerting.

Concept_Name: Startup and shutdown
Core_Definition: The service should restart automatically upon machine boot and include proper operating system hooks for orderly shutdowns.
Key_Actors: Operating systems; boot-time scripts; process management systems such as Ubuntu Upstart, Apache Mesos, or Google Omega; uninterruptible power supply systems; quality assurance teams.
Operational_Workflow: Services use boot-time scripts or process managers to ensure they restart after a reboot or move to a different machine in the event of hardware failure; orderly shutdowns use OS hooks to stop the service properly; after a crash, the system automatically performs data validations or repairs before resuming service; crash-only software models may be used where the orderly shutdown process is bypassed to ensure the recovery procedure is frequently exercised and tested.
Rules_and_Constraints: Startup and shutdown times must be documented for disaster recovery and UPS battery capacity planning; crash recovery procedures must receive significant testing to ensure system reliability; most UPS batteries sustain a system for about five minutes; high-availability services should focus on crash-only software principles to align recovery procedures with frequent testing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Sudden crashes require automatic repair processes before service is restored; concurrent power-ups in large datacenters can overload power systems due to the draw of thousands of disk motors spinning up simultaneously; one to five percent of machines may fail to boot on the first attempt after a large-scale power outage.

Concept_Name: Queue draining
Core_Definition: An orderly shutdown process, also known as lame-duck mode, where a service stops accepting new requests but completes all current in-flight requests.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend replicas; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: When maintenance is required, a replica is told to enter draining mode; the replica continues to process its existing requests but intentionally fails health check requests from the load balancer; the load balancer stops sending new traffic to that replica; once all existing requests are completed and no new traffic has been received for a period, it is safe to kill the replica for upgrades; services can also start in drained mode to allow operations to perform testing before the load balancer sends live traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: This mechanism is essential for software upgrades in load-balanced environments with multiple replicas; the replica must intentionally fail health checks to signal the load balancer; the process prevents the loss of in-flight requests during maintenance or swaps.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system lacks the ability to be drained, any attempt to shut it down will result in the loss of messages or requests that were in flight.

Concept_Name: Software upgrades
Core_Definition: The ability to implement software updates without taking the service down, typically by managing replicas behind a load balancer.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; service replicas; operations staff; development teams; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Replicas are upgraded one at a time by removing them from the load balancer, applying the update, and returning them to service; nonreplicated systems may require cloning the system, upgrading the clone, and swapping it into place; configuration toggles or flag flips are used to enable new features at specific times independently of the code deployment; if a new feature causes problems, it is disabled via the toggle rather than rolling back the entire software binary.
Rules_and_Constraints: Upgrades must be possible without service downtime; nonreplicated upgrades are risky and often involve improvised solutions; feature toggles should be used to separate roll-outs from deployments and to allow for beta testing with small groups of users; new features should be enabled for successively larger groups once validated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Upgrading systems while they are running is riskier and requires extensive design and testing; clones of production systems are often imperfect because it is difficult to ensure no changes are in progress during the clone; problematic updates are easier to manage via toggles than by performing a full binary rollback.

Concept_Name: Backups and restores
Core_Definition: The ability to back up and restore a service's data while the system is running to ensure availability for users.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; read-only database replicas; service accounts; users; special APIs for data insertion.
Operational_Workflow: A replica is removed from service and frozen to perform the backup while the system continues to run; this replica is later added back to the system; for restores, a special API is used to insert data during a live operation; the process allows for the restoration of a single account or group live so they can continue using the service while messages or data appear.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed to permit live backups and restores rather than requiring downtime; the architecture should allow for the restoration of individual accounts without locking out the user; additional load from backups and restores must be accounted for in capacity planning and latency calculations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Legacy systems may require being taken down for backups; configuration data might be omitted from backups, which is often only discovered during a disaster recovery exercise or emergency; restoring an account might be difficult if the system does not support granular live restores.

Concept_Name: Redundancy
Core_Definition: A reliability and scaling technique based on the ability to run multiple, redundant replicas of a service.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; load balancers; users.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple replicas of a service are deployed to support scaling and reliability; these replicas are typically placed behind a load balancer; state must be replicated between these instances to ensure consistent service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should be intentionally designed to support redundant configurations; state replication challenges must be addressed; developers should not rely on luck to make a service work behind a load balancer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a user's login state is stored locally on a web server but not communicated to replicas, the user may be repeatedly asked to log in as the load balancer sends requests to different replicas; systems not designed for redundancy may develop problems that are difficult to trace.

Concept_Name: Replicated databases
Core_Definition: A method for scaling database access in a distributed system by creating one or more read-only replicas to handle non-mutating queries.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas; developers; services.
Operational_Workflow: The master database handles all transactions that mutate or change the database; updates are passed to read-only replicas via bulk transfers; software creates two connections, one to the master and one to a read-only replica; developers categorize queries so that mutations go to the master while read-only queries are off-loaded to replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be specifically engineered to support read-only replicas; queries should be segregated even if replicas do not yet exist to prepare for future scaling; developers must trade off speed for freshness when choosing between replica and master connections.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Race conditions can occur where a component reads outdated information from a replica before it has been updated by the master; the master database can become overloaded if too many read queries are sent to it; developers may refuse to fix problems if the replicated configuration was not originally supported.

Concept_Name: Hot swaps
Core_Definition: The ability to swap service components in or out of their roles without triggering an overall service outage.
Key_Actors: Service components; load balancers; redirection services; physical components like power supplies or disk drives; operating systems.
Operational_Workflow: Software or hardware components are replaced while the system remains operational; software components may work with a load balancer or redirection service to control the swap; physical components like power supplies are replaced while electrically powered on; some devices like hard drives or network cards may require administrative tasks like OS scans or software restarts to be recognized.
Rules_and_Constraints: Components should be self-sufficient for the swap or compatible with redirection services; systems must be tested directly to understand how application-level software responds to the swap.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A hard drive might not be recognized unless the operating system is told to scan for new disks; application server software may not see a new network interface card without being restarted unless it is specifically programmed to scan for them.

Concept_Name: Toggles for individual features
Core_Definition: A configuration setting used to enable or disable specific features independently of software deployment.
Key_Actors: Software releases; users; trusted testers; configuration settings.
Operational_Workflow: Software is deployed early with new features disabled; a configuration setting is changed at the desired time to enable the feature via a flag flip; features can be enabled for small groups like trusted testers first to validate them; once validated the toggle enables the feature for successively larger groups or all users; if a new feature causes problems the toggle is used to disable it instead of performing a full binary rollback.
Rules_and_Constraints: A toggle should be present for each new feature; feature roll-outs should be independent of the timing of new software binary pushes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a new feature causes problems the toggle provides a faster and easier alternative to rolling back to the previous software binary.

Concept_Name: Graceful degradation
Core_Definition: A design principle where software maintains functionality by acting differently or providing limited service when it becomes overloaded or when its dependencies are down.
Key_Actors: Software; users; databases; dependent services; caches; temporary web servers.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors for overload or dependency failures; if the system is overloaded or at risk of hitting bandwidth limits it switches from a rich interface to a lightweight text-only mode; if a database stops accepting writes due to corruption or administration the service enters read-only mode; if a database is completely inaccessible the system serves partial results from its cache to avoid error messages; if dependent services are down related features are hidden rather than displaying broken pages; in total failure scenarios a temporary web server displays a status page to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must act smartly during outages; systems should provide partial results or generic status pages rather than broken links or 404 error messages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A service may become read-only as an administrative defense when corruption is detected in the database.

Concept_Name: Access controls and rate limits
Core_Definition: Mechanisms that regulate which users or processes are authorized to access a service and the volume of requests they are permitted to make.
Key_Actors: APIs; users; processes; Access Control Lists; Public Key Infrastructure; digital certificates.
Operational_Workflow: The system identifies users via mechanisms like IP addresses or digital certificates; the Access Control List checks the identity against an ordered list of users or groups to permit or deny access; the system applies a default closed policy with a whitelist or a default open policy with a blacklist; the system assigns specific queries per second rates based on user categories such as premium or unpaid; requests that exceed the established rate limit are denied.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs should include both Access Control List mechanisms and rate-limiting settings; digital certificates using a Public Key Infrastructure are preferred over IP addresses for identity verification; teams should negotiate queries per second rates as part of capacity planning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: IP addresses are the weakest form of identification because they can be easily forged; teams that have not negotiated specific rates may be given access at a very low rate limit to allow for experimentation without requiring manual intervention from the service team.

Concept_Name: Data import controls
Core_Definition: Mechanisms that allow operations staff to restrict or augment data that is periodically imported from external sources to ensure quality.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; data providers; whitelist; blacklist; augmentation file.
Operational_Workflow: Data is received from periodic imports; operations staff use a blacklist to reject known bad records or a whitelist to accept only data from verified regions; an augmentation file is used to add locally provided data or overrides for known bad data to the incoming stream; the system monitors the percentage of records changed in an import; if the change exceeds an established limit the system requires manual approval before the import is finalized.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff must be able to block bad records via configuration rather than software updates; data quality must be verified before adding a region to a whitelist; change limits should be established to prevent disasters caused by bugs or bad batches of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the quality of data for a specific region drops it is removed from the whitelist; manual approval is triggered if a weekly data import affects more than a specified percentage of records such as thirty percent.

Concept_Name: Auditing
Core_Definition: The setup of logging, permissions, and role accounts to enable a service to be examined for security and compliance audits.
Key_Actors: Service components; legal department; international regulatory bodies such as SOX, PCI DSS, and the EU Data Protection Directive; system administrators; internal and external auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are configured with logging and access controls that meet legal requirements; the legal department is consulted for the latest laws; auditors examine the service to verify compliance; administrators perform approved changes while ensuring they do not violate local mandates across different geographic regions.
Rules_and_Constraints: A service cannot exist without these features as they are essential for support; compliance with relevant laws is a major concern for corporations using public cloud services; labor laws and data protection mandates must be followed even when performing remote administration across company intranets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An administrator in one country moving a virtual server in another country may inadvertently violate local mandates; failure to pass an audit can lead to massive fines or the inability to conduct business.

Concept_Name: Debug instrumentation
Core_Definition: The generation of logs recording parameters sent to and returned from important function calls to assist in troubleshooting software.
Key_Actors: Software modules; developers; operations personnel; marketing, product management, and legal personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Developers add logging statements for relevant information; the software generates logs that are both human-readable and machine-parseable; debug logging is enabled on individual modules to manage data volume; operations personnel use the source code as documentation to understand and consume the log messages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Debugging logs should be exempt from bureaucratic requirements such as documentation translations or approvals from marketing and legal teams because they are not visible to external users; every developer should be empowered to add logging statements as they see fit; messages must be both human-readable and machine-parseable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If debug logging is not enabled on individual modules, the volume of information can become overwhelming; bureaucratic paralysis can occur if debug messages are subjected to the same translation and approval rules as external documentation.

Concept_Name: Exception collection
Core_Definition: A software framework that detects severe errors that cause a program to exit, gathers associated error messages, and submits them to a central database for analysis.
Key_Actors: Software framework; centralized database; software authors; human operators; maintenance teams.
Operational_Workflow: The framework detects a software exception; it gathers the error message and relevant metadata; the information is submitted to a central database; maintenance teams study the history of exceptions for trends such as repetition or increased volume; exception data is used to determine if software roll-outs should be cancelled or if quality is improving.
Rules_and_Constraints: Exceptions should be collected centrally; maintenance such as new software releases should be cancelled if there is a sharp increase in exceptions; exception levels should be correlated to specific software releases to identify bad releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain data corruption scenarios are better handled by a human than by software; exceptions may go unnoticed in systems with automatic restart capabilities unless an exception collector makes the invisible visible.

Concept_Name: Automated means
Core_Definition: The use of automated systems and protocols to manage configuration, changes, and operational tasks without manual intervention.
Key_Actors: Automated systems; APIs; configuration files; source code repositories; monitoring systems; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Automated systems generate configuration files in text format; automation reads and updates system state through an API or configuration file; locking protocols prevent collisions during updates; tools perform sanity checks on configurations without activating them on live systems; monitoring systems detect and report when configurations are provided directly rather than through automated repositories.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be configurable for both initial setup and subsequent changes; configurations should take the form of plain text files with well-defined formats for easy parsing; a locking protocol must exist to prevent automation and services from reading incomplete states; ideally systems read configurations directly from a source code repository.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Opaque binary blobs are an inferior configuration option because they cannot be easily parsed or archived; APIs for extracting configurations might omit critical data which is only discovered during emergencies; during emergencies or experimentation, automated source code repository features may be disabled to provide configurations directly, but this must be exposed on dashboards to prevent temporary fixes from being forgotten.

Concept_Name: Source code repository
Core_Definition: A centralized storage system where configuration files and service data are kept, managed, and historically tracked.
Key_Actors: Automated systems; operations personnel; monitoring systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Configuration files in the form of text are stored in the repository; systems may be configured to read their settings directly from the repository for convenience; operations staff use the repository to analyze changes over time and archive different revisions of the system state.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be possible to disable the feature that reads configurations from the repository to allow for direct configuration during emergencies; the use of the repository for configuration must be exposed so that monitoring systems can detect it and display the status on dashboards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the source code repository is down, the system must be able to accept configurations provided directly; if the feature to read from the repository is disabled for an extended period, the monitoring system should generate alerts to ensure temporary fixes are not forgotten.

Concept_Name: UNIX diff
Core_Definition: A standard text comparison tool used to analyze and view the differences between different versions or archived copies of text-based configuration files.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; automated auditing systems.
Operational_Workflow: The tool is used to compare one archived copy of a configuration file against another revision to identify specific changes; this process is often used to debug strange problems by identifying small changes that might have caused a system failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: This tool requires the configuration to be stored in a well-defined plain text format rather than an opaque binary blob; the configuration must be archived in a way that allows standard text comparison tools to parse it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This type of analysis is not possible if the configuration file is an opaque binary blob, as such files are not human-readable and do not allow for the tracking of changes over time.

Concept_Name: API
Core_Definition: An interface used by automation to read and update the state of a system, extract configurations, or perform specific data operations like restores.
Key_Actors: Automated tools; operations teams; developers; internal products and services; external customers.
Operational_Workflow: Automation uses the interface to interact with the system state or update configuration files; during data recovery, a special interface may be used to insert data into the system; the interface applies access control lists to verify the identity of the requester and enforce rate limits on queries.
Rules_and_Constraints: An API should include an Access Control List mechanism to determine authorized access; it should include rate-limiting settings to manage queries per second; any API used for extracting configurations must represent the entire configuration without omitting data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: APIs may occasionally provide an incomplete representation of the configuration, an omission often only found during disasters or emergencies; if a team has not negotiated a specific rate limit, they may be restricted to a very low default rate.

Concept_Name: Locking protocol
Core_Definition: A mechanism required when configuration state is updated dynamically to prevent simultaneous access conflicts.
Key_Actors: The running service; external automation tools.
Operational_Workflow: The protocol manages access to a configuration file when it is being read or updated by either the service itself or external automation; it ensures that no entity reads a file that is in an incomplete state.
Rules_and_Constraints: The protocol must exist if a file is used for dynamic updates; it must prevent update collisions between the service and automation; it must ensure data integrity so that the system does not activate a partial configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Opaque binary blob
Core_Definition: An inferior configuration file format that is not human readable.
Key_Actors: Automated systems; the service; external automation; human operators.
Operational_Workflow: The system stores its configuration in a binary format that cannot be easily parsed or read by humans; the service reads this state to function; because the file is opaque, operators cannot use standard text comparison tools to see changes over time or keep a history of the configuration.
Rules_and_Constraints: Plain text files are preferred over opaque binary blobs because they are easier to archive, audit, and analyze with tools like UNIX diff.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Analysis of small configuration changes that cause large problems is impossible if the file is not plain text.

Concept_Name: GUI
Core_Definition: A graphical user interface added to system administration tools to facilitate configuration.
Key_Actors: Product managers; developers; customers; Perl scripts.
Operational_Workflow: Developers build a graphical interface for a tool; operators or customers interact with the system; in practice, many customers bypass the interface in favor of writing scripts to generate configuration files.
Rules_and_Constraints: Easy configuration does not require a GUI; automation through scripting is often preferred over manual graphical interaction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Organizations may spend significant resources adding a GUI only to find that the majority of customers do not use it.

Concept_Name: Alertable event
Core_Definition: A specific system state or action that can be detected by monitoring systems to trigger notifications.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; production systems; users; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: A specific action is taken, such as providing a configuration directly rather than from a source code repository; the monitoring system detects this state; the status is exposed via dashboards; an alert is generated to notify users that the feature is in use.
Rules_and_Constraints: The use of manual configuration overrides on production systems should be an alertable event; alerts should be generated if a temporary fix is left in place for more than a certain amount of time to ensure it is not forgotten.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Boot-time script
Core_Definition: A script installed to ensure that a service restarts automatically when a machine boots up.
Key_Actors: Machines; services; operating systems; process management systems like Ubuntu Upstart, Apache Mesos, or Google Omega.
Operational_Workflow: A machine undergoes a reboot; the operating system executes the script; the service is automatically started without manual intervention.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should be designed to restart automatically upon boot-up; scripts are a simple way to achieve this, though more complex process management systems can also be used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine crashes suddenly, the system must perform data validations or repairs upon restart before providing service; during large-scale power-ups, 1 to 5 percent of machines may fail to boot on the first try and require manual resuscitation.

Concept_Name: Process management system
Core_Definition: A system designed to monitor individual processes and ensure they are restarted as needed to maintain service availability.
Key_Actors: Processes; machines; Ubuntu Upstart; Apache Mesos; Google Omega.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors the status of running processes; it automatically restarts a process when the host machine reboots; advanced systems can detect the failure of a machine and restart the affected process on an entirely different machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machine death; in this event the system can restart the process on a different machine.

Concept_Name: Ubuntu Upstart
Core_Definition: A specific system used to monitor processes and ensure they restart automatically.
Key_Actors: Processes; machines.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors active processes and triggers a restart of the service whenever a machine boots up.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Apache Mesos
Core_Definition: A comprehensive process management system that provides automated restarts across different machines.
Key_Actors: Processes; machines.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors processes; it restarts a process when a machine completes a reboot; it manages machine failure by restarting the process on an entirely different machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machine death; the system handles this by moving and restarting the process on a different machine.

Concept_Name: Google Omega
Core_Definition: A comprehensive process management system used to ensure processes are restarted after reboots or hardware failures.
Key_Actors: Processes; machines.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors the health of processes; it automatically restarts a process when a machine reboots; it detects machine death and restarts the process on a different machine to maintain service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machine death; the system responds by restarting the process on an entirely different machine.

Concept_Name: Disaster recovery
Core_Definition: The process of restoring service and system functionality after a major failure or emergency.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; automated configuration systems.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams document the time required for system startup and shutdown to facilitate planning; they perform exercises to verify that configuration data is complete and can be restored; during an actual disaster or power outage, teams use these procedures to resuscitate machines and restore archived configurations to bring the service back online.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each new software release should be tested to verify that configuration data does not omit anything necessary for recovery; expected startup times and procedures must be documented to reduce recovery time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Omissions in configuration data are often discovered only during an emergency or recovery exercise; after a concurrent power loss, 1 to 5 percent of machines may fail to boot on the first try, requiring a large team to manually resuscitate them.

Concept_Name: Uninterruptible power supply (UPS)
Core_Definition: A hardware system that provides emergency battery power to systems when the main power source fails.
Key_Actors: Battery systems; servers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The UPS sustains the system for a short period immediately following a power outage; operations staff use this time to perform a safe shutdown of the system based on documented shutdown times; the system remains powered until the shutdown is complete or the battery is exhausted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Most UPS batteries are designed to sustain a system for approximately five minutes; battery capacity must be planned according to the known time required to safely shut down the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When thousands of servers attempt to spin up hard disk motors at the same time after power is restored, they can create a huge power draw that overloads power systems.

Concept_Name: Crash-only software
Core_Definition: Software designed to only be stopped by crashing and only be started by using the crash recovery system, eliminating orderly startup and shutdown procedures.
Key_Actors: Candea and Fox (authors/researchers); software developers; quality assurance (QA) teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers intentionally do not implement orderly shutdown or startup procedures; the only method to stop the software is to crash it; every time the software is started, it is forced to exercise the crash recovery system.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is used to ensure the recovery procedure is exercised frequently and receives the same level of testing as other critical functions; high availability services should rarely use an orderly shutdown process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Post-crash recovery procedure
Core_Definition: An automated process that performs data validations and repairs when a system restarts after a sudden crash.
Key_Actors: Automated recovery systems; quality assurance (QA) teams; servers and hard disks.
Operational_Workflow: A machine crashes suddenly without an orderly shutdown; upon the next system restart, the software automatically triggers data validation routines; the system performs necessary repairs to corrupted or incomplete data; once repairs are finished, the system resumes providing service to users.
Rules_and_Constraints: The procedure must be critical to system reliability; it should receive a significant amount of quality assurance testing to ensure it functions correctly during a restart.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Traditionally, this procedure receives a disproportionately small amount of quality assurance testing; in large-scale systems, a small percentage of machines may fail to successfully complete the boot and recovery process on the first attempt after a crash.

Concept_Name: Orderly shutdown procedure
Core_Definition: A process used to take a system out of service for maintenance by properly signaling the operating system and service to stop.
Key_Actors: Operating system hooks; the service; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: An orderly shutdown process is triggered to take the system out of service; the system uses specific operating system hooks to shut the service down properly; in high availability environments, the service may be told to stop accepting new requests while finishing those currently in progress before the process is ended.
Rules_and_Constraints: The amount of time required to shut down a system should be documented to plan battery capacity for uninterruptible power supply systems; services expected to have high availability should rarely use this process to ensure the crash recovery procedure is the primary method tested and exercised.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine crashes suddenly rather than following an orderly procedure, the system must perform data validations or repairs upon the next restart; simply killing a replica without an orderly process results in the loss of in-flight requests.

Concept_Name: Orderly startup procedures
Core_Definition: The documented and automated steps taken to restart a service and verify its readiness after a machine boots or a process is initiated.
Key_Actors: Boot-time scripts; process management systems such as Ubuntu Upstart, Apache Mesos, or Google Omega; hardware components like hard disk motors; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: A machine boots up and triggers a boot-time script or a process management system; the management system restarts the process on the local machine or an entirely different machine in the event of hardware failure; the service performs necessary data validations or repairs before it begins providing service to users.
Rules_and_Constraints: Expected startup times must be documented for disaster recovery planning; services should be designed to restart automatically when a machine boots; systems should be tested for concurrent power loss scenarios where thousands of servers attempt to spin up at once.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During massive concurrent startups, 1 to 5 percent of machines may fail to boot on the first try; large teams of people may be required to resuscitate machines that do not boot; crash-only software advocates for the removal of these procedures so that only the crash recovery system is exercised for starting the software.

Concept_Name: Drain
Core_Definition: An operational state, also known as lame-duck mode, where a service continues to process existing requests but refuses to accept any new ones.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; load balancers; operations staff; health check mechanisms.
Operational_Workflow: The service is triggered to enter a draining mode for maintenance or upgrades; the replica continues to process existing requests but intentionally fails the health check requests from the load balancer; the load balancer detects the failed health checks and stops sending new traffic to that specific replica; once no new requests have been received for a period and all existing requests are completed, the replica is safely killed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should be able to start in a drained mode to allow for testing by operations staff before signaling the load balancer to send production traffic; this feature is essential for performing software upgrades without losing active requests in a load-balanced environment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service lacks a drain feature, any attempt to shut it down will result in the immediate loss of all messages or requests currently being processed; if the health check fails to communicate the drained status to the load balancer, the system may continue to receive traffic it cannot handle.

Concept_Name: In flight
Core_Definition: The status of active requests or messages that have been accepted by a system and are currently being processed but are not yet finished.
Key_Actors: Individual requests; data messages; the service application.
Operational_Workflow: A request is received by the service and enters the in-flight state during its processing lifecycle; when a shutdown or maintenance event is initiated via a drain, the system stops accepting new work but remains active to complete these specific items; the system only exits once the count of in-flight requests reaches zero.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed to track and complete in-flight requests during maintenance windows to maintain service reliability and data integrity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service is killed or crashes, all in-flight requests are lost; problems arise in systems that lack the introspection to identify which requests are currently in flight during a shutdown.

Concept_Name: Lame-duck mode
Core_Definition: A state where a service is told to stop accepting new requests but continues to complete any requests that are currently in flight; it is also referred to as a drain.
Key_Actors: Service or replica; Load balancer; In-flight requests; Operations team.
Operational_Workflow: An orderly shutdown process is triggered to take a system out of service; the service enters lame-duck mode and stops accepting new requests; the replica intentionally fails the load balancer's health check requests; the load balancer stops sending new requests to the replica upon seeing the failed health checks; the replica completes its existing in-flight requests; once no new requests are received and existing ones are finished, the replica is killed for maintenance or upgrades.
Rules_and_Constraints: This mechanism is essential when using a load balancer with multiple backend replicas to ensure requests are not lost; it allows for seamless software upgrades by removing and upgrading one replica at a time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A service may also start in a drained mode, which prevents the load balancer from sending new traffic but allows the operations team to send messages directly to the replica for testing before it is undrained for public use.

Concept_Name: Load balancer
Core_Definition: A system that distributes traffic among multiple backend replicas to support high availability and enable operational tasks like software upgrades.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; Backend replicas; Health check requests; Service traffic.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer directs incoming traffic to various backend replicas; it periodically sends health check requests to each replica to monitor its status; if a replica fails a health check, the load balancer stops sending it new requests; software upgrades are managed by having the load balancer redirect traffic away from one replica at a time so it can be updated and returned to service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must be specifically designed to support being behind a load balancer; simple services may work through luck but more complex ones require careful handling of state, such as user login data, to ensure consistency when a user is directed to different replicas.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system is not designed for a load balancer, it may seem to work but develop problems later that are difficult to trace; systems without replicas are difficult to upgrade without downtime and may require risky, improvised cloning solutions.

Concept_Name: Multiple backend replicas
Core_Definition: Redundant copies of a service or database that allow for scaling, reliability, and the ability to perform maintenance without service outages.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; Load balancer; Master database; Read-only replicas.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple service replicas are placed behind a load balancer to handle traffic; software is upgraded by swapping out replicas one by one; in database systems, a master database handles all mutations while updates are passed to read-only replicas for non-mutating queries; for backups, a replica can be removed from service, frozen, used for the backup, and then returned to service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should be designed to support redundant configurations and the replication of state between replicas; software that uses a database must be engineered to specifically support read-only replicas by maintaining separate connections for master and replica servers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Race conditions can occur if a component reads from a replica too soon after an update on the master, potentially leading to the consumption of outdated information; if a service does not communicate local state like login sessions between replicas, users may be forced to log in multiple times.

Concept_Name: Health check requests
Core_Definition: Periodic signals or queries sent by a load balancer to a replica to verify its availability and readiness to process traffic.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; Service replica.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer sends regular requests to a replica to confirm it is functioning; if the replica responds successfully, the load balancer continues to send it traffic; during an orderly shutdown or drain, the replica is configured to intentionally fail these requests; once the load balancer detects the failed health checks, it stops directing new requests to that specific replica.
Rules_and_Constraints: The replica must be able to intentionally fail health checks to signal to the load balancer that it is entering a maintenance or draining state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Swapping out replicas
Core_Definition: A method for implementing software upgrades by removing individual service components from a load balancer one at a time to be upgraded and then returned to service.
Key_Actors: Software replicas; load balancers; operations team.
Operational_Workflow: The software is positioned behind a load balancer; one replica at a time is removed from service; the removed replica is upgraded; the upgraded replica is returned to service; the process repeats until all replicas are updated.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process is typically used for services located behind a load balancer; it is intended to allow upgrades without taking down the entire service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a replica is simply killed instead of being drained of in-flight requests, those requests will be lost; some systems can be upgraded while running without swapping, but this requires careful design and extensive testing.

Concept_Name: Downtime
Core_Definition: A period when a service is unavailable to users, often required for maintenance or upgrades in systems that lack redundancy or live-operational features.
Key_Actors: Nonreplicated systems; legacy systems; customers or users; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The service is taken offline; maintenance tasks such as software upgrades or data backups are performed; the service is restarted and returned to users.
Rules_and_Constraints: While downtime may be acceptable for small offices with few users, it is not considered reasonable for websites with hundreds or millions of users; systems should be designed to avoid downtime through features like live backups and replica swapping.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Nonreplicated systems are particularly difficult to upgrade without downtime; legacy systems often must be taken down specifically to perform backups or restores.

Concept_Name: Clones of production systems
Core_Definition: An identical copy of a production system created to facilitate upgrades when a system cannot be updated while running.
Key_Actors: Nonreplicated systems; customers; production systems.
Operational_Workflow: A clone of the existing system is created; the upgrade is performed on the clone; the newly upgraded system is swapped into the production role faster than customers will notice.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is often used as an alternative for nonreplicated systems that cannot be upgraded without downtime; the swap must be executed quickly to minimize user impact.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This is often a risky or improvised solution; clones tend to be imperfect copies because it is difficult to ensure the clone was made precisely when no changes were in progress.

Concept_Name: Live backups
Core_Definition: The process of backing up a service's data while the system remains running and available to users.
Key_Actors: Read-only replicas; databases; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: A replica is removed from service and frozen; the backup is performed using the frozen replica; the replica is added back to the system; alternatively, a specific replica is dedicated permanently to this process.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must be designed to permit backups without interfering with the active service; the additional load created by backups must be accounted for in capacity planning, headroom, and latency calculations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Legacy systems often lack this capability and must be taken down to perform backups; the design of systems permitting live backups is fundamentally different from those that require downtime.

Concept_Name: Read-only replica of the database
Core_Definition: A copy of a database used to scale access by handling queries that do not mutate data.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas; services; developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The master database handles all transactions that mutate data; updates are passed to read-only replicas via bulk transfers; services open separate connections to both the master and a replica; developers code each query to use the appropriate connection based on whether the data is being changed; the majority of work is off-loaded to replicas since most access is read-only.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be engineered specifically to support multiple connections; queries should be segregated even if replicas do not yet exist; developers must trade off speed for data freshness; every query sent to the master consumes precious resources and should be avoided if a replica can be used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Race conditions can occur if a component reads outdated information from a replica immediately after a master update; the master database can become overloaded if queries are not properly segregated; relying on luck for configuration support instead of official developer support can lead to disaster during software releases.

Concept_Name: Live restores
Core_Definition: The ability to restore service data while the system remains operational and accessible to users.
Key_Actors: Architecture; special API; users; groups.
Operational_Workflow: The system uses a special API designed for inserting data during a restore; the restoration process runs while the service is active; the architecture ensures the data is returned to the system without requiring downtime.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed specifically to permit restores while running, unlike legacy systems that require downtime; the process creates additional load on the system that must be planned for in advance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Legacy systems often cannot perform this and must be taken down; the additional load created during the restore can impact system performance if not accounted for in capacity planning and latency calculations.

Concept_Name: Single account restoration
Core_Definition: A recovery process that allows for the restoration of data for an individual user or group rather than the entire system.
Key_Actors: Users; groups; email systems; API.
Operational_Workflow: The system restores a specific account or group; the restoration occurs live while the user continues to use the service; messages or data appear to the user as they are being restored.
Rules_and_Constraints: The architecture should allow for this process without locking the specific user or group out of the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Capacity planning
Core_Definition: The operational task of predicting and preparing for future resource needs of a service.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; teams; data collection and analysis systems.
Operational_Workflow: Staff predict resource requirements for three to twelve months in the future; teams negotiate queries per second rates for service access; the load from backups and restores is calculated to determine required headroom; automated systems may collect and analyze data to make these predictions automatically.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planning must account for the additional burden of backups and restores on latency and headroom; teams that fail to negotiate rates may be restricted to low rate limits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Manual capacity planning can be labor-intensive and time-consuming; monitoring hundreds of replicas manually may require huge investments in staff; if thresholds are not accurately predicted or automated, the task of fine-tuning them can become endless.

Concept_Name: Latency calculations
Core_Definition: Latency calculations are the assessments used to determine the impact of system burdens on service response times.
Key_Actors: System capacity planning; backups; restores; system load.
Operational_Workflow: The additional load created by operational tasks such as backups and restores is measured and then factored into the overall capacity planning for the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designers must account for the burden of live data operations when calculating expected latency to ensure the system has enough headroom.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service replicas
Core_Definition: Service replicas are multiple redundant copies of a service component designed to improve reliability and scaling.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend replicas; operations staff; master databases; read-only replicas.
Operational_Workflow: Replicas are typically placed behind a load balancer; during maintenance, a replica enters a draining mode where it stops accepting new requests while finishing in-flight tasks; once empty, the replica is upgraded and returned to service; for databases, a master replica handles writes while passing updates to read-only replicas for scaling.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must be specifically designed to support multiple replicas; replicas should support a lame-duck or drained mode for maintenance; in-flight requests should not be lost during upgrades; health checks should be used by load balancers to determine if a replica is available for traffic.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Nonreplicated systems may require risky cloning for upgrades; if a service is not designed for replicas, users may experience problems such as lost login states when their requests are routed to different instances.

Concept_Name: Login state
Core_Definition: Login state refers to the session information that tracks a user’s authentication status within a web service.
Key_Actors: Users; web servers; service replicas; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A web server stores a user’s login state locally when they authenticate; as the user continues their session, the load balancer routes their requests to available backend replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Login state must be communicated between replicas or handled centrally; if the state is only stored locally on one replica, other replicas will not recognize the user.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the login state is not shared across replicas, a user will be repeatedly asked to log in whenever the load balancer directs them to a different replica until they have authenticated with every individual instance.

Concept_Name: Database scaling
Core_Definition: Database scaling is the process of increasing database capacity, usually by segregating write operations from read operations using replicas.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas; developers; software connections.
Operational_Workflow: The master database handles all transactions that mutate data; these updates are passed to read-only replicas via bulk transfers; software is written to open two separate connections, one for the master and one for the replicas; non-mutating queries are sent to replicas to off-load work from the master.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be engineered to support separate connections for master and read-only databases; developers must choose the appropriate connection for each query by trading off data freshness for speed; queries should be segregated even if replicas are not yet in use to simplify future scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Race conditions can occur if a system reads from a replica before the update from the master has arrived; the master database can become overloaded if too many queries are sent to it just in case rather than being properly off-loaded to read-only replicas.

Concept_Name: Master database
Core_Definition: The central database component that performs all transactions that mutate or make changes to the database.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas; software services; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The master database handles all mutation transactions and passes updates to read-only replicas via bulk transfers; services access the master database for full-service access to the freshest data; software specifically engineered for database scaling opens a dedicated connection to the master for write operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every query sent directly to the master consumes precious resources so they should only be used for mutations or when the freshest data is required; software must be specifically engineered to support two connections if it is to use a master-replica model; developers must give serious consideration to which connection a query should use to trade off speed for freshness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the master database becomes overloaded due to too many read queries being sent to it or increased usage patterns, an outage can occur; a component may be reconfigured to send all reads and writes to the master if it becomes confused by outdated info on replicas, though this is a temporary fix.

Concept_Name: Bulk transfers
Core_Definition: The mechanism used to pass updates from the master database to read-only replicas in a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas.
Operational_Workflow: Mutations are performed on the master database; these updates are then transmitted to read-only replicas through bulk transfers; the replicas then provide fast but slightly out of date access to the data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Race condition
Core_Definition: A situation where a component reads data from a replica soon after it was updated on the master and becomes confused because it sees outdated information.
Key_Actors: Master database; read-only replicas; software components; development teams.
Operational_Workflow: A system sends a write operation to the master database; a component then reads from a read-only replica before the update has propagated; the component receives stale data because the replica is slightly out of date.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams may temporarily reconfigure a component so that both write and read connections go to the master to avoid confusion, but this can lead to master overload if usage increases over time.

Concept_Name: Segregate queries
Core_Definition: The practice of specifically engineering software to route database requests to either a master or a read-only replica based on the type of operation.
Key_Actors: Developers; database master; read-only replicas; software applications.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create two database connections including one to the master and one to a replica; as each query is coded, the developer determines if it should be sent over the master connection for mutations or the replica connection for read-only access; this off-loads the majority of the work to the replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Query segregation is best done when a query is originally invented; developers must trade off speed for freshness; read-only queries should not be sent to the master just in case because it consumes resources; queries should be segregated even if the database does not currently have replicas to prepare for future scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If retrofitting a system after the fact, it may be more efficient to identify a few heavy hitter queries to move to a replica rather than examining every query in the source code; failure to properly segregate queries can lead to a master database outage if usage patterns change and the master becomes overloaded.

Concept_Name: Heavy hitters
Core_Definition: Specific queries or processes within a system that consume a disproportionately large amount of resources.
Key_Actors: Databases; read-only replicas; master database; developers; operations staff; source code.
Operational_Workflow: During the process of retrofitting a system to support database scaling, operations staff identify a few resource-intensive queries; these specific queries are then redirected to a read-only replica rather than the master database; this targeted approach avoids the need to examine every single query in the source code.
Rules_and_Constraints: Identifying heavy hitters is presented as a more efficient use of time than examining every query in the source code when retrofitting a system for read-only replicas.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Outage
Core_Definition: A state where a service is unavailable to users, potentially caused by power loss, system crashes, or resource overloads.
Key_Actors: Service components; power systems; master database; operations staff; load balancers; users.
Operational_Workflow: An outage can occur if a master database becomes overloaded by frequent queries or if a datacenter loses power concurrently; following a power outage, thousands of servers must be restarted, a process that can be complex due to power draws from disk motors; systems should be designed with graceful degradation to provide partial results or status pages during these events.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service components should be designed to be swapped in or out without triggering an overall service outage; expected startup and shutdown times must be documented to plan for disaster recovery and UPS capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During a mass restart after a power outage, 1 to 5 percent of machines can be expected to fail to boot on the first try; an outage may also occur if a component that was previously low-usage changes its patterns and overloads the master database.

Concept_Name: Hot-swappable devices
Core_Definition: Physical components that can be changed or replaced while a machine is still electrically powered on without affecting the rest of the system's operations.
Key_Actors: Power supplies; disk drives; physical hardware; application-level software; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: An operations staff member identifies a component that needs replacement, such as a power supply; the component is removed and a new one is installed while the machine continues to run; the replacement is completed without stopping service operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The device must be able to be changed without affecting the rest of the machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Vendor definitions of this term can be unclear, requiring direct testing to see how application-level software responds to the hardware change.

Concept_Name: Hot-pluggable devices
Core_Definition: Devices that can be installed or removed while a machine is running, though they may require additional administrative steps to be fully integrated or decommissioned.
Key_Actors: Hard drives; network interface cards; operating system; application server software; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: A device is physically added to or removed from a running machine; an administrator may then need to perform specific tasks, such as triggering an operating system scan for new disks or restarting application software to recognize a new network interface.
Rules_and_Constraints: Administrative tasks may be required before or after the installation or removal of the device.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A hard drive may not be recognized by the system until the operating system is manually told to scan for new disks; a new network interface card may be recognized by the OS but remains invisible to application server software unless the software is restarted or specifically programmed to scan for new interfaces.

Concept_Name: Toggle (feature)
Core_Definition: A configuration setting used to enable or disable specific features within a software system.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; trusted testers; specific user groups.
Operational_Workflow: Developers build a toggle into the software for each new feature; the software is deployed to production with the feature initially disabled; operations staff enable the feature at a later time by changing a configuration setting; the feature can be enabled for small groups of testers or successively larger user populations to validate performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: A toggle should be present for every new feature; toggles must allow the roll-out of new software releases to be independent of when features are made available to users; they should support activation for specific groups such as beta testers or premium customers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a new feature causes problems after activation, the toggle allows operations to disable the individual feature quickly as an alternative to rolling back the entire software binary.

Concept_Name: Flag flip
Core_Definition: The act of changing a configuration setting to activate or deactivate a feature that has already been deployed.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; software systems.
Operational_Workflow: The software binary is deployed to the system ahead of time with the feature in a disabled state; at the desired moment for the feature to go live, operations staff update the configuration setting; the system acknowledges the change and makes the feature available to users without requiring a new binary push.
Rules_and_Constraints: Flag flips are used to decouple software release timing from feature launch timing; they are intended to provide control over feature availability without service interruption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A flag flip is used as a recovery mechanism to disable a feature if it is found to be causing operational issues after being enabled.

Concept_Name: Roll-outs
Core_Definition: The process of implementing software upgrades or making new features available to users across a service.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; load balancers; backend replicas; exception collectors; developers.
Operational_Workflow: For software upgrades, replicas are removed from a load balancer one at a time, upgraded, and returned to service; for feature releases, toggles are updated to enable code for specific groups or the entire user base; exception collectors monitor the health of the system throughout the process to detect increases in errors.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software upgrades must be possible without taking down the service; feature roll-outs should be independent of software release schedules; roll-outs should be cancelled if the system shows high exception levels or stopped if a new release causes a sharp increase in exceptions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a sharp increase in exceptions is detected, it indicates a bad release and the roll-out process should be stopped immediately.

Concept_Name: Roll back
Core_Definition: Reverting a system to a previous stable state by returning to an older software version or disabling a new feature.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; software systems.
Operational_Workflow: When a deployment or new feature causes system problems, operations staff return the service to its prior working state; this is achieved by either rolling back to the previous software binary or by using a flag flip to disable the problematic feature.
Rules_and_Constraints: Disabling an individual feature via a flag flip is considered easier and more efficient than performing a full rollback to a previous software binary.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Trusted testers
Core_Definition: A small group of users who are granted access to new features for validation purposes before those features are released to the general public.
Key_Actors: Users designated as testers; operations and development teams.
Operational_Workflow: Features are deployed within a software release but are initially disabled; sophisticated toggles are used to enable the feature specifically for the small group of trusted testers; once the feature is validated by this group, the toggle can be used to enable it for larger groups.
Rules_and_Constraints: Access should be managed via configuration toggles rather than requiring a new binary push.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Early access
Core_Definition: The provision of new features to a specific subset of users or testers before a full-scale roll-out to the entire user base.
Key_Actors: Trusted testers; specific groups of users; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Software is deployed early with new features controlled by configuration settings; the feature is enabled for a limited group via a flag flip; the feature is validated during this period of limited access; the toggle is then used to enable the feature for successively larger groups.
Rules_and_Constraints: Roll-outs of new software releases should be independent of when the new feature is made available to users; using individual toggles allows for safer validation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a feature provided during early access causes problems, it can be disabled via a flag flip rather than rolling back the entire software binary.

Concept_Name: Beta testing
Core_Definition: The phase of a service life cycle where new features are enabled on a per-user basis to ensure operational stability and effectiveness.
Key_Actors: Users; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff use feature toggles to control the visibility of new functionality; access is granted to specific users to monitor the impact of the feature; this mechanism allows the team to test features in the production environment without a global launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: Feature controls should support enabling or disabling features on a per-user basis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Overloaded (state)
Core_Definition: A condition where a system, component, or power infrastructure is subject to more demand or load than it can effectively process.
Key_Actors: Software systems; power systems; master databases; web servers.
Operational_Workflow: When software detects it is becoming overloaded, it may switch to graceful degradation mode; the system might switch from a rich user interface to a lightweight text-only mode; it may also become read-only or work from a cache to provide partial results instead of error messages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should be designed to act smartly when overloaded; capacity planning and latency calculations must account for additional burdens like backups and restores; power systems must be designed to handle the concurrent spin-up of thousands of machines to avoid overloading during recovery.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An overloaded state can lead to full outages or crashes; a database master can become overloaded if queries that could be handled by read-only replicas are incorrectly sent to it; concurrently booting thousands of servers can overload datacenter power systems causing them to fail.

Concept_Name: Bandwidth limits
Core_Definition: A threshold of data transfer capacity that, when reached or at risk of being hit, triggers a system to switch to a more efficient or lightweight mode of operation.
Key_Actors: Web sites; users; system interfaces.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors its current load and data transfer rates; if the system becomes overloaded or is at risk of hitting bandwidth limits, it switches from a rich interface full of images to a lightweight, all-text mode; this allows the service to continue functioning for users rather than failing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Switching to a lightweight mode is a form of graceful degradation used to maintain service during periods of high demand or resource constraints.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Read-only mode
Core_Definition: A functional state where a service allows data access and retrieval but prevents any transactions that mutate or change the database.
Key_Actors: Services; databases; read-only database replicas; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: A service detects that a database has stopped accepting writes or that only a read-only replica is available; the software automatically switches its operating state to read-only; the service continues to provide data to users while preventing any data corruption or failed write attempts.
Rules_and_Constraints: Read-only mode is used as a form of graceful degradation; it is often employed as a common administrative defense when data corruption is detected.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a database becomes completely inaccessible rather than just read-only, the software may have to transition to working from a cache to provide partial results.

Concept_Name: Cache
Core_Definition: A local or client-side storage mechanism that preserves data so that software can continue to function when primary databases or servers are inaccessible.
Key_Actors: Software; databases; users; client-side JavaScript; web browsers.
Operational_Workflow: A service loses access to its primary database or backend server; the software shifts to retrieving data from its internal cache or client-side storage; in specific applications like Google Docs, client-side JavaScript works with cached data in the browser; the system displays this stored data to the user to avoid showing an error message.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using a cache is a graceful degradation technique intended to show users partial results rather than a broken page or a 404 error during an outage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Partial results
Core_Definition: Incomplete or cached data displayed to a user when a system cannot access its full data source, used as a preferred alternative to an error message.
Key_Actors: Software; users; databases; cache.
Operational_Workflow: A database becomes completely inaccessible to the software; the system identifies that it cannot provide a full or current data set; instead of displaying a 404 page not found or an error message, the software retrieves whatever information is available in its cache; the user is presented with these limited results to maintain some level of service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Providing partial results is a principle of graceful degradation designed to make software act smartly during outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 404 page not found error
Core_Definition: An error message displayed to a user when a web page is broken or when a service is unable to access the underlying dependent systems required to fulfill a request.
Key_Actors: Users; web servers; dependent services.
Operational_Workflow: A user attempts to access a service or feature; if the primary service cannot reach a necessary dependency, the system might return this error; the text suggests this is a suboptimal outcome that should be avoided through graceful degradation techniques.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designers should aim to prevent these errors by allowing related features to disappear or by displaying partial results from a cache when dependencies are inaccessible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Generic front page
Core_Definition: A backup landing page used to inform users of the system status when a service is entirely unavailable.
Key_Actors: Users; temporary web server software; primary service.
Operational_Workflow: The primary service becomes unavailable; a temporary web server is put in place; this server serves the same page for every query to inform users that the site is under construction or to show the current system status.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is considered better practice to provide this page than to allow users to receive no response at all; the page should ideally display the system status.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: System status
Core_Definition: The current health, availability, or configuration state of a service as communicated to users and operations staff.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; users; operations personnel; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems detect the state of the service or specific configuration overrides; this information is displayed on dashboards to keep users aware of the situation; during total outages, the status is displayed on a generic front page.
Rules_and_Constraints: The use of emergency configuration features must be detectable by monitoring systems so it can be shown in dashboards; temporary fixes should be alerted on to ensure they are not forgotten.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Access Control List (ACL)
Core_Definition: A mechanism used to determine which users or processes are authorized to access a system and to define their allowed rate-limiting settings.
Key_Actors: Users; groups; API services; operations staff; digital certificate infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: A user or process identity is verified, preferably via digital certificates; the API checks this identity against an ordered list to permit or deny access; specific rate limits, such as queries per second, are applied based on the identity or group.
Rules_and_Constraints: Policies can be default closed using a whitelist or default open using a blacklist; identity should be proven using a public key infrastructure because IP addresses can be forged; ACLs can specify access for individuals or groups; rate limits can be used to distinguish between premium, regular, and unpaid customers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In Google's internal RPC protocol, teams that have not formally negotiated capacity are granted a very low rate limit by default to allow them to try new services without manual intervention.

Concept_Name: Rate-limiting settings
Core_Definition: Control points within an API that define the frequency or speed at which users are permitted to make requests to a system.
Key_Actors: Services; APIs; Access Control Lists; Users; Premium, regular paid, and unpaid customers; Teams.
Operational_Workflow: Teams negotiate queries per second rates for service access during capacity planning; the service API identifies the user and checks their assigned rate limit via an Access Control List; the system monitors the volume of requests; the service denies any requests that exceed the negotiated or assigned queries per second rate.
Rules_and_Constraints: Access Control Lists can be used to set specific rate limits for different classes of users; premium customers may receive unlimited access while other tiers are restricted to moderate or low rates; rate limits are often negotiated as part of a service's capacity planning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams that have not negotiated specific rates are granted access at a very low rate limit to allow for experimentation and trial use without requiring the service team to manually negotiate every lightweight or casual request.

Concept_Name: Internet Protocol (IP) addresses
Core_Definition: Numerical identifiers for network locations used as a basic method for determining system access permissions.
Key_Actors: Users; Processes; Access Control Lists.
Operational_Workflow: Access is restricted by adding specific IP addresses or blocks to an Access Control List; the system evaluates the source address of an incoming request against this list; the system either permits or denies the connection based on the defined whitelist or blacklist policy.
Rules_and_Constraints: IP addresses are considered the weakest form of identification because they can be easily forged.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because IP addresses are easily forged, the text recommends using a public key infrastructure with digital certificates for more reliable identification.

Concept_Name: Public key infrastructure (PKI)
Core_Definition: A security framework that uses digital certificates to provide strong proof of identity for authentication and group membership verification.
Key_Actors: Remote Procedure Call protocols; Services; Clients; Groups.
Operational_Workflow: A connection is established using an authenticated protocol; the public key infrastructure authenticates the connection to assure the service of the client's identity; the system uses this identity to identify which globally defined groups the client belongs to for authorization via an Access Control List.
Rules_and_Constraints: Public key infrastructure is used as a superior alternative to IP-based identification; identities and groups within the system represent products and services globally rather than just individual external customers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Digital certificates
Core_Definition: Security credentials used within a public key infrastructure to provide proof of identity for users, processes, or services.
Key_Actors: Users; Processes; Public key infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: A digital certificate is presented by a user or process to a service as part of a public key infrastructure; the system verifies the certificate to prove the identity of the client during the authentication process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Digital certificates are utilized to provide a more robust and secure identification mechanism than Internet Protocol addresses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Default closed policy
Core_Definition: An access control approach where the system is configured to ban everyone by default except for those specifically listed as permitted. It is considered the most simple form of an Access Control List.
Key_Actors: Users; Access Control List (ACL) mechanism; identification systems such as Internet Protocol (IP) addresses or a public key infrastructure (PKI) using digital certificates.
Operational_Workflow: The system identifies a user or process attempting to access the service; it checks the Access Control List to see if the user is explicitly mentioned; if the user is on the permitted list, access is granted; if the user is not mentioned in the list, the system performs a fail closed action and denies access.
Rules_and_Constraints: This policy requires the maintenance of a whitelist to function; identity should ideally be proven via digital certificates because IP addresses are considered the weakest form of identification and can be easily forged.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Whitelist
Core_Definition: A list used to specify which users are permitted access to a system or which specific data is to be accepted for import. It functions by rejecting all entities or data points that do not appear on the list.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; users or processes; data providers; identification mechanisms.
Operational_Workflow: In an access control context, the system verifies an identity against the list and permits only those present; in a data import context, operations staff use the list to indicate which regions or sources are of high enough quality to include; data is added to the list once verified and removed if its quality drops; all other data is rejected during the import process.
Rules_and_Constraints: A whitelist is the primary component of a default closed policy; it is used to ensure that only known good records or authorized users interact with the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the quality of data from a specific provider or region drops, it must be removed from the whitelist to prevent bad data from entering the system.

Concept_Name: Default open policy
Core_Definition: A policy where the default action of the system is to grant access to all users. Access is only restricted for specific entities that have been identified and placed on an exclusion list.
Key_Actors: Users; Access Control List (ACL) mechanism.
Operational_Workflow: The system receives an access request; it checks if the user or process appears on a blacklist; if the entity is not found on the blacklist, the system permits the access by default; if the entity is on the blacklist, access is denied.
Rules_and_Constraints: This policy is the reverse of a default closed policy; it is characterized by a fail open behavior for any user not explicitly mentioned in the Access Control List.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Blacklist
Core_Definition: A list used to specify individual users who are denied access or specific data inputs that are to be rejected. It operates with the assumption that all other users or data are acceptable by default.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; users; data providers; bad or incorrect data records.
Operational_Workflow: The system evaluates incoming requests or data batches; it compares the entries against the blacklist; if a match is found, the system blocks the user or rejects the specific data record; all other entries that are not on the blacklist are accepted and processed.
Rules_and_Constraints: A blacklist is the functional mechanism for a default open policy; it is used to block known bad records, such as those identified as incorrect in past data batches.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Blacklists are used when a bad record causes a problem with the system, allowing operations staff to block it via configuration rather than waiting for a software update.

Concept_Name: Queries per second (QPS) rates
Core_Definition: QPS rates are measurements used to establish rate limits for API access, determining how many requests a user or service can make within a specific timeframe.
Key_Actors: Premium customers; regular paid customers; unpaid customers; internal teams; service developers; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: A service identifies the user making a request and checks the Access Control List for their assigned rate limit; the system monitors the volume of incoming requests against this limit; requests that stay within the allocated QPS are processed normally while requests exceeding the rate are denied; monitoring systems may also compare actual QPS against prediction models to trigger alerts if levels deviate significantly from expected patterns.
Rules_and_Constraints: Different users are permitted different QPS rates based on their tier, such as unlimited for premium and low or no access for unpaid users; internal teams must negotiate QPS rates as part of a service's capacity planning; rate limits are often annotated within Access Control Lists alongside permission settings.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a team has not negotiated a specific rate, they may be granted a very low default rate limit for casual or trial use; alerts may be generated if the actual QPS is more than 10 percent above or below the predicted model.

Concept_Name: Fail open
Core_Definition: Fail open is a default policy for an access control list where a user is permitted access if they are not explicitly mentioned in the list.
Key_Actors: Access Control List (ACL) mechanism; users; service components.
Operational_Workflow: The system receives an access request and checks the identity against the Access Control List; if the specific user or group is not found in the list, the system defaults to a permit action; the user is granted access despite the lack of an explicit entry.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is associated with a default open policy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Users who are explicitly identified on a blacklist or marked with a deny action will still be denied access regardless of the fail open default.

Concept_Name: Fail closed
Core_Definition: Fail closed is a default policy for an access control list where any user not explicitly mentioned in the list is denied access.
Key_Actors: Access Control List (ACL) mechanism; users; service components.
Operational_Workflow: The system evaluates an identity requesting access against the Access Control List; if the identity does not appear on the list with an explicit permission, the system defaults to a deny action; the access request is rejected.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is associated with a default closed policy and the use of a whitelist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Only users specifically added to the whitelist are permitted access, while everyone else is banned by default.

Concept_Name: Google's Remote Procedure Call (RPC) protocol
Core_Definition: This is an internal protocol used by all Google internal APIs that incorporates a sophisticated Access Control List system for authentication and resource management.
Key_Actors: Internal service teams; clients; Public Key Infrastructure (PKI); products and services.
Operational_Workflow: Connections are authenticated using a Public Key Infrastructure to verify client identity and determine group memberships; the system consults Access Control Lists to check if the identity is permitted, denied, or permitted with a specific rate limit; teams negotiate their required QPS rates during the capacity planning phase to ensure adequate resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Identity and groups are defined globally and represent products and services rather than individual external customers; ACLs must be able to specify permit, deny, or rate-limited access.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: To eliminate the need for negotiating hundreds of lightweight or casual requests, teams that have not yet negotiated rates are granted access at a very low rate limit.

Concept_Name: Authenticated (state)
Core_Definition: The status of a connection once a service has verified the identity and group memberships of a client.
Key_Actors: Client systems; services; public key infrastructure; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A connection is established between a client and a service; the system uses a public key infrastructure to verify digital certificates provided by the client; the service confirms the identity of the client and determines which groups that client belongs to; the service uses this identity and group information to apply specific access controls and rate limits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Identity should be proven using a public key infrastructure with digital certificates because identification based on IP addresses can be easily forged; identity and groups should be globally defined for the organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a user login state is stored only on a local web server and not shared among replicas, a load balancer may send the user to a different replica where they are forced to log in again.

Concept_Name: Negotiated rates
Core_Definition: Pre-arranged queries per second limits agreed upon between service providers and client teams to manage capacity and resource usage.
Key_Actors: Service teams; client teams; access control systems.
Operational_Workflow: Teams meet to discuss their access needs as part of the service capacity planning process; a specific queries per second rate is agreed upon for the client team; this negotiated rate is programmed into the service access control list; the service identifies the client via authentication and enforces the specific agreed-upon rate.
Rules_and_Constraints: Rate limits can be tiered to provide different levels of access for premium, regular, and unpaid customers; negotiated rates should be used to prevent a single team from overloading a master database.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams that have not negotiated specific rates are granted access at a very low default rate limit to allow for experimentation and casual use without requiring manual intervention from the service team.

Concept_Name: Augmentation file
Core_Definition: A local file used by operations staff to supplement, override, or add data to a stream of information imported from an external provider.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; data import systems; external information providers.
Operational_Workflow: A service periodically receives data from an external directory or information provider; operations staff identifies errors, omissions, or necessary overrides in the incoming data; staff creates an augmentation file containing the locally produced data; the import system combines the provider data with the contents of the augmentation file to create the final data set.
Rules_and_Constraints: Augmentation files should be used to correct known bad data independently of the external provider's update cycle, which may take months to reflect changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The augmentation file can be used to intentionally insert jokes or Easter eggs into the data listings.

Concept_Name: Change limit
Core_Definition: A safety threshold based on the percentage of records modified during a data import that triggers a requirement for manual approval.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; automated data import systems.
Operational_Workflow: A baseline is established for the typical percentage of records changed during periodic data imports; a specific percentage limit is configured in the import system; the system calculates the percentage of records affected by a new data batch; if the change exceeds the pre-defined limit, the system halts the import and requires manual intervention by operations staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: Change limits are established as a safeguard to prevent disasters that might be caused by software bugs or a bad batch of incoming data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Software bug
Core_Definition: An error in software code or configuration that can cause system problems, ranging from strange behaviors to full-scale disasters.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff analyze changes to plain text configuration files to find small changes that cause problems; feature toggles or flag flips are used to disable new features that cause issues without rolling back the entire binary; operations staff file feature requests to identify problems to be solved by developers; teams schedule small releases specifically dedicated to fixing bugs from previous major releases.
Rules_and_Constraints: Small software releases should be expected to fix bugs from the previous major release; debug logging used to find bugs should be exempt from bureaucratic documentation and translation rules to maintain productivity; software should be configurable by automated means to allow for easy comparison and archiving of configurations to aid debugging.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If operations staff write features themselves because developers are unwilling, any bugs in that code will receive magnified blame; software bugs in weekly data imports can cause disasters if manual approval for large changes is not required.

Concept_Name: Bad batch of new data
Core_Definition: Information from external sources or periodic imports that is incorrect, mangled, or otherwise useless for the service.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; information providers; data directory services; the automated data import system.
Operational_Workflow: Data is periodically received from external providers and processed by an import system; operations staff use a blacklist to specify input to be rejected and a whitelist to specify data that is known to be high quality; an augmentation file is used to add local data or override known bad records; a change limit is established to require manual approval if the import affects more than a certain percentage of records.
Rules_and_Constraints: Mechanisms must be established that permit operations staff to control which data is accepted, rejected, or replaced; bad records must be blockable via configuration rather than waiting for a software update.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Corrections reported back to information providers may take months to appear in the data feed; data from a single provider may be high quality for certain regions while being categorically bad for others.

Concept_Name: Metrics
Core_Definition: Data points and measurements exposed by system components to provide visibility into how a system is functioning.
Key_Actors: System components; monitoring system; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Every component within a system exposes specific measurements to a central monitoring system; the monitoring system collects and displays these measurements for operations staff; the data is utilized to track system performance, perform capacity planning, and assist in troubleshooting.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each component of a system must expose metrics to the monitoring system; metrics are an essential requirement for monitoring availability and performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Availability
Core_Definition: The state of a service being accessible and functional for users, often achieved through automation and redundant design.
Key_Actors: Operations engineers; developers; load balancers; service replicas; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Highly available services are built with features that allow for the automation of operational tasks; redundant service replicas are placed behind load balancers to allow for maintenance and upgrades without service interruption; queue draining is used to stop new requests while finishing in-flight tasks before a replica is taken out of service; graceful degradation techniques allow the system to provide limited functionality during overloads or dependency outages.
Rules_and_Constraints: The best strategy for high availability is to build features into the software that enhance the ability to perform and automate operational tasks; software upgrades must be possible without taking down the service; the system should be designed to support multiple redundant replicas.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During a database outage, a service might switch to a read-only mode or work from a cache to provide partial results instead of an error; if a machine crashes suddenly, the next restart must automatically perform validations or repairs before service is restored.

Concept_Name: Performance
Core_Definition: Performance is the measure of a service's speed, stability, and reliability during operation, particularly as it relates to scaling and cost-effective management.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; development teams; monitoring systems; database master and read-only replicas.
Operational_Workflow: System components expose performance metrics to the monitoring system; operations staff utilize these metrics for capacity planning and troubleshooting; developers select between master and read-only database connections to optimize speed and resource usage; capacity planning accounts for the additional load and latency created by tasks such as backups and restores.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each component must expose metrics to the monitoring system; performance needs must be addressed early in the design phase; backup and restore burdens must be accounted for in latency calculations; developers must trade off speed for freshness when deciding which database connection a query should use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unoptimized queries can overload a master database if usage patterns change over time; software that relies on luck rather than official support for replication configurations may experience performance failures during new software releases.

Concept_Name: Logging
Core_Definition: Logging is the generation of human-readable and machine-parseable records used for technical debugging, troubleshooting, and meeting security or compliance audit requirements.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; legal and compliance departments; automated monitoring and parsing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Software generates logs that record parameters from important function calls during operation; debug logging is enabled on specific modules to manage information volume; logs are stored and examined during security and compliance audits to meet regulatory mandates; developers add debug statements as needed to reveal the internal state of the software.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logs should be both human-readable and machine-parseable; debug logs should be exempt from bureaucratic approval, documentation, and translation processes; auditing logs must satisfy specific regulatory requirements such as SOX, PCI DSS, or the EU Data Protection Directive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large systems can produce an overwhelming volume of information if logging is not restricted to specific modules; productivity can be halted if debug logging is subjected to the same bureaucratic paralysis as external-facing documentation.

Concept_Name: Permissions
Core_Definition: Permissions are access control mechanisms and settings used to manage authorized entry and ensure a service complies with security and legal audit standards.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; legal department; internal and external auditors; users and processes.
Operational_Workflow: Permissions are configured within the service to manage access controls and rate limits; these settings are maintained to ensure the service can pass security and compliance audits; identity is verified using mechanisms like digital certificates within a public key infrastructure; auditors examine these permissions to verify compliance with global standards and regulatory mandates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Permissions must be set up to enable the service to pass security and compliance audits; identity mechanisms should use digital certificates rather than easily forged IP addresses; systems must adhere to relevant regulatory mandates which can include more than 150 different standards worldwide.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Moving a virtual server across borders could violate local labor laws or data protection mandates if the administrator does not account for specific regional compliance issues; weak identification methods like IP addresses can be easily forged, compromising the permission system.

Concept_Name: Role accounts
Core_Definition: Role accounts are specific account configurations established to enable a service to be examined for and pass security and compliance audits.
Key_Actors: Legal department; operations staff; security and compliance auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Role accounts are established as part of the service's operational infrastructure; these accounts provide the necessary structure for the service to be examined for compliance; they are used during the audit process to verify that the service meets relevant security laws and standards.
Rules_and_Constraints: Role accounts must be set up to enable the service to be examined for and pass security and compliance audits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Security audits
Core_Definition: The process of examining a service's logging, permissions, and role accounts to ensure it meets security standards and requirements.
Key_Actors: Legal department; operations staff; auditors; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with setting up appropriate logging, permissions, and role accounts within the service. Operations staff then ensure the service can be examined by internal or external entities. This involves staying updated on rapidly changing requirements by consulting with the legal department to ensure all security protocols align with current laws.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must consult their legal department for information about the latest laws as the area is changing rapidly; debug logs are typically exempt from the strict documentation and translation rules that apply to other logged information.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Compliance audits
Core_Definition: Formal examinations to ensure a corporation follows relevant laws, regulatory mandates, and global standards governing their business operations.
Key_Actors: Legal department; corporations; system administrators; international regulatory bodies; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations set up logging and access controls to prepare for examination. Legal departments are consulted to navigate over 150 regulatory mandates such as SOX, PCI DSS, and the EU Data Protection Directive. Administrators must perform tasks like moving virtual servers or making changes while remaining compliant with local labor laws and data protection mandates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporations must comply with specific regulations like SOX, j-SOX, c-SOX, PCI DSS, the EU Data Protection Directive, and Singapore MAS; global standards like CobiT 5 and ISO/IEC 27001 may also apply; administrators must be aware that performing IT administration across international borders can trigger compliance issues.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failure to pass an audit can lead to massive fines or the corporation being prevented from conducting business; inadvertent violations can occur if a system administrator moves data across a company intranet without knowledge of local labor laws or data protection mandates.

Concept_Name: Public cloud services
Core_Definition: External computing resources and platforms utilized by corporations that must be evaluated for adherence to governance and legal requirements.
Key_Actors: Corporations; public cloud providers; legal departments; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Corporations considering these services must first determine if the provider allows them to remain compliant with the laws governing their specific business. This involves assessing whether the use of the service will lead to a failed audit and subsequent fines.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service must allow the corporation to pass security and compliance audits; use of the service must not violate local labor laws or international data protection directives.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a public cloud service causes a corporation to fail an audit, the business may face massive fines or be legally barred from conducting business until a new audit is passed.

Concept_Name: IT administration
Core_Definition: The management and operation of IT systems, which includes performing scheduled changes, managing resources, and ensuring systems meet regulatory mandates.
Key_Actors: System administrators; operations staff; developers; legal departments.
Operational_Workflow: Administrators perform daily tasks such as configuration, backups, and software upgrades. They may move virtual servers across company intranets as part of approved changes. Over time, the role shifts from performing repetitive manual tasks to building, maintaining, and improving the automation that handles those tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Personnel must comply with local labor laws and data protection directives when working on systems in different countries; administration should be supported by features like plain text configuration files and automated startup/shutdown hooks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Administrators may inadvertently make an employer non-compliant and subject to sanctions if they perform administrative tasks on servers located in jurisdictions with different labor laws or data protection mandates than their own.

Concept_Name: EU Data Protection Directive
Core_Definition: A regulatory mandate with specific IT audit requirements governing data protection and the movement of data across borders within the European Union.
Key_Actors: System administrators; legal departments; employers; the European Union.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams must ensure that administrative tasks, such as moving virtual servers across a company intranet, comply with regional mandates; the process involves consulting legal departments and setting up logging, permissions, and role accounts to pass security and compliance audits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performing IT administration from one country on a server located in another country may violate local labor laws or data protection mandates; non-compliance can make an employer subject to sanctions and fines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A system administrator may inadvertently violate mandates even when performing a scheduled and approved change if they lack knowledge of local laws.

Concept_Name: SOX
Core_Definition: A regulation that imposes specific IT audit requirements on corporations to ensure security and compliance.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditing teams; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Logging, permissions, and role accounts are set up to enable the service to be examined for and pass security and compliance audits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must comply with the relevant laws governing their business to avoid massive fines or being prevented from conducting business.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: j-SOX
Core_Definition: One of many regulatory mandates worldwide that includes specific IT audit requirements for organizations.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditing teams; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Services are configured with logging, permissions, and role accounts to facilitate formal examination during security and compliance audits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Companies must pass audits to avoid potential fines or being blocked from conducting business.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: c-SOX
Core_Definition: A regulatory mandate with specific IT audit requirements used to govern corporate security and compliance.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditing teams; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Operational staff establish logging and access controls so that a service can be successfully audited for compliance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must adhere to these mandates to prevent the risk of massive fines or business disruption following a failed audit.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: PCI DSS
Core_Definition: PCI DSS is a regulatory mandate that includes specific IT audit requirements for corporations to ensure security and compliance.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditors; public cloud service providers.
Operational_Workflow: The operational staff sets up logging, permissions, and role accounts to allow the service to be examined during security and compliance audits; the legal department is consulted for the latest information on the law to ensure the corporation does not fail its next audit.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed to be examined for and pass audits; failure to comply can result in massive fines or the corporation being prevented from conducting business.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Corporations using public cloud services may face the risk of failing audits and being subject to sanctions if those services do not meet compliance requirements.

Concept_Name: Singapore MAS
Core_Definition: Singapore MAS is a specific regulatory mandate with IT audit requirements that affect corporate governance.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditors; public cloud services.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations configure their services with logging and access controls to enable security and compliance examinations; legal departments are consulted to ensure the service adheres to the latest version of the mandate.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporations must adhere to these mandates to avoid significant fines and remain authorized to conduct business.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a company fails an audit under this mandate, it may be prevented from conducting further business until it passes a subsequent audit.

Concept_Name: CobiT 5
Core_Definition: CobiT 5 is a global standard that applies to various IT governance and compliance scenarios.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: This standard is used to structure auditing features like logging and role accounts so that a service can be audited for compliance and security.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a global standard used to address various governance scenarios in the corporate environment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ISO/IEC 27001
Core_Definition: ISO/IEC 27001 is a global standard utilized by corporations to manage IT governance and compliance auditing.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: The standard is implemented by setting up a service to be examined for security audits through the use of logging, permissions, and role accounts.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a global standard applied to various scenarios involving governance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ISO/IEC 27002
Core_Definition: A global standard that applies to various IT governance scenarios and auditing requirements.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: This standard is used to help services be examined for and pass security and compliance audits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ISO/IEC 27005
Core_Definition: A global standard that applies to various IT governance scenarios and auditing requirements.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: This standard is used to help services be examined for and pass security and compliance audits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: IT governance
Core_Definition: A framework involving global standards and regulatory mandates used to ensure a service meets security, legal, and compliance requirements.
Key_Actors: Corporations; legal departments; system administrators; regulatory bodies such as those overseeing SOX, PCI DSS, or the EU Data Protection Directive.
Operational_Workflow: Logging, permissions, and role accounts are set up to enable the service to be examined for security and compliance audits; organizations use global standards to ensure they do not face fines or business restrictions; administrators must manage systems in accordance with local labor and data protection laws.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must be able to pass audits to avoid massive fines or being prevented from conducting business; systems must comply with various international mandates such as the EU Data Protection Directive or Singapore MAS.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A system administrator may inadvertently make an employer non-compliant by moving a virtual server across a company intranet if they are unaware of local labor laws or regional data protection mandates.

Concept_Name: Debug logs
Core_Definition: Records generated by software that capture the parameters sent to and returned from important function calls to assist in troubleshooting.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; marketing; product management; legal personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Developers add logging statements to modules to capture functional data; logging is enabled on individual modules to manage data volume; operations personnel use the source code as documentation to understand and consume the logged information.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logs should be both human-readable and machine-parseable; debug logs should be exempt from rules requiring documentation translation or approval by marketing and legal because they are not visible to external users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If debug logging cannot be enabled on individual modules, the volume of information can become overwhelming for the system.

Concept_Name: Human-readable (logs)
Core_Definition: Debug logs generated by software that are formatted in a way that people can easily understand when troubleshooting function calls and parameters.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; software modules.
Operational_Workflow: Developers add debug logging statements to individual software modules to record parameters sent to and returned from important function calls; the software generates these logs during execution; operations personnel read and interpret these logs to diagnose and repair systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Debugging logs should be both human-readable and machine-parseable; debug logging should be able to be enabled on individual modules to prevent data volume from becoming overwhelming; these logs should be exempt from bureaucratic approval and translation processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If logging cannot be toggled for individual modules in large systems, the volume of information can become too overwhelming for humans to effectively process.

Concept_Name: Machine-parseable (logs)
Core_Definition: Debug logs formatted specifically to allow automated systems and software tools to easily extract and process data regarding function calls.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; automated analysis tools.
Operational_Workflow: Software generates logs during execution that include parameters from important function calls in a well-defined format; automated tools or scripts parse this data to provide analysis or facilitate debugging; operations staff use the resulting data to monitor or repair the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logs must be both machine-parseable and human-readable; debug logging should be distinct from the logging used for security and compliance auditing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Source code
Core_Definition: The readable program instructions that define software behavior and serve as the primary documentation for understanding debug logs.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; source code repositories; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers write software instructions and create configuration files that are stored in a source code repository; systems may be configured to read their settings directly from the repository; operations personnel access the repository to examine the code as documentation for troubleshooting or to analyze configuration history using text comparison tools.
Rules_and_Constraints: Source code should be available to operations personnel to act as documentation for logs; configurations stored in source code repositories should be in plain text to allow for auditing and comparison with tools like UNIX diff; it must be possible to disable direct configuration reads from the repository during emergencies or for experimentation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a configuration is stored as an opaque binary blob instead of source-compatible text, history tracking and change analysis become impossible; when the source code repository is down, systems must have a way to provide configurations directly to ensure the service can still function.

Concept_Name: Exception
Core_Definition: A severe software error condition that causes a program to intentionally exit rather than attempting an unlikely or difficult recovery.
Key_Actors: Software authors; operations staff; exception collection frameworks; centralized databases.
Operational_Workflow: The software encounters an unrecoverable situation or data corruption and triggers an exit; an exception collection framework detects the exit and gathers the error message and relevant metadata; this data is submitted to a centralized database for storage; operations staff analyze the history and volume of exceptions to determine system health or identify trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: Exceptions should be collected centrally for analysis; exception volume should be used as a metric to determine if software roll-outs should be stopped or if maintenance should be cancelled.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automatic restart capabilities can cause exceptions to go unnoticed if they are not specifically collected and made visible; certain data corruption scenarios are handled as exceptions so that human intervention can replace automated recovery processes.

Concept_Name: Software framework
Core_Definition: A library or system written by operational staff that developers can link to their code to automate operational functions and improve system operability.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; developers; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Operational staff create frameworks that provide standardized ways to handle operational tasks; developers link these frameworks into their applications; the framework then performs specific functions such as making it easy to report status to a monitoring system or gathering exceptions and core dumps for centralized analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational staff should write these frameworks to make developers self-sufficient rather than dependent on the operations team; the frameworks should simplify the reporting of status and the collection of debug information.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Exception collector
Core_Definition: A software framework that detects software exceptions, gathers error messages and associated information, and submits that data to a centralized database.
Key_Actors: Software systems; operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The collector detects a software exception when a program intentionally exits due to a severe error; it gathers the error message and other relevant information; this data is submitted to a centralized database to make invisible errors visible; operations staff analyze the collected history for trends in volume or repetition and correlate exception levels to specific software releases; the system helps determine if maintenance or software roll-outs should be proceeded with or cancelled.
Rules_and_Constraints: Maintenance or software roll-outs should be cancelled if the collector shows many exceptions; a sharp increase in exceptions during a roll-out requires the process to stop; repetitive exceptions should be detected and fixed at the root cause before they escalate; exceptions should be collected centrally for analysis because automatic restart capabilities can cause them to go unnoticed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Operating system "panic"
Core_Definition: A specific type of software exception where a severe error occurs and the operating system intentionally exits.
Key_Actors: Software authors; the operating system; human administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The software encounters a situation that the author has determined is unlikely to happen or too difficult to recover from; the program declares an exception and intentionally exits; the system stops functioning to prevent further issues; a human is then required to intervene and handle the situation, which is often preferable to software-led recovery in scenarios like data corruption.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: blue screen of death
Core_Definition: An example of a severe software exception where the system stops and exits intentionally due to a critical error.
Key_Actors: Software authors; the operating system; human administrators.
Operational_Workflow: An error occurs that is so severe the software author decided recovery was unlikely or difficult; the system triggers an exception and performs an intentional exit; this halt prevents the software from attempting to handle complex scenarios like data corruption; the event is intended to be handled or analyzed by a human rather than the software.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automatic restart capability
Core_Definition: A feature where a service restarts automatically upon machine boot or process failure to ensure continuous availability.
Key_Actors: Ubuntu Upstart; Apache Mesos; Google Omega; operating system hooks; automated monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The service is configured with boot time scripts or process management systems; the system monitors for process death or machine reboots; if a machine crashes, the next restart automatically triggers data validations or repairs; the process management system restarts the service on the same machine or an entirely different machine to restore functionality.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service should include proper operating system hooks for orderly shutdown; startup and shutdown times must be documented for disaster recovery and UPS capacity planning; crash recovery procedures should be exercised frequently to ensure reliability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In large scale power outages, 1 to 5 percent of machines may fail to boot on the first try; automatic restarts can cause certain software exceptions to go unnoticed unless a centralized collection system is used.

Concept_Name: Software quality
Core_Definition: The reliability and robustness of a system as indicated by its ability to handle exceptions and process data without failure.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations personnel; exception collection systems.
Operational_Workflow: Software generates exceptions during severe errors which are collected by a centralized framework; these exceptions are stored in a database for analysis; teams study the history and frequency of these exceptions to identify trends; a decrease in the frequency of specific exceptions over time indicates that software quality is improving.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code should meet corporate wide high standards for quality and style to facilitate collaboration; documentation for debugging information is ideally the source code itself which should be accessible to operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Quality can be compromised if post crash recovery procedures receive a disproportionately small amount of testing; software bugs or bad data batches can trigger high rates of data change that require manual override.

Concept_Name: Playbook
Core_Definition: A collaborative document containing operating procedures that combines technical developer steps with operational and business requirements.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; database teams; management teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers and operations staff work together to document operational scenarios and error situations; the playbook defines when specific procedures like failovers should be performed; staff follow a step by step process including notifying relevant teams and verifying dependencies; a test suite is executed to verify the success or failure of the operation; the results are communicated back to stakeholders.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation must be a collaborative effort rather than the sole responsibility of one team; every procedure must include a test suite that verifies success or failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Hot spare
Core_Definition: A redundant system component kept in a ready state to take over service roles in the event of a failover.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; database teams; monitoring systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: An impending failover is announced to relevant teams and managers; operations staff verify that the spare system has sufficient resources such as free disk space; dependencies are checked to ensure they are operating within parameters; the failover procedure is executed to transition service to the spare; the system verifies that dependencies have successfully switched to the hot spare.
Rules_and_Constraints: Procedures for switching to a hot spare should be documented in the playbook; specific checks for disk space and dependency health must be performed before and after the transition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Failover
Core_Definition: The precise steps required to shift a system from its primary state to a hot spare.
Key_Actors: Developers who write the technical steps; operational staff who perform the operation and document the business context; the db-team and manager-team who receive notifications.
Operational_Workflow: Announce the impending operation to relevant mailing lists; verify the hot spare has sufficient resources such as free disk space; check that all system dependencies are operating within parameters; execute the system failover procedure; verify that dependencies have successfully switched to the spare and are functioning; communicate the outcome via email to all original recipients.
Rules_and_Constraints: Procedures must be documented in a playbook; every procedure must include a test suite to verify success or failure; the playbook must specify when the action is to be done and who to notify.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Test suite
Core_Definition: A set of checks integrated into an operational procedure to confirm whether the task was successful or unsuccessful.
Key_Actors: Operations team; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Built into playbook procedures as a collaborative effort; used during a task to verify system state and dependencies; executed following an operation to ensure the service is operating correctly.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is critical that every operational procedure include a test suite; success or failure must be verifiable through these steps.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automation
Core_Definition: The use of software and frameworks to handle repetitive tasks such as configuration, process management, and resource allocation without manual intervention.
Key_Actors: Operations staff who build and maintain automation; developers who provide APIs and configuration hooks; process management systems such as Ubuntu Upstart, Apache Mesos, or Google Omega.
Operational_Workflow: Document manual processes to identify steps for automation; solidify processes as candidates for automated implementation; provide configuration via text files or APIs for automated systems to read and update; implement frameworks that allow developers to be self-sufficient; deploy process managers to restart services automatically on reboot or across different machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be configurable by automated means; configuration should take the form of text files with well-defined formats to ensure they are easy to parse and generate; locking protocols must be used to prevent update collisions or reading incomplete states.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a machine crashes suddenly the next restart should automatically perform data validations or repairs; if a feature that reads configuration from a source repository is used it must be possible to disable it during emergencies when the repository is down.

Concept_Name: Prioritization
Core_Definition: The process of selecting which operational features to implement by weighing their expected impact against the effort required.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; business teams.
Operational_Workflow: Identify missing features and file requests; document risks and business impact to justify the request; plot projects on a graph comparing effort to impact; focus exclusively on high-impact items; execute low-effort and high-impact projects first; selectively choose high-impact projects that require larger effort.
Rules_and_Constraints: Focus on impact rather than just effort; ignore low-hanging fruit if it has very little impact; focus exclusively on high-impact items; fixing the biggest bottleneck typically yields the highest impact.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-performing teams focus on impact while low-performing teams often waste resources on easy tasks with little impact because they are emotionally satisfying.

Concept_Name: Expected impact
Core_Definition: A measurement used to prioritize operational feature requests based on the business risks they mitigate and the magnitude of the improvement they bring to service stability.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; business teams.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff identify a missing operational feature and file a formal request; the staff members detail the specific risks and impact to the business if the feature is not implemented; the request is plotted on a prioritization graph against the required effort; high-impact items are then selected for implementation to improve the service life cycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: High-performing teams must focus exclusively on high-impact items; the expected impact should be the primary driver for prioritizing developer resources for operational features; impact is often maximized by identifying and fixing the biggest bottlenecks in the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Focusing on tasks with very little impact wastes organizational resources and fails to solve significant operational problems; even if a task provides emotional satisfaction; it should be avoided if the impact is low.

Concept_Name: Effort
Core_Definition: The amount of work or difficulty involved in implementing a specific operational change; ranging from easy to hard.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: An operational need is identified and translated into a feature request; the development or operations team assesses the resource intensity and time required for implementation; this effort level is used as one axis on a prioritization matrix to decide which projects to fund.
Rules_and_Constraints: Implementation strategies should favor projects that offer the biggest impact for the smallest amount of effort; larger effort projects should be selected selectively and only if they offer high impact.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Low-hanging fruit
Core_Definition: Operational tasks that are categorized as requiring the smallest amount of effort to complete.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Teams identify tasks that are simple or easy to implement; these tasks are mapped to the low-effort end of the prioritization scale; developers and managers often gravitate toward these tasks because they are easier to complete than high-effort projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resources should not be wasted on low-hanging fruit if the tasks have very little impact; teams should avoid the temptation to prioritize ease of implementation over actual operational results.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While these tasks may be emotionally satisfying to finish; they are considered a trap if they do not solve actual operational problems.

Concept_Name: Bottleneck
Core_Definition: A specific point of congestion or a constraint in a system that limits overall operational effectiveness.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Staff analyze the service to identify the primary factor restricting performance or reliability; this item is marked for remediation as a high-priority project; resolving this constraint is used as a tactic to achieve the highest possible impact for the effort expended.
Rules_and_Constraints: Fixing the biggest bottleneck usually provides the biggest impact; identifying and addressing bottlenecks is a key behavior of high-performing teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 80/20 Rule (for operational features)
Core_Definition: A resource allocation strategy that alternates developer focus between new features and operational needs based on the size of the software release.
Key_Actors: Product managers; developers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The organization alternates between big releases and small releases; during big releases 20 percent of developer time is allocated to operational requests; during small releases 80 percent of developer time is spent on operational requests; because small releases involve fewer total hours the actual time spent on operations remains consistent across both cycles.
Rules_and_Constraints: Big releases must prioritize major new features; small releases must prioritize fixing bugs from the previous major release; the goal is to balance the time spent on operational issues versus new features.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small releases may include one or two high-priority features even though they are primarily intended for bug fixes and operational requests.

Concept_Name: Big releases
Core_Definition: Major software updates primarily focused on introducing significant new functionality and features to a service.
Key_Actors: Product managers; developers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers spend the vast majority of their effort building major new features; as part of a negotiated 80/20 rule 20 percent of developer time during these cycles is dedicated to addressing operational issues requested by the operations team.
Rules_and_Constraints: These releases are expected to provide the major features required by the business or end users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Small releases
Core_Definition: Software updates primarily designed to address bugs from previous major releases and improve operational stability.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Developers focus on stabilizing the system and addressing maintenance needs; under the 80/20 rule 80 percent of developer time is spent on requests from the operations team; these releases follow big releases to ensure quality and reliability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Small releases are not intended to add major new features; the focus is on bug fixes and operational requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: One or two high-priority features might be included in a small release if they are deemed necessary.

Concept_Name: Coding standards
Core_Definition: A set of internal rules, quality benchmarks, and style guidelines that ensure software is written consistently and remains compatible with existing architecture.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; external contributors.
Operational_Workflow: Development teams follow established internal infrastructure and architectural visions when writing code; outside contributors must have their code undergo an approval process featuring feedback and revisions; at organizations like Google corporate-wide standards are used to ensure compatibility between different teams.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code must be compatible with the internal infrastructure and the future software architecture; any code submitted by outsiders must meet the team's high standard for quality and style; developers may reject code from operations staff if it does not adhere to these standards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small code submissions from outsiders are typically easier to accept than large ones; open source projects are more accustomed to receiving code contributions from many sources provided they meet standards.

Concept_Name: Internal infrastructure
Core_Definition: The underlying technical systems and foundational standards used by a development team that may be unfamiliar to operations staff if they are not integrated into the development process.
Key_Actors: Developers who possess knowledge of the infrastructure; operations staff who may be treated as outsiders to it.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff must understand the internal infrastructure and coding standards to successfully contribute code; without this knowledge, any features they write may be rejected or contain bugs that receive magnified blame.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Software architecture
Core_Definition: The fundamental design and structure of a system that must be engineered to support operational functions like maintenance, updates, and monitoring throughout its life cycle.
Key_Actors: Developers who design the architecture; operations staff who operate what is built; business teams who drive requirements.
Operational_Workflow: Designing for operations involves building features into the architecture during early planning stages to enhance automation; providing control points for key interactions; and ensuring the architecture supports live data restores and database scaling through read-only replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: The architecture should allow for the restoration of single accounts without locking out users; it must be designed to support multiple redundant replicas; it should be configurable by automated means using text files or APIs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Architectures not designed with operations in mind often lack control points and force operations staff into improvising solutions; nonreplicated systems within an architecture are difficult to upgrade without downtime.

Concept_Name: Operational services
Core_Definition: Specialized frameworks, libraries, and tools created by operations personnel to support the primary services and improve the overall operability of the system.
Key_Actors: Operational staff who code the services; developers who use the frameworks to improve their own software.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff spend time coding services such as status reporting libraries and exception gathering tools; these are then integrated into the development process to make services easier to monitor and debug.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational services should be designed to make developers self-sufficient rather than dependent on the operations team for routine tasks like core dump analysis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Ecosystem (operational)
Core_Definition: The technical environment and support structure in which primary services run, established through the creation of operational services.
Key_Actors: Operational staff who build and maintain the ecosystem; developers who operate within it.
Operational_Workflow: Operational staff create the ecosystem by developing frameworks and coding operational services that simplify the running of distributed systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Library
Core_Definition: A software framework or set of functions written by operations staff that can be linked to an application to perform specific operational tasks.
Key_Actors: Operations staff who create the library; developers who integrate the library into their code.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff develop a library that handles operational needs such as reporting status to a monitoring system; developers link their software to this library; the software then uses the library functions to become more observable or manageable without manual operations effort.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff should prioritize writing libraries that make developers self-sufficient; the goal is to create an ecosystem in which services run effectively.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Core dumps
Core_Definition: Data generated by software during an exception or crash that is gathered for subsequent debugging and analysis.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; automated gathering tools.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff write a tool that automatically gathers exceptions and core dumps; the tool provides these files to developers for analysis; this replaces the need for developers to email requests to operations to retrieve files manually after a crash.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation on how to consume the information in these files is the source code itself; operations personnel should have access to this source code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Open source projects
Core_Definition: Publicly accessible software initiatives that encourage collaborative code contributions from a wide community of external participants.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; external developers; the wider community of users.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff identify a need for a tool or a wrapper around vendor software; they develop the software and publish it externally as an open source product; a community of users develops around the project to provide mutual support and reduce dependency on the original author.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code submissions from outsiders are typically easier to accept when they are small; collaborative organizations are more accustomed to receiving contributions from many sources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Code approval process
Core_Definition: A formal system for reviewing and refining code contributions from outsiders to ensure they meet team quality and style standards before integration.
Key_Actors: Outside contributors; the project team responsible for the code base.
Operational_Workflow: An outsider submits a code change to a project; the system facilitates a process of feedback and revisions between the contributor and the team; once the code is deemed acceptable by both parties, it is accepted into the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must ensure the code meets the team standards; corporate-wide high standards for code quality and style facilitate compatibility between different teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Code quality
Core_Definition: A high standard for software excellence that ensures compatibility and facilitates the acceptance of code contributions across different teams.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; organizations like Google; third-party contributors.
Operational_Workflow: Code is submitted for review through a code approval process; the submission is evaluated against established team or corporate-wide standards; once the code is deemed acceptable by both parties, it is merged into the system; the quality is tracked over time by monitoring the frequency of software exceptions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standards for quality should be corporate-wide to ensure compatibility; quality assurance testing should prioritize critical procedures like crash recovery; a decrease in exception frequency is used as an indicator that software quality is improving.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If code quality does not meet standards, bugs in the code may receive magnified blame; a sharp increase in exceptions during a software roll-out may indicate a bad release that requires the roll-out to be cancelled.

Concept_Name: Code style
Core_Definition: A set of established coding standards and conventions used by a development team to ensure software consistency and readability.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; outside contributors.
Operational_Workflow: Contributors learn the internal coding standards and overall vision of the development team; code is written to match these styles; the code undergoes an approval process to verify it meets team standards before being accepted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporate-wide high standards for style help ensure that code written by one team is compatible with others; outsiders must adhere to these standards to successfully contribute to a project.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a contributor is an outsider who does not know the internal coding standards, their code may be rejected by the developers; bugs found in code that does not follow standards may lead to magnified blame.

Concept_Name: Embedded with developers (operations staff)
Core_Definition: A collaborative arrangement where operations personnel work directly within development teams to gain deep technical knowledge of the software.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff are placed within the development team; they spend time learning the specific code base of the service; they become familiar with the established release and testing processes; they build a direct relationship with the code they are responsible for operating.
Rules_and_Constraints: This arrangement should be implemented when possible to improve technical understanding; the text notes that this is not a replacement for having developers implement operational features themselves.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Embedded developers with operations staff
Core_Definition: A management strategy where software developers are assigned to work within the operations team to understand the practical needs of running a service.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Developers are placed into the operations environment; they may participate in this arrangement through specific periods such as six-month rotations; they perform or observe operational duties to gain firsthand insight into why operational features and requirements are necessary.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is considered better than embedding operations with developers because it directly educates the people writing the code about operational needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Postmortem report
Core_Definition: A document used to provide context for a feature request by illustrating the real-world impact of an issue to a third-party vendor.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; Third-party vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff identify a deficiency or issue within a vendor's product; they draft a report that documents the specific incident or context surrounding the problem; the report is submitted to the vendor along with a feature request to encourage a constructive resolution.
Rules_and_Constraints: The report must be written in a constructive way; it should include a specific feature request; it serves to raise the visibility of issues when a vendor might otherwise be unresponsive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Wrapper
Core_Definition: A custom code framework built around third-party software to provide necessary operational services that the original software does not handle gracefully.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; Third-party vendor software.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff identify specific operational functions, such as startup and shutdown services, that a vendor's software handles ungracefully; they write a code framework to surround the software; this framework manages the operational tasks while interfacing with the underlying vendor product.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is recommended that these systems be published externally as open source products if they solve common problems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Open source products
Core_Definition: Software projects published externally that allow for collaborative development and are typically more accustomed to receiving code contributions from various sources.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; External developers; Outsiders contributing code.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff develop a tool, framework, or wrapper to solve an operational need; the code is published externally for others to use; the project accepts small code submissions from outside contributors to improve the software.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code submissions from outsiders are generally easier for teams to accept when the changes are small; publishing such products is recommended for tools that others may also need.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Community (around code)
Core_Definition: A group of external users and developers who contribute to and help maintain a piece of published software.
Key_Actors: Original authors (operations staff); External users; External contributors.
Operational_Workflow: An operations team publishes their custom-built tools or wrappers as open source products; they foster a group of users and developers around the project; this group takes on a role in supporting the code over time.
Rules_and_Constraints: The primary goal of developing a community is to make the long-term support of the software less dependent on the efforts of the original author.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Force multiplier
Core_Definition: A great design for operations that helps eliminate certain operational duties entirely, providing an impact equivalent to hiring an additional person.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; automation systems; system designers.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are designed to embed knowledge or capability directly into the operational process; this design replaces the need for manual intervention by staff; the operations team shifts its focus from performing repetitive tasks to building and maintaining the automation that handles those tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Strive to create systems that embed knowledge or capability into the process; replace the need for operational intervention whenever possible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Resource allocations
Core_Definition: The process of requesting and assigning computing assets such as RAM, disk space, and CPU to specific jobs or users.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; users; APIs; provisioning systems; clusters.
Operational_Workflow: In manual systems, users request allocations via email which are then processed by operations staff; in improved models, an API enables a self-service process where users manage their own resources; advanced systems automatically monitor usage and allocate resources without manual input.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should maintain an effective balance among all jobs on a cluster; resources should be reallocated and shifted over time based on monitored use; providing an API is necessary to transition from manual processing to self-service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Manual processing via email is mentioned as an inefficient historical practice that is replaced by APIs.

Concept_Name: Self-service
Core_Definition: A model that allows users and developers to manage their own resources or perform tasks independently using tools and APIs provided by operations.
Key_Actors: Users; developers; operations staff who build the frameworks; APIs.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff write frameworks, libraries, or tools that handle operational tasks; these tools are made available to developers; developers use these tools to be self-sufficient for tasks like managing resources or analyzing exceptions rather than being dependent on operations staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff should spend time coding services that create an ecosystem where developers can be self-sufficient; tools should be designed to gather data such as exceptions and core dumps for analysis without manual operational intervention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If self-service tools are not provided, developers remain dependent on operations and must email requests for common tasks like gathering core dumps.

Concept_Name: Provisioning systems
Core_Definition: Software systems used to define and distribute hardware resources like CPU, RAM, and disk space to various jobs.
Key_Actors: Provisioning software; jobs; clusters; automated monitoring components.
Operational_Workflow: Basic provisioning systems require a user to specify the exact amount of RAM, disk, and CPU each job needs; advanced provisioning systems operate without manual resource specifications by monitoring actual usage; the system automatically allocates what is needed and reallocates resources across the cluster to maintain balance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Better provisioning systems should not require users to specify resources manually; the system must maintain an effective balance among all jobs on a cluster over time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: RAM
Core_Definition: A memory resource used by jobs within a cluster that is managed and allocated by a provisioning system.
Key_Actors: Provisioning systems; jobs; and automated monitoring and allocation mechanisms.
Operational_Workflow: Users may manually specify RAM requirements when submitting a job to a provisioning system; a more advanced system monitors usage and automatically allocates RAM while maintaining a balance across all cluster jobs; the system reallocates and shifts resources around the cluster over time as usage patterns change.
Rules_and_Constraints: Advanced provisioning systems should not require users to specify resources manually but should instead monitor usage and allocate resources automatically to maintain an effective balance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Disk
Core_Definition: A physical storage device and a resource category that requires monitoring and capacity planning to support service operations.
Key_Actors: Hard disk motors; provisioning systems; operating systems; operations staff; and managers.
Operational_Workflow: Disk space is allocated to jobs by provisioning systems or monitored automatically for usage; physical drives can be hot-swapped or hot-plugged while a machine is running; during a failover procedure, operations staff verify the available disk space on a hot spare before completing the transition.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operating systems must be specifically told to scan for new disks for them to be recognized after a hot-plug operation; failover procedures should include steps to verify specific space requirements, such as ensuring a hot spare has at least 10 terabytes of free disk space.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Thousands of hard disk motors spinning up simultaneously can overload power systems during a mass boot event; between 1 and 5 percent of machines might fail to boot on the first attempt after a total power loss.

Concept_Name: CPU
Core_Definition: A processing resource allocated to jobs to enable their execution within a distributed system or cluster.
Key_Actors: Provisioning systems; jobs; and automated allocation software.
Operational_Workflow: CPU resources are requested for a job or automatically assigned by a provisioning system; the system monitors usage and reallocates or shifts resources over time; an effective balance is maintained among all jobs running on the cluster through continuous monitoring.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automated systems are preferred over manual resource specification to maintain balance and efficiency across the cluster infrastructure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Job (workload)
Core_Definition: A task or service instance running on a cluster that consumes hardware resources such as RAM, disk, and CPU.
Key_Actors: Provisioning systems; automated monitoring systems; and resource allocation algorithms.
Operational_Workflow: A job is defined and its resource requirements are identified within a provisioning system; the system assigns the necessary RAM, disk, and CPU to the job; the system continuously monitors the job's resource consumption and shifts resources or reallocates the job across the cluster to ensure an effective balance.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system should ideally manage resource allocation automatically rather than requiring the user to specify fixed resource amounts for each job.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Alert thresholds
Core_Definition: Limits or boundaries established within a monitoring system that trigger notifications when a specific metric deviates from expected values.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators or the monitoring system itself set specific numerical or percentage limits for system metrics such as Queries Per Second (QPS); the monitoring system tracks actual performance in real time; an alert is generated if the actual performance is more than a certain percentage, such as 10 percent, above or below the expected value.
Rules_and_Constraints: Creating and fine-tuning thresholds manually is considered an endless task that should be avoided when possible; thresholds can be set based on a prediction model to eliminate manual intervention; alerts can also be used to ensure that temporary emergency configurations are not forgotten if they remain active for more than a certain amount of time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Prediction model
Core_Definition: A data-driven mechanism used to forecast expected system performance or resource requirements for future points in time.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The system analyzes historical data to develop a forecast of activity, such as the expected Queries Per Second (QPS) for every hour of the year; this forecast is used by the monitoring system to establish dynamic baselines; the system then automatically sets alert thresholds based on these predictions rather than relying on manual input.
Rules_and_Constraints: The model must be accurate to be effective in reducing operational workload; it is used as a force multiplier to eliminate repetitive tasks and allow the system to scale better with less operational support.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 3. Selecting a Service Platform
Section: 
============================================================

Concept_Name: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)
Core_Definition: Computer and network hardware, real or virtual, ready for you to use. It provides bare machines networked and ready for the customer to install an operating system and software.
Key_Actors: Operators looking to build their own platforms; third-party vendors or service providers; development and business teams.
Operational_Workflow: The service provider manages the physical infrastructure including machines, power, cooling, and networking. The customer installs the operating system and software while coordinating and tuning the various parts to work together. Customers maintain total control of the operating system and must locate services in geographic reliability zones to manage uptime and failover.
Rules_and_Constraints: Providers charge for compute time, storage, and network traffic. Customers should benchmark local storage, remote storage, CPU, and network performance at different times of the day. Service providers must segment services into multiple zones and guarantee that planned downtime does not strike more than one zone at a time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Downtime is inevitable due to maintenance or natural disasters. High network charges can occur if information is not cached or stored locally. Virtual machines may experience steal time which requires deleting and re-creating the machine to find a less oversubscribed host.

Concept_Name: Platform as a Service (PaaS)
Core_Definition: Your software running in a vendor-provided framework or stack. It manages all aspects of the infrastructure and much of the application stack for the developer.
Key_Actors: Developers; third-party vendors or providers.
Operational_Workflow: A developer uploads application-level software to a framework provided by a vendor. The framework automatically handles load balancing, scaling, bandwidth, CPU allocations, and authentication. The vendor manages the specific resources dedicated to the application while the developer focuses on software creation without managing the underlying operating system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users are restricted to using the vendor-provided platform and do not have direct access to the operating system. Charges are based on CPU, bandwidth, and storage usage. Processes run in a secure jail to prevent them from breaking out of the service framework.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Users may be unable to add binaries or use specific libraries until the vendor incorporates them into the service. Users must manually limit resource use to control costs.

Concept_Name: Software as a Service (SaaS)
Core_Definition: An application provided as a web site. It is a web-accessible service where the provider handles all details of hardware, operating system, and platform.
Key_Actors: End users; SaaS providers; marketing and sales teams; customer support personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The provider manages software installation, upgrades, and operations while the user interacts with the application through a web browser. Users sign up, import data, and enable features through self-service interfaces on the website. Major updates are often provided through different tracks such as rapid release or scheduled release.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers must avoid features that require client software or browser plug-ins. The service must be designed for mobile access and global availability. Users should be able to export or retrieve their data to avoid vendor lock-in. Privacy policies must be a superset of all customers' privacy policies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Frequent upgrades may occur without warning or opportunity for training. Data privacy and application hosting policies may conflict with those of specific customers. Heightened security or segmentation may be required for certain clients.

Concept_Name: Server
Core_Definition: Software that provides a function or API. It is explicitly defined as not being a piece of hardware.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Servers are software components that are combined to create user-visible systems or products called services. Multiple servers can be run on a single machine to reduce stranded capacity by sharing resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: A server is distinct from a machine, which is the physical or virtual hardware. When multiple servers share a machine, an overloaded server can consume excessive resources and negatively impact the performance of other servers on that same machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Running multiple servers on one machine requires that any operating system patches or upgrades be approved by all services using that specific machine.

Concept_Name: Service
Core_Definition: A user-visible system or product composed of many servers.
Key_Actors: End users; service providers; software servers.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple individual servers, which are software components providing functions or APIs, are integrated to function as a single product or system that is accessible and visible to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: A service must be user-visible; it is defined as being composed of many servers rather than being a single piece of hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services may be segmented into geographic partitions or reliability zones to ensure that if one zone fails due to maintenance or natural disasters, the service remains available via failover to another zone.

Concept_Name: Machine
Core_Definition: A virtual or physical computing unit used to run operating systems and applications.
Key_Actors: Physical hardware subsystems (CPU, memory, disk, network); operating systems; virtual machine monitors (VMM); virtualization-enabled CPUs.
Operational_Workflow: In a physical context, the operating system coordinates processes sharing hardware resources; in a virtual context, a physical machine is partitioned by a VMM to run multiple separate operating systems that act as if they are on independent hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: Physical machines provide predictable performance because resources are actual hardware; virtual machines provide isolation at the OS level and are programmable through APIs for rapid creation and destruction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Ephemeral machines are short-lived virtual machines created for a specific task and deleted upon completion; virtual machines can experience steal time when the physical CPU is busy handling other virtual machines on an oversubscribed host.

Concept_Name: Oversubscribed
Core_Definition: A state where a system provides capacity X but is used in a context where Y capacity is needed, such that X is less than Y.
Key_Actors: Resource providers; virtual machines; physical host machines.
Operational_Workflow: This occurs when the total resources requested by or allocated to multiple users or virtual machines exceed the actual physical capacity of the underlying system, leading to shared or reduced performance during periods of high demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: This term is used to describe a potential or actual need; it is characterized by the mathematical relationship where provided capacity is less than required capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a virtual machine detects high steal time due to an oversubscribed host, an operator might delete and re-create the virtual machine in hopes of it being placed on a less oversubscribed physical machine.

Concept_Name: Undersubscribed
Core_Definition: A state where the provided capacity of a system is greater than the capacity required by its users or applications.
Key_Actors: Computing systems; resource management entities.
Operational_Workflow: The system maintains a surplus of resources where the available capacity exceeds the current load or demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is defined specifically as the opposite of oversubscribed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Partitions (reliability zones)
Core_Definition: Geographic segments of an IaaS service designed to provide regional uptime guarantees and isolation from disasters.
Key_Actors: Service providers; customers; load balancing services.
Operational_Workflow: The service provider segments its infrastructure into multiple zones that are built and managed to have limited dependencies on each other. Customers locate their service in a primary zone and establish a failover plan in a secondary zone. In a more sophisticated setup; the service runs in all zones simultaneously with load balancing shifting traffic away from any zone that is down. User data is stored in one zone with a backup copy maintained in a different zone to ensure accessibility during an outage.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planned downtime must not occur in multiple zones at the same time; each zone must be far enough apart that natural disasters are unlikely to strike more than one zone at a time; zones must be built and managed to have limited dependencies on each other.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Users served from a backup zone during an outage may experience slower service; unavoidable circumstances like natural disasters can cause downtime despite reliability efforts.

Concept_Name: Relational (SQL) databases
Core_Definition: A type of managed storage service offered by IaaS and PaaS platforms for handling structured data.
Key_Actors: IaaS providers; PaaS providers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Providers offer these as high-level managed services that are available to users of both IaaS and PaaS platforms. Developers integrate these databases into their application frameworks to manage data through a vendor-provided stack or on provisioned machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Databases with particularly demanding high I/O requirements may be run on physical machines instead of virtual machines to ensure performance goals are met.

Concept_Name: Non-relational (NoSQL or key/value) databases
Core_Definition: A variety of storage options provided by cloud platforms that offer alternatives to traditional relational data models.
Key_Actors: IaaS providers; PaaS providers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: These databases are provided as managed storage services within the cloud infrastructure or framework. Developers select and utilize these options based on the specific needs of their applications and the high-level services offered by the vendor.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: High-speed storage options
Core_Definition: Performance-focused storage services offered by cloud providers to support data-intensive applications.
Key_Actors: Service providers; customers; development teams; business teams.
Operational_Workflow: Providers include high-speed storage as part of an expanded suite of IaaS and PaaS offerings. Customers benchmark local and remote storage performance at different times of the day to compare providers. Development and business teams evaluate the costs of storage against network traffic and compute time to architect the application effectively.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance benchmarks must be conducted for both local and remote storage; benchmarks should be repeated at various times of the day to detect peak-time performance issues; designers must account for the fact that remote storage speeds vary wildly between different providers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High packet loss at daily peak times can negatively impact performance for some providers; significant differences in remote storage speed between providers may necessitate different design decisions when switching platforms.

Concept_Name: Cold storage
Core_Definition: Bulk data storage that is inexpensive but has latency on the order of hours or days.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers; customers with bulk data storage needs.
Operational_Workflow: A customer utilizes the IaaS provider storage option to hold large amounts of data; when the customer attempts to access or retrieve this data, they experience a wait time or latency that lasts for several hours or days.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The high latency of hours or days is a notable caveat for this storage type compared to high-speed storage options.

Concept_Name: Virtual private network (VPN)–accessible private networks
Core_Definition: An advanced networking option provided by Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers to create private network environments.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers; network operators.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load balancing services
Core_Definition: Services that distribute traffic across multiple machines or locations to ensure availability and manage capacity.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers; Platform as a Service (PaaS) frameworks; local and global load balancing systems; end users.
Operational_Workflow: IaaS providers offer local and global load balancing options to customers; in a PaaS environment, the framework automatically provides load balancing by allocating more machines as users become more active; for high-reliability plans, load balancing shifts traffic away from any geographic zone that is currently down.
Rules_and_Constraints: A sophisticated reliability plan should have the service run in each geographic zone with load balancing between all locations; load balancing should automatically shift traffic away from any zone that is down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A web load balancer may be a candidate for physical hardware rather than virtualization to take advantage of high-bandwidth network interface cards or predictable performance from dedicated connections.

Concept_Name: Elastic scaling services
Core_Definition: Services that automatically allocate and configure additional machines or resources on demand as capacity is needed.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers; Platform as a Service (PaaS) frameworks; application developers; operators.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors user activity and demand; as load increases, the service automatically allocates and configures more machines; in a PaaS environment, the platform handles these additional resources without any input required from the user; users can limit resource use to control the resulting costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: PaaS offerings handle additional load without input from the user; the user is generally not aware of the specific resources dedicated to the application.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Jail (UNIX chroot restricted environment)
Core_Definition: A secure restricted environment that aims to prevent processes from breaking out of a service framework or accessing files outside of a specific subdirectory.
Key_Actors: Processes; PaaS framework; host machine; system administrators; FreeBSD containers.
Operational_Workflow: Processes are run within the restricted environment where the file system consists of a subdirectory on the host machine; the processes see this subdirectory as their root directory and cannot access files outside of it; commands like ps run within the environment display only processes running in that specific container; the host machine maintains global visibility and acts as the administrator for all containers.
Rules_and_Constraints: A jail aims to prevent processes from breaking out of the service framework; users are often restricted to the tools and libraries provided by the vendor; compiled languages may be restricted within the framework to maintain security.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Users may be unable to install popular libraries if they include portions written in compiled languages not supported by the framework; the host machine can peer into the jail while the jail cannot see outside processes.

Concept_Name: Machine Learning service
Core_Definition: A high-level esoteric service offered by some Platform as a Service providers to help developers build specific application functions.
Key_Actors: PaaS providers like Google; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The service is provided as part of a vendor-managed framework; developers utilize the provided machine learning tools to build application-level features such as recommendation engines.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a high-level managed service that is announced periodically by providers as part of their platform offerings.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Vendor lock-in
Core_Definition: The practice of making it difficult or impossible for customers to export their data or migrate to a different service provider.
Key_Actors: SaaS providers; customers; competitors.
Operational_Workflow: A provider creates a service where data export or account closure is not easily accessible via self-service; the customer becomes dependent on the product because they cannot easily migrate out at the end of a contract or if they are dissatisfied.
Rules_and_Constraints: This practice is considered a red flag that a product is not trustworthy; it is considered unethical to lock people into a product; providers should demonstrate confidence by making it easy for users to leave and back up their data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Customers often express concern about their ability to migrate if they are dissatisfied with the service level.

Concept_Name: Rapid release track
Core_Definition: An upgrade path for Software as a Service customers that provides new features and updates immediately without delay.
Key_Actors: SaaS providers; customers desiring immediate features.
Operational_Workflow: The service provider releases upgrades frequently; customers on this track receive these updates as soon as they are available rather than waiting for a published schedule; this track exists alongside a scheduled release track that typically delays features by two to three weeks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Upgrades on this track are often performed without warning; users should still have a mechanism to access major releases for training and user acceptance testing purposes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Frequent upgrades without warning can provide little opportunity for organizations to perform necessary training for their users.

Concept_Name: Scheduled release track
Core_Definition: A service delivery option for Software as a Service customers where new features and updates are provided on a published schedule rather than immediately upon development.
Key_Actors: Software as a Service providers; customers; end users.
Operational_Workflow: The provider maintains two separate tracks for updates; the scheduled release track receives major new releases on a published schedule which is typically two to three weeks after they appear on the rapid release track; the provider offers a mechanism for users to select the specific day they will be moved to the major releases.
Rules_and_Constraints: This track is intended for customers who require time for planning, training, and user acceptance testing; updates should follow a published schedule to avoid the disruption of frequent upgrades that occur without warning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Physical machine
Core_Definition: A traditional computer consisting of hardware subsystems including one or more CPUs, memory, disk, and network, all coordinated by an operating system.
Key_Actors: Operating system; running processes; system administration team; service providers.
Operational_Workflow: The operating system acts as a traffic cop to coordinate all processes that want to share hardware resources; human labor is required to rack, cable, and configure the machines; resources are allocated to processes as actual hardware resources; in a bare metal cloud, physical machines are managed as a pool via an API for allocation, OS reinstallation, and reboots.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance is relatively predictable because processes use actual hardware; physical machines are preferred for services with demanding disk or network I/O requirements such as databases or web load balancers; they may be used in a strategy to maximize hardware output by squeezing every bit of efficiency out of the computer and avoiding virtualization loss.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Physical machines often have stranded capacity because they are faster and more powerful than what some applications require; downtime of the machine for repairs or maintenance affects all services or containers running on it.

Concept_Name: Process (program running on the system)
Core_Definition: An individual program running on a computing system that utilizes hardware resources as coordinated by the operating system.
Key_Actors: Operating system; other processes; containers; host machine; administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The process shares hardware resources like CPU and memory with other programs; in a container environment, processes are grouped together and isolated from other groups; processes in a container are restricted to a specific subdirectory as their root directory; a process can generally only interact with or kill other processes within its own container unless it is running on the host machine with global visibility.
Rules_and_Constraints: In a container, resource limits for memory and disk bandwidth are enforced on the group of processes as a whole; containers allow each process to have its own copy of libraries to avoid dependency conflicts; processes in a container run on the same operating system or kernel as the host.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a process becomes overloaded or consumes excessive CPU, disk space, or memory, it can negatively affect the performance of other processes sharing the same machine; dependency hell occurs when different processes on the same machine require conflicting versions of the same library.

Concept_Name: Virtual machine
Core_Definition: A partitioned segment of a physical machine that runs its own separate operating system and is isolated from other virtual machines on the same hardware.
Key_Actors: Physical machine; operating system; virtual machine monitor (VMM); CPU; service providers; system administration team.
Operational_Workflow: A physical machine is partitioned using a VMM and specialized CPU features; the VMM manages access to I/O devices through chip-level emulation or device-level paravirtualization; virtual machines are created and managed via an API; a VMM can put a virtual machine to sleep, copy its state to a different physical machine, and wake it up to continue operations elsewhere.
Rules_and_Constraints: Virtual machines are allocated fixed amounts of disk space, memory, and CPU; they provide isolation at the operating system level; they should be organized into reliability zones to provide regional uptime guarantees and facilitate failover.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Resource contention occurs when multiple virtual machines compete for shared resources like disk I/O or network bandwidth; steal time occurs when a virtual machine is denied CPU time because it was allocated to other virtual machines; virtual machines are heavy-weight and run a full complement of background daemons and a complete operating system, which adds to the operational burden.

Concept_Name: Stranded capacity
Core_Definition: Excess computing power on a physical machine that is unusable in its current form because it is not required by the applications or virtual machines running on it. It represents the gap between a machine's total resources and the fraction actually utilized.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; applications; servers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A high-powered physical machine is deployed to host services; specific applications or virtual machines are assigned to the machine but do not use its full CPU, memory, or disk resources; these unused resources remain tied to the physical host and cannot be accessed by other systems unless the machine is partitioned or shared.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sharing a large physical machine's power among many smaller virtual machines helps reduce stranded capacity by permitting the creation of virtual machines that are the right size for their requirements; running multiple servers on the same machine can also mitigate the issue.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Virtual disks are often difficult to resize, leading operators to create them larger than needed, which results in stranded resources that cannot be used by other virtual machines.

Concept_Name: Ephemeral machine (short-lived machine)
Core_Definition: A virtual machine that is created for a specific task and deleted immediately upon the completion of that task. These machines are characterized by their very short lifespan and rapid deployment.
Key_Actors: Virtualization systems; APIs; parallel compute jobs; data processing projects; advertising campaigns.
Operational_Workflow: A user or automated system requests a virtual machine via an API; the virtualization system spins up the machine in less than a minute; the machine performs a specific task such as a parallel compute job or handling a burst of web traffic; the machine is destroyed and its resources are returned to the pool once the job is finished.
Rules_and_Constraints: These machines must be fast to create and destroy with very little lead time; virtualization systems must be programmable to orchestrate the creation and destruction of hundreds of machines at a large scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Ephemeral computing is often used for handling unexpected bursts of website traffic or short-term advertising campaigns where infrastructure is needed only briefly.

Concept_Name: Virtual machine monitor (VMM)
Core_Definition: A layer of virtualization control software that manages virtual machines and coordinates with CPU features to partition hardware resources. It handles the creation, state management, and emulation of hardware for guest systems.
Key_Actors: Physical CPUs; guest operating systems; virtual machines; I/O devices like disks and network cards.
Operational_Workflow: The VMM utilizes special features in modern CPUs to partition memory and CPU time for individual virtual machines; it handles I/O requests by emulating hardware devices or capturing high-level calls; it can put a machine to sleep, copy its memory and state to a different physical host, and resume operations to avoid downtime during maintenance.
Rules_and_Constraints: The VMM must load-share virtual cores across the available physical cores of the host machine; it must ensure that virtual machines cannot access the resources of other machines running on the same hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In hardware virtual machines, the VMM must intervene every time the guest OS attempts to access hardware controllers, which can lead to slower performance.

Concept_Name: Hardware virtual machine (HVM)
Core_Definition: A virtualization approach that performs I/O emulation at the chip level to allow virtual machines to run unmodified operating systems. The guest OS operates as if it were interacting with actual physical hardware controllers.
Key_Actors: Guest operating systems; SATA hard drive controllers; physical CPUs; Virtual Machine Monitor (VMM).
Operational_Workflow: The unmodified guest operating system attempts to access a hardware controller like a SATA drive; the CPU's virtualization feature detects this access and stops the virtual machine; control is passed to the VMM which performs the request by emulating the behavior of a real controller; the VMM places the result in the appropriate location and allows the virtual machine to resume.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method must provide a convincing emulation of hardware so that the guest operating system does not require any special modifications or drivers to function.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Chip-level emulation is described as being rather slow because the CPU must stop the virtual machine and hand control to the VMM for every hardware access attempt.

Concept_Name: Paravirtualization (PV)
Core_Definition: A method of performing I/O emulation at the device level that requires modifications to the operating system to send requests to the virtual machine monitor.
Key_Actors: Modified operating system; virtual machine monitor; device drivers for hard disks, video displays, and keyboards.
Operational_Workflow: The modified operating system initiates I/O calls as requests to the virtual machine monitor; the virtual machine monitor handles the I/O operation and returns the result to the operating system; device drivers that appear as standard hardware facilitate this communication by talking directly to the monitor.
Rules_and_Constraints: The operating system must be modified to perform I/O through requests to the virtual machine monitor; device drivers used must be designed to look like standard hard disks or video displays while communicating with the monitor.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Paravirtualization is described as more efficient than hardware virtual machines because it captures I/O requests at a higher level of abstraction.

Concept_Name: Steal time
Core_Definition: The amount of CPU time that a virtual machine is missing because it was allocated to other virtual machines sharing the same physical machine.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; physical machine; virtual machine monitor; IaaS providers; Linux and the Xen hypervisor.
Operational_Workflow: A physical machine with multiple cores has its resources shared among various virtual machines; the virtual machine monitor load-shares virtual cores across the physical cores; when multiple virtual machines require more total cores than are physically available, each receives a smaller fraction of the CPU and runs more slowly; the virtual machine identifies this loss as steal time.
Rules_and_Constraints: IaaS providers generally cannot provide guarantees regarding how much steal time will exist; providers typically do not offer mechanisms to control the amount of steal time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Organizations like Netflix handle high steal time by deleting the affected virtual machine and re-creating it in hopes that the new instance will be placed on a less oversubscribed physical machine.

Concept_Name: Resource contention
Core_Definition: A situation involving a shortage of shared resources such as disk I/O bandwidth or network capacity among multiple machines.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; hard drives; host machines; Solaris containers; Linux containers.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple virtual machines or containers share physical resources in an unbounded manner; one virtual machine may generate excessive network traffic or disk I/O; other virtual machines on the same host suffer performance degradation because the physical hardware can perform only a limited amount of I/O per second.
Rules_and_Constraints: A hard drive can perform only a specific amount of disk I/O per second based on the bandwidth between the computer and the disk.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Solaris containers can regulate network bandwidth to control contention; Linux containers can assign different amounts of disk cache to each container to prevent buffer thrashing from affecting others.

Concept_Name: Container
Core_Definition: A group of processes running on an operating system that are isolated from other groups but share the same kernel and resources at a fine-grained level.
Key_Actors: Group of processes; operating system kernel; host machine; PaaS providers; system administrators; standardized systems like Docker.
Operational_Workflow: Processes are grouped and isolated with a specific process name space and network configuration; the system provides a subdirectory on the host machine to serve as the root directory for the processes; resources such as memory and disk bandwidth are enforced and regulated on the group as a whole; standardized containers are created to include software and all its dependencies for easy distribution and execution across different systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Processes in a container must all run on the same operating system or kernel; a container can only interact with processes within its own group; containers require their own copy of packages and libraries to avoid dependency conflicts; background daemons like SSH do not run inside the container as they are available on the host.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Planned or unplanned downtime of the host machine affects all containers running on it; containers are less granular for resource allocation than virtual machines but avoid the problem of stranded resources because they consume only what they need.

Concept_Name: Process name space
Core_Definition: A feature of process containers that provides an isolated environment where a group of processes can only see and interact with other processes within that same group. It is part of the mechanism that keeps containers separate from each other on a single operating system.
Key_Actors: Containers; operating system kernel; groups of processes; host machine; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The operating system groups processes into a container and assigns them a private name space. When a command like ps is run inside the container, it only displays processes belonging to that specific container because the environment has no visibility to other processes. The host machine maintains global visibility and can see all processes across all containers to serve as an administrator.
Rules_and_Constraints: A process within a container can only kill or interact with other processes in that same container; all processes in the name space must run on the same operating system or kernel.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Processes running on the host machine outside of any container are not restricted by the name space and can interact with or kill any process on the system, including those inside containers.

Concept_Name: Dependency hell
Core_Definition: A common situation where different software programs require incompatible versions of the same shared library or package, preventing them from operating correctly on the same system. This conflict occurs when the environment cannot support multiple versions of a dependency simultaneously.
Key_Actors: Software programs; shared libraries; software packages; containers.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple programs are installed on a single host and attempt to utilize the system's shared libraries. When two programs have conflicting version requirements for the same library, the installation or execution of one or both programs fails. To resolve this, each program is placed into a separate container with its own private copy of the required libraries, allowing the conflicting versions to coexist on the same machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each container must have its own copy of packages and supporting files to ensure that two containers running on the same machine do not have version conflicts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Standardized shipping containers
Core_Definition: A delivery mechanism that uses uniform shapes and sizes to transport groups of items efficiently. In a computing context, this refers to technology like Docker that packages software and its entire environment into a single unit that can run on any compatible system.
Key_Actors: Malcom McLean; SeaLand; freight industry; customs officials; software developers; Docker.
Operational_Workflow: Individual items or software components are loaded into a container of a standard size and shape at a factory or developer environment. The items are then transported as a single group, which allows for much faster loading and unloading compared to handling individual pieces. Because the container is a self-contained unit, it simplifies delivery and distribution by eliminating the need to manage individual system requirements at each stop.
Rules_and_Constraints: Containers must be of a standard shape and size to improve processing speed; for software, the container must include the software and everything needed for it to run.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Customs officials can approve and seal a physical container at one point, which eliminates the need for further checks at remaining hops as long as the seal is not broken.

Concept_Name: Intermodal shipping
Core_Definition: A logistics concept where a standardized container remains a single unit as it is transferred between different modes of transportation. This approach allows goods to move seamlessly from one system to another without being unpacked or reorganized.
Key_Actors: Factories; trucks; trains; ships; standardized containers.
Operational_Workflow: A standardized container is loaded at a factory and then moved onto a truck. From the truck, the entire unit is transferred to a train and eventually onto a ship, remaining as a single unit throughout the entire journey. This process relies on all different transportation sectors adopting the same container standard to ensure compatibility.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process requires various modes of transportation to adopt the same standardized shipping container.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Docker
Core_Definition: A system that defines a standardized container for software, allowing a container to be created once and run on many systems.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Software is distributed as a standardized container that includes the application and everything needed for it to run; this container is deployed to any system that supports the standard instead of shipping a software package with a separate list of dependent packages and requirements.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system requires a standardized container and a supporting host system; containers must be self-contained to eliminate dependencies and version conflicts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Solaris Zones
Core_Definition: A type of process container used on the Solaris operating system that allows for the regulation of network bandwidth and the allocation of network interfaces.
Key_Actors: Solaris operating system; containers; network interfaces.
Operational_Workflow: The operating system allocates specific network interfaces to the zones and regulates network bandwidth to manage and control resource contention between different containers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Solaris Zones can be allocated network interfaces and have their network bandwidth regulated to control bandwidth resource contention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: FreeBSD Jail
Core_Definition: A restricted environment that isolates groups of processes, providing each with its own process name space, network configuration, and file system root.
Key_Actors: Processes; host machine; shell command ps; host administrator.
Operational_Workflow: Processes run within a secure restricted environment where they have no visibility to processes outside their container; the ps command displays only the processes running in that specific jail; the administrator on the host machine maintains global visibility and can see all processes including those inside each container.
Rules_and_Constraints: The jail aims to prevent processes from breaking out of the service framework; the file system is restricted to a subdirectory on the host machine; processes in a jail can only interact with or kill other processes within the same jail.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Public cloud
Core_Definition: A computing environment where a third party owns the infrastructure and provides services to multiple customers through shared or segmented resources.
Key_Actors: Third-party vendors; customers; end users; developers; operators; law enforcement officials; external auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Vendors manage the hardware, power, cooling, and datacenter operations while customers use the platform to run applications or frameworks; providers charge based on resource consumption such as CPU, storage, and bandwidth; service providers segment their infrastructure into geographic reliability zones to provide uptime guarantees and manage latency.
Rules_and_Constraints: Companies must evaluate compliance, privacy, cost, and control before moving data to a provider; vendors must maintain transparency and submit to external audits to build trust; providers should ensure planned downtime does not occur in multiple reliability zones simultaneously; geographic zones should be far enough apart to prevent a single natural disaster from affecting more than one zone.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Companies may fail compliance audits if data leaves certain jurisdictions; law enforcement can access data via the vendor without the customer's knowledge; data may be accidentally exposed due to software bugs, employee mistakes, or improper disposal of old equipment; virtual machines may experience high steal time due to CPU contention on oversubscribed hosts; service providers may experience high packet loss during peak daily times.

Concept_Name: Private cloud
Core_Definition: A computing infrastructure run by a company on its own premises for a dedicated internal project or as an internal service provider.
Key_Actors: The company; internal projects; internal departments; internal service provider; large company achieving economies of scale.
Operational_Workflow: A company builds and manages its own infrastructure; the infrastructure is made available to internal projects and departments; the organization maintains control over hardware selection and network topology.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decisions to use it are based on compliance; privacy; cost; and control; it is often required by highly regulated industries.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hybrids can be created such as private clouds run in rented datacenter space; companies may contract for an on-premises but externally run service to mitigate privacy issues.

Concept_Name: Hybrid cloud
Core_Definition: A computing infrastructure where private clouds are run in rented datacenter space or where in-house baseline capacity is supplemented by public cloud services.
Key_Actors: The company; public cloud provider; rented datacenter or colocation provider.
Operational_Workflow: The company establishes baseline capacity requirements and builds those in-house; it uses the public cloud to burst beyond normal capacity when needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is often more cost-effective than either building in-house capacity or exclusively using a public provider.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Compliance
Core_Definition: Adherence to regulations governed by a company's business; size; locations; and status which is verified through audits.
Key_Actors: Companies; auditors; regulatory bodies; public cloud providers.
Operational_Workflow: A company identifies applicable regulations based on its business and location; it undergoes audits to verify adherence; failing an audit requires passing a subsequent audit to conduct business.
Rules_and_Constraints: Compliance with all applicable regulations can be audited; using a public cloud for certain data or services may cause a company to fail a compliance audit unless sufficient controls are in place.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failing an audit can result in the company being unable to conduct business until a subsequent audit is passed.

Concept_Name: EU Data Protection Directive
Core_Definition: A regulation dictating that certain data about EU citizens may not leave the European Union.
Key_Actors: EU citizens; companies; public cloud providers; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: Companies ensure that data about EU citizens remains within the EU boundaries; public cloud providers must implement controls to ensure data does not leave the EU even during failover.
Rules_and_Constraints: Moving data into a public cloud that lacks sufficient controls to keep data within the EU results in a failed compliance audit.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failover scenarios must be accounted for to ensure data does not leave the EU during a service disruption.

Concept_Name: Privacy
Core_Definition: The risk and management of data and code when it resides on equipment and in facilities owned by a third-party provider. It involves protecting information from unauthorized access by the provider's employees or outside entities.
Key_Actors: Third-party service providers; provider employees; customers; law enforcement officials.
Operational_Workflow: Service providers define data protection terms within customer contracts; vendors establish trust by maintaining transparency regarding their internal policies; providers submit to external audits to verify they are following their own rules; customers may seek heightened security or segmentation from other users to protect sensitive information.
Rules_and_Constraints: A provider's privacy policy must be a superset of all its customers' privacy policies; companies must comply with regional regulations such as the EU Data Protection Directive; providers must maintain transparency to retain customer trust.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Curious or malicious employees may use diagnostic tools to view data without a customer's knowledge; data may be leaked if a provider disposes of old equipment without properly erasing storage systems.

Concept_Name: External audits
Core_Definition: A process where independent third-party entities verify that a service provider is complying with its own stated policies and applicable business regulations.
Key_Actors: Service providers; external auditors; regulatory bodies; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The provider establishes specific rules and policies for data management and security; the provider submits to examinations by outside auditors; the auditors verify adherence to the rules and transparency of policies; the results are used to demonstrate trustworthiness to existing and potential customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Companies are governed by regulations based on business size and location; failing a compliance audit can result in significant consequences such as being unable to conduct business until a subsequent audit is passed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A company might fail a compliance audit if it moves data to a public cloud that lacks sufficient controls to meet legal mandates, such as ensuring data does not leave a specific geographic region even during failover scenarios.

Concept_Name: Law enforcement requests
Core_Definition: Official demands by government authorities to access stored data, typically through legal mechanisms such as warrants.
Key_Actors: Law enforcement officials; third-party service providers; private cloud owners.
Operational_Workflow: Officials obtain a legal warrant for specific data; they present the request to a third-party service provider rather than the data owner; the provider grants access to the data; the provider may fulfill the request without making the customer aware that their information was accessed.
Rules_and_Constraints: In a private cloud environment, the standard avenue for law enforcement to access data involves making the owner aware of the request.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Clandestine techniques can be used by authorities to hide data access even when the infrastructure is located at the customer's own site.

Concept_Name: Accidental exposure of your data
Core_Definition: The unintended release of private information to unauthorized parties resulting from technical failures, human mistakes, or improper hardware decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Service provider employees; software systems; other customers; competitors; the general public.
Operational_Workflow: A software bug or an error by a provider employee occurs; data becomes accessible to other customers of the service or the entire world; in a public cloud, the exposure may become public news and reach competitors.
Rules_and_Constraints: In a private cloud setting, accidental exposure is often restricted to members of the same company, which is generally considered an acceptable and containable risk.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Data can be leaked by a service provider that disposes of old equipment without properly erasing storage systems; software bugs may specifically cause data to be exposed to a customer's competitors in a shared public environment.

Concept_Name: Cost
Core_Definition: The financial expenditure associated with resource consumption such as compute time, storage, and network traffic on a service platform.
Key_Actors: Service providers; development teams; business teams; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: Providers monitor and charge for specific metrics like CPU usage, bandwidth, and storage; engineering teams analyze these charges to make design choices such as whether to cache data locally or retrieve it over the network; development and business teams collaborate to evaluate the resulting costs and tradeoffs of these choices; periodic benchmarks are conducted to ensure the performance received justifies the monthly bill.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software and operational choices have direct financial consequences; network charges can be reduced by caching but may increase local storage costs; users must proactively manage resource use to control expenses; performance benchmarks should be repeated at different times of day to account for potential service degradation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Engineering details that are ignored can lead to startlingly large bills at the end of the month; high packet loss during peak times may decrease the cost-efficiency of the provider; opportunity cost may occur if a company moves too slowly to save money and misses a market window.

Concept_Name: Vertical integration
Core_Definition: A model where a company handles all levels of service delivery itself to avoid the added costs of profit margins and middle men.
Key_Actors: Internal company departments; third-party service providers; middle men.
Operational_Workflow: A company chooses to build and operate its own infrastructure rather than using a third party; the organization manages every layer of the service delivery stack internally; the uplift in cost typically paid as profit to external providers is eliminated.
Rules_and_Constraints: The strategy is based on the principle that doing it yourself saves money under specific conditions; there is a break-even point where this model becomes more economical than renting; it is generally more appropriate for long-term projects where the infrastructure will eventually pay for itself.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Building infrastructure can be a waste of time if the product is experimental and its success is uncertain; small companies may find it difficult to achieve the same level of professional operation as external providers without a large dedicated staff.

Concept_Name: Total cost of ownership (TCO)
Core_Definition: A calculation used to compare the comprehensive long-term costs of building a private infrastructure versus renting services from a public provider.
Key_Actors: Business planners; engineering talent; technical experts; datacenter designers.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify all necessary expenses including datacenter cooling, electrical service, real-estate management, and technical expertise; these costs are aggregated to determine the total price of building and running a private cloud; this figure is compared against public cloud fees to determine the most cost-effective solution.
Rules_and_Constraints: TCO must account for the high investment required for physical engineering and specialized knowledge; it is used as part of a rent versus own decision-making process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Return on investment (ROI)
Core_Definition: A financial metric used to determine which platform selection strategy provides the best economic value over the life of a project.
Key_Actors: Business teams; project stakeholders; company leadership.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations develop a business plan that compares the total project costs between in-house infrastructure and public cloud options; they evaluate the potential gains and duration of the project; the strategy that offers the highest return relative to its investment cost is selected.
Rules_and_Constraints: ROI calculations should factor in the project timeline; multiyear projects often favor in-house infrastructure whereas short-term projects favor public cloud providers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Control
Core_Definition: Control refers to the degree of influence and authority a user has over the details and configuration of the raw machine and infrastructure. It is inversely related to the level of service abstraction.
Key_Actors: Operators; developers; service providers; business teams.
Operational_Workflow: Users gain more control by moving closer to the raw machine in lower levels of abstraction like IaaS where they manage the operating system and coordinate all infrastructure pieces; in higher levels like PaaS and SaaS the vendor manages more aspects of the stack and the user focuses on the application; a private cloud provides the most control by allowing for specific hardware selection and network topology setup.
Rules_and_Constraints: The closer a user is to the raw machine the more control they have; users with total control of the operating system are responsible for its management; in a public cloud users must choose from a finite set of features provided by the vendor.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In PaaS environments users are restricted to the vendor framework and may be unable to add specific binaries or libraries; letting a vendor handle hardware selection results in losing the ability to specify low-level requirements like CPU types or storage products.

Concept_Name: Hosting Provider Contract Questions
Core_Definition: A set of essential inquiries used to establish the baseline obligations and service expectations between a customer and a vendor.
Key_Actors: Potential providers; customers; vendors.
Operational_Workflow: The customer evaluates the provider by asking about data portability during contract exit; the ability to purchase physical machines; procedures during vendor bankruptcy; bandwidth provision and redundancy; backup frequency and testing; and SLA refund policies.
Rules_and_Constraints: Questions should determine if internet bandwidth is provided or requires separate arrangement; customers must know if backups are for their use or only for vendor emergencies; the contract must define what happens to data and servers if the vendor goes bankrupt.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Servers and data might be tied up in bankruptcy proceedings; backups may not be accessible by customer request if they are intended solely for vendor use in emergencies.

Concept_Name: Service level agreement (SLA)
Core_Definition: A contractual guarantee provided by a vendor regarding specific performance metrics and service availability.
Key_Actors: Service providers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The provider sets specific numbers for metrics like capacity and bandwidth; IaaS providers use reliability zones to segment services geographically and provide regional uptime guarantees; customers monitor these metrics to ensure the vendor is meeting the established baseline.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service providers should ensure planned downtime does not occur in multiple reliability zones at the same time; customers should ask if refunds are given in the event of an SLA violation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unavoidable circumstances such as natural disasters may cause downtime despite guarantees; if a provider violates the SLA the contract determines whether a refund is issued.

Concept_Name: Colocation
Core_Definition: A service where a datacenter owner rents facility space to tenants who house their own computing equipment.
Key_Actors: Datacenter owners; tenants; Internet Service Providers (ISPs).
Operational_Workflow: Tenants rent space in a facility to avoid the investment and time required to build a private datacenter; the owner manages cooling, power, and physical security while the tenant installs their own hardware; ISPs often extend networks into these spaces so tenants can connect directly.
Rules_and_Constraints: Colocation is ideal for companies needing small or medium amounts of space or those needing single racks in multiple geographic locations; providers may offer internet access as a service by blending connections from multiple ISPs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Default to Virtual
Core_Definition: This strategy involves using containers or virtual machines as the standard platform for all services, reserving physical machines only for instances where performance requirements cannot be met otherwise. It prioritizes the flexibility and efficiency of virtualization over traditional hardware deployments.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; containers; physical machines; high-demand databases; web load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The organization selects virtual machines or containers as the primary deployment option for all new services. Technical teams monitor the service performance to ensure goals are being met. If a service like a database or load balancer demonstrates high disk or network I/O requirements that virtualization cannot satisfy, the team then migrates or deploys that specific service onto physical hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: Use virtualized or containerized environments as the default choice; only use physical machines when performance goals are unachievable through virtualization; prioritize physical hardware for tasks with high I/O demands.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Particularly demanding databases with high I/O requirements may need to remain on physical machines; web load balancers are candidates for physical hardware to take advantage of high-bandwidth network interface cards or to ensure predictable performance from dedicated connections.

Concept_Name: Make a Cost-Based Decision
Core_Definition: This strategy requires comparing the total cost of ownership for public cloud services versus private infrastructure through a business plan. The platform that provides the lower calculated cost for the specific duration of the project is chosen.
Key_Actors: Public cloud providers; private infrastructure; business planning teams; project stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: A business plan is created to detail the total costs of a project if it is performed in-house on private infrastructure compared to using a public cloud provider. The team evaluates these costs over the expected life of the project. The organization then selects the platform option with the lower total cost.
Rules_and_Constraints: Create a formal business plan comparing total costs; select the lower-cost option; factor in the project duration when making financial comparisons.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Multiyear projects may be more cost-effective when done in-house; short-term projects are typically less expensive when utilizing public cloud providers.

Concept_Name: Leverage Provider Expertise
Core_Definition: This approach utilizes the specialized skills of cloud providers to manage infrastructure, allowing a company's internal staff to focus their efforts on application development. It is especially beneficial for smaller organizations that cannot professionally manage infrastructure at their current scale.
Key_Actors: Public cloud providers; employees; developers; startups; small companies; dedicated infrastructure staff.
Operational_Workflow: A company identifies that it lacks the staff or scale to build and manage its own infrastructure professionally. The organization selects a public cloud provider to handle all infrastructure operations. Internal employees then dedicate their time to building and managing the application rather than the underlying systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Use public cloud services to free up staff for application-focused tasks; rely on public clouds for their professional management standards driven by competition; acknowledge that private clouds are difficult to run professionally at a small scale without dedicated staff.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Get Started Quickly
Core_Definition: This strategy uses public cloud services to launch a product as rapidly as possible to capture business opportunities and avoid opportunity costs. It emphasizes speed and testing product viability over long-term infrastructure planning.
Key_Actors: Public cloud providers; early testers; development teams; business owners.
Operational_Workflow: A team identifies a potential business opportunity or an experimental product idea. They contract with a public cloud provider to quickly set up a minimal version of the product. The product is then released to early testers for rapid iteration and to determine if the concept is viable before any major investment in private infrastructure is made.
Rules_and_Constraints: Prioritize speed of launch to avoid missing opportunities; use public clouds for experimental products or those with uncertain success; consider moving to private infrastructure only after the product has proven its viability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Public cloud solutions may be more expensive than building private infrastructure; building internal infrastructure is considered a waste of time if the product success is uncertain or if the product eventually fails.

Concept_Name: Opportunity cost
Core_Definition: The potential loss of a market advantage or project success that occurs when an organization moves too slowly to implement an idea.
Key_Actors: Developers; companies; early testers; public cloud providers.
Operational_Workflow: An organization identifies a new product idea or market opportunity; the organization chooses to contract with a public cloud provider to get a minimal product to early testers as quickly as possible; the organization iterates over features rapidly to determine if the product is viable; if the product is successful, the organization evaluates building private infrastructure; if the product fails, the organization moves on without having wasted time on infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Building infrastructure is considered a waste of time when a product's success is uncertain; the speed of moving to market is prioritized over the potential long-term savings of building in-house.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the product is not viable, the cost of the public cloud is justified because no time was wasted building infrastructure that is no longer needed.

Concept_Name: Ephemeral computing
Core_Definition: The practice of rapidly setting up a large, short-lived computing infrastructure for a specific task and deleting it immediately upon completion.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; virtualization control software or virtual machine monitors; APIs; public cloud providers; organizations performing large data analysis.
Operational_Workflow: An organization identifies a short-term need such as a parallel compute job, a biotechnology data mining project, or a brief advertising campaign; an API is used to orchestrate the creation of hundreds or thousands of virtual machines across many physical machines; the machines perform the required compute tasks or handle the temporary load; once the task or event is finished, the software destroys the virtual machines to stop incurring costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Virtual machines must be fast to create and destroy with minimal lead time; the system must be programmable via an API to allow software to orchestrate the lifecycle of the machines on a large scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This strategy can be used to handle unexpected bursts of website traffic.

Concept_Name: Use the Cloud for Overflow Capacity
Core_Definition: A strategy where an organization builds in-house capacity for its baseline requirements and utilizes a public cloud for demand that exceeds that baseline.
Key_Actors: In-house infrastructure; public cloud providers.
Operational_Workflow: An organization analyzes its computing needs to establish baseline capacity requirements; the organization builds the necessary infrastructure to handle this baseline in-house; when demand bursts beyond normal capacity, the organization allocates additional resources from a public cloud provider; the organization scales back to its in-house baseline once the overflow demand has passed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method is noted for being more cost-effective than building for peak capacity in-house or using a public provider for all needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Leverage Superior Infrastructure
Core_Definition: A strategy focused on gaining a competitive advantage by creating and utilizing highly customized computing infrastructure.
Key_Actors: Organizations; Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) providers.
Operational_Workflow: An organization chooses to build its own datacenters to exercise total control over costs and resource utilization; the organization selects IaaS over Platform as a Service (PaaS) to maintain the ability to customize at the operating system and software levels; the organization leverages these customizations to perform better or more efficiently than competitors.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Develop an In-House Service Provider
Core_Definition: A strategy where a large company builds its own large-scale computing infrastructure to act as an internal service provider for its various departments and projects.
Key_Actors: Large companies; in-house customers; internal projects; internal departments; engineering talent; technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: A large organization builds a substantial computing infrastructure on its own premises or within rented datacenter space; the organization manages this infrastructure as a private service provider; the resources are shared among many in-house customers, projects, and departments to achieve economies of scale similar to those of public cloud providers.
Rules_and_Constraints: The infrastructure must be built at a very large scale to be cost-effective; the platform must remain in-house to maintain privacy; the strategy is often utilized to meet the criteria of highly regulated industries; the company must possess the necessary engineering talent and technical expertise to run a datacenter and provide services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach is difficult to implement professionally at a small scale; without a dedicated staff, private clouds may not be run as professionally as public clouds; if a company cannot reach the break-even point where vertical integration becomes economical, a public provider may be more appropriate.

Concept_Name: Maximize Hardware Output
Core_Definition: A strategy of squeezing every bit of productivity and efficiency out of computers by eschewing virtualization.
Key_Actors: Infrastructures with hundreds of thousands of computers or millions of cores; services; physical machines.
Operational_Workflow: Physical machines are used instead of virtual machines and services are tightly packed on them to maximize utilization.
Rules_and_Constraints: This strategy is used because the loss of efficiency from virtualization is considered a huge expense; improving efficiency by one percent can be equivalent to gaining thousands of new computers in very large infrastructures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Bare Metal Cloud
Core_Definition: A strategy where physical machine infrastructure is managed and provisioned like a virtual machine cloud using an API.
Key_Actors: Companies; physical machine pools; departments or individuals; identically configured hardware.
Operational_Workflow: Companies purchase hundreds or thousands of machines all configured the same way and manage them as a pool; these machines are reserved by departments or individuals as needed through an API.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations opt to purchase identical hardware rather than selecting exact custom configurations for each machine; physical machines are provided via the same API used for provisioning virtual machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The allocations of physical machines may not be as fast or as dynamic as virtual machines.

Concept_Name: API for physical machine management
Core_Definition: A programmatic interface used for the automated lifecycle management of physical machines, including allocation, installation, and rebooting.
Key_Actors: The management API; physical machines; hardware pools; administrators or users.
Operational_Workflow: The API is used for allocating machines from a pool and wiping and reinstalling their operating systems; it provides functions for rebooting machines and controlling access; it manages the process of returning machines to the pool.
Rules_and_Constraints: The API should provide physical machines via the same interface used for provisioning virtual machines; planning is required to ensure the benefits of spinning up virtual machines can be applied to physical hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 4. Application Architectures
Section: Introduction
============================================================

Concept_Name: Patterns
Core_Definition: Solutions to recurring problems in a context.
Key_Actors: Application designers; Christopher Alexander; architects of services and applications.
Operational_Workflow: The design process involves identifying recurring architectural problems and applying established solutions; this begins with small building blocks like a single web server and expands to multi-machine designs for large global services; architects examine architectures behind the scenes such as message buses and service-oriented architectures to move up the application architecture layer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Patterns must solve recurring problems within a specific context; they are used as building blocks for designing applications and services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Single-Machine Web Server
Core_Definition: A design pattern where a single self-sufficient machine provides web service by running software that speaks the HTTP protocol.
Key_Actors: Single physical or virtual machine; web server software; local storage; database software; user or client.
Operational_Workflow: The machine receives an HTTP request from a client; the web server software parses the request and processes it; the server generates a result by reading static files from local storage or running programs for dynamic content; the server sends the generated HTML or file as a reply to the client; the client displays the content to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: The machine must be self-sufficient; it is typically used for small websites and applications; the database and its data must reside on the same machine as the web server if used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The system is only as reliable as a single machine and service stops if the machine crashes; capacity is limited by the single machine's CPU, memory, and I/O; maintenance and upgrades often require downtime; buffer thrashing may occur when the operating system cannot self-tune for both web and database server memory footprints; traffic growth eventually requires implementing a different architecture once hardware physical limits are reached.

Concept_Name: Static Content
Core_Definition: Files that are read from local storage and sent to the user unchanged.
Key_Actors: Local storage; web server software; end user.
Operational_Workflow: The web server receives a request for a specific file; the server accesses the file on local storage; the file is transmitted to the user exactly as it exists on the disk without modification.
Rules_and_Constraints: Content types include HTML pages, images, music, video, and downloadable software.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A static web server is a specific configuration that has no dynamic content.

Concept_Name: Dynamic Content
Core_Definition: HTML and other output generated by programs running on a web server rather than being read from a fixed file.
Key_Actors: Programs running on the web server; web server software; database (in database-driven cases); user input.
Operational_Workflow: The web server receives an HTTP request; the server executes specific programs; these programs generate HTML or other output either independently or based on input received from the user; the server sends this generated output to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Output is created at the time of the request; it may or may not require a database depending on the application needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Database-driven dynamic content is a special case where programs must consult a database on the same machine to generate the page; running both the web server and the database server on one machine can lead to non-optimal disk cache algorithms and buffer thrashing.

Concept_Name: Database-Driven Dynamic Content
Core_Definition: A special case of dynamic content where programs running on a web server consult a database for information and use that to generate the web page.
Key_Actors: Web server; programs running on the web server; database software; database data.
Operational_Workflow: Programs running on the web server consult a database for information; the programs use that information to generate HTML or other output; the resulting content is sent to the user; in this architecture, the database software and its data are located on the same machine as the web server.
Rules_and_Constraints: The database software and its data must reside on the same machine as the web server in a single-machine architecture; the system is limited by the capacity of the machine's CPU, memory, and I/O capacity; the amount of data is limited to what can fit on a single machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the machine crashes or dies, the web service stops until the machine is repaired; maintenance, software upgrades, and content changes are disruptions that may require downtime; as traffic grows, the system eventually reaches physical hardware or design limits, requiring a different architecture.

Concept_Name: buffer thrashing
Core_Definition: A problem where an operating system is unable to self-tune its disk cache effectively because it is running multiple applications with complex or conflicting memory footprints.
Key_Actors: Operating system; disk cache; web server; database server; memory.
Operational_Workflow: The operating system uses unused memory as a disk cache to improve disk I/O performance; the OS selects algorithms to decide which blocks to discard when memory is needed; the OS tunes itself based on the specific memory footprint of a running application; when a machine runs both a web server and a database server, the OS attempts to pick an algorithm for the complex footprint; the OS may pick an algorithm optimal for only one application or a non-optimal default scheme.
Rules_and_Constraints: Modern operating systems use all otherwise unused memory as a disk cache; algorithms must be used to decide which blocks to discard when memory is needed for other processes or newer disk blocks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The operating system may give up on tuning and pick a default scheme that is non-optimal for all applications involved.

Concept_Name: Linux “containers” systems
Core_Definition: Systems designed as an attempt to improve the operating system's ability to self-tune for complex memory footprints and avoid problems like buffer thrashing.
Key_Actors: Linux operating system; applications such as web servers and database servers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: LXC
Core_Definition: A specific example of a Linux containers system used to address issues where an operating system cannot optimally manage disk caching for multiple applications on the same machine.
Key_Actors: Linux operating system.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lmctfy
Core_Definition: A Linux container system used to manage the memory footprint of different applications running on the same machine.
Key_Actors: Linux operating system; web server software; database software; container management system.
Operational_Workflow: The system isolates applications like web servers and databases into containers on a single host; this isolation allows the operating system to better manage disk cache and memory allocation for each specific process; it prevents the operating system from defaulting to non-optimal block replacement algorithms caused by complex, overlapping memory footprints.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used as an attempt to improve situations where a machine is running both a web server and a database server simultaneously; it addresses the problem of buffer thrashing in modern operating systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Three-Tier Web Service
Core_Definition: An architecture pattern composed of a load balancer layer, a web server layer, and a data service layer.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; web server replicas; backend data server; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Requests enter the system through the load balancer; the load balancer selects one machine from the web server layer and relays the request; the web server processes the request and may query the backend data server for information; the web server generates a reply and sends it back to the user via the load balancer.
Rules_and_Constraints: All web servers must rely on a common backend data server; web servers must be configured as replicas capable of servicing the same URLs; the data service layer is typically an SQL database that is grown independently from the web servers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a backend web server dies, the load balancer must skip it and route traffic to remaining replicas; when local state is stored on a web server, a user may be forced to log in again if the load balancer sends their next request to a different replica; if the backend data server is slow or not optimized for shared state, it can hinder performance.

Concept_Name: load balancer
Core_Definition: A system component that receives incoming requests and forwards them to one of many backend replicas to distribute traffic load.
Key_Actors: Load balancer (Layer 3, 4, or 7); web server replicas; DNS servers; end users.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives a request acting as the frontend for many replicas; it applies a selection algorithm such as Round Robin, Least Loaded, or Latency-based to choose a backend; it forwards the request to the selected server; in most cases, it obscures the original client IP and may inject an X-Forwarded-For header to provide the original source IP to the backend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decisions can be made at the network layer, session layer, or application layer; the load balancer must be able to detect downed machines to skip them; it can be used to serve multiple different replica groups or web sites independently.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: DNS Round Robin load balancing is difficult to control and unresponsive to replica failures due to heavy DNS caching; when replicas are overloaded, specific algorithms like Latency-based balancing or Utilization Limits are used to manage bursts or pathologically slow situations; if a load balancer is not transparent, debugging can be difficult unless special headers are used.

Concept_Name: web server layer
Core_Definition: The middle tier in a multi-tier architecture consisting of multiple machines that process HTTP requests and generate content.
Key_Actors: Web server replicas; load balancer; data service layer.
Operational_Workflow: Individual machines in this layer receive relayed requests from a load balancer; the servers process the requests by reading static content from local storage, running programs for dynamic content, or consulting the data service layer; the generated reply is sent back through the load balancer to the client.
Rules_and_Constraints: All machines in the layer must be configured to service the same URLs as replicas; the layer can be expanded by adding more replicas until the total required capacity is achieved; machines in this layer generally focus on application logic while relying on other layers for traffic distribution and data storage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the layer is not supported by a shared state mechanism, users may lose session information when moving between different servers in the layer; local disk cache performance can be degraded if the machines in this layer are also forced to run database services without containerization.

Concept_Name: data service layer
Core_Definition: The data service layer is the final tier in a web service architecture where web servers or application servers access shared data sources or common backend data servers.
Key_Actors: load balancer; web servers; application servers; backend data servers; SQL database.
Operational_Workflow: A request enters the system through a load balancer and is forwarded to a web server in the middle layer; the web server processes the request and queries the data service layer to retrieve necessary information; the data service layer processes the query and returns the results to the web server; the web server uses this data to generate a reply and sends it back via the load balancer.
Rules_and_Constraints: In a three-tier architecture, all web servers rely on a common backend data server; the layer can be moved to its own platform to grow independently of other tiers; multiple data stores can be used, with some dedicated to specific replica groups and others shared across the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the data service layer is on the same machine as the web server, it is limited by the physical capacity of that single machine; databases within this layer may respond slowly if used for shared state storage because they are not optimized for that specific operation.

Concept_Name: replicas
Core_Definition: Replicas are multiple web servers configured so that they can all service the same URLs, allowing a load balancer to distribute traffic among them.
Key_Actors: load balancer; web servers; end users.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives a request from a user who interacts with it as if it were a single web server; the load balancer uses an algorithm to select one machine from the group of replicas; the request is relayed to the selected replica for processing; the replica generates the response and sends it back to the user through the load balancer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas must be configured to service the same set of URLs; replicas can be added to a group until the total required capacity is reached; different groups of replicas can be grown independently to serve different websites or applications.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a replica dies unexpectedly, the load balancer must route traffic to the remaining healthy replicas; if sequential requests from a single user are sent to different replicas, the second replica may not recognize the user is logged in unless a shared state or sticky connection strategy is implemented.

Concept_Name: SQL database
Core_Definition: An SQL database is a common type of backend data server used in multi-tier architectures to store and manage information required for database-driven dynamic content.
Key_Actors: web servers; application servers; backend data server.
Operational_Workflow: A program running on a web server or application server receives a request for content; the program initiates a query to the SQL database for specific information; the database retrieves the requested data and provides it to the program; the program uses this data to generate a web page or response for the client.
Rules_and_Constraints: The database software and its data can be hosted on the same machine as the web server or moved to an independent platform for better scaling; by splitting it onto its own platform, it can be grown independently.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: On a single machine, the database and web server may compete for memory, leading to non-optimal operating system self-tuning or buffer thrashing; databases may respond slowly for shared state storage compared to specialized systems like Memcached or Redis.

Concept_Name: DNS Round Robin
Core_Definition: DNS Round Robin is a load balancing technique that lists the IP addresses of all available replicas in the DNS entry for a web server name.
Key_Actors: DNS server; web browsers; replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A web browser queries a DNS server for the IP address of a web server; the DNS server returns the IP addresses of all replicas associated with that name; the web browser receives the full list and randomly picks one IP address to try first; when many browsers visit the site, the load is distributed across the replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method is easy to implement and free because it requires no specialized hardware beyond the existing DNS server.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This technique is rarely used because it is difficult to control and not very responsive to failures; if a replica dies, browsers that have cached the dead IP address will continue to try to access it until the cache expires; there is no simple way to reduce traffic to a specific replica if it becomes overloaded.

Concept_Name: Layer 3 and 4 Load Balancers
Core_Definition: Load balancers that receive each TCP session and redirect it to one of many replicas by passing every packet through the balancer to the replica and back.
Key_Actors: TCP sessions; packets; replicas; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives a TCP session and redirects it to a replica; every packet of that session travels first through the load balancer and then to the replica; reply packets from the replica return back through the balancer; the system uses algorithms like Round Robin, Least Loaded, or Latency to determine which backend receives the traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems are simple and fast; Layer 3 refers to the network (IP) layer and Layer 4 refers to the session (TCP) layer; most are not transparent and obscure the source IP address of the request, requiring the balancer to inject an X-Forwarded-For header so backends can identify the original requester.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a replica goes down unexpectedly, the load balancer will route traffic to the remaining replicas; balancing algorithms are configured to skip machines that are down.

Concept_Name: L3 load balancers
Core_Definition: A type of load balancer that tracks TCP sessions based on source and destination IP addresses at the network layer.
Key_Actors: Source IP addresses; destination IP addresses; network layer; backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: The balancer identifies incoming traffic at the network layer; it ensures all traffic from a specific source address is sent to the same server regardless of how many TCP sessions that source generates.
Rules_and_Constraints: These are characterized as simple and fast; they operate according to the ISO protocol stack definition for Layer 3.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a replica fails, the load balancer redirects traffic to the remaining available replicas.

Concept_Name: L4 load balancers
Core_Definition: A type of load balancer that tracks source and destination ports in addition to IP addresses at the session layer.
Key_Actors: Source ports; destination ports; IP addresses; session layer; backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: The balancer tracks traffic at the session layer; it uses port and IP information to permit a finer granularity of traffic distribution than L3 balancers.
Rules_and_Constraints: These are characterized as simple and fast; they operate according to the ISO protocol stack definition for Layer 4.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a replica fails, the load balancer redirects traffic to the remaining available replicas.

Concept_Name: Layer 7 Load Balancer
Core_Definition: Load balancers that make distribution decisions based on information seen by peering into the application layer of the protocol stack.
Key_Actors: Application layer; HTTP protocol; cookies; headers; URLs; backend servers; logged-in users.
Operational_Workflow: The balancer examines the internal details of the application layer protocol; it looks for specific cookies, headers, or URLs; it uses these findings to make routing decisions, such as detecting a special cookie and sending that traffic to a specific set of especially fast servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: These offer a richer mix of features than L3 or L4 balancers; they can handle logged-in users differently than other traffic; they use algorithms like Round Robin or Least Loaded to skip down machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: transparent load balancers
Core_Definition: A load balancer that forwards requests to backend servers without altering the source IP address of the original request.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend web servers; original requester.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer receives an incoming request from a client; the load balancer selects a backend replica to handle the query; the load balancer relays the request to the backend while preserving the original client source IP address.
Rules_and_Constraints: The source IP address of the request must remain unaltered as it passes from the load balancer to the backend.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: X-Forwarded-For:
Core_Definition: An additional HTTP header injected by a load balancer or proxy to indicate the IP address of the original requester when the load balancer obscures the source IP.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; proxies; backend web servers; applications; original client.
Operational_Workflow: A non-transparent load balancer or proxy receives a request and obscures the original source IP; the first proxy or load balancer adds the X-Forwarded-For: header containing the original client IP address and its own IP address; additional proxies and load balancers append their own IP addresses to the header as the request progresses; the backend or application accesses the header information to identify the original requester for tasks like debugging and log analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: The first proxy or load balancer must add the header with the original client IP and its own IP; subsequent intermediaries must append their own IP addresses to the existing header.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When load balancers are not transparent and do not use this header, all requests appear to come from the load balancer itself, making debugging and log analysis confusing or impossible.

Concept_Name: Round Robin (RR)
Core_Definition: A load balancing algorithm where incoming requests are distributed to backend machines by rotating through them in a continuous loop.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend replica machines.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer maintains a list of available backend replicas; it assigns an incoming request to the first machine in the sequence; it moves to the next machine for the subsequent request; it continues this process until it reaches the end of the list and then returns to the first machine to repeat the rotation.
Rules_and_Constraints: The machines must be rotated in a loop; any machines that are down must be skipped.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Down machines are skipped during the rotation.

Concept_Name: Weighted RR
Core_Definition: A load balancing method similar to Round Robin that distributes more queries to backend servers that have higher processing capacity.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend servers; administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A weight is assigned to each backend based on its capacity; the load balancer receives incoming requests; the load balancer rotates requests among the backends such that those with higher weights receive a larger proportion of the traffic in the sequence.
Rules_and_Constraints: The weight assigned to each backend is usually manually configured; backends with more capacity are given more queries.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Least Loaded (LL)
Core_Definition: A load balancing method where the load balancer receives information from each backend regarding its current load level and directs incoming requests to the backend with the smallest load.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; backend replicas.
Operational_Workflow: Each backend server monitors its own load status; backends communicate their current load levels to the load balancer; when an incoming request arrives, the load balancer identifies which backend is currently the least loaded; the load balancer forwards the request to that specific backend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Incoming requests must always go to the least loaded backend based on the information provided to the load balancer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This method has specific problems that are addressed by the Least Loaded with Slow Start variation.

Concept_Name: Least Loaded with Slow Start
Core_Definition: A load balancing scheme similar to Least Loaded that prevents newly activated backends from being overwhelmed by gradually increasing their traffic volume.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; newly online backend replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A new backend server is brought online; the load balancer identifies the new backend but does not immediately send it a full share of queries; the load balancer begins sending a low rate of traffic to the new backend; the traffic rate is slowly increased over time; the traffic continues to build until the backend is receiving an amount of traffic appropriate to its capacity and load.
Rules_and_Constraints: New backends must not be immediately flooded with queries; traffic must build slowly to fix problems inherent in the basic Least Loaded algorithm.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Slow Start
Core_Definition: A mechanism used by load balancers to introduce traffic to a new or newly available resource in a gradual manner rather than at full capacity immediately.
Key_Actors: Local load balancers; global load balancers; backend replicas; datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: A resource such as a backend server or a datacenter becomes available for traffic; the load balancer initiates the connection with a low rate of queries; the rate of traffic is incrementally increased; the build-up continues until the resource reaches its appropriate share of the total load.
Rules_and_Constraints: Slow start is used in both local load balancing and global load balancing specifically when the nearest datacenter is selected until it is full.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Utilization Limit
Core_Definition: A load balancing method where each server estimates its remaining capacity and informs the load balancer how much additional traffic it can manage.
Key_Actors: Individual backend servers; load balancer.
Operational_Workflow: Each server performs an estimation of how many additional queries per second (QPS) it is capable of handling; the servers communicate these capacity estimates to the load balancer; the load balancer uses these estimates to make forwarding decisions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Estimates may be based on current throughput or data gathered from synthetic load tests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Latency
Core_Definition: The time delay associated with data traveling across the internet or the time taken by a backend to process a request.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend replicas; end users; internet service providers; internal network backbones.
Operational_Workflow: Local load balancers monitor the time taken by backends to fulfill requests and may stop forwarding traffic to a specific backend if its response time exceeds a threshold; in global architectures, data and computation are moved closer to users in multiple datacenters to reduce the time packets spend traveling long distances.
Rules_and_Constraints: Load balancers may assume a backend is overloaded if requests take more than 100 ms; latency and physical distance are often correlated but can differ based on network routing and infrastructure quality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High latency over great distances can make interactive applications unusable; network routes may be non-optimal, such as historical traffic between South American countries being routed through the United States.

Concept_Name: Cascade
Core_Definition: A load balancing method where requests are sent to the first replica until it reaches capacity, after which overflow is sent to the next replica.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer directs all incoming traffic to the first replica in a sequence; once the first replica is at its maximum capacity, the load balancer directs any additional requests to the second replica, and continues this pattern for subsequent machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: The load balancer must be configured with the precise traffic capacity of each replica; these capacity limits are typically determined through static configuration based on synthetic load tests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: shared state
Core_Definition: A design approach where user information and session data are stored in a central location accessible to all web server backends.
Key_Actors: Web server backends; shared storage systems like Memcached, Redis, or SQL databases; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: When a user performs an action like logging in, the backend server stores the resulting session ID or profile info in a shared data store; for every subsequent HTTP connection, whichever backend receives the request fetches the user's state from this shared area to maintain continuity.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach ensures that users are not asked to log in again even if the load balancer switches them to a different backend; information is often held in RAM for the fastest possible access.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Standard databases may be too slow for this purpose because they are not optimized for fast state retrieval; using a directory on an NFS server is discouraged because it lacks reliable file locking and failover capabilities.

Concept_Name: Sticky Connections
Core_Definition: A load balancer feature that directs a user's subsequent HTTP requests to the same backend server that handled their initial request.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; web server backends; end users.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer identifies a returning user and routes their traffic specifically to the backend where their session information was originally stored locally.
Rules_and_Constraints: Stickiness is used as a strategy to maintain user sessions without requiring every request to fetch data from a shared store; it is sometimes used in a hybrid model alongside shared state for applications where moving state between servers is highly inconvenient or resource-intensive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the specific backend machine fails or is taken down for maintenance, the load balancer is forced to send the user to a different backend, which will not have the local state and may require the user to log in again.

Concept_Name: Memcached
Core_Definition: A system specifically designed for shared state storage that typically holds information in RAM for the fastest possible access.
Key_Actors: Web server backends; shared state storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Web backends store user information such as login status and profile data in Memcached; for each incoming HTTP request the backend fetches the user state from this shared area so that the user experience remains consistent regardless of which machine the load balancer selects to process the request.
Rules_and_Constraints: All information is held in RAM for fast access; it is used as a common backend for all web servers to access shared state information.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Redis
Core_Definition: A system specifically designed for shared state storage that holds all information in RAM for fast access by multiple backends.
Key_Actors: Web server replicas; shared state systems.
Operational_Workflow: When a user generates state information like login status, the web server backend stores this data in Redis; subsequent HTTP requests from the same user can be handled by any replica because each replica retrieves the necessary state from Redis to maintain session continuity.
Rules_and_Constraints: It should be used as an alternative to slower databases for maintaining state across replicas because databases are not optimized for this type of operation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: NFS server
Core_Definition: A server providing a directory service that can be used for storing and retrieving information shared between backend machines.
Key_Actors: Backend servers; NFS storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Backend servers use a directory on the NFS server to store and retrieve sharing state so that multiple web servers can access common user information.
Rules_and_Constraints: Administrators should avoid using a directory on an NFS server for shared state storage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An NFS server does not provide failover in the event of a server outage; it does not provide reliable file locking.

Concept_Name: network address translation (NAT)
Core_Definition: A networking process where many different machines are often seen by a server as using the same IP address.
Key_Actors: Client machines; web application backends.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple machines behind a NAT appear to external backends as having a single identical source IP address; the backend generates a random secret or session ID called a cookie and sends it to the browser so that it can identify the user in future requests independently of the IP address.
Rules_and_Constraints: Applications must not use the source IP address of an HTTP request as a form of user identity; session IDs must be randomly generated and difficult to guess to ensure they can be trusted.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: cookie
Core_Definition: A scheme used by web applications to identify a user across multiple distinct HTTP requests by generating and exchanging a secret randomly.
Key_Actors: Web application; web browser; user.
Operational_Workflow: When a user logs into a web application, the application generates a random secret and includes it in the reply sent to the web browser. For all future HTTP requests to that same web application, the browser sends the secret back to the application. The application uses this secret to verify that the request is from the same user who previously logged in.
Rules_and_Constraints: The secret must be generated randomly; the secret must be difficult to guess; the secret is intended to be unique to a specific user on a specific web browser.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Backends cannot rely on source IP addresses for identity because network address translation often causes multiple machines to appear as the same IP; additionally, IP addresses frequently change when a device moves between different networks or is rebooted.

Concept_Name: session ID
Core_Definition: The specific, randomly generated secret used within a cookie scheme to maintain user identity throughout a web session.
Key_Actors: Web application; web browser; user.
Operational_Workflow: The web application creates a session ID during the user authentication process. This identifier is transmitted to the user's browser and then presented by the browser in subsequent HTTP requests to allow the backend to recognize the user without requiring repeated logins.
Rules_and_Constraints: The session ID must be difficult for others to guess so that the web application can safely trust the identity of the requester.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Replica Groups
Core_Definition: A configuration pattern where a load balancer manages several distinct sets of web servers, with each set dedicated to a different web site.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; groups of web server replicas; multiple independent web sites.
Operational_Workflow: A single load balancer is configured to serve multiple different web sites. For each site, the load balancer maintains a separate group of replicas. Administrators can scale the capacity of the system by adding or removing replicas within a specific group based on the unique traffic demands of that group's assigned web site.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas must be configured so they can all service the same URLs for their assigned site; the number of replicas in each group must be able to grow independently.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dual Load Balancers
Core_Definition: A redundancy pattern for the three-tier architecture involving multiple load balancers where each serves as a replica of the other.
Key_Actors: Primary load balancer; secondary load balancer.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple load balancers are deployed in a configuration where they replicate each other's functions. The systems monitor for failures, and if the active load balancer fails, another load balancer takes over the task of distributing incoming requests to the backend web servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each load balancer in the pair or group must be a replica of the other.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If one load balancer fails, the other can take over to maintain service availability.

Concept_Name: Multiple Data Stores
Core_Definition: A variation of the three-tier web service architecture that utilizes many different data stores to manage service information.
Key_Actors: Replica groups; data store systems; shared state storage; product catalogs.
Operational_Workflow: Each replica group within a service can be assigned its own dedicated data store or all groups can share a set of data stores. These data stores may utilize identical or different technologies depending on the type of data they manage; for example, one store might be dedicated to shared state while another manages a product catalog.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data stores can be shared or independent; different technologies can be used within the same architecture to suit specific data needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Four-Tier Web Service
Core_Definition: An architecture used when multiple individual applications share a common frontend infrastructure to decouple user-facing interactions from application logic.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; frontends; application servers; data sources; application service teams; centralized frontend platform team.
Operational_Workflow: Web requests enter the system through a load balancer which distributes traffic among a group of frontends. The frontends manage user interactions and communicate with application servers to retrieve content. Finally, the application servers access shared data sources in the fourth layer to process the requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: The application and web servers must run on different machines; centralized management is required to prevent teams from creating architectural exceptions; hardware should be selected based on the specific needs of each layer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This design requires a high degree of trust between individual application teams and the centralized team managing the frontend platform.

Concept_Name: Frontends
Core_Definition: The layer in a four-tier architecture responsible for handling tasks common to all applications to reduce individual application complexity.
Key_Actors: End users; frontend servers; application servers; specialized security or platform teams.
Operational_Workflow: Frontends receive requests from load balancers and process tasks such as cookie management, session pipelining, compression, and encryption. They handle user authentication and pass pre-authenticated usernames to the application layer. They can also act as protocol gateways, receiving modern protocols like HTTP 2.0 or IPv6 and translating them into simpler protocols for application servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Encryption and certificate management should be centralized at this layer to be managed by experts; frontends should serve as the primary attack surface to reduce security exposure for the rest of the system; frontends should independently handle protocol upgrades and fixes for broken header implementations in backends.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Frontends can fix compatibility issues such as downcasing headers for application servers that erroneously expect case-sensitivity or providing IPv4 connectivity to application servers that do not support IPv6.

Concept_Name: Application Servers
Core_Definition: The layer in a four-tier architecture that receives processed queries from frontends to execute specific application logic.
Key_Actors: Frontends; application servers; data service layers; individual application teams.
Operational_Workflow: Application servers receive queries from frontends, often using specialized or purpose-built protocols rather than standard HTTP for better efficiency. They process the application-specific requests and interact with the data service layer to retrieve or store information.
Rules_and_Constraints: Application servers should be split from frontends to allow for independent scaling of different applications; hardware for these servers should be configured specifically based on the needs of the application; isolation between application servers ensures that a failure in one application does not affect others or the frontend tier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some application servers may have broken implementations that require frontends to clean up or modify protocol headers before they can be processed.

Concept_Name: customer-facing interaction
Core_Definition: The management of user communication, protocols, and security issues that is decoupled from core application logic and handled by a centralized frontend layer.
Key_Actors: Users; frontend systems; application servers; centralized frontend platform team; application service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Web requests enter the system through a load balancer which distributes them among various frontends; these frontends manage the initial interaction with the user including session and protocol handling; the frontends then communicate with separate application servers to obtain content; finally the application servers access shared data sources in the back layer to fulfill the request.
Rules_and_Constraints: The application and web servers must run on different machines; management must maintain strict discipline to avoid allowing exceptions to this architectural separation; application teams must trust a centralized team to manage the frontend infrastructure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Frontends can mitigate broken application server implementations by automatically downcasing case-insensitive HTTP headers that the application servers incorrectly expect to be case-sensitive; frontends can also translate incoming IPv6 requests to IPv4 for backends that do not support the newer protocol.

Concept_Name: session pipelining
Core_Definition: A process managed by frontend servers that allows for the handling of multiple HTTP requests over a single TCP connection.
Key_Actors: Frontend servers; users; application servers.
Operational_Workflow: The frontend establishes a TCP connection with the user web browser; the frontend then processes multiple individual HTTP requests within that same established session rather than opening a new connection for every query; this streamlined communication is then interfaced with the back-end application servers which may use a simpler version of the protocol.
Rules_and_Constraints: This functionality is centralized at the frontend tier to reduce complexity for the application servers; it is part of a suite of common tasks handled by the frontend along with cookie processing and encryption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: compression
Core_Definition: The reduction of data size performed at the frontend tier to conserve network bandwidth and improve transmission efficiency.
Key_Actors: Frontends; application servers; users; global load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: In a four-tier architecture, the frontend tier performs compression on data before sending it to the end user; for large-scale services, bulky compressed data such as map tiles may be identified by specific hostnames; the global load balancer can then route this compressed, bandwidth-heavy traffic over slower but less expensive network paths while keeping user interface data on faster paths.
Rules_and_Constraints: Compression tasks are decoupled from application servers and centralized in the frontend layer; even when data is compressed, it may still be considered bulky and require specific network routing strategies to manage costs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large compressed files like map tiles may load slowly without hindering user experience if they are loaded off-screen by background scripts, allowing the service to prioritize cheaper transmission paths despite the latency.

Concept_Name: encryption
Core_Definition: The centralization of cryptographic security functions and certificate management at the frontend layer to ensure expert oversight and minimize the attack surface.
Key_Actors: Specialized frontend team; application service teams; frontend servers; users.
Operational_Workflow: Encryption tasks are moved from individual application servers to a centralized frontend tier; a specialized team of experts manages the complex lifecycle of cryptographic certificates and keys; this team ensures that security protocols are correctly implemented before data is sent across the internet to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: Encryption should be centralized because certificate management is too complex for non-experts; the policy follows the principle of putting all eggs in one basket and making it a strong basket; individual application teams should not be expected to manage their own crypto certificates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If crypto certificates are managed by many individual teams, there is a higher risk of keys being exposed or stolen; an accidental leak of a shared key by any one team would weaken security for all applications in the infrastructure; non-expert management can lead to mistakes that either weaken security or cause the service to stop working entirely.

Concept_Name: certificate management
Core_Definition: The centralized administration of cryptographic certificates and encryption functions at the frontend layer to ensure security expertise and service continuity.
Key_Actors: Specialized frontend platform team; security experts; individual application service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Encryption is centralized at the frontend layer of a four tier web service; experts manage cryptographic certificates to prevent mistakes that could weaken security; the specialized team handles keys to ensure they are not exposed or stolen; the frontend handles these complex tasks so that individual application teams do not need to be highly qualified in crypto management.
Rules_and_Constraints: Centralize encryption at one layer to manage complexity; avoid letting non experts manage certificates; place trust in one specialized team rather than many individual teams; follow the security policy of putting all eggs in one strong basket.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Mistakes by non experts can cause the service to stop working or weaken security; an accidental leak of a key by any one team weakens security for all applications if they use the same shared key.

Concept_Name: IPv6
Core_Definition: A network protocol version that can be received by frontends to provide modern connectivity even when backend application servers do not support it.
Key_Actors: Frontends; backend application servers.
Operational_Workflow: The frontend receives an incoming internet request via the IPv6 protocol; the frontend then communicates with the backend application server using the IPv4 protocol.
Rules_and_Constraints: Frontends can act as a gateway to support protocols that application servers have not yet implemented.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: IPv4
Core_Definition: A network protocol version used for communication between frontends and backends when the backend server does not support newer standards like IPv6.
Key_Actors: Frontends; backend application servers.
Operational_Workflow: The frontend accepts external requests using newer protocol versions; the frontend then speaks IPv4 to the backends to ensure the application remains functional.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: reducing the attack surface area
Core_Definition: A security approach that limits the number of system entry points exposed to the internet to simplify protection and enable rapid patching.
Key_Actors: Frontends; internet; applications; application teams.
Operational_Workflow: Frontends are established as the only part of the system directly exposed to the internet; security functions are decoupled from the applications; bugs and security holes are fixed at the frontend level independently of backend application schedules.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decouple security functions from the applications; fix bugs and security holes rapidly; upgrade frontends independently of any application server upgrade schedule.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Application teams may have priorities that prevent them from performing software upgrades immediately, which makes the ability to upgrade frontends independently essential.

Concept_Name: Reverse Proxy Service
Core_Definition: A service that enables one web server to transparently provide content from another web server, presenting multiple applications as a single cohesive website to the user.
Key_Actors: Users; reverse proxy software; backend web servers or services such as sports news, weather, finance, and email.
Operational_Workflow: The user sends a request to the reverse proxy; the proxy interprets the URL and identifies the appropriate backend server; it collects the required pages from that server or service; it relays the results back to the original requester.
Rules_and_Constraints: Used to combine different web features into a unified experience; facilitates the centralization of security and administrative tasks such as maintaining per-domain crypto certificates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because the functionality is relatively simple, it is often a built-in feature of general web server software rather than a dedicated standalone service.

Concept_Name: Cloud-Scale Service
Core_Definition: Globally distributed services that replicate infrastructure across multiple datacenters around the world to bring data and computation closer to users.
Key_Actors: Global load balancers; datacenters; users; Internet Service Providers (ISPs); points of presence (POPs); satellites.
Operational_Workflow: Infrastructure is replicated in multiple datacenters globally; global load balancers direct users to the nearest location; private wide area network connections may be used to link datacenters for better control and performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designed to reduce latency for interactive applications; necessitates building or renting space in datacenters worldwide; often utilizes an internal backbone to connect datacenters and POPs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a user's specific data is stored in a different datacenter than the one they are directed to, the local frontend must communicate across the private backbone to fetch that data; some services may split traffic types to use different paths based on cost and performance needs.

Concept_Name: global load balancer (GLB)
Core_Definition: A specialized DNS server that directs traffic to the nearest or most appropriate datacenter by examining the requester's source IP address.
Key_Actors: GLB (DNS server); requesters; datacenters; geolocation database providers.
Operational_Workflow: The GLB receives a DNS query to translate a domain name into an IP address; it determines the requester's location using geolocation data; it selects the best replica based on configured metrics; it returns the IP address of that specific replica to the requester.
Rules_and_Constraints: Can use various selection methods including nearest, nearest with limits, or nearest by other metrics like latency or cost; must include slow start for new sites to avoid flooding them with traffic.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: GLBs function at the DNS level and cannot identify specific users or where their personal data is stored; they may be configured to return different hostnames for different types of content, such as user interface elements versus bulk data, to optimize speed and cost.

Concept_Name: Geolocation
Core_Definition: The process of determining the physical location of a machine on the internet based on its IP address.
Key_Actors: Internet-connected machines; geolocation companies; global load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Companies use various methods and estimations to determine where IP subnets are physically located; this information is compiled into databases; global load balancers use these databases to determine the origin of a query.
Rules_and_Constraints: Described as a difficult task because IP addresses have no concrete geographic meaning; relied upon by global load balancers to make traffic routing decisions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Geolocation is less accurate than phone numbers; IP addresses for a particular machine can change when moving between networks, such as transitioning from WiFi to cellular or when a machine is rebooted.

Concept_Name: Nearest (GLB method)
Core_Definition: A global load balancing technique where a DNS server selects the datacenter physically closest to the requester based on geolocation.
Key_Actors: Global load balancer; DNS server; requester; datacenters; IP geolocation database providers.
Operational_Workflow: The global load balancer receives a DNS query to translate a domain name to an IP address; it examines the source IP address of the query to determine the requester's geolocation; it consults a list of replicas and their physical locations; it identifies which datacenter is geographically closest to the requester; it returns the IP address of that nearest datacenter in the DNS reply.
Rules_and_Constraints: The method requires the global load balancer to maintain a list of replicas, their locations, and their IP addresses; it relies on geolocation data which maps IP subnets to physical locations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Geolocation is a difficult task that involves guessing because IP addresses have no concrete geographic meaning; the physically nearest datacenter might not be the best choice if network paths are indirect or if other metrics like latency or cost are prioritized.

Concept_Name: Nearest with Limits
Core_Definition: A global load balancing method that directs traffic to the closest datacenter until it reaches its capacity, after which traffic is sent to the next nearest location.
Key_Actors: Global load balancer; datacenters; local load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The global load balancer identifies the nearest datacenter to the requester; it monitors the capacity or load of that site; if the site has available capacity, the requester is directed there; if the site is at its limit, the global load balancer selects the next nearest datacenter to handle the overflow traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method incorporates slow start to prevent new or recovered sites from being immediately flooded with traffic; the system must have defined capacity limits for each site.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a new backend comes online, slow start ensures it receives a low rate of traffic that builds slowly rather than being overwhelmed by the least loaded or nearest algorithms.

Concept_Name: Nearest by Other Metric
Core_Definition: A global load balancing strategy where the best datacenter is determined by performance or business metrics such as latency or cost rather than physical distance.
Key_Actors: Global load balancer; datacenters; transit ISPs.
Operational_Workflow: The global load balancer evaluates available datacenters based on a specific metric like network latency or the cost of data transmission; it compares the metrics associated with different network paths; it returns the IP address of the datacenter that optimizes the chosen metric for the specific request.
Rules_and_Constraints: Latency and distance are usually the same but can differ based on network routing; cost is not always a function of distance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Historical network routes, such as those between South American countries that passed through the United States, can result in the physically nearest site having higher latency than a more distant one.

Concept_Name: wide area network (WAN) connections
Core_Definition: Private, dedicated network links that connect geographically distributed datacenters, points of presence, and satellites to form an internal backbone.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; points of presence; satellites; frontends; application servers; internal platform teams.
Operational_Workflow: A company establishes dedicated links between its various global facilities to create a private backbone; when a user request reaches a frontend in one datacenter, the frontend uses the internal backbone to communicate with application servers in other datacenters as needed; this allows for the retrieval of user-specific data or cross-site service coordination without using the public internet.
Rules_and_Constraints: A private WAN gives a company more control and more reliable performance than the public internet; it is not visible to the internet at large; it connects datacenters, points of presence, and satellites.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Using the private backbone incurs direct costs for the company for every packet sent; to manage costs, a company may route small, latency-sensitive traffic like user interface elements over the private WAN while routing large, bulky data like map tiles over cheaper, slower public ISP paths.

Concept_Name: Internal Backbone
Core_Definition: Private WAN links that connect datacenters to form a private network that is not visible to the internet at large.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; points of presence (POPs); satellites; private wide area network (WAN) links.
Operational_Workflow: Private WAN links are used to interconnect various datacenters; this internal network also extends to points of presence and satellites; the backbone connects to the internet in multiple locations wherever a datacenter exists; frontends use this backbone to communicate with application servers located in different datacenters.
Rules_and_Constraints: The backbone must be private and hidden from the general internet; it must be engineered to provide specific latency and bandwidth requirements; the owning company must pay the costs associated with every packet sent over the backbone.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a company does not own a private backbone, it may be forced to communicate between datacenters over the public internet, which results in less control and less reliable performance.

Concept_Name: ISP
Core_Definition: A local network provider that connects a company's datacenter or point of presence to the internet.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; local ISPs; transit ISPs; points of presence (POPs).
Operational_Workflow: A datacenter connects to multiple local ISPs in its geographic area to reach users; when a direct connection to a specific ISP is unavailable, data is routed through intermediate transit ISPs; companies may establish POPs near local ISPs that do not operate in the same region as the main datacenter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Connecting directly to an ISP is preferred because it provides speed and cost benefits; if an ISP is not in the same state or country as the datacenter, the company must extend its network to a location near that ISP.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: transit ISP
Core_Definition: An intermediate network provider that charges other ISPs for the privilege of allowing packets to travel over its network to reach their destination.
Key_Actors: Transit ISPs; local ISPs; company datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: When a company lacks a direct connection to a specific destination ISP, it sends data to a transit ISP; the transit ISP relays the packets across its network to the next ISP in the chain; this process repeats until the data reaches the user's ISP.
Rules_and_Constraints: Transit ISPs charge fees to the other ISPs involved in the connection.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: As the number of transit ISPs between a company and its customers increases, the connections become significantly slower, less reliable, and more expensive.

Concept_Name: Point of Presence (POP)
Core_Definition: A small, remote facility used to provide a connection point to local ISPs that cannot connect directly to a company's datacenter.
Key_Actors: Network equipment; telecom providers; colocation centers; local ISPs; satellites.
Operational_Workflow: A company extends its private network to a new location by placing a rack of equipment in a colocation center or small closet; the facility is connected to various local telecom providers and ISPs; the POP facilitates the exchange of traffic between the private backbone and the local internet.
Rules_and_Constraints: A POP contains network equipment and telecom connections but does not house general-purpose computers; it is typically located in a space resembling a closet or a rack in a colocation facility.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a POP is modified to include a small number of computers for tasks like content distribution or terminating HTTP sessions, it is then referred to as a satellite.

Concept_Name: colocation center
Core_Definition: A facility that houses a rack of equipment or a small space resembling a closet used for network connections.
Key_Actors: Network equipment; telecom providers.
Operational_Workflow: It serves as a physical location for a point of presence where network equipment and connections from various telecom providers are hosted to facilitate connection to local internet service providers.
Rules_and_Constraints: It contains network equipment and connections from telecom providers but no general-purpose computers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: satellite
Core_Definition: A point of presence facility that includes both network equipment and a small number of computers.
Key_Actors: Network equipment; telecom providers; frontend servers; content distribution servers; application servers in other datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: The facility uses its included computers to terminate HTTP connections and proxy them to application servers in distant datacenters; it also utilizes content distribution servers to cache and serve large amounts of content locally.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must consist of a point of presence combined with a small number of computers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Content distribution servers
Core_Definition: Specialized machines used to cache and store large amounts of data to be served to users from a closer network location.
Key_Actors: Machines; cached content such as popular videos.
Operational_Workflow: These servers store frequently accessed or popular content, such as the most popular videos being viewed, so that the data can be delivered to the user from the local cache rather than a remote datacenter.
Rules_and_Constraints: They are typically located within satellites as part of a private network backbone.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Message Bus Architectures
Core_Definition: A many-to-many communication mechanism where servers distribute information by publishing to and subscribing from specific channels.
Key_Actors: Publishers; subscribers; message bus master; channels.
Operational_Workflow: A master manages server connections and determines the shortest network path for each channel; publishers send messages to specific channels; the system delivers messages via IP multicast on the same subnet or IP unicast between subnets; subscribers listening to those channels receive the data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Messages are transmitted only to subscribed machines; subscribers are responsible for detecting and skipping duplicate messages; code should not depend on perfectly ordered messages; a secondary mechanism should exist to allow clients to catch up on missed data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The master may become a bottleneck if all messages must pass through it; subscribers may miss messages during long outages depending on the system implementation; messages may arrive out of order during retries; some systems may hold messages in RAM briefly before queuing to disk or giving up.

Concept_Name: channels
Core_Definition: A channel is a communication mechanism within a message bus architecture, similar to a radio channel, where servers send or listen for specific messages. It acts as the medium for distributing information among different services in one-to-many, many-to-many, or many-to-one configurations.
Key_Actors: Servers; publishers; subscribers; message bus master.
Operational_Workflow: Publishers send messages containing data or updates to a specific channel; the message bus master manages which servers are connected to which channels; subscribers listen to the channels they need; the system transmits messages only to the subset of machines currently subscribed to that channel.
Rules_and_Constraints: Messages are only transmitted to subscribed machines to conserve network bandwidth and processing; channels can be open to any machine or tightly controlled using Access Control Lists (ACLs) to determine who can publish or subscribe; the system must optimize the topology of each channel separately to determine the shortest path for message delivery.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Messages may be received out of order or missed entirely if a subscriber is offline for an extended period; some systems may hold messages in RAM for a limited time before queueing them to disk or giving up on delivery.

Concept_Name: publisher
Core_Definition: A publisher is a server that sends messages to specific channels within a message bus architecture.
Key_Actors: Servers; message bus system; channels; subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: A server acting as a publisher generates a message containing data like real-time updates, status changes, or logging information; it sends this message to a designated channel on the message bus; the message bus then handles the distribution to any interested subscribers without the publisher needing to manage the recipient list.
Rules_and_Constraints: A server can function as a publisher, a subscriber, or both for any given channel; publishers allow for efficient information distribution because they do not need to be updated when new subscribers are added to the system; in centralized management models, the primary responsibility of an operations team is often ensuring the publisher remains functional.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the message bus master is designed such that all messages must first pass through it, the master may become a bottleneck for the publisher; publishers may be restricted by ACLs that determine their right to send messages to specific channels.

Concept_Name: subscribers
Core_Definition: Subscribers are the receiving servers in a message bus architecture that listen to specific channels to gather the information they need.
Key_Actors: Servers; receivers; message bus; channels.
Operational_Workflow: A server identifies the channels it needs to monitor and subscribes to them via the message bus; it receives messages transmitted to those channels and processes the data; in some systems, the subscriber sends an acknowledgment back to the message bus to confirm receipt of the message.
Rules_and_Constraints: Subscribers only receive messages for the specific channels they have joined; they must be designed to handle messages that arrive out of order using timestamps or other methods; they are responsible for detecting and skipping duplicate messages that might be sent if a previous acknowledgment was not received.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a subscriber returns to service after a crash, it may receive a flood of backlogged unacknowledged messages; if a subscriber is down longer than a defined length of time, it may lose messages permanently; subscribers may require a separate non-message-bus mechanism to catch up on missed data, such as a daily synchronization check.

Concept_Name: master (message bus)
Core_Definition: The master is a central authority or component of the message bus system that manages which servers are connected to which channels and determines how messages are routed.
Key_Actors: Message bus master; publishers; subscribers; physical network topology.
Operational_Workflow: The master learns the underlying physical network topology of the environment; for every channel, it calculates the shortest path a message must follow to reach all subscribers; it manages the connections between publishers and subscribers; it may utilize IP multicast for machines on the same subnet or IP unicast for transmissions between subnets.
Rules_and_Constraints: The master is responsible for optimizing the unique topology of thousands of channels; some systems utilize multiple masters or one master per channel to improve performance; the master manages security by enforcing ACLs on who can publish or subscribe to specific channels.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The master can become a performance bottleneck if the architecture requires all messages to flow through it for distribution; sophisticated designs allow the master to control the topology without requiring all messages to pass through it to mitigate this risk.

Concept_Name: message queue
Core_Definition: A many-to-many communication mechanism between servers used to distribute information among different services. It is identified in the text as one of the names for message bus technology.
Key_Actors: Publishers who send messages; Subscribers who receive messages; Channels used as virtual paths for communication; A central authority or master that manages server connections.
Operational_Workflow: A server acting as a publisher sends a message to a specific channel. A master authority learns the underlying physical network topology and determines the shortest path for message delivery. The message is transmitted using IP multicast for machines on the same subnet or IP unicast for transmission between subnets or when multicast is unavailable. Only machines subscribed to the specific channel receive the message while others ignore it to conserve network bandwidth and processing.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system allows for one-to-many; many-to-many; and many-to-one communication patterns. Messages can contain any type of data including real-time updates; database updates; or logging information. Access to channels may be managed through access control lists that determine which entities are permitted to publish or subscribe.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Message systems do not guarantee that messages will be received in the same order as they were sent. If a subscriber crashes it may receive duplicate copies of unacknowledged messages upon returning to service. During long subscriber outages messages may be lost or held in RAM before being queued to disk; leading to a flood of messages once the subscriber comes back online.

Concept_Name: queue service
Core_Definition: A term used to describe message bus technology that enables servers to communicate by sending messages to channels for other servers to receive.
Key_Actors: Publishers; Subscribers; Message bus master; Channels.
Operational_Workflow: Publishers transmit data to specific channels while subscribers listen for the information they require. A central master manages the connectivity and identifies the most efficient network topology for delivery. This approach ensures that clients receive a message only if they are subscribed; which is more efficient than broadcast systems that require all machines to filter out irrelevant data.
Rules_and_Constraints: This architecture is utilized as an efficient alternative to repeatedly polling a database to check for new information. It is described as operations-friendly because command-line clients can be easily connected to listen to and peer into information flows for debugging.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Subscribers must be designed to handle messages arriving out of order because enforcing order would delay millions of other messages. Timestamps are typically included in messages to assist subscribers in detecting when delivery order has deviated from the original sending sequence.

Concept_Name: pubsub service
Core_Definition: A messaging technology where servers publish information to specific channels and other servers subscribe to the channels they need to receive that information.
Key_Actors: Publishers; Subscribers; Master; Google (referenced in relation to PubSub2 and Thialfi systems); Hosts.
Operational_Workflow: A publisher emits messages to a designated channel while subscribers listen to that channel. A master manages the connections and optimizes the delivery path based on the network topology. The system transmits messages to all subscribed machines; often using IP multicast to send data to multiple machines on a subnet simultaneously or IP unicast for inter-subnet delivery.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pubsub services facilitate one-to-many communication to quickly send information to large sets of machines. The system is designed to be more efficient than broadcast methods by sending messages only to the machines that have actively subscribed to a channel.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Google's PubSub2 system is noted for its ability to handle hundreds of channels and tens of thousands of hosts; while its Thialfi system can handle millions of subscribers. Because messages may be missed or lost during outages; services usually maintain a secondary mechanism that allows clients to catch up on missed data; such as receiving a daily list of database keys and update dates.

Concept_Name: Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS)
Core_Definition: A specific implementation of message bus or message queue technology provided by Amazon.
Key_Actors: Amazon; Application servers acting as publishers and subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: MCollective
Core_Definition: MCollective is a publish-subscribe middleware used as a many-to-many communication mechanism between servers.
Key_Actors: Publishers who send messages; subscribers who receive the messages; a central authority or master that manages channel connections and topology.
Operational_Workflow: Servers acting as publishers send messages to specific channels; the message bus master determines the shortest path for delivery by learning the underlying physical network topology; subscribers listen only to the channels they require; the master manages which servers are connected to which channels to ensure network bandwidth and processing are conserved.
Rules_and_Constraints: Messages are transmitted only to machines that are actively subscribed; subscribers are responsible for detecting and skipping duplicate messages; code must be designed to handle messages that arrive out of order; services should maintain a separate mechanism to catch up on data missed during outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The master may become a bottleneck if all messages must pass through it; subscribers may miss messages during long outages or receive a massive backlog of messages from a disk queue once they return to service; messages may be lost if a subscriber is down for more than a defined length of time.

Concept_Name: RabbitMQ
Core_Definition: RabbitMQ is a message broker used as a many-to-many communication mechanism between servers within a message bus architecture.
Key_Actors: Publishers who emit messages; subscribers who receive messages; a central authority or master that manages channel connections.
Operational_Workflow: A server acting as a publisher sends messages to channels while other servers act as subscribers to the channels they need; the master determines the shortest path for each message based on the physical network topology; messages are transmitted only to the subset of machines subscribed to a specific channel to optimize efficiency.
Rules_and_Constraints: Network bandwidth is conserved by only sending messages to subscribed machines; subscribers must handle messages that arrive out of order using timestamps; subscribers must detect duplicate messages; a secondary synchronization mechanism is required to handle data missed during message bus failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The master can become a bottleneck depending on whether it requires all messages to pass through it or simply controls topology; long subscriber outages can lead to missed messages or a flood of backlogged messages once the subscriber returns to service; reception order is not guaranteed because retrying one message would delay all subsequent messages.

Concept_Name: IP multicast
Core_Definition: A networking method used to send a message to many machines on the same subnet at the same time.
Key_Actors: Message bus master; subscribed machines; network subnets.
Operational_Workflow: The message bus master learns the physical network topology and identifies the shortest path for a specific channel; it then utilizes IP multicast to distribute a single message to all subscribed machines residing on the same subnet simultaneously.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used for machines sharing the same subnet; determining and optimizing the unique topology for each channel requires significant calculations when thousands of channels exist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When IP multicast is not available within a network, the system must utilize IP unicast as an alternative.

Concept_Name: IP unicast
Core_Definition: A networking method used to transmit messages between different subnets or as a fallback when multicast is not available.
Key_Actors: Message bus master; individual servers; network subnets.
Operational_Workflow: The message bus master analyzes the physical network topology and utilizes IP unicast to move messages across subnet boundaries or to deliver messages when the network does not support multicast.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used for inter-subnet transmission; the master determines the routing based on shortest-path calculations for each individual channel.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ACLs
Core_Definition: Access control mechanisms used within a message bus system to regulate permissions for specific communication channels.
Key_Actors: Message bus system; publishers; subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: The message bus system uses ACLs to determine which entities are permitted to publish messages to a channel and which entities are permitted to subscribe to a channel.
Rules_and_Constraints: Channels may be open to anyone or tightly controlled by these lists to restrict access to specific publishers and subscribers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: PubSub2
Core_Definition: Google's Publish Subscribe Version 2 system which is a specific implementation of a message bus architecture.
Key_Actors: Google; channels; hosts.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system is designed to handle hundreds of channels and tens of thousands of hosts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Thialfi
Core_Definition: A message bus system developed by Google that is optimized for extremely large numbers of subscribers.
Key_Actors: Google; subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system is documented to handle up to 2.3 million subscribers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: acknowledgment
Core_Definition: A reliability mechanism where a subscriber confirms the receipt of a message to the message bus system.
Key_Actors: Message bus system; subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: The message bus sends a message to a subscriber; the subscriber receives the message and sends an acknowledgment back to the system; the system tracks which messages have been acknowledged and which are still pending; if a subscriber crashes and returns to service, the system resends all unacknowledged messages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Subscribers are required to acknowledge each message received to guarantee delivery; the subscriber is responsible for detecting and skipping duplicate messages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an acknowledgment does not make it back to the message bus system, the subscriber may receive a second copy of the same message; during long subscriber outages, messages may be lost or stored only for a defined length of time depending on the specific system design.

Concept_Name: duplicates
Core_Definition: Redundant copies of a message that a subscriber may receive from a message bus system, typically occurring when an acknowledgment of receipt is not successfully returned.
Key_Actors: Message bus systems; subscribers.
Operational_Workflow: A message bus system sends a message to a subscriber; the subscriber receives the message but then crashes or the acknowledgment fails to reach the message bus system; upon the subscriber returning to service, the message bus system transmits all unacknowledged messages again; the subscriber receives a second copy of the same message.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is the responsibility of the subscriber to detect and skip these messages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: out of order (messages)
Core_Definition: A situation in a message bus architecture where messages are not received by a subscriber in the same sequence in which they were originally sent.
Key_Actors: Message bus systems; subscribers; messages.
Operational_Workflow: A message bus system attempts to send a sequence of messages; if one message fails and requires a retry, the system continues sending other messages to avoid delaying the entire flow; the subscriber receives subsequent messages before the retried message finally arrives; the subscriber uses included timestamps to detect the discrepancy in sequence.
Rules_and_Constraints: Subscribers must be designed to handle messages arriving in any sequence; reordering is described as difficult or impossible because it is unclear how long a system should wait for a late message; code should be written so that it does not depend on perfectly ordered messages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a message bus attempted to guarantee strict ordering, a single message being retried could cause millions of other messages to be delayed.

Concept_Name: Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA)
Core_Definition: An architecture where a large service is managed as a collection of self-contained subsystems, each providing its functionality via an API.
Key_Actors: Subsystems; application service teams; API governance; management.
Operational_Workflow: Each subsystem is designed to provide a specific functionality as a discrete service; these services communicate with one another by making API calls; as teams and systems grow, management can split teams into smaller units that focus on specific sets of these subsystems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should be loosely coupled to ensure they do not know the internal workings of other systems; all services should use the same underlying RPC protocol for their APIs; consistent monitoring, load balancing, and coding standards should be applied across all services; organizations should adopt API governance to maintain standards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tightly coupled systems that have become difficult to maintain can be evolved into SOAs by identifying and splitting out components one at a time, focusing on pieces that most need flexibility or ease of upgrade.

Concept_Name: API (Application Programming Interface)
Core_Definition: A high-level abstraction and communication mechanism used by applications and services to make requests, gather information, or interact with other subsystems.
Key_Actors: Mobile phone apps; application servers; subsystems in a Service-Oriented Architecture; API governance teams.
Operational_Workflow: A client or service makes a request to a specific interface using a protocol; the application server or subsystem receives the request, processes it, and provides the required service or data; in an SOA, these calls enable different services to cooperate without being tightly bound to each other's internal logic.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs should present services at a high level of abstraction to maintain loose coupling; in a large organization, the same underlying RPC protocol should be used for all service APIs to leverage common tools; API governance is recommended to maintain organizational standards and avoid repeating past failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In tightly coupled systems, an API may be too closely linked to internal workings, such as a locking mechanism, which forces all users of the API to change their code if the internal design of the service is updated.

Concept_Name: loosely coupled
Core_Definition: A design goal for service-oriented architectures where each API presents its service at a high level of abstraction. This approach ensures that systems do not know the internal workings of other systems they interact with.
Key_Actors: APIs; services; subsystems; development and operation teams.
Operational_Workflow: Services communicate with one another by making API calls to a high-level abstraction; subsystems are managed as discrete systems that can be combined in unexpected ways to create new applications; changes to the internal implementation of a service are made without requiring changes to the services that consume its API.
Rules_and_Constraints: The replacement for any service must provide the same high-level abstraction as the original; touch-points between services must be well-defined APIs; components should be decoupled to achieve flexibility and ease of upgrade or replacement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems often start as tightly coupled for resource efficiency or development speed and must undergo a long and difficult journey to be decoupled one piece at a time.

Concept_Name: tightly coupled
Core_Definition: A system architecture where an API is linked to the internal workings of a service, requiring users to specify details about how a job functions rather than just what is needed. This design creates dependencies that make components difficult to divide or manage independently.
Key_Actors: APIs; developers; service teams; internal mechanisms such as locking systems.
Operational_Workflow: Users of the API specify details related to how jobs work; if an internal design change is proposed for one component, the change cannot be made without updating the code of all services that use that specific API.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tightly coupled systems are often justified by being more resource efficient or faster to develop initially; these systems cannot be easily divided and managed by separate teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When maintenance becomes too difficult, a tightly coupled system may be evolved into a loosely coupled system by identifying and splitting out the most problematic pieces as services.

Concept_Name: job scheduler service
Core_Definition: A service that accepts requests to perform actions, coordinates their execution, and reports on their progress. It is used as a primary example to illustrate the differences between tightly and loosely coupled architectures.
Key_Actors: Job scheduler service; API; users or services requesting actions.
Operational_Workflow: The service accepts requests for various actions; it schedules and coordinates these actions; the service reports back progress to the requester as it executes.
Rules_and_Constraints: In a loosely coupled implementation, the API should provide job status at a high level of abstraction such as whether a job is waiting, running, its location, or if it can be stopped.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In a tightly coupled implementation, the API might provide direct access to internal details like the status of the lock mechanism used to prevent duplicate job execution.

Concept_Name: Google site reliability engineers (SREs)
Core_Definition: A specialized group at Google responsible for maintaining and operating large-scale systems such as Gmail.
Key_Actors: Google site reliability engineers; Gmail system; specialized subteams for storage, anti-spam, message receiving, and message delivery.
Operational_Workflow: A single group of SREs originally maintains a system; as the service grows, the group splits into focused subteams to manage specific subsystems such as the storage layer or the anti-spam layer.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ability to split teams by functionality is dependent on the service-oriented architecture (SOA) design of the system being managed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: RPC protocol
Core_Definition: A communication mechanism used to implement APIs across different services within a service-oriented architecture.
Key_Actors: Services; APIs; tools associated with the RPC mechanism.
Operational_Workflow: Services utilize an underlying RPC protocol to communicate with one another; any tool built for the specific RPC mechanism can then be leveraged across all services that share the protocol.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a best practice to use the same underlying RPC protocol for all services to ensure consistency and tool reuse.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While not detailed as an exception to RPC itself, the text notes that purpose-built protocols are sometimes used instead of general protocols like HTTP to serve API requests more efficiently and quickly.

Concept_Name: API governance
Core_Definition: The practice of maintaining organizational standards for the design and operation of application programming interfaces.
Key_Actors: Organizations; API designers.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations implement governance by establishing and enforcing standards that dictate how various APIs should function and interact.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standards used in governance should reflect knowledge learned through past failures to prevent those same issues from recurring.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: composition
Core_Definition: A rudimentary architectural element used as a building block for more complex application patterns.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Composition serves as a basic pattern that is combined with other elements to create the larger application architectures described throughout the chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: server trees
Core_Definition: A rudimentary architectural pattern commonly utilized for database services that require significant disk storage capacity.
Key_Actors: Database services; data storage subsystems.
Operational_Workflow: Server trees are integrated into larger application architectures to manage data storage needs at the data service layer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 5. Design Patterns for Scaling
Section: Chapter 5. Design Patterns for Scaling
============================================================

Concept_Name: Scaling
Core_Definition: A system’s ability to process a growing workload, usually measured in transactions per second, amount of data, or number of users.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; web-based services; batch-processing data analytics platforms; engineers; system administrators; users.
Operational_Workflow: Distributed systems are designed for scalability from the beginning because growth is expected; initial requirements include approximations of data size, throughput, and expected growth rates to guide the design; once the system is running, performance limits are discovered and design features are used to enable further growth; scaling solutions are evaluated using measurements from the real system to ensure they are effective; results are analyzed to verify if a hypothesis about a fix was correct.
Rules_and_Constraints: A distributed system is not automatically scalable and must be engineered to scale; there is a limit to how far a system scales before reengineering is required; any effort not spent focused on the bottleneck is wasteful; scaling solutions must be evaluated using evidence and data; past experience should inform decisions but scientific process and measurement must trump hunches or guesses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service does not scale or becomes too slow, users will go elsewhere; a slow service can be more frustrating than one that is down; scaling up by using faster equipment fails if software is single-threaded or if protocols perform badly when latency is high; fixing a problem too soon can waste effort on problems that never arise.

Concept_Name: O’Dell’s Axiom
Core_Definition: A principle stating that the only real problem is scaling, and everything else is a sub-problem.
Key_Actors: O’Dell (attributed author).
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: This axiom serves as an underlying philosophy for the design and operation of large distributed systems, suggesting that scalability is the primary challenge to be solved.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Premature optimization
Core_Definition: Spending too much time preventing scaling problems that may or may not happen in the future.
Key_Actors: Engineers; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify potential scaling issues that might occur as a system grows; they must prioritize writing code for immediate issues over addressing future potential problems; if they dedicate excessive time to future-proofing instead of current needs, it is categorized as premature optimization.
Rules_and_Constraints: Premature optimization should be avoided; design and coding effort for future potential scaling issues is lower priority than fixing immediate issues of the day.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Bottleneck
Core_Definition: A point in a system where congestion occurs because it is resource starved in a way that limits performance.
Key_Actors: Systems; resources; system administrators; junior staff who perform analysis.
Operational_Workflow: Administrators identify the bottleneck by finding where a backlog of work accumulates; if a system is performing well, the bottleneck can be found by generating additional load in a test environment to see where performance suffers; once found, the bottleneck is fixed to permit better performance; measurements are analyzed to predict when a bottleneck will become a problem in the future to allow time for engineering a solution.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every system has a bottleneck; any effort not spent focused on the bottleneck is wasteful; optimizations upstream of the bottleneck make the backlog grow faster; optimizations downstream of the bottleneck do not improve total throughput; a constraint only becomes a problem if it actually hampers the system’s ability to achieve its goal.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Fixing a bottleneck too early can waste effort; starting a solution too late results in the problem arising before the solution is deployed; waiting much too long leads to firefighting and rushed engineering which causes mistakes.

Concept_Name: Reengineering
Core_Definition: The process of rewriting parts of a system to improve speed, functionality, or resource consumption.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; system components; code; design structures.
Operational_Workflow: Identifying scaling issues that cannot be resolved through simple adjustments like configuration changes; performing engineering work to rewrite system parts to permit growth beyond current limits.
Rules_and_Constraints: Required when a system reaches a limit to how far it can scale; the effort must be prioritized against immediate daily issues to avoid premature optimization; it is often preceded by refactoring to make the process easier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The process can be made difficult by earlier design decisions that resulted in specific code or design structures.

Concept_Name: Refactoring
Core_Definition: Restructuring an existing body of code by altering its internal structure without changing its external behavior.
Key_Actors: Existing body of code; internal code organization; application code; engineering efforts.
Operational_Workflow: Replacing current code with functionally equivalent code that has an internal organization designed to make other reengineering efforts easier to accomplish.
Rules_and_Constraints: The external behavior of the code must remain the same; it is often used as a preliminary step before reengineering; it may be required when moving from an unsegmented database to a segmented one on the z-axis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Scaling up
Core_Definition: A methodology for scaling a system by using bigger, faster equipment or increasing the size of existing hardware components.
Key_Actors: Hardware components such as CPUs, RAM, disks, and network interfaces; single-threaded or multi-threaded software.
Operational_Workflow: Moving a slow system to a machine with more or faster resources; upgrading specific attributes of an existing computer like adding more CPUs or faster network interfaces to increase size.
Rules_and_Constraints: Does not require a redesign of the software; limited by the maximum power of the largest computers available on the market; often uneconomical as costs increase faster than performance gains; performance improvements are not guaranteed if software design is not compatible with the hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Single-threaded software will not run faster on multi-processor machines; performance improvements may stall due to lock contention; faster network connections may fail to improve throughput if the protocol performs badly with high latency.

Concept_Name: Lock contention
Core_Definition: A performance bottleneck that occurs when multiple threads or processors are prevented from making progress because they are competing for the same locked resource.
Key_Actors: Multi-threaded software; multiple processors or CPU cores; shared memory resources; locks; signaling flags or semaphores.
Operational_Workflow: Software spreads tasks across multiple processors; locks or semaphores are utilized to prevent resource collisions in memory; threads wait for access to these resources, which can limit the overall throughput of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Locking is described as complex and error-prone; it can prevent software from seeing performance improvements even when more CPUs are added to a system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: AKF Scaling Cube
Core_Definition: A conceptual model developed by Abbott, Keeven, and Fisher that categorizes scaling methodologies into three dimensions represented as x, y, and z axes. It provides a framework for scaling systems to massive proportions through replication, functional splits, and data segmentation.
Key_Actors: Abbott; Keeven; Fisher; distributed systems; x-axis (horizontal duplication); y-axis (functional or service splits); z-axis (lookup-oriented splits).
Operational_Workflow: The cube organizes scaling strategies into three distinct directions for growth. The x-axis scales by replicating the entire system to increase throughput. The y-axis scales by splitting the system into individual functions or services so that resources can be allocated to specific components. The z-axis scales by splitting the data into identifiable segments or chunks, each with dedicated resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: All methodologies for scaling to massive proportions are boiled down to these three basic options. The model requires identifying whether to replicate the whole, split by function, or split by data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many real-world scaling techniques combine multiple axes of the cube, such as using segments plus replicas (z plus x) or moving shards to different technologies based on demand.

Concept_Name: Horizontal duplication
Core_Definition: A scaling technique that increases system throughput by replicating the entire service across multiple instances. It is categorized as the x-axis of the AKF Scaling Cube.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; load balancers; resource pools; web servers.
Operational_Workflow: The service is cloned into multiple replicas that are grouped into a resource pool. Each replica is configured to handle the same transactions and produce equivalent results. Incoming traffic is typically distributed across these replicas using a load balancer, such as placing multiple web server replicas behind a single load balancing entry point.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each replica must be able to handle the same transactions resulting in the same or equivalent results. Performance improvement is proportional to the number of replicas if each transaction can be completed independently.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This method does not scale well with increases in data volume or complex transactions requiring special handling. Efficiency is lost when transactions require replicas to communicate with each other, such as when write operations must be synchronized across all replicas according to the CAP Principle.

Concept_Name: Horizontal scaling
Core_Definition: A synonym for horizontal duplication and scaling out that involves adding more resources to a pool to handle growing workloads. It focuses on increasing capacity by adding more machines, disk spindles, or network connections.
Key_Actors: Resource pools; replicas; disk spindles; network connections; machines.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators increase the capacity of a distributed system by adding additional units of hardware or software to an existing pool. This can include adding more machines to a cluster, increasing the number of disk spindles for storage, or adding more network connections for bandwidth. These added resources work in parallel to process incoming transactions.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach is part of the x-axis scaling strategy. It assumes that the work can be distributed across many similar resources. It is most effective when transactions are independent and do not require coordination between the added resources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the software is single-threaded or has bottlenecks like lock contention, adding more CPUs or processors through horizontal methods may not result in proportional performance improvements.

Concept_Name: Scaling out
Core_Definition: A term used interchangeably with horizontal scaling and horizontal duplication to describe the process of adding more replicas of a service to increase throughput. It is the opposite of scaling up, which involves using bigger or faster individual machines.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; distributed systems; resource pools.
Operational_Workflow: When a service reaches its performance limits, new instances or replicas of that service are deployed. These new instances are added to the existing infrastructure to share the total workload. This allows the system to process a growing number of transactions per second or handle more users by spreading the load across a wider set of resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scaling out is a core principle of distributed systems design because growth is expected. It falls under the x-axis of the AKF Scaling Cube. The design must be engineered to support this replication from the start.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Scaling out may be less efficient than other axes of the AKF Scaling Cube when dealing with very large datasets that cannot be easily replicated or when transaction types vary significantly in their resource requirements. Not explicitly covered in this chapter beyond its relationship to x-axis scaling.

Concept_Name: Resource pool
Core_Definition: A group of shared resources created to increase system throughput through horizontal duplication.
Key_Actors: Replicas; machines; disk spindles; network connections; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The system is scaled out by replicating the service across multiple entities; each replica is added to the shared pool; the system directs transactions to these replicas which must each be capable of handling the same transactions; the replicas process the work to produce the same or equivalent results.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each replica in the pool must be able to handle the same transactions and produce equivalent results; the performance improvement is generally proportional to the number of replicas if transactions can be completed independently.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Scaling efficiency decreases when replicas must communicate with one another; transactions that involve writing new data may require all replicas to pause future transactions until the update is successfully received by every member of the pool.

Concept_Name: CAP Principle
Core_Definition: A principle that applies to distributed systems when replicas must communicate to maintain consistency, often requiring a trade-off between availability and consistency.
Key_Actors: Replicas; primary storage; caches; main storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: When data is updated in a distributed system, the change must be communicated to all replicas or caches; the system must determine if it will prioritize consistency by pausing query processing until all nodes are updated; if perfect consistency is required, the system ensures all caches are notified of a change before allowing subsequent queries for that entry.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas must be kept up-to-date; for perfect consistency, processing of queries involving an updated entry must pause until all caches have been notified.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems that can tolerate occasional outdated information may choose to ignore the principle's strict consistency requirements; in such cases, obsolete entries might only be replaced eventually via standard cache replacement algorithms.

Concept_Name: Functional or service split
Core_Definition: A scaling strategy, also known as y-axis scaling, that involves dividing a system into individual functions or services so they can be allocated dedicated resources.
Key_Actors: Web servers; databases; application servers; dedicated machine pools; specific transaction types; batch processing systems; interactive services.
Operational_Workflow: Major system functions are identified and separated from one another; these functions are moved to separate machines or dedicated pools to eliminate competition for CPU, disk, and memory; the functions are made loosely coupled to enable them to be scaled independently of the rest of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Functions should be loosely coupled to simplify independent scaling; it is generally less complicated and less expensive to replicate a single small part of a system than the entire system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Burdensome or infrequent transaction types might be split into their own pools to prevent them from negatively affecting general transactions; traffic from web crawlers may be directed to dedicated replicas to prevent them from exhausting the cache used by regular users.

Concept_Name: Quality of Service (QoS)
Core_Definition: Network settings used to manage and prioritize different types of traffic to ensure appropriate performance for various services.
Key_Actors: Interactive services; batch processing systems; network interfaces.
Operational_Workflow: Traffic is categorized based on its requirements, such as separating interactive services from batch processing; interactive services are directed to machines or networks that use specific QoS settings to maintain low latency; these settings ensure that time-sensitive requests are not hindered by less urgent background tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lookup-oriented split
Core_Definition: A method of scaling a system by dividing data into identifiable segments that are each assigned dedicated resources. This approach, also known as z-axis scaling, focuses on partitioning the data itself rather than the processing functions.
Key_Actors: Databases; data segments; dedicated resources; root servers; domain-specific servers; users.
Operational_Workflow: The system segments data based on specific criteria such as date, geography, or hash prefixes. In a date-based implementation, a new database server is initiated whenever the current one reaches capacity; queries are then routed to the specific server containing the relevant timeframe or sent to multiple servers and combined if the query spans multiple segments. In geographical splits, user data is stored on the nearest server to provide faster access. This can also be implemented hierarchically, as seen in DNS where lookups progress from root servers down to domain-specific servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scaling on the z-axis is typically undertaken only after x-axis and y-axis scaling options have been exhausted because it often requires significant refactoring of application code. Segments can be organized by hash prefix, customer functionality, utilization, organizational division, or arbitrary groups.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a specific data segment becomes overloaded due to high access frequency, it may require further scaling using x-axis replication. Early implementations, such as Twitter's initial date-segmented architecture, faced challenges where older machines received little traffic while newer machines were overwhelmed, leading to unsustainable logistical complexity.

Concept_Name: Sharding
Core_Definition: A flexible and resilient form of z-axis scaling that partitions a database based on the hash value of database keys. It distributes data across many machines using a distributed hash table approach to ensure approximately even data distribution.
Key_Actors: Hash functions such as MD5; database keys; shards; replicas; machines; distributed hash table.
Operational_Workflow: A hash function maps database keys of varying lengths to fixed-length values. The system determines which shard will store a key by calculating the remainder of the key's hash divided by the total number of shards. Multiple small shards are often stored on a single machine to allow for variations in machine size and to compensate for uneven shard data volumes. To accommodate growth, the number of shards can be doubled, and the hash of each key is re-evaluated to determine if it should stay in its current shard or move to a new one.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using a power of 2 for the number of shards is a best practice because it optimizes the mapping process by allowing the system to simply look at the last bits of the hash. Replicas of shards must be kept synchronized while abiding by the CAP Principle. New hardware models should be benchmarked to determine usable capacity for CPU, storage, and network bandwidth before being placed into service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Performing shard expansion on a live system requires complex algorithms to manage incoming queries during the transition. If a shard update involves copying over old data, the server may be unable to respond to requests until the transfer is complete; failure during this transfer can render the shard unusable. Draining a shard to perform upgrades reduces the number of available replicas, which can cause performance to suffer if not globally coordinated.

Concept_Name: Dynamic Replicas
Core_Definition: A scaling technique where the number of system replicas is automatically adjusted in real-time based on current performance metrics and demand.
Key_Actors: Replicas; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system constantly monitors performance levels such as latency and resource utilization. If the latency becomes too high, the system adds more replicas to handle the load. Conversely, if the utilization levels are too low, the system removes replicas to optimize resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas are added and removed specifically to achieve the required performance levels.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Architectural Change
Core_Definition: A combination scaling strategy where replicas or data shards are moved between different types of hardware technology based on how frequently they are accessed.
Key_Actors: Replicas; shards; solid-state drives; traditional disks; archival media like tape or optical disks.
Operational_Workflow: The system identifies the access frequency of different shards or data segments. Shards experiencing high demand are moved to faster, more expensive technology such as solid-state drives. Infrequently accessed shards are migrated to slower, less expensive traditional disks. Very old data segments that are rarely needed are eventually moved to archival storage such as tape or optical disks.
Rules_and_Constraints: The choice of technology is dictated by current need and the frequency of access to the specific data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Cache
Core_Definition: A small data store using fast or expensive media that is intended to improve the performance of a slow or cheap bigger data store. It serves as an optimization of the z-axis of the AKF Scaling Cube.
Key_Actors: Fast and expensive media such as RAM, SSDs, or L1 CPU cache; slow and cheap bigger data stores such as disks, databases, or remote servers; software applications; cache servers; web browsers.
Operational_Workflow: When a system performs a lookup, it first checks the cache to see if the result is present; if found, the result is used immediately; if the result is not found, a normal lookup is performed from the primary storage; the result is returned to the user and is also stored in the cache so that future duplicate requests will be faster; when the cache becomes full, a replacement algorithm is used to throw away old data to make room for new entries.
Rules_and_Constraints: The cache medium must be faster than the main medium; a cache is considered a net benefit only if the time saved during hits exceeds the time lost from additional overhead; it is only cost-effective if the benefit is greater than the cost of implementation; cache size is usually fixed by a specific number of entries or a fixed amount of total storage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At startup the cache is usually empty or cold, leading to low hit ratios until it is warmed; primary storage changes can make cache entries obsolete or outdated; sudden influxes of little-used data can flush more useful data out of the cache; if a cache is too small, performance may suffer more than having no cache at all.

Concept_Name: Cache hit
Core_Definition: A successful lookup where the requested result is found within the cache.
Key_Actors: Requesting software or system; cache data store.
Operational_Workflow: The system initiates a lookup for a specific piece of data; the system checks the cache; the result is successfully found within the cache media; the result is returned and used immediately without requiring access to the slower primary data store.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In systems with multiple caches, a cache hit may potentially return outdated information if the primary storage has changed and the cache has not been updated or invalidated.

Concept_Name: Cache miss
Core_Definition: A lookup where the requested result is not found in the cache, requiring a standard lookup from the primary data store.
Key_Actors: Requesting software; cache data store; primary storage media such as disks or remote servers.
Operational_Workflow: The system initiates a lookup for data; the system checks the cache and fails to find the result; a normal lookup is performed from the slower primary storage; the result is returned to the requester; the result is stored in the cache to facilitate faster responses for future duplicate requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: When a miss occurs and the data is added to a full cache, some existing data must be discarded based on a replacement algorithm such as Least Recently Used or Least Frequently Used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A series of misses can occur during operations like database backups, which may fill the cache with little-used data and leave the cache cold; high miss rates indicate that the cache may be too small or that the cache hit ratio is too low to be effective.

Concept_Name: Cache hit ratio
Core_Definition: The ratio of the number of cache hits over the total number of lookups, also known as the cache hit rate.
Key_Actors: System administrators; performance monitoring tools; scientific analysis processes.
Operational_Workflow: The system tracks the number of times lookups are serviced successfully from the cache; the system tracks the total number of lookups performed; the number of hits is divided by the total number of lookups to determine the effectiveness; administrators use this data to evaluate performance and determine if the cache size needs to be adjusted.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ratio is used to determine if a cache is too small; it is used in mathematical formulas to estimate if a cache provides a net benefit in performance; it should be monitored and reevaluated periodically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The ratio will be very low when a cache is cold, such as immediately following a system restart; if the ratio is low even under theoretically perfect conditions with an infinite cache, it indicates that caching will not help the system scale.

Concept_Name: Cache hit rate
Core_Definition: The ratio of the number of cache hits over the total number of lookups, used to measure the effectiveness of a cache.
Key_Actors: Cache system; primary data store; system administrators or analysts who monitor performance.
Operational_Workflow: A system performs a specific number of lookups against a cache; the number of instances where the result is found in the cache is recorded as hits; the cache hit ratio is calculated by dividing the number of hits by the total number of lookups; this data is used to evaluate if the cache is performing effectively or requires resizing.
Rules_and_Constraints: A cache is considered a net benefit only if the time saved during hits exceeds the time lost from additional overhead; administrators should use a weighted average formula where the cache is effective if the hit time multiplied by the ratio plus the miss time multiplied by the remaining ratio is less than the normal lookup time; measurements should be taken before and after cache changes to provide a baseline for comparison.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If measurements indicate a very low cache hit rate, the cache is likely too small; if a theoretical analysis of query logs shows few duplicate requests even with an infinite cache, the hit rate will remain low and a cache will not be helpful.

Concept_Name: Local caching
Core_Definition: A cache placement strategy where the software performs its own caching to avoid requesting lookups from external resources.
Key_Actors: Software application; internal cache storage; external resources or data stores.
Operational_Workflow: The software application initiates a data lookup; it first checks its own internal local cache for the required information; if found, the software services the request using the cached data; if the data is not present, the software proceeds to request a lookup from an external resource.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When multiple caches are used, such as a local cache alongside other external caches, some entries may become outdated, requiring the application of the CAP Principle for consistency.

Concept_Name: Web cache
Core_Definition: A cache located between a web browser and a web server that intercepts requests and services them when content is available in the cache.
Key_Actors: Web browser; web cache; web server.
Operational_Workflow: A web browser sends a request for a web resource; the web cache intercepts the request before it reaches the server; the cache checks its storage for the requested content; if found, it serves the browser directly; if not found, it passes the request to the web server and caches the returned result for future requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: Servers can provide a time to live (TTL) value or expiration date to indicate how long an item should be cached; however, if the administrator does not control the client software, they cannot be certain the instructions will be followed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many web browsers may ignore expiration dates sent by HTTP servers; some internet service providers may cache entries for a minimum of 24 hours regardless of specific settings, which can interfere with global load balancing.

Concept_Name: L1 cache
Core_Definition: A high-speed cache integrated into a CPU that stores data from the computer's main memory to accelerate processing.
Key_Actors: Central Processing Unit (CPU); L1 cache; main memory (RAM).
Operational_Workflow: The CPU identifies a need for data located in the main memory; it first checks the L1 cache for the required data; if the data is present, the CPU processes it at high speed; if the data is missing, the CPU must retrieve it from the slower main memory.
Rules_and_Constraints: The cache medium, in this case very fast RAM, must be faster than the main medium being cached.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Cold cache
Core_Definition: A cache state where the store is empty or filled with little-used data, resulting in a low hit ratio and slow performance.
Key_Actors: The system; the cache; database records.
Operational_Workflow: When a system starts, the cache is usually empty; the system processes initial queries with a very low cache hit ratio; performance remains slow until a sufficient number of queries are processed to populate the cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Processes such as backing up a database can fill a cache with records that are only used once; this flushes the useful data and leaves the cache cold, causing performance to suffer.

Concept_Name: Persistent cache
Core_Definition: A cache that survives system restarts by storing data on a medium that is not lost when power is removed.
Key_Actors: The system; disk storage; RAM.
Operational_Workflow: The cache stores data on a disk; when the system restarts, the data remains available for immediate use; alternatively, a system may save a RAM-based cache to disk before a shutdown and read it back into memory during startup.
Rules_and_Constraints: RAM is not persistent and is lost between restarts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Warmed cache
Core_Definition: A cache that has been populated with enough query results to significantly improve system performance and the cache hit ratio.
Key_Actors: The system; the cache; incoming queries.
Operational_Workflow: The system transitions from a cold state by processing queries and storing the results in the cache; as the cache fills with frequently accessed data, the number of cache hits increases; this process continues until the cache hit ratio is high enough to meet performance goals.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance will remain slow until the cache has been sufficiently warmed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some caches are slow to warm up, which may require the system to use persistence features like disk snapshots to avoid a period of unacceptably slow performance.

Concept_Name: Redis
Core_Definition: A caching system that stores data in RAM to support high-performance web sites and databases.
Key_Actors: Redis servers; Stack Exchange web sites; databases.
Operational_Workflow: Redis caches database information in RAM to provide fast access; it can snapshot data to disk or use a second Redis server to store a copy of the cache; upon restart, it may take ten to fifteen minutes for the cache to warm up and reach acceptable performance levels.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If Redis is restarted, its performance will be unacceptably slow while the cache warms unless its specialized features for data snapshots or replication are utilized.

Concept_Name: Cache replacement algorithms
Core_Definition: These are various methods used to manage the content of a cache when it becomes full and existing data must be discarded to make room for new information. They are used to decide which entries to remove following a cache miss and a subsequent regular lookup.
Key_Actors: The software application performing the caching; the cache storage medium; the data results gathered from regular lookups.
Operational_Workflow: A cache miss occurs during a data request; the system performs a regular lookup from the primary storage; if the cache is currently full, the system executes a replacement algorithm to identify specific data to throw away; the selected data is removed; the new data gathered from the lookup is added to the cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: Better algorithms track more usage information to improve the cache hit ratio; the choice of algorithm should be based on the specific data access patterns of the system; most algorithms are variations of the LRU, LFU, and ARC options.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Least Recently Used (LRU)
Core_Definition: An algorithm that tracks the timing of each access to cache entries and discards the entry that has not been accessed for the longest period of time.
Key_Actors: Cache entries; DNS servers; system users.
Operational_Workflow: The system maintains a record of when each cache entry was last used; when the cache reaches its capacity limit and new data must be stored, the algorithm identifies the entry with the oldest access timestamp; this least recently accessed entry is discarded; the new data is then inserted into the cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: This algorithm works well for access patterns where specific queries are repeated frequently within a short timeframe.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Data resulting from typos is noted as an example of information that rarely repeats and will eventually expire from the cache under this algorithm.

Concept_Name: Least Frequently Used (LFU)
Core_Definition: An algorithm that maintains a count of the number of times each cache entry is accessed and discards the entries with the lowest activity levels.
Key_Actors: Cache entries; video services.
Operational_Workflow: The system tracks the number of accesses for each cache entry, potentially using total counts or time-based metrics such as hourly or daily counts; when space is required for new data, the algorithm compares these access counts; the entries identified as the least active are discarded from the cache; new data is then stored in the vacated space.
Rules_and_Constraints: This algorithm is a recommended choice for scenarios where popular data tends to be accessed the most.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Adaptive Replacement Cache (ARC)
Core_Definition: An algorithm invented in 2003 that places newly cached data into a probationary state to prevent large scans of little-used data from flushing frequently used items from the cache.
Key_Actors: Newly cached data; the probationary cache; the main cache; database backup processes.
Operational_Workflow: Data that is newly added to the cache is initially placed in a probationary state; if that data is accessed a second time, it is moved from the probationary state into the main cache; during processes that read many records once, such as a database backup, the probationary cache is flushed while the main cache remains protected.
Rules_and_Constraints: This algorithm is designed to maintain performance during a sudden influx of otherwise little-used data that would typically leave a cache cold.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Most other algorithms do not perform well with sudden influxes of little-used data; database backups can specifically cause performance to suffer in other algorithms by filling the cache with data that will not be reused.

Concept_Name: Probationary state
Core_Definition: A temporary status used by the Adaptive Replacement Cache (ARC) algorithm to protect the main cache from being overwhelmed by a sudden influx of little-used data.
Key_Actors: Adaptive Replacement Cache (ARC) algorithm; newly cached data; probationary cache; main cache.
Operational_Workflow: When data is first cached, the ARC algorithm places it in a probationary state; the system monitors whether this data is requested again; if the data is accessed a second time, it is promoted out of probation and moved into the main cache; a single pass through a large dataset (such as a database backup) will flush only the probationary cache rather than the main cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data must be accessed at least twice to qualify for the main cache; the algorithm is designed to prevent the cache from becoming cold after large operations involving little-used data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Cache Entry Invalidation
Core_Definition: The process of removing or marking cache entries as obsolete when the corresponding data in the primary storage location has changed.
Key_Actors: Primary storage (database); cache; cache entries; client software.
Operational_Workflow: The system may ignore changes and let entries be replaced by standard algorithms if the system can tolerate outdated data; the entire cache may be invalidated whenever the database changes, which simplifies implementation but leaves the cache cold; the main storage can communicate directly with the cache to invalidate specific entries when data changes; the cache can automatically expire entries after a fixed number of seconds based on a timestamp; the cache can poll the server to check for entry freshness.
Rules_and_Constraints: For perfect consistency, processing of queries for an updated entry must pause until all caches are notified; frequent updates can create high workloads if there are many caches.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the cache is very small, obsolete entries might eventually be replaced via normal algorithms without a formal invalidation process; if a query for freshness cannot be processed faster than sending a full answer, polling is not effective.

Concept_Name: Time to live (TTL)
Core_Definition: A numerical value sent by a server indicating the number of seconds a specific response or data entry is permitted to be stored in a cache.
Key_Actors: DNS servers; HTTP servers; client software (such as web browsers); ISPs; cache servers.
Operational_Workflow: A server provides an answer to a query and includes a TTL value; the receiving cache records the entry and notes the expiration time; the cache services future requests for that data using the stored entry until the TTL expires; once the time limit is reached, the entry is considered invalid.
Rules_and_Constraints: The TTL value dictates the maximum duration of a cache entry's validity; HTTP servers may use expiration dates as an alternative to a second-based TTL.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the administrator does not control the client software, there is no assurance clients will abide by the TTL; ISPs often cache DNS entries for a minimum of 24 hours regardless of the TTL; some web browsers ignore expiration dates provided by servers.

Concept_Name: Hash function
Core_Definition: An algorithm used in data sharding that maps input data of varying lengths to a fixed-length, probabilistically unique value.
Key_Actors: Database keys; MD5 algorithm; shards; distributed hash tables (DHT).
Operational_Workflow: The system takes a database key and passes it through the algorithm to generate a hash; the system uses the hash (often using a modulo operation) to determine which specific shard will store the data; because the hash values are randomly distributed, the data is spread approximately evenly across all available shards; in a distributed hash table, these hashes determine the physical machine where the data is located.
Rules_and_Constraints: Small changes in input data must result in large changes in the hash value; use of a power of 2 for the number of shards allows for faster mapping by looking at the last bits of the hash.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While hashes divide keys evenly, the actual amount of data per key may vary, causing some shards to be larger than others; when doubling the number of shards, the hash of every existing key must be re-evaluated to determine if it should stay in its current shard or move to a new one.

Concept_Name: Distributed hash table (DHT)
Core_Definition: A design pattern that distributes data over many machines and uses hashes to determine the specific location where that data is stored.
Key_Actors: Databases; database keys; hash functions such as MD5; shards; replicas; machines or servers.
Operational_Workflow: A hash function maps a database key of any length to a fixed-length value; the system uses the hash value to determine which shard should store the key, often by calculating the remainder of the hash divided by the number of shards; data is stored in the assigned shard on a specific machine; shards may be replicated across multiple machines to allow each replica to process a share of the queries.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hash values must be randomly distributed so that each shard stores approximately the same number of keys automatically; the hash-to-shard mapping process often uses a power of 2 to optimize speed by looking at the last bits of the hash; small changes in input data must create large changes in the resulting hash.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Even if keys are evenly distributed, individual keys might store different amounts of data leading to imbalanced shard sizes; machines may suffer performance drops if CPU or network bandwidth is exhausted by too many shards; increasing the number of shards while a system is live requires complex algorithms to manage incoming queries during the expansion.

Concept_Name: Draining
Core_Definition: The process of unadvertising a data shard on a specific machine until no new requests are received for it.
Key_Actors: Data shards; replicas; servers; global coordination systems.
Operational_Workflow: A server stops advertising that a particular shard is available on that machine; the system waits for existing requests to finish until no new requests are being processed; the shard is then upgraded or updated with new data; once the update is complete, the shard can be advertised again.
Rules_and_Constraints: Shard upgrades must be globally coordinated to ensure that a sufficient number of replicas remain available at any given moment to maintain reliability and meet performance goals.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While a shard is being drained, the total number of available replicas for that data is reduced, which may cause system performance to suffer.

Concept_Name: Threading
Core_Definition: A technique used by operating systems to allow sequences of instructions to execute independently as subsets of processes.
Key_Actors: Modern operating systems; main thread; worker threads; CPU cores; locks or semaphores.
Operational_Workflow: A main thread receives incoming requests; the system creates a new worker thread for each individual request to perform the work and send a reply; multiple worker threads execute in parallel to utilize all available CPU cores on a machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is typically faster to switch operations among threads than among processes; if multiple threads must access the same memory resources, locks or semaphores are required to prevent resource collision.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Threading is difficult to implement because locking is complex and error-prone; there are physical limits to the number of threads a machine can handle based on RAM and CPU core limits; if a single core becomes overloaded, performance will drop for that core; extreme connection floods can still result in dropped requests even with multithreading.

Concept_Name: Head of line blocking
Core_Definition: A condition where a single large or long request at the front of a queue stalls all subsequent requests behind it.
Key_Actors: Single-thread processes; incoming queries or requests.
Operational_Workflow: A single-thread process receives a query and begins processing it; a large or complex request occupies the process for an extended period; all other requests waiting in the queue are blocked and cannot be serviced until the large request at the head of the line is completed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This phenomenon is a primary disadvantage of single-threaded processing models where instructions are executed strictly one at a time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This condition results in high latency for requests that otherwise could have been serviced quickly, leading to a painful and frustrating experience for users.

Concept_Name: Worker thread
Core_Definition: A subset of a process created by a main thread to execute a sequence of instructions independently for a specific request.
Key_Actors: Main thread; worker threads; CPU cores; system RAM.
Operational_Workflow: The main thread receives a new incoming request and immediately creates a worker thread. The worker thread performs the actual processing work and sends the reply to the requester. This parallel execution allows the main thread to stay available to receive further requests without dropping them.
Rules_and_Constraints: Thread creation must be fast to handle floods of requests; multithreaded code must utilize all available CPU cores to maximize machine use; locks or semaphores are required to prevent resource collisions when threads access the same memory; the total number of threads is limited by the machine's RAM and CPU core limits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a single CPU core becomes overloaded, performance drops for that core; connection floods can still lead to dropped requests if kernel limits are exceeded; locking is described as complex and error-prone.

Concept_Name: Queueing
Core_Definition: A scaling technique that uses a data structure to hold incoming requests until worker threads or processes are available to handle them.
Key_Actors: Master thread; worker threads or processes; queue data structure; requests.
Operational_Workflow: A master thread collects incoming requests and places them into a queue data structure. A fixed number of worker threads or processes each take a request from the queue, process it, send a reply, and then return to the queue for the next item.
Rules_and_Constraints: The number of worker threads should be fixed and remain constant to avoid overloading the machine; use fair queueing algorithms to prevent low-priority items from being starved by high-priority items; overhead from thread creation is avoided by retaining the same threads for multiple requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Bursty or intermittent traffic may overload the system or starve certain priorities if specific algorithms are not used; worker threads or processes may need to kill and re-create themselves periodically to mitigate memory leaks.

Concept_Name: First in, first out (FIFO) processing
Core_Definition: A queue management method where elements are released for processing in the exact chronological order in which they were received.
Key_Actors: Queue; requests; worker threads.
Operational_Workflow: Requests enter the queue and wait in the order of arrival. When a worker thread is ready to perform work, the queue releases the request that has been waiting the longest.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is the standard behavior for most queue data structures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Priority schemes can override FIFO by placing high-priority requests at the head of the queue; fair queueing algorithms can dynamically shift priorities to prevent starvation of low-priority items.

Concept_Name: Feeding from a queue
Core_Definition: The specific operational cycle in a queueing model where a worker thread pulls an item from a queue for processing.
Key_Actors: Worker threads; queue; requests.
Operational_Workflow: A worker thread takes a single request from the queue. It performs the necessary processing and sends the result to the requester. Once finished, the thread immediately takes another item from the queue to begin the cycle again.
Rules_and_Constraints: This workflow is typically performed by a fixed number of threads to ensure the machine is not overloaded.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Fair queueing
Core_Definition: An algorithm used within a queueing model to ensure that low-priority requests are not ignored or starved when there is a high volume of high-priority traffic.
Key_Actors: Master thread; worker threads; high-priority requests; low-priority requests.
Operational_Workflow: A master thread collects incoming requests and places them into a data structure called a queue; a fixed number of worker threads feed from this queue by taking a request, processing it, sending a reply, and returning for the next item; the fair queueing algorithm specifically manages the order of these items to prevent high-priority requests from entirely blocking lower-priority work.
Rules_and_Constraints: High-priority requests may be moved to the head of the queue; the number of worker threads remains constant to prevent overloading the machine; the algorithm must ensure every item eventually gets processed regardless of its priority level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Bursty or intermittent traffic can potentially overload the system, requiring the algorithm to dynamically shift priorities to maintain processing flow.

Concept_Name: Prefork processing module
Core_Definition: An implementation of the queueing model used by the Apache web server that utilizes a fixed population of worker processes rather than threads to handle incoming requests.
Key_Actors: Apache web server; master process; subprocesses.
Operational_Workflow: Upon starting, the Apache master process forks a specific number of subprocesses; as requests arrive, the master process distributes them to these existing subprocesses; each subprocess handles its assigned request and then becomes available for another; subprocesses are periodically killed and re-created after handling a set number of requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: Subprocesses must be configured to die and be refreshed after every n requests to mitigate memory leaks; the number of subprocesses can be adjusted dynamically based on demand; the use of processes provides isolation as a corrupted subprocess cannot affect others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The practice of killing and re-creating processes can hide underlying memory leaks and other software problems, making them more difficult for administrators to diagnose.

Concept_Name: Content Delivery Network (CDN)
Core_Definition: A web-acceleration service that caches content on servers distributed globally to deliver web pages, images, and video to users from the cache nearest to them.
Key_Actors: CDN provider; cache servers; origin web site; web browser; ISPs.
Operational_Workflow: A web site replaces its native URLs with URLs pointing to the CDN provider; when a browser requests the content, the CDN uses geolocation to identify the nearest cache server; if the content is already in that cache, it is served immediately; if the content is missing, the CDN fetches it from the native URL, stores it in the cache, and then delivers it to the user; usage trends are monitored to determine where content should be replicated.
Rules_and_Constraints: Best practice requires a software switch to easily toggle between CDN URLs and native URLs; the CDN URL must encode the native URL to allow for automatic fetching; cache servers are often colocated in ISP datacenters to reduce traffic costs between networks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: CDNs may experience outages or configuration errors requiring a quick fallback to native URLs; some ISPs or browsers may ignore expiration instructions like Time to Live (TTL) values; secret or unreleased features may need to bypass the CDN during testing to prevent premature exposure.

Concept_Name: Geolocation techniques
Core_Definition: Methods used to identify the network location of a requesting web browser.
Key_Actors: Content Delivery Network (CDN) system; requesting web browser; cache servers.
Operational_Workflow: When a request for content is made to a content delivery network, the system applies these techniques to determine the specific network location of the user; this information is then used to route the request to the geographically or network-wise closest cache server.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Colocation
Core_Definition: An arrangement where content delivery networks place their cache servers directly within the datacenters of internet service providers.
Key_Actors: Content delivery networks; internet service providers.
Operational_Workflow: A content delivery network installs its own server equipment inside the facility of an internet service provider; these servers cache content locally for users of that provider; this reduces the volume of traffic that must travel between different internet service providers.
Rules_and_Constraints: This setup is utilized because it reduces internet service provider to internet service provider traffic, which is a cost-saving measure since those providers charge each other for such traffic.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Native URL
Core_Definition: A link that points to content as it is normally served from the original web server.
Key_Actors: Original web site servers; content delivery networks; developers; users.
Operational_Workflow: The web site generates pages using these standard links to serve content from its own infrastructure; if a content delivery network is being used for the first time for specific content, it uses this link to load and cache the data from the source.
Rules_and_Constraints: Best practice is to use a flag or software switch to determine whether these or alternative links are output during page generation; systems should be designed to switch back to these easily if a content delivery network has problems or configuration errors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These links are preferred during the testing phase of a new web site or when testing secret features that should not be exposed to the world.

Concept_Name: CDN URL
Core_Definition: A link pointing to a content delivery network server that contains the encoded original address of the content.
Key_Actors: Content delivery networks; web site generation software; web browsers.
Operational_Workflow: The web site software replaces standard links with these specific links when generating HTML; when a user loads the page, the browser requests the content from the delivery network server; the network either serves the content from its local cache or fetches it from the original source encoded in the link.
Rules_and_Constraints: The link must encode the original address to make content uploading automatic and eliminate special upload steps; a software switch should be maintained to allow easy toggling between these and original links to manage outages or configuration mistakes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Private CDN
Core_Definition: A content delivery network built and operated by a company for its own use rather than using a third-party provider.
Key_Actors: Extremely large web services; internal operations and engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: A company that has reached extreme scale establishes its own datacenter space in locations all over the world; it builds and manages its own infrastructure to cache and deliver content to its users globally.
Rules_and_Constraints: This model is typically pursued once a site becomes large enough that running its own infrastructure is more cost-effective than paying a third-party service; it is used to achieve higher levels of uptime and performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 6. Design Patterns for Resiliency
Section: Chapter 6. Design Patterns for Resiliency
============================================================

Concept_Name: Resiliency
Core_Definition: Resiliency is a system’s ability to constructively deal with failures by detecting them and routing around them. It allows a system to survive and continue providing service rather than falling down when faced with a malfunction.
Key_Actors: Software systems; hardware components like CPUs and disks; virtual or physical machines; users; operations personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The system assumes failure will happen and uses mechanisms to detect malfunctions; once a failure is detected, the system reacts and responds intelligently to route around the issue; the system continues to provide service to users while decoupling the component failure from a user-visible outage; in distributed systems, software intelligence is used to manage and mark unusable hardware components like failed RAM so processes can be restarted automatically.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be built with spare capacity or redundant units that can fail independently; software solutions are favored over hardware because they are more economical and malleable; a one in a million failure must be treated as a daily occurrence in large systems; N plus 1 redundancy is considered the minimum for a service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system lacks enough spare capacity during a double failure at peak usage time, the remaining servers become oversubscribed and may face more load than they are designed to handle; traditional software often fails this concept by assuming a perfect malfunction-free world.

Concept_Name: Graceful degradation
Core_Definition: Graceful degradation is a design strategy where software survives failures or periods of high load by providing reduced functionality.
Key_Actors: Software; user-facing services; movie streaming services; internet connections.
Operational_Workflow: The software detects a failure or a period of high load such as an overloaded internet connection; it automatically reduces certain functionalities to conserve bandwidth or resources; for example, a streaming service might reduce video resolution; the system continues to operate in this limited state to ensure some level of service is still provided to the user.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is the primary strategy for dealing with temporary periods of high load in user-facing services; functionality must be reduced automatically to survive the load.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: defense in depth
Core_Definition: Defense in depth is a strategy where all layers of the design are engineered to detect and respond to failures.
Key_Actors: Software design layers; single processes; systems; datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: Designers implement detection and response mechanisms across every layer of the system; detection occurs for failures as small as a single process; detection also occurs for failures as large as an entire datacenter; each layer independently responds to the failure to ensure overall system survival.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every layer of the design must participate in both detecting and responding to malfunctions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: dependency analysis
Core_Definition: Dependency analysis is a predictive strategy for achieving reliability by verifying that each system depends on high-quality parts and calculating the predicted reliability of the entire system.
Key_Actors: Manufacturers; components; systems; engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Manufacturers calculate the reliability of their individual components and publish mean time between failure (MTBF) ratings; engineers perform an analysis of all dependencies within the system; engineers verify that the system depends on high-quality parts; the MTBF for the entire system is predicted based on the collected data.
Rules_and_Constraints: The MTBF of the system is only as high as that of its lowest-MTBF part; this is considered an older and more expensive strategy compared to software-based resiliency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Outages will still occur even when this strategy is pursued, though they will be rare.

Concept_Name: mean time between failure (MTBF)
Core_Definition: A reliability rating calculated and published by manufacturers for components to predict how long a part will function before failing. It is also used to predict the overall reliability of a system based on its internal dependencies.
Key_Actors: Manufacturers who calculate and publish ratings; components and hardware parts; systems engineers who perform dependency analysis.
Operational_Workflow: Manufacturers determine the reliability of individual components and publish these as MTBF ratings; engineers perform a dependency analysis on the entire system; the MTBF for the complete system is predicted based on the lowest-MTBF part within that system.
Rules_and_Constraints: A system is only as reliable as its lowest-MTBF part; MTBF is used in predictive strategies to verify that each system depends on high-quality parts; the probability of an outage occurring during a repair window is calculated as the reciprocal of the mean time between failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In large distributed systems even a high MTBF like one million hours results in frequent daily failures because the scale of the system magnifies the probability of occurrence; the probability of a second failure happening while the first is being repaired increases significantly if the mean time to repair is high relative to the MTBF.

Concept_Name: Outage
Core_Definition: A lack of service that is visible to the user. In resilient systems, this is decoupled from the failure of individual components.
Key_Actors: Users who experience the lack of service; components or subsystems whose failure might trigger the event; resilient systems designed to hide these events from users.
Operational_Workflow: A component, machine, or subsystem fails; in a traditional system this failure results in an immediate loss of service for the user; in a resilient system the failure is detected and the system routes around it; the user continues to receive service despite the underlying component failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resilient systems must decouple component failure from user-visible outages; outages can result from physical failures, software crashes, human errors, or unexpected load; a service is traditionally monitored to determine if it is up or down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Scheduled maintenance windows can cause random connectivity losses or outages; double failures in an N plus M redundant system can result in an oversubscribed state where capacity is insufficient to meet peak demand, leading to a visible lack of service.

Concept_Name: Failure
Core_Definition: A state in which a system, subsystem, or component has stopped working. It is considered a fact of life in large-scale systems and can occur at any level of the environment.
Key_Actors: Systems, subsystems, and components; software processes; hardware devices such as CPUs, RAM, and disks; humans who cause errors or typos.
Operational_Workflow: A component or software process stops functioning; a resilient system detects the failure; the system removes the failed unit from service; processing is routed to surviving replicas or spare capacity so the overall system continues to provide service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Failure must be assumed as a certainty in large systems rather than avoided solely through better hardware; resilient systems must be designed to react and respond intelligently to failure; failures are categorized into physical failures, attacks, human errors, and unexpected load.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failures often cluster together, such as when old solder joints crack or multiple components from the same manufacturing batch reach their mortality curve simultaneously; a one in a million failure becomes a daily occurrence in systems with millions of parts; failures in master service machines can be more critical because their information may not be replicated.

Concept_Name: Malfunction
Core_Definition: A term used interchangeably with failure to describe components or systems that have stopped working properly. It encompasses issues at the component, device, system, and regional levels.
Key_Actors: Electronic parts and chips; hard drives and motherboards; computers and network equipment; regional infrastructure such as power grids and datacenters; humans who commit typos or malicious attacks.
Operational_Workflow: A malfunction occurs at any level from a single chip to an entire region; a resilient system assumes the malfunction will happen and uses software intelligence to detect it; the system then works around the malfunction to ensure the service survives.
Rules_and_Constraints: Malfunctions are a part of every environment and happen eventually; resilient systems are responsive to malfunctions rather than just avoiding them; the traditional approach to computing assumes a malfunction-free world which does not exist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Humans cause malfunctions through actions ranging from accidental cable disconnections to intentional malicious attacks; regional malfunctions can be caused by natural disasters or large-scale power grid failures.

Concept_Name: Server
Core_Definition: Software that provides a function or API. It is specifically defined as a software entity rather than a piece of hardware.
Key_Actors: Software applications; API consumers; process watchers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Servers are intended to be long lived and provide functions or APIs continuously; they are often replicated so that multiple instances respond to the same queries and provide equivalent results; automated process watchers monitor these software instances to ensure they remain running and restart them if they crash.
Rules_and_Constraints: Servers should be treated as malleable software that is easier to fix, upgrade, and replace than hardware; they should be monitored for both crashes and hangs; restarts should be automated but must include escalation thresholds to prevent wasting CPU time on persistent failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A regular crash occurs when the OS kills the process for prohibited actions; a panic occurs when the software intentionally exits due to detected internal corruption; software hangs or infinite loops can stop a server from processing requests; a query of death can cause a server to crash or hang.

Concept_Name: Service
Core_Definition: A user-visible system or product composed of one or more servers.
Key_Actors: End users; software servers; load balancers; operations personnel.
Operational_Workflow: A service is built using one or more server replicas to provide capacity and resiliency; traffic is distributed across these replicas typically via a load balancer; operations teams monitor the total capacity of the service rather than just an up or down status to determine if more units need to be added.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should decouple component failures from user-visible outages; they should be built with spare capacity using N+M redundancy where N is the required units and M is the extra; N+1 is the minimum redundancy for any service; user-facing services often require at least N+2 diversity across failure domains.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An outage is defined as a user-visible lack of service; oversubscription occurs when a service has less capacity available than needed due to multiple failures; services can be targeted by denial-of-service or scraping attacks; load shedding may be required to turn away some users during extreme overload.

Concept_Name: Machine
Core_Definition: A virtual or physical machine that hosts software and hardware components.
Key_Actors: CPUs; RAM; disks; power supplies; operating systems; automated reinstallation systems.
Operational_Workflow: Machines provide the underlying processing power for servers; they can be automatically power cycled to resolve certain issues or undergo automated OS reinstallation; in large systems, machines can be allocated from a shared pool of idle units to handle traffic surges.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resilient systems often use commodity machines because software intelligence handles failures more economically than expensive hardware; machines should be monitored for component-level malfunctions even if the machine remains operational; periodic reboots are recommended to ensure machines can successfully boot after power outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Physical malfunctions can occur at the component, device, or system level; machines may fail to boot after power outages due to marginal power supplies; kernel hangs may occur which require a watchdog timer for a hardware-level reboot; entire racks or datacenters containing many machines can fail simultaneously.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: Queries per second, which usually measures how many web hits or API calls are received by a system every second.
Key_Actors: Users; server replicas; monitoring systems; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: QPS is used to measure the workload and the capacity of individual replicas; total system capacity is calculated by determining how much QPS the combined replicas can handle; this measurement is used to determine if the system has sufficient spare capacity to survive failures during peak usage times.
Rules_and_Constraints: Peak QPS levels are used to determine the required number of units (N) for a service; the system remains resilient only if the surviving replicas can handle the total QPS after a failure; load balancer utilization must stay low enough that the loss of a replica does not lead to an oversubscribed state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Traffic surges can cause the QPS load to exceed the capacity of active replicas; denial-of-service attacks involve sending a large volume of queries to intentionally exhaust QPS capacity; a query of death may involve a single request that triggers high internal QPS across many servers due to fan-out.

Concept_Name: redundant array of inexpensive [independent] disks (RAID)
Core_Definition: A traditional hardware-based strategy that uses multiple disks to create a redundant storage system, allowing software to function as if disk failures do not occur.
Key_Actors: Hardware systems engineers; software developers; disks; RAID controllers; file systems like ZFS, BTFS, and Hadoop HDFS.
Operational_Workflow: Data is distributed across multiple disks so that the system can survive the malfunction of individual components; software assumes a perfect file system while the underlying RAID layers manage the actual disk malfunctions; when a disk fails, the system often undergoes a rebuild process to restore redundancy.
Rules_and_Constraints: RAID level 1 or higher is used to achieve N + 1 redundancy or better; it is recommended for strategic use such as on master service machines that store critical non-replicated configuration; it is not always needed for data nodes if the software layer manages replication.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Internal firmware can be difficult to configure without interrupting service; rebuild times can be long and slow, during which the system remains at N + 0 and is at risk of a second failure; RAID is unnecessary if using distributed file systems that provide their own redundancy functionality.

Concept_Name: replicas
Core_Definition: Duplicate servers or service units that respond to the same queries and provide equivalent results to ensure service continuity.
Key_Actors: Web servers; load balancers; primary and secondary replicas; client software.
Operational_Workflow: A service is divided into multiple units that share the workload or act as standbys; load balancers distribute traffic among active replicas; when a failure is detected, the failed replica is removed from service and its load is shifted to the surviving duplicates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas should fail independently of one another; they can be configured as active-active load sharing pairs or active-passive master-slave pairs; they are often distributed across different failure domains like racks or datacenters to ensure diversity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If too many replicas fail at once the remaining units may become oversubscribed and overloaded; adding new replicas in some systems can flood the network with data replication tasks; fixing a hung replica and returning it to service is sometimes more efficient than creating a brand-new replica from scratch.

Concept_Name: N + M redundancy
Core_Definition: A resiliency strategy where N units provide the required capacity and M units provide extra spare capacity to survive failures.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; discrete system units; monitoring systems; operations personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The system is designed with N units to handle the expected peak load plus M additional units for insurance; failures are detected and the affected units are removed from service; the system continues to operate at reduced total capacity using the remaining spare units.
Rules_and_Constraints: Selecting the granularity of the unit helps manage cost efficiency; the amount of redundancy is determined by calculating the mean time to repair and the mean time between failures; N + 2 is recommended if a second outage is likely to occur while the first is being fixed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the number of failed units exceeds M, the system becomes oversubscribed and may no longer be able to handle the traffic; the system is no longer monitored as simply up or down but rather by the amount of remaining capacity relative to the danger zone.

Concept_Name: N + 1 redundancy
Core_Definition: A specific form of redundancy where a system has enough spare capacity to survive the failure of exactly one discrete unit.
Key_Actors: Servers; disks; power supplies; network interfaces; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The system is built with one extra unit beyond what is needed for peak capacity; when a failure occurs, the load is rebalanced across the surviving N units; the system then operates in an N + 0 state until the failed unit is repaired or replaced.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is the minimum redundancy requirement for a service to be considered fault tolerant; it is used for hardware components like redundant power supplies and network links as well as software replicas; for disks, it is achieved through RAID level 1 or higher.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A second failure occurring before the first is repaired will lead to an oversubscribed system or data loss; if the remaining N units are at 100 percent utilization after a failure, the system is at extreme risk; the presence of a load balancer does not guarantee N + 1 redundancy if the total traffic exceeds what the surviving units can handle.

Concept_Name: N + 2 redundancy
Core_Definition: A system design that includes N units required to provide service capacity plus two additional units of spare capacity to survive two simultaneous failures.
Key_Actors: Units; replicas; servers; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The system starts with a total number of units equal to N plus two; if two replicas fail simultaneously they are detected and taken out of service; the load is then balanced over the remaining N units to maintain sufficient capacity for peak times.
Rules_and_Constraints: N represents the units needed for capacity while the number two represents the extra capacity; units must be the smallest discrete system providing the service; N + 2 is recommended if a second outage is likely to occur while fixing the first one.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If three units fail the system becomes oversubscribed; the system remains operational after two failures but loses its redundancy buffer.

Concept_Name: oversubscribed
Core_Definition: A state where a system has less capacity available than is needed to handle the current query load or traffic.
Key_Actors: Replicas; servers; users; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: A system experiences multiple failures that exceed its spare capacity; the remaining active servers are faced with more traffic than they are designed to handle; the system must then employ techniques such as load shedding or graceful degradation to manage the excess demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: Occurs when the number of failed units is greater than the number of spare units available; it can be described using a ratio of needed replicas to existing replicas such as 3:2.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Oversubscription might not lead to a service impact if it occurs during off-peak hours with low user demand; if it happens at peak usage time it may cause the remaining servers to cease functioning.

Concept_Name: double failure
Core_Definition: The occurrence of a second failure within a system before the first failure has been successfully repaired or replaced.
Key_Actors: Redundant units; subsystems; machines; repair staff.
Operational_Workflow: A system starts with a redundancy level such as N + 1; an initial failure occurs and reduces the system to N + 0; a second failure happens during the repair window which results in the system becoming oversubscribed or experiencing machine death.
Rules_and_Constraints: The probability of a double failure is determined by the mean time to repair divided by the mean time between failures; in an N + 1 system a double failure results in a lack of necessary capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A system configured with N + 2 redundancy can survive a double failure without a user-visible outage; a double failure in certain configurations like RAID can lead to permanent data loss.

Concept_Name: mean time to repair (MTTR)
Core_Definition: The time it takes to repair or replace the capacity that has gone down.
Key_Actors: Processes; hardware components; servers; repair personnel; shipping and delivery services.
Operational_Workflow: A failure occurs and is detected by the system; the repair process is initiated which may involve automated restarts for software or ordering and installing parts for hardware; the process is complete once the unit is restored to active service.
Rules_and_Constraints: MTTR is used as a factor to calculate the likelihood of a second failure occurring during the repair window; it is affected by the speed of automated systems or the physical distance of technicians to the hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A process that needs a restart has a very fast MTTR while a server in a distant datacenter may take a month to repair; in a RAID configuration the system remains in a vulnerable state until a long rebuild time is finished even after the physical disk is replaced.

Concept_Name: N + 0
Core_Definition: A system state where the number of units providing a service exactly matches the capacity required to handle the load, leaving no remaining redundancy.
Key_Actors: Replicas; servers; monitoring systems; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A system is initially built with spare capacity such as N + 1 redundancy; a failure occurs in one unit of capacity; the failed unit is taken out of service; the system continues to run using the remaining N units; the system is now in an N + 0 state where it is still operational but any additional failure will result in the system being oversubscribed.
Rules_and_Constraints: N represents the smallest discrete units required to provide the service capacity; systems in this state must be monitored closely because they have no redundancy; the goal of resilient design is to avoid staying in an N + 0 state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a failure occurs while a system is in an N + 0 state, the system becomes oversubscribed, meaning there is less capacity available than needed to handle the load; if this occurs at peak usage time, the remaining servers may be faced with more traffic than they are designed to handle.

Concept_Name: load sharing
Core_Definition: A strategy where all redundant units are active and share the workload and spare capacity approximately equally.
Key_Actors: Active replicas; load balancers; network links; client-side libraries.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple replicas are deployed to provide a service; a load balancer or client-side library distributes incoming queries or API calls across all active replicas; each replica processes a portion of the total traffic while maintaining a portion of the total spare capacity; if one replica fails, the load balancer detects the failure and redistributes the traffic among the surviving active replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Replicas should share the workload and spare capacity approximately equally; for the system to be resilient, the total utilization across all replicas must be low enough that the surviving units can absorb the load of a failed unit; active-active and master-master are terms sometimes used to describe this configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the replicas are running at high utilization, such as two machines at 80 percent, a failure will lead to the survivor being 160 percent utilized and potentially ceasing to function; some network load sharing algorithms may cause packets to arrive out of order.

Concept_Name: primary and secondary replicas
Core_Definition: A redundancy configuration where a single primary unit handles the entire workload while one or more secondary units remain ready to take over if the primary fails.
Key_Actors: Primary replica or master; secondary replica or slave; load balancers; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system designates one unit as the primary to receive all traffic; secondary replicas are connected to the system and kept in a standby state; a monitoring system or the secondary itself tracks the health of the primary; upon detection of a primary failure, a failover occurs and a secondary replica becomes the active primary to continue the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is often referred to as a 1 + M configuration; there is typically only one master or active unit at a time; the secondary must be ready to take over at any time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In a simple failover, any TCP connections that were in flight at the time of the primary's failure are disconnected because the primary was unaware of them; stateful failover must be implemented to exchange connection information between replicas to prevent this loss.

Concept_Name: hot spare
Core_Definition: A redundancy strategy where a spare unit is connected to the system and running so that it can be switched into operation instantly.
Key_Actors: Primary unit; spare unit; heartbeat messages; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A spare replica is powered on and connected to the system; the primary replica receives the entire workload while the spare remains in a standby mode; the spare or a load balancer monitors the health of the primary using heartbeat messages; if the primary fails or a loss of heartbeat is detected, the spare is switched into active operation instantly.
Rules_and_Constraints: The spare must be hot, meaning it is currently running and connected to the system; it is also known as a hot standby, active-passive, or master-slave configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unless the system uses stateful failover, the switch to a hot spare will result in the loss of all in-flight TCP connections; the system remains vulnerable if the hot spare itself fails while the primary is also down or being repaired.

Concept_Name: hot standby
Core_Definition: A strategy where a spare replica is connected to the system and running so that it can be switched into operation instantly.
Key_Actors: Primary replica; secondary replica.
Operational_Workflow: The primary replica receives the entire workload while the secondary replica is kept running in a hot state; the secondary is prepared to take over operations immediately if the primary unit fails.
Rules_and_Constraints: The spare must be connected and running to be considered hot; this approach is used in situations where replicas are not sharing the workload equally.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: active–passive
Core_Definition: A redundancy arrangement where one primary replica handles the entire workload and a secondary replica remains ready to take over at any time.
Key_Actors: Primary replica; secondary replica; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The primary replica receives all traffic while the secondary remains passive; if a loss of heartbeat is detected from the primary, the secondary takes over and becomes the active unit.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is also known as a hot standby or master-slave pair; it is the opposite of active-active or load-sharing configurations where all replicas are active.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In simple failover versions of this strategy, any TCP connections that were in flight are disconnected during a handoff because the secondary is unaware of them.

Concept_Name: master–slave pair
Core_Definition: A configuration where one primary unit acts as the master and one or more secondary units act as slaves.
Key_Actors: Master (primary replica); one or more slaves (secondary replicas).
Operational_Workflow: The master replica handles the entire workload; the slave units serve as secondary replicas that are ready to take over at any time; this is often used in the database world or for tightly coupled systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: These setups are defined as 1 plus M configurations because there is only one master; the term is used interchangeably with hot standby and active-passive.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 1 + M configurations
Core_Definition: Redundancy configurations that consist of a single master unit and M number of secondary units.
Key_Actors: One master; M secondaries.
Operational_Workflow: A single master handles all processing or service requests; M number of secondaries are maintained as spares that provide redundancy by being ready to take over if the master unit fails.
Rules_and_Constraints: This specific configuration label is used because there is only one master involved in the pair or group.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: active–active
Core_Definition: A term used to refer to two replicas that are load sharing the workload. It is most commonly used in the context of network links.
Key_Actors: Replicas; network links; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Two or more replicas are set up to be active simultaneously, sharing the workload approximately equally and maintaining equal amounts of spare capacity. When a failure is detected in one replica, it is removed from service and the load is balanced over the surviving replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must be built with enough spare capacity to handle the load of a failed replica. For example, if two machines are at 80 percent utilization, they represent an N plus 0 configuration and cannot provide resiliency because the remaining machine would be overloaded.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the system is oversubscribed due to multiple failures, the remaining active replicas may be faced with more traffic than they are designed to handle, leading to potential system failure.

Concept_Name: master–master
Core_Definition: A configuration where two replicas are load sharing and are typically tightly coupled. This term is most commonly used in the database world.
Key_Actors: Replicas; databases; tightly coupled systems.
Operational_Workflow: Replicas work together to share the processing of queries and data, with both units being active and sharing the workload. This functions as a form of load sharing where the units are duplicates that respond to the same queries and give equivalent results.
Rules_and_Constraints: This term is preferred over active-active when referring specifically to database environments or situations where the replicas are tightly coupled.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Failure domain
Core_Definition: The bounded area beyond which a specific failure has no impact. It is used to define the scope of risk and impact within a system.
Key_Actors: Servers; racks; power circuits; datacenters; power grids; cooling systems.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers may use a prescriptive approach to design groups of servers into independent domains to ensure they rely on independent infrastructure. Alternatively, they may use a descriptive approach to reverse-engineer and determine what the failure domain has become after changes or malfunctions have occurred.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each group of servers should be engineered so that the failure domains they rely on, such as power or networking, are independent. Determining a failure domain is done within a particular scope or based on assumptions about how large an outage is considered an acceptable risk.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Moving a server to a spare machine in another rack due to a failure can alter the descriptive failure domain. Unexpected misalignments of factors like cooling or connectivity can cause an outage to exceed its intended boundaries.

Concept_Name: Unaligned Failure Domains
Core_Definition: A condition where the boundaries of different infrastructure subsystems do not match, leading to an expanded and unpredictable impact during a failure or maintenance event.
Key_Actors: Power buses; network subsystems; racks; cooling systems; cluster managers.
Operational_Workflow: In an unaligned system, a power bus might support one set of racks while a network subsystem supports a different overlapping set. If power is lost to one rack that also happens to provide power to the network subsystem, racks outside the original power group also lose connectivity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Modern datacenter designs should align all physical failure domains to a common multiple to ensure that maintenance or failures in one area do not cause collateral damage to unrelated systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These misalignments are often seen as unfair by users who suffer outages even though the repair or failure did not directly affect their specific hardware. Such misalignments are major factors that contribute to reduced system availability.

Concept_Name: software bugs
Core_Definition: Human-responsible malfunctions in code that can lead to software failures such as crashes, hangs, or unexpected behavior.
Key_Actors: Humans; software developers; software; CPU manufacturers.
Operational_Workflow: Humans introduce errors during the software development process; these errors may remain undetected until triggered by specific conditions or inputs such as a query of death; in large distributed systems, even extremely rare bugs occur frequently enough to show up as patterns in crash detection systems; as software ages and these bugs are identified and fixed, the software becomes stronger and more resilient.
Rules_and_Constraints: Large systems magnify small problems so that one in a million bugs happen daily; software solutions for resiliency are favored because software is malleable and bugs can be fixed or upgraded more easily than hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A query of death is a specific bug triggered by a particular API call that exercises an untested code path; bugs in CPUs may be so rare they are difficult to detect in isolation but become visible in distributed computing environments.

Concept_Name: regular crash
Core_Definition: A type of software failure that occurs when a program attempts to perform an action prohibited by the operating system.
Key_Actors: Software; operating system.
Operational_Workflow: The software attempts an illegal operation such as writing to memory marked as read-only; the operating system detects this prohibited action; the operating system terminates the software process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Server software is generally intended to be long-lived and expected to run until decommissioned or until a configuration change requires a manual restart.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: panic
Core_Definition: An intentional software crash that occurs when the program detects an internal error or corrupted state that cannot be safely corrected.
Key_Actors: Software; software authors.
Operational_Workflow: The software detects a situation that should not exist according to its logic; it identifies that internal data structures are corrupted; the software determines that continuing to operate would be unsafe; the software exits immediately to prevent further errors or data corruption.
Rules_and_Constraints: A panic is considered the safest course of action when a software author decides a situation is unrectifiable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automated Restarts
Core_Definition: A resiliency technique where a program called a process watcher automatically restarts software that has crashed or exited prematurely.
Key_Actors: Process watcher; software processes; humans; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: A software process crashes or exits prematurely; the process watcher detects that the process is no longer active; the process watcher immediately restarts the software to address transient problems; the watcher monitors the frequency of these restarts; if the software crashes repeatedly and meets a specific threshold, the watcher stops the restarts and escalates the problem to a human.
Rules_and_Constraints: Restarts must be automated because manual human monitoring is inefficient for systems with thousands of servers; the process watcher must have an escalation mechanism to avoid wasting CPU time on constant crash loops; a typical escalation threshold is more than five restarts in one minute.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a restart happens infrequently, such as once per hour, it may not trigger the process watcher's escalation threshold and might instead be detected by the monitoring system.

Concept_Name: process watcher
Core_Definition: A program that monitors software processes to detect if they have crashed or exited and handles the task of restarting them automatically.
Key_Actors: software processes; the process watcher program; human operators.
Operational_Workflow: The process watcher monitors thousands of processes across various servers to identify any that are down; upon detecting a crashed process, the watcher automatically attempts to restart it; the watcher tracks the number of restarts performed within a specific timeframe; if the process reaches a predefined threshold of restarts, such as five times in one minute, the watcher stops attempting to restart and moves to an escalation phase.
Rules_and_Constraints: Restarts should be automated to maintain efficiency in large systems; watchers must avoid wasting CPU time on processes that immediately crash again; developers or administrators must define specific thresholds for what constitutes an acceptable frequency of restarts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a process crashes repeatedly and immediately, the watcher must stop trying to fix it and report the issue to a human; slower restart issues, such as one crash per hour, may not be caught by the process watcher and instead are typically detected by the monitoring system.

Concept_Name: escalate the issue
Core_Definition: The process of shifting a software failure from automated recovery attempts to human intervention when a problem is deemed persistent.
Key_Actors: process watcher; human operators such as system administrators or developers; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: A process watcher attempts to automatically restart a failed software process; the watcher counts the number of times the process has been restarted within a specific time interval; if the behavior exceeds a set threshold, such as x times in y minutes, the watcher ceases restart attempts; the watcher then reports the problem to a human for manual investigation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Escalation is mandatory when automated restarts fail to improve the situation to prevent the unnecessary consumption of CPU resources; clear thresholds must be established to differentiate between transient problems and those requiring human attention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automated Crash Data Collection and Analysis
Core_Definition: A system for the centralized gathering and computational evaluation of data generated during software crashes to identify patterns and fix bugs.
Key_Actors: automated collection systems; central storage systems; software processes; machines; developers; vendors.
Operational_Workflow: When a software process crashes, the system generates a crash report; an automated system immediately collects and logs the report and associated files like coredumps before the information is lost or the machine is removed; the collected reports are stored in a central repository; the system performs data mining and analysis on the aggregated data to identify which systems crash the most or to find bugs in common software libraries and hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every crash should be logged; collection must be fast to ensure information is not deleted or lost; storing reports centrally is required to enable analysis across hundreds of machines and thousands of processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Analysis can sometimes reveal rare hardware bugs, such as those in a core CPU design, which might be reported back to the vendor for correction.

Concept_Name: crash report
Core_Definition: A collection of data and statistics generated at the time of a software's premature exit that provides diagnostic information for debugging.
Key_Actors: software processes; operating system; developers; automated collection and storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: A software process terminates unexpectedly due to an operating system violation or an internal panic; the system generates a report containing state information; the report is captured by automated tools and stored centrally; developers analyze the traceback and memory contents within the report to identify the cause of the failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: A crash report should ideally include RAM and CPU usage at the time of death; it should provide a traceback of the function call and line of code that was executing; it may include a coredump containing the contents of the process's memory.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the data in a crash report is not collected quickly by automation, it may be lost due to machine decommissioning or data deletion.

Concept_Name: traceback
Core_Definition: A detailed record included in a crash report that indicates which function call and line of code was executing at the moment a software process died.
Key_Actors: Server software; operating system; automated collection and storage systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A software process terminates prematurely due to a regular crash or a panic; the system generates a crash report containing the traceback; automated systems collect and store the report centrally before it is deleted or the machine becomes unavailable; developers retrieve the stored traceback to identify the specific code path that caused the failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Information must be collected quickly because it may be lost if the machine goes away or files are deleted; in large systems with hundreds of machines and thousands of processes, collection must be automated rather than manual.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: coredump
Core_Definition: A file generated during a software crash that captures the entire contents of the process's memory at the time of failure.
Key_Actors: Software processes; operating systems; automated collection systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: When a software process crashes, the operating system writes the memory contents to a file; automated tools detect the new coredump and move it to central storage; developers use the file as a diagnostic tool to aid in debugging the state of the software.
Rules_and_Constraints: Storage should be centralized to facilitate access across a distributed system; collection should be automated to handle the scale of hundreds of thousands of processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: data mining and analysis
Core_Definition: The central storage and systematic examination of crash reports and logs to identify patterns, engineering metrics, and underlying bugs in software or hardware.
Key_Actors: Automated collection systems; central storage repositories; engineering teams; hardware and software vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Crash reports and hang logs are automatically harvested from across the server fleet; the data is consolidated into a central storage system; analytical tools or engineers process the data to generate metrics such as which systems crash most frequently; intricate analysis is used to identify recurring patterns that reveal bugs in libraries, operating systems, or specific hardware chips; findings are reported to vendors or developers for fixes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Storing reports centrally is a prerequisite for permitting this analysis; analysis should provide useful engineering metrics such as identifying the most unstable systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In distributed systems, bugs with extremely low probabilities that would be undetectable on a single machine appear as observable patterns through analysis, sometimes revealing flaws in CPU designs that have existed for multiple generations.

Concept_Name: hangs
Core_Definition: A software failure where a program remains running but stops responding to requests or becomes trapped in an infinite loop without exiting.
Key_Actors: Server software; monitoring systems; load balancers; watchdog timers; operating system kernels.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems detect a hang by observing a lack of processed requests or by sending active heartbeat pings that fail to receive a timely reply; upon detection, the system may generate an alert, remove the replica from a load balancer rotation, or attempt an automated restart; in embedded systems or kernels, a hardware watchdog timer may automatically reboot the system if the software fails to reset a counter within a specific time limit.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hangs must be logged and analyzed to identify hardware issues, locking problems, or other bugs; returning a repaired hung replica to service is often preferred over adding a new replica if the latter requires heavy data replication that would flood the network.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A query of death can trigger a hang by exercising untested code paths that lead to infinite loops; in the Google File System, returning a repaired replica to service is a lighter-weight task than adding a new one because it only requires data revalidation rather than full replication of terabytes of files.

Concept_Name: infinite loop
Core_Definition: A software failure state where a program gets caught in a repeating cycle of instructions instead of crashing or exiting. This condition causes the software to hang and stop responding to requests.
Key_Actors: Server software; software developers; monitoring systems; queries of death.
Operational_Workflow: A software process encounters a specific code path or receives a specific query that triggers the loop; the software continues to run but fails to process new requests; monitoring systems detect the lack of responsiveness through active testing; the hang is logged and analyzed to fix underlying bugs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infinite loops are considered a form of software hang rather than a crash; they should be detected by monitoring systems to prevent long-term service unavailability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A particular API call or query that triggers an infinite loop is known as a query of death; these can be mitigated by using a banned query list or canary requests to prevent the loop from affecting multiple servers.

Concept_Name: pings
Core_Definition: Active, light-weight requests sent to a system to verify basic functionality. They are used to test if a system generates a reply within a certain amount of time.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; servers; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring system sends a light-weight request to a server; the server processes the request and returns a reply; the monitoring system verifies the reply is received within the expected timeframe to confirm the server has not crashed or hung.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pings must be designed to be light-weight; they should verify basic functionality; they can be used to passively observe request counts or actively test the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a ping reveals a hung replica behind a load balancer, the system may remove that replica from the rotation for investigation rather than performing an immediate restart, especially if adding a new replica is a heavy-weight task involving large data replication.

Concept_Name: heartbeat requests
Core_Definition: Active requests sent at a specific, periodic rate to detect both software hangs and crashes. They serve as a constant signal that a system or component is still functioning.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; servers; primary and secondary load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring entity sends requests at regular intervals to a target system; the target system responds to each request to indicate it is alive; if the monitoring entity fails to receive the expected response, it generates an error, sends an alert, or triggers a restart or failover.
Rules_and_Constraints: Heartbeats must be sent at a specific, periodic rate; they are used to detect both hangs and crashes; in load balancer pairs, the secondary uses heartbeats to monitor the health of the primary.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In a simple failover configuration, a loss of heartbeat from the primary load balancer causes the secondary to take over, though any TCP connections currently in flight are disconnected because the primary is unaware of them.

Concept_Name: watchdog timer
Core_Definition: A hardware-based resiliency mechanism that uses a clock to reboot a system if the software fails to reset a counter. It ensures a system restarts if the software or operating system hangs.
Key_Actors: Hardware clock; hardware subsystem; operating system kernels; embedded systems; running software.
Operational_Workflow: A hardware clock continuously increments a counter; software running on the system is programmed to reset this counter to zero after every successful operation; if the software hangs, the resets stop and the counter eventually exceeds a predefined value; the hardware subsystem detects the threshold and reboots the system automatically.
Rules_and_Constraints: The software must reset the counter frequently enough to prevent an accidental reboot; it is most commonly used with operating system kernels and embedded systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Enabling a kernel watchdog timer on systems like Linux can reduce the need for physical maintenance visits or expensive remote power control systems when a kernel hangs.

Concept_Name: Linux kernel watchdog timer
Core_Definition: A hardware-based mechanism used to detect and recover from software hangs or kernel failures by automatically rebooting the system.
Key_Actors: Linux kernel; hardware clock; hardware subsystem; software running on the system.
Operational_Workflow: A hardware clock continuously increments a counter while software running on the system resets this counter to zero after every successful operation; if the software hangs or stops functioning the resets cease; once the counter exceeds a predefined value the hardware subsystem detects the timeout and initiates a system reboot.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must have appropriate hardware to support the timer; it is used to reduce the necessity of physical site visits or the purchase of expensive remote power control systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Query of Death
Core_Definition: A specific API call or query that triggers an untested code path resulting in a service crash, a long delay, or an infinite loop.
Key_Actors: Users; service frontends; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: A user discovers a query that causes a service to malfunction; word of the query spreads quickly among other users who attempt it; the service or its underlying servers crash or hang; in systems with large fan-out a single query of death can kill thousands of servers simultaneously along with all other in-flight queries.
Rules_and_Constraints: The optimal long-term solution is to eliminate the underlying bug in the code although immediate mitigation is often required to maintain service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: banned query list
Core_Definition: A list of known problematic queries that service frontends are programmed to automatically reject.
Key_Actors: Frontends; human operators or administrators.
Operational_Workflow: When a query of death is identified it is added to a central list; this list is communicated to all frontends in the system; the frontends check incoming requests against the list and automatically reject any matches.
Rules_and_Constraints: The list must be easy to update and easy to communicate across all frontends to be effective as a quick fix.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This strategy is limited because it requires manual human intervention to identify and add queries to the list.

Concept_Name: canary requests
Core_Definition: A preventive measure where a potentially dangerous request is sent to a small number of test servers before being distributed to the entire fleet.
Key_Actors: Leaf servers; automated system controllers.
Operational_Workflow: The system selects one or two leaf servers to act as canaries; the query is sent to these specific servers while the rest of the servers wait; the system monitors the canary servers for a reply within a reasonable time; if the canary servers crash or hang the request is flagged as dangerous and blocked from reaching the remaining servers; if the canary servers respond successfully the query is then sent to the rest of the servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is used to achieve robustness against difficult-to-predict programming errors and malicious denial-of-service attacks; queries are sent to the remaining servers only if replies to the canary requests are received in a reasonable period of time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When leaf servers crash or hang during the process the system prevents further crashes by not sending the request to the remaining leaf servers.

Concept_Name: parity bit
Core_Definition: An additional bit stored with each byte in memory systems that enables the system to detect errors.
Key_Actors: Memory systems; the operating system.
Operational_Workflow: When writing to parity bit memory, the system counts how many 1 bits are in the byte; it stores a 0 in the parity bit if the total is even or a 1 if the total is odd; anytime memory is read, the parity is checked; if a mismatch is found, the error is reported to the operating system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Memory systems using this method store one additional bit with each byte.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This method is sufficient to detect all single-bit errors; however, it cannot detect multiple-bit errors that preserve parity; it detects errors but does not provide a mechanism to correct them.

Concept_Name: error-correcting code or ECC memory
Core_Definition: A memory system that uses two additional bits to enable the detection and correction of memory errors.
Key_Actors: Memory systems; monitoring systems; low-end chipsets; hardware components.
Operational_Workflow: The system uses two additional bits and specific algorithms to manage data integrity; it automatically corrects single-bit errors and detects multiple-bit errors; while most failures are corrected invisibly, the failures are still detected and tracked via monitoring systems; if the failures persist, the hardware component must be replaced.
Rules_and_Constraints: ECC memory uses two additional bits per byte; using ECC is recommended over low-end chipsets without parity or ECC because software-based checksumming and error correction is slow and costly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: ECC can correct single-bit errors but only detect multiple-bit errors; some hard errors can be detected but not corrected by ECC; because ECC adds 25 percent more bits to the system, the mean time to failure (MTTF) statistically becomes 25 percent worse even if errors are corrected invisibly.

Concept_Name: Hamming code algorithms
Core_Definition: Specific algorithms used by ECC memory to provide error detection and correction capabilities.
Key_Actors: ECC memory systems.
Operational_Workflow: These algorithms work in conjunction with two additional bits in ECC memory to identify and fix errors; they enable the system to correct single-bit errors and detect the presence of multiple-bit errors during memory operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Solid-state drives (SSDs)
Core_Definition: A type of physical storage device used in computers that contains no moving parts.
Key_Actors: Physical hardware components; distributed systems.
Operational_Workflow: SSDs function as data storage devices within the physical layer of a distributed system; unlike traditional disks with moving parts, they operate entirely through electronic components; data is written to blocks within the drive as needed by the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each block on an SSD is rated to be written only a certain number of times.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: SSDs wear out over time because of the limited number of writes allowed per block.

Concept_Name: ZFS
Core_Definition: A file system that provides its own RAID or RAID-like functionality to store data reliably.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The file system manages data reliability through software-based mechanisms that perform RAID-like tasks internally to ensure the integrity of stored information.
Rules_and_Constraints: When this file system is used to provide data reliability, hardware RAID controllers are not required.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: BTFS
Core_Definition: A file system that handles data storage reliability by providing its own internal RAID or RAID-like functionality.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: It functions by incorporating reliability features directly into the file system layer, which allows the system to store data safely without relying on external hardware controllers.
Rules_and_Constraints: The use of this file system eliminates the need for hardware RAID controllers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Hadoop HDFS
Core_Definition: A file system used in distributed computing environments that stores data reliably through software replication and internal RAID-like functionality.
Key_Actors: Apache Hadoop system; master service machines; data nodes.
Operational_Workflow: The system utilizes data nodes to store replicas of data files while software monitors for failures and automatically creates new replicas as needed; it also manages data placement to achieve rack diversity by ensuring at least one replica of a data block is in a different rack than the others.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data nodes are typically built without hardware RAID because the system replicates data as needed; however, RAID is normally used on master machines because they store critical configuration information that is not replicated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the critical configuration information on master service machines is lost, it is difficult to rebuild; the system must also account for potential network congestion during replication processes.

Concept_Name: hardware RAID controllers
Core_Definition: Physical components used to manage disk arrays and provide redundancy so software can operate under the assumption that disks never fail.
Key_Actors: Hardware systems engineers; master service machines.
Operational_Workflow: These controllers manage multiple disks to achieve RAID level 1 or higher, providing N plus 1 redundancy or better; they are deployed strategically on critical systems that do not have software-level replication.
Rules_and_Constraints: They should be used strategically and only where required; they are considered more expensive than software-based solutions and are often unnecessary when using file systems like ZFS, BTFS, or HDFS.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Internal firmware is often a source of frustration because it can be difficult to configure without interrupting service; even after a failed disk is replaced, there may be a long and slow rebuild time during which the system remains in an N plus zero state.

Concept_Name: Apache Hadoop system
Core_Definition: A widely used distributed computing environment that stores data reliably by providing its own RAID or RAID-like functionality through its file system.
Key_Actors: master service machines; data nodes; Hadoop HDFS.
Operational_Workflow: The system stores critical configuration on specialized master machines while distributing replicas of data across multiple data nodes; it actively monitors for failures within the cluster; when a failure is detected, the system automatically creates new replicas of the data to maintain redundancy.
Rules_and_Constraints: The first three machines must be master service machines; data nodes are added to scale capacity; the system must attempt to achieve rack diversity by ensuring at least one replica of any data block is located in a different rack than the others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While data nodes are built without hardware RAID because the software handles replication, master machines require RAID because their configuration information is not replicated and is difficult to rebuild if lost.

Concept_Name: master service machines
Core_Definition: The first three machines in a Hadoop cluster that are dedicated to storing critical configuration information for the environment.
Key_Actors: Apache Hadoop system; hardware RAID controllers.
Operational_Workflow: These machines hold the essential settings and metadata required for the Hadoop cluster to function; because this specific information is not replicated by the Hadoop software itself, these machines rely on hardware-level redundancy to prevent data loss.
Rules_and_Constraints: RAID is normally used on these machines to ensure reliability; there are typically no more than three such machines in a cluster, representing a fixed hardware cost.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the configuration information on these machines is lost, it is difficult to rebuild; these machines are considered a strategic place to spend money on expensive hardware unlike the more commodity-based data nodes.

Concept_Name: data nodes
Core_Definition: The machines in a Hadoop cluster responsible for storing replicas of data and providing scalable storage capacity.
Key_Actors: Apache Hadoop system; Hadoop HDFS; master service machines.
Operational_Workflow: Data nodes are added to a cluster whenever more storage capacity is needed; they store redundant copies of data blocks as directed by the system; if a node fails, the Hadoop system detects the malfunction and creates new replicas on the remaining functional nodes.
Rules_and_Constraints: These nodes are typically built without hardware RAID to save costs since the software manages replication; they are intended to be inexpensive units that allow the system to expand economically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a data node fails, the system must ensure that the replication process does not violate rack diversity rules; the software must coordinate the creation of new replicas to replace those lost on the failed node.

Concept_Name: redundant power supplies
Core_Definition: Hardware components available in N + 1 or N + 2 configurations that allow a machine to continue functioning if one or more power supply units fail.
Key_Actors: servers; network equipment; power systems.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple power supplies are installed in a single machine to convert electric voltages for the computer; they provide spare capacity so that the failure of an individual unit does not result in a machine-level outage.
Rules_and_Constraints: Redundant power supplies should be used strategically rather than universally; they are best utilized for systems that are not already part of a replicated service or protected by higher-level resiliency techniques; they should be used for systems that lack other forms of redundancy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Power supplies are noted as components that frequently die; in large-scale environments like datacenters, a power outage can expose marginal power supplies that are unable to provide enough juice to spin up many disks simultaneously during a reboot.

Concept_Name: Network interfaces
Core_Definition: Physical hardware components that enable a machine to communicate over a network and are subject to failure.
Key_Actors: Network interface controllers; daughterboards; machines.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple interfaces are utilized in N + 1 configurations to ensure connectivity; packets are distributed across these physical links using various algorithms; health monitoring determines if a port should be active or passive.
Rules_and_Constraints: Redundant interfaces may be configured as load sharing or active-passive; some configurations require all near-end machine connections to be plugged into the same network interface controller daughterboard.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If two physical ports share the same daughterboard, the failure of one may cause the other to fail; depending on the distribution algorithm used, packets may arrive out of order, which some protocols do not handle well.

Concept_Name: network connections
Core_Definition: The physical links and communication paths between machines and switches that facilitate data transfer.
Key_Actors: Network cables; switches; machines.
Operational_Workflow: Links are established between machines and networking equipment; multiple links are used in redundant configurations to provide resiliency against individual link or switch failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: Connections can be load sharing or active-passive; some configurations require all far-end connections to be plugged into the same switch, while other configurations allow connections to different switches to provide resiliency against switch failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Network connections often fail independently of the machine or the interface; a failure of a shared uplink can result in a rack-wide outage if all connections in that rack share a single network path.

Concept_Name: Longitudinal Studies on Hardware Failures
Core_Definition: Meticulous research conducted by Google over several years that collects failure data from a massive fleet of machines in real-world datacenter environments.
Key_Actors: Google researchers; datacenter machine fleet; monitoring and data collection systems.
Operational_Workflow: Component failure information is systematically gathered from the entire fleet of active machines over a period of years; this field data is analyzed to provide insights into actual failure patterns rather than laboratory-based predictions.
Rules_and_Constraints: These studies are prioritized over lab studies because they reflect the complex realities and hard errors encountered in large-scale distributed systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The studies found that error rates for components like memory were orders of magnitude higher than previously reported and were often dominated by hard errors that ECC could detect but not correct.

Concept_Name: Failure Trends in a Large Disk Drive Population
Core_Definition: A specific longitudinal study that analyzed a massive population of hard disks over multiple years to identify factors contributing to drive failure.
Key_Actors: Pinheiro; Weber; Barroso; hard disk drives.
Operational_Workflow: Researchers tracked various metrics including temperature, activity levels, and scan errors across thousands of drives over several years to find correlations with failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The study identifies a bathtub failure curve where disk failures tend to occur either within the first month of operation or many years later.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The study found that temperature and activity levels do not actually correlate with drive failures; however, the detection of a single scan error makes a drive 39 times more likely to fail within the following 60 days.

Concept_Name: DRAM Errors in the Wild: A Large-Scale Field Study
Core_Definition: A longitudinal study published by Google that analyzed memory errors in a large fleet of datacenter machines over a 2.5-year period.
Key_Actors: Google researchers Schroeder; Pinheiro; and Weber; a large fleet of datacenter machines.
Operational_Workflow: Researchers collected component failure information across an entire fleet of machines in a real-world datacenter environment rather than a laboratory; the data was analyzed over several years to identify actual memory failure patterns and rates.
Rules_and_Constraints: The study focused on field data from actual datacenter operations to provide insight into real-world failure patterns.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The study found that error rates were orders of magnitude higher than previously reported and were dominated by hard errors; temperature was found to have a comparatively small effect on failure rates compared to other factors.

Concept_Name: bathtub failure curve
Core_Definition: A hardware failure pattern where malfunctions tend to occur either during the first month of use or many years later.
Key_Actors: Hard disks; Google researchers Pinheiro; Weber; and Barroso.
Operational_Workflow: Components are monitored throughout their lifecycle; failure data is recorded and analyzed to identify when during the component's lifespan failures are most likely to occur.
Rules_and_Constraints: This curve was identified specifically during a longitudinal study of a large population of hard disks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: hard errors
Core_Definition: A type of memory error that Error-Correcting Code memory is able to detect but cannot correct.
Key_Actors: Error-Correcting Code (ECC) memory; DRAM; Google researchers.
Operational_Workflow: Memory systems use ECC bits to monitor for bit flips and malfunctions; when a hard error occurs, the system identifies the error as one that exceeds its correction capabilities.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: power cycled
Core_Definition: The process of turning a machine off and then back on, often with a delay, to revive it after a crash.
Key_Actors: Crashed machines; automated recovery systems.
Operational_Workflow: A machine experiences a crash; the power is turned off; a delay is observed to allow components to drain; the power is turned back on to reboot the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process can be automated; automation must be able to distinguish between a machine being truly down and a machine that is simply unreachable over the network.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a power cycle does not successfully revive the machine, the system must be manually diagnosed, repaired, and brought back into service.

Concept_Name: drains machines
Core_Definition: A controlled operational process where machines are taken out of service one by one to perform maintenance tasks.
Key_Actors: Google; machines; kernel upgrades.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves selecting individual machines within a fleet to be taken out of service; performing necessary maintenance such as kernel upgrades; rebooting the machine in a controlled manner; returning the machine to service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each machine is rebooted in a controlled way approximately every three months.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This practice reduces the number of surprises found during power outages by ensuring that components are capable of booting up successfully on a regular basis.

Concept_Name: Load balancers
Core_Definition: A software or hardware technology that distributes network traffic across multiple replicas of a service to provide scaling or resiliency.
Key_Actors: Replicas; hardware appliances; software-based programs; client-side libraries; servers.
Operational_Workflow: A load balancer receives incoming queries or API calls; it distributes these requests among a set of server replicas; the system monitors the health of these replicas to detect failures or hangs; if a failure is detected, the load balancer automatically removes the failing replica from the rotation and routes traffic to the surviving replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Load balancers provide resiliency only when used to exceed capacity, meaning spare capacity must exist to handle the load of a failed replica; a load balancer used at high utilization provides scale but not resiliency; N + 1 redundancy is the minimum for a service; N + 2 redundancy is needed if a second outage is likely during the repair window.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system is oversubscribed and a replica fails, the remaining replicas may become overloaded and cease to function; the load balancer itself can become a single point of failure if not configured in a redundant pair; client-side load balancing requires software updates across all clients to fix bugs or change algorithms.

Concept_Name: single point of failure (SPOF)
Core_Definition: A specific component within a system that, upon failing, causes a user-visible lack of service because no redundancy is in place to handle its function.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; system components; hardware devices.
Operational_Workflow: A system relies on a single device or software instance to perform a critical task; when that component malfunctions or fails, the entire service or failure domain it supports goes offline; engineers remedy this by introducing redundant units of capacity that can fail independently.
Rules_and_Constraints: Redundant pairs are frequently used as a strategy to remedy the shortcoming of having a single point of failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Redundant pairs of load balancers
Core_Definition: A configuration of two load balancers, a primary and a secondary, designed to eliminate the load balancer as a single point of failure.
Key_Actors: Primary load balancer; secondary load balancer; heartbeat messages; TCP connections.
Operational_Workflow: In a simple failover strategy, the primary load balancer receives all traffic while the secondary monitors the primary's health via heartbeat messages; if the secondary detects a loss of heartbeat, it takes over as the active balancer; in a stateful failover strategy, the two load balancers exchange state information so both are aware of existing TCP connections.
Rules_and_Constraints: Redundant pairs can be active-passive or master-slave configurations; for non-stateful load balancers, a common best practice is to home half of the services on one balancer and half on the other to minimize the impact of a failover.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In a simple failover without state sharing, all TCP connections that were in flight are disconnected when the primary fails because the secondary has no record of them.

Concept_Name: simple failover
Core_Definition: A load balancing resiliency strategy where a primary unit handles all traffic while a secondary unit monitors its health.
Key_Actors: Primary load balancer; secondary load balancer; heartbeat messages.
Operational_Workflow: The primary load balancer receives all incoming traffic; the secondary load balancer monitors the health of the primary by sending heartbeat messages to it; if a loss of heartbeat is detected, the secondary takes over and becomes the active load balancer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Any TCP connections that were in flight during the failover are disconnected because the secondary load balancer is unaware of the state held by the primary.

Concept_Name: stateful failover
Core_Definition: A resiliency strategy for load balancers that involves the exchange of connection state information between units to maintain active sessions during a transition.
Key_Actors: Primary load balancer; secondary load balancer; TCP connections.
Operational_Workflow: Two load balancers exchange enough state information so that both units are aware of all existing TCP connections; in the event that the primary unit fails, the secondary unit takes over the workload.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unlike simple failover, TCP connections are not lost in the event of a failover because the secondary unit has the necessary state information to continue the sessions.

Concept_Name: hardware appliances
Core_Definition: Physical devices that are purpose-built and highly tuned for specific tasks such as load balancing.
Key_Actors: Hardware load balancers; vendors; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: These devices are integrated into the network to manage traffic across server replicas; they are used to provide both scale by distributing capacity and resiliency by routing around failed units.
Rules_and_Constraints: These appliances are usually highly tuned and feature-rich compared to software alternatives.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hardware appliances can become a single point of failure, which often necessitates the use of redundant pairs to maintain service availability.

Concept_Name: software-based programs
Core_Definition: Load balancing solutions implemented as software running on standard computers or within client libraries.
Key_Actors: Software load balancers; standard computers; client software; service machines.
Operational_Workflow: Load balancing software runs on standard machines to distribute traffic; for smaller services, the software may run directly on the same machines providing the service; in client-side load balancing, the client software is responsible for knowing which servers are available, performing health checks, and selecting a server.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software-based programs are more flexible than hardware appliances; they are not recommended for high-volume load balancing when running on the same machine as the service because the load balancer competes for CPU and resources with the service itself.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When load balancing is pushed to the client side, fixing bugs or changing algorithms requires updating the software everywhere it is used; if the software is running on the service box, high utilization by the service can impact the performance of the load balancer.

Concept_Name: client-side load balancing
Core_Definition: A software-based load balancing approach where the client software itself is responsible for knowing which servers are available and performing health checking and server selection.
Key_Actors: Client software; client library; internal services.
Operational_Workflow: The client library loads configuration from a central place; the library performs health checking and selects a server; it load balances the requests and detects then routes around failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is frequently used for internal services because they are usually more tightly controlled; it pushes the load balancing function down the stack to the clients themselves.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: To change algorithms or fix bugs, the client library must be changed, which requires updating the software anywhere it is used.

Concept_Name: client library
Core_Definition: A specific software component utilized by clients to implement load balancing and server management logic locally.
Key_Actors: Client software; central configuration system; internal services.
Operational_Workflow: The library retrieves configuration data from a central location; it manages the health checking and selection of servers; it facilitates the detection of server failures to ensure requests are routed correctly.
Rules_and_Constraints: It allows for load balancing to be pushed further down the stack to the clients rather than using centralized hardware or software balancers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Updating the library for any reason, such as fixing bugs or changing load balancing algorithms, requires the redeployment of the software everywhere it is utilized.

Concept_Name: rack
Core_Definition: A physical steel structure used to house servers and networking equipment that typically serves as a bounded failure domain.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; top-of-rack switches; power feeds; network uplinks.
Operational_Workflow: Machines within the rack connect to a shared top-of-rack switch providing non-blocking bandwidth; all equipment in the rack shares common resources such as a single power feed or network uplink.
Rules_and_Constraints: Intentionally designing each rack to be its own failure domain is considered a manageable and effective size for distributed systems; bandwidth is plentiful within a rack but sparse between racks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While the steel racks do not usually fail, rack-wide outages occur due to shared power or network failures; intrusive maintenance is often performed one rack at a time, resulting in temporary loss of all equipment in that rack.

Concept_Name: rack diversity
Core_Definition: A resiliency design pattern where replicas of a service or data are distributed across multiple racks to prevent a rack-level failure from causing a total service outage.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; DNS servers; Hadoop data blocks; data nodes.
Operational_Workflow: A service is broken into many replicas and one replica is placed in each different rack; in systems like Hadoop, the software ensures at least one replica of a data block is stored in a different rack than the other blocks.
Rules_and_Constraints: This strategy is used to ensure that a service like DNS remains available even if an entire rack fails; it is a primary method for providing resilience against physical hardware failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: rack locality
Core_Definition: A design pattern where a service component is made self-contained within a single rack to take advantage of plentiful internal bandwidth and localized failure domains.
Key_Actors: Service components; replicas; machines within a rack; pods.
Operational_Workflow: Small queries enter the rack; machines within the rack utilize high internal bandwidth to process the data and generate an answer; a small or medium-sized reply then leaves the rack.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service components should be designed to fit within the constraints of a single rack; the model should be replicated across many racks; each replica should be self-contained to maintain the rack-sized failure domain.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: switch at the top of the rack
Core_Definition: The central networking device in a rack that provides connectivity for all machines located within that specific rack.
Key_Actors: Machines in the rack; the network switch; rack uplinks.
Operational_Workflow: Every machine in the rack connects to this single switch; the switch facilitates internal communication between machines at full bandwidth and manages outgoing traffic via uplinks.
Rules_and_Constraints: The switch must provide enough internal bandwidth to allow any machine to talk to any other machine in the rack at full speed simultaneously.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the switch or its associated power feed and network uplink fail, the entire rack becomes a failure domain and all equipment within it loses connectivity.

Concept_Name: non-blocking bandwidth
Core_Definition: A network performance scheme where a switch has enough internal capacity to allow all machines in a rack to communicate with each other at full bandwidth at the same time.
Key_Actors: Top-of-rack switch; machines within the rack.
Operational_Workflow: The switch manages internal data transfers so that no machine's communication is restricted by the concurrent data transfers of other machines in the same rack.
Rules_and_Constraints: This scheme is typically limited to the environment within a single rack; it requires the switch to have sufficient internal bandwidth to handle all potential simultaneous connections.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This level of bandwidth is generally not available between racks, where bandwidth is described as sparse and subject to contention.

Concept_Name: Rack uplinks
Core_Definition: The shared network connections that provide connectivity between a specific rack and the rest of the datacenter or external networks.
Key_Actors: All machines in the rack; the top-of-rack switch; the datacenter network.
Operational_Workflow: Data originating from any machine in the rack that is destined for another rack must pass through these shared links.
Rules_and_Constraints: Uplinks are often ten times faster than the links between individual machines; they are a shared resource typically used by 20 to 40 machines; bandwidth is considered sparse compared to intra-rack speeds.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because the resource is shared, contention for bandwidth occurs between racks; a failure of the network uplink results in a rack-wide outage.

Concept_Name: pods
Core_Definition: Rack-sized replicas that are self-contained units of a service component.
Key_Actors: Billing systems; specific groups of customers.
Operational_Workflow: A service component is designed to be self-contained within a single rack to take advantage of high internal bandwidth and rack-sized failure domains; these components are then replicated across many racks; each rack-sized replica or pod handles specific tasks such as bill processing for a designated group of customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pods should be self-contained; they often form their own security domain; they are designed to fit within the constraints of a single rack.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: security domain
Core_Definition: A bounded environment often formed by a self-contained pod within a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Pods; billing systems.
Operational_Workflow: A service component is organized into a self-contained pod within a rack; this pod functions as its own security domain while handling specific operational tasks such as customer billing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security domains are often aligned with the boundaries of self-contained pods.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Clos Networking
Core_Definition: A networking design known since 1953 that provides non-blocking, full-speed connectivity between any two machines in an entire datacenter.
Key_Actors: Network products; machines within a datacenter.
Operational_Workflow: Implementation of these network products allows for any machine in a datacenter to communicate with any other machine at full speed without blocking; the availability of such products on the open market is expected to change how services are designed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must provide non-blocking and full-speed connectivity across all machines in a datacenter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The text notes that while the theory has existed since 1953, the widespread availability of such products on the open market is a future expectation that will alter service design.

Concept_Name: Datacenters
Core_Definition: Large-scale physical facilities that house computing equipment and serve as failure domains within a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; operations personnel; ISPs.
Operational_Workflow: Replicas of a service are designed to be self-contained within a single datacenter; the overall service is then distributed across multiple datacenters to achieve datacenter diversity; this allows the system to remain operational if an entire datacenter goes offline due to power, cooling, or connectivity failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each replica of a service should be self-contained within a datacenter; services should maintain datacenter diversity; Google requires a minimum of N plus 2 diversity for user-facing services; physical failure domains like power and cooling should be aligned to a common multiple to avoid extended outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Entire datacenters can fail due to natural disasters, cooling failures, power outages, or backhoes cutting network connections; unexpected misalignment of internal domains can cause a failure in one area, such as cooling, to take out connectivity for the entire building.

Concept_Name: datacenter diversity
Core_Definition: A resiliency design pattern where a service is distributed across multiple geographic locations to ensure the entire system remains operational even if an entire datacenter fails.
Key_Actors: User-facing services; service replicas; multiple datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: A service is broken into multiple replicas; each replica is placed in a different datacenter; these replicas are designed to be self-contained within their respective locations; the system is managed so that if one datacenter goes offline due to natural disasters, power failures, or cooling issues, the other datacenters continue to provide the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each replica must be self-contained within its datacenter; the entire service should maintain diversity across multiple locations; Google requires a minimum of N plus 2 diversity for user-facing services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: One datacenter may be intentionally brought down for maintenance while another simultaneously fails due to unforeseen circumstances; large scale disasters or events like a backhoe cutting all network connections can take out an entire datacenter failure domain.

Concept_Name: datacenter locality
Core_Definition: A design principle where service components are engineered to be self-contained and operate entirely within the boundaries of a single datacenter to leverage local resources.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; individual datacenters; internal network infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: A service replica is designed to function within the high-bandwidth environment of a single datacenter; it processes queries and generates replies using the high-speed local network; this avoids the latency and costs associated with sending data between different datacenters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service replicas should be self-contained within the datacenter; bandwidth within a datacenter is generally fast while inter-datacenter bandwidth is slower and often billed for by the gigabyte.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dynamic Resource Allocation
Core_Definition: An automated strategy used to handle high levels of load by adding machine capacity to a service on demand from a reserve of available resources.
Key_Actors: Automated configuration systems; overloaded services; shared pools of idle machines; public cloud computing providers.
Operational_Workflow: The system detects that a specific service is becoming overloaded; an automated system identifies and allocates an unused machine from a pool; the automated system configures the machine for the specific service needing help; the new capacity is added to the service to resolve the overload; the service releases the machine back to the pool once the high-demand period ends.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resource allocation should be handled by automated systems; shared pools can be used for machines or storage; additional capacity can also be sourced from public cloud providers where costs are only incurred for active usage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: pool of idle machines
Core_Definition: A shared collection of running but unconfigured machines held in reserve to provide rapid capacity increases for multiple services.
Key_Actors: Idle machines; automated systems; a group of services sharing the pool.
Operational_Workflow: A group of machines is kept in a running state but remains unconfigured for any specific task; when a service in the group faces a traffic surge, it allocates a machine from this shared pool; the machine is automatically configured to support that service; after the surge subsides, the machine is returned to the pool to be available for other services.
Rules_and_Constraints: One shared pool should serve a group of services to mitigate the cost of keeping machines idle; the pool must be large enough to handle situations where more than one service becomes overloaded at the same time; services must have a specific mechanism to give back machines when they are no longer needed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the pool is not sized correctly, it may fail to provide enough capacity if multiple services become overloaded simultaneously; having idle capacity is an expense that must be balanced against the need for resiliency.

Concept_Name: shared pool
Core_Definition: A single pool of idle, running, but unconfigured machines that serves a group of services to provide dynamic capacity while mitigating the cost of idle resources.
Key_Actors: Idle machines; services within a group; automated systems for configuration; public cloud computing providers.
Operational_Workflow: A system detects that a specific service is becoming overloaded; it allocates an unused machine from the idle pool; an automated system configures the newly allocated machine; the configured machine is used to add capacity to the overloaded service; the service returns the machine to the pool once the extra capacity is no longer required.
Rules_and_Constraints: The pool must be large enough to support more than one service becoming overloaded at the same time; there should be a mechanism for services to relinquish machines when the need disappears.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: public cloud computing provider
Core_Definition: An external service provider that can be utilized as a shared pool for additional system capacity.
Key_Actors: Public cloud computing provider; services needing additional capacity.
Operational_Workflow: A service identifies a need for more capacity beyond its internal resources; it uses the public cloud provider as a shared resource pool; it allocates and pays for resources only when they are needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users usually do not have to pay for unused capacity when utilizing a public cloud computing provider.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load Shedding
Core_Definition: A strategy where a service turns away some users or postpones certain tasks to ensure the remaining users receive a high-quality experience during periods of overload.
Key_Actors: Overloaded services; users; operations personnel; databases; social networks; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system detects an overload; it identifies new users or low-priority tasks to be turned away or delayed; it provides an immediate response like a come back later web page to rejected users rather than making them wait for a timeout; it queues up tasks that can be put off until later; it eventually enables delayed systems one at a time to catch up.
Rules_and_Constraints: Rejected users should receive an immediate response; a service level agreement must be established to determine how long tasks can be delayed and what actions should be taken; operations staff must have visibility into the system, including task age and predicted processing time, to make prioritization decisions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tasks that are put off for too long can cause problems if they are delayed forever; if many systems are turned off simultaneously, they might need to be re-enabled one by one to prevent further overload.

Concept_Name: low-priority database updates
Core_Definition: Specific database tasks that can be queued and processed at a later time to reduce the immediate burden on an overloaded system.
Key_Actors: Databases; automated systems; operations personnel.
Operational_Workflow: During periods of high load, these updates are diverted into a queue instead of being processed immediately; the updates remain in the queue until the system has enough resources to handle them or until they reach a specific priority threshold; operations staff may monitor the freshness of shards via a dashboard to see which frontends are out of date.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be a plan for handling the delays associated with these updates; updates should be monitored to gauge when delayed items should be continued.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These updates might become high priority after a certain amount of time has passed; neglecting these updates forever can cause system problems.

Concept_Name: service level agreement (SLA)
Core_Definition: An agreement used to determine how long a task can be delayed and to identify a timeline of actions to mitigate problems or extend deadlines.
Key_Actors: Operations personnel; systems performing load shedding.
Operational_Workflow: An SLA is established to manage situations where tasks are delayed due to load shedding; it defines the acceptable duration for delays and triggers a timeline of mitigation actions or deadline extensions as needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Low-priority updates might become a high priority after a certain amount of time specified in the agreement; a plan must exist for any activity that is delayed due to load shedding.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If many systems are turned off due to load shedding, it might be possible to enable them one at a time to let each catch up.

Concept_Name: prioritization decisions
Core_Definition: Choices made by operations personnel regarding when to continue delayed items based on system visibility and task metrics.
Key_Actors: Operations personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Personnel gain visibility into the system to check the age of a task; they predict how long a task will take to process; they indicate how close a task is to its deadline; they use these factors to gauge when delayed items should be resumed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Management of these situations requires visibility into the system; decisions must be based on task age, predicted processing time, and deadline proximity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: quality of the search system
Core_Definition: A performance metric measured in terms of how fresh the corpus is across all web frontends.
Key_Actors: User-facing web frontend; updater system; search index corpus.
Operational_Workflow: Updates to the search index arrive in large chunks; the updater system processes these updates; the updates are distributed to each frontend; the system monitors how up-to-date the corpus remains across all frontends to determine quality.
Rules_and_Constraints: Quality is directly tied to the freshness of the corpus across the frontends.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When the system is overloaded, the updater system may be paused to free up resources for handling additional load, which reduces the quality of the system.

Concept_Name: freshness of shards
Core_Definition: The status of specific segments of the search index categorized by how recently they have been updated.
Key_Actors: Monitoring dashboard; operations staff; web frontends; shards.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring dashboard displays the status of shards in each frontend; shards are sorted into freshness buckets such as up-to-date, one hour old, two hours old, or four hours old; operations staff review the dashboard to identify when something is wrong and determine which frontends are the most out of date.
Rules_and_Constraints: Visibility into these buckets is required for operations staff to understand the effects of pausing updates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Operations staff can unpause high-priority updates during an overload to maintain at least minimal freshness.

Concept_Name: monitoring dashboard
Core_Definition: A visual tool used to display system status and performance metrics such as the freshness of data shards across different frontends.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; user-facing web front-ends; updater system.
Operational_Workflow: The dashboard displays the freshness of shards in each frontend by listing how many shards fall into specific freshness buckets such as up-to-date or various hourly intervals; operations staff monitor these displays to detect when something is wrong and identify which frontends are most out of date; during periods of system overload when the updater system is paused to free up resources, staff use the dashboard to understand the effects of the pause and decide which high-priority updates to unpause.
Rules_and_Constraints: The dashboard must provide visibility into the age of tasks and system state to permit operations personnel to gauge when delayed items should be continued.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When the updater system is paused to mitigate load, the dashboard allows staff to selectively unpause specific high-priority updates to maintain at least minimal freshness.

Concept_Name: denial-of-service (DoS) attack
Core_Definition: An attempt to bring down a service by sending a large volume of queries to the system.
Key_Actors: Attackers; frontends; leaf servers.
Operational_Workflow: Attackers send a high volume of requests or specific queries that require large amounts of resources; frontends receive these queries and may become overloaded; if a specific query is identified as causing a crash or hang, it is added to a banned query list; frontends then automatically reject any incoming query that matches the banned list.
Rules_and_Constraints: Because fixing code and pushing a new release takes a long time, a quick fix like a banned query list is required; the list must be easy to update and communicate to all frontends.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In situations where a query has a large fan-out and could kill thousands of servers, canary requests are used to send the query to only one or two leaf servers first; if these canary servers crash or hang, the system flags the request as dangerous and prevents it from being sent to the rest of the fleet.

Concept_Name: distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack
Core_Definition: A coordinated attack using many computers around the Internet to create an extremely large denial-of-service event.
Key_Actors: Attackers; botnets; ISPs; CDN vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Attackers coordinate thousands of machines, often via botnets, to flood a service with traffic; these attacks may attempt to exhaust network bandwidth or consume excessive processing time; because attackers can forge packets to obscure the source, building filters to block the attack without affecting legitimate traffic is often impossible; defense typically requires having more bandwidth than the attacker or receiving mitigation help from an ISP or CDN vendor.
Rules_and_Constraints: Simply not responding to requests still hogs bandwidth used to receive the attack; the attack must usually be blocked from outside the target network by the ISP.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the attack aims to consume processing load rather than bandwidth, a banned query list can be used to block specific resource-intensive queries.

Concept_Name: botnets
Core_Definition: Large collections of computers around the world that have been successfully infiltrated and are controlled centrally without the knowledge of their owners.
Key_Actors: Central controllers; infiltrated computers; unsuspecting owners.
Operational_Workflow: Computers are infiltrated and joined into a large collection; these machines are then used in a coordinated fashion to initiate and sustain extremely large distributed denial-of-service attacks against a target service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DDoS attack mitigation services
Core_Definition: Services used by smaller companies to protect against distributed denial-of-service attacks by leveraging the large amounts of bandwidth available through the provider.
Key_Actors: Smaller companies; DDoS attack mitigation service providers; CDN vendors; ISPs; attackers.
Operational_Workflow: A smaller company employs a mitigation service to handle traffic volumes they cannot manage internally; the service blocks the attack from outside the target's network to prevent the company's bandwidth from being hogged; the service utilizes its superior bandwidth capacity to absorb or filter the coordinated query volume from botnets.
Rules_and_Constraints: Attacks must be blocked from outside the company network because simply not responding to requests still consumes the bandwidth used to receive the attack; most ISPs do not provide this kind of filtering service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Very large companies may not require these third-party services as they are capable of using their own extensive bandwidth as a primary line of defense.

Concept_Name: CDN vendors
Core_Definition: Content Delivery Network providers that offer DDoS attack mitigation services to customers by utilizing their high bandwidth availability.
Key_Actors: CDN vendors; smaller companies; attackers.
Operational_Workflow: CDN vendors provide a service layer between the internet and a company's infrastructure; they use their distributed network and large bandwidth capacity to mitigate denial-of-service attacks; they act as a defense for smaller companies that are unable to withstand extremely large volumes of coordinated queries.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: scraping attack
Core_Definition: An automated process that mimics a web browser to query for information and extract useful data from the resulting HTML pages.
Key_Actors: Automated scraping programs; web frontends; central scraping detector service; search engine crawlers.
Operational_Workflow: An automated program sends millions of search requests to a service like Amazon; the program parses the received HTML pages and extracts specific data to build its own database; the web frontends of the targeted service report these query patterns to a detector to identify the activity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scraping is generally considered an attack because it often violates a company's terms of service and can prevent theft of information; very fast scrapers are equivalent to denial-of-service attacks and must be defended against to maintain service availability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some scraping is considered permitted or even desired, such as the activity performed by search engine crawlers.

Concept_Name: scraping detector service
Core_Definition: A central service that analyzes query information reported by web frontends to identify and warn against suspected automated scraping activity.
Key_Actors: Central scraping detector service; web frontends; suspected attackers; humans; permitted agents such as search engine crawlers.
Operational_Workflow: All web frontends report data about the queries they receive to the central detector service; the detector identifies suspected attacks and sends warnings back to the frontends; frontends block queries if confidence in the attack is high or issue a challenge such as a Captcha if confidence is low.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service must maintain a whitelist that permits search engine crawlers and other authorized agents to perform their work; the system must distinguish between human and machine input when attack confidence is not high enough for an outright block.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the detector has low confidence that a source is an attacker, the frontends may respond with a Captcha to verify if the user is a human rather than immediately refusing the connection.

Concept_Name: Captcha
Core_Definition: A system used to distinguish human from machine input by asking the user to prove they are human.
Key_Actors: users; frontends; central scraping detector service.
Operational_Workflow: Frontends report information about all received queries to a central scraping detector service; the detector identifies and warns the frontends of suspected scraping attacks; if the detector has low confidence in the source of an attack, the frontends are instructed to challenge the user; the user must interact with the Captcha to prove they are a human rather than an automated program.
Rules_and_Constraints: Captchas should be used when confidence in the source of a suspected scraping attack is low; the system is used as a defense to distinguish human input from machine input.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: whitelist
Core_Definition: A list maintained within a scraping detector that permits search engine crawlers and other desired agents to perform automated scraping tasks.
Key_Actors: scraping detector; search engine crawlers; permitted agents.
Operational_Workflow: The central scraping detector receives query information from frontends and monitors for scraping activity; the detector checks the source of suspected scraping against its whitelist; if the agent is found on the whitelist, the detector allows the agent to continue its automated work without blocking it.
Rules_and_Constraints: A scraping detector should have a whitelist to ensure that desired scraping and permitted agents are allowed to perform their jobs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: search engine crawlers
Core_Definition: Automated agents that scrape information from web pages and are categorized by the system as permitted or desired scrapers.
Key_Actors: search engine crawlers; scraping detector; whitelist.
Operational_Workflow: Crawlers send automated queries to frontends to extract information from HTML pages; the scraping detector identifies the behavior as scraping; the detector references the whitelist to identify the crawler as a permitted agent; the crawlers are allowed to continue their job because they are recognized as desired.
Rules_and_Constraints: Search engine crawlers should be included on a whitelist within the scraping detector to prevent them from being blocked.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: human error
Core_Definition: Malfunctions caused by humans including typos, software bugs, physical accidents like kicking a power cable, or intentional malicious attacks.
Key_Actors: humans; automation systems; pre-check systems; source code control systems; second-pair-of-eyes reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: Operational practices are established to exercise skills and behaviors; automation is implemented to remove humans from the loop and ensure consistent error checking; a human attempts a task such as a configuration change; a pre-check automated system validates the input and prevents the process from running if tests fail; a second human reviews and approves the change within a source code control system before it is accepted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Humans should be removed from the loop through automation; pre-checks should verify that configuration files have no syntax errors and meet quality criteria; large changes based on input size or output growth require additional approval; all changes should be checked by a second human.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Pre-checks tend to lag behind experience because they are often added to the system only after an outage has occurred to prevent future repetitions of a specific error.

Concept_Name: operational practices
Core_Definition: Strategies aimed at improving human performance and reducing errors by exercising specific skills and behaviors.
Key_Actors: Humans; operations personnel; systems administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Identifying skills and behaviors that most need improvement and implementing practices that exercise those specific areas to ensure humans are better prepared to manage systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational practices are categorized as a strategy for dealing with human error by making the people involved more effective.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: automation
Core_Definition: Software-based mechanisms designed to perform tasks without human intervention and consistently check work to reduce the likelihood of error.
Key_Actors: Process watchers; automated crash data collection systems; automated restart programs; software-based load balancers; automated OS installation systems.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring processes for failures; automatically restarting software when it crashes or hangs; collecting and storing crash reports and memory coredumps centrally for data mining; automating software upgrades to introduce features frequently; and automatically re-installing operating systems on failed machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation will always check its work once written; software upgrades should be automated to allow for frequent replacement; process watchers should follow escalation thresholds such as x restarts in y minutes to avoid wasting CPU time on repeated crashes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automation must be able to distinguish between an unreachable machine and a machine that is actually down; if an automated restart fails repeatedly the issue must be escalated to a human.

Concept_Name: pre-check
Core_Definition: Automated tests that evaluate inputs and quality criteria to prevent a process from running if the tests fail.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; humans providing inputs or configuration changes; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: Verifying configuration files for syntax errors and quality criteria before use; measuring the size of changes or the growth of output files; blocking the implementation of changes that fail tests; and triggering a requirement for additional human approval if a change is unusually large.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pre-checks are intended to prevent human errors from causing outages; they can be based on the number of changed lines or the percentage growth of output compared to previous versions; a failed pre-check prevents a configuration file from being put into use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Pre-checks tend to lag behind experience and are often added reactively after an outage has occurred to prevent that specific human error from recurring.

Concept_Name: source code control systems
Core_Definition: Systems used for managing changes to files and software that can enforce peer review and approval policies.
Key_Actors: Users; second users acting as approvers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Performing system administration tasks by making changes to files stored within a repository; submitting those changes for review; and requiring a second user to approve the changes before the system accepts them.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems can be configured to not accept changes until a second user approves them; this ensures a second pair of eyes checks all administrative changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: source code repository
Core_Definition: A source code control system used to store and manage files that are used for system administration tasks.
Key_Actors: Users who perform system administration; second users who act as approvers; Google.
Operational_Workflow: System administration is conducted by making changes to files stored within the repository; the source code control system holds the changes and prevents them from being accepted immediately; a second user reviews the proposed changes; once the second pair of eyes approves the work the changes are accepted into the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system may be configured to require that a second user approves any changes before they are accepted; all system administration performed via the repository must be checked by two people.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 7. Operations in a Distributed World
Section: Introduction
============================================================

Concept_Name: operations
Core_Definition: The work done to keep a system running in a way that meets or exceeds operating parameters specified by a service level agreement. It includes all aspects of a service’s life cycle from initial launch to the final decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; Site Reliability Engineers (SRE); developers; product management; stakeholders; oncall teams; ticket duty personnel; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with a service launch often guided by a readiness checklist and lead by a launch lead. Once live, the team manages the service through a rotation where specific members handle emergency oncall duties and others handle ticket duty for normal customer requests. The team performs ongoing maintenance, software and hardware upgrades, and project work focused on automation. Finally, the service is decommissioned through the removal of users, deallocation of resources, and disposal of hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation is a requirement because manual processes do not scale in distributed environments. SRE operational load should be capped at 50 percent to limit manual toil. Operations must balance the desire for stability with the developer desire for change by aligning goals around service level agreements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Human processes such as stakeholder relationships or evaluating new technology cannot be fully automated but can be streamlined. Rare or infrequent tasks may not be worth the investment of automation and are managed using documented playbooks. Emergency outages can interrupt planned project work and require immediate intervention.

Concept_Name: service level agreement (SLA)
Core_Definition: A specification of how reliable a system is going to be, providing the operating parameters the service must meet. It serves as the standard by which all operational work is ultimately measured.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; Site Reliability Engineers; developers; product management; engineering management.
Operational_Workflow: All services are assigned an SLA during or before launch. Performance is then measured and reported against these metrics on an ongoing basis. When instability occurs, it consumes a portion of a calculated Error Budget; if the budget is exhausted, launches may be gated until stability is restored. Any major event that violates the SLA triggers a formal postmortem process to identify root causes and prevent recurrence.
Rules_and_Constraints: All services must have an SLA. Operations staff are typically rewarded for achieving compliance with SLAs related to uptime. Aligning the goals of developers and operations requires both parties to be responsible for SLA compliance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Major SLA-related events or outages require a written postmortem report that focuses on process and technology rather than blame. If engineering management prioritizes new features over the bug fixes needed to maintain the SLA, the system may destabilize and spin out of control.

Concept_Name: availability
Core_Definition: A focus of operational work centered on maintaining demanding 24 by 7 uptime requirements. It is a key component of system reliability, where downtime is something to be avoided even during maintenance.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; oncall responders; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The team focuses on preventing problems through non-intrusive maintenance and monitoring for conditions that precipitate outages. When an alert is received, the oncall person employs quick fixes to return the service to normal operating parameters as fast as possible. After an event, the team investigates root causes and updates playbooks to ensure future stability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service should not stop while undergoing maintenance. Stability-related work is prioritized over new features to ensure the system remains available. Oncall teams must be large enough to ensure skills do not get stale and members are not overstressed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Software roll-outs, network changes, and shifting workloads all have a destabilizing effect on availability. A service launch can fail if it becomes so successful that it receives too much traffic and becomes overloaded.

Concept_Name: speed and performance
Core_Definition: Operational focus areas involving the management of latency, or how fast a request takes to process, and overall throughput. If a service is slow, users will assume it is broken even if it is technically available.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; operations teams; launch readiness engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are constantly scaled to handle more traffic and larger workloads while managing latency and throughput. Efficiency is monitored to prevent waste across thousands of machines. Before new software or hardware is deployed, it undergoes user acceptance tests and qualification to verify there are no performance regressions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance must be measured and reported against the SLA. Statistical analysis of measurements collected from every stage of production is used to improve system throughput.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Performance regressions, which are unexpected declines in speed, can occur during upgrades and must be detected during testing. Without proper capacity planning, a system will become overloaded and fail to perform. Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: security
Core_Definition: The operational focus on protecting systems from being taken down by outsiders and identifying vulnerabilities to ensure system reliability.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; security team; privacy audit team; outsiders.
Operational_Workflow: Conduct vulnerability assessments during the software and hardware upgrade process to detect issues; perform security and privacy audits as mandatory steps for launch readiness; remediate all identified security issues before service launch; securely erase disks and scrub data from hardware during the decommissioning phase; utilize focused project themes to address accumulated security-related issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security audits and privacy audits must be completed and all issues remediated prior to launch approval; hardware must be scrubbed of data before it is repurposed, sold, or disposed of; systems must be secured because insecure systems are vulnerable to being taken down by outsiders; automating and eliminating processes reduces the potential for security flaws.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system is insecure, outsiders can take it down; improper decommissioning can lead to resources remaining allocated or data not being properly scrubbed from media.

Concept_Name: capacity planning
Core_Definition: The practice of forecasting and providing the necessary computing, network, and storage resources to prevent a system from becoming overloaded and failing.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; Site Reliability Engineers; product management; network team; storage team; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Establish the expected initial volume of users and queries per second (QPS) based on load tests; determine emergency capacity plans for scenarios where volume is 2x or 5x higher than expected; forecast network and storage requirements for 1, 3, and 12 month intervals; coordinate with network and storage teams for confirmation of requirements; collect forecasts from stakeholders using self-service portals or APIs to streamline the process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Proper capacity planning is essential to prevent system failure due to overloading; in SRE environments, efficiency is a primary concern because small amounts of waste per machine become significant when scaled across thousands of machines; capacity planning must account for both latency and overall throughput; automation of machine allocation is a requirement for scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Without proper capacity planning, systems will become overloaded and fail; successful service launches can ironyically fail if they receive too much traffic and become overloaded without an emergency capacity plan.

Concept_Name: software/hardware upgrades
Core_Definition: The process of deploying new software releases and hardware platforms to fix bugs, add features, and increase capacity while maintaining system stability.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; Launch Readiness Engineer (LRE); Launch Committee.
Operational_Workflow: Build and test new software releases; conduct system tests by developers followed by user acceptance tests (UAT) by operations; verify that no performance regressions occur during UAT; perform vulnerability assessments to detect security issues; conduct hardware qualification for new platforms to test compatibility and performance; deploy upgrades using non-intrusive procedures that avoid stopping the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Upgrades must be performed to fix bugs and innovate; poor upgrade processes result in downtime; software roll-outs always have a destabilizing effect known as the change-instability cycle; stability-improvement work should be budgeted alongside new feature requests; new hardware must go through qualification to check for changes in operational processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If upgrades are not done at all, bugs will go unfixed; if upgrades are done badly, they result in system downtime; shifting workloads away from machines about to be upgraded can create a destabilizing effect even if the software itself has no bugs.

Concept_Name: Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)
Core_Definition: An operational discipline, originated at Google, where software engineers are tasked with maintaining live services and managing them through automation and defined reliability metrics.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers (SREs); developers; oncall teams; product management.
Operational_Workflow: Hire engineers with coding skills to automate manual tasks; establish Service Level Agreements (SLAs) and measure performance against them; use Error Budgets to balance the velocity of new launches against stability needs; participate in a common staffing pool with developers; manage oncall rotations to respond to outages; conduct blameless postmortems for every SLA-related event to improve technology and process.
Rules_and_Constraints: SRE operational load is capped at 50 percent to prevent excessive manual toil; engineers must be able to code and automate repetitive tasks; oncall teams should consist of at least eight people at one location or six people at each of multiple locations; aim for a maximum of two events per oncall shift; all services must have an SLA; excess operational work overflows to the developer team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a major crisis occurs, the oncall person may pull other team members away from project work; if a team spends more than 25 percent of its time on ticket duty and oncall, it indicates a lack of automation or excessive firefighting; postmortems must remain blameless to ensure the organization learns from failures without fear of retribution.

Concept_Name: Machine
Core_Definition: A virtual or physical machine.
Key_Actors: Operations staff who manage them remotely; datacenter technicians who act as remote hands for physical changes.
Operational_Workflow: Machines are allocated, configured, and managed through automated processes rather than manual manipulation; they are used to run servers and services; physical maintenance is performed by technicians at distant datacenter locations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Manual processes for machines do not scale in distributed systems; efficiency is critical because small amounts of waste per machine become significant across thousands of units.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Oncall
Core_Definition: Being available as first responder to an outage or alert.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; Site Reliability Engineers; developers who handle overflow or a small percentage of operational work.
Operational_Workflow: A team member is assigned a shift to respond to alerts via SMS or pager; they work to resolve the issue using a quick fix to restore the service; they then categorize the alert in a journal, file bugs for long-term solutions, and produce postmortem reports for major events.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should consist of at least eight people in one location or six in multiple locations; shifts should aim for a maximum of two events; oncall must drive long-term fixes rather than just reaction; postmortems must be blameless.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a major crisis occurs the oncall person can pull other team members away from project work; if shifts are too long or frequent the individual becomes overloaded with follow-up work and skills can become stale if shifts are too rare.

Concept_Name: Server
Core_Definition: Software that provides a function or API, distinct from hardware components.
Key_Actors: Operations teams who manage and scale them; developers who release new software for them.
Operational_Workflow: Servers are part of the larger service structure and are managed through automated installation and configuration; they are scaled to handle increasing traffic and workloads; their performance is measured in terms of latency and throughput.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation is required for managing servers at scale; efficiency is prioritized to prevent waste across large numbers of instances.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service
Core_Definition: A user-visible system or product composed of one or more servers.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; product management; end users; stakeholders; developers; Launch Readiness Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: A service moves through a life cycle starting with a launch or soft launch; it undergoes maintenance through emergency and nonemergency tasks; it is upgraded with new software and hardware; it is eventually decommissioned by removing users and deallocating resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every service must have a Service Level Agreement; stability and uptime are prioritized; launches should follow a checklist or review process to prevent failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Success-induced failures where too much traffic overloads a new launch; bespoke systems that are home-grown rather than off-the-shelf; incorrect decommissioning where resources remain allocated.

Concept_Name: Soft launch
Core_Definition: The act of launching a new service without a public announcement to allow traffic to grow slowly through word of mouth.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; initial customers; word of mouth participants.
Operational_Workflow: The service is made live quietly so that traffic increases gradually. This provides the operations team with a cushion to identify and fix problems or scale the system effectively before the service is exposed to a large audience.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a strategy used during the service launch phase of the service life cycle and is an option to be specified in the launch readiness review process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Stakeholders
Core_Definition: People and organizations that are recognized as having a vested interest in the success of a project.
Key_Actors: Product management; end users; service teams; marketing; legal; security and privacy teams; project managers.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams interact with stakeholders to maintain relationships and manage their requests. This involves collecting capacity requirements through self-service portals; coordinating on roadmaps; and obtaining formal sign-offs from departments like legal and marketing during the launch readiness review process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Interactions with stakeholders should be streamlined through documentation; video instruction; and self-service APIs to reduce the manual burden on the operations team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If stakeholders are not provided with adequate documentation or self-service tools; they may require significant manual interaction and individual meetings; which can become a full-time burden for project managers.

Concept_Name: enterprise IT
Core_Definition: A traditional IT department responsible for desktop and client-server computing infrastructure across an organization.
Key_Actors: Internal end users; external contractors; IT staff.
Operational_Workflow: Enterprise IT manages broad responsibilities including everything with a power plug; such as desktop and back-office services. They often utilize systems built by external contractors and typically manage systems that are upgraded rarely. Capacity is managed by building systems large enough to handle projected workloads for several years until the system is replaced.
Rules_and_Constraints: Customers are the end users themselves; services often use off-the-shelf or turnkey components; and there is usually flexibility regarding scheduled downtime. SLAs typically focus on how quickly service can be restored after an outage occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Recently; enterprise IT departments have begun adopting management techniques from distributed computing as they face new demands for uptime and scalability.

Concept_Name: uptime
Core_Definition: The period during which a service is functioning and available to users in compliance with a service level agreement.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; operations teams; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Uptime is maintained by focusing on problem prevention rather than just reacting to outages. Operations teams perform complex but non-intrusive maintenance procedures to ensure service does not stop during upgrades. Performance against uptime requirements is measured and reported against the SLA.
Rules_and_Constraints: In distributed systems; uptime requirements are often 24 by 7. Stability is prioritized over change to ensure uptime; and operations teams are frequently rewarded based on achieving SLA compliance related to uptime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Poor capacity planning; security failures; or badly managed upgrades can lead to downtime. If a service is slow; users may perceive it as a lack of uptime even if the system is technically running. Error budgets define the acceptable amount of downtime or instability allowed each month.

Concept_Name: Latency
Core_Definition: Latency is defined as how fast a particular request takes to process within a system.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing services; Site Reliability Engineers; end users.
Operational_Workflow: In distributed computing environments, latency is managed as a primary performance metric alongside throughput; SREs monitor it to ensure efficiency across hundreds or thousands of machines where small delays can accumulate into significant waste.
Rules_and_Constraints: Latency must be actively managed to meet demanding uptime requirements; efficiency is a central concern because waste per machine becomes a large aggregate waste in scaled environments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service is slow due to high latency, users will assume it is broken; failure to manage speed and performance results in an unreliable system.

Concept_Name: throughput
Core_Definition: Throughput refers to the overall volume of work or requests a system processes, often improved through statistical analysis of production stages.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; automated assembly line processes; distributed computing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams collect measurements from every stage of production; they then apply statistical analysis to these measurements to help improve the system's overall performance and capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Throughput is managed in conjunction with latency; it is a key focus for systems that are constantly being scaled to handle more traffic and larger workloads.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: bespoke systems
Core_Definition: Bespoke systems are systems built on home-grown platforms or integrated from open source and other third-party components rather than being purchased as complete units.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) teams; separate management teams; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: These systems are actively managed and constantly changing due to new software releases and additions to capacity; they require bespoke operational and management practices tailored to their specific architecture.
Rules_and_Constraints: Because of their unique requirements, distributed computing services using bespoke systems are best managed by a separate team with its own management and specialized practices.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: off the shelf systems
Core_Definition: Off the shelf systems, also known as turn key systems, are standardized products that are typically unchanged from their initial delivery state.
Key_Actors: Traditional enterprise IT departments; external contractors.
Operational_Workflow: These systems are often built by external contractors who leave once the system is stable; they are upgraded rarely and are typically built large enough to handle a projected workload for several years until the system is replaced.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems are characteristic of traditional enterprise IT; they often have SLAs focused on how quickly service can be restored after an outage rather than maintaining constant uptime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: turn key systems
Core_Definition: Systems that are off the shelf or ready to use without requiring the bespoke development or active management typical of distributed systems.
Key_Actors: External contractors who build the systems; traditional enterprise IT departments that run them.
Operational_Workflow: External contractors build the system and then go away once it is stable; the enterprise IT department runs the service which may remain unchanged from its initial delivery state.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems are contrasted with SRE managed systems which are actively managed and built on platforms integrated from open source or home-grown components.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: change-instability cycle
Core_Definition: The process where a system moves from a baseline of stability to a state of instability due to a change and eventually returns to stability through intervention.
Key_Actors: Developers who initiate change; operations teams who prioritize stability; software and network systems.
Operational_Workflow: A system starts at a baseline of stability; a change such as a software roll-out or network update is made; the change creates a destabilizing effect such as bugs or workload shifts; the system eventually becomes stable again usually through interventions like workarounds or new releases.
Rules_and_Constraints: All software roll-outs and even non-software changes like network updates have some kind of destabilizing effect; stability is achieved through fixes or the propagation of the change.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If engineering management neglects bug fixes to focus exclusively on new features, the system can slowly destabilize until it spins out of control.

Concept_Name: DevOps movement
Core_Definition: An organizational philosophy and structure where developers and operations staff work as one team to align their goals.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; the unified engineering team.
Operational_Workflow: The organization restructures so that developers and operations function as a single unit; the team shares responsibility for Service Level Agreement compliance and the velocity of system changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Both developers and operations must have components of their annual performance reviews tied to both SLA compliance and the on-time delivery of new features.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Error Budgets
Core_Definition: A management tool that defines a permissible amount of instability per month based on a Service Level Agreement to balance the rate of change with the need for stability.
Key_Actors: Google Site Reliability Engineering teams; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: A Service Level Agreement specifies the required reliability of a service; the difference between perfect reliability and the SLA becomes the error budget; each software roll-out or bug uses a portion of this budget; launches are gated based on whether budget remains.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers can increase the number of allowable roll-outs by dedicating effort to improve code stability; the budget creates a positive feedback loop for code quality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Site Reliability Practices
Core_Definition: A set of twelve principles and core practices refined by Google to maintain live production services through engineering and automation.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; Developers; Engineering Management; Google.
Operational_Workflow: The workflow involves hiring coders who automate manual tasks; establishing and reporting performance against Service Level Agreements; using Error Budgets to control software launches; utilizing a common staffing pool; overflowing excess operational work to developers; and capping manual operational load at 50 percent. It also includes maintaining specific oncall team sizes; limiting events per shift; and conducting blameless postmortems for every event to focus on process and technology.
Rules_and_Constraints: SREs must be able to solve nontrivial problems by writing code; all services must have an SLA; SRE operational load is capped at 50 percent; oncall teams must have at least eight people at one location or six at each of multiple locations; a maximum of two events per oncall shift is targeted; postmortems must be blameless.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If engineering management is pressured to focus on new features and neglect bug fixes, the result is a system that slowly destabilizes until it spins out of control.

Concept_Name: common staffing pool
Core_Definition: An engineering resource allocation strategy where a project is assigned a specific number of engineers who can be either developers or SREs.
Key_Actors: SREs; Developers; Project Management.
Operational_Workflow: Projects are allocated a certain number of total engineers from a single pool; the team then decides the mix of SREs and developers needed. For every SRE required for the project, the number of developers available to write new features is reduced by one.
Rules_and_Constraints: This system encourages developers to create systems that can be operated efficiently to minimize the number of SREs needed; it contrasts with traditional companies where system administrators and developers are allocated from separate budgets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Ops work overflow to the Dev team
Core_Definition: A practice where operational tasks that exceed the capacity or predefined limits of the SRE team are assigned to the development team.
Key_Actors: SRE team; Developer team.
Operational_Workflow: When the operational load exceeds the SRE team's cap or capacity, the excess work is diverted to the developers. Developers also share a standard 5 percent of total operational work.
Rules_and_Constraints: This practice is intended to discourage developers from taking shortcuts that create undue operational load, as they will share the resulting burden; it helps developers stay in tune with operational realities.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: oncall shift
Core_Definition: A rotating period of responsibility where a team member acts as the first responder to outages, alerts, or service level agreement events.
Key_Actors: Oncall team members; SREs; Developers; Users (affected by outages).
Operational_Workflow: The designated person works on projects until an alert is received; they then employ a quick fix to return the service to normal parameters; after resolution, they categorize the alert, update playbooks, file bugs for long-term fixes, and identify root causes. If an SLA violation occurs, they write a postmortem report; at the end of the shift, they provide a report to the team including an alert log and handoff status.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should have at least eight members at one location or six at multiple locations; the goal is a maximum of two events per shift; oncall should be scheduled in a rotation; shifts must be short enough and spaced far enough apart to allow for follow-up work; oncall should drive behaviors that encourage long-term fixes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a major crisis occurs, the oncall person will pull other team members away from their projects to assist; if shifts are too long or too close together, the member may be overloaded with follow-up work or unable to complete long-term solutions.

Concept_Name: Postmortem
Core_Definition: A written report produced after an outage or major SLA-related event that includes details of what happened, analysis, and suggestions for how to prevent such a situation in the future.
Key_Actors: The person oncall who is responsible for responding to the event and producing the report; the organization or company that learns from the experience; the user community.
Operational_Workflow: An outage or major SLA-related event occurs; the oncall person responds to the event and employs a fix to return the service to normal parameters; an investigation is conducted to ascertain the root cause of the problem; a written report is produced containing the incident details and suggestions for prevention; the report is shared within the company to increase organizational memory; a version of the report, possibly redacted for technical content, may be shared with the user community to build confidence.
Rules_and_Constraints: A postmortem should be done for every event; they must be written; they should focus on process and technology rather than people; the report must include suggestions for how to prevent such a situation in the future.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a postmortem report is not produced, the team may continually muddle through ad hoc workarounds and the issues will not get the attention required to improve the system.

Concept_Name: Blameless postmortems
Core_Definition: A specific approach to post-event reporting that focuses on process and technology failures rather than finding individuals to blame.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; the entire organization.
Operational_Workflow: Following a major event, a report is drafted that avoids pointing fingers at people; the analysis identifies flaws in the technology or the established processes; the findings are shared broadly across the organization to facilitate collective learning.
Rules_and_Constraints: They must focus on process and technology, not people; they are identified as one of the twelve core site reliability practices.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Manual processes
Core_Definition: Tasks that require human effort and manipulation, which do not scale because the amount of effort required increases linearly with the number of tasks.
Key_Actors: System administrators; SREs; developers; stakeholders; customers; oncall staff; ticket duty staff.
Operational_Workflow: A task such as configuring an operating system or installing software arises; a human performs the work manually; for processes that are not yet automated, a playbook is used to help make the process repeatable and consistent; tasks are tracked via ticket systems or alert journals; efforts are made to identify which manual tasks are worth automating based on their frequency and difficulty.
Rules_and_Constraints: Manual processes do not scale; they have a lower return on investment than writing code to replace the labor; SRE operational load is capped at 50 percent to limit the amount of manual labor done.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some manual tasks are not worth automating because they happen infrequently, are too difficult to automate, or the process changes too often; human processes such as maintaining relationships with stakeholders, evaluating new technology, or negotiating oncall schedules cannot be automated but can be streamlined or eliminated.

Concept_Name: Automation
Core_Definition: The use of code to perform operational tasks, allowing processes to scale across thousands of machines, servers, or services.
Key_Actors: SREs who write and maintain the code; developers; automated systems; chat bots that provide services or announce alerts.
Operational_Workflow: An SRE identifies a task that requires multiple iterations; the SRE writes code to automate the task rather than performing it manually; the automation is tested and deployed; the code is used to handle tasks like allocating machines, configuring systems, or installing software across the distributed environment; measurements are collected from the automated stages to apply statistical analysis for system improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation is a must-have for large systems; SREs are required to have software development experience to facilitate automation; it is an investment in time and effort where the return on investment must be considered; automating the common case is often prioritized over handling every edge case.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automation takes time to create, test, and deploy, resulting in tasks that are not yet automated; it may not be viable if the ROI is low due to infrequent use or if the process changes too often; human-centric processes cannot be replaced by automation but may be accelerated or facilitated by it.

Concept_Name: assembly line
Core_Definition: A model for large-scale operations where system administration is treated as an automated manufacturing process rather than a manual craft.
Key_Actors: System administrators who maintain the robotics; automated systems; developers who identify common case processes.
Operational_Workflow: A common or repetitive case is identified within the system; the end-to-end process for that case is standardized; the task is automated to replace manual manipulation; the system administrator then transitions from performing the manual labor to maintaining the automation robotics that perform the work.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation is a requirement at large scale because manual processes do not scale with the number of tasks; processes involving many machines or services must be automated; creating a playbook to describe the process accurately is often a prerequisite for coding the automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tasks that happen infrequently or are too difficult to automate are excluded; human processes such as stakeholder relationship management or team negotiations cannot be automated; unique edge cases may be eliminated if the automated common case provides superior service.

Concept_Name: mass production techniques
Core_Definition: Operational practices borrowed from manufacturing that use statistical analysis of production data to enhance system efficiency.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; analytical systems monitoring production stages.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are collected from every stage of the automated production process; statistical analysis is applied to these measurements; the resulting data is used to identify improvements for system throughput.
Rules_and_Constraints: These techniques become viable only when operations have been automated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: continuous improvement
Core_Definition: A manufacturing principle that serves as the business driver for the ongoing automation and optimization of operational processes.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; people involved in the process; business stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The business desire for improvement inspires tasks focused on process improvement and automation; teams dedicate project time to evolving the system; the system is refined through many small projects and software updates rather than large sudden changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Process improvement is best performed by the people involved in the process rather than outside observers; it is one of the three primary sources of operational work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the manual operational load or toil exceeds fifty percent of a team's capacity, the team may be required to pause new feature development to focus on the improvements necessary to solve the underlying problems.

Concept_Name: Three Ways of DevOps
Core_Definition: A framework of principles for the DevOps movement that is based on manufacturing techniques such as continuous improvement.
Key_Actors: Integrated teams of developers and operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations restructure themselves so that developers and operations work as one team to align goals for both stability and the velocity of changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: The framework is based on manufacturing techniques; it requires aligning developer and operations goals through shared responsibility for service level agreement compliance and the rate of system change.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: procedural documentation
Core_Definition: Written instructions created for processes that have not yet been automated to ensure they are repeatable and consistent.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; system administrators; stakeholders; Launch Readiness Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Describing a manual process accurately to ensure it can be performed the same way every time; using these written descriptions as a foundation for future automation because the coding is easier once the process is defined; providing documentation to stakeholders to help them be self-sufficient and reduce the need for manual support.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation should include entries for all operational tasks and alerts; it should be reviewed by a Launch Readiness Engineer for accuracy and completeness during the launch process; it should be updated regularly to build organizational memory.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A high number of tickets requesting information from the operations team is an exception that indicates the documentation is insufficient and needs improvement; documentation-related bugs must be kept low to ensure service stability.

Concept_Name: Playbook
Core_Definition: A specific type of procedural documentation that details how to handle operational tasks and alerts to make processes repeatable and easier to automate.
Key_Actors: Oncall staff; ticket duty staff; Launch Readiness Engineers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Creating entries for every possible operational task and alert before a service is launched; consulting the playbook during oncall shifts to respond to alerts or during ticket duty to process requests; updating the playbook after an alert is resolved to incorporate new lessons learned.
Rules_and_Constraints: A playbook must exist and include entries for all operational tasks and alerts as part of the launch readiness review; an LRE must review each entry for accuracy; any ticket created by an automated system should have a corresponding playbook entry.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an alert occurs that is not in the playbook, the oncall person should update the playbook entry after resolution; if a process is too complex or changes too often, it may remain in the playbook rather than being automated.

Concept_Name: return on investment (ROI)
Core_Definition: A measure of the benefit gained from an investment of time or effort, used to decide whether to automate a task or perform it manually.
Key_Actors: SRE teams; management; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Evaluating the frequency and difficulty of a task to determine if the time spent automating it will be recovered; prioritizing code-writing over manual labor because automation scales while manual effort does not; capping manual labor at a certain percentage to ensure staff focus on high-ROI activities.
Rules_and_Constraints: Manual labor is generally viewed as having a lower ROI than writing code; automation is not considered viable if the ROI is low due to the task being infrequent or the process changing too often.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Rare edge cases may not be worth automating due to low ROI, though automating the common case can sometimes lead to the elimination of these edge cases.

Concept_Name: edge cases
Core_Definition: Rare or unique situations that do not fit the common or standard process and often require specialized handling.
Key_Actors: Customers with unique requirements; system administrators; automation developers.
Operational_Workflow: Identifying variations of a task that are infrequent or highly complex; focusing automation efforts on the common case first to demonstrate value; consolidating or eliminating these rare cases once the automated common case provides superior service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Edge cases can stall automation projects if they are treated as equal in priority to the common case; they should be evaluated to see if they can be eliminated through better documentation or standardized processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Customers may insist their unique requirements are essential until an automated common-case solution becomes available, at which point the perceived need for the edge-case behavior often vanishes.

Concept_Name: self-service
Core_Definition: A mechanism that allows stakeholders and internal teams to fulfill their own requests or perform tasks independently through automation and documentation.
Key_Actors: Stakeholders; customers; service teams; operations staff; Launch Readiness Engineer or Launch Committee.
Operational_Workflow: Stakeholders use introductory and user documentation to solve problems independently; customers provide capacity forecasts via web interfaces or APIs; developers use automated systems to list availability for oncall scheduling; teams use a self-service launch process involving a checklist and instructions to initiate service launches independently.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation such as best practices and style guides should be provided to increase stakeholder self-sufficiency; automation should be used to replace or accelerate team processes and eliminate manual interactions; a status dashboard should be provided to allow people to track their own requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When self-service systems involve complex handoffs between teams, the system should automatically notify teams and provide dashboards so people can deal specifically with exceptions and problems.

Concept_Name: web user interface
Core_Definition: A web-based portal or system used to collect data from stakeholders and provide visibility into the status of automated processes.
Key_Actors: Stakeholders; customers; project managers; service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Stakeholders enter capacity requirements and forecasts into the interface; users log in to check the status of orders as they move through approval cycles; service teams use portals to request service activations, allocations, or configuration changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Web interfaces should provide status dashboards and automatic notifications for hand-offs to reduce the need for manual status reports and project managers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The system is designed so that people only need to intervene manually to deal with exceptions and problems identified through the interface.

Concept_Name: API
Core_Definition: A programmatic interface provided by a server or service that allows for the automated collection of data or the execution of functions by other systems and teams.
Key_Actors: Servers; internal users; other service teams; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Automation systems use the API to collect forecasting data from hundreds of different teams; internal service teams interact with the API to use a service's functions; developers provide APIs to automate technical parts of a service launch or to make manual configuration requests obsolete.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs should be utilized to eliminate the need for people to open manual tickets for service activations or configuration changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: supply-chain management system
Core_Definition: An enterprise system that integrates with automation tools to manage the procurement and planning of resources based on gathered data.
Key_Actors: Project managers; stakeholders; automation systems.
Operational_Workflow: Automation tools collect capacity forecasts from stakeholders across the organization and integrate that information directly into the supply-chain management system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Proper automation and integration with this system should be used to eliminate the need for project managers to meet individually with customers to understand future capacity requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: status dashboard
Core_Definition: A web-based system or metrics display that provides visibility into the state of subsystems or the progress of complex processes. It serves to facilitate meta-processes like communication and tracking across different teams.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; project managers; automated notification systems; service teams; internal stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The system tracks the status of a request or order as it moves through various approval processes; it monitors major subsystems and provides visual metrics; when a process involves complex handoffs between different teams, the dashboard automatically notifies the relevant parties when a handoff occurs; this visibility allows staff to focus on exceptions and problems rather than manual status reporting.
Rules_and_Constraints: Dashboards should exist for all major subsystems; metrics should be presented in business terms rather than purely technical terms; the system should be designed to reduce the need for manual project management by automating notifications.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a status dashboard is not used for complex inter-team handoffs, the organization may require legions of project managers to manually track and coordinate interactions.

Concept_Name: process optimization
Core_Definition: The act of identifying common operational tasks and transforming them into streamlined, assembly-line processes to improve throughput and efficiency. It involves applying manufacturing techniques like continuous improvement and statistical analysis to system administration.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; stakeholders; automated systems; service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Staff identify labor-intensive tasks such as technology evaluation and isolate the most common cases; these tasks are turned into standardized, assembly-line processes; measurements are collected at every stage of production; statistical analysis is applied to these measurements to identify areas for improvement; the optimized process is then documented or automated to ensure repeatability and consistency.
Rules_and_Constraints: Optimization should prioritize tasks with a high return on investment; the process should focus on creating an assembly-line model rather than a craft-based approach; results from optimized processes should be stored automatically for future analysis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain human processes such as negotiating oncall schedules or maintaining stakeholder relationships cannot be fully automated, though they can be streamlined through self-service portals or better documentation.

Concept_Name: Elimination
Core_Definition: A strategy of process optimization where a task or requirement is entirely removed so that it no longer requires performance, maintenance, or security oversight. It is described as the most effective form of optimization.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service teams; automated approval systems.
Operational_Workflow: Teams identify redundant or overly complex requirements that create manual work; they simplify the environment, such as by reducing the number of supported operating systems; they streamline human processes by creating systems for automatic approval of certain user types; this removes the need for human intervention or the maintenance of specific configurations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Eliminated tasks do not need to be maintained and cannot have bugs or security flaws; narrowing down technical variations is a primary method of elimination; automatic approvals should be used to replace lengthy manual processes where appropriate.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The text notes that customers who believe they have unique needs may resist the elimination of their specific processes until a superior automated common-case method is provided.

Concept_Name: service life cycle
Core_Definition: The entire lifespan of a service from its initial launch and maintenance to upgrades and final decommissioning. Operations is responsible for managing every aspect of this cycle to ensure it meets service level agreements.
Key_Actors: Operations teams (SRE); developers; product management; stakeholders; Launch Readiness Engineers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The cycle begins with a service launch where a system is brought to life and checked for undiscovered problems; the service then enters a maintenance phase consisting of emergency tasks like handling outages and nonemergency tasks like processing user requests; new software releases and hardware platforms are deployed through an upgrade process involving testing and qualification; project work is performed to scale or evolve the system; finally, the service is decommissioned by removing users, deallocating resources, and disposing of hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: All aspects of the life cycle must focus on availability, performance, security, and capacity; a service level agreement must be used to measure performance across the life cycle; checklists should be maintained for infrequent stages like launches and decommissioning to preserve organizational memory.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failure to perform any stage well results in an unreliable system; if upgrades are done badly, they cause downtime; if capacity planning is neglected, the system becomes overloaded and fails.

Concept_Name: Service Launch
Core_Definition: The phase of the service life cycle where a service is brought to life for the first time, initial customers begin using it, and previously undiscovered problems are remedied.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service teams; product managers; Launch Readiness Engineer (LRE); Launch Committee; launch lead; marketing; security team; legal department.
Operational_Workflow: Maintaining and following a checklist of actions or questions; socializing the checklist with product managers; utilizing the checklist throughout product development; completing a launch readiness review survey; undergoing an audit by an LRE or Launch Committee; appointing a single launch lead to coordinate tasks and track issues until approval; verifying sign-offs from marketing, legal, and other departments; executing the launch; managing post-launch problem resolution.
Rules_and_Constraints: Checklists must be available, advertised, and part of the company culture; a single person should take a leadership or coordinator role; teams must plan for launch effort in advance; technical and business requirements must be satisfied before external visibility is granted.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Soft launches where a service is launched without public announcement to grow traffic slowly; failed public launches due to overload from excessive traffic; teams surprising operations at or near the launch date without prior planning or homework.

Concept_Name: Emergency Tasks
Core_Definition: Handling exceptional or unexpected events, including responding to outages and fixing conditions that precipitate them.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; first responder; operations team members; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: Receiving an alert through SMS, text message, or pager; working the issue until resolved; employing a quick fix to return the service to normal operating parameters; annotating an electronic alert journal for trend discovery; filing bugs for long-term fixes; updating playbook entries; writing a blameless postmortem report for user-visible outages or SLA violations; conducting investigations to identify root causes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Emergency issues must receive immediate attention; returning service to normal parameters is the highest priority; postmortems must focus on process and technology rather than people; oncall shifts should catch no more than two events to allow for follow-through.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Major crises that require the oncall person to pull other team members away from project work; false alarm alerts; spammy alerts that require adjustment or correction.

Concept_Name: Nonemergency Tasks
Core_Definition: Performing all manual work required for a normally functioning system, including periodic maintenance and processing non-urgent requests from users.
Key_Actors: Person assigned to ticket duty; internal users; other service teams; customer support representatives for external users.
Operational_Workflow: Managing work through a request ticket system; triaging and prioritizing tickets; performing repeatable manual processes; responding to non-urgent trouble reports and informational questions; providing consulting to other teams; batching similar tickets for efficiency; updating documentation and playbooks; providing daily status updates for urgent tickets; emailing a shift report to the team at the end of the duty cycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: One person on the team should be focused on responding to these requests; response time is typically measured in days; management should not expect project work to be completed during ticket duty; ticket duty should be used as a strategy to reduce overall work by improving processes and documentation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tickets created by automated systems for non-urgent detections; long-running tickets that must be handed off to the next shift; customers who must be poked every three days if they have not responded to requests for information.

Concept_Name: Upgrades
Core_Definition: The process of deploying new software releases and hardware platforms to provide new functions, fix bugs, or increase capacity.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; stakeholders; external contractors.
Operational_Workflow: Building and testing new software releases; performing system tests by developers; conducting user acceptance tests (UAT) by operations; performing vulnerability assessments for security; conducting hardware qualification to test for compatibility and performance regressions; shifting workloads away from machines about to be upgraded; performing non-intrusive maintenance procedures.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service should not stop while maintenance is being performed; UAT must verify there are no performance regressions; hardware qualification must detect changes in operational processes; stability-related work and bug fixes should be prioritized or budgeted alongside new features.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Upgrades done badly resulting in downtime; bugs going unfixed if upgrades are neglected; destabilizing effects caused by the process of shifting workloads during a release.

Concept_Name: system tests
Core_Definition: A category of software testing performed during the upgrade stage of a service life cycle to verify a new release.
Key_Actors: Developers.
Operational_Workflow: Within the upgrade phase, a new software release is built; developers then conduct system tests on that release before it proceeds to further testing stages or deployment.
Rules_and_Constraints: System tests are performed by developers and are required after the software is built but before it is deployed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: user acceptance tests (UAT)
Core_Definition: Testing conducted by the operations team to ensure that a new software release is acceptable for deployment.
Key_Actors: Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: After developers complete initial system tests, the operations team performs UAT on the new software release; this process may include specific checks for performance regressions; once the software passes these tests, it is ready for deployment.
Rules_and_Constraints: These tests must be performed by operations as part of the upgrade process before software is moved into production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: performance regressions
Core_Definition: Unexpected and undesired declines in the performance metrics of a system or service.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; hardware qualification processes.
Operational_Workflow: During the upgrade phase, operations teams may test for these regressions as part of the user acceptance testing process; additionally, new hardware must undergo a hardware qualification process to test for compatibility and performance regressions before being utilized.
Rules_and_Constraints: Checking for performance regressions is a required component of both the software user acceptance testing process and the hardware qualification process for new platforms.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Vulnerability assessments
Core_Definition: Security-focused assessments and audits conducted to identify and detect issues or flaws within a system.
Key_Actors: Security teams; operations teams; launch readiness engineers; launch committees.
Operational_Workflow: During software upgrades, assessments are performed to detect security issues; in the context of a service launch, a security team performs an audit and the launch readiness engineer or committee ensures that all identified security issues are remediated before granting launch approval.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security audits must be completed and all identified issues must be remediated as a condition for service launch approval; vulnerability assessments are a standard part of testing new software releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: security issues
Core_Definition: Vulnerabilities or threats that can lead to system downtime or unauthorized access, impacting the overall reliability and stability of a service.
Key_Actors: Outsiders; operations team; security team; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Security issues are identified through vulnerability assessments performed during the software release and hardware upgrade process; the security team audits and approves services during the launch readiness review to identify and remediate issues; privacy audits are also conducted; for existing services, security-related bugs are tracked in a bug tracking system and can be addressed during dedicated themes or fix-it days.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be secure because outsiders can take them down; all security and privacy issues must be remediated before a service launch is approved; security-related bugs should take priority over new features if they affect stability; automation is encouraged to eliminate manual processes that could lead to security flaws.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: hardware qualification
Core_Definition: A testing process conducted during hardware upgrades to ensure new platforms are compatible and performant within the system.
Key_Actors: Operations team; hardware vendors.
Operational_Workflow: New hardware is introduced as part of the upgrade phase of the service life cycle; the operations team performs tests to verify compatibility with existing infrastructure; tests are conducted to detect any performance regressions or unexpected declines in speed; the team identifies and documents any necessary changes to operational processes required by the new hardware.
Rules_and_Constraints: Qualification must include testing for compatibility; it must specifically check for performance regressions; it must account for changes in operational processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Decommissioning
Core_Definition: The final stage of the service life cycle that involves turning off a service, removing users, and reclaiming or disposing of resources.
Key_Actors: Operations team; product management; users; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the removal of users through notification or migration to a new service; user data is archived or moved as required; resources are deallocated including removing DNS entries, disabling database connections, and powering off machines; references to the service are removed from related configurations; disks and media are securely erased or scrubbed; finally, hardware is repurposed, sold, or scrapped.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decommissioning must be guided by a checklist to ensure tasks are completed in the correct order; resource deallocation must respect complex dependencies; for example, DNS entries should not be removed until the machine is no longer in use; all data must be securely erased before hardware disposal.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain resources like network connections must remain in place if other deallocation tasks depend on them, even if the primary service is no longer active.

Concept_Name: Project Work
Core_Definition: Large-scale tasks such as system evolution, automation, or scaling that require dedicated planning and focused resources.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; stakeholders; developers; managers; launch lead.
Operational_Workflow: Work is initiated through life-cycle management, stakeholder interactions, or process improvement goals; team members use a bug tracking system to prioritize and track their work; most staff members spend project-focused days developing software or shepherding launches with long periods of uninterrupted time; work is often done in small teams using Agile methodology to ensure frequent progress and feedback; designs undergo peer review to improve quality.
Rules_and_Constraints: Project work should be the primary focus for most of the team at any given time; it must be prioritized over manual toil to ensure long-term system improvement; solo projects are discouraged in favor of peer-reviewed team efforts; progress should be made visible to management and the wider team through frequent shipping; a single bug tracking system should be used to manage status.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Project work may be interrupted if an oncall person requires assistance with a major crisis; if firefighting and manual requests exceed 25 percent of the team's capacity, project work may be stalled, indicating a need for more automation.

Concept_Name: Checklist
Core_Definition: A tool used to capture organizational memory and ensure that tasks performed infrequently are executed with consistent attention to detail and according to learned best practices.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service teams; product managers; launch leads; Launch Readiness Engineers; Launch Committees.
Operational_Workflow: The checklist is developed to record lessons learned from previous launches or decommissioning events; it is socialized among product managers and advertised in documentation so that service teams can use it as a guide during development; a launch lead works through the items by delegating tasks and filing bugs for any missing requirements; the checklist is updated after each completion of a service life cycle stage to include new lessons for future use.
Rules_and_Constraints: Checklists should be easy to access and integrated into the company culture; they can consist of either actions to be completed or a series of questions to be audited; for decommissioning, the checklist must ensure that tasks are performed in a specific order to account for complex dependencies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If launches are rare, a checklist is necessary to prevent forgetting lessons between events; if a service team is unaware of the checklist, they may fail to plan for the launch effort and surprise operations teams near the launch date without having completed necessary preparations.

Concept_Name: Launch Readiness Engineer (LRE)
Core_Definition: An engineer who reviews a service team's readiness for launch by auditing responses to a survey and evaluating technical and operational standards.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineer; service teams; SRE engineer.
Operational_Workflow: The service team completes a launch readiness review survey before meeting with the LRE; the LRE evaluates the provided information regarding architecture, capacity, and monitoring; the LRE reviews playbook entries for accuracy and completeness; at Google, an SRE assigned as an LRE shepherds the team through the process to ensure all sign-offs from departments like marketing and legal are finished before external visibility is granted.
Rules_and_Constraints: The LRE holds the launch team to high standards; the LRE must approve the service launch based on the evaluation of the checklist and the team's demonstration of preparedness for situations like excessive traffic.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Launch Committee
Core_Definition: A group responsible for auditing a series of launch readiness questions as part of a sophisticated service launch strategy.
Key_Actors: Launch Committee; service teams.
Operational_Workflow: The committee reviews the launch readiness review survey and checklist responses provided by a team; they audit the information to determine if the service meets the necessary technical and business criteria for production.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Hard launch
Core_Definition: A type of service launch characterized by a public announcement of the new service.
Key_Actors: Service teams; operations teams; public users; marketing department.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies the launch as a hard launch during the general information gathering phase; the service is brought to life and made available to the public following approval from the Launch Readiness Engineer or Launch Committee; operations teams must monitor the service closely for problems that were not discovered prior to the launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A hard launch may be so successful that it receives traffic significantly higher than expected, which can lead to the service becoming overloaded and going down; teams must have a plan in place for cases where first-day usage is much greater than projected to avoid an embarrassing failure.

Concept_Name: system architecture
Core_Definition: The structural design of a service that describes its components and how they are organized to function together. It includes the arrangement of virtual or physical machines and their interactions.
Key_Actors: Service teams; Launch Readiness Engineers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The service team documents the system architecture during the development process; links to these architecture documents are provided in the Launch Readiness Review survey; a Launch Readiness Engineer evaluates the architecture to ensure it meets requirements for scaling and failure handling before approving a launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: Architecture documentation must be provided or linked during the launch readiness review; the design must explicitly account for scaling under normal conditions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Failover
Core_Definition: The mechanism or process by which a system maintains operation when specific components or infrastructure layers experience a failure.
Key_Actors: Service teams; Launch Readiness Engineers; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers design failover logic into the system architecture to handle various failure tiers; during the Launch Readiness Review, teams must describe how failover functions for specific scenarios; these plans are audited by a Launch Readiness Engineer to ensure service resilience.
Rules_and_Constraints: Failover plans are required for single-machine, rack, and datacenter failures; these plans must be documented and reviewed as a prerequisite for service launch.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: single-machine failure
Core_Definition: The loss of an individual virtual or physical machine within a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Service teams; Launch Readiness Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: The service team identifies how the system will react when a single machine stops functioning; they document the specific failover behavior for this scenario; this information is submitted as part of the architecture section of the Launch Readiness Review survey for evaluation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Describing the response to a single-machine failure is a mandatory requirement for the launch readiness review checklist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: rack failure
Core_Definition: A failure event that impacts all machines or services located within a single physical rack.
Key_Actors: Service teams; Launch Readiness Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: The team determines how the service architecture is designed to survive the loss of an entire rack; this failover process is documented and shared with the Launch Readiness Engineer; the engineer reviews the plan to confirm that the service remains available during such an event.
Rules_and_Constraints: A documented failover strategy for rack-level failures is a required component of the architecture evaluation during a Launch Readiness Review.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: datacenter failure
Core_Definition: An event where an entire facility providing computing resources becomes unavailable, necessitating a failover to maintain service.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; Launch Readiness Engineers; architecture teams; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: During the architecture phase of a service launch, teams must describe how failover functions to handle the loss of a datacenter; the system is designed to shift workloads or recover in a way that meets uptime requirements; architecture documents must link to these failover plans.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed with failover mechanisms to handle such events as part of launch readiness; information about failover must be reviewed by a Launch Readiness Engineer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a datacenter failure is part of a planned move to a new facility, it is categorized as highly technical project work that cannot be automated due to its infrequent nature.

Concept_Name: load tests
Core_Definition: Testing procedures used to determine and validate a system's performance limits and capacity by simulating traffic and query volumes.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineers; development teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Teams determine the expected initial volume of users and queries per second; they perform tests to validate these projections; results and reports are documented and linked in the launch readiness survey for evaluation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Expected capacity numbers provided during the launch process must be supported by load tests and corresponding reports.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: emergency capacity documents
Core_Definition: Documentation that outlines the plan and expected system behavior when traffic volume significantly exceeds projections.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineers; service teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: During launch planning, teams analyze scenarios where user volume is much higher than anticipated such as two times or five times the expected amount; they document the technical response and system behavior for these spikes; these documents are linked and reviewed as part of the launch readiness survey.
Rules_and_Constraints: These documents must specifically address scenarios of 2x and 5x expected volume and be linked during the capacity evaluation of a service launch.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dependencies
Core_Definition: External systems, services, or resources that a specific service requires to function or that must be managed during its life cycle.
Key_Actors: Primary service teams; external dependency groups; Launch Readiness Engineers; decommissioning teams.
Operational_Workflow: During launch, dependencies are identified in a data flow diagram and Remote Procedure Call limits are established with external groups; requests for dependency usage are filed via tickets and acknowledged; during decommissioning, dependencies are used to determine the specific order in which resources like DNS and network connections must be deallocated.
Rules_and_Constraints: Dependency use must be positively acknowledged by the external groups; RPC limits must be documented along with plans for what happens if those limits are exceeded.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During decommissioning, certain resources cannot be deallocated before others because complex dependencies may require network connectivity or other services to remain active until the process is complete.

Concept_Name: RPC limits
Core_Definition: Constraints or thresholds placed on the number of remote procedure calls made to dependent systems to ensure they can handle the traffic volume.
Key_Actors: The new service being launched; external groups or dependency teams; Launch Readiness Engineer.
Operational_Workflow: Identify all systems the service depends on; determine which specific RPC limits are in place with these dependencies; obtain positive acknowledgment and confirmation from external groups that they can handle the projected traffic; document the ticket number where the dependency use and QPS rate were requested; define the consequences or system behavior if these limits are exceeded.
Rules_and_Constraints: Confirmation must be obtained from external groups regarding their ability to handle traffic; usage and QPS rates must be formally requested and acknowledged via the ticket system; dependency and data flow diagrams must be linked in documentation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The launch readiness process specifically requires planning for what happens when RPC limits are exceeded.

Concept_Name: Monitoring
Core_Definition: The ongoing process and strategy of observing all subsystems to detect outages, handle exceptional events, and ensure the system meets operating parameters.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; automated monitoring systems; subsystems; Launch Readiness Engineer.
Operational_Workflow: Define a monitoring strategy and document what is being monitored; ensure all subsystems are covered by the monitoring system; send alerts via SMS or pager when emergency issues or pending outages are detected; generate tickets for non-urgent situations; track and categorize alerts in an electronic journal; adjust or correct spammy alerts to maintain operational hygiene.
Rules_and_Constraints: All subsystems must be monitored; the number of false alarm alerts in the last month must be below a specified threshold; the number of alerts received in a typical week should be less than a defined limit; monitoring must be capable of initiating emergency issue responses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: False alarm alerts or spammy alerts are considered deviations that must be adjusted or corrected in a timely manner to ensure the monitoring system remains effective.

Concept_Name: Dashboard
Core_Definition: A visual status display used to provide visibility into major subsystems or the progress of complex processes and handoffs.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; project managers; service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Establish a dashboard for all major subsystems during the service launch phase; integrate automated notifications when handoffs between teams occur; use the dashboard to allow people to see the status of orders or processes as they move through approval stages.
Rules_and_Constraints: A dashboard must exist for all major subsystems before a launch is approved; dashboards should facilitate dealing with exceptions and problems by providing clear status visibility.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Dashboards are specifically mentioned as a way to reduce the need for project managers when a process has many complex handoffs between teams.

Concept_Name: metrics dashboards
Core_Definition: Specific visual displays of system measurements and performance data translated into business-oriented language.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; business stakeholders; Launch Readiness Engineer.
Operational_Workflow: Create visual representations of system metrics; translate technical data into business terms; verify the existence and terminology of these dashboards during the launch readiness review.
Rules_and_Constraints: Metrics dashboards must be framed in business terms rather than technical terms.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: false alarm alerts
Core_Definition: Monitoring notifications that trigger without a valid underlying issue requiring immediate action or representing a true service failure.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; Site Reliability Engineers; Launch Readiness Engineers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems detect conditions and send alerts to the oncall person; the oncall person identifies if an alert is a false alarm or spammy; as part of operational hygiene these alerts are adjusted or corrected in a timely manner to prevent future occurrences; during the service launch process a Launch Readiness Engineer audits the number of false alarms to ensure they stay below a specified threshold.
Rules_and_Constraints: The number of false alarm alerts in the month prior to a service launch must be less than a specific threshold defined by the organization; alerts must be adjusted or corrected in a timely manner as part of operational hygiene; oncall shifts should be managed to minimize the receipt of spammy alerts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: oncall schedule
Core_Definition: A rotating arrangement that designates specific team members to be available as first responders to service outages or automated alerts.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; team managers; automation systems for scheduling.
Operational_Workflow: Team members take turns being responsible for emergency issues in a rotation such as an eight-week cycle; the designated person responds to alerts received via SMS or pager; the responder employs quick fixes to restore the service to normal parameters; the responder documents the event in an electronic alert journal and files bugs for long-term fixes; for SLA violations the responder writes a blameless postmortem report; the shift concludes with an emailed report to the entire team passing on status and trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should consist of at least eight people at one location or six people at multiple locations; shifts should catch no more than two events per person; the team manager must be part of the rotation to stay aware of operational load; oncall must be managed to avoid unsustainable stress and drive long-term problem prevention; excess operational work must overflow to the development team if it exceeds established caps.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the event of a major crisis the oncall person may pull other team members away from project work to assist; if more than 25 percent of a team is dedicated to oncall and ticket duty it indicates a serious problem with firefighting or a lack of automation.

Concept_Name: Disaster Preparedness
Core_Definition: The process of planning and testing for extreme or unexpected events to ensure service survival and recovery.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineers; operations teams; launch leads; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Teams develop specific plans for handling extreme scenarios such as first-day usage being ten times greater than expected; the team demonstrates that these plans have been tested ahead of time; the status of backups and the success of restore tests are verified; the Launch Readiness Engineer audits these plans as part of the formal launch review process.
Rules_and_Constraints: A plan must exist for handling significant traffic spikes beyond initial expectations; backups must be functional and restores must have been successfully tested before a service launch can be approved.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Backups
Core_Definition: Processes and systems used to preserve data so it can be recovered following a failure or service decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; Launch Readiness Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Backups are established for all relevant service data; periodic tests are conducted to perform restores and verify data integrity; during the launch readiness review the status of backups and restores is audited; during service decommissioning old data is archived or erased as required.
Rules_and_Constraints: Backups must be functional and restores must be tested as a requirement for service launch; data must be archived or securely erased from hardware during the decommissioning phase of the service life cycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Restores
Core_Definition: The process of recovering data or system state from backups to ensure disaster preparedness.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineer; launch team.
Operational_Workflow: Within the context of a Launch Readiness Review, teams must verify that backup systems are functional and then actively test the recovery process to ensure the service can be restored in the event of failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Restores must be tested prior to a service launch as part of the disaster preparedness checklist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Operational Hygiene
Core_Definition: A set of practices and standards used to maintain system health through proactive alert management and rigorous bug tracking.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineer; operations team; launch team.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves adjusting or correcting spammy alerts in a timely manner; filing bugs for all issues including minor annoyances or known workarounds to raise visibility; and prioritizing stability-related bug fixes over new features.
Rules_and_Constraints: Stability-related bugs must take priority over new features; spammy alerts must be corrected promptly; a system must be in place to ensure the total number of open bugs remains low.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Even issues with commonly known workarounds must have bugs filed rather than being ignored to ensure long-term visibility.

Concept_Name: spammy alerts
Core_Definition: Frequent or low-value notifications from a monitoring system that may not represent urgent or actionable problems.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; operations team; Launch Readiness Engineer.
Operational_Workflow: During the service launch phase and ongoing maintenance, these alerts are identified and then adjusted or corrected to prevent them from overwhelming the staff or obscuring real issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: These alerts must be adjusted or corrected in a timely manner; the number of false alarms and the total number of alerts received per week should be kept below a specific threshold defined during the launch readiness review.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Bug
Core_Definition: A software defect or system flaw that introduces instability or requires technical intervention.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations and SRE staff; customers; Launch Readiness Engineer.
Operational_Workflow: Bugs are identified during software roll-outs, through monitoring alerts, or via user reports; they are then filed in a tracking system to raise visibility, prioritized for fixing, and resolved through new software releases or workarounds.
Rules_and_Constraints: Bug fixes should be prioritized over new feature requests to maintain system stability; all issues must have bugs filed even if they have known workarounds; the number of open bugs must be kept low.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A software release may introduce no new bugs but still cause instability due to the process of shifting workloads away from machines during the upgrade.

Concept_Name: security team
Core_Definition: A specialized organizational unit responsible for evaluating and authorizing the safety of a new service prior to its release.
Key_Actors: The security team members; service developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: As part of the Launch Readiness Review process, the security team performs an audit of the service to identify potential risks; the team must then provide formal approval before the service can move forward with its launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service must be audited and approved by the security team as a mandatory step in the launch checklist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: privacy audit
Core_Definition: A formal review process conducted before a service launch to ensure the system handles data according to privacy standards and requirements.
Key_Actors: Reviewing auditors; the service team responsible for the launch.
Operational_Workflow: The audit is initiated as a requirement of the launch readiness checklist; auditors examine the service for privacy concerns; if issues are found, the service team must remediate them; the audit is considered successful only after all discovered issues are resolved.
Rules_and_Constraints: A service cannot be launched until the audit is completed and all identified issues have been remediated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: launch lead
Core_Definition: A single person who takes a leadership or coordinator role to manage the complex moving parts of a new service launch.
Key_Actors: The launch lead; developer teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The launch lead works through the launch checklist by delegating work to various team members; they file bugs for any omissions and track all identified issues until the launch is approved and executed; they may also coordinate problem resolution during the period immediately following the launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: If developer and operations teams are highly separate, one person from each team may be selected to represent their respective groups to the lead.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The launch lead manages post-launch problem resolution if issues arise after the service has gone live.

Concept_Name: Google load balancing system
Core_Definition: A technical infrastructure component used by Google to distribute traffic across its services.
Key_Actors: Google service teams; SRE engineers.
Operational_Workflow: During the Launch Readiness Review (LRR) process, a team must ensure their service is configured to use the load balancing system correctly; SRE engineers verify this technical implementation as part of the checklist requirements before the service is made visible externally.
Rules_and_Constraints: Proper usage of the system is a mandatory technical checklist item that must be verified by an SRE before launch approval.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Launch Readiness Review (LRR)
Core_Definition: A formal process and checklist used to evaluate a new service before its public launch to ensure it meets technical, operational, and business standards.
Key_Actors: Launch Readiness Engineer (LRE); Launch Committee; Launch Lead; Developer teams; Operations teams; Stakeholders including Marketing, Legal, Security, and Privacy teams.
Operational_Workflow: The service team completes a launch readiness review survey or checklist before meeting with an LRE; a single launch lead is designated to coordinate the process by delegating work and tracking all issues; the LRE or Launch Committee audits the responses and shepherds the team through the requirements; marketing, legal, security, and privacy departments provide necessary sign-offs; once all checklist items and audits are remediated and approved, the service is made visible externally; the launch lead may also coordinate post-launch problem resolution.
Rules_and_Constraints: The checklist must be documented, advertised, and integrated into the company culture; it must include technical items, cautionary lessons from past failures, and business-related sign-offs; teams are encouraged to use the checklist throughout development rather than just at the end; stability-related bugs must take priority over new features; the review must verify that disaster plans, such as handling 10 times the expected traffic, have been tested.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If launches are rare, the checklist serves to maintain organizational memory; if launches are frequent, it facilitates knowledge sharing among engineers of different experience levels; teams may fail to allocate time for launch planning and surprise operations at the last minute if they are unaware of the checklist requirements.

Concept_Name: Decomm
Core_Definition: The final stage of the service life cycle which involves systematically turning off a service and reclaiming its components.
Key_Actors: Product management; Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the removal of users; this is followed by the deallocation of resources such as network and database connections; finally, the process concludes with the disposal of resources including hardware and storage media.
Rules_and_Constraints: A checklist should be maintained and updated over time to ensure tasks are completed in the correct order and nothing is missed; the process must be done completely to prevent resources from remaining allocated unnecessarily.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If decommissioning is done incorrectly or specific steps are missed, resources will remain allocated and unused.

Concept_Name: removal of users
Core_Definition: The first major phase of decommissioning a service where the existing user base is transitioned off the system.
Key_Actors: Product management.
Operational_Workflow: Product management notifies users that they must move to a different service; users may be technically moved to a replacement service; user data is either moved to a new location or archived for long-term storage.
Rules_and_Constraints: This phase is typically a prerequisite for further decommissioning steps, as many resources cannot be deallocated until the last user has left the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: deallocation of resources
Core_Definition: The phase of the decommissioning process where technical configurations and connections are disabled and shut down.
Key_Actors: Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: DNS entries are removed; machines are powered off; database connections are disabled; network connections are removed only after any other services that depend on them have been deallocated.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resource deallocation involves complex dependencies where certain items cannot be removed before others; for example, a DNS entry is typically not removed until the associated machine is no longer in use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: disposal of resources
Core_Definition: The third and final phase of decommissioning a service that involves the final handling of storage media and physical equipment.
Key_Actors: Operations teams who are responsible for the entire service life cycle.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins after the removal of users and the deallocation of resources; it involves securely erasing disks and other media; finally, the hardware is repurposed, sold, or scrapped.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations should use a checklist that is updated over time to ensure the process is done completely and in the right order; disks and media must be securely erased before the hardware is repurposed, sold, or disposed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DNS entries
Core_Definition: Network resource records that must be removed during the deallocation phase of a service’s decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: DNS entries are removed as part of resource deallocation once users have been removed from the service; this task must be coordinated according to complex dependencies where certain resources cannot be deallocated before others.
Rules_and_Constraints: A DNS entry is typically not removed until the machine associated with it is no longer in use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: securely erasing disks
Core_Definition: A security task performed during the resource disposal phase to clear storage media of information.
Key_Actors: Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: This task is performed after users are removed and resources are deallocated; it is a mandatory step that occurs before hardware is repurposed, sold, or scrapped.
Rules_and_Constraints: This must be performed on disks and other media as part of the final disposal of resources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: scrubbing data
Core_Definition: The process of removing information from hardware before it is reused or discarded.
Key_Actors: Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Data is scrubbed or erased from hardware during the resource disposal phase of decommissioning; this happens after user removal and resource deallocation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hardware must be erased or scrubbed before it is repurposed, sold, or disposed of.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: life-cycle management
Core_Definition: The set of activities and processes required to manage a service from its initial launch through its ongoing maintenance and upgrades to its final decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; product management; Launch Readiness Engineers or Launch Committees; launch leads; marketing, security, and legal departments; users.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with a service launch involving readiness reviews and checklists to ensure the system is prepared for traffic; it continues with maintenance tasks including emergency response and nonemergency requests; periodic upgrades for software and hardware are performed alongside project work to scale or evolve the system; the cycle concludes with decommissioning where users are removed, dependencies are deallocated, and resources are disposed of or repurposed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations must meet or exceed service level agreements (SLAs); work must prioritize availability, speed, performance, security, and capacity planning; checklists must be used to maintain organizational memory for infrequent tasks; new hardware must undergo qualification to test for compatibility and performance regressions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services may experience failed public launches if they become overloaded by unexpected traffic volumes; decommissioning can result in resource leakage if the checklist is followed incorrectly; hardware disposal requires secure erasure of data to prevent security breaches before the equipment is sold or scrapped.

Concept_Name: interacting with stakeholders
Core_Definition: The ongoing effort to maintain relationships with and fulfill the requirements of people and organizations who have an interest in a service’s success or rely on its functionality.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; product management; internal service teams; customers; project managers; marketing and legal departments.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams identify and communicate with internal and external parties who depend on the service; they receive and triage requests via a ticket tracking system; team members provide consulting and documentation to help stakeholders use the service; they collaborate with product managers on service roadmaps; and they coordinate with various departments like legal and marketing to secure approvals for service launches.
Rules_and_Constraints: SRE efforts should focus on select business metrics rather than being diverted by individual user priorities; interactions should be minimized through high-quality introductory documentation and user guides; common requests should be transitioned to self-service portals or APIs; communication mediums should be chosen based on the fidelity required, using voice or video for intense discussions and email for lasting records.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams may surprise operations with last-minute launch requests if they are unaware of the required planning effort; tracking communication across large growing teams can become a manual burden; some labor-intensive tasks like technology evaluation cannot be fully automated but can be standardized into assembly-line processes.

Concept_Name: process improvement and automation
Core_Definition: The practice of using software engineering to replace manual labor with automated systems and refining procedures to increase operational efficiency and scalability.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; datacenter technicians; project managers.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify manual tasks that are repetitive or do not scale; they write code to automate these functions such as provisioning machines or installing software; for processes not yet automated, they create procedural playbooks to ensure repeatability; they apply statistical analysis to measurements to improve system throughput; and they streamline human processes by creating self-service interfaces or automated notification systems for hand-offs.
Rules_and_Constraints: SREs must be able to solve nontrivial problems by writing code; manual labor known as toil must be capped at fifty percent of the operational load; automation is a requirement for scaling systems to thousands of machines; teams must prioritize automation based on the return on investment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tasks that happen infrequently or change too often may not be worth the investment of time required to automate them; unique edge cases in manual processes often become unnecessary once an automated common case provides superior service; human processes like technology evaluation and team negotiations cannot be fully eliminated but can be accelerated through automation.

Concept_Name: Emergency Issues
Core_Definition: Exceptional or unexpected events such as service outages or pending failures that require immediate response to restore normal operating parameters.
Key_Actors: Oncall team members; first responders; monitoring systems; users.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring system detects an issue and triggers an alert to the designated oncall person; the responder implements a quick fix to return the service to normal as specified by the SLA; the alert is recorded in an electronic journal to facilitate trend analysis; the team conducts a root cause investigation and produces a blameless postmortem report; finally bugs are filed to ensure long-term solutions are implemented through project work.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall teams should have at least eight members at one location or six members at each of multiple locations; shifts should catch no more than two events; every event must result in a written postmortem focusing on technology and process; postmortems must be blameless to encourage organizational learning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Major crises may require the oncall person to pull other team members away from their focused project work; if oncall shifts are too long or occur too frequently, the responder may be overloaded with follow-up work; "spammy alerts" or false alarms must be adjusted promptly to maintain the health of the oncall rotation.

Concept_Name: alert
Core_Definition: A notification sent by a monitoring system to notify an oncall responder of an emergency issue, outage, or pending outage that can be prevented. It is typically delivered via SMS, text message, or pager to ensure immediate attention.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; oncall team member; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system detects a condition indicating an outage or potential failure; an alert is generated and sent to the person currently oncall; the oncall person works the issue until it is resolved, often employing a quick fix to restore service; the alert is categorized and annotated in an electronic alert journal to track trends; the oncall person updates the relevant playbook entry and files a bug if a long-term fix is needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall teams should aim for a maximum of two events per oncall shift; every alert should have a corresponding playbook entry; false alarm alerts and spammy alerts must be adjusted or corrected in a timely manner; major alerts resulting in SLA violations require a written postmortem report.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some automated systems may detect situations that are not urgent enough for an immediate alert and instead create a request ticket; alerts may be false alarms that require the monitoring system to be adjusted.

Concept_Name: monitoring system
Core_Definition: An automated system responsible for watching for trouble across all subsystems of a service and collecting measurements to ensure reliability. It serves as the primary mechanism for detecting outages and reporting performance against service level agreements.
Key_Actors: Operations team; oncall staff; developers; subsystems.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors all subsystems and generates dashboards for major components; it tracks metrics in both technical and business terms; when trouble is detected, it sends alerts via SMS or pager for urgent issues or opens tickets for non-urgent situations; it broadcasts alerts into team chat rooms via bots.
Rules_and_Constraints: All subsystems must be monitored; the number of false alarms must be kept low; monitoring should be automated as part of the assembly-line approach to operations; configurations should be periodically updated to meet team standards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The system may produce spammy alerts that need to be silenced or adjusted to prevent oncall fatigue; it may fail to detect issues if a subsystem is not properly integrated into the monitoring strategy.

Concept_Name: SMS or pager
Core_Definition: The communication channels used by a monitoring system to transmit urgent alerts to the person currently oncall. These methods are intended to interrupt the responder to ensure immediate action on emergency issues.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; oncall team member.
Operational_Workflow: When the monitoring system identifies an emergency issue or pending outage, it initiates a message; the message is delivered to the oncall responder's mobile device or pager; the responder acknowledges the alert and begins the resolution process.
Rules_and_Constraints: These channels are reserved for emergency issues that indicate an outage or a condition that must be prevented immediately; their use is a primary feature of being oncall.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Normal Requests
Core_Definition: A category of operational work involving repeatable processes that have not yet been automated, non-urgent trouble reports, informational questions, and initial consulting. These tasks are typically managed through a request ticket system rather than through immediate alerts.
Key_Actors: Internal users; other service teams; ticket duty staff member; ticket tracking system.
Operational_Workflow: A request is initiated by a user or an automated system through a ticket; a dedicated team member on ticket duty triages and prioritizes the incoming tickets; the staff member works on the requests, which may include activating services or updating documentation; if a ticket cannot be completed during a shift, it is included in a shift report for the next person in the rotation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Response times for these requests are generally measured in days; one person on the team should be dedicated to ticket duty to allow others to focus on project work; the goal is to eliminate these requests over time through self-service portals and better documentation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Urgent tickets may require daily status updates while others have longer stretches between updates; customers may be poked every three days if the team is waiting for a reply to a ticket.

Concept_Name: request ticket system
Core_Definition: A system used to receive and manage normal operational requests, non-urgent trouble reports, informational questions, and initial consulting. It serves as the primary intake mechanism for work that does not qualify as an emergency.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; internal users such as other service teams; automated monitoring systems that detect non-urgent conditions.
Operational_Workflow: A request is initiated by a customer or an automated system and enters the ticket system. The team member currently assigned to ticket duty triages and prioritizes the request. The assigned staff member may batch similar tickets together for efficiency and provides regular status updates to the requester. If a ticket is not completed by the end of a shift, its status is documented in a shift report to facilitate a handoff to the next person in the rotation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Response times for tickets are generally measured in days rather than minutes; operations teams should aim to eliminate the need for tickets by improving documentation or creating self-service portals; every ticket created by an automated system should have a corresponding playbook entry; tickets should be used to identify trends for long-term process improvement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a ticket is waiting for a customer response, the customer should be politely poked every three days to ensure the issue is not forgotten; automated systems may generate tickets for issues that are not urgent enough to trigger a pager alert.

Concept_Name: Silos of responsibility
Core_Definition: An organizational structure where an operations team is divided into subteams that focus exclusively on a single source or category of work. This arrangement is described as a hindrance to effective process improvement.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; subteams; engineering management.
Operational_Workflow: Management organizes the team into separate groups, such as one subteam for projects and another for emergency response. This separation prevents the people involved in a process from being the ones who improve or automate it. Instead, process improvement is left to observers, which is less effective than having the practitioners drive the changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should avoid creating subteams based on work categories; process improvement is best performed by those actively involved in the operational work rather than external observers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Firefighting
Core_Definition: An unsustainable operational state where the team is constantly interrupted by emergency issues and alerts, leaving no time for project work or system improvements.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; team managers.
Operational_Workflow: The team spends its time reacting to a continuous stream of emergencies and alerts. Because no time is dedicated to fixing the underlying causes of these issues, the team remains stuck in a cycle of reacting to outages. This state continues until the team members become burned out.
Rules_and_Constraints: If more than 25 percent of a team is required for ticket duty and oncall, the team has a serious problem with firefighting; managers should participate in the operational rotation to stay aware of the firefighting load; project work must be protected and prioritized to automate the tasks that cause firefighting.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During a major crisis, the person oncall may be forced to pull other team members away from their dedicated project work to assist with the emergency.

Concept_Name: ticket duty
Core_Definition: A rotating responsibility where a specific team member is assigned to focus on triaging and responding to non-emergency requests from internal customers.
Key_Actors: The assigned operations team member; internal customers such as other service teams; team manager.
Operational_Workflow: A team member is assigned to the role for a specific period, such as one week in an eight-week rotation. The individual focuses on requests in the ticket system, including questions, service activations, and problem reports. They batch similar tasks and perform deep-dives into issues to fix broken processes or update documentation. At the end of the shift, they send an email report to the entire team detailing trends and the status of pending items for the next person.
Rules_and_Constraints: The person on ticket duty should prioritize tickets over other project work; only one or a small number of people should be assigned to this duty at a time to allow the rest of the team to focus on projects; the individual should use the time to improve playbooks and reduce future work; management should not expect the person on ticket duty to complete other project milestones during their shift.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the volume of tickets is low, the person may work on other projects as a bonus; if a ticket cannot be finished during the shift, it must be handed off via a shift report so the next person can continue the work.

Concept_Name: operational rotation
Core_Definition: A system where team members take turns being responsible for specific categories of work, such as emergency issues and normal requests, to ensure all tasks receive attention while allowing others to focus on project work.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; oncall person; ticket duty person; team manager.
Operational_Workflow: The team establishes a cycle where members rotate through specific duties; for example, in an eight-person team on an eight-week cycle, one member is assigned as oncall to respond to alerts for a week, while another is assigned to ticket duty to triage and respond to request tickets; the remaining team members focus on project-focused days; at the end of a shift, the responsible person provides a report to the next person in the rotation; the team manager also participates in the rotation to stay aware of operational load and firefighting.
Rules_and_Constraints: SRE operational load should be capped at 50 percent; oncall teams should have at least eight people at one location or six at multiple locations; members should aim for a maximum of two events per oncall shift; rotations should be limited to specific individuals rather than large meetings to ensure smoother handoffs; management should not expect project work to be completed by those currently assigned to oncall or ticket duty.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If more than 25 percent of a team is dedicated to ticket duty and oncall, it indicates a serious problem with firefighting and lack of automation; if oncall shifts are too long or too close together, the staff member may become overloaded with follow-up work or lack time to complete long-term fixes; major crises may require the oncall person to pull project-focused members away from their work to assist.

Concept_Name: Handoffs
Core_Definition: The process of transferring responsibility and status information regarding ongoing operational tasks or tickets from one rotating team member to the next.
Key_Actors: Outgoing oncall or ticket duty member; incoming oncall or ticket duty member; project managers for complex inter-team transfers.
Operational_Workflow: The outgoing person prepares a shift report or email for the entire team; the report includes a log of alerts received, actions taken, and the status of any long-running tickets or incomplete tasks; in cases involving complex handoffs between teams, a status dashboard or automated notification system may be used to reduce the need for project managers; the transfer is ideally conducted between two individuals to keep the process smooth.
Rules_and_Constraints: Status updates for urgent tickets should be posted daily; long-running tickets require a status update in the shift report so the next person can pick up where the previous person left off; customers waiting for a reply should be contacted every three days to ensure issues are not forgotten.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Complex handoffs between different teams may require additional coordination through status dashboards or specific project management roles to avoid communication burdens; if a handoff is not managed via a specific person-to-person rotation, it may result in less efficient large-scale meetings.

Concept_Name: Solo projects
Core_Definition: Project work assigned to or undertaken by a single individual rather than a collaborative small team.
Key_Actors: Individual team members.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Project work is best done in small teams rather than as solo projects; individuals should seek feedback to ensure they are not working on items with only marginal benefit.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Solo projects can damage a team by making members feel disconnected or by permitting individuals to work without constructive feedback; they may result in members working on projects that do not align with team benefits.

Concept_Name: Peer review
Core_Definition: A collaborative process where team members examine each other's work or designs to provide feedback and ensure quality.
Key_Actors: Team members; project members.
Operational_Workflow: Team members submit designs or project work for evaluation by their colleagues; feedback is provided frequently to allow for course corrections; the process aims to bring multiple perspectives to a project to improve the final outcome.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designs are considered better when they include at least some level of peer review; feedback must be frequent to be effective for course corrections.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A lack of peer review, often seen in solo projects, can lead to individuals working on projects that have marginal benefit or lack technical oversight; conversely, very large teams may fail to benefit from review if they become stalled by a lack of consensus.

Concept_Name: Agile methodology
Core_Definition: A project management approach where systems are evolved through many small projects and frequent changes rather than large, sudden updates.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; small project teams.
Operational_Workflow: Work is organized into small teams to perform project tasks; teams focus on shipping progress quickly to ensure visibility to management and the wider team; frequent feedback is used to make necessary course corrections during the development process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Projects should be performed by small teams to prevent lack of consensus or feelings of disconnection; designs should undergo peer review; the methodology should favor many small, incremental changes over time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Project-Focused Days
Core_Definition: Days where the primary responsibility of operational staff is to work on projects that automate or optimize the team's responsibilities.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; stakeholders; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The staff member starts the day by checking the bug tracking system for assigned or high-priority unassigned tasks; they spend the majority of their time developing software for automation or optimization; they may also perform non-software tasks such as working with stakeholders on future requirements or shepherding service launches.
Rules_and_Constraints: Most days for operational staff should be designated as project days; staff should be given long periods of uninterrupted time to maximize productivity; project work should be the main priority for those not assigned to oncall or ticket duty.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Staff members on project-focused days may be pulled away to assist if a major crisis occurs that the oncall person cannot handle alone.

Concept_Name: bug tracking system
Core_Definition: A centralized system used by a team to prioritize, track, and manage issues and project tasks.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; team members.
Operational_Workflow: Team members use the system to review their assigned bugs and search for high-priority unassigned issues; bugs are filed to document problems, minor annoyances, or technical debt; the system tracks the progress and status of these items to ensure visibility.
Rules_and_Constraints: A single system should be used to reduce the time spent checking multiple sources for status updates; bugs should be filed for all issues to raise visibility; organizations should have a system in place to ensure the total number of open bugs remains low.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Software development
Core_Definition: The practice of writing code to automate manual tasks and solve nontrivial problems within an operational environment.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; common staffing pool engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify manual tasks that require repetition; they perform the task manually at first and then write code to automate the remaining work; this development often follows Agile principles by evolving the system through small, frequent improvements.
Rules_and_Constraints: SREs must possess enough coding experience to solve nontrivial problems and communicate effectively with developers; operational load should be capped at fifty percent to allow time for development; development should focus on replacing manual labor to achieve a higher return on investment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Software development is not applied to tasks that occur infrequently, are excessively difficult to automate, or involve human processes like negotiations and relationship management.

Concept_Name: virtual machines
Core_Definition: A type of machine used in distributed systems that provides functions or APIs and allows for remote management without physical hardware contact.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; automation systems; internal customers.
Operational_Workflow: Virtual machines are provisioned to host servers and software; customers request machines through manual or automated processes; automation can reduce provisioning time from days to minutes without administrator intervention; operations staff manage these machines from remote virtual offices.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation of virtual machine provisioning is a requirement for scaling; administrators should prioritize automating common provisioning cases even if unique edge cases are initially excluded; manual processes should be eliminated because they do not scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Customers may claim their needs are unique snowflakes to justify manual provisioning; manual processes often lead to users waiting days for a system administrator to become available.

Concept_Name: Datacenter technicians
Core_Definition: On-site professionals who perform manual hardware tasks at a datacenter location on behalf of remote operations teams.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians; remote operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff manage systems remotely; when a task requires physical intervention with the hardware, datacenter technicians act as remote hands to apply the necessary physical changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: These technicians are utilized because operations staff typically work far from the physical machines and heavily rely on remote management and virtual machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Oncall Days
Core_Definition: Scheduled days during which a team member acts as the first responder to alerts and outages while otherwise focusing on projects.
Key_Actors: Oncall staff members; monitoring systems; other operations team members.
Operational_Workflow: The assigned member works on project tasks until an alert is received via pager or SMS; the person works the issue until it is resolved, often using a quick fix to restore service levels; after resolution, the staff member logs the event in an alert journal, files bugs for long-term fixes, updates the playbook, and conducts root cause analysis; for major events, the member writes a postmortem report.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall duty should be scheduled in a rotation; oncall shifts should catch no more than two outage events to allow for follow-up work; teams should have at least eight people at one location or six people at multiple locations; the goal of oncall is to protect the rest of the team from interruptions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a major crisis occurs, the oncall person is permitted to pull other team members away from their project work to assist with the response.

Concept_Name: alert journal
Core_Definition: An electronic record used by operations staff to categorize and annotate details about alerts received during a shift.
Key_Actors: Oncall staff members.
Operational_Workflow: Once an emergency alert has been resolved, the oncall person enters the details and categorizes the event within the journal; the journal is used by the team to discover trends and recurring issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Annotating the journal is a required follow-up step to ensure organizational memory and to raise the visibility of issues for long-term fixes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: long-term fixes
Core_Definition: Solutions designed to permanently address the underlying issues of a problem to prevent its recurrence and improve system stability.
Key_Actors: Oncall team members; Site Reliability Engineers.
Operational_Workflow: An oncall member responds to an outage or alert; the member resolves the immediate issue; if a quick fix was used, the member files a bug requesting a longer-term solution; the member uses dedicated time during or between shifts to perform the required long-term fix.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall shifts must be short enough to allow members time to follow through on each event with a long-term solution; oncall teams must be sufficiently staffed to ensure members have time to pursue such projects between shifts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If oncall shifts are too long, the person will be overloaded with follow-up work; if shifts are too close together, there will not be enough time to complete the long-term fixes.

Concept_Name: quick fix
Core_Definition: A solution employed during an emergency to return a service to its normal operating parameters as rapidly as possible, often on a temporary basis.
Key_Actors: Oncall person.
Operational_Workflow: An alert is received by the oncall person; the issue is investigated; a quick fix is applied to restore service functionality; a bug is filed to ensure a long-term fix is eventually implemented.
Rules_and_Constraints: Returning the service to normal operating parameters is considered paramount during an active alert.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SLA violation
Core_Definition: An event or outage that causes a service to fail to meet the operating parameters and reliability standards specified in its Service Level Agreement.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; stakeholders; user community.
Operational_Workflow: A service failure or performance drop occurs; the oncall person responds to the event; once resolved, a written postmortem report is created; the report is shared within the organization and sometimes with the user community.
Rules_and_Constraints: All services must have an SLA to serve as the standard for work measurement; any major SLA-related event must be followed by a written postmortem; postmortems must be blameless and focus on process and technology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Root Cause Identification
Core_Definition: An investigation conducted after an alert or outage to determine the fundamental source of the problem.
Key_Actors: Oncall person.
Operational_Workflow: An alert is resolved; the oncall person conducts an investigation to ascertain why the problem occurred; the findings are used to create postmortem reports and file bugs; these actions raise the visibility of issues to ensure they are addressed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process is required to avoid muddling through ad hoc workarounds and to ensure the system is improved over time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: uninterrupted time
Core_Definition: Long periods of work free from distractions which are identified as a key factor in software developer productivity.
Key_Actors: Software developers; operations team members; the assigned oncall person.
Operational_Workflow: To provide this time for the majority of the team, a specific person is assigned to oncall duty to handle all incoming alerts and emergencies; this allows the rest of the team to remain focused on project work rather than being distracted by immediate issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: A specific person must be assigned to oncall duty at any given time to act as a shield for the rest of the team; this enables the others to maintain their focus on developing software that automates or optimizes responsibilities.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a major crisis appears, the oncall person will pull other team members away from their projects and interrupted their work to provide assistance.

Concept_Name: Ticket Duty Days
Core_Definition: Specific days in a rotation where an operations team member is assigned to focus on triaging and responding to non-emergency requests from internal customers.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; internal users of the service such as other service teams using an API.
Operational_Workflow: A team member is assigned a week in a rotation where their highest priority is triaging and responding to request tickets; the individual checks for unassigned issues or long-running tickets from the previous shift; they may batch similar tickets for efficiency and perform deep-dives into bugs or documentation updates; at the end of the shift they email a report to the team to pass on status information to the next person.
Rules_and_Constraints: Team members should work on other projects only if there is remaining time after ticket responsibilities; ticket duty should be treated as the person's main project for that period; management should not expect other projects to get done during this time; if more than twenty-five percent of a team is dedicated to ticket duty and oncall, it indicates a serious problem with firefighting and lack of automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a ticket is not completed by the end of a shift, its status must be included in a shift report so the next person in the rotation can pick up where the previous person left off.

Concept_Name: customer support representatives
Core_Definition: Personnel assigned to handle service requests and tickets specifically coming from external users.
Key_Actors: External users; customer support representatives; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: External tickets should be handled by these representatives rather than the operations team members who are performing ticket duty for internal customers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: response time
Core_Definition: The expected timeframe within which a team acknowledges or addresses a non-emergency request or ticket.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; internal customers; automated monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: While emergency alerts require very fast reaction times, normal tickets have a response time measured in days; for tickets waiting on customer replies, a policy may dictate a follow-up every three days; for urgent tickets, status updates might be posted daily.
Rules_and_Constraints: Response times for tickets are generally slower than those for oncall alerts; the expected response time is typically measured in days.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: poked
Core_Definition: A polite follow-up communication sent to a customer to ensure an issue is not forgotten while waiting for their response.
Key_Actors: The operations staff member on ticket duty; the customer or internal user of the service.
Operational_Workflow: A staff member on ticket duty identifies an open request where they are waiting for a reply from the customer; if the customer has not responded within a certain timeframe, the staff member sends a polite reminder; the ticket remains open until the customer provides the necessary information to move forward.
Rules_and_Constraints: A common policy is to poke the customer every three days to ensure the issue remains active and is not forgotten.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: shift report
Core_Definition: An email sent to the entire team at the end of an oncall or ticket duty rotation to communicate status, trends, and advice.
Key_Actors: The outgoing oncall or ticket duty staff member; the incoming staff member; the entire operations team.
Operational_Workflow: As a staff member completes their oncall or ticket duty rotation, they compile a summary of their activities; the report includes trends noticed during the shift, advice for the person taking over, and the status of any incomplete tickets; for oncall shifts, the report also includes a log of alerts received and the responses taken; the report is emailed to the team to facilitate a smooth handoff.
Rules_and_Constraints: The report must include trends and advice; it should contain status information for the next person in the rotation; oncall reports specifically require a log of alerts and responses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Handoffs are described as smoother when rotations involve specific individuals passing information to one another rather than large team meetings.

Concept_Name: FAQ
Core_Definition: A document containing frequently asked questions and answers used to help users find information without opening a support ticket.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; internal users or other service teams.
Operational_Workflow: An operations staff member receives a ticket requesting information; instead of simply answering the ticket, the staff member adds the requested information to the service FAQ; the staff member then directs the user to the FAQ; this process helps make users more self-sufficient and reduces the future ticket load for the team.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should have a goal of eliminating information-request tickets by improving the FAQ; directing users to the FAQ is preferred over providing one-off manual answers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: user documentation
Core_Definition: Materials provided to stakeholders and users to enable self-sufficiency and explain how to use a service.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; stakeholders; internal service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams identify the need for information among their users and stakeholders; they create materials such as introductory documentation, best practices recommendations, and style guides; these materials are made accessible to stakeholders so they can solve problems independently; when common questions arise through tickets, the staff updates the documentation to prevent future inquiries.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation becomes increasingly important as the number of teams using a service grows; providing good documentation is a strategy for streamlining human processes that cannot be automated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Video instruction is mentioned as a useful supplement to written documentation, especially if it can be created easily by recording existing presentations.

Concept_Name: web-based portal
Core_Definition: A self-service interface or API used to automate common requests and eliminate the need for manual intervention from operations teams.
Key_Actors: Stakeholders; customers; internal service teams; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: A user accesses a web user interface or API to provide information such as capacity forecasts or requests for service activation; the automated system collects the data or performs the configuration change; the system may integrate with other internal systems like supply-chain management to complete the request without manual status reports or ticket processing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Portals should be used to make common requests self-service; they are intended to make requests like service activation, allocations, or configuration changes obsolete; they should be designed to reduce the need for project managers or manual tracking.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a process has many complex handoffs, the system should provide a status dashboard to allow people to deal with just the exceptions and problems.

Concept_Name: Focus or Theme
Core_Definition: An organizing strategy where a team selects a specific category of issues or a subsystem to address for a duration of time.
Key_Actors: Operational team; team members.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies a theme such as security, monitoring, or a specific subservice; all team members commit to focusing on issues related to that theme until the work is complete; once the goals of the current theme are met, the team transitions to the next theme.
Rules_and_Constraints: Themes can be changed periodically or upon completion of the current focus; the strategy is used to enhance team cohesion and productivity by ensuring everyone is familiar with the same code base; it can serve as a motivational tool for the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Toil Reduction
Core_Definition: The practice of monitoring the amount of manual labor performed by a team and taking action to lower that ratio relative to project work.
Key_Actors: Management; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The team calculates the ratio of hours spent on manual work versus normal project work; if the ratio exceeds a pre-defined threshold, the team adjusts its priorities; all work on new features is paused so that the team can focus on solving the fundamental problems that are causing the manual labor.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ratio of toil should be as low as possible; management may set a specific threshold such as 50 percent to trigger a pause in feature work; the goal is to address the source of exhausting manual tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Toil
Core_Definition: Manual work that is particularly exhausting and has a lower return on investment than automation.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Toil occurs when staff must manually perform repetitive tasks such as performing 30 iterations of a job; an SRE is expected to perform the first few iterations and then write code to automate the rest; the total amount of time spent on these manual tasks is tracked as part of the operational load.
Rules_and_Constraints: SRE operational load is capped at 50 percent to limit manual labor; automation is considered a requirement because manual processes do not scale; manual labor has a lower return on investment than writing code to replace it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain human processes cannot be automated such as maintaining stakeholder relationships, evaluating new technology, or negotiating within a team to assemble an oncall schedule.

Concept_Name: Fix-It Days
Core_Definition: A day or series of days set aside to reduce technical debt by focusing on small unfinished amounts of work that have accumulated over time.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; the company-wide organization if the event is held at that scale.
Operational_Workflow: A day or series of days is scheduled for the event; all regular work is stopped so the team can focus exclusively on technical debt; a list of tasks or bugs is published at the start; participants resolve as many items as possible.
Rules_and_Constraints: The work targeted should be the accumulation of small pieces of work that are not individually urgent; the event can be turned into a game with prizes for those who resolve the most bugs or T-shirts for those who participate.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: technical debt
Core_Definition: The accumulation of small unfinished amounts of work that are not individually urgent but become a problem as they grow in volume.
Key_Actors: Operations teams responsible for maintaining and evolving a system.
Operational_Workflow: Small tasks are left incomplete because they are not urgent at the time; these items accumulate over the course of regular operations; once the accumulation becomes a problem, the team must set aside dedicated time to resolve them.
Rules_and_Constraints: Technical debt is characterized by bits and pieces of work that are not urgent by themselves; it requires specific intervention once it starts to impact the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Documentation Fix-It Day
Core_Definition: A specific event where an entire team stops all other work to focus exclusively on resolving bugs related to documentation that needs improvement.
Key_Actors: The entire operations team.
Operational_Workflow: All project and operational work is halted for the day; the team focuses their collective effort on documentation bugs; the quality and accuracy of documentation is improved throughout the day.
Rules_and_Constraints: The focus must remain on documentation-related technical debt rather than other types of unfinished work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Fix-It Week
Core_Definition: A week-long period declared to focus the team on bringing specific systems or configurations up to a particular standard.
Key_Actors: Operations team members.
Operational_Workflow: A week is designated for the fix-it effort; the team focuses on a broad category of technical debt; members work to ensure that all targeted items, such as monitoring configurations, meet a defined standard.
Rules_and_Constraints: The effort is sustained over a full week and typically targets a specific category of improvements or standards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Virtual office
Core_Definition: A work environment where operations teams work from home or various locations rather than a physical office, utilizing network connections and virtual meeting spaces.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; remote workers; team members.
Operational_Workflow: Team members work from anywhere with a network connection; they meet in chat rooms or other virtual meeting spaces instead of physical rooms; communication is made more intentional to ensure status updates are shared and remote workers are included in all discussions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Those not working from an office must take responsibility for staying in touch and clearly communicating their status; the entire team must take responsibility for ensuring remote workers do not feel isolated; everyone should know what their team members are working on; teams should establish a communication agreement to clarify which methods are used for specific situations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Virtual teams benefit from periodic in-person meetings where everyone travels to the same place for a few days of meetings focused on long-term planning, team building, and issues that cannot be solved online.

Concept_Name: Remote hands
Core_Definition: Datacenter technicians who perform physical changes and touch hardware when needed on behalf of operations staff who work from remote locations.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff manage services and virtual or physical machines from distant locations; when a physical intervention is required on a machine in a datacenter, datacenter technicians execute the task as requested by the remote staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff generally do not physically touch hardware because they work far from datacenters and often utilize virtual machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Chat rooms
Core_Definition: Virtual communication channels used by teams for daily interaction and staying in touch throughout the workday.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; remote workers; chat room bots.
Operational_Workflow: Team members join the room for primary communication; ephemeral discussions take place in real-time; chat room transcripts are stored for accessibility; if a decision is reached or an announcement needs to be made, the information is broadcast via email to ensure it carries across shift or day boundaries.
Rules_and_Constraints: Chat room transcripts should be stored and accessible so people can read what they missed; chat rooms should be used primarily for ephemeral discussions; announcements with lasting effects must be recorded in the team wiki or other document systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Text-chat is poorly suited for conveying emotions; teams should switch to higher-fidelity communication systems like voice, video, or screen sharing when an intense or heated debate starts.

Concept_Name: chat room bots
Core_Definition: Software robots that join chat rooms to provide various automated services and information to the team.
Key_Actors: Software robots; team members; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The software joins the chat room to automate routine tasks; it provides transcription services; it passes messages to members who are currently offline; it announces changes in oncall shifts; it broadcasts alerts generated by monitoring systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Bots can be used for entertainment purposes, such as tracking virtual high-fives or randomly assigning blame for humorous effect when a user claims an issue is not their fault.

Concept_Name: voice and video systems
Core_Definition: Higher-bandwidth communication systems used by remote teams to achieve better fidelity and convey emotions more effectively than text-chat.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; virtual office workers; team members involved in discussions or debates.
Operational_Workflow: Team members use these systems for staying in touch throughout the day; users switch from lower-fidelity text systems to these higher-bandwidth systems specifically when an intense or heated debate starts or when conveying emotions is paramount.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should always switch to these higher-fidelity systems when conveying emotions is more important than the simple exchange of text.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: screen sharing applications
Core_Definition: A type of higher-bandwidth communication system that provides higher fidelity than text-chat for remote team collaboration.
Key_Actors: Virtual operations teams; remote team members.
Operational_Workflow: These applications are utilized as part of a virtual office setup to facilitate communication and collaboration when text-based chat is insufficient.
Rules_and_Constraints: These are categorized as higher-bandwidth systems intended to improve the fidelity of communication among team members who are not in the same physical office.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: in-person meeting
Core_Definition: The communication medium with the highest possible fidelity where virtual team members gather at the same physical location.
Key_Actors: Virtual teams; team members who usually work from different places.
Operational_Workflow: Everyone on the team travels to the same physical location for a period of a few days to interact directly.
Rules_and_Constraints: These meetings should be held periodically and focus on long-term planning, team building, and other issues that cannot be solved through online communication.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: communication agreement
Core_Definition: A team policy that clarifies which specific communication methods and tools will be used for different types of situations.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; team members.
Operational_Workflow: The team establishes a chat-email-document paradigm; ephemeral discussions happen in chat rooms; decisions or announcements are broadcast via email; and major policies or design decisions are recorded in a wiki or document system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Chat rooms are restricted to ephemeral discussions; decisions made in chat must be broadcast via email to carry across shifts; announcements with lasting effects must be recorded in a document system and announced via email.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: chat–email–document paradigm
Core_Definition: A communication agreement and policy used by teams to clarify which specific communication methods should be used for different types of information to reduce communication problems.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; team members; remote workers.
Operational_Workflow: Team members use chat rooms for ephemeral discussions throughout the day; if a decision is made or an announcement is required it is broadcast via email to reach people across oncall shifts or day boundaries; announcements with lasting effects such as major policies or design decisions are recorded in the team wiki or document system; the creation of these documents is subsequently announced via email.
Rules_and_Constraints: Chat rooms should be restricted to ephemeral discussions; email must be used for information that needs to carry across shifts or day boundaries; major policies or design decisions must be recorded in a document system; the creation of lasting documents must be announced via email.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When conveying emotions is important or when intense or heated debates start the team should switch from text-based chat to higher-fidelity communication systems like voice and video.

Concept_Name: team wiki
Core_Definition: A centralized document system used by operational teams to record and store information that has lasting effects.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; team members.
Operational_Workflow: When a team makes a major policy decision or a design decision with lasting effects the information is recorded in the wiki; once the document is created or updated the team is notified of its existence via an email announcement.
Rules_and_Constraints: The wiki is designated for announcements with lasting effects rather than ephemeral discussions; the creation of a document in the wiki must be accompanied by an email announcement to the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 8. DevOps Culture
Section: DevOps Culture
============================================================

Concept_Name: DevOps
Core_Definition: A combination of culture and practices where software developers and operational engineers work together as one team that shares responsibility for a service and its availability.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; web operations staff; managers; release managers; product owners; legal department; vice presidents; CEOs; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by breaking down silos and removing bottlenecks in the delivery lifecycle to enable change to flow quickly from specification to production; developers and operations staff align their priorities by sharing responsibility for uptime and participating in all stages of the service life cycle; organizations identify value streams and implement the Three Ways, which include improving workflow speed from left to right, amplifying feedback loops from right to left, and fostering a culture of continual experimentation and learning; technical practices include using a common toolchain, keeping configurations under version control, and automating the entire build, test, and deployment process; nontechnical practices include early collaboration, shared oncall duties, and conducting failure analyses.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must prioritize people and process over tools; developers and operations staff must share oncall duties; all environments from development to production must be under software version management; software must be released in small batches to reduce risk and latency; no local optimizations should be permitted to degrade global performance; every outage must be followed by a postmortem; deployments should be automated and repeatable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a team exhausts its monthly error budget, developers are prohibited from performing new releases except for emergency security fixes; when things go wrong during game day exercises or fire drills, teams are standing by to manually fix service disruptions; recurring patterns of minor failures are treated as indicators of larger gaps in process that must be addressed in the development backlog.

Concept_Name: Agile methodology
Core_Definition: A collection of software development principles and collaborative practices that prioritize direct connections between business objectives and the development team.
Key_Actors: The Agile Alliance; software developers; product owners; scrum teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process bypasses traditional waterfall methods for collaborative ones such as pair programming where two developers work on code together or scrum where teams work in one to four week sprints; requirements are provided through plain statements called user stories which are maintained in a prioritized backlog; unit and integration tests are created alongside new feature code and applied automatically during the build process; the development cycle remains agile to respond to changing business needs easily.
Rules_and_Constraints: Individuals and interactions are valued over processes and tools; working code is valued over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration is valued over contract negotiation; responding to change is valued over following a plan; requirements must be structured as user stories.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Why Do Internet Services Fail, and What Can Be Done about It?
Core_Definition: A seminal 2003 research paper by Oppenheimer et al. that identifies human operator error and process weaknesses as the primary causes of web service failures.
Key_Actors: Human operators; front-end software developers; Oppenheimer; et al.
Operational_Workflow: The paper analyzes failure data across services and identifies that operator error is the largest single cause of failure; it highlights that configuration errors are the most common type of operator error; it notes that failures in custom-written front-end software are significant; the paper suggests that more extensive online testing and better detection of component failures would reduce failure rates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational aspects must improve for web services to be successful in the future; technological reliability has outpaced the reliability of the processes used to manage it; operator errors take a long time to repair.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: quality management
Core_Definition: A set of canonical techniques, such as Lean Manufacturing or the Shewhart cycle, applied to improve large-scale operations and optimize value streams.
Key_Actors: W. Edwards Deming; Spear and Bowen; Kim, Behr, and Spafford; IT organizations.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations identify value streams that provide value to the business and ensure each step in the process is repeatable and reliable; defects are never passed to the next step through the use of validation or quality assurance checks; feedback loops are amplified to make problems visible rather than hidden; measurements are collected on the length, frequency, and failure rate of process steps; the team identifies and fixes the bottleneck in the process while avoiding local optimizations elsewhere.
Rules_and_Constraints: Ad hoc steps must be replaced with repeatable processes; no local optimizations should degrade global performance; rework and redundant work are the two biggest inefficiencies to be eliminated; optimizations must be applied to the bottleneck because optimizations anywhere else are wasted energy; management must allocate time for projects that improve the system and address technical debt.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Once a process is smooth and optimized, the organization should intentionally introduce defects or faults into the system to verify they are detected and to increase overall resilience.

Concept_Name: Shewhart cycle
Core_Definition: A quality management technique used to improve large-scale operations.
Key_Actors: W. Edwards Deming.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lean Manufacturing
Core_Definition: A quality management technique that serves as a basis for the Three Ways of DevOps strategy and the Toyota Production System Kaizen improvement model.
Key_Actors: Spear; Bowen; Toyota.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: The Phoenix Project
Core_Definition: A book that explains DevOps principles and the Three Ways strategy through a fictional story about a team that learns these principles while reforming a failing IT organization.
Key_Actors: Gene Kim; Kevin Behr; George Spafford; a fictional IT team.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: total cost of ownership (TCO)
Core_Definition: A financial concern regarding the overall cost of a project throughout its life cycle rather than just the initial purchase price.
Key_Actors: Companies; business organizations.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations use the concern for TCO to place greater importance on the operational part of their business; this shift in focus is used to achieve higher reliability and velocity of change through improvements in efficiency and innovation.
Rules_and_Constraints: It requires evaluating a project based on its total costs instead of only the initial purchase price.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DevOp
Core_Definition: A term used to describe a job title that the text explicitly states does not exist; instead, DevOps is a combination of culture and practices. It involves software developers and operational engineers working together as one team with shared responsibility for a service.
Key_Actors: Software developers; operational engineers; system administrators; web operations staff; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Developers and system administrators share responsibility for a service and its availability; both groups participate in all stages of the development cycle and share oncall duties; the process involves breaking down silos to allow change to flow quickly and reliably from specification through to running features in a customer-facing environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: You cannot hire a DevOp; it is not a product or software package; the culture requires everyone to understand how the entire system works and the underlying business value they provide; availability is the problem of the entire organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In traditional environments, operator error and configuration errors are the largest causes of failures; the opposite of DevOps is described as despair.

Concept_Name: Choose Your Own Adventure
Core_Definition: A seminal talk by Adam Jacob at the Velocity 2010 conference that defines DevOps as an inclusive movement that codifies a culture.
Key_Actors: Adam Jacob; every person involved in the organizational system; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The movement functions by ensuring everyone involved in the system knows how the entire system works; individuals must be clear about the underlying business value they bring to the table; responsibility for availability shifts from being a solo task for system administrators to a problem for the entire organization.
Rules_and_Constraints: DevOps is not a job description; it is not a product or software that can be purchased; there is no button to press to magically have DevOps.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DevOps is not a technology problem. DevOps is a business problem
Core_Definition: A principle emphasized by Damon Edwards stating that DevOps is about collaboration and optimization across the whole organization rather than just technical tools.
Key_Actors: Damon Edwards; the whole organization; developers; system administrators; business departments; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The process expands to help the journey from initial idea to the customer; it involves identifying value streams that the business depends on and applying principles like the Three Ways of DevOps; it aligns operations with business and customer needs to achieve higher uptime and lower operational costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: The focus must be on people and process over the ad hoc use of tools; it is not just about leveraging cool new software; it requires a culture of innovation and a willingness to take risks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Striving for perfection can discourage innovation; error budgets are used to balance the risk of innovation with the need for stability; if the error budget is exhausted, only emergency security fixes are allowed.

Concept_Name: waterfall methodology
Core_Definition: A traditional software development approach where each step is performed by a different team in isolation from the other steps.
Key_Actors: Gather requirements team; design team; implementation team; testing team; verification team; deployment team; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Information flows unidirectionally downward through a series of stages; teams perform their specific task and produce a deliverable to be handed off to the next team in the sequence; system administrators are involved only at the final deployment stage and are thereafter solely responsible for operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each step is done in isolation; features are locked after the requirements gathering stage; operational requirements are not considered until it is too late to influence the software development; information flow is unidirectional.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The methodology is considered risky and invites failure because design iterations are never confined to successive steps; it results in developers not being affected by operational issues caused by their code; it leads to ad hoc solutions and optimizations that are limited in scope.

Concept_Name: waterfall development
Core_Definition: A traditional software development methodology where the process flows unidirectionally through sequential steps performed by different teams in isolation. It is characterized by information flowing down from the business side to the operations side like a cascading waterfall.
Key_Actors: Requirement gathering teams; design teams; implementation teams; testing teams; verification teams; deployment teams; software developers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with gathering requirements and moves through design, implementation, testing, and verification before reaching the deployment stage. Each team performs its specific step and produces a deliverable to be handed off to the next team in the sequence. Information and deliverables flow in one direction, typically leaving system administrators responsible for the final software without prior involvement in the earlier stages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Features are locked after the requirements gathering stage is complete. Each step must be done in isolation from the other steps. System administrators are involved only during the deployment phase and are thereafter solely responsible for operations and uptime requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Operational requirements are often not considered until the design is complete or the software is in active use, making it too late to address them. The methodology is noted as risky because design iterations are rarely confined to successive steps in practice, and unidirectional information flow prevents necessary feedback.

Concept_Name: silos
Core_Definition: An organizational structure where different departments work independently and in isolation from one another. In these environments, teams are often unaware of the concerns of other groups and lack a shared understanding of the big picture.
Key_Actors: Software developers; system administrators; operations staff; CEO.
Operational_Workflow: Software developers create applications without a motivating sense of their future use or operational requirements. The software is then passed to system administrators who struggle to meet high availability requirements with code that may be buggy or difficult to manage. Communication between these groups is minimal, and their organizational hierarchies may only meet at the executive management level.
Rules_and_Constraints: Groups work with conflicting goals rather than shared responsibilities. Departments operate without a clear understanding of the underlying business value they bring to the table. Information regarding the development to operations delivery lifecycle is restricted and not shared across the organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Excellence in operational efficiency and uptime is hindered because improvements are generally limited to ad hoc solutions and optimizations of restricted scope. The lack of integration between silos creates bottlenecks and risks that disrupt the entire delivery lifecycle.

Concept_Name: oncall duties
Core_Definition: A shared responsibility between developers and operations staff for maintaining the availability and performance of a service. This practice ensures that the people who write the code and the people who manage the infrastructure are both responsible for uptime.
Key_Actors: Software developers; system administrators; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Responsibility for service uptime is shared among the team members who participate in pager duties. The team holds weekly meetings to review oncall trends, outages, and compliance with service level agreements. When issues occur, everyone is empowered to research the problems using shared access to monitoring data and build output. Insights from these incidents are processed through postmortems and added to the development backlog for future action.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers must have a vested interest in ensuring their software meets high availability requirements. Both developers and operations staff must have full access to all monitoring and deployment output. Failure analysis must be conducted for every outage to identify process gaps or recurring patterns of failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a failure occurs, a postmortem analysis is performed to prevent recurring patterns of minor failures from pointing to larger issues. Management may establish escalation paths to ensure developers are reachable after hours for critical failures or maintenance.

Concept_Name: The Three Ways of DevOps
Core_Definition: A strategy for improving operations that describes the values and philosophies framing DevOps practices through workflow optimization, feedback amplification, and continual learning. It uses principles from Lean Manufacturing to enhance the delivery of services from development to the customer.
Key_Actors: Development teams; operations teams; business departments; management; internal and external customers.
Operational_Workflow: The team first identifies its value streams and defines repeatable processes for moving work from development to operations. The First Way focuses on increasing the flow of work and ensuring defects are never passed forward. The Second Way shortens and amplifies feedback loops so that information moves back upstream to improve the system. The Third Way creates a culture of experimentation and practice where risks are taken to achieve mastery and innovation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Ensure no local optimizations degrade the global performance of the system. Each step in the process must be done in a repeatable way rather than through ad hoc actions. Feedback loops must be made as direct and short as possible by removing unnecessary management layers. Knowledge must be embedded in the process via documentation and source control. Management must allocate resources to address technical debt and rewarded risk-taking.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Optimizations performed on any part of the system other than the primary bottleneck are considered wasted energy. If feedback is hidden by individuals using personal workarounds, the system loses the opportunity to improve. To increase resilience, faults are intentionally introduced into the system through fire drills to verify that redundant systems function correctly.

Concept_Name: Kaizen improvement model
Core_Definition: A model from the Toyota Production System that serves as one of the foundations for the Three Ways of DevOps strategy.
Key_Actors: The Toyota Production System; Lean Manufacturing practitioners; organizations adopting DevOps.
Operational_Workflow: The model is used to frame the values and philosophies that guide DevOps processes, procedures, and practices; it specifically influences the Three Ways strategy which involves identifying value streams and applying principles of workflow, feedback, and continuous experimentation.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used in conjunction with Lean Manufacturing principles; it provides the framing for the structured improvement of operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Workflow
Core_Definition: The first way of DevOps which focuses on getting a process correct from beginning to end and improving the speed at which that process can be completed.
Key_Actors: Business and development teams (represented on the left of the timeline); customer and operations teams (represented on the right of the timeline).
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by ensuring each step is performed in a repeatable way rather than through ad hoc methods; testing and validation are performed as early as possible to ensure no defects are passed to subsequent steps; local optimizations are evaluated to ensure they do not degrade the performance of the entire system; finally the process is analyzed to increase the flow of work by automating steps or eliminating redundant work.
Rules_and_Constraints: Repeatable processes must replace haphazard steps; defects must never be passed to the next step; global performance must not be sacrificed for local optimizations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A local optimization may occur where a step is skipped to save time for one team, such as developers pulling software directly from a repository instead of packaging it, but this is a regression if it introduces uncertainty or errors in later stages of the workflow.

Concept_Name: value stream
Core_Definition: A process that provides value to the business or a series of steps that the business depends upon.
Key_Actors: The business; the IT team; developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The team first identifies the specific value streams requested by or done for the business; each process is performed several times until it can be completed from beginning to end without failure; every step is clearly defined so it can be repeated with the same result by any reasonably trained person; once defined the feedback loops are amplified to collect measurements on failure rates and speed; finally the process is optimized by eliminating rework and redundant work while focusing energy on fixing the bottleneck.
Rules_and_Constraints: Steps must be clearly defined and repeatable; optimizations must be focused on the bottleneck to be effective; the process must be able to be completed from beginning to end without failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Every process has a bottleneck where work is delayed; optimizations made anywhere other than the bottleneck are considered wasted energy because work simply accumulates above the bottleneck or workers remain starved for tasks below it.

Concept_Name: flow rate
Core_Definition: The speed at which a process or value stream moves from the business and development stage through to the customer and operations stage.
Key_Actors: Development teams; operations staff; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: Flow rate is improved after steps in a process have been made repeatable; the process is analyzed to identify where work is redone or where manual steps can be replaced with automation; the speed is increased by eliminating these inefficiencies and ensuring that work moves smoothly from left to right on the process timeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be repeatable before it can be effectively analyzed for flow improvements; flow rate is increased by automating steps and eliminating duplicate effort.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Organizations may attempt to achieve audacious improvements in flow rate, such as reducing the time from one week to one day, as part of a culture of continual experimentation; however, flow is hindered if local optimizations are chosen that cause confusion or errors later in the process.

Concept_Name: flow
Core_Definition: The speed at which a process, known as a value stream, moves from the business or development stage on the left to the customer or operations stage on the right.
Key_Actors: Software developers; System administrators; Web operations staff; Business units; Customers.
Operational_Workflow: A software release process is mapped on a timeline where code moves through various stages including being committed to a repository, unit-tested, packaged, integration-tested, and deployed into production. To improve this process, each step is transformed into a repeatable process, automation is applied to increase speed, and duplicate work is identified and eliminated to increase the flow rate.
Rules_and_Constraints: Ensure each step is done in a repeatable way; replace haphazard or ad hoc steps with repeatable processes; never pass defects to the next step; perform testing as early as possible; include validation or quality assurance checks at each step; ensure no local optimizations degrade global performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the flow rate is hindered by work being redone multiple times, that redundant work must be eliminated to restore efficiency.

Concept_Name: local optimizations
Core_Definition: Ad hoc solutions or improvements that are limited in scope to a specific step or team rather than the entire process.
Key_Actors: Developers; System administrators; Individual teams.
Operational_Workflow: In traditional or siloed environments, teams often create workarounds for problems that affect only their specific area of responsibility. An example includes developers choosing not to package software to save themselves time, which requires subsequent steps to pull software directly from a source repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: Local optimizations must not be allowed to degrade global performance; energy put into optimizations anywhere other than the system bottleneck is considered wasted energy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A local optimization can become a global regression if it introduces uncertainty or errors for the rest of the organization, such as creating confusion about whether all steps are working with the same exact bits of code.

Concept_Name: global performance
Core_Definition: The overall efficiency, performance, and uptime of the entire development to operations delivery lifecycle.
Key_Actors: The entire organization; Software developers; System administrators; Business departments.
Operational_Workflow: Global performance is managed by looking at the entire value stream from specification through to running features in a customer-facing environment. It involves identifying the single bottleneck in a process where work is delayed and focusing all improvement energy there to ensure work does not accumulate above the bottleneck or leave workers below it starved for tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Prioritize the big picture over isolated concerns; align operations with business and customer needs; ensure that everyone involved knows how the entire system works; do not allow local optimizations to cause global regressions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Global performance is hindered when teams work in silos or use waterfall methodologies, as these approaches prevent operational requirements from being considered until it is too late to influence the design.

Concept_Name: feedback loop
Core_Definition: A communication channel where information, such as complaints or requests, is communicated upstream or downstream to improve a system.
Key_Actors: Internal customers at each process step; External customers; Developers; Operations staff; Management.
Operational_Workflow: As a process moves from development to operations, lessons are learned and information about problems or concerns is gathered. This feedback is then amplified and made visible, then communicated back through the system to the beginning of the process. The loop is shortened by making communication as direct as possible between the person who found the problem and the person who can fix it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Understand and respond to all internal and external customers; shorten feedback loops to make communication direct; amplify all feedback to make issues visible rather than hiding them with workarounds; embed knowledge where it is needed using documentation and source code control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a message must pass through too many stages of management, the loop becomes less effective. When individuals muddle through problems with their own workarounds, they are hiding the feedback and preventing the system from being improved.

Concept_Name: Shorten feedback loops
Core_Definition: The practice of making communication as direct as possible by reducing the number of stages or people a message must pass through to reach its destination.
Key_Actors: The person who experiences a problem; the person who can fix the problem; managers and vice presidents who may act as intermediaries in longer loops.
Operational_Workflow: Information about a problem or potential improvement is identified at a specific stage of the process; the message is communicated directly from the source of the observation to the individual with the authority or skill to implement a fix; this avoids traversing through multiple organizational layers such as being typed up by a manager and presented to executives before returning down the chain to an engineer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Communication is less effective the more stages a message must pass through; the loop is considered at its shortest when the person experiencing the issue communicates directly with the person who can fix it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Ineffective loops occur when feedback must pass through a hierarchy of managers and vice presidents before reaching an engineer; this increased length makes the communication less effective.

Concept_Name: Amplify all feedback
Core_Definition: A principle of ensuring that information regarding problems, concerns, or potential improvements is made visible and brought to the surface rather than being hidden or bypassed.
Key_Actors: Individual team members who notice problems; management who may need to make decisions based on surfaced information; customers who provide requests or complaints.
Operational_Workflow: A team member identifies a defect or a potential improvement during the workflow; instead of using a personal workaround to muddle through, the individual files a bug report or stops the process; this information is communicated back to the development side of the system to improve the process; management evaluates the surfaced feedback to decide on necessary systemic changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Never pass defects to the next step in the workflow; respond to both internal and external customers; do not allow local optimizations to degrade global performance; lessons learned while moving through the system must be communicated back to the beginning and made visible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individuals acting as heroes by working around problems with their own solutions are actually hiding issues and preventing them from being fixed; a process may be dramatically stopped until a management decision is made regarding how to proceed when significant feedback is surfaced.

Concept_Name: Embed knowledge
Core_Definition: The practice of placing specialized information such as configuration requirements or business needs directly into the process through documentation and source code control.
Key_Actors: Development and operations teams involved in the workflow.
Operational_Workflow: Specialized knowledge including configuration information or business requirements is gathered; this information is documented and managed within source code control systems; as the workflow moves from development toward operations the necessary details remain available at every stage; participants access this information within the loop rather than having to seek it from external sources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Knowledge must be managed via source code control; details must be available at every stage of the process; the process should not require going outside of the feedback loop to acquire necessary information.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: source code control
Core_Definition: A unified management system used to store and version product code, operational tools, system configurations, and documentation to ensure consistency across all environments.
Key_Actors: Software developers; system administrators; operations staff; release engineers.
Operational_Workflow: All environments from development through production are brought under version management; configurations and scripts are treated as source code and stored in the repository; the system provides a shared toolchain where both development and operations can access the same code and configurations; automated builds and deployments pull from this central repository to ensure the exact same bits are used throughout the lifecycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: All configurations and scripts must be kept in source control; the system should be unified to include product code as well as operational tools; changes to configurations should be subject to the same change management as the rest of the code; binary packages should only be built once from the source code to avoid errors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Bypassing the system by checking out code directly from a repository to live systems or compiling on each host is considered a failure of the process; risk is introduced when different teams build packages from slightly different source code revisions; the process is undermined if team members attempt to slip in small fixes that have not gone through the full end-to-end testing cycle.

Concept_Name: Continual Experimentation and Learning
Core_Definition: The Third Way of DevOps that creates a culture where innovation is fostered by encouraging risk-taking and understanding that mastery comes from repetition and learning from failures.
Key_Actors: Everyone in the organization; management; IT teams.
Operational_Workflow: Rituals are established to reward risk-taking; management allocates specific time for projects that improve the system; faults are intentionally introduced into the system to test resilience; audacious or crazy goals are set to drastically improve flow time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Risk-taking must be rewarded at review time even if experiments fail; valuable lessons must be learned from failures; repetition and practice are required to master skills; resources must be allocated to fix bugs identified through feedback; mistakes must not be repeated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failure is embraced as a necessary step to transition a system from being merely optimized to being resilient.

Concept_Name: technical debt
Core_Definition: A backlog of bugs and system improvement tasks that are identified through feedback and require dedicated time and resources to resolve.
Key_Actors: Management; IT teams.
Operational_Workflow: Feedback loops are amplified to make system problems visible; bugs are filed based on the surfaced information; management allocates time and resources for the IT team to work on the backlog of identified issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Management must consider the backlog of debt as important; resources must be specifically allocated to ensure mistakes are not repeated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Fire drills
Core_Definition: Also known as game day exercises, these are deliberate, planned attempts to induce service disruption to verify that failover and redundancy systems function correctly.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; standby teams of people; machines; networks.
Operational_Workflow: Faults such as taking down machines or networks are intentionally triggered in a planned fashion; standby teams monitor the system to ensure redundant components kick in; the exercise verifies that defects are detected and handled; manual intervention is applied if the automated systems fail.
Rules_and_Constraints: Exercises must be conducted in a planned fashion; teams must be standing by to ensure the right thing happens; standby staff must be ready to fix things manually if the system does not recover; exercises should verify both detection and handling of failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If redundant systems do not function as intended during the drill, the standby teams must intervene manually to fix the service.

Concept_Name: Small batches
Core_Definition: A principle of performing frequent, small releases containing only a few features to lower risk and increase learning through practice.
Key_Actors: Developers; system administrators; customers; the business.
Operational_Workflow: Releases are broken down into small sets of features; code moves quickly from check-in to production to reduce latency; the release process is repeated over many iterations; bugs are isolated within the small changes for easier resolution.
Rules_and_Constraints: Many small releases should be favored over a small number of large releases; high velocity and low latency must be prioritized to generate return on investment sooner; the tendency to avoid frequent deployments due to perceived risk must be overcome.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If businesses minimize deployment frequency to avoid risk, they inadvertently create bigger and riskier deployments while ensuring the team remains out of practice.

Concept_Name: code latency
Core_Definition: A measurement of the speed at which code moves from the initial check-in to the production environment.
Key_Actors: Software code; developers; production systems.
Operational_Workflow: Code is initially checked in; it moves through the release and deployment process; it is placed into production where it can begin generating value.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code latency is reduced by utilizing small batches; high velocity and low latency are achieved together through frequent releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: return on investment (ROI)
Core_Definition: The financial gain or value generated by software code once it has been successfully deployed to the production environment.
Key_Actors: Production-ready code; business organizations; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Code is developed and moved through the delivery lifecycle; the code is deployed to production; the business begins generating financial returns from the features in that code.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code going into production sooner allows it to generate return on investment sooner; reducing code latency is a key requirement for faster ROI.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Velocity
Core_Definition: A metric representing the frequency of software shipments or changes made within a specific timeframe, typically measured per month.
Key_Actors: Web companies; developers; system administrators; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Software changes are grouped into small batches; these batches are released to the production environment; the total number of these shipments is calculated over a monthly period.
Rules_and_Constraints: High velocity is realized through releasing small batches; velocity is increased to improve efficiency and to introduce new features and innovations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Traditionally, frequent change was viewed as a potential destabilizer, but the DevOps approach demonstrates that rapid and frequent infrastructure change can actually increase overall stability.

Concept_Name: bottleneck
Core_Definition: A specific point in a process where work is delayed because it is waiting on other dependencies.
Key_Actors: Work tasks; dependencies; workers positioned above the bottleneck; workers positioned below the bottleneck.
Operational_Workflow: Work flows through a value stream until it encounters a dependency; incomplete work accumulates at the point of the bottleneck; workers below the bottleneck are starved for work and remain underutilized.
Rules_and_Constraints: The most beneficial place to put energy into improvement is at the bottleneck; optimizations anywhere else are considered wasted energy; optimizing steps above the bottleneck only makes more work accumulate; optimizing steps below the bottleneck simply improves steps that are already underutilized.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DevOps Days
Core_Definition: A series of mini-conferences created to bring developers and operations staff together to share knowledge and experiences regarding collaborative system management.
Key_Actors: Patrick Debois; software developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The conferences facilitate the physical gathering of professionals from development and operations backgrounds to exchange lessons learned from independently evolving the practice of system administration into collaborative web operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The name of the event is derived from the combination of the terms developers (dev) and operations (ops).
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DevOps Cafe Podcast
Core_Definition: An audio program that serves as a clearing-house for the exchange of ideas and discussions concerning DevOps culture and practices.
Key_Actors: John Willis; Damon Edwards; Jesse Robbins; Jez Humble.
Operational_Workflow: The podcast provides a platform for discussing DevOps principles and interviewing industry figures to highlight success stories and practical applications of the methodology.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: #devops
Core_Definition: A specific hashtag used on the Twitter service to categorize content and identify members of the DevOps community.
Key_Actors: DevOps followers; Twitter platform users.
Operational_Workflow: Followers of the movement append the hashtag to their social media posts to identify themselves and facilitate the discovery of DevOps-related discussions online.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: USENIX LISA Conference
Core_Definition: A technical conference focused on Large Installation System Administration that incorporated DevOps into its core programming.
Key_Actors: USENIX; system administrators; DevOps practitioners.
Operational_Workflow: The conference establishes a central theme for its sessions; in 2011 it selected DevOps as the theme and subsequently evolved to permanently incorporate a focus on DevOps practices.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: pair programming
Core_Definition: A collaborative software development method where two developers work on code together.
Key_Actors: Two developers.
Operational_Workflow: Two developers work together on the same code to build software that meets specific business objectives; this process is used as an alternative to traditional waterfall methodology to foster collaboration and integration.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is an Agile practice used to bypass waterfall methods; it is not a strictly required technique for creating a DevOps environment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: scrum teams
Core_Definition: A collaborative software development practice where a whole team commits to short cycles of work to complete features from a prioritized list.
Key_Actors: The whole development team; product owners.
Operational_Workflow: The team commits to sprints of one to four weeks; during these sprints, they work on a prioritized backlog of features; developers work closely with product owners to ensure business objectives are met.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sprints must last between one and four weeks; requirements are provided through plain statements called user stories; work is based on a prioritized backlog of feature stories that can change with business needs; scrum teams are not a mandatory requirement for a DevOps environment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Amazon AWS
Core_Definition: A service that popularized the ability for developers to allocate virtual machines and manage infrastructure independently.
Key_Actors: Developers; virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: Developers use the service to allocate virtual machines using API calls; this allows developers to perform system administration tasks themselves without the need for full-time system administrators; developers bring an emphasis on automation and repeatability to the system skills they learn through the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: API calls
Core_Definition: Programmatic requests used by developers to automate the allocation of virtual infrastructure.
Key_Actors: Developers; virtual machine allocation systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers execute these calls to automatically allocate virtual machines; this process replaces the manual setup tasks that previously required the training of a system administrator.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Site Reliability Engineer (SRE)
Core_Definition: A role that recognizes all functions of system administration, from capacity planning to security, as crucial to the reliability of a site.
Key_Actors: Google; developers; operational engineers.
Operational_Workflow: SREs build tools that are specifically engineered to make operations self-service for developers; these tools allow developers to achieve high-quality results without advanced knowledge.
Rules_and_Constraints: SREs empower developers to do their own operations; the focus is on engineering tools that make high-quality results easy to achieve.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: There are not enough SREs to go around to support every team directly.

Concept_Name: SRE model
Core_Definition: An organizational approach that organizes developers and operational engineers to share responsibility for reliability and performance.
Key_Actors: Google; developers; operational engineers; small product or infrastructure component teams.
Operational_Workflow: Each product or infrastructure component is assigned a small team; for critical and highly visible systems, developers and SREs work together in an arrangement that mirrors the DevOps model; SREs build tools that enable developers to do their own operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The model is intended to function as DevOps at large scale; it prioritizes shared responsibility for reliability and performance across the organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because there are not enough SREs to go around, the vast majority of teams consist of developers using tools developed by SREs rather than working with SREs directly.

Concept_Name: Software eats the world
Core_Definition: A phrase coined by Marc Andreessen describing the increasing pervasiveness of software across all industries and facets of society.
Key_Actors: Marc Andreessen; society; all facets of computing.
Operational_Workflow: As software becomes integral to all facets of society, there is an increasing requirement for well-run operations across all industries; this leads to the application of DevOps principles and practices to all facets of computing.
Rules_and_Constraints: DevOps is not limited to web-centric environments and must be applied to all facets of computing as software expands.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Relationships
Core_Definition: One of the four main areas of DevOps practice that emphasizes the coordination and connections between different teams and roles over tools and scripts.
Key_Actors: Developers; release managers; system administrators; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Teams establish collegial connections through face-to-face discussions and shared social activities; they identify mutual problems and describe projects in terms of mutual benefits; members attend each other's planning meetings and maintain regular communication to ensure coordination.
Rules_and_Constraints: The governing motto is people over process over tools; the right people must perform the right process consistently before automation tools are created; all roles must be in close coordination to achieve the shared goal of highly reliable and continuously improving services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A sign that integration and relationships have not been achieved is if system administrators primarily associate or eat lunch with other system administrators rather than colleagues from software development, web operations, networking, or security.

Concept_Name: Integration
Core_Definition: Integration involves breaking down organizational silos to ensure that processes are linked across different domains of responsibility to deliver end-to-end functionality. It focuses on combining tools and data with human processes like peer reviews and coordination meetings.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; web operations staff; networking teams; security teams.
Operational_Workflow: Operations are viewed as end-to-end processes rather than isolated scripts; different communities responsible for service operation collaborate regularly; teams from disparate departments like development and security interact to ensure processes are linked across their respective domains.
Rules_and_Constraints: Processes must be integrated across teams; communities responsible for different parts of the service must be integrated; teams should not work in isolation or silos.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Integration is considered lacking if system administrators primarily associate only with other system administrators and fail to regularly interact with colleagues in development, networking, or security.

Concept_Name: Automation
Core_Definition: Automation is the practice of using scripts and tools to ensure simplicity and repeatability in system management. It involves treating configurations and management scripts as source code that is kept under version control.
Key_Actors: Developers; system administrators; automated build systems; configuration management tools such as CFEngine, Puppet, and Chef.
Operational_Workflow: A process is first thoroughly understood and performed consistently by people; once the process is stable, it is scripted and automated to the fullest extent possible; configurations are managed through code; automation tools describe tasks at a high level and determine the specific steps needed for various operating systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automate everything; keep all scripts and configurations in source control; design solutions that are simple, repeatable, and reusable; automate the process for releasing and deploying to make it reliable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automation should not be implemented before the right people are performing the right process consistently; if a task is difficult or painful, it should be done more often to facilitate its eventual automation rather than being avoided.

Concept_Name: Continuous improvement
Core_Definition: Continuous improvement is a mindset and set of practices focused on making processes dependably repeatable and functional through constant refinement. It involves learning from failures, addressing root causes, and eliminating inefficiencies.
Key_Actors: All members of the DevOps team including developers and operations staff; management.
Operational_Workflow: Feedback loops are established to communicate problems or potential improvements upstream and downstream; every failure results in new tests added to the release process to detect that failure mode in the future; processes are analyzed to find and fix bottlenecks; management allocates time to address technical debt and fix bugs identified through feedback.
Rules_and_Constraints: Never pass defects to the next step; improve continuously; look for root causes rather than local optimizations; if something is difficult, do it more often to improve it; build quality into the process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Opportunities to improve are missed if lessons learned from failures are thrown away; the system is prevented from improving when individuals hide problems by using their own ad hoc workarounds instead of amplifying feedback.

Concept_Name: People over process over tools
Core_Definition: This is a defining DevOps principle stating that human relationships and team coordination are more important than the specific processes or tools used by an organization. It prioritizes the interactions between various roles to achieve shared goals.
Key_Actors: Developers; release managers; system administrators; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The organization focuses on establishing close coordination and collegial connections among different teams; once the right people are aligned, they define and perform the right processes consistently; tools are only introduced or created to automate the function after the people and processes are working effectively.
Rules_and_Constraints: Give more weight to relationships among teams than to scripts and tools; focus on people and process over writing a script and then figuring out who should run it; foster a culture where everyone knows how the entire system works.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In traditional environments, this hierarchy is often ignored by treating tools and scripts as the primary focus of operational maintenance rather than the relationships between the teams.

Concept_Name: Simplicity
Core_Definition: A practice within the automation area of DevOps that aims to increase efficiency, speed of communication, and the avoidance of confusion.
Key_Actors: Developers; release managers; system administrators; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Practitioners design simple, repeatable, and reusable solutions while handling configurations and scripts as source code under version control; building and management of source code are scripted to the fullest extent possible once the entire process is understood; abstracted administration is used to describe tasks at a high level while allowing automation to decide the specific steps for a given operating system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Solutions must be simple, repeatable, and reusable; the focus should be on people and process before creating tools for automation; abstracted administration should simplify the interface between goals and configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: root causes
Core_Definition: The underlying sources of system failures or inefficiencies that must be identified and addressed to ensure long-term stability and continuous improvement.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; the overall project team.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations make monitoring available to developers and ask them to perform joint analysis of issues or failures; teams look for recurring patterns of minor failures to identify larger gaps in process; after a failure, the root cause is found and fixed; the process is then worked again to verify that the problem has disappeared.
Rules_and_Constraints: Problems must be fixed by looking for root causes rather than making local optimizations that degrade global performance; teams must avoid the temptation to patch a failure and proceed without finding the underlying cause.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Local optimizations that degrade global performance; patching a failure and proceeding with patches rather than finding the source of the problem.

Concept_Name: Early Collaboration and Involvement
Core_Definition: A nontechnical practice where operations staff are integrated into the development process from the beginning to ensure services are architected correctly for the production environment.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; development planning meetings.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff are included in development planning meetings while developers are given full access to operations monitoring; key issues such as architecting for scalability are jointly developed during the initial planning stage.
Rules_and_Constraints: Participation must happen early in the development cycle; both developers and operations must have access to each other's data and monitoring.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: New Features Review
Core_Definition: A process where operations staff participate in the design phase of new features to guide development toward best practices for operability.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff participate in and guide development during design time; teams define key monitoring indicators for services through collaboration; deployment details are sketched out so that the development of deployment code and tests are integrated into the main development effort.
Rules_and_Constraints: Guidance must be provided during design time rather than as an afterthought; deployment code and tests must be part of the main development effort.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Shared Oncall Responsibilities
Core_Definition: A practice where software developers and operational engineers share the responsibility for service availability and pager duties.
Key_Actors: Software developers; system administrators; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Developers and operations staff share pager duties for a service; they participate in weekly meetings to review service level agreement compliance and any outages; all team members are given full access to monitoring output and build or deploy output to enable research into issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers and system administrators must be jointly responsible for uptime; both groups must be empowered with the same information and access to tools; the practice aligns the priorities of developers and operations by making them both responsible for service health.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Postmortem Process
Core_Definition: A thorough review and analysis conducted for every outage to identify recurring patterns of failure and necessary corrective actions.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; development team members responsible for backlogs.
Operational_Workflow: The team holds a regular stand-up meeting to review outages and trends; a detailed analysis is performed for every outage to investigate root causes; tasks required to correct the identified issues are formulated; these tasks are added to the current development backlog and prioritized.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be performed for every outage; analysis should look for recurring patterns of minor failures that may point to larger process gaps; findings must result in tasks that are prioritized within the development cycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: failure analysis
Core_Definition: The investigation into the root causes of service outages or issues that arise during oncall shifts.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; project teams.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring data and build output are made available to the overall project team; developers and operations staff perform joint root cause analysis of failures; the team identifies recurring problems and collaborates on potential solutions to prevent future occurrences.
Rules_and_Constraints: All team members must be fully empowered to research any issues that come up; the analysis must focus on root causes rather than local optimizations; feedback from the analysis must be amplified and made visible rather than hidden.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Game Day Exercises
Core_Definition: Planned events where service disruptions are deliberately triggered to test the effectiveness of failover and redundancy systems.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; redundant system components.
Operational_Workflow: Faults such as taking down machines or networks are intentionally introduced into the system; teams of people stand by to observe if the redundant systems function properly; if the systems do not behave as expected, the standby teams manually intervene to fix the issues; the exercise verifies the resilience of the service under failure conditions.
Rules_and_Constraints: The exercises are deliberate and planned; teams must be standing by to ensure the right thing happens or to intervene manually; these drills are used to increase resilience by embracing and inducing failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the automated systems fail to handle the introduced disruption, the teams on standby must manually fix the service.

Concept_Name: Error Budgets
Core_Definition: A system that brings innovation and risk into equilibrium by permitting a certain amount of downtime each month as a budget.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations.
Operational_Workflow: A certain amount of permitted downtime is allocated to developers each month; developers are allowed to perform as many releases as they wish until that budget is exhausted; once the budget is depleted, developers are restricted from further releases for the remainder of the month.
Rules_and_Constraints: Striving for perfection is discouraged because it limits innovation; the budget aligns the priorities of operations and developers to help them work together; developers can conserve their budget by dedicating more time to testing and building frameworks that ensure successful releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Emergency security fixes are permitted even after the monthly error budget has been exhausted.

Concept_Name: SLA compliance
Core_Definition: The adherence to service level agreements regarding uptime and availability, which is monitored through shared responsibility between teams.
Key_Actors: Developers; Ops staff.
Operational_Workflow: Teams share oncall duties and participate in a weekly meeting to review compliance trends and outages; everyone is empowered to research issues using full access to monitoring and build output.
Rules_and_Constraints: Shared review of oncall trends is required; both developers and operations staff must have full access to all monitoring and deployment data to analyze failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Same Development and Operations Toolchain
Core_Definition: A technical practice where development and operations teams use the same set of tools to facilitate communication and shared understanding.
Key_Actors: Development; operations.
Operational_Workflow: Teams adopt shared tools for tasks such as bug tracking and source code management; this ensures that both groups speak the same language when addressing development, operations, or deployment issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: The unified source code management system should store not only the product source code but also the source code for operational tools and system configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: bug-tracking system
Core_Definition: A shared tool used for recording and managing issues related to both software development and operations or deployment tasks.
Key_Actors: Development; operations; deployment.
Operational_Workflow: Issues from both the development side and the operations or deployment side are entered into the same system; this allows teams to use a common platform to track and resolve problems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using the same system for both development and operations is recommended as a way for the two groups to speak the same language.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: unified source code management system
Core_Definition: A centralized system that stores and manages the product source code alongside the source code for operational tools and system configurations.
Key_Actors: Software developers; operational engineers; development and operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: All various environments from development through test and production are brought under version management; development and operations use the same system to store application code, tools, and configurations to facilitate shared communication and synchronization.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must store not just the product source code but also operational tools and system configurations; it should be used by both development and operations to ensure they speak the same language.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Consistent Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)
Core_Definition: A unified process where the application code and the deployment or operations code follow the same development, testing, and release cycle in sync.
Key_Actors: Development teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Deployment tools are developed and tested in lockstep with the applications themselves; the teams follow a shared release cycle to keep the application and its deployment code synchronized throughout the lifecycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: The model must avoid throwing code over the wall to deployment; development and operations must be tightly coupled and follow a shared release cycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Managed Configuration and Automation
Core_Definition: The practice of keeping application configuration files and automation scripts in source code control while subjecting them to standard change management.
Key_Actors: Development and operations teams; source code control systems.
Operational_Workflow: Configuration files required for the service and all automation scripts are placed into source code control; these files are then managed using the same change management processes applied to the rest of the application code.
Rules_and_Constraints: All application configuration files and automation scripts must be subject to the same change management as the rest of the code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Infrastructure as Code
Core_Definition: The method of maintaining a description of an entire infrastructure as code that is kept under revision control.
Key_Actors: Software-defined datacenters; virtual machines; revision control systems.
Operational_Workflow: Descriptions of the infrastructure are treated as software code and maintained within revision control systems specifically in software-defined datacenter environments.
Rules_and_Constraints: The infrastructure description must be maintained under revision control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: software-defined datacenter
Core_Definition: A datacenter environment where the entire infrastructure is described as code that can be maintained under revision control.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: A description of the entire infrastructure is kept as code; this code is maintained and managed under version control systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: virtual machines
Core_Definition: Computational resources that can be allocated via software interfaces rather than physical hardware setup.
Key_Actors: Developers; API calls.
Operational_Workflow: Developers allocate virtual machines using API calls; this allows developers to manage system skills and automation without the need for traditional manual machine setup by system administrators.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automated Provisioning and Deployment
Core_Definition: A technical practice where every step of the deployment process is automated and scripted to allow for self-testing and rapid builds.
Key_Actors: CPU systems; storage systems; memory and bandwidth resources; predictive models.
Operational_Workflow: Every step of the deployment process is scripted; a build is triggered that proceeds through self-testing to deployment; alternatively, a separate build command can trigger deployment; resources like CPU, storage, and bandwidth are allocated based on predictive models as the system detects they are needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every step of the deployment process must be automated or scripted; assembling stages of a build by hand is counter to repeatability and ease of operation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If any step in the automated process fails, the principle of continuous delivery suggests one must stop the line, find the root cause, and start again rather than patching the failure and proceeding.

Concept_Name: Artifact-Scripted Database Changes
Core_Definition: A practice where changes to database schemas are treated as versioned code rather than manual manipulations.
Key_Actors: Databases; staging environments.
Operational_Workflow: Changes to the database are written as scripts; these scripts are tested and versioned; the scripts are then released into staging environments.
Rules_and_Constraints: Database changes must be scripted, tested, and versioned; manual manipulation of the database schema is avoided.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automated Build and Release
Core_Definition: A process where the output of a build cycle is a valid set of application and deployment objects that can be deployed to a staged environment without manual assembly.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; build systems; source code repositories.
Operational_Workflow: The process is triggered by code check-ins or specific commands; the system uses configuration files to treat the build as a series of dependencies and contracts to fulfill; it generates a complete set of application and deployment objects; these objects are then ready for deployment to a staged environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hand-assembling stages of a build is prohibited because it runs counter to repeatability; builds must treat the process as a series of dependencies and contracts to fulfill.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a build or its associated testing fails, the process stops; the system is designed to detect build-related problems as soon as possible.

Concept_Name: makefiles
Core_Definition: Configuration files used in the build process to treat the build as a series of dependencies and contracts to be fulfilled.
Key_Actors: Build systems; automated triggers; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: The build system utilizes the makefile to identify and resolve necessary code dependencies; the file outlines the specific contracts that must be met to create valid objects; the execution of these files is triggered by code check-ins or manual commands.
Rules_and_Constraints: They must provide a structured way to handle dependencies and contracts; they should facilitate automation and repeatability in the build cycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Release Vehicle Packaging
Core_Definition: The automated creation of deployment-ready packages for an application as the end product of a build cycle.
Key_Actors: Build cycle systems; deployment tools; target live systems.
Operational_Workflow: The build cycle generates the necessary packaging for the application automatically; these packages are prepared for deployment without requiring manual intervention; the software is then deployed to live systems using these prepared packages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hand-packaging software for deployment is not allowed; software must not be deployed to live systems via repository checkouts; software must not be compiled on each host before use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Abstracted Administration
Core_Definition: A high-level description of system administration tasks that allows automation to decide the specific steps required for a given operating system.
Key_Actors: System administrators; automation tools such as CFEngine, Puppet, or Chef; various operating systems like Linux and Windows.
Operational_Workflow: Administrators define high-level goals in a configuration file; the automation tool interprets these high-level descriptions; the tool determines the specific procedural steps for the target operating system, such as modifying specific files in Linux or updating Active Directory in Windows.
Rules_and_Constraints: Administration tasks should be described at a high level rather than as individual procedural steps; tools must be used for initial setup to simplify the interface between goals and configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: CFEngine
Core_Definition: A tool used for abstracted administration that describes system administration tasks at a high level and allows automation to decide the right steps to perform for a given operating system.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: System administration tasks are described at a high level, such as a request to create a user; the tool then determines the specific steps required for the target operating system, such as modifying password files on Linux or Active Directory on Windows; this approach manages the initial setup work by acting as an interface between administrative goals and system configurations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tasks must be described at a high level rather than through manual operating system steps; the tool should be used to simplify the interface between goals and configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Puppet
Core_Definition: An abstracted administration tool that enables high-level descriptions of system administration tasks while automation determines the necessary execution steps for various operating systems.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Administrators define goals in configuration files at a high level; the automation decides the correct actions for the specific operating system involved; the tool handles the initial setup work to bridge the gap between high-level goals and technical configurations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The tool is intended to simplify the interface between goals and configurations; administration tasks are required to be described at a high level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Chef
Core_Definition: A tool used for abstracted administration that allows system administration tasks to be described at a high level for automation to translate into platform-specific steps.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The tool receives high-level descriptions of administrative tasks; it then decides and performs the appropriate steps for the given operating system; this process allows the initial setup work to be handled by the tool rather than through manual configuration.
Rules_and_Constraints: System administration tasks must be described at a high level; the tool is used to simplify the interface between goals and configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Release engineering
Core_Definition: The process of taking software in its source form, building it, packaging it, testing it, and deploying it into the field.
Key_Actors: Release managers; software developers; system administrators; release engineers.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with a continuous build where software is compiled and packaged immediately after changes are detected; the software then undergoes continuous automated testing to prevent problems from becoming embedded in the system; after testing, the software is moved through automated deployment into test or live environments; finally, automated provisioning may be used to allocate resources like CPU and storage based on predictive models.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process for releasing and deploying must be repeatable and reliable; everything including code, configurations, and scripts must be kept in source control; binary packages should be built only once per release cycle to ensure consistency across environments; smoke tests must be performed on every deployment to verify basic functionality; deployment tools must be developed and tested in lockstep with the applications following a shared release cycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a build or test fails at any stage, the process must be stopped and restarted; the build or deployment will error out if built-in diagnostic or smoke tests fail; when errors occur, the focus should be on finding the root cause and fixing the process rather than applying manual patches or local optimizations.

Concept_Name: Continuous Build
Core_Definition: A practice where the code base is compiled and packaged software is generated with each change to detect build-related problems as soon as possible.
Key_Actors: Developers; release engineers; automated build systems.
Operational_Workflow: The workflow is triggered immediately after new source code changes are detected; the system attempts to compile the code base; if successful, the system produces a valid set of application and deployment objects; these objects are packaged to facilitate their deployment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Binary packages must be built only once for any given release cycle; the build must use the same exact operating system release, compiler release, and build environment; the build must be treated as a series of dependencies and contracts fulfilled through makefiles or configuration files; assembling stages of a build by hand is prohibited.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the build fails, the process stops; build systems may check the MD5 hash of the compiler and OS kernel to ensure the correct toolchain is used and will prevent a build if a mismatch is detected.

Concept_Name: Continuous Test
Core_Definition: The automated testing of software with each change to the code base to prevent problems from becoming embedded in the system.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Unit and integration tests are created alongside new feature code; the automated testing process is triggered upon the successful completion of a build; quality assurance or validation checks are performed at every step; new tests are added to the release process whenever a failure mode is detected in production to prevent a recurrence.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing must be conducted as early as possible in the process rather than only on the final product; quality must be built into the process; defects must never be passed to the next step.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a test fails, the process stops; findings from failure analyses or postmortems are added to the development backlog as tasks to correct the identified issues.

Concept_Name: Automated Deployment
Core_Definition: The automation of every step in the deployment process so that software can be moved into test and live environments without manual intervention.
Key_Actors: Software developers; operations staff; automated scripts; deployment tools.
Operational_Workflow: Deployment tools and tests are developed and tested in lockstep with the applications themselves; a deployment is triggered via a check-in or a specific build command; the system uses a repeatable process to move the software from a repository onto the system; basic functionality tests, also known as smoke tests, are performed on the deployment to ensure requirements are met.
Rules_and_Constraints: The same repeatable process must be used for deployment to every environment; software must not be deployed to live systems via checkout from a repository or compiled on each host before use; database schema changes must be treated as code and released as scripted, versioned artifacts rather than through manual manipulation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If anything fails during the deployment or the smoke test, the process must stop and start again; manual intervention may be required for certain edge cases until the automation tools are improved to handle them.

Concept_Name: Continuous Deployment
Core_Definition: A practice where the decision to deploy a release is automated following successful fully automated build and test cycles, allowing multiple releases to be deployed each day.
Key_Actors: Developers; system administrators; automated release pipelines.
Operational_Workflow: Code is checked into the repository; the system performs an automated build; the system performs automated testing; upon successful completion of the tests, the software is automatically deployed to the field; the process can occur as frequently as new packages are available.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process for releasing and deploying must be repeatable and reliable; all components and configurations must be kept in source control; the goal is to reduce code latency and increase velocity to generate a faster return on investment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an organization uses error budgets and the budget is exhausted, the team may be restricted to only emergency security fixes for the remainder of the month; in some contexts, the actual push to production remains a manual business decision even if the rest of the pipeline is automated.

Concept_Name: Automated Provisioning
Core_Definition: The allocation of additional resources such as CPU, storage, memory, and bandwidth based on a predictive model.
Key_Actors: The system; automation tools.
Operational_Workflow: The system detects that more resources are required; it automatically allocates those resources for use based on a predictive model; high-level descriptions of tasks are used to allow automation to determine the specific steps required for a given operating system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every step of the deployment process must be automated or scripted; provisioning should be based on a predictive model; administration should be abstracted so that automation decides the right steps to perform for a given operating system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: product backlog
Core_Definition: A prioritized list of user stories, features, and technical debt that guides the work of development and operations teams.
Key_Actors: Product owners; developers; operations staff; management.
Operational_Workflow: User stories are created to provide requirements for the system; the product owner maintains and prioritizes the list based on business needs; findings from postmortems and technical debt are added to the list; resources are allocated to address items based on their priority.
Rules_and_Constraints: The backlog must be prioritized according to business needs; it should include tasks necessary to correct issues identified in postmortems; it is intended to maintain agility and avoid wasted effort or rework.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Agile
Core_Definition: A collection of software development principles that emphasize collaboration, iterative development, and responsiveness to change over rigid documentation and processes.
Key_Actors: Agile Alliance; developers; product owners; scrum teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers and product owners collaborate to build software based on business objectives; requirements are defined as user stories; teams work in sprints of one to four weeks on a prioritized backlog; unit and integration tests are automatically applied during the build process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Individuals and interactions are valued over processes and tools; working code is valued over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration is valued over contract negotiation; responding to change is valued over following a plan; testing is performed as early as possible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The waterfall methodology is considered the antithesis of Agile because its isolated steps make it risky and prone to failure.

Concept_Name: continuous delivery
Core_Definition: A discipline and set of practices for the ongoing delivery of software and updates from source code to installation-ready packages.
Key_Actors: Developers; release engineers; automated testing systems; quality assurance teams.
Operational_Workflow: New source code changes trigger an automated build process; successful completion of the build triggers automated testing; once tests pass, software packages are made available for use.
Rules_and_Constraints: Binaries must be built only once per release cycle; the deployment process must be repeatable and reliable; everything must be kept in source control; difficult or painful tasks should be done more often to improve and automate them; basic smoke tests must be performed on deployments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The process stops immediately if the build or testing fails; if a failure occurs, the line must be stopped to find the root cause and restart the process rather than applying patches to proceed.

Concept_Name: Agile Manifesto
Core_Definition: A highly influential set of software development principles created by the Agile Alliance that prioritizes collaboration, working code, and adaptability.
Key_Actors: The Agile Alliance; representatives from nontraditional software practices; developers; product owners.
Operational_Workflow: Developers work closely with product owners to build software that meets specific business objectives; requirements are established using plain statements called user stories; a prioritized backlog of these stories is maintained to allow for adjustments based on business needs; unit and integration tests are created alongside new feature code; testing is applied automatically during the build process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Individuals and interactions are valued over processes and tools; working code is valued over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration is valued over contract negotiation; responding to change is valued over following a plan; the items on the left of these comparisons are valued more than the items on the right.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The manifesto represents a shift away from the waterfall methodology where requirements are locked early and testing is done in isolation.

Concept_Name: Extreme Programming
Core_Definition: A nontraditional software practice that contributed to the Agile Manifesto and served as the origin for the discipline of continuous delivery.
Key_Actors: Nontraditional software practice representatives.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Scrum
Core_Definition: A collaborative software development method where a whole team commits to focused work cycles to address a prioritized backlog of features.
Key_Actors: Development teams; product owners.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies features from a prioritized backlog; the team commits to a work cycle known as a sprint; the team works on the selected features for a period of one to four weeks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Work must be performed in sprints; sprints are constrained to a duration of one to four weeks; work must be based on a prioritized backlog of feature stories.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Pragmatic Programming
Core_Definition: One of the nontraditional software practices represented by the group that created the Agile Manifesto.
Key_Actors: Nontraditional software practice representatives.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: The Agile Alliance
Core_Definition: A group of representatives from various nontraditional software practices who gathered to create the Agile Manifesto.
Key_Actors: Representatives from Extreme Programming; representatives from Scrum; representatives from Pragmatic Programming.
Operational_Workflow: Representatives from different software practices convened in an unusual summit; they authored the Agile Manifesto to codify their shared values; these values were established to move development away from the traditional waterfall methodology toward more collaborative methods.
Rules_and_Constraints: Individuals and interactions are valued over processes and tools; working code is valued over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration is valued over contract negotiation; responding to change is valued over following a plan.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The alliance acknowledges there is value in processes, documentation, contract negotiation, and following plans, but they explicitly value the opposing human-centric and functional items more.

Concept_Name: product owners
Core_Definition: Individuals who work closely with developers to ensure software meets specific business objectives by managing requirements.
Key_Actors: Product owners; developers; business stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The product owner maintains a prioritized backlog of feature stories; they provide requirements to developers in the form of user stories; they update and adjust the prioritized list as business needs change to ensure the development team avoids wasted effort; they collaborate with developers throughout the development cycle to build software that aligns with business goals.
Rules_and_Constraints: Requirements must be maintained as a prioritized list; product owners must focus on business objectives; user stories are used instead of traditional functional specifications or requirements documents.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: user stories
Core_Definition: Simple statements that describe requirements for software development from the perspective of a user.
Key_Actors: Product owners; developers; customers or users.
Operational_Workflow: Requirements are written as plain statements explaining what a user wants to achieve; these stories are added to a prioritized backlog by the product owner; developers use these stories as the primary source of requirements for building features; unit and integration tests are created to correspond with the features defined by the stories.
Rules_and_Constraints: User stories must be plain statements; they must be kept in a prioritized backlog; they often serve as the only documentation for code releases in place of formal functional specifications.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: sprints
Core_Definition: Fixed development cycles where a team commits to working on a prioritized backlog of features over a set duration.
Key_Actors: Scrum teams; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A team selects a set of features from a prioritized backlog; the team commits to a specific period of development; the team works collaboratively to complete the features within the allotted time; the process is repeated to maintain agility and respond to changing business needs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sprints must last between one and four weeks; work must be performed on a prioritized backlog; the process must involve team commitment to the tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Continuous delivery (CD)
Core_Definition: A set of principles and practices for the delivery of software and updates on an ongoing basis. It represents the process by which software moves from source code to ready-to-use installation packages.
Key_Actors: Developers; product owners; organizational teams; automated build and testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: New source code changes are detected which trigger the build process; if the build is successful, an automated testing process is triggered; if tests pass, the software packages are made available for use.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must emphasize automation, packaging, and repeatability; it requires a culture of shared responsibility for outcomes; every step is considered part of the ultimate delivery; the process must occur constantly at every stage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The entire process stops if either the build or the testing phase fails.

Concept_Name: Eight Principles of Continuous Delivery
Core_Definition: A codified set of foundational guidelines designed to ensure the software delivery process is efficient, reliable, and high-quality.
Key_Actors: Everyone involved in the release process; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Teams ensure the release process is repeatable and reliable while automating all possible steps; everything is maintained in source control and quality is built into the process; everyone takes responsibility for the release until it is working properly in the hands of the end user.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process for releasing and deploying must be repeatable and reliable; automate everything; if something is difficult or painful, do it more often to improve and automate it; keep everything in source control; done means released, working properly, and in the hands of the end user; build quality in; everybody has responsibility for the release process; improve continuously.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Four Practices of Continuous Delivery
Core_Definition: A specific set of four operational methods used to execute the continuous delivery model effectively and prevent errors.
Key_Actors: Build systems; deployment systems; diagnostic tests; QA and deployment teams.
Operational_Workflow: Binaries are built exactly once per release cycle; the same process is used for deployment across all environments; smoke tests are performed to verify basic functionality; the process is halted immediately upon any failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Build binaries only once; use the same repeatable process for deployment to every environment; do basic functionality tests on your deployment; if anything fails, stop the line and start again.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a failure occurs, practitioners must find the root cause and restart the process from the beginning rather than patching the failure and proceeding.

Concept_Name: smoke test
Core_Definition: A basic functionality test, such as a built-in diagnostic, used to verify that a deployed system meets minimum requirements. 
Key_Actors: Build automation; deployment automation; diagnostic tools.
Operational_Workflow: Diagnostic tests are integrated into the automated build and deployment process; the tests are executed immediately upon deployment to ensure the system does not fail at a fundamental level.
Rules_and_Constraints: These tests should be included as part of build and deployment automation; they are intended to provide high confidence that basic functionality is met.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The build or deployment process will trigger an error and stop if the built-in diagnostic tests fail.

Concept_Name: diagnostics
Core_Definition: Built-in functionality tests used to verify that a deployed service meets basic requirements.
Key_Actors: Build and deployment automation systems; software applications.
Operational_Workflow: Diagnostics are included as part of automated smoke tests during the deployment process; the automation system runs these tests to ensure the application is working properly; the build or deployment process produces an error if these tests fail.
Rules_and_Constraints: Diagnostics should be included as part of smoke tests in the build and deployment automation to provide high confidence in the deployment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a diagnostic test fails, the build or deployment system errors out to notify the team that the functionality is not meeting requirements.

Concept_Name: Version-Controlled Builds
Core_Definition: A build system approach where a specific software version is required to be built using a precise, verified toolchain to ensure long-term consistency.
Key_Actors: Build tools team; build system; software developers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The build tools team defines the specific toolchain including the compiler and operating system kernel required for a software version; the build system verifies these components before allowing a build; the system prevents any attempt to build the software with an unauthorized toolchain; this allows developers to recreate the exact environment years later to apply patches.
Rules_and_Constraints: The build system must not allow a version to be built with any toolchain other than the one specifically determined by the build tools team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: MD5 hash
Core_Definition: A unique identifier used to verify the integrity and specific version of files within a build toolchain.
Key_Actors: Build system.
Operational_Workflow: The build system checks the MD5 hash of the operating system kernel, compiler, and other critical files; it compares these hashes against the required specifications for the version being built to ensure the environment has not changed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the MD5 hash of a toolchain component does not match the expected value, the build system stops the process and prevents the software from being built.

