Concept_Name: Chef
Core_Definition: A software framework used for configuration management.
Key_Actors: Chef software framework; machines.
Operational_Workflow: The framework permits the creation of modules for configuring specific services and applying those modules to different machines as needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: convergent orchestration
Core_Definition: A configuration strategy where the system takes a description of a desired state and makes individual changes to ensure the environment converges on and stays in that state.
Key_Actors: Configuration management systems such as CFEngine, Puppet, or Chef; users; environment machines.
Operational_Workflow: The system receives a description of how the environment should be configured; it detects if an undesired change has occurred due to user action or machine failure; it makes the fewest changes required to return the system to the desired state; when a new configuration is defined, the system begins moving toward that new definition.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be architected specifically to support this strategy; it is described as getting the environment to a particular state and keeping it there; it requires the configuration management system to detect and correct deviations automatically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a process requires a specific order of steps where multiple states must be transitioned through sequentially, such as a database migration, this method is considered unwieldy; enterprise commercial products that cannot be modified often present a barrier to using this method.

Concept_Name: direct orchestration
Core_Definition: A method used to execute a multistep process in a specific order while ensuring certain conditions remain true throughout the duration of the change.
Key_Actors: Database clients; primary and replica database machines; load balancers; web servers; human operators.
Operational_Workflow: A sequence of tasks is performed in a strict order, such as configuring a replica, waiting for synchronization, placing clients in read-only mode, swapping machine roles, and reconfiguring clients; the system ensures that specific invariants are maintained during these transitions.
Rules_and_Constraints: The steps must happen in a certain order; the process must prevent the system from painting itself into a corner; currently this is often a manual and error-prone process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A major challenge occurs when multiple processes happen at the same time, such as adding a load balancer while simultaneously reconfiguring web servers, which requires complex coordination to prevent conflicts.

Concept_Name: invariant
Core_Definition: A specific condition or rule that must hold true throughout the execution of a multistep operational process.
Key_Actors: Database; database clients; orchestration system.
Operational_Workflow: In the context of direct orchestration, the system executes various configuration steps while monitoring or ensuring that the defined condition, such as client access, is never violated.
Rules_and_Constraints: An example provided in the text is the requirement that clients always have access to the database during a migration from one machine to another.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: approval chain
Core_Definition: A list of specific people, delegates, or departments that must sign off on a release before it is promoted to a production environment.
Key_Actors: Product manager; director of marketing; director of engineering; security department; legal department; privacy compliance department.
Operational_Workflow: Once automated and manual testing is finished and a release is designated as a production candidate, members of the chain are asked to sign off; after all required parties have given their approval, the release is promoted and pushed into the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: The production environment generally requires positive confirmation by the entire chain; different environments may have different approval chains; deployments to development and testing environments are often automatically approved.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Environments for demos or beta users may be automatically upgraded periodically or on a set schedule, bypassing the full scrutiny of the standard production approval chain.

Concept_Name: System Testing
Core_Definition: Testing that brings together all the various pieces of the service to test the final product or system as a whole.
Key_Actors: Developers.
Operational_Workflow: System testing is performed on the service while it is running in the testing environment; it involves testing every individual feature as well as multistep workflows such as making a purchase; testing frameworks may be used to automate these tests by acting as a user would.
Rules_and_Constraints: Passing system tests is a mandatory precondition for a release to be used in production or any environment that includes external customers; these tests are used as a gate to ensure developers do not ship products with defects.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If system tests fail, the release is prevented from being promoted from the test environment to the production environment.

Concept_Name: Selenium WebDriver
Core_Definition: An open source project and testing framework used to automate web site testing.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The tool automates testing by sending HTTP requests to the service as if they originated from various different web browsers.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used to perform tests as if they are being done by a human user; it is applicable to web-based interfaces regardless of how the user interacts with the software.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Performance Testing
Core_Definition: Tests designed to determine the speed and behavior of a service under various different conditions.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: This testing is performed on the service while it is in the testing environment or in a specially built performance testing environment; the results are measured and then compared against written specifications or the results of previous test runs.
Rules_and_Constraints: The testing should determine if the service meets its written requirements; because specifications are often vague or nonexistent, the primary rule is to compare current results against historical data to ensure no degradation has occurred.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the system or a specific feature performs significantly more slowly than the previous release, the test results in a failure due to a performance regression.

Concept_Name: performance regression
Core_Definition: A condition where the entire system or a specific feature works significantly more slowly than it did in the previous software release.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: A performance regression is identified during the performance testing phase when the speed of the current release is compared to and found to be significantly worse than previous results.
Rules_and_Constraints: The identification of a performance regression causes the performance test to fail; it serves as a criterion for rejecting a new release candidate.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load Testing
Core_Definition: A special kind of performance testing used to determine the maximum amount of traffic or load a system can sustain.
Key_Actors: Testing environment; special performance testing environments; search clusters; Linux kernels.
Operational_Workflow: The service is subjected to increasingly larger amounts of traffic or load to determine the maximum it can process. For example, search queries are artificially generated at larger and larger rates until a cluster maxes out or response times exceed required thresholds.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is usually performed in a testing environment or a specialized performance testing environment. Results are typically compared to previous releases to ensure the system can still sustain the same or better load.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the maximum load sustained is significantly less than the previous release, the upgrade or change is rejected. The test also fails if the system becomes so slow that it cannot answer queries within the required number of milliseconds.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: A metric used during load testing to measure the volume of queries processed by a system, such as a search cluster, per second.
Key_Actors: Search clusters; Linux kernels; testing systems that generate artificial queries.
Operational_Workflow: Queries are artificially generated at higher and higher rates to find the point where a cluster is unable to handle more or where latency becomes unacceptable.
Rules_and_Constraints: The maximum QPS achieved is compared against the maximum QPS of previous releases to determine if a performance regression has occurred.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: User Acceptance Testing (UAT)
Core_Definition: Testing performed by customers to verify that a system meets their specific needs and requirements and to ensure they do not receive products with defects.
Key_Actors: Customers; developers; producers.
Operational_Workflow: Customers run their own tests, which may include running through every business process that involves the service. They use these tests to verify claims made by the producer and to check for defects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Ideally, tests developed for UAT should be shared with developers to be included in earlier testing stages. UAT may also be used to determine if internal processes require revision.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: UAT may involve tests using live data that cannot be shared with developers, such as personally identifiable information.

Concept_Name: personally identifiable information (PII)
Core_Definition: Sensitive live data that is used in certain testing scenarios but cannot be shared outside of specific environments.
Key_Actors: Customers; privacy compliance departments.
Operational_Workflow: This data is utilized by customers during User Acceptance Testing to verify a system using real-world scenarios.
Rules_and_Constraints: PII is specifically noted as data that cannot be shared, which prevents certain UAT tests from being integrated into the developers' earlier automated testing batteries.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Handoffs and Gates
Core_Definition: Handoffs represent the transitions between different stages of the service delivery flow, while gates are specific tests or checks designed to prevent defects from being passed forward to the next stage. This concept uses an assembly-line analogy to ensure quality at each step of the process.
Key_Actors: Automated testing modules; software packagers; testing environments; production environments; StackExchange (as a provided example).
Operational_Workflow: The process begins when code is built and subjected to unit tests to gate the packaging stage; digital signature verification then gates whether the packager can pass code to the test environment; promoting a release from the test environment to production is gated by system tests; finally, the success of a production environment upgrade is gated by health checks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every step in the service delivery flow must have a gate; defects must not be passed forward to subsequent stages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: unit tests
Core_Definition: Automated tests performed on each individual component of the software during the build phase. They serve as the initial quality gate in the service delivery process.
Key_Actors: Software components; the build phase system; automated testing frameworks.
Operational_Workflow: Code is initially built during the build phase; unit tests are executed against each individual component; the results of these tests gate the transition to the packaging step.
Rules_and_Constraints: Unit testing must be performed on each component; these tests must be passed before the code can be packaged for further deployment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: digital signature verification
Core_Definition: A security gate used to authorize the movement of code from the packaging stage to the testing environment. It ensures that the software being deployed is from a verified and trusted source.
Key_Actors: The software packager; the testing environment.
Operational_Workflow: After the software is packaged, the system performs a digital signature verification; if the signature is verified, the packager is permitted to pass the code into the test environment for further evaluation.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process acts as a specific gate between the packaging stage and the test environment stage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: production candidate
Core_Definition: A software release that has successfully passed all required automated and manual testing and is ready for the final sign-off process. It is the version of the software that is eligible to be installed in the live production environment.
Key_Actors: Product manager; director of marketing; director of engineering; security department; legal department; privacy compliance department; approval chain.
Operational_Workflow: The software undergoes system, performance, load, and user acceptance testing; once all tests pass, the release is designated as a production candidate; the candidate is then put through an approval process where members of the approval chain provide sign-offs; after all approvals are received, the candidate is promoted and installed in the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: A release is only called a production candidate if all tests pass; the production environment requires the most scrutiny and generally necessitates positive confirmation by the entire approval chain; environments exposed to revenue-generating customers must be gated with the highest level of scrutiny.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: operations console
Core_Definition: A web-based software system used to manage operational processes and deployment steps.
Key_Actors: Users who initiate production launches; security and authorization systems; teams managing the deployment phase.
Operational_Workflow: The system manages the steps of the deployment phase; it provides a platform to view results and past history; it maintains statistics regarding process duration and success rates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tighter controls may be implemented regarding who is permitted to initiate a launch for a new production release; security and authorization are emphasized because the console manages processes that affect live services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: build console
Core_Definition: A web-based system that facilitates viewing results and history of the build phase, serving as a template for the operations console.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The console allows for the viewing of results and past history; it keeps statistics about success rates and process duration.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
