Concept_Name: standardization
Core_Definition: A strategy to reduce infrastructure complexity by using a limited number of hardware configurations or virtual machine sizes to make resources interchangeable and manageable.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure teams; physical machines; virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations create a few specific hardware models such as one for heavy computation and one for mass storage; physical machines are treated as interchangeable pools based on these categories; virtual machine sizes are selected so that multiples of smaller sizes perfectly fill the capacity of a physical machine; an API is used to allocate these standardized resources as needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standardizing on a few hardware configurations makes machines interchangeable; virtual machine sizes should be standardized to ensure full utilization of physical hosts and easier reorganization; using multiples of Fibonacci numbers for VM sizes is a suggested method to help utilize physical machine space.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When virtual machines are custom sizes instead of standard sizes, moving them to reorganize space becomes highly complex and inefficient, similar to the Towers of Hanoi problem.

Concept_Name: pool of machines
Core_Definition: A collection of interchangeable, standardized hardware resources that can be allocated for tasks and returned when no longer in use.
Key_Actors: Management APIs; standardized hardware categories.
Operational_Workflow: Physical machines are standardized into categories such as computation-heavy or storage-heavy; these machines are grouped into a pool where they are treated as interchangeable; an API permits the allocation of a machine from the pool when needed; the machine is returned to the pool once its task is complete.
Rules_and_Constraints: Machines must be standardized into a few configurations to be treated as a pool; the allocation process should be designed to be as easy as creating virtual machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: API
Core_Definition: A software interface used to automate the allocation of resources and the control of infrastructure components like storage, machines, and networks.
Key_Actors: Developers; testing tools such as Selenium WebDriver; infrastructure management systems; DNS services.
Operational_Workflow: A system or user makes an API call to allocate a pool of standardized machines or to create virtual machines; testing frameworks use APIs to automate software tests by sending requests as if they came from browsers or consoles; the service delivery platform uses APIs to manipulate virtual infrastructure like storage volumes and network topologies; APIs are used to update DNS zones rapidly as machines are brought up or turned down.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs should permit allocations to happen as easily as creating virtual machines; infrastructure controlled by APIs allows for the implementation of infrastructure as code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: VMs
Core_Definition: Virtual machines are software-based emulations of physical computers that can be rapidly allocated, configured, and moved through API calls.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual disks; storage area networks; software frameworks like Vagrant, Docker, and Netflix Aminator.
Operational_Workflow: A virtual machine is prepared via an API call using standardized sizes to ensure physical hardware is fully utilized; an operating system and service are installed using either a baked image method or configuration management; the VM may be configured to mount a virtual disk from a remote storage system so that the boot disk remains disposable while data is preserved; during upgrades, the existing VM can be deleted and re-created from a new image.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standardized sizes such as small, medium, and large should be used to prevent unused space on physical machines; boot disks should be decoupled from persistent data to make machines interchangeable and disposable; preparing a VM should ideally be a matter of a single API call.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If VMs are created with custom sizes, moving them to reorganize physical machine space becomes inefficient and complex; physical machines may suffer from fragmentation where space is available but not in a single block large enough for a new custom VM.

Concept_Name: Fibonacci numbers
Core_Definition: A mathematical sequence used as a strategy for standardizing the sizes of virtual machines to optimize the utilization of physical hardware.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; physical machines.
Operational_Workflow: Virtual machines are allocated in sizes that are multiples of Fibonacci numbers; when a larger unit is deallocated, it leaves behind a specific amount of space that perfectly fits smaller Fibonacci-sized units; for example, deallocating a 5-unit machine creates space that can be filled by one 2-unit machine and one 3-unit machine or five 1-unit machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using these numbers as a sizing standard helps in fully utilizing physical machines and makes the process of reorganizing them easier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Towers of Hanoi problem
Core_Definition: A reference to a complex puzzle used to describe the difficulty of moving and reorganizing virtual machines when they are not standardized in size.
Key_Actors: Custom-sized virtual machines; physical machines.
Operational_Workflow: An administrator attempts to move virtual machines between physical hosts to create enough contiguous space for a new allocation; because the machines have custom rather than standardized sizes, the process requires many intermediate steps to shuffle resources; this results in a high degree of inefficiency and complexity during infrastructure management.
Rules_and_Constraints: This problem is a consequence of failing to use standardized VM sizes; it is avoided by implementing fixed sizes or sizes based on Fibonacci numbers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: image method
Core_Definition: A deployment strategy that involves creating a disk image for each kind of service which is then copied onto a disk to bring a machine up in a preconfigured state.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; containers; software frameworks including Vagrant, Docker, and Netflix Aminator; service delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: A disk image is created containing the operating system, all required packages, and the service in a ready-to-run form; the image is copied onto the disk of a physical machine, virtual machine, or container; the machine is rebooted; the service comes up preconfigured and ready for use.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is known as the baked image method because the service is baked in; images can be used for building new services or upgrading existing ones; upgrading involves taking a machine out of rotation, deleting it, and re-creating it from the image so all machines have a well-understood configuration; creation of images should be automated so descriptions can be kept under version control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Maintaining many images can be a burden because tasks like installing patches are labor intensive; if done manually there is no version control; for machines with irreplaceable data like database servers the boot disk is replaced while the data is maintained on separate virtual disks mounted from remote storage.

Concept_Name: configuration management method
Core_Definition: A deployment strategy that uses an installer to get a minimal operating system running and then uses configuration management tools to install packages and configure the service.
Key_Actors: Software frameworks such as CFEngine, Puppet, and Chef; installers; configuration management tools; minimal operating system.
Operational_Workflow: A minimal operating system is established via an installer or other mechanism; configuration management tools install additional packages; machine-specific settings and data are applied; specific tasks like configuring domains or notifying load balancers are performed; health checks are conducted to verify the system is running.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is known as frying the image because the image is cooked up while you wait; it is often faster and less disruptive than the image method because it makes only the minimum changes needed; it can use convergent orchestration to maintain a desired state or direct orchestration to execute a multistep process with specific invariants.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machines can end up in a less well-understood configuration because entropy or small differences may creep in over time; future upgrades require testing for multiple configurations which adds complexity; direct orchestration is currently done manually at large scale because automation is an error-prone process that researchers are only just beginning to consider.

Concept_Name: disk image
Core_Definition: A file or storage container that includes an operating system, required packages, and a service in a ready-to-run state.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; containers; software frameworks such as Vagrant, Docker, and Netflix Aminator.
Operational_Workflow: The image is created from a language description that specifies the base operating system, packages, and file settings; the image is copied to a machine disk; the machine is rebooted to start the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: When the service is included it is referred to as a baked image; images can be deployed to physical machines, virtual machines, or containers; images allow for rapid installation because configuration is done ahead of time; descriptions used to create images should be kept under version control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Disk images are not suitable for systems with persistent data that is not part of the build process such as database or file servers; in these cases, the boot disk image is replaced while the irreplaceable data is decoupled and mounted from remote storage like a storage area network.

Concept_Name: containers
Core_Definition: A type of virtual infrastructure that can be used as a deployment target for disk images and services.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platform; baked images; infrastructure as code; developers; quality assurance personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Images for containers are built and packaged; infrastructure as code describes the container environment; the containers are deployed and managed through the service delivery platform; individuals use containers to build their own sandbox or test environments.
Rules_and_Constraints: Containers can receive baked images containing the operating system and services; they are part of the infrastructure as code approach where settings are stored in source repositories and revision controlled; they help minimize differences between testing and production environments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: baked image
Core_Definition: A disk image for a service that contains the operating system, all required packages, and the service in a ready-to-run form.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; containers; service delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: A disk image is created for a specific kind of service; the image is copied onto a disk; the machine is rebooted; the machine starts up with the service already preconfigured and running.
Rules_and_Constraints: Baked images can be used for building new services or upgrading existing ones; when used for upgrades, existing servers are taken out of rotation, deleted, and re-created from the image; they are intended to provide a well-understood configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Maintaining many images can be a significant burden; installing patches on many images is labor intensive; if not automated, there is no version control for the images which is considered bad.

Concept_Name: frying the image
Core_Definition: A configuration management strategy where a minimal operating system is installed first and the service is then configured while you wait.
Key_Actors: Configuration management tools such as CFEngine, Puppet, or Chef; installers; operating systems.
Operational_Workflow: An installer or other mechanism is used to get a minimal operating system running; configuration management tools then install any additional required packages; the tools bring up the service to reach the desired state.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method is often faster than installing a full image for upgrades; it is less disruptive because it makes the minimum number of changes needed; machines are not wiped during this process so no data is lost.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machines can end up in a less well-understood configuration compared to those built from scratch; entropy can accumulate on the machines over time; small configuration differences may creep in between machines that were upgraded versus those that were newly installed; future upgrades require testing that covers both upgraded and fresh states which adds complexity.

Concept_Name: Automated Baking
Core_Definition: The process of using software frameworks to create baked images from descriptions kept under version control.
Key_Actors: Software frameworks such as Vagrant, Docker, and Netflix Aminator; version control systems; service delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: A description is written in a framework-specific language specifying the base operating system, packages, and file settings; the description is stored in a source repository under version control; the service delivery platform uses this description to automatically build, test, and deploy the images.
Rules_and_Constraints: The image must be created from a scratch description; the process should be integrated into the service delivery platform to allow for automated building and testing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Vagrant
Core_Definition: A software framework that provides a language for describing how to build a baked image from scratch.
Key_Actors: Software engineers; service delivery platform; virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: Users define the base operating system release, packages, and file settings in the Vagrant language; the description is kept under version control; the service delivery platform utilizes the description to build and test images; software engineers may also use it to build sandbox environments using virtual machines on their own laptops.
Rules_and_Constraints: Vagrant is used to ensure that image descriptions are revision controlled and can be deployed through the service delivery platform.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Docker
Core_Definition: A software framework used for creating baked images that contain the operating system, all required packages, and the service in a ready-to-run form.
Key_Actors: Software frameworks; service delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: The framework provides a language for describing how to build an image from scratch by specifying the base operating system release, packages, and file settings; the image is then created from this description; the description is kept under version control so the service delivery platform can build, test, and deploy the images to physical machines, virtual machines, or containers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Image descriptions should be kept under version control to allow the service delivery platform to build and test them; baked images should be used to ensure all servers running a release have a well-understood configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Netflix Aminator
Core_Definition: A software framework used to automate the creation of baked images for service deployment.
Key_Actors: Software frameworks; service delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: The framework uses a language to describe the process of building an image from scratch including the base operating system release, packages, and file settings; this description is stored in version control; the service delivery platform then uses the description to build, test, and deploy images.
Rules_and_Constraints: Descriptions used by the framework should be kept under version control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: version control
Core_Definition: A system and process for storing and managing revisions of software packages, infrastructure descriptions, and configuration data.
Key_Actors: Source repository; service delivery platform; developers; QA personnel; software engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Descriptions for building images from scratch are kept under version control so they can be built, tested, and deployed by the service delivery platform; code describing infrastructure is stored in a source repository and revision controlled; configuration management code and data are built and packaged using the same workflow as application software.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure code should be treated exactly like application code; changes must be tested in a test environment before being approved for production deployment; using version control for infrastructure and image descriptions is a best practice to avoid the lack of versioning found in manual image creation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When installation is performed using manual image methods without automated frameworks, there is no version control, which is described as a negative state for the environment.

Concept_Name: Persistent Data
Core_Definition: Irreplaceable data hosted on a server, such as a database or file server, that is not included as part of the automated build or configuration process.
Key_Actors: Database servers; file servers; storage area network (SAN); remote storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Persistent data is placed on virtual disks that are mounted from a storage area network or other remote storage system; when a machine is upgraded or reinstalled, the boot disk is replaced, but the system reattaches to the required data by mounting the virtual disk during the boot process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Where storage is provided must be decoupled from where it is used to ensure machines remain disposable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unlike standard web servers, machines with persistent data cannot simply be wiped and reinstalled without first ensuring the data is decoupled and stored on a remote system.

Concept_Name: virtual disks
Core_Definition: Storage volumes used to house irreplaceable data, such as database or file server information, that is not included in the automated build or configuration process.
Key_Actors: Database servers; file servers; storage area networks; remote storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Irreplaceable data is placed on virtual disks instead of the local boot disk. When a machine boots up, it mounts the virtual disk from a remote storage system to reattach the system to the persistent data it needs. This decoupling allows the machine's boot disk to be replaced or wiped while maintaining data continuity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Storage provision must be decoupled from where it is used to ensure machines remain interchangeable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machines containing persistent data cannot simply be wiped and reinstalled unless that data has been specifically moved to virtual disks.

Concept_Name: storage area network (SAN)
Core_Definition: A remote storage system used to provide and manage virtual disks for machines in a distributed environment.
Key_Actors: Virtual disks; database servers; file servers; remote storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: The SAN serves as the backend repository for virtual disks. Machines are configured to mount these disks over the network during the boot process to access persistent data that exists outside of the standard software installation.
Rules_and_Constraints: The SAN is used to facilitate the decoupling of storage from the physical or virtual machines using it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: disposable
Core_Definition: A characteristic of machines that allows them to be wiped, deleted, or replaced at any time without loss of critical data or service identity.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; load balancers; boot disks; virtual disks.
Operational_Workflow: Machines are made disposable by separating the operating system and service configuration from persistent data. When an upgrade is required, a machine is removed from the load balancer rotation, deleted, and re-created from a fresh baked image.
Rules_and_Constraints: To achieve disposability, there must be a decoupling of where storage is provided from where it is used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Database servers and file servers with irreplaceable data are not naturally disposable unless their storage is moved to remote virtual disks.

Concept_Name: entropy accumulate
Core_Definition: The gradual buildup of undocumented or small configuration differences on a machine that occurs when updates are applied incrementally to a running system.
Key_Actors: Web servers; configuration management tools; baked images.
Operational_Workflow: When configuration management is used to upgrade machines individually without wiping them, the machines can end up in a less well-understood state than those created from a fresh image. Over time, these small differences between machines increase, leading to a state of accumulated entropy.
Rules_and_Constraints: Proponents of baked images suggest that refreshing a machine from scratch is preferable to allowing entropy to build up through incremental updates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When entropy has accumulated, future upgrades become more complex because testing must cover all the different configuration variations that have developed across the fleet.

Concept_Name: environment-specific packages
Core_Definition: Separate software packages created to hold files, credentials, or certificates that must be different across various environments like testing and production.
Key_Actors: Configuration management systems; installation process; testing and production environments.
Operational_Workflow: Environment-specific files are moved into their own separate packages; each environment is assigned a unique configuration package; the installation process installs the main application package plus the specific configuration package designated for that environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: The use of these packages should be kept to an absolute minimum; they should preferably be restricted to low-risk files or data that can be tested through alternative methods.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These packages essentially bypass the standard testing process; they are typically used for sensitive data such as certificates that should not be exposed to all environments.

Concept_Name: pre-submit checks
Core_Definition: A testing mechanism used to verify environment-specific files or low-risk data that might otherwise bypass the standard testing process.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: They are identified as a best practice for testing files that cannot be included in the general application package due to environment-specific requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Continuous Delivery (CD)
Core_Definition: A technique where testing is fully automated and triggered for each build so that every change is proven to be deployable at any time.
Key_Actors: Automated testing systems; testing environments; beta environments.
Operational_Workflow: Each build triggers the automated creation of a testing environment; automated tests including system, performance, and user acceptance tests are run; the release is delivered as ready for use in other environments.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing must be fully automated to adopt CD; it makes working in small batches economical and low risk; it functions as an extension of continuous integration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If some tests are not automated, CD can deliver the release to a beta environment for manual testing; the process does not require every change to be deployed to production, only that it is ready for deployment.

Concept_Name: continuous integration (CI)
Core_Definition: A practice that serves as the foundation for continuous delivery, focused on finding and fixing problems early by working in small batches.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: It enables finding problems sooner so they are easier to fix; it makes it economical and low risk to work in small batches.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: small batches
Core_Definition: Small batches are a working technique where changes are handled in limited increments to ensure problems are found sooner and fixed more easily. This approach is facilitated by continuous delivery which makes working with smaller changes economical and low risk.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Testing is fully automated and triggered for each build; every change is proven to be deployable at any time; smaller increments of work are processed through the service delivery flow; problems are identified sooner during the automated testing phase; issues are fixed more easily due to the limited scope of the change.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing must be fully automated; the process must ensure that every change is deployable at any time; the technique should be used to find and fix problems sooner.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Infrastructure as Code
Core_Definition: Infrastructure as code is an approach where the code describing infrastructure is stored in a source repository, revision controlled, and treated like application software. It allows infrastructure to be built, tested, and deployed through automation that is managed by the service delivery platform.
Key_Actors: Teams; individuals; developers; quality assurance personnel; software engineers.
Operational_Workflow: Infrastructure configuration is encoded into software; the configuration code and data are stored in a source repository and kept under revision control; the code is built and packaged like application software; it is then tested in a test environment before being approved for deployment in the live production environment; the same code is used to build development, testing, and production environments by adjusting specific settings like machine counts and replicas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure for all environments should be built through automation treated just like any other service; code must be revision controlled and tested before production deployment; the same code should build all environments to minimize differences and improve testing accuracy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: service delivery platform (SDP)
Core_Definition: A service delivery platform is a system pattern that manages the deployment phase by turning software packages into running services through automated promotion, installation, and configuration. It handles both infrastructure and application layers to create testing and production environments.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; quality assurance personnel; software engineers; approval chain members such as product managers and directors of marketing or engineering.
Operational_Workflow: Packages are retrieved from a package repository and promoted for use in a desired environment; the platform installs packages on machines using an installer and runs pre-install and post-install scripts; local settings and machine-specific data are applied during the configuration step; the platform facilitates system, performance, and load testing to verify the release; the system manages infrastructure as code and interacts with auxiliary services like authentication, DNS, and configuration management databases.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure for all environments must be built through automation; deployment should be database driven; security facilities are required to provide authentication, authorization, and accounting; health checks must gate the configuration process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Manual coordination is often required for direct orchestration to prevent conflicts when multiple processes overlap; commercial products that cannot be modified represent a barrier to adopting convergent orchestration; direct orchestration is currently an error-prone manual process when attempted at a large scale.

Concept_Name: source repository
Core_Definition: A source repository is a storage system where code describing infrastructure is kept under revision control. It serves as the foundation for the infrastructure as code approach by managing configuration files and automation data.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Infrastructure configuration code and data are stored in the repository; the system tracks different versions of the code through revision control; the code is retrieved from the repository to be built, packaged, and tested; it provides a controlled environment for configuration management modules before they are deployed to production.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure code must be stored in the source repository just like application code; all infrastructure descriptions must be revision controlled.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Authentication
Core_Definition: A service within a service delivery platform used to identify users so that the system can restrict who is allowed to access specific resources.
Key_Actors: The service delivery platform; users; operations console.
Operational_Workflow: The system requires users to provide identification to verify their identity before they can access platform services; this process is specifically utilized within the operations console to manage and restrict who is permitted to initiate a launch for a new release into production.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security and authorization are considered more important for the operations console because its processes can affect live services; tighter controls are recommended for initiating production launches.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: security facilities
Core_Definition: Infrastructure components within a service delivery platform that provide the technical means for managing access and tracking.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platform; security departments; authentication, authorization, and accounting (AAA) services.
Operational_Workflow: These facilities function by delivering the combined services of authentication, authorization, and accounting to the overall service delivery platform to ensure secure operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: These facilities are identified as a necessary category of other platform services involved in a service delivery platform.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: authentication, authorization, and accounting (AAA)
Core_Definition: A set of security functions provided by security facilities to identify users, define their access levels, and track their activities.
Key_Actors: Security facilities; LDAP; Kerberos; Open Directory; Active Directory.
Operational_Workflow: The platform utilizes these functions to ensure that only identified and permitted users can perform specific actions while maintaining a record of those activities for accountability.
Rules_and_Constraints: This framework is often implemented using a combination of LDAP plus Kerberos, Open Directory, or Active Directory.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: LDAP
Core_Definition: A specific service that often functions as a component of the security facilities to provide authentication, authorization, and accounting.
Key_Actors: Security facilities; Kerberos; Open Directory; Active Directory.
Operational_Workflow: LDAP is integrated into the service delivery platform to facilitate the identification and permission management required for AAA security services.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is frequently used in conjunction with other systems such as Kerberos, Open Directory, or Active Directory to fulfill security requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Kerberos
Core_Definition: A security facility used to provide authentication for a service delivery platform.
Key_Actors: LDAP; security facilities providing authentication, authorization, and accounting.
Operational_Workflow: It is often used in combination with LDAP to restrict who can access various parts of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Open Directory
Core_Definition: A security facility used to provide authentication for a service delivery platform.
Key_Actors: LDAP; security facilities providing authentication, authorization, and accounting.
Operational_Workflow: It functions as part of a service that provides authentication, authorization, and accounting to control system access.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Active Directory
Core_Definition: A security facility used to provide authentication for a service delivery platform.
Key_Actors: LDAP; security facilities providing authentication, authorization, and accounting.
Operational_Workflow: It is utilized as a form of authentication to restrict system access to authorized individuals.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DNS
Core_Definition: A service that translates the names of machines into IP addresses.
Key_Actors: Machines; service delivery platform; API; dynamic DNS or DynDNS or DDNS.
Operational_Workflow: Because machines in a service delivery platform are brought up and turned down rapidly, the system updates DNS zones via an API or dynamic DNS to maintain accurate mapping between names and addresses.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is important to have the ability to update DNS zones via an API or dynamic DNS.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: dynamic DNS (DynDNS/DDNS)
Core_Definition: A system that translates machine names to IP addresses and allows for the automated updating of DNS zones via an API or dynamic mechanism.
Key_Actors: DNS; machines; Service Delivery Platform; APIs.
Operational_Workflow: Machines within a service delivery platform are brought up and turned down rapidly; the system uses an API or dynamic DNS updates to modify DNS zones to reflect these changes in machine names and IP addresses.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is important to have the ability to update DNS zones via an API or dynamic DNS because machines change rapidly in a service delivery platform.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Configuration Management Database (CMDB)
Core_Definition: A database where configurations and machine relationships are stored to drive the deployment phase of a service.
Key_Actors: Database; environment-building tools; configuration management tools; machines such as web server frontends.
Operational_Workflow: Configurations and machine relationships are stored in the database; tools that build the environment use this information to guide their work; modifying the environment is done by updating the database; configuration management tools use the updated database information to bring up services on specified machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: The deployment phase should be database driven.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service delivery engineering
Core_Definition: A large and changing field concerned with the deployment phase where software packages are turned into running services.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 11. Upgrading Live Services
Section: Chapter 11. Upgrading Live Services
============================================================

Concept_Name: code push
Core_Definition: The process of upgrading a production environment with a new software release.
Key_Actors: SREs; developers; production environments; on-call teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves modifying a running system by deploying new code packages through various techniques such as rolling upgrades, canaries, or phased roll-outs; it includes preparing the release, selecting a deployment strategy, executing the upgrade across servers or replicas, and verifying the new version.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pushes should ideally occur during the day when teams are awake and co-workers are available; risk is reduced through automated testing and small batches rather than late-night deployments; continuous deployment should be a goal unless external constraints exist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a push fails, the system may require a roll back to the last known good release or a roll forward to a subsequent fix; emergency hotfixes may be used for specific insurmountable problems but are considered risky.

Concept_Name: downtime
Core_Definition: A period during which a service is unavailable to users while an upgrade is performed.
Key_Actors: Users; developers; development and demo environments.
Operational_Workflow: The service is taken down entirely; the new code is pushed to all systems; the service is brought back up after testing the newly upgraded service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Downtime is generally unacceptable for most live services but may be permitted for development and demo environments where it is prescheduled.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For certain background components like a data pipeline, downtime may be acceptable if users do not immediately notice the outage, even if the freshness of results eventually deteriorates.

Concept_Name: global load balancer (GLB)
Core_Definition: A system that divides incoming traffic among multiple working service replicas located in different environments or datacenters.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; traffic; global load balancer system.
Operational_Workflow: The GLB distributes the total traffic load across all active replicas; during an upgrade, a specific replica is removed from the GLB to be drained of traffic; once upgraded and checked, the replica is added back to the GLB to resume handling its portion of the load.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be enough spare capacity across the remaining replicas so that the service remains functional while one replica is down for maintenance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: draining
Core_Definition: The process of removing a server or replica from active service by stopping new requests and allowing existing work to finish.
Key_Actors: Servers or machines; local or global load balancers; in-flight requests.
Operational_Workflow: The server is removed from the load balancer configuration or enters lame duck mode where it reports as unhealthy to the load balancer; the system waits until all in-flight requests are completed and no new traffic is received; the server is then upgraded and the draining process is undone once the upgrade is verified.
Rules_and_Constraints: Draining must ensure that no new traffic is sent to the target machine while ensuring that users with active requests do not experience an interruption; the process requires sufficient spare capacity in the remaining systems to handle the load.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In rolling upgrades, draining a server creates a temporary reduction in capacity; if there are 10 servers, the service operates at 90 percent capacity during the upgrade of a single machine.

Concept_Name: Blog Search service
Core_Definition: A service at Google used to demonstrate an architecture where customer-facing stacks are replicated across multiple datacenters to allow for upgrades without service interruption.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineers; Google; customers; customer-facing stack replicas; global load balancer.
Operational_Workflow: The customer-facing stack is replicated in four independent datacenters; traffic is drained from one stack at a time by removing it from the global load balancer; while one stack is down for upgrading and checking, the remaining stacks handle the entire traffic load; once the upgrade is verified, the stack is added back to the global load balancer.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service requires enough spare capacity so that any one stack can be offline while the others handle the full traffic load; each replica must be independent of the others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: pipeline
Core_Definition: A backend service within the Blog Search system responsible for scanning, ingesting, and distributing search data to customer-facing stacks.
Key_Actors: Blog Search service; customer-facing stacks; blog posts.
Operational_Workflow: The pipeline scans for new blog posts and ingests them; it produces a new corpus of search data; it distributes this corpus to four customer-facing stacks; upgrades are performed by taking the entire pipeline service down at once.
Rules_and_Constraints: Uptime for the pipeline is considered important but not essential because customers do not notice when the pipeline is down; downtime is acceptable because the service does not face the customer directly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the pipeline is down for an extended period, the freshness of the search results will deteriorate.

Concept_Name: rolling upgrade
Core_Definition: An upgrade technique where individual machines or servers are sequentially removed from service, upgraded, and returned to service until all elements have been updated.
Key_Actors: Individual machines or servers; local load balancer; customers; replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A server is drained by reconfiguring the load balancer to stop sending requests or by the replica entering lame duck mode; the system waits until all in-flight requests are completed; the server is upgraded and verified; the draining process is undone; the process repeats with the next server in the sequence.
Rules_and_Constraints: The technique requires planning to ensure sufficient capacity remains while the service is at reduced capacity; individual outages must be hidden from customers; load balancer stickiness or feature toggles are used to maintain consistency for the user.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A customer may see new features appear and disappear as sequential requests are routed between upgraded and non-upgraded machines, although this is rare.

Concept_Name: local load balancer
Core_Definition: A component that manages traffic to individual machines and hides maintenance-related outages from customers during a rolling upgrade.
Key_Actors: Individual servers or machines; customers; replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer is reconfigured to stop sending requests to a specific machine during the draining phase; it may also stop sending traffic if a replica reports it is unhealthy or in lame duck mode; it ensures continuous service by distributing requests to the remaining active machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must hide individual outages from the customer; it is often configured with stickiness to ensure a particular customer consistently reaches the same server.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: load balancer stickiness
Core_Definition: A load balancer feature that ensures sequential requests from a specific customer are directed to the same server. This provides a consistent experience for the user during periods when different versions of software are running simultaneously.
Key_Actors: Local load balancers; customers; individual machines or servers.
Operational_Workflow: As a rolling upgrade progresses, some machines in a cluster are upgraded while others remain on the old version; the load balancer identifies requests from a specific customer and routes them to the same machine they previously accessed; this ensures the customer does not see new features appear and disappear between different requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the absence of stickiness, a customer might experience inconsistent service behavior as their sequential requests are distributed between old and new software versions.

Concept_Name: features toggled off
Core_Definition: The practice of disabling new or incomplete features using software flags or configuration settings. This allows code to be deployed to production without making the features visible or accessible to users.
Key_Actors: Developers; product management; beta users; configuration management systems like Chubby or Zookeeper.
Operational_Workflow: New features are associated with a flag that is set to off by default; developers merge small amounts of code into the main branch with the feature hidden behind the flag; the code is pushed to production; when a feature is ready for release, the flag is flipped to on; the flag can be flipped back to off if problems are discovered.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique decouples deployment from release; it allows for merging small amounts of code to reduce merge conflicts; it supports A-B testing and differentiated service levels; flags should be used to enable features on demand rather than as part of a fixed release schedule.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Basic flag implementations using command-line arguments or environment variables require a process restart to change, causing service interruption; modern systems can notify servers of flag changes dynamically to avoid restarts; flags can be used to isolate bugs or manage dark launches where code is active but has no user-visible components.

Concept_Name: lame duck mode
Core_Definition: An operational state where a service replica reports itself as unhealthy to a load balancer despite being functional. This is used to gracefully drain traffic from a server before an upgrade.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; local load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: A replica is instructed to enter lame duck mode; the replica lies to the load balancer by reporting an unhealthy status; the load balancer stops sending new requests to that replica; the replica continues to process and finish all existing in-flight requests; once the requests are finished and no new traffic is arriving, the server is taken down for upgrading.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: automated testing
Core_Definition: The use of automated systems to verify the correctness and stability of software releases. It is a fundamental component of reducing risk in code pushes and enabling continuous deployment.
Key_Actors: Developers; SREs; automated testing environments; continuous integration and delivery pipelines.
Operational_Workflow: Code is subjected to comprehensive tests in a dedicated environment that mimics production; in a canary process, simplistic verification tests check that upgraded code is not crashing and queries are succeeding; for continuous deployment, every release must pass automated tests before being pushed; automated metrics such as build health and code coverage are used to gate the deployment process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing must be performed in a testing environment rather than on live users; testing environments must be kept in sync with production hardware and software versions; automated tests are generally more reliable at catching regressions than humans; successful testing is a prerequisite for automated code pushes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Flakey tests that intermittently fail can cause the deployment pipeline to pause; a failed canary process indicates a failure in the testing process and should trigger a stop in development to add new tests; emergency hotfixes may bypass full testing but are noted as risky; human intervention may be required to pause automated testing and deployment during scheduled freezes or high-risk periods.

Concept_Name: small batches
Core_Definition: A strategy for reducing risk in code deployments by merging and pushing small amounts of code changes frequently rather than large or infrequent updates.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; automated testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers build features through a series of small changes to a main source branch rather than waiting to merge large features; these changes are merged frequently to minimize error-prone merge conflicts; the process is often paired with automated testing to ensure stability and reduce risk.
Rules_and_Constraints: Small batches are considered a primary strategy for reducing risk during code pushes; merging small amounts of code is considered less error-prone than merging large blocks of code which becomes more difficult and risky over time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: canary process
Core_Definition: A special form of rolling upgrade used for large numbers of machines where a very small number of replicas are upgraded first to detect obvious problems before moving to larger groups.
Key_Actors: Replicas; servers; load balancers; SREs; developers; automated verification tests.
Operational_Workflow: A single machine is upgraded and monitored for five to ten minutes; simplistic verification tests are run to ensure the code is not crashing and queries succeed; if the canary lives a group of machines is upgraded followed by another wait and more testing; finally larger groups are upgraded until the process is complete; a common pattern involves upgrading one server then one per minute until one percent are upgraded followed by one per second for the remainder.
Rules_and_Constraints: Canarying should prevent bad pushes from being visible to users and acts as an insurance policy against accidental bad releases; it is not a testing process and it is not acceptable for a canary to fail; a failed canary should be rare enough to cause a complete halt in development to determine what additional testing is needed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If trouble is found the process is stopped immediately and the upgraded servers may be rolled back or shut down if there is enough capacity.

Concept_Name: roll back
Core_Definition: The process of returning a service to its previous stable state after a failed code push by uninstalling the new software and reinstalling the last known good release.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; software releases; feature flags; database schemas.
Operational_Workflow: When a failed push is detected the software is uninstalled and the most recent good release is reinstalled; if the issue is confined to a specific feature the associated software flag can be flipped to disable it; in database schema changes a roll back involves reverting to the previous phase of the expand and contract technique.
Rules_and_Constraints: Roll backs are used when features fail in ways that cannot be mitigated through other techniques; they should be tracked carefully and projects should be created to eliminate the need for them in the future.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Critics argue true roll back is impossible because uninstalling and reinstalling is an untested change in production; a failed roll back can be a larger problem than the original failed push; some environments prefer rolling forward to a fix instead of rolling back.

Concept_Name: system testing environment
Core_Definition: A dedicated environment separate from production where software and tools are tested to ensure they work as intended before being deployed to live users.
Key_Actors: Developers; SREs; repositories; automated testing systems; production hardware and software configurations.
Operational_Workflow: New releases and operational tools are tested in an environment that mimics production; the process includes testing the specific combination of service releases, kernel versions, virtualization frameworks, and hardware used in production; tools developed by operations are incorporated into the repository and development process for consistent testing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing must be conducted in the testing environment and not on live users; the environment must be kept in sync with production to ensure accuracy; it is considered a success when the testing environment detects a defective release before it reaches production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Divergence between the testing and production environments can lead to outages where software passes tests but fails in production due to differences in virtualization technology or kernel drivers.

Concept_Name: phased roll-outs
Core_Definition: A strategy where users are partitioned into groups that are upgraded one at a time.
Key_Actors: Users; dedicated clusters; web communities; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: Users are partitioned into groups based on their tolerance for risk; the first phase is upgraded and monitored for problems for a certain amount of time; if no problems are found the next phase starts automatically; the process continues until the largest and most active groups are upgraded.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each group or phase must be identified by its tolerance for risk; the earliest phases should be those that can tolerate more outages such as non-revenue-generating units.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The process is halted if any phase encounters problems; confidence in the release increases as it successfully passes through earlier phases before reaching the most critical ones.

Concept_Name: phase
Core_Definition: An individual group within a roll-out identified by its specific tolerance for risk.
Key_Actors: Employees; test environments; meta-communities; user clusters.
Operational_Workflow: A specific group of users or systems is designated as a phase; the phase receives the upgrade first to act as a tester for the new release; results are monitored before moving to the next group.
Rules_and_Constraints: Phases are categorized by risk tolerance; for example employees may be willing testers while meta-communities or less populated areas serve as intermediate steps.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: proportional shedding
Core_Definition: A deployment technique where a new service is built on new machines in parallel to the old service and traffic is gradually moved by a load balancer.
Key_Actors: Load balancer; old cluster; new service machines; traffic.
Operational_Workflow: The new service is constructed on a separate set of machines alongside the existing service; the load balancer sheds a small percentage of traffic to the new service; the percentage is increased if the initial transfer succeeds; once all traffic is moved the old cluster is turned down.
Rules_and_Constraints: The old cluster must not be turned down until the entire process is complete; the technique requires double the capacity during the transition phase.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If problems are discovered the load can be transferred back to the old cluster; to manage high costs in large environments older machines can be recycled and redeployed as part of the new cluster once enough traffic has been diverted.

Concept_Name: blue-green deployment
Core_Definition: A deployment method using two environments on the same machine where one is live and the other remains dormant.
Key_Actors: Blue environment; green environment; machine; web server; virtual hosts.
Operational_Workflow: Two environments exist on a single machine as separate subdirectories or virtual hosts; the live environment handles traffic while the other remains dormant and consumes few resources; traffic is directed to the dormant environment to go live; the names or roles of the environments are then swapped.
Core_Definition: A deployment method using two environments on the same machine where one is live and the other remains dormant.
Rules_and_Constraints: Applications must support being installed in two different locations on the same machine; this technique provides zero-downtime deployments for applications not originally designed for it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This system permits rolling back to the previous environment to take place easily if the new release fails.

Concept_Name: blue environment
Core_Definition: The dormant environment in a blue-green deployment model that exists on the same machine as the live environment.
Key_Actors: Machines; subdirectories; virtual hosts; web servers.
Operational_Workflow: The blue environment remains dormant while the green environment serves live traffic; when a new software release is ready to go live, traffic is directed to the blue environment; once the transition is complete, the names of the blue and green environments are swapped.
Rules_and_Constraints: The blue environment must consume very little resources while it is in a dormant state; it must be able to exist on the same machine as the live environment; the application must support being installed in two different locations on the same machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If problems are discovered after traffic is moved to the blue environment, the system permits an easy rollback to the previous environment.

Concept_Name: green environment
Core_Definition: The environment that is currently live and serving traffic in a blue-green deployment setup.
Key_Actors: Machines; subdirectories; virtual hosts; web servers.
Operational_Workflow: The green environment functions as the active production environment while the blue environment is dormant; when an upgrade occurs, traffic is shifted away from the green environment to the blue environment; the environment names are then swapped to reflect the new status.
Rules_and_Constraints: The green environment exists on the same machine as the blue environment; it is often implemented as a virtual host of a web server using a specific subdirectory.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: zero-downtime deployments
Core_Definition: Deployment techniques that allow a service to be upgraded while it remains fully accessible to users.
Key_Actors: Blue-green deployment systems; Erlang programs; event-driven finite-state machines.
Operational_Workflow: One method involves directing traffic to a dormant blue environment and then swapping names with the live green environment; another method uses the Erlang language where a service receives a notification of a code upgrade and triggers a function to convert data structures while subsequent events use the new function versions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Blue-green deployment provides zero-downtime for applications not originally designed for it as long as they support multiple installations on the same machine; Erlang programs must be structured as event-driven finite-state machines to support this process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: No processes can survive a restart of the Erlang engine itself; upgrades must be carefully planned to ensure zero downtime is maintained throughout the data conversion and event handling process.

Concept_Name: toggling features
Core_Definition: The practice of linking new software features to configuration settings or flags so they can be individually turned on or off.
Key_Actors: Software flags; configuration settings; developers; users; centralized flag systems such as Google Chubby or Apache Zookeeper.
Operational_Workflow: Features are hidden behind flags that are initially disabled; code is deployed to the production environment with these flags off; when a feature is ready for use, the flag is flipped to on; specialized systems like Zookeeper can be used to notify thousands of servers when a flag changes without requiring a process restart.
Rules_and_Constraints: Feature toggling decouples the technical act of deployment from the business act of release; incomplete features must be hidden behind flags to enable rapid development and small code merges; flags can be implemented via command-line arguments, shell environment variables, or flag files.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Changing flags via command-line or environment variables requires a process restart which causes a service interruption; if a new feature is found to be ill-behaved or contains a bug, it can be disabled by flipping its flag back to the off position instead of performing a full software rollback.

Concept_Name: software flag
Core_Definition: A software flag is a toggle tied to an individual feature that allows it to be turned on or off independently of the rest of the software. It is a mechanism used to decouple the deployment of code from the actual release of a feature to users.
Key_Actors: Software developers; operations staff; services or applications; centralized configuration systems like Google's Chubby or Apache Zookeeper.
Operational_Workflow: A developer ties a new feature to a flag in the source code; the code is pushed into the production environment with the flag initially set to off; when the feature is ready for use, the flag is updated to the on position; the service identifies the flag status through command-line arguments, environment variables, configuration files, or notifications from a centralized system to determine whether to execute the feature logic.
Rules_and_Constraints: Flags can be implemented as command-line flags, shell environment variables, or settings in a file; using a centralized system to store and notify servers of flag changes is preferred to avoid service restarts; flag systems should support dozens or hundreds of flags at any given time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Changing flags via command-line, environment variables, or files requires a process restart, which interrupts service; if a feature is ill-behaved, the flag can be flipped back to the off position as a dynamic roll back.

Concept_Name: configuration setting
Core_Definition: A configuration setting is a parameter, often used interchangeably with a software flag, that controls the behavior of a service or enables specific features. It serves as a switch that can be adjusted to modify the application's functionality without changing the code.
Key_Actors: System administrators; developers; configuration management projects like Apache Zookeeper; paid and unpaid users who receive different settings.
Operational_Workflow: The setting is defined within the application's logic to govern a specific behavior or feature; the value is stored in a location accessible to the service such as a file or a specialized namespace in a configuration service; the service reads the setting at startup or receives dynamic updates while running; based on the value of the setting, the service provides different levels of access, such as differentiated services for paid versus unpaid users.
Rules_and_Constraints: Settings should be used to enable different services for different users; they can be used for statistical sampling by specifying a value for the sampling percentage; settings should be stored in a way that allows them to be updated at will to avoid service interruptions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: flag flipping
Core_Definition: Flag flipping is the action of toggling a software flag or configuration setting to change the state of a feature. It is a technique used to enable or disable features dynamically.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; product management; automated systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A feature is deployed into production with its toggle in a specific state; a decision is made to change the feature's availability; the flag is flipped via a command, file update, or centralized configuration change; the system immediately reflects the new state of the feature; if the change causes a problem, the flag is flipped back to the previous state to restore service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Flag flipping is used for rapid development, gradual introduction of new features, finely timed release dates, and dynamic roll backs; it is considered a safer and easier method for bug isolation than performing multiple individual software releases; flag flips can be controlled dynamically to occur at specific times.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the case of a bug like a memory leak, a binary search of flag flips can be used to identify which of many recently added features is creating the problem; if a new release has many features, flipping just one flag allows disabling a single bad feature without rolling back the entire release.

Concept_Name: hiding a feature behind a flag
Core_Definition: Hiding a feature behind a flag is the practice of deploying new or incomplete code with its associated software toggle set to the off position. This ensures the feature remains inactive and invisible to live users while still residing in the production environment.
Key_Actors: Developers; product management; beta users; employees.
Operational_Workflow: Developers merge small changes for a large feature into the main source branch; the feature is marked with a disabled flag; the code is pushed to production as part of regular deployment cycles; the feature remains dormant until it is ready for preview or full release; the flag may be enabled specifically for beta users or in a demo environment for product management review before a global launch.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique allows for dark launches where code is launched into production with no user-visible component; it enables the decoupling of technical deployment from business release; it is a best practice for managing large features that take a long time to develop to avoid difficult and risky code merges.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Sometimes a flag is used to disable the entire logic of a new feature, while at other times it may only obscure the visibility of the feature in the user interface.

Concept_Name: decoupling deployment and release
Core_Definition: The general principle of separating the technical act of putting a software package into an environment from the act of making a feature available to users.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; users; feature toggles; software flags.
Operational_Workflow: This principle is often implemented through feature toggling; code is deployed into the production environment with new features hidden behind flags that are initially disabled; deployment occurs as a continuous technical activity while the actual release of the feature to users is triggered separately on demand by flipping the flag.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment must be a purely technical activity that users are unaware of; release should happen on demand; incomplete or unready features must remain hidden behind flags until they are ready for use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a released feature causes issues, it can be disabled by toggling the flag back to the off position, effectively reversing the release without needing to undo the deployment.

Concept_Name: deployment
Core_Definition: The technical activity of putting a software package into an environment.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; automated testing and integration systems; production environments; noncritical environments such as beta or UAT environments.
Operational_Workflow: Code is moved into an environment using various techniques such as rolling upgrades, canary processes, or proportional shedding; in a continuous deployment model, every release that passes automated tests is pushed to production automatically; the process involves moving packages, automating approvals, and performing technical checks like verifying that code is not crashing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment is different from release and should be invisible to users; it requires continuous integration and continuous delivery; it should be based on data and science rather than human hunches; deployments should ideally occur during the day when staff are awake and available.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Deployment may be paused or blocked due to factors such as low build health, flakey tests, active production alerts, scheduled change freezes, or resource contention like low disk space or high CPU utilization.

Concept_Name: release
Core_Definition: The process of making a software feature or service available to users.
Key_Actors: Users; product management; beta testers; developers; global load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Release can be achieved through the technical deployment of a package or through the flipping of a software flag; it can be executed in phases by partitioning users into groups based on risk tolerance; releases can be precisely timed for announcement dates or refined through A-B testing and one percent testing to observe user behavior; in loosely coupled environments, each component can be released at its own velocity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Release is a business decision that happens on demand; only production-ready packages should be released; incompatible changes should be announced in advance and managed to prevent service failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a release fails, teams may roll back to the last known good release, roll forward to a new release containing a fix, or apply an emergency hotfix.

Concept_Name: Chubby
Core_Definition: A system used to store flags and efficiently notify thousands of servers when those flags change.
Key_Actors: Google; servers; flag and configuration systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system acts as a repository for software flags; when a flag is updated within the system, it automatically and efficiently notifies all connected servers of the change; this mechanism allows flags to be updated at will without requiring a service restart.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Apache Zookeeper
Core_Definition: A project system used to store software flags and efficiently notify thousands of servers when those flags change.
Key_Actors: The Apache Zookeeper project; thousands of servers; system flags.
Operational_Workflow: The system stores configuration flags in a central location; it provides a namespace that services use to find their configuration; when a flag is updated, the system efficiently notifies all connected servers of the change without requiring a process restart.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used as a more efficient alternative to command line flags, shell environment variables, or flag files which require process restarts to update.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Rapid Development
Core_Definition: A process where features are built using a series of small changes to a main source branch to minimize merge risks.
Key_Actors: Developers; main source branch; software flags.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create large features through small, incremental code changes; these small amounts of code are merged into the main line source frequently; flags are used to hide incomplete features and keep them disabled until they are ready for use.
Rules_and_Constraints: Small amounts of code should be merged to remain less error prone; the longer one waits to merge code changes into the main line source, the more difficult and risky the merge becomes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the source code is changed by other developers before a merge is completed, it creates a merge conflict.

Concept_Name: merge conflict
Core_Definition: A difficult and risky situation that occurs when source code has been changed by other developers, complicating the integration of new code into the main line.
Key_Actors: Developers; main line source code.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple developers work on the same source code simultaneously; a developer attempts to merge their changes into the main source branch; if significant time has passed and others have modified the same code, the changes conflict and create an integration risk.
Rules_and_Constraints: Merge conflicts are noted as a consequence of waiting too long to merge code changes into the main line source.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Gradual Introduction of New Features
Core_Definition: A strategy where specific features are introduced to certain segments of users, such as beta testers, rather than the entire population at once.
Key_Actors: Beta users; logged-in users; software flags.
Operational_Workflow: New features are deployed to production but controlled by toggles; whether a user has access to a feature is dynamically controlled by adjusting flags when they log in; individual features can be moved in and out of beta testing status as needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is used to avoid the high cost of setting up complete replicas of the server environment for different user groups; it allows for granularity that permits testing specific features rather than an all-or-nothing proposition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Finely Timed Release Dates
Core_Definition: A method of using flags controlled dynamically to flip at a specific time, allowing for precise control over when a new feature is visible to users.
Key_Actors: Systems with many servers; software flags or configuration settings.
Operational_Workflow: Rather than attempting to time a software deployment exactly to a scheduled announcement, the code is deployed in advance with the feature disabled; at the specific release time, such as noon on Tuesday, the flags are flipped dynamically to enable the feature across all servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Timing a flag flip is easier than timing a software deployment, especially when dealing with a large number of servers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dynamic Roll Backs
Core_Definition: A technique to disable an ill-behaved new feature by turning off its specific software flag rather than reverting the entire system to an older release.
Key_Actors: Software flags; specific new features; software releases.
Operational_Workflow: When a new software release contains multiple new features and one of them is found to be problematic, the operator disables the individual flag for that feature; this leaves the rest of the new release intact while mitigating the specific problem.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is considered easier to disable a flag than to perform a full rollback to an older software version; this avoids the need to lose all new features due to one bad feature.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Bug Isolation
Core_Definition: The use of software toggles to identify which specific change or feature in a release is responsible for a problem like a memory leak.
Key_Actors: Toggled features; test environment; production environment.
Operational_Workflow: Each new change is associated with a flag; if a bug is detected, the flags are toggled to determine which feature is creating the problem; this process often utilizes a binary search to find the culprit efficiently.
Rules_and_Constraints: Associating each change with a flag helps isolate bugs; using flags for bug detection in production is considered more sane than performing many individual software releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a binary search fails to isolate the problem in a test environment, the process can be performed in production via flags.

Concept_Name: binary search
Core_Definition: A search method used in conjunction with feature toggles to pinpoint which specific feature among a group of new additions is causing a technical issue.
Key_Actors: Recently added features; software toggles.
Operational_Workflow: When a problem such as a memory leak appears in a set of new features, each attached to a toggle, the operator uses a binary search pattern to toggle features on or off until the problematic feature is identified.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: A-B Testing
Core_Definition: A method used to determine if users prefer one design over another by implementing both versions and observing the behavior of different user groups.
Key_Actors: Users; developers; flag flip systems.
Operational_Workflow: Two different designs or mechanisms are implemented for a single feature; users are selected and divided into two groups where Group A sees one design and Group B sees the other; user behavior and results are observed and compared; whichever design yields better results is selected for all users; flag flips are used to control the test and eventually enable the winning design.
Rules_and_Constraints: Flag flips can be used to both control the experiment and deploy the final version of the feature.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: One Percent Testing
Core_Definition: A process that exposes a new or experimental feature to a small statistical sample of users to gather information or test readiness.
Key_Actors: Users; developers; the service being tested.
Operational_Workflow: A feature is released to a limited statistical sample of the user base; the reaction to the experimental feature is identified or performance data is collected; the results are used to decide whether the feature should be deployed globally or at all.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process can be used to test new features before global deployment; identify reactions to experimental features; or collect information via statistical sampling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: statistical sampling
Core_Definition: A technique used to gather data from a limited portion of total traffic to prevent overloading a system with monitoring requests.
Key_Actors: The service; JavaScript code; monitoring systems; flag systems.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring code such as JavaScript is inserted into a service to collect data; a flag is set to a specific value representing a sampling percentage; the system only executes the data collection for the specified fraction of requests; the collected data is transmitted back to the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is used when collecting data from every query would double the queries reaching a service and overload the system; flags can be used to specify the sampling percentage or disable it entirely.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The flag can be set to 0 to disable collection entirely.

Concept_Name: Differentiated Services
Core_Definition: The practice of enabling different features or service levels for different categories of users through the use of flags.
Key_Actors: Paid customers; unpaid users; flag systems; membership levels.
Operational_Workflow: Users are assigned to different categories such as membership levels; a flag system is used to associate specific sets of flags with each membership level; the system enables different features for paid customers compared to unpaid users based on these flags.
Rules_and_Constraints: A good flag system is required to manage the different features seen by various user groups.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: dark launch
Core_Definition: The practice of launching code into production that has no user-visible component.
Key_Actors: Facebook; Chuck Rossi; developers; users; specific media outlets such as TechCrunch.
Operational_Workflow: Code for major upcoming features is deployed to the production environment months before it is intended to be revealed; the code remains active but hidden from users; a management tool controls the specific timing and audience for the eventual feature reveal.
Rules_and_Constraints: It allows for the decoupling of deployment and release; it requires a mechanism for fine-grained control over feature visibility; visibility can be filtered based on user attributes like country, age, or datacenter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Specific groups of users, such as employees of known media organizations, may be intentionally excluded from seeing features during or after a dark launch.

Concept_Name: Gatekeeper
Core_Definition: An in-house tool used by Facebook to manage dark launches and control feature visibility.
Key_Actors: Facebook; Chuck Rossi; users; excluded media employees.
Operational_Workflow: The tool manages code that has been launched into production without user-visible components; it provides fine-grained control over which features are revealed to which users; it applies various filters to determine the audience for any given feature.
Rules_and_Constraints: Access can be restricted using filters for country, age, and datacenter; it supports the strategy of maintaining code for major launches in production well in advance of their public release.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Live Schema Changes
Core_Definition: The process of modifying a database schema to support new software releases without incurring service downtime.
Key_Actors: Web server frontends or replicas; database servers; software versions; Stephen McHenry.
Operational_Workflow: The process follows the McHenry Technique or expand/contract method; in Phase 1 the code reads and writes the original schema; in Phase 2 the schema is expanded by adding new fields without removing old ones; in Phase 3 the code is updated to use new fields and data conversion is performed while live; in Phase 4 the code referencing old fields is removed; in Phase 5 the old unused fields are removed from the database schema.
Rules_and_Constraints: Downtime is generally considered unacceptable in web environments; software must be written to ignore new or unexpected fields; SQL queries should explicitly name required fields rather than using SELECT *; changes should ideally be spread over two software releases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Roll backs are painless during Phase 2 because new fields are not yet in use; changing a schema during a standard rolling upgrade causes chaos as working and failing replicas swap roles; older software will fail if the database schema is modified before the software is updated.

Concept_Name: database views
Core_Definition: Database abstractions that provide different interfaces to the same data to decouple software upgrades from schema changes.
Key_Actors: Relational database systems; software versions; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A unique view is created for each specific software version; when the underlying schema is changed the code for each view is modified to maintain the expected abstraction for the software; the schema change and view code update occur atomically; views can convert data between old and new formats to ensure compatibility.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique allows for zero-downtime deployments by ensuring different software versions see the database as they expect; it requires a database system that supports the views feature.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This technique is not used frequently because views are a rare feature on relational databases; as of the text's writing no NoSQL or key-value store systems support views.

Concept_Name: Key-Value store (NoSQL) systems
Core_Definition: A type of database system that as of the chapter's writing does not support database views for decoupling software upgrades from schema changes.
Key_Actors: Web server frontends or replicas; database servers; software applications.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple web server replicas talk to the same database server; software accessing these databases tends to request specific fields by convention; during upgrades involving schema changes, software must be written to ignore new fields until it is specifically updated to use them.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems do not support database views; any software using SQL-like queries should avoid SELECT * if it makes assumptions about the number or position of fields to remain robust.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the software and database schema are not updated in a coordinated fashion, the replicas may malfunction or crash because they cannot understand the modified schema.

Concept_Name: McHenry Technique
Core_Definition: A method for performing live database schema changes over a period of two software releases to avoid service downtime.
Key_Actors: Stephen McHenry; software developers; database administrators; web server replicas; batch jobs.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the original state where code uses the old schema; the schema is expanded by adding new fields without removing old ones; the software is modified to use new schema fields and pushed to production; data conversion is performed while the system is live; the software is modified again to remove references to old unused fields and pushed; finally the old fields are removed from the database schema.
Rules_and_Constraints: No code changes should be made during the initial schema expansion phase; the software must be written to handle empty fields or ignore unexpected fields during the transition; roll backs are possible at each phase by reverting to the previous state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the unlikely event that a roll back is needed after the final phase, the database would simply revert to the previous phase where the code still supported the old fields.

Concept_Name: expand/contract
Core_Definition: A technique for live schema changes that involves adding new fields to a database before eventually removing obsolete ones across multiple software releases.
Key_Actors: Database schema; software developers; web server replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The schema is first modified by adding new fields while retaining old ones; software is updated to use the new fields while the system remains live; once the old fields are no longer referenced by any running code, those obsolete fields are removed from the schema.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique requires the schema change to be spread across at least two software releases; one release adds the use of new fields and a subsequent release removes the use of old fields.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a roll back is needed during the expand phase, it is considered painless because the new fields are not yet being used by the production code.

Concept_Name: legacy code
Core_Definition: Software code that references old or unused database fields and is slated for removal during the final stages of a schema upgrade.
Key_Actors: Software developers; software releases; database schema.
Operational_Workflow: After new database fields have been added and the software has been updated to use them, a new release is pushed to specifically remove the legacy code that references the old fields; once this release is deployed, the obsolete database fields can be safely deleted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Legacy code must be removed and the new release pushed into production before the old fields can be removed from the database schema; software must be written to handle the transition from legacy fields to new fields, such as handling empty values.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In some cases, a batch job may be required to convert legacy data to the new format before the legacy code can be fully removed.

Concept_Name: batch job
Core_Definition: A process used during the transition phase of a live database schema change to migrate legacy data to a new format.
Key_Actors: Database management systems; user profile data; conversion software or scripts.
Operational_Workflow: The job is run during Phase 3 of a schema change after a software release that can handle both old and new fields has been deployed; the job identifies data entries still utilizing the old schema fields; it converts these entries to the new field formats while the system remains live to prepare for the eventual removal of old fields.
Rules_and_Constraints: The job should be executed after the software is capable of writing to the new fields and marking old fields as unused; it is used to ensure all legacy data is converted before code referencing the old fields is removed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SELECT *
Core_Definition: A SQL query command used to retrieve all columns of data from a specified table.
Key_Actors: SQL database engines; application software; NoSQL databases following similar conventions.
Operational_Workflow: Application code executes this command to request data from a database; during a schema expansion where new fields have been added but not yet utilized, the database returns these additional fields to the software; the software must process the result set despite the presence of unexpected data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software should be written to ignore unexpected fields and must not make assumptions about the number or position of fields in the received data; following these constraints is considered a good practice that makes code more robust.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If software is not designed to ignore unexpected fields, the addition of new fields to the database schema during Phase 2 of a live upgrade may cause the software to break or malfunction.

Concept_Name: Live Code Changes
Core_Definition: A technique that allows for the deployment of new software releases by modifying a system while it is currently running.
Key_Actors: Specialized programming languages like Erlang; event-driven finite-state machines.
Operational_Workflow: The running service receives a notification that its code has been upgraded; a specific function is triggered to convert or upgrade any existing data structures; all events received by the system after this point trigger the new versions of the functions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Programs must be properly structured as event-driven finite-state machines; upgrades must be carefully planned to ensure zero downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While the code can be updated live, no processes within the system survive if the underlying engine itself is restarted.

Concept_Name: Erlang
Core_Definition: A programming language designed to support the upgrading of services while they are running with zero downtime.
Key_Actors: Erlang engine; event-driven finite-state machines; data conversion functions.
Operational_Workflow: A service written in this language is structured as an event-driven finite-state machine; the service receives a notification event indicating that the code has been upgraded; a function is called to convert data structures; all subsequent events trigger the updated versions of the functions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must be properly structured as event-driven finite-state machines to utilize the live upgrade capabilities of the language.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Processes running within the system do not survive if the Erlang engine itself is restarted.

Concept_Name: event-driven finite-state machines (FSM)
Core_Definition: A software architecture where a program is structured to call a specific function for every event it receives.
Key_Actors: Erlang programs; the Erlang engine.
Operational_Workflow: The service receives an event notifying it that code has been upgraded; a specific function is called that is responsible for upgrading or converting data structures; all events received after this point trigger the new versions of the functions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Programs must be properly structured to support this type of live upgrading; processes within the service do not survive a restart of the Erlang engine itself.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: OTP
Core_Definition: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Key_Actors: Erlang language.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
