Concept_Name: Continuous Deployment
Core_Definition: A practice where every software release that passes automated tests is automatically deployed to the production environment.
Key_Actors: Development teams; automated testing systems; monitoring systems; oncall engineers; business stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Software passes through continuous integration and continuous delivery; automated systems verify build health, test reproducibility, and production health; the system checks for schedule permissions and resource availability; the release is automatically pushed to production; the push is allowed to soak to ensure stability before subsequent pushes occur.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process requires continuous integration and continuous delivery as prerequisites; it should be the goal for most organizations unless restricted by external regulations; operations must be based on data and science rather than human hunches; automated checks must be executed consistently for every push; specific subsystems like APIs may use it while others like web UIs remain manual.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Pushes are automatically blocked if recent build failure rates are high or if test reproducibility is low; deployments are paused during change freezes, holidays, or periods of high CPU and low disk space; a manual stop button is available for designated personnel to halt production if a defect is found; pushes may be paused during sleeping hours to avoid disturbing oncall engineers.

Concept_Name: continuous integration
Core_Definition: A development process that serves as the foundation for continuous delivery and continuous deployment.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated integration systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers merge small amounts of code into the main line source branch frequently to prevent difficult merge conflicts; this frequent integration provides the starting point for the automated delivery and deployment pipeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: Continuous deployment and delivery are built upon this process; code should be merged in small batches to reduce risk and error.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: continuous delivery
Core_Definition: A process that builds on continuous integration to produce software packages that are production ready. It ensures that code is always in a state where it could be deployed, though the actual move to production remains a business decision.
Key_Actors: Automated testing systems; approval processes; business decision makers.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts with continuous integration and proceeds through automated testing and approval workflows; it results in the creation of a production-ready package; a business decision is then made to determine if and when the package is actually deployed into the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Continuous delivery is a prerequisite for continuous deployment; it results in packages that are production ready; the decision to deploy is separate from the technical readiness of the package.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Continuous delivery may be paused or gated by various factors such as build health, low test coverage, flakey tests, or production health issues like outstanding monitoring alerts.

Concept_Name: UAT environments
Core_Definition: Noncritical environments used as a staging area for testing software before it reaches full production.
Key_Actors: Automated deployment systems; developers; testers.
Operational_Workflow: Within a continuous deployment pipeline, releases that pass initial tests are automatically deployed to these noncritical environments; this allows for verification in a setting that mirrors production without risking service to live users.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pushing to UAT environments is used as a precaution to build confidence in a release before it is moved to critical production systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: service-oriented architectures
Core_Definition: An architectural approach where a system is composed of many loosely coupled subservices or subsystems, each with its own release schedule and velocity.
Key_Actors: Subsystems; APIs; backend services; web UI subsystems; service owners.
Operational_Workflow: Each service or subservice is developed and tested independently; continuous deployment may be applied to high-confidence subsystems like APIs and backends while other components like web UIs undergo manual testing; changes are pushed at the velocity of the individual component rather than waiting for the entire system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Components should be loosely coupled to allow for independent testing and problem isolation; incompatible API changes must be announced long in advance; tools should be used to detect and warn service owners about soon-to-be deprecated features.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If components become tightly coupled, they must be tested and deployed as a set or tuple, which reduces the rate of change because all components must move in lock-step.

Concept_Name: oncall engineer
Core_Definition: A technical role responsible for monitoring the health of a service and responding to issues that arise during operations or code pushes.
Key_Actors: Individual engineers; primary location teams; teams located in different time zones; oncall teams.
Operational_Workflow: Oncall engineers are available to handle problems if something goes wrong during a deployment; automated deployment systems may check the oncall schedule to pause pushes during typical sleeping hours to avoid waking the engineer; multiple teams may use a follow-the-sun arrangement to ensure someone is always awake and available.
Rules_and_Constraints: The best time to do a code push is during the day when the oncall engineer is wide awake and co-workers are available; critical upgrades should not be performed while half-asleep; the oncall schedule can be used as an automated factor to decide whether to pause continuous delivery.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In some organizations, code pushes are done late at night to decrease exposure, though this is noted as a higher risk due to engineer fatigue; SREs or oncall staff may have to scurry to fix tools or incompatibilities if a release breaks production-specific automation.

Concept_Name: Build Health
Core_Definition: A measure of the recent stability of software builds where the frequency of failures serves as an indicator of potential stability issues.
Key_Actors: Developers; continuous delivery systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors the outcomes of recent software builds; it calculates the number of failures within a specific window of recent build attempts; the automated push process is blocked if the number of failures reaches a predefined threshold.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automatic pushes can be blocked if n of the last m builds failed; build health serves as a proxy for measuring developer stress or rushed work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Test Comprehensiveness
Core_Definition: A metric or standard used to ensure that code has been sufficiently tested before it is allowed to be automatically pushed to production.
Key_Actors: Testing systems; automated deployment pipelines.
Operational_Workflow: The code undergoes testing as part of the continuous delivery pipeline; the system evaluates the depth and extent of the testing performed; the automated push process only proceeds if the code is determined to be highly tested.
Rules_and_Constraints: Only highly tested code should be automatically pushed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: percentage of code coverage
Core_Definition: A specific measurement used to indicate the portion of the software code that is exercised by automated tests.
Key_Actors: Automated testing tools; continuous delivery systems.
Operational_Workflow: Testing tools analyze the code during the execution of the test suite; the system generates a percentage representing how much of the code was covered; this metric is monitored to ensure it meets established standards before an automated push.
Rules_and_Constraints: This metric is used as an indicator that testing has become lax; it is used as a factor to determine if a release is suitable for continuous deployment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Test Reproducibility
Core_Definition: The consistency of test results when run multiple times, ensuring that tests do not pass or fail randomly.
Key_Actors: Testing systems; automated deployment pipelines.
Operational_Workflow: Tests are executed multiple times to ensure consistent results; the tests are run in a random order to detect inconsistencies; the system identifies flakey tests that fail to produce reproducible outcomes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Delivery can be paused automatically if test reproducibility is low.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Flakey tests
Core_Definition: Tests that sometimes pass and sometimes fail during the evaluation process.
Key_Actors: Continuous delivery systems; automated testing frameworks.
Operational_Workflow: Flakey tests are identified by running the test suite multiple times and in random order to determine if results are consistent. If consistency is low, the continuous delivery process is paused automatically.
Rules_and_Constraints: Test reproducibility must be maintained to avoid pausing delivery.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Production Health
Core_Definition: A measurable state used as a proxy indicator to determine if a system is stable enough to receive an automated code push.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; continuous deployment gates.
Operational_Workflow: The deployment system checks for the presence of outstanding alerts in the monitoring system. If no alerts are found, the system is considered healthy enough for the deployment to proceed.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text specifies that there is no single test that indicates a system is healthy, so the absence of monitoring alerts is used as a standard proxy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: monitoring system
Core_Definition: A system that tracks the status of production environments and identifies active issues.
Key_Actors: Automated deployment systems; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system continuously observes the production environment and generates alerts when issues occur. In the context of continuous deployment, the deployment system queries the monitoring system for active alerts as a prerequisite for pushing new code.
Rules_and_Constraints: The status of the monitoring system is used as a data-driven proxy for production health.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: outstanding alerts
Core_Definition: Active notifications or warnings from a monitoring system indicating that there are current problems within the production environment.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; continuous delivery automation.
Operational_Workflow: Before an automated code push begins, the system checks for any active alerts. If outstanding alerts are present, they serve as a signal to block or pause the automated deployment until the system is clear.
Rules_and_Constraints: Having no outstanding alerts is the specific condition used to validate production health.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Schedule Permission
Core_Definition: A factor used to decide whether to pause continuous delivery based on pre-set dates or events such as holidays or financial cycles.
Key_Actors: Finance department; key people on vacation; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: A simple list of dates is maintained to identify when pushes should be paused; the deployment system checks this list before executing an automated push; if a conflict with a listed date is found, the deployment is forbidden and paused.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automated pushes are forbidden during a scheduled change freeze for holidays, quarterly financial reporting, or on days when too many key people are on vacation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: change freeze
Core_Definition: A scheduled period during which automated pushes are prohibited to minimize risk during critical business times or holidays.
Key_Actors: Finance department; key personnel; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: Dates for the freeze are added to a simple list that gates automated deployments; the system refers to this list prior to starting a code push; if the current date falls within a freeze period, the push is automatically paused.
Rules_and_Constraints: Change freezes are typically scheduled for holidays, quarterly financial reporting periods, or times when staffing is insufficient to handle potential outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Oncall Schedule
Core_Definition: A deployment constraint that pauses automated pushes during the typical sleeping hours of the responsible engineers.
Key_Actors: Oncall engineers; multiple oncall teams; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system evaluates the current time relative to the oncall team's waking hours; pushes are paused if they would occur while the oncall engineer is asleep; the system may adjust for multiple teams across different time zones or follow-the-sun arrangements.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pushes should be paused during typical sleeping hours to avoid dragging the current oncall engineer out of bed; different teams may have different ideas of waking hours based on their geographic location.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Manual Stop
Core_Definition: A mechanism that allows authorized individuals to immediately halt automated pushes with the click of a button.
Key_Actors: Authorized individuals on a specific list; assembly-line workers used as a conceptual model.
Operational_Workflow: A list of people authorized to halt production is maintained; when a defect is found or a pause is desired, an authorized person triggers the stop; the automated push process is immediately suspended.
Rules_and_Constraints: The manual stop is akin to an assembly line where anyone can halt production if a defect is found; the reason for the manual pause does not have to be an emergency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Push Conflicts
Core_Definition: A situation where multiple subservices within a larger service have overlapping or independent release schedules that may interfere with one another.
Key_Actors: Subservices; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: The deployment system evaluates the release schedules of individual subservices; it monitors active deployments to ensure only one subservice is being pushed at a time; the system prevents a new push from starting until the current push has successfully finished.
Rules_and_Constraints: Only one subservice deployment should be permitted at a time; a subsequent push must not be initiated if the current one is still in progress.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Intentional Delays
Core_Definition: A scheduled pause introduced between code pushes to allow a recently deployed change to be verified for stability.
Key_Actors: Automated deployment systems; production environments.
Operational_Workflow: A code push is completed in the environment; the system initiates a programmed pause; the environment is monitored during this period; the deployment of the next release is held until the delay period has passed to ensure the previous change did not introduce instability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Delays should be used to provide enough time to verify that a push is stable; pushes should not be performed too rapidly to avoid difficulty in isolating when a problem began.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: soak
Core_Definition: The process of running a newly deployed release for a sufficient duration to confirm its stability before proceeding with further updates.
Key_Actors: Software releases; monitoring systems; automated deployment pipelines.
Operational_Workflow: A new version of the code is pushed to production; the system enters a waiting phase where the code runs under live conditions; monitoring data is collected to ensure the service remains stable; once the stability is verified over time, the deployment is considered successful and the system may proceed to the next task.
Rules_and_Constraints: The release must run long enough to verify stability; soaking is intended to prevent the confusion caused by doing pushes too rapidly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Resource Contention
Core_Definition: A state in which system resources are too low or utilized too heavily to safely permit a code push.
Key_Actors: CPU; disk space; service replicas; monitoring and deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: The deployment system checks the current health and capacity of the infrastructure; it identifies if disk space is low or if there is unusually high CPU utilization; it verifies if the number of available replicas provides sufficient redundancy; if the system is currently flooded with load or lacks redundancy, the push is paused.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pushes should be paused if disk space is low or CPU utilization is high; the load must be below a particular threshold before a push occurs; sufficient redundancy must exist, such as having N plus 2 replicas available.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: N + 2
Core_Definition: A specific level of redundancy required for service replicas before an automated code push can safely proceed.
Key_Actors: Service replicas; automated deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: The automated deployment system checks the current status of the production environment; it evaluates if the number of available and healthy service replicas meets the N + 2 threshold; if the threshold is met the push continues; if the threshold is not met the push is paused to avoid resource contention.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automated pushes should be paused if redundancy is insufficient; the text explicitly states not to push if replicas are not N + 2.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: regression
Core_Definition: A defect where a new software release performs worse than a previous version, such as by requiring significantly more RAM or CPU.
Key_Actors: Automated testing systems; development teams; monitoring systems; oncall engineers.
Operational_Workflow: New code is developed and run through testing or pushed to production; monitoring systems or automated tests detect performance issues or resource leaks; the data is compared against previous release metrics to identify a decline in quality; the automated deployment system may pause or alert based on these findings.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automated tests are considered as good as or better than humans at catching regressions; continuous deployment should not ignore warnings regarding resource usage regressions; performance regressions are particularly devastating for tightly coupled components like load balancer software and the OS kernel.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: People often miss regressions even when automated systems detect them and provide warnings; some performance regressions only become evident in highly demanding production environments.

Concept_Name: hard failure
Core_Definition: A catastrophic scenario where a software release refuses to start or fails immediately after starting.
Key_Actors: Software releases; service replicas; canary process; testing environments.
Operational_Workflow: A new code release is pushed to a production environment; the software crashes or fails to initialize upon startup; the canary process ideally identifies this failure on a single replica; the failing replica is taken out of production to prevent user impact.
Rules_and_Constraints: A hard failure should ideally be caught in a test environment rather than production; if a hard failure occurs in production the canarying process should be used to detect it and stop the rollout.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not all services are replicated in a way that makes canarying possible; if a hard failure is not detected by a canary it may require a roll back or an emergency hotfix.

Concept_Name: roll forward
Core_Definition: A method of addressing a failed code push by deploying a subsequent release that contains a fix rather than reverting to an older version.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; development teams; automated deployment systems; service replicas.
Operational_Workflow: A failure or bug is detected in the current production release; developers produce a new release that specifically fixes the identified problem; this new release is pushed through the deployment pipeline to replace the faulty release; the service continues running on the new fixed version.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique requires reducing code lead time until it is short enough to make the fix viable; the failed release must be resilient enough to remain usable or have workarounds until the next release is ready; it works best when servers are highly replicated and canarying is used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Roll forward is not possible if the next release is hours or days away or if the failure is too catastrophic; in such cases teams may be forced to use a roll back as a last resort.

Concept_Name: SDP code lead time
Core_Definition: The duration of time required to develop, test, and deploy a new software release.
Key_Actors: Development and operations teams responsible for the software delivery pipeline.
Operational_Workflow: Teams monitor this lead time and work to reduce it until it is short enough to make rolling forward to a new fix a viable alternative to rolling back to a previous version.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be short enough to ensure that a fix for a failed release can be deployed within an acceptable timeframe.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: emergency hotfix
Core_Definition: A small, targeted software change pushed into production to address a specific, insurmountable problem.
Key_Actors: Development and operations staff who identify issues and deploy the fix.
Operational_Workflow: When a code push fails and other methods are not viable, a small change is pushed to fix the specific problem; the hotfix is tracked carefully; a subsequent project is started to eliminate the root cause of the issue.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is considered risky because it typically does not receive full testing; it should be used only as an expedient measure when roll forward or roll back is not possible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Release Atomicity
Core_Definition: The concept of whether a software release consists of an entire system of components updated together or individual specific components.
Key_Actors: Tightly coupled subsystems like virtualization management, storage systems, and OS kernels.
Operational_Workflow: Different component versions are grouped into a tuple; the entire tuple is tested, approved, and deployed as a set; if any part fails, the whole set is improved and retested from scratch; roll backs return the system to the previous approved tuple.
Rules_and_Constraints: Required for tightly coupled systems where individual component changes might cause devastating regressions; only approved version combinations should be used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach can reduce the overall rate of change because a delay in one component release causes all other ready components to wait.

Concept_Name: loosely coupled systems
Core_Definition: A system architecture consisting of multiple subsystems that maintain independent versioning and can be upgraded separately.
Key_Actors: Small, independent services and the owners who manage their specific release schedules.
Operational_Workflow: Each service is tested independently and pushed at its own velocity; incompatible changes are announced in advance; automated tools are used to detect and warn about the use of deprecated APIs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Requires a focus on upward compatibility; problems are isolated to the specific changed component or the incompatibility it introduced.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In these systems, it is often impossible or a waste of resources to test all possible combinations of all component versions.

Concept_Name: tightly coupled
Core_Definition: A system architecture where multiple subsystems are highly dependent on specific versions of each other and must be upgraded in lock-step.
Key_Actors: Software components such as Service A and Service B; load balancer software and its plug-ins; the operating system kernel; virtualization management systems such as Ganeti or OpenStack.
Operational_Workflow: A particular combination of component versions is identified; the group is tested as a set; the set is approved and then deployed as a unit; if a roll back is required the system reverts to the previous approved set.
Rules_and_Constraints: Components must move in lock-step; testing must verify specific combinations of versions; only approved combinations should be used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: One delayed release for a single component will cause all other ready components to wait which reduces the potential rate of change.

Concept_Name: tuple
Core_Definition: A specific combination of different component versions that are tested, approved, and deployed as a single set.
Key_Actors: Independent software services or subsystems such as Service A, Service B, and Service D.
Operational_Workflow: Versions of different services are grouped together; the combination is tested from scratch; if the test is successful the set is approved for deployment; if a test fails one or more components are improved and a new combination is formed for testing.
Rules_and_Constraints: Only combinations that have been specifically approved are used; a roll back must return the system to the previous approved version set.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: upward compatibility
Core_Definition: The requirement for services in a loosely coupled system to remain functional with other components as they undergo independent upgrades.
Key_Actors: Loosely coupled services; service owners; infrastructure ecosystems like Google.
Operational_Workflow: Each service is tested independently and pushed at its own velocity; service owners take responsibility for maintaining compatibility within the ecosystem; incompatible changes are announced long in advance to allow others to adapt.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service owners must take upward compatibility seriously because the entire infrastructure cannot be stopped for individual system testing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Incompatible changes are managed via long-term announcements and specific tools that detect and track affected users.

Concept_Name: deprecated
Core_Definition: Features or API uses that are identified as being slated for future removal or incompatible change.
Key_Actors: Service owners; API users; tracking and detection tools.
Operational_Workflow: When an API is scheduled to change in an incompatible way tools are used to detect current uses that will soon be unsupported; the system warns the service owners who are affected by these upcoming changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Incompatible changes regarding deprecated features must be announced long in advance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

