Chapter 12. Automation
Section: Chapter 12. Automation
============================================================

Concept_Name: Automation
Core_Definition: Automation is when computers do work for us with the motivation of achieving more precision, more stability, and more speed.
Key_Actors: Computers; system administrators; software developers; software delivery platforms; robots; tools; consultants.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by performing a task manually and documenting every step; the documentation is followed and corrected for clarity; prototyping follows by creating tools for individual steps in isolation; individual tools are combined into a single program featuring logging and sanity checking; conditions that normally trigger a human to act are identified to instead trigger the software; finally the role of the human changes from performing the task to maintaining the automation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automating work should account for the majority of a system administrator's job; changes should be made to code and data files in a revision-controlled repository rather than directly to the running system; automation should be viewed as a human-computer system rather than just functional replacements for human tasks; not all tasks should be automated such as rare or extremely complex one-time installations; automation must include complete logging and robust error handling; automation and configuration files must be kept under revision control; tools and automation should be developed in a dedicated environment and staged for testing before being pushed to production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Most systems experience exceptions that cannot be handled by automation and require human intervention; if humans have no insight into how the automation works they cannot handle failures appropriately; automation can have unintended negative consequences if human factors are not considered; automation can lead to operators becoming less skilled which makes emergency response brittle; some rare or difficult tasks are better outsourced to consultants rather than automated.

Concept_Name: Software delivery platform
Core_Definition: A platform that picks up changes from a revision-controlled central repository to test and deploy them to production.
Key_Actors: Revision-controlled central repository; testing systems; deployment systems; system administrators; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: Changes are made to code and data files in a central repository; the software delivery platform identifies and picks up these changes; the platform runs tests on the changes; the platform deploys the changes to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: The platform must include continuous test, build, and deploy capabilities; it should be a centralized and shared service used by everyone involved in software development; automation and configuration files should go through development, beta, and production phases using the platform.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: System administrators
Core_Definition: Professionals whose role involves instructing automation to make changes to systems and maintaining that automation to ensure system scale and reliability.
Key_Actors: System administrators; operations staff; junior staff; consultants; management; users.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators write code and scripts to configure machines and create accounts; they document manual processes to prepare them for automation; they build tools and combined automated systems for tasks like hardware repair; they manage their time to prioritize automation over manual tasks; they conduct code reviews and follow style guides; they use issue tracking systems to manage bugs and feature requests.
Rules_and_Constraints: System administrators must be software developers because system administration can scale only through automation; they should spend less than half their time on manual operational work; they should not make changes to systems directly but through automation; they must keep all configuration files under revision control; they should prioritize fixing the biggest bottlenecks first; they should use test-driven development to maximize confidence in their code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: System administrators must handle exceptions that automation cannot; they may face a toil emergency if manual work becomes a huge burden requiring a pause in projects to restore balance; they may become less skilled in manual maintenance due to high levels of automation; they can experience mental fatigue from only tackling the most difficult problems that cannot be automated.

Concept_Name: Software developers
Core_Definition: Individuals who write code and data files to create automation and system features, a role that modern system administrators have integrated into their own.
Key_Actors: System administrators; programmers; automated build systems; users.
Operational_Workflow: Developers write code and data files in a revision-controlled repository; they use test-driven development by writing tests before functional code; they receive immediate feedback from automated tests during the build process; they participate in code reviews to find errors and share knowledge; they use style guides to ensure code remains maintainable by the team.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scripting does not require heavy computer science or formal training but involves learning more software development skills over time; developers should write just enough code to satisfy requirements without excessive future-proofing; code must conform to formatting and language feature standards in a style guide; all changes must be reviewed by at least one other person before being committed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Developers may fall into the trap of writing unused code or features which creates maintenance burdens and introduces more bugs.

Concept_Name: Domain-Specific Language (DSL)
Core_Definition: A language that was purpose-built for a particular use, such as system administration, mathematics, or text manipulation.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management systems such as CFEngine and Puppet.
Operational_Workflow: A system administrator writes declarative code that describes the desired state of a system. The configuration management system then interprets this code to determine which changes are necessary to achieve that state, leveraging the system's internal knowledge to handle implementation details. This allows administrators to build up libraries of definitions where changes to a base server definition automatically propagate to all inherited server classes.
Rules_and_Constraints: DSLs in configuration management are typically declarative rather than imperative, meaning they specify the end goal rather than the individual steps to reach it. They allow administrators to define things concisely at a high level and enshrine best practices in shared definitions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The primary disadvantages of using a DSL include a steep learning curve and the requirement to initially create all necessary definitions manually.

Concept_Name: Toil
Core_Definition: Toil is defined as exhausting physical labor and represents the extreme opposite of the goal of automation.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; system administrators; management; Google SRE teams.
Operational_Workflow: Toil typically builds up within an operations team starting with small, manual tasks that repeat over time until they become a huge burden. When toil becomes excessive, the team must review how time is spent, identify root cause problems, and establish projects to eliminate the manual work. Organizations may use an official process to declare a toil emergency to pause other projects and focus on restoring balance.
Rules_and_Constraints: A small amount of toil is considered normal, but a red flag is raised if more than half of a team's collective time is spent on operational toil. Management should reprioritize work to emphasize automation and may hire temporary staff or consultants to free up senior people for automation projects.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If everything is perfectly automated with no toil, it may indicate that the rate of innovation and change has been stifled. Excessive toil leads to team burn-out and a lack of morale.

Concept_Name: Left-over principle
Core_Definition: An approach to automation design where as much as possible is automated within reason, with people expected to handle whatever situations are too rare or complicated for the automation.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; automation designers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Designers classify tasks along two axes ranging from rare to frequent and easy to difficult. Rare and easy tasks remain manual; rare and difficult tasks are documented; frequent and easy tasks are automated; and frequent and difficult tasks are automated by acquiring existing software. The automation handles the bulk of the work, and the human component handles the remaining exceptions.
Rules_and_Constraints: This principle assumes that people are infinitely versatile and adaptable with no capability limitations. It aims for efficiency by automating all feasible tasks without explicitly considering the human component of the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If operators have no insight into how the automation works, it is harder for them to handle failures. This approach can also cause mental fatigue because simple tasks are eliminated, leaving only the most difficult problems for humans to solve on a continual basis.

Concept_Name: Compensatory principle
Core_Definition: An approach to automation design where the work is divided between people and automation based on which one is better at which task.
Key_Actors: Humans, viewed as information processing systems; machines and automated systems; Fitts, the creator of Fitts's list.
Operational_Workflow: Designers use Fitts's list of attributes to compare human and machine capabilities when deciding what to automate. For example, a machine is assigned to collect monitoring data at five-minute intervals from thousands of machines, while humans are assigned to tasks involving unpredictable environments or judgment. The process aims to optimize human-computer interactions to make the overall system more efficient.
Rules_and_Constraints: This principle is based on the assumption that the capabilities of people and machines are reasonably static. The division of labor aims to avoid putting excessive demands on people by assigning tasks based on identifiable separate strengths.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Complementarity principle
Core_Definition: An approach to automation design that evaluates how human behavior and learning change as a result of automation to improve the long-term health of the combined human-computer system.
Key_Actors: People; computers; automation designers; system administrators; operators.
Operational_Workflow: Designers consider the knowledge people accumulate by doing tasks manually and how that process is altered by automation; automation is built using existing accumulated knowledge while ensuring the design does not inhibit further human learning; the system is structured with functional overlap between people and machines rather than a rigid segregation of tasks; functions are redistributed between components as situational dynamics, capabilities, and needs change over time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designers must focus on long-term human efficiency and system health rather than just short-term effects; people must be viewed as adaptive, resourceful learning partners essential to the functioning of the whole; designers must account for unintended consequences of automation on human behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: There is no silver bullet or easy-to-follow formula for applying this principle; closer coupling between people and automation creates thorny issues regarding task allocation and final responsibility.

Concept_Name: Fitts’s list
Core_Definition: A set of attributes proposed by Fitts in 1951 used to divide work between humans and machines based on which entity is better suited for specific tasks.
Key_Actors: People; machines; automation designers.
Operational_Workflow: Designers evaluate work by comparing the specific capabilities of humans against those of machines; machines are assigned tasks where they excel such as collecting monitoring data at regular intervals from thousands of sources or working in toxic environments; people are assigned to roles involving information processing where they have higher capabilities; the interaction between the two is optimized to make the overall processes more efficient.
Rules_and_Constraints: This list is the basis of the compensatory principle; work must be described as separate and identifiable tasks assigned to each party; the capabilities of both people and machines are assumed to be reasonably static.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Joint cognitive system (JCS)
Core_Definition: A combined system of people and automation characterized by its ability to maintain control of a situation despite disrupting influences from the environment or the process itself.
Key_Actors: People (acting as proactive and reactive agents); automation; the environment.
Operational_Workflow: The system maintains control by treating people as adaptive and resourceful partners who take an active part in the system; people handle goal-oriented and event-driven tasks while the automation performs its functions; the system utilizes functional overlap to allow for the redistribution of tasks between partners; the system adapts its dynamics as capabilities and needs vary over time or in different situations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must stay in control of the situation despite disruptions; components must have functional overlap rather than rigid segregation; people must be viewed as essential partners to the whole system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Capabilities and needs within the joint cognitive system may vary over time and with different situations.

Concept_Name: Cognitive systems engineering (CSE)
Core_Definition: An engineering approach that views people and automation together as a single joint cognitive system rather than as separate components.
Key_Actors: Automation; people; system designers.
Operational_Workflow: Designers evaluate the automation and human components as a unified whole; the approach applies the complementarity principle to assess how human behavior changes as a result of automation; systems are designed to accommodate people who are driven by both goals and events; functional overlap is established to ensure the system remains adaptive to changing dynamics.
Rules_and_Constraints: Designers must consider the human factor and long-term effects on the people running the system; it must avoid viewing automation as simply a functional replacement for human tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: A Model for Types and Levels of Human Interaction with Automation
Core_Definition: A framework proposed by Parasuraman, Sheridan, and Wickens that categorizes automation into four stages of information processing and describes varying degrees of human-computer interaction ranging from manual oversight to full autonomy.
Key_Actors: Parasuraman; Sheridan; Wickens; information; system; human operators.
Operational_Workflow: Information is gathered by the system; information is analyzed; a decision is made based on the analysis; an action is taken; the interaction level ranges from the system making suggestions for approval to executing decisions unless vetoed or acting autonomously without human input.
Rules_and_Constraints: Information gathering and analysis stages are appropriate for high levels of automation; the decision stage should be automated based on function risk; low-risk functions should be highly automated while high-risk functions should be less automated; taking action for high-risk functions should involve a middle level of automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: A Mature Role for Automation
Core_Definition: A model of automation design that emphasizes a partnership between automation and operations staff to enhance mutual value, applying the complementarity principle rather than simple task replacement.
Key_Actors: Allspaw; operations staff; automation systems; Parasuraman et al.
Operational_Workflow: Automation and operations staff work together as a team; the partnership applies the complementarity principle to improve long-term system health; designers use specific methods to ensure automation is trustworthy and safe for human operators.
Rules_and_Constraints: Apply the complementarity principle; view automation and people together as a joint cognitive system; incorporate trust and safety as key elements of teamwork; treat automation and operations staff as partners.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Appropriate trust
Core_Definition: A principle for making automation trustworthy by designing for a level of reliance that matches the system's actual reliability and purpose through transparency and training.
Key_Actors: Lee; See; Allspaw; operators; designers.
Operational_Workflow: Past performance of the automation is shown to the operator; intermediate results of processes and algorithms are revealed in a comprehensible way; the purpose and design basis are explained in relation to user goals; operators are trained on expected reliability and governing mechanisms.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design for appropriate trust rather than greater trust; simplify algorithms and operations to increase understanding; train operators on the mechanisms governing behavior and intended use; evaluate anthropomorphizing carefully.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Anthropomorphizing the automation, such as using speech to create a synthetic conversational partner, must be carefully evaluated to ensure it does not create a level of trust that is inappropriate for the system's function.

Concept_Name: Tracking
Core_Definition: The first stage in the long-term operation of a system involving event detection and short-term control in response to inputs or detected events.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; monitoring systems; system administrators; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The system performs event detection; short-term control responses are executed based on detected events or inputs; data is collected regarding defects or failures; edge cases requiring manual intervention are identified and prioritized for future automation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation typically starts at the tracking level; statistics on failures should be used to drive business decisions and improve reliability; pervasive edge cases should be prioritized for automation; tracking is the first of four stages followed by regulating, monitoring, and targeting.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Incomplete automation leads to edge cases requiring manual intervention; these deviations should be tracked and categorized to determine if they occur frequently enough to justify further automation.

Concept_Name: Regulating
Core_Definition: Regulating is a stage of long-term system operation that involves managing the transitions between different states. It serves as a form of long-term control within the operational chain.
Key_Actors: Automation systems; system administrators; physical or virtual machines being managed.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves detecting the current state of a system component; determining the necessary state transition; and executing the management tasks required to move the system from one state to another.
Rules_and_Constraints: Regulating is positioned above the tracking level but below monitoring and targeting in the functional chain of system operation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: As automation moves into higher levels like regulation, operators can become disconnected from the system logic, which may jeopardize long-term health if the reasons for state transitions are not understood by the people running the system.

Concept_Name: Monitoring
Core_Definition: Monitoring is a high-level operational stage that covers longer-term controls and the interpretation of system state. It is used to select plans that keep the system within its desired operating parameters.
Key_Actors: Automated monitoring systems; system administrators; hardware and capacity planning teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system interprets current state data; the data is analyzed against desired parameters; and specific plans or actions, such as capacity planning or hardware selection, are initiated to maintain system stability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Machines are considered better suited than people for the repetitive collection of monitoring data at frequent intervals from large numbers of machines; information gathering and analysis stages are appropriate for high levels of automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If people are not involved in the process or have no insight into how the monitoring automation works, they will struggle to handle exceptions or understand why the automation failed.

Concept_Name: Targeting
Core_Definition: Targeting is the highest stage of system operation and involves setting the overall goals for the system based on corporate objectives. It is used to drive the desired behavior of the system as a whole.
Key_Actors: Corporate leadership; system administrators; people responsible for goal setting.
Operational_Workflow: Higher-level goals are established based on corporate requirements; these goals are used to define system-wide targets; and key performance indicators are applied to measure and drive the system toward these objectives.
Rules_and_Constraints: Higher-level tasks like targeting are generally more suited to people than to machines; it sits at the top of the chain that includes tracking, regulating, and monitoring.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automating at this level can lead to people becoming disconnected from the purpose and logic of the system, which can threaten the long-term health of the joint human-computer system.

Concept_Name: Key performance indicators (KPIs)
Core_Definition: KPIs are specific metrics used within the targeting stage of system operation to drive desired behavior. They link the overall goals of the system back to the broader goals of the corporation.
Key_Actors: Corporate entities; system administrators; human teams responsible for defining performance metrics.
Operational_Workflow: Corporate goals are identified; these goals are translated into measurable performance indicators; and these indicators are then used to set targets and guide the behavior of the system and its operators.
Rules_and_Constraints: KPIs are used specifically to facilitate the targeting stage of long-term system operation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Opportunity cost
Core_Definition: The burdensome cost resulting from missed growth opportunities when a company cannot hire or train enough people to manage highly complex automated systems.
Key_Actors: Companies; SREs; recruiting teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: A company implements super automated systems that require expensive and specialized training; hiring becomes extremely difficult due to the high skill requirements; the company's ability to grow at its desired rate is limited by these staffing shortages; the resulting loss in potential business or growth becomes a cost that may outweigh the savings provided by the automation.
Rules_and_Constraints: This cost must be weighed against the efficiency gains of the system; management must decide if the difficulty of hiring justifies the level of automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large companies like Google attempt to mitigate this by implementing super aggressive recruiting campaigns to hire SREs.

Concept_Name: SREs
Core_Definition: Site Reliability Engineers are specialized system administrators who focus on scaling through automation and managing large-scale distributed systems.
Key_Actors: SRE teams; management; recruiting departments; Google.
Operational_Workflow: SREs are responsible for managing specific subsystems and services; they use issue tracking systems to manage feature requests and bugs; they follow established policies to maintain a balance between manual toil and automation work; they may participate in declaring a toil emergency to reprioritize their workload.
Rules_and_Constraints: A team should spend no more than half of its collective time on operational toil; management must review and approve reprioritization plans if this balance is lost; SREs are encouraged to use style guides and code reviews to maintain high quality in their automation code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a team is overloaded by small urgent tasks or a specific bottleneck person, they may be unable to complete important projects, requiring a pause in other work to fix the root cause.

Concept_Name: Tool building
Core_Definition: The development of software or hardware that improves a manual task so it can be done better, faster, or more consistently without eliminating the human role in the process.
Key_Actors: System administrators; developers; users; consultants.
Operational_Workflow: A process is first performed manually and documented step-by-step; individual tools are created to perform specific steps in isolation; these tools are used to make the manual process more reliable and less error-prone; eventually these tools are combined into larger programs as a stepping stone toward full automation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tool building is considered a prerequisite to automation; it should reduce the skill required for a task and lower the barrier to entry; tools should include logging and sanity checking of input parameters; tool building is useful for rare or difficult tasks where full automation is not yet feasible.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For extremely rare and complex tasks that only happen once, it is often more efficient to hire a consultant to build a set of tools for common follow-up tasks rather than building full automation.

Concept_Name: Robotic painting systems
Core_Definition: Automated systems used in auto manufacturing to perform the task of painting metal car panels without human intervention.
Key_Actors: Auto manufacturing plants; robotic systems; maintenance employees.
Operational_Workflow: The process evolved from manual painting by skilled people to the use of high-power paint sprayers as tools; eventually the process was fully automated with robotic systems; the human role transitioned from painting panels to maintaining the robotic system itself.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment of these systems eliminates the specific manual job category of car panel painter; the system transforms the work from performing a task to maintaining the automation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load balancers
Core_Definition: Systems that manage the distribution of service traffic across multiple replicas.
Key_Actors: Replicas; load balancers; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Typically; load balancers require manual configuration or specialized tools to add replicas to the system. In a fully automated solution; the load balancer is configured only once; then individual replicas communicate their own availability to the load balancer. The load balancer then automatically includes those available replicas in its traffic rotation.
Rules_and_Constraints: A fully automated approach should aim to eliminate the need for constant reconfiguration by inventing new ways to operate; such as having replicas report their own availability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Virtual cluster management system
Core_Definition: A system that runs large clusters of physical machines which in turn provide virtual machines to a high volume of users.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines; users; system administrators; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The system manages the lifecycle of virtual machines across a large pool of physical hardware. Because hardware failures are frequent at scale; the system utilizes an automated repair life cycle. This process involves detecting hardware issues; migrating virtual machines to healthy hardware; notifying technicians of specific repairs needed; and re-integrating machines once they are fixed.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system requires automation that is improved over time to handle complex; error-prone operations with more precision and error checking than a person could manually provide.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a physical machine unexpectedly dies; the system uses recovery tools to attempt power cycles and; if those fail; restarts virtual machines from their last snapshot on a different physical machine.

Concept_Name: Ganeti
Core_Definition: An open source virtual cluster management system used to run large clusters of physical machines that provide virtual machines to thousands of users.
Key_Actors: Google; system administrators; Ganeti team; datacenter technicians; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Ganeti is used to manage clusters where hardware failures are treated through an automated repair life cycle. It integrates with monitoring systems and repair status consoles to trigger specific tools for draining; recovery; and re-assimilation of machines. It can be managed through a centralized Puppet server system that provides multitenant access for the Ganeti team.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system should be managed using the complementarity principle; where automation and operations staff work together as a partnership to stay in control of the situation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automation logs from Ganeti are used to drive business decisions; such as identifying and accelerating the move away from specific hardware models that prove to be the least reliable.

Concept_Name: Drain Tool
Core_Definition: An automation tool that migrates all virtual machines from a physical machine to other machines when pending hardware problems are detected.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; virtual machines; physical machines.
Operational_Workflow: The tool is triggered when monitoring systems detect signs of pending hardware failure; such as correctable disk or RAM errors. It then migrates all virtual machines currently running on the suspect hardware to other physical machines within the cluster to prevent service interruption.
Rules_and_Constraints: The tool is designed to automate complex operations that would otherwise require a high level of expertise and be prone to human error.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Recovery Tool
Core_Definition: A tool designed to restore service when a physical machine unexpectedly dies by attempting hardware resets or migrating workloads.
Key_Actors: Physical machines; virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: The tool makes several attempts to power the physical machine off and on; if these power cycling efforts fail to recover the machine, the tool restarts the virtual machines from their last snapshot on another physical machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: This tool is utilized specifically when a machine has already died rather than as a preventive measure for pending failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the physical hardware cannot be recovered through power cycling, the tool performs a failover of the virtual machines to alternate hardware.

Concept_Name: Send to Repairs Tool
Core_Definition: A tool used to automate the notification and diagnostic reporting process when a machine requires physical hardware maintenance.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians; machine repair API; physical machines.
Operational_Workflow: The tool gathers problem reports related to hardware failures; it uses the machine repair API to request the necessary work; it provides specific details to technicians such as the serial number of failing disks or the memory slot of failing RAM.
Rules_and_Constraints: The tool is intended to direct repair technicians to the exact problem to reduce overall repair time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Machine repair API
Core_Definition: A programmatic interface that allows automation tools to communicate with the repair status console and request maintenance from technicians.
Key_Actors: Send to Repairs Tool; repair status console; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: It receives gathered problem reports and repair requests from the Send to Repairs Tool; it facilitates the creation of triggers based on the repair status console to coordinate technician activities.
Rules_and_Constraints: It acts as the bridge between the automated monitoring/diagnostic tools and the manual repair workflow.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Re-assimilate Tool
Core_Definition: A tool that evaluates and prepares a machine for reintegration into the cluster after it has returned from physical repairs.
Key_Actors: Repaired machines; cluster management system.
Operational_Workflow: When a machine returns from the repair process, the tool evaluates its current state; it performs necessary configurations; it re-adds the machine to the active cluster.
Rules_and_Constraints: This tool is activated by a state-tracking system when a machine transitions from the in repairs state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Monitoring system
Core_Definition: A system designed to collect data from machines at regular intervals and analyze that information to detect events or pending problems.
Key_Actors: Machines; system administrators; automation software; monitoring system APIs.
Operational_Workflow: The machine collects monitoring data at specific intervals, such as every five minutes, from thousands of machines; the gathered information is analyzed to detect events or signs of pending hardware problems like disk or RAM errors; detections act as triggers that activate automation tools at the appropriate time based on the detected state.
Rules_and_Constraints: Information gathering and analysis stages are appropriate for a high level of automation; monitoring covers longer-term controls including interpreting system state and selecting plans to remain within desired parameters; the automation level for decisions based on monitoring should be appropriate for the function’s risk.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When monitoring detects pending hardware problems, a drain tool may be triggered to migrate virtual machines; if a physical machine unexpectedly dies, recovery tools attempt to power it off and on or restart virtual machines from snapshots.

Concept_Name: Repair status console
Core_Definition: A system that provides an API to track and manage the various stages of a machine's repair lifecycle.
Key_Actors: System administrators; datacenter technicians; automated repair tools; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The console works with a monitoring system to track whether a machine is alive, having problems, in repairs, or being re-assimilated; automation tools use the console's API to send problem reports to technicians, including specific details like failing disk serial numbers or memory slots; triggers activate different tools based on the state tracked in the console to move machines through the repair process.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must track the specific state of a machine to ensure the right tool is activated at the right time; logs from the repair status console should be used to drive business decisions regarding hardware reliability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine fails to recover through automated power cycles, the console is used to request physical repairs from datacenter technicians.

Concept_Name: Configuration management tools
Core_Definition: Domain-specific systems used for maintaining the configuration of machines from low-level settings to high-level service configurations through a declarative language.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management systems; version control systems; target servers.
Operational_Workflow: The administrator writes declarative code describing the desired state of a machine; the tool compares the current state to the desired state and identifies which changes are needed; it then brings the system to that state using convergent or directed orchestration; settings can be organized into libraries where specific definitions inherit attributes from more generic server definitions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Configuration languages are declarative and should concisely define settings at a high level; all configuration files must be revision controlled; tools should not modify configuration files outside of the version control system; a permission model should be used in multitenant environments to protect different teams from unauthorized changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These tools often have a steep learning curve and require the initial creation of all necessary definitions; they may fail to handle certain edge cases if the underlying automation is incomplete, requiring manual intervention.

Concept_Name: CFEngine
Core_Definition: A popular declarative configuration management system and domain-specific language purpose-built for system administration tasks.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management engines.
Operational_Workflow: The user specifies the desired state of a system component, such as a symbolic link name and its destination, using a declarative syntax; the engine handles the logic of checking if the component already exists, correcting it if it is wrong, or creating it if it is missing; the system leverages the built-in knowledge of the tool's creators to handle these low-level operations correctly.
Rules_and_Constraints: It uses a declarative approach rather than an imperative one; it is intended to enshrine best practices in shared definitions and processes; it is often used as a starting point for automating machine configuration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Puppet
Core_Definition: A popular configuration management system that uses a declarative domain-specific language designed specifically for system administration tasks.
Key_Actors: System administrators; centralized Puppet teams; individual teams acting as tenants; the Puppet server system.
Operational_Workflow: A programmer writes manifests using a declarative syntax to describe the desired state of a system; the Puppet system figures out which changes are needed to achieve that goal and executes them; in a multitenant environment, tenants manage their own configurations in isolated areas for development, testing, and production while relying on a central system for shared features.
Rules_and_Constraints: Puppet uses a declarative rather than imperative syntax; users must initially create definitions or classes which can then be inherited by other definitions; manifests run as root and can change any file on the target machine; it requires a steep learning curve for its domain-specific language.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In shared environments, a permission system must be used to prevent one team from modifying the files of another team to avoid unauthorized access across platforms.

Concept_Name: Toil emergency
Core_Definition: An official process used by a team to pause regular projects and address an excessive accumulation of exhausting manual labor.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: A team identifies that more than half of its collective time is being spent on operational toil; the team reviews its time usage to identify root cause problems; an official declaration is made to management; the team creates a plan to fix the biggest sources of toil; other projects are put on hold while the team executes the plan to restore a 50/50 balance between toil and project work.
Rules_and_Constraints: Triggered when toil exceeds 50 percent of the team's workload; management must review and approve the reprioritization plans; projects established must strike at the root of the problems rather than just mitigating symptoms.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Prototyping
Core_Definition: The step in the automation process where individual tools are created to perform specific manual steps in isolation.
Key_Actors: System administrators; programmers.
Operational_Workflow: The administrator first documents a manual process in a step-by-step manner and verifies the documentation; self-contained tools are built to perform each individual step independently; the administrator ensures each tool works correctly before eventually combining them into a single program.
Rules_and_Constraints: Prototyping follows manual documentation and precedes the creation of a fully automated system; shell scripting languages are noted for allowing very fast prototyping because they permit the programmer to work at a high level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Self-service tool
Core_Definition: An automated system that empowers non-system administrators or end users to perform tasks that previously required an expert.
Key_Actors: System administrators; non-system administrators; end users; human resources personnel.
Operational_Workflow: A system administrator identifies a frequently requested manual task and automates it; the administrator creates an interface or API that allows non-experts to trigger the process; a permission model is implemented to control who can use the tool; users perform the task on a self-service basis while the administrator shifts to a role of maintaining the automation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security is a primary concern requiring a permission model; the tool must include checks to prevent users from accidentally performing harmful actions or circumventing the system to gain privileged access.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Permission model
Core_Definition: A security framework used to control access to automation tools and ensure that users cannot perform harmful actions or gain unauthorized privileged access.
Key_Actors: Non-system administrators; end users; tenants; individual teams or groups; service delivery platforms.
Operational_Workflow: When a task is converted into a self-service tool for non-system administrators, a permission model is established to control who may use the tool. In a multitenant environment, the system provides isolated areas for different groups to manage their own code base. The permission system enforces boundaries so that each team is protected from changes by other teams, maintaining separate security domains even though a single service provides for everyone.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be a permission model to control who may use a tool; the system must include checks to verify that people cannot accidentally do bad things or circumvent the system to gain privileged access; multitenant frameworks must ensure each group usage is isolated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a powerful permission system is not in place, manifest programs running as root could allow one team to modify the files of another team, essentially giving them unauthorized access across the entire system.

Concept_Name: Shell Scripting Languages
Core_Definition: Languages that provide the commands typed at an operating system command-line prompt and allow sequences of those commands to be turned into a script.
Key_Actors: Programmers; system administrators; automated processes such as cron.
Operational_Workflow: A sequence of commands typed interactively at a command-line prompt is converted into a script. This script is then executed as a single command, which can be done manually by a person or automatically by another process.
Rules_and_Constraints: These languages should be used for very fast prototyping; they allow the programmer to work at a very high level; they are used to combine commands or programs to make larger and more powerful programs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Shell-scripted solutions do not scale as well as other languages; they are difficult to test; they cannot easily do low-level tasks.

Concept_Name: Bash
Core_Definition: The Bourne Again Shell, which is a superset of the Bourne Shell and is the most common UNIX shell.
Key_Actors: UNIX system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A sequence of commands typed at the Bash prompt is turned into a script that can be run as a single command. This allows an administrator to automate administrative tasks by executing the script manually or through an automated process.
Rules_and_Constraints: As a shell scripting language, it is used for fast prototyping and high-level work, enabling the combination of various commands or programs into larger tools.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Like other shell languages, Bash solutions do not scale as well as other languages, are difficult to test, and are generally inappropriate for low-level tasks.

Concept_Name: PowerShell
Core_Definition: A powerful system in the Microsoft Windows world used to automate and coordinate Windows systems and products.
Key_Actors: Microsoft Windows system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Sequences of commands are typed at the PowerShell prompt and converted into scripts. These scripts are then run as single commands to facilitate the coordination and automation of various Microsoft Windows products and systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used as a tool for rapid prototyping and high-level programming by combining multiple commands into larger programs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Solutions created with PowerShell do not scale as well as those in other languages, are difficult to test, and have limitations regarding low-level tasks.

Concept_Name: Scripting Languages
Core_Definition: Interpreted languages designed for rapid development that often focus on systems programming and are more flexible and versatile than shell scripts.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; prewritten libraries; networks; storage; databases.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators use these languages to create tools and automation by leveraging large libraries of modules; developers glue various modules together to perform tasks like file manipulation, date and time handling, and database access; these languages allow programmers to organize code using modern practices like object-oriented and functional programming.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scripting languages should be used for rapid development and when better testing tools or prewritten libraries are required; they provide better error checking than shell scripts; their speed is often acceptable for system administration tools where performance is bounded by factors like disk input and output rather than language execution speed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Scripting languages execute more slowly than compiled languages; they are inappropriate for very large software projects exceeding tens of thousands of lines; because they are not strongly typed, variable type errors are only discovered at run-time and can cause the program to crash in rarely used or untested code.

Concept_Name: Perl
Core_Definition: An older scripting language that is popular with system administrators because its syntax is similar to C and awk.
Key_Actors: System administrators; UNIX system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators use Perl to perform common tasks by leveraging a large library of modules for functions such as file manipulation and database access; it follows an imperative model where the programmer must explicitly write code to check for existing conditions and handle specific error states.
Rules_and_Constraints: Perl requires the programmer to have a high level of expertise to manually handle edge cases and test code under various conditions; it allows for rapid development and systems programming through its expressive syntax and modular structure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Like other scripting languages, Perl is not recommended for projects with tens of thousands of lines of code; it lacks strong typing, meaning errors such as type mismatches between strings and numbers may only be discovered when the code is executed in production.

Concept_Name: Python
Core_Definition: A scripting language designed for rapid development that features a cleaner design and more readable code than its predecessors.
Key_Actors: System administrators; programmers; PyLint style-checking software.
Operational_Workflow: Python is used to create automation by combining prewritten libraries and modules for tasks like network transactions and file handling; programmers follow style guides and use tools like PyLint to ensure code quality and consistency before committing code to a central repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: Python encourages modern coding practices including object-oriented and functional programming; it should be used for tasks that require better code organization and scaling than shell scripts provide; formatting should follow established style guides such as standardizing on specific indentations and blank lines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Python is not appropriate for very large software projects; variable types are not checked until they are used, which can result in run-time crashes; speed disadvantages compared to compiled languages are mitigated by faster interpreter technology but still exist.

Concept_Name: Ruby
Core_Definition: A scripting language popular in the system administrator community that shares syntax characteristics with Perl and Python while adding features to create purpose-built mini-languages.
Key_Actors: System administrators; mini-language developers.
Operational_Workflow: Ruby allows administrators to create domain-specific mini-languages for specific tasks, after which more programs can be written within those specialized languages; it leverages modules to perform system-level operations such as accessing databases or manipulating files.
Rules_and_Constraints: Ruby permits better code organization and scales better than shell scripts; it enables the use of modern practices like object-oriented and functional programming; it relies on faster interpreter technology to mitigate the inherent speed limitations of interpreted code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Ruby is inappropriate for massive software projects containing tens of thousands of lines; its lack of strong typing means variable errors are not caught during a compilation phase but only at run-time; programs may crash if the language cannot automatically reconcile variable types during execution.

Concept_Name: Compiled Languages
Core_Definition: A category of programming languages where code is translated into a machine-readable format before execution, typically catching more errors at compile time and offering better scalability for large-scale automation projects. These languages are often statically typed, meaning variable types are checked during the compilation process.
Key_Actors: System administrators who write large-scale automation; software compilers that analyze and translate code.
Operational_Workflow: The system administrator writes the source code for an automation task; the code undergoes a compilation process where the language checks for type errors and other logic issues; if the code is valid, it is converted into a binary that can be deployed into the field for use.
Rules_and_Constraints: These languages are recommended for large software projects that include tens of thousands of lines of code; they require variables to have defined types that are checked before the code is ever run; they are preferred over scripting languages when high-level scalability and run-time reliability are required.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unlike scripting languages that discover errors at run-time or only in rarely used code paths, compiled languages are designed to catch these issues during the build phase, long before the code is used in production.

Concept_Name: C
Core_Definition: A compiled language co-created by Ken Thompson that is used by system administrators for building large-scale automation. It is a statically typed language that served as a syntactical influence for languages like Perl.
Key_Actors: Ken Thompson (co-creator); system administrators who develop automation tools.
Operational_Workflow: A system administrator writes automation code using C syntax; the code is processed by a compiler to identify errors and generate an executable; the resulting tool is used to perform system administration tasks.
Rules_and_Constraints: As a compiled language, it is expected to catch errors at compile time and is suitable for large-scale automation projects.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: C++
Core_Definition: A compiled language used by system administrators to create automation for large distributed systems. It belongs to the class of statically typed languages that verify variable types during the compilation phase rather than at run-time.
Key_Actors: System administrators responsible for large-scale automation and operations.
Operational_Workflow: The administrator writes automation programs in C++; the code is compiled to find errors before the program is deployed; the final compiled program is used to scale operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is considered a good choice for large-scale automation and must undergo a compilation step to catch errors before entering the field.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Go
Core_Definition: A compiled language used by system administrators for large-scale automation tasks. It is characterized by being statically typed and catching errors during the compilation process.
Key_Actors: System administrators designing and operating large distributed systems.
Operational_Workflow: The administrator develops automation tools in Go; the code is passed through a compiler to catch errors and ensure type safety; the compiled binary is then used to automate manual tasks within the environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is categorized as a language that typically scales better than scripting languages for large-scale automation and follows the rules of static typing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Configuration Management (CM) languages
Core_Definition: Domain-specific languages created specifically for system administration tasks such as maintaining machine configurations from low-level network settings to high-level service files.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management systems like CFEngine and Puppet; server types including generic, web, and blog servers.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators write code describing the desired state of a system; the language then determines which changes are required to reach that state; administrators build up definitions using classes where specific server types inherit attributes from more generic server definitions.
Rules_and_Constraints: System administrators should define items concisely at a high level; best practices should be enshrined in shared definitions and processes; all configuration files must be kept under revision control.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These languages involve a steep learning curve and require the initial creation of all necessary definitions.

Concept_Name: Declarative
Core_Definition: A programming approach where the code describes the desired end state of the system rather than the specific steps needed to achieve it.
Key_Actors: Programmers; configuration management systems such as CFEngine and Puppet.
Operational_Workflow: The programmer writes code that describes how the world should be; the configuration management language then figures out the necessary changes to achieve that specific goal.
Rules_and_Constraints: The programmer specifies the link name and destination; the system leverages the knowledge and experience of the system creator to handle the execution correctly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Imperative
Core_Definition: A programming style where the code must explicitly list the sequence of commands and checks required to perform a task.
Key_Actors: Programmers; scripting languages like Python and Perl.
Operational_Workflow: The programmer writes code that manually checks if a resource already exists; the code must then correct the resource if it is wrong; the code must create the resource if it is missing; the code must manually handle all error conditions.
Rules_and_Constraints: This style requires a high level of expertise to identify and handle all possible edge cases properly; code must be tested under various conditions to ensure reliability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach is described as being very tricky and difficult to get exactly right because of the complexity of manual edge-case handling.

Concept_Name: Convergent orchestration
Core_Definition: An approach to system management focused on bringing a system to a desired state.
Key_Actors: Configuration management systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system evaluates the current state of a machine and performs the necessary actions to move it toward a pre-defined desired state.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Directed orchestration
Core_Definition: A design approach in configuration management systems that focuses on the ability to follow a specific multistep plan of action.
Key_Actors: Configuration management systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The system follows a predefined sequence of steps or a plan to execute changes rather than solely focusing on bringing the system to a desired state through convergence.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is identified as one of two primary ways configuration management systems are designed to operate, the other being convergent orchestration.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Definitions
Core_Definition: Modular descriptions of settings and software within configuration management systems used to specify how different types of servers should be configured.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management systems; specific server types such as generic servers, web servers, blog servers, and image servers.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators create a base definition for a generic server containing universal settings; they then create more specific definitions that inherit the attributes of the generic server while adding specialized software or configurations; for example, a web server definition inherits from a generic server, and a blog server definition inherits from the web server.
Rules_and_Constraints: Definitions should be used to build up a flexible and efficient library; changes made to a base definition must automatically propagate to and affect all other definitions that inherit from it; system administrators should define things concisely at a high level to enshrine best practices.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Classes
Core_Definition: A collection of inherited definitions within a configuration management system that allow for the efficient management of server configurations.
Key_Actors: System administrators; configuration management systems.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators build a library of classes where specialized server types inherit characteristics from more general server types; this allows for centralized changes where updating a parent class automatically updates all related child classes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Classes should be used to build a flexible library and specify desired states concisely; they allow for the sharing of best practices across an organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The text notes that there is a steep learning curve for the domain-specific languages used to create these classes and a high initial effort required to create all necessary definitions.

Concept_Name: Issue tracking systems
Core_Definition: Centralized shared services used by development and operations teams to record, manage, and track bug reports and feature requests.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; management; site reliability engineering teams; users.
Operational_Workflow: All bugs and feature requests are entered into the system to ensure visibility; issues are categorized or tagged by subsystem to allow for easier management and potential reorganizations; bugs are phrased as what is currently wrong while feature requests follow a specific template describing user goals; bugs go through a workflow of being received, verified, and fixed, followed by verification by a separate person before being closed.
Rules_and_Constraints: All work must be managed through the system to prevent overlapping tasks and facilitate handoffs; naming standards should be uniform; feature requests should follow an agile template stating who wants the goal and why; issue tracking software should be distinct from helpdesk systems as they focus on the bug or feature rather than the individual user.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a feature request is rejected it is marked as resolved, but if a bug is not fixed it remains open at a low priority; the text warns against the temptation to skip reporting well-known issues as this hides them from management and prevents them from being officially tracked.

Concept_Name: Bug trackers
Core_Definition: Systems used for recording and managing bug reports to increase visibility and ensure that work is recognized and tracked through completion.
Key_Actors: Software developers; system administrators; SRE teams; management; subsystem owners.
Operational_Workflow: A bug is reported to the system to raise its visibility; the issue is assigned to a specific queue or tagged by subsystem; the bug is received, verified, and fixed by a team member; a different person verifies that the fix is successful; the issue is finally closed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every bug report should go through the system rather than being ignored or assumed to be common knowledge; bugs should be phrased specifically in terms of what is wrong; issue naming standards must be uniform to ensure clarity; bugs that the team chooses not to fix should remain open but be marked with a low priority.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If two different people report the same bug, the reports are merged or the second one is closed as a duplicate; during team reorganizations, tickets may need to be mass-moved between queues or categories to reflect new ownership.

Concept_Name: Feature requests
Core_Definition: Documented requests for new capabilities or system changes that are tracked within an issue tracking system but managed with different workflows than bugs.
Key_Actors: Users desiring new capabilities; SREs; management responsible for resource allocation.
Operational_Workflow: Feature requests are submitted and recorded in the tracking system; they are categorized into separate queues or tagged specifically as feature requests; resources are allocated for development based on separate priorities from bug fixes; if a request is rejected, it is marked as resolved and the matter is settled.
Rules_and_Constraints: Requests should be described from the perspective of the person desiring the new capability; the recommended template for a request is As a type of user, I want some goal so that some reason; feature requests must be tracked separately from bugs because resources are often allocated differently for the two tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unlike bugs which stay open even if not fixed, a feature request is marked as resolved if the team decides not to implement it.

Concept_Name: IT helpdesk ticket systems
Core_Definition: Communication mechanisms focused on the user and their specific problems or fulfillment requests rather than on the software issues themselves.
Key_Actors: End users; IT helpdesk staff; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: A user submits a request or problem to the system; the system facilitates back-and-forth communication between the staff and the user; the staff works to fulfill the request or solve the user problem until completion.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must focus on the user experience and communication; these systems should be separate from software issue tracking systems because they require different software and workflows; similar requests from different people are not merged because each request is unique to the individual user.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If two people submit similar requests, they are treated as separate tickets rather than duplicates to ensure each user is assisted individually.

Concept_Name: Version control system (VCS)
Core_Definition: A central repository for storing, accessing, and updating source code and configuration files while maintaining a complete history of all changes made.
Key_Actors: Programmers; system administrators; repository owners; code reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: A user checks out the source code from the central repository to a local directory; the user edits the files as needed; the user commits or checks the changes back into the system; in distributed systems, users maintain their own local repositories and merge sets of changes to a master repository when ready.
Rules_and_Constraints: Both source code and configuration files must be revision controlled; tools and automation must not modify configuration files outside of the VCS; changes should be validated by pre-submit tests before they are committed; all code should conform to an established style guide to ensure quality and consistency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Older VCS frameworks may permit only one person to check out a project at a time to prevent conflicts; newer systems allow multiple checkouts but require users to manually or automatically merge overlapping changes; in distributed systems, a user can check in changes to a local repository independently of the central master system.

Concept_Name: Distributed version control system (DVCS)
Core_Definition: A type of version control system where every user has their own complete repository, including the entire revision history, and changes are transmitted between these repositories.
Key_Actors: Individual users or developers; local repositories; the master or main repository.
Operational_Workflow: A user performs a check-out which creates a local copy of the entire repository and its history; the user checks in changes to this local repository independently of the central system; changes from the main repository are pulled into the local repository in a controlled manner; finally, changes made in the local repository are merged upstream to the main repository when desired.
Rules_and_Constraints: Users are the masters of their own local repositories; development happens in parallel with the main repository; changes are merged upstream only when the user chooses to do so.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When multiple people check out the same project, the first person to check in has an easy process while others must merge their changes; the system handles non-overlapping merges automatically but requires a manual merge process using an editor for overlapping changes.

Concept_Name: Software Packaging
Core_Definition: The practice of distributing software in structured packages to leverage automated update systems and maintain consistency across environments.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; service delivery platforms; automated update systems.
Operational_Workflow: Software is developed and then prepared into a package format; the package is moved through development, beta, and production phases; automated systems use these packages to keep software tools up-to-date on all relevant machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software should be distributed via packages rather than ad hoc manual copying; packages should follow the same development and testing lifecycle as other software; all packages should be part of a centralized service delivery platform.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If software is distributed through ad hoc means rather than packaging, tools often become out of date across different systems, leading to maintenance problems.

Concept_Name: Development environment
Core_Definition: A dedicated area separate from production where automation tools and configuration files are created and undergo initial testing.
Key_Actors: System administrators; developers; version control systems; multitenant system tenants.
Operational_Workflow: Code for automation and configuration is written and tested within this environment; all files are stored in a revision-controlled repository; once initial development is complete, the tools are moved to a staging or testing area for quality assurance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation tools must not be developed or tested in the production environment; the environment should be a centralized, shared service to facilitate collaboration; in multitenant systems, development areas should be compartmentalized to prevent different teams from interfering with one another.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Beta environment
Core_Definition: A staging area or testing phase where software and automation tools undergo quality assurance tests after development but before being deployed to production.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; quality assurance teams; service delivery platforms.
Operational_Workflow: Software packages and tools are moved from the development environment into this staging area; quality assurance tests are performed to verify the tools; upon successful testing, the code is pushed to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software packages must pass through this phase just like any other software release; the environment is used to ensure that tools are staged and tested before they hit production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Production environment
Core_Definition: The final destination in the software delivery process where tested changes and automation tools are deployed for live operational use.
Key_Actors: Software delivery platform; system administrators; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: Changes are first made to code and data files stored in a revision-controlled central repository; the software delivery platform picks up these changes; the changes are tested; the changes are deployed to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation tools should not be developed or tested in the production environment; they should instead be developed in a dedicated development environment and staged to a testing area for quality assurance before being pushed to production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Outages can occur in the production environment if configuration errors or bugs are not caught by pre-production safeguards like code reviews or testing areas.

Concept_Name: Style Guides
Core_Definition: A standard document or set of rules indicating how source code should be formatted and which language features are encouraged, discouraged, or banned.
Key_Actors: Programmers; team members; less experienced staff; managers; style-checking programs such as PyLint or puppet-lint.
Operational_Workflow: A programmer writes code according to the style guide standards; before the code is committed, source repositories call pre-submit tests and style-checking programs; these programs validate the files and stop those with style violations from being committed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Guides typically standardize indenting with spaces instead of tabs; eliminate extra whitespace; specify blank lines between functions; recommend specific language features over others; ban features that have proved difficult to support; use special notations for comments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Major open source projects often have their own specific guides for their communities; companies may publish internal guides with redactions for public use; guides can be used during code reviews to provide feedback on style violations.

Concept_Name: TODO comments
Core_Definition: A special notation used within source code to mark sections that are temporary, represent short-term solutions, or are functional but not yet perfect.
Key_Actors: The programmer writing the comment; future maintainers.
Operational_Workflow: When a programmer implements a temporary solution or identifies a feature to add later, they insert a TODO comment; the annotation is followed by the username of the person who wrote the comment; this acts as a placeholder to prevent unnecessary future-proofing.
Rules_and_Constraints: These must be followed by the username of the person who wrote the comment; they should be used for code that is good enough but not perfect.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Adding a TODO comment listing features a programmer would like to add can reduce the emotional need to actually write the extra code immediately, helping the developer stick to writing just enough code.

Concept_Name: NB comments
Core_Definition: A special notation used in source code to provide an explanation for a non-obvious decision made by the programmer.
Key_Actors: The programmer writing the comment; code reviewers; future maintainers.
Operational_Workflow: A programmer identifies a part of the code where the logic or decision-making might not be immediately clear to others; they add an NB comment followed by their username to explain the reasoning; this information is then available for others during code reviews or maintenance.
Rules_and_Constraints: The annotation must be followed by the username of the person who wrote the comment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: FIXME comments
Core_Definition: A special notation used within source code comments to point out specific sections of code that need to be fixed.
Key_Actors: Programmers; developers; or system administrators writing and maintaining code or configuration files.
Operational_Workflow: A developer identifies a portion of code that requires a fix; they insert a comment using the FIXME tag; they include the specific bug ID of the associated issue; they append their username to the end of the comment for identification.
Rules_and_Constraints: The comment must point out something that needs to be fixed; the comment must list the bug ID of the issue; the annotation must be followed by the username of the person who wrote the comment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Pre-submit tests
Core_Definition: Automated programs called by source repositories to validate files for style violations and other errors before they are committed to the version control system.
Key_Actors: Source repositories; automated testing systems; style-checking programs; and developers or team members committing code.
Operational_Workflow: A user attempts to commit a file to a source code repository; the repository triggers the pre-submit tests automatically; the tests run style-checking programs or home-grown validation scripts; if the files contain style violations or formatting errors, the repository rejects the commit and prevents the files from being integrated.
Rules_and_Constraints: These tests should be used to enforce consistency across the team; they leverage tools to validate files before they are committed; they can be set to pedantically reject entries such as CHANGELOGs if they are not perfectly formatted.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: PyLint
Core_Definition: A style-checking program used to validate Python files for adherence to formatting and quality standards.
Key_Actors: Developers writing Python code; automated pre-submit test systems; and source code repositories.
Operational_Workflow: PyLint is executed as part of the pre-submit test process; it scans Python source files for style violations; it reports errors that can cause the repository to reject a commit if the code does not conform to the established style guide.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used specifically for Python files; it is typically run automatically to ensure consistency is enforced consistently across the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: puppet-lint
Core_Definition: A style-checking program used to validate Puppet files to ensure they meet established formatting and coding standards.
Key_Actors: System administrators or developers writing Puppet manifests; source repositories; and automated validation systems.
Operational_Workflow: The program is called by pre-submit test frameworks during the code commit process; it analyzes Puppet files for style violations; it enables the enforcement of standards by flagging or rejecting code that deviates from the prescribed style guide.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used specifically for Puppet files; it helps maintain high standards and consistency regardless of the size of the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Test-driven development (TDD)
Core_Definition: A software engineering practice where developers write the code to test the code before writing the functional code itself. It is designed to minimize bugs and maximize confidence in the software.
Key_Actors: Software developers; programmers.
Operational_Workflow: The developer starts by writing code that tests a specific function; the developer runs the tests which initially fail because the functional code does not yet exist; the developer performs fast iterations of writing code and rerunning the tests; the cycle continues until all the tests pass.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tests must be retained and not deleted after the code is finished; developers should stop coding immediately once all tests pass to avoid writing unnecessary code; the style guide should be used in conjunction with TDD to prevent excessive future-proofing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When code behavior needs to be modified, the developer updates the tests first to expect different results and then modifies the code until the tests pass again; big changes can be made with confidence that if unit tests do not break, the changes likely have no unintended side effects.

Concept_Name: Unit tests
Core_Definition: Automated tests that call a specific function with example inputs and compare the output against an expected result to verify correctness.
Key_Actors: Developers; modern programming languages; automated testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create a list of various input examples that a function must handle; they write code to call the function with these examples and compare the actual results against what the result should be; these tests are run periodically during development and kept for long-term use.
Rules_and_Constraints: Unit tests must cover many different variations and formats of input to be effective; they are retained throughout the life of the code to ensure future modifications do not break existing functionality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unused code often contains more bugs because it is not exercised or tested; if unit tests pass after significant code changes, it provides confidence that no unintended side effects were introduced.

Concept_Name: Code Reviews
Core_Definition: A process where at least one person other than the author reviews changes to files before they are committed to a version control system. It is used to find errors, suggest better algorithms, and ensure quality.
Key_Actors: Reviewers; original authors or reviewees; managers; team members.
Operational_Workflow: An author creates a change and submits it for review; a reviewer finds errors and makes suggestions for improvements; the reviewer adds commentary and feedback to specific lines; the author updates the files based on that feedback; this process repeats until all reviewers approve the change and it is integrated into the central repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reviewers must criticize the code and not the person to avoid undermining team cohesion; the time spent on a review should be proportional to the importance of the file; managers should role-model the ability to give criticism without being critical; code reviewers should enforce the style guide and identify style violations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Code reviews can be applied to configuration files to prevent outages before they hit production; they can also be used to delegate tasks to others while maintaining quality control through a central team's approval.

Concept_Name: Code review system (CRS)
Core_Definition: A software tool that provides a user interface for conducting code reviews, allowing reviewers to add commentary and feedback to particular lines of a file.
Key_Actors: Reviewers; software authors; specific functional teams such as a load balancer team.
Operational_Workflow: The system displays proposed file changes in a user interface; it allows reviewers to attach feedback and commentary directly to the relevant lines of code; it facilitates the cycle of updates and re-reviews; it manages the final approval before integration into the version control system.
Rules_and_Constraints: The CRS should be a centralized, shared service used by everyone involved in software development; it should be used for both source code and configuration files kept in a revision-controlled repository.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A CRS can be used as a self-service mechanism where anyone can propose a change to a sensitive configuration file, but the change only becomes active once a specific authoritative team approves it through the system.

Concept_Name: Writing Just Enough Code
Core_Definition: The practice of writing only the code required to satisfy the specific requirements of a feature or to fix a bug.
Key_Actors: Programmers; developers; team members; future maintainers.
Operational_Workflow: The developer identifies the requirements for a feature or bug fix; code is written to meet those specific needs; the developer stops coding once all requirements are met; test-driven development is often used to determine the exact point when the code is complete.
Rules_and_Constraints: Code should be neither more nor less than what is required; programmers should avoid making code more flexible or configurable than currently needed; style guides should explicitly discourage excessive coding and encourage the aggressive deletion of unused or obsolete code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Writing too little code results in unsatisfied requirements or code that is so terse it is difficult to understand; writing too much code creates a maintenance burden, introduces more bugs, and wastes development time.

Concept_Name: Futureproofing
Core_Definition: The practice of writing code that is more flexible or configurable than currently required or adding features that the programmer believes might be needed someday.
Key_Actors: Programmers; development teams; future maintainers.
Operational_Workflow: A programmer anticipates future needs while writing software; the programmer adds extra features or flexibility beyond the current requirements; constant values such as hostnames or filenames are made settable via command-line flags or configuration settings; major functions are split into libraries for potential future reuse.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text recommends resisting the temptation to future-proof because predictions about the future are often wrong; programmers should use TODO comments to list desired features instead of writing the actual code; style guides should explicitly discourage the practice.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Predicted future needs are wrong 80 percent of the time; unused code lacks exercise and testing, leading to the discovery of bugs only when the feature is finally needed months or years later; the requirements and environment may change by the time the future-proofed feature is actually required.

Concept_Name: Multitenancy
Core_Definition: A system design in which a single system provides service for multiple groups, with each group compartmentalized and protected from the actions of others.
Key_Actors: Central system providers; individual groups or teams; tenants.
Operational_Workflow: A centralized framework or system is created to serve multiple groups; each group or tenant is allowed to control its own code base or configuration within the system; a permission model is established to isolate usage and ensure security between the groups; any improvements or features added to the central system benefit all participating tenants.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each group must be isolated from other groups; a permission model must be in place to protect each team's security domain; the system should be usable on a self-service basis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the absence of proper protections, one team could potentially modify another team's files or gain unauthorized access to their specific systems or configurations.

Concept_Name: Staging environments
Core_Definition: Dedicated areas used for testing and quality assurance of software and configurations after development but before they are deployed to the production environment.
Key_Actors: Developers; quality assurance testers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Automation tools and configuration changes are first created in a dedicated development environment; the changes are then moved to a staging or testing area for quality assurance tests; once the changes are validated, they are pushed to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation tools and configuration files must not be developed or tested in the production environment; separate environments should be maintained for development, testing, and production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Puppet server system
Core_Definition: A centralized configuration management framework that provides multitenant access for different teams to manage their own machine configurations through a shared infrastructure.
Key_Actors: The centralized Puppet team that maintains the server; individual tenant teams such as the Mac team, Ubuntu team, and Ganeti team; client machines being configured; and developers who create manifests.
Operational_Workflow: Individual tenant teams manage their specific configurations within dedicated source code-controlled areas; configuration changes are moved through separate staging environments for development, testing, and production; the server executes Puppet manifests, which are programs that run as root to change files on target machines; the system allows machines to stay updated by providing secure access from outside the corporate firewall.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must use a permission model to ensure that each group is isolated and protected from the actions of others; individual teams are prohibited from modifying the files of other teams to prevent unauthorized access; configurations must be written in a declarative domain-specific language that describes the desired state of the system; code should be validated using style-checking programs like puppet-lint before being committed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: To prevent a situation where one team gains root access to another team's machines, the permission system strictly isolates manifests; the system is designed to handle a large number of clients through central server scaling; mobile machines that are not on the internal network can still be updated via secure external access.

