Concept_Name: Design documents
Core_Definition: Written descriptions of proposed or completed projects that serve as a roadmap for projects and documentation of accomplishments.
Key_Actors: Authors who contribute to the document; reviewers who provide feedback; approvers who must sign off on the project; team members who use the documents as a reference; managers who sign off on resources; internal customers dependent on project results.
Operational_Workflow: An author creates a draft document using a standardized template; the document is used as a communication vehicle to seek consensus and feedback from reviewers and subject matter experts; the author updates the document with revisions and increments the revision number; the document undergoes an approval process ranging from simple email replies to formal review boards; once finalized or approved, the document is added to a self-service central archive or repository such as a wiki for future reference.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documents should include specific details such as algorithms and rationale for choices; the format should be standardized with sections like title, status, and goals; goals must be cross-checked against the design; revision numbers should be used like software releases; email should not be used as a substitute for a design document; the archive must be a single, easy-to-search repository.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Short design documents can be used for small changes instead of full projects; informational documents may have a lightweight or no approval process; different approval workflows (simple, informal, or formal) are applied based on the scope, impact, or compliance requirements of the project.

Concept_Name: press release
Core_Definition: A document written at the beginning of a project that describes what the customer will see when the product is announced.
Key_Actors: Engineers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers start the project by writing the press release to define the end vision; they work backward from this customer-facing description to build the actual design; the technical design is only created after this vision document and other supporting materials are finished.
Rules_and_Constraints: This concept is part of the working backward process used at Amazon to ensure the project achieves a specific customer-centric vision.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: marketing materials
Core_Definition: Documents describing how the customer will benefit from a product and identifying its key features.
Key_Actors: Engineers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: These materials are designed early in the process as part of developing the vision for what will be created; they are completed after the press release but before the actual design work begins.
Rules_and_Constraints: These materials are used to identify features and benefits from the customer's perspective as part of a working backward design methodology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: FAQs
Core_Definition: A document that answers frequently asked questions to help develop the vision of what will be created for the customer.
Key_Actors: Engineers; customers.
Operational_Workflow: FAQs are authored alongside the press release and marketing materials at the start of a project; they serve to clarify the product vision before any engineering design work is initiated.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a component of the Amazon process for working backward from the customer experience to the design.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: change control process
Core_Definition: A formal organizational procedure for managing and approving changes where design documents serve as the foundation for change requests.
Key_Actors: Project teams; external review boards; compliance or audit entities; management.
Operational_Workflow: A design document is created to capture the specific details of a proposed change and is used to get team input; the document is then included as part of a formal change request if such a process is in place; the approval workflow for the document is determined by the specific change control or compliance requirements of the organization.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design documents are useful for small changes like router configurations or naming schemes; the process may include mandatory review boards or multiple approval steps depending on the scope and impact of the document; design documents can be made a mandatory requirement for specific approval processes such as budget reviews or security reviews.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: email archive
Core_Definition: A collection of past electronic messages that teams often mistakenly use as a repository for historical decisions and technical rationale.
Key_Actors: Existing team members; new team members; document archive searchers.
Operational_Workflow: Decisions are made and discussed through email correspondence; these records become sequestered within individual mailboxes; over time this information becomes inaccessible to new team members and does not appear in centralized document searches.
Rules_and_Constraints: Email is considered speech rather than writing; information should be written in design documents rather than left in email; sequestered information prevents teams from growing faster or transferring knowledge effectively.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For simple workflows, email is considered a sufficient medium for passing a document around for comments and review.

Concept_Name: Title
Core_Definition: The formal name assigned to a design document that identifies its subject matter.
Key_Actors: Authors; readers; automated scripts for index generation.
Operational_Workflow: The author provides a title at the top of the document as part of the standard anatomy; the title is used to list the document on a central archive page; users search for the title to find the specific documentation they need.
Rules_and_Constraints: The title is a mandatory section that must be included in the design document template; it should be listed in the document archive alongside filenames and revision numbers to facilitate ease of use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Date
Core_Definition: The specific calendar day marking the most recent revision of a design document.
Key_Actors: Authors; reviewers; automated indexing scripts.
Operational_Workflow: The date is recorded in the document header upon creation or update; as the document undergoes review, the date is updated to track the history of changes; the archive uses these dates to organize documents by their most recent updates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every design document template must include a date field for the last revision; dates are used in conjunction with revision numbers to track changes throughout the review and approval stages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Author(s)
Core_Definition: The individuals who contribute to the creation of the design document and record the project details.
Key_Actors: Project contributors; team members; early adopters; managers.
Operational_Workflow: An author creates a written description of a proposed or completed project by following a standardized template; they document the goals, design, and rationale; they seek feedback from the team to achieve consensus; they update the document as the project evolves or based on reviewer input.
Rules_and_Constraints: Authors are encouraged to start by describing the customer experience and working backward; they must include specific details such as algorithms and configuration parameters; they should use the organization's approved template and formats; authors should act as role models by strictly adhering to the document format.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an author finds the template does not personally benefit them it may indicate the template itself needs revision; when authors fail to use a template they should be queried in a nonconfrontational way to identify barriers to adoption.

Concept_Name: Reviewer(s)
Core_Definition: People whose feedback is requested to identify errors, ask questions, and provide expert input on the design.
Key_Actors: Team members involved in the project work; subject-matter experts; impacted stakeholders from other groups; external review boards.
Operational_Workflow: Reviewers are identified in the document header; they receive the draft via email or through an archive; they examine the proposal to ensure it does not repeat past mistakes; they provide meaningful comments that the author uses to revise the document.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reviewers are expected to provide feedback that may require changes to the document; adding someone as a reviewer serves as a formal notification of project impact; reviewers have a voice in the process but do not necessarily have a vote to block progress.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In simple workflows there may be no formal approval beyond the review phase; a review board may act as reviewers to give feedback without providing a final stamp of approval.

Concept_Name: Approver(s)
Core_Definition: Individuals who must provide formal sign-off for a document and its associated project to proceed.
Key_Actors: Internal customers; managers responsible for resources; specialized service teams like security or logging; audit and control personnel for regulations such as SOX or HIPAA.
Operational_Workflow: Approvers are listed in the document to clarify who has the authority to move the project forward; they review specific sections relevant to their expertise such as privacy or compliance; they provide a yes-or-no decision on the proposal; their approval is often tracked by revision numbers or dates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Approvers can block a project if their requirements are not met; commentary from an approver usually only accompanies a negative decision; the organization's individual process determines if approvals must be unanimous or if negative votes can be overridden.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At Google specialized approvers like the central log team must sign off on specific sections like logging justifications; informational documents may bypass the approval process entirely.

Concept_Name: Revision Number
Core_Definition: A numerical identifier for the document version used to track changes over time, similar to software release numbering.
Key_Actors: Authors; document archive scripts; review boards.
Operational_Workflow: A revision number is assigned to the initial draft; the number is updated each time review comments are incorporated or changes are made to the design; the most recent revision number is recorded in the document archive index to help users find the current version.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documents should have revisions numbered like software releases; the revision number is a standard part of the document anatomy along with the title and date; revision numbers are used in formal workflows to track changes across multiple approval stages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Status
Core_Definition: A section in a design document that indicates the current state of the document within its lifecycle.
Key_Actors: Document authors; reviewers; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The status is established at the start of the document creation process; it is updated as the document moves through various stages of the review and approval process; ideally, the document repository main page is updated as these revisions and status changes occur.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standard status categories often include draft, in review, approved, or in progress; organizations may choose to have fewer or more categories depending on their specific needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Executive Summary
Core_Definition: A brief overview of the project that captures the primary objective and the method for its achievement.
Key_Actors: Readers requiring a high-level understanding; document authors.
Operational_Workflow: The author places this summary at the beginning of the document anatomy to aid reader comprehension; it provides the highest-level information before the document proceeds into more specific technical details.
Rules_and_Constraints: The summary must be between two and four sentences long; it must contain the major goal of the project and explain how that goal is to be achieved.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Goals
Core_Definition: A section that identifies what the project is intended to achieve, encompassing both tangible results and process-oriented objectives.
Key_Actors: Document authors; project teams; reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: The author lists the project objectives; the design document is then cross-checked against each goal to ensure the design addresses every item; in some organizations, goals are assigned specific numbers which are then referenced throughout the subsequent design descriptions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Goals are typically presented as a list; the section may also be titled In Scope; the list must include non-tangible and process goals such as standardization or metrics achievements; the design must be verified to ensure it addresses each goal listed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Non-goals
Core_Definition: A section that explicitly identifies what is not included in the scope of a project to prevent misunderstandings about its purpose.
Key_Actors: Document authors; reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: The author identifies specific items or problems that the project will not address; this information is used during the review process to manage expectations and clarify the boundaries of the work.
Rules_and_Constraints: This information is typically presented as a list; the section may alternatively be titled Out of Scope; it should explicitly identify what is not included in the project scope.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This section is used to head off review comments that complain about the project not solving a specific problem by communicating upfront that the project was never intended to solve that specific issue.

Concept_Name: Background
Core_Definition: A section of the design document providing the history and context necessary for a typical reader to understand the project and its constraints.
Key_Actors: Authors; typical readers; company policy makers.
Operational_Workflow: Provide a brief history of how the current situation was reached; identify and define acronyms or unusual terminology used in the document; document any previous decisions that have placed limitations or constraints on the project.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must include any company policies that preclude certain solutions, such as a requirement to use only open source software; should contain information necessary for a typical reader to understand the design.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: High-Level Design
Core_Definition: A brief overview that describes the principles of the design and how it functions at a high level.
Key_Actors: Authors; readers; team members.
Operational_Workflow: Describe the primary design principles; summarize how the project works at a high level without focusing on implementation details.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must remain brief; should focus on design principles rather than implementation specifics.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Detailed Design
Core_Definition: The full and detailed description of the project including implementation specifics, technical artifacts, and the rationale for choices made.
Key_Actors: Authors; implementation team members; reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: Provide detailed descriptions of algorithms; specify packaging parameters and installation locations for binary files; explain technical choices such as configuration file paths; include diagrams and sample configuration files; cite specific goals by number to show how the design addresses each one.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must go into specific detail about the proposed project or change; should explain why specific choices were made; each design description should cite the appropriate goals; must be cross-checked against the goals section of the document.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Alternatives Considered
Core_Definition: A list of proposed solutions that were rejected during the design process and the specific reasons for their rejection.
Key_Actors: Authors; critics; reviewers.
Operational_Workflow: Identify various alternatives that were considered but not chosen; document the specific rationale for rejecting each alternative; use this section to address and quiet potential critics of the chosen design.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must include the reasons for rejection; should be placed in a specific, dedicated location in the document; can include information that might otherwise overlap with the background section to head off critical comments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Special Constraints
Core_Definition: A section within a design document that lists specific requirements or limitations related to security, auditing controls, privacy, and other organizational mandates.
Key_Actors: Project authors; corporate oversight entities.
Operational_Workflow: The author identifies specific constraints applicable to the project; these items are documented either in a single consolidated section or within individual sections for each constraint; mandatory project review processes are identified and assigned their own sections in the design document template.
Rules_and_Constraints: Special constraints are sometimes considered optional sections; any mandatory company process for project review should be given its own section in the template; the section serves to document activities that are part of the standard process at a specific company.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: architectural review
Core_Definition: A mandatory organizational process documented in a design document to evaluate the proposed design and prevent the repetition of past mistakes.
Key_Actors: Project authors; external review boards; project reviewers and approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The author documents the review activity within the special constraints section or a dedicated section of the design document; the document is submitted to an external review board that provides feedback and warnings based on institutional knowledge; authors may use a published checklist from the review board to prepare for the session.
Rules_and_Constraints: Mandatory review processes should have a dedicated section in the design document template; review boards can consist of approvers, reviewers, or a mix of both; checklists should be published by the board to be respectful of the author's time and ensure documents are not deficient.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: compliance checks
Core_Definition: The documentation and verification of a project's adherence to corporate policies and its plans to meet regulatory obligations.
Key_Actors: Project authors; compliance auditors for regulations such as SOX, HIPAA, PCI, or FISMA; project approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The author includes specific details in the design document regarding compliance with corporate policies; the author outlines audit plans for meeting regulatory obligations under applicable laws; auditors who oversee specific regulations review the document and act as approvers in the sign-off process.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design documents must record compliance with corporate policies; the document should specifically include compliance and audit plans for meeting obligations under regulations such as SOX, HIPAA, PCI, or FISMA; approvers for these checks are generally a yes-or-no voice.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Cost Projections
Core_Definition: An optional design document section detailing the initial capital and operational expenses plus the forecasted costs to maintain the system.
Key_Actors: Project authors; managers who sign off on resources; support staff.
Operational_Workflow: The author calculates the initial capital and operational costs for the project; a forecast is developed for the ongoing costs of keeping the systems running; these projections are integrated with support requirements; managers use this information during the approval process to sign off on the resources required for the project.
Rules_and_Constraints: Projections must include both initial capital and operational costs; the section must include a forecast of costs to keep the systems running; costs for support requirements must include staff salary, benefits, hardware, and licensing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Support Requirements
Core_Definition: Operational maintenance requirements for a project or system.
Key_Actors: Support staff.
Operational_Workflow: This section identifies the human and material resources needed to maintain the system after its launch; it functions as a component of the document that informs cost projections by accounting for staff salaries, benefits, hardware, and licensing fees.
Rules_and_Constraints: Support requirements must be linked to the cost projections section of the design document.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Schedule
Core_Definition: A timeline detailing which project events happen when and their relation to each other.
Key_Actors: Project authors; team members; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The schedule is integrated into the design document to serve as a roadmap; it outlines the sequence of project events to ensure the team understands the timing of milestones.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Security Concerns
Core_Definition: Special mention of issues regarding security related to a project, particularly the protection of data.
Key_Actors: Project authors; security review boards; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The author identifies potential security risks and data protection requirements in the design document; these details are then reviewed by specialized security boards or approvers who may provide feedback or require changes before granting sign-off.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security concerns may be listed as part of a general special constraints section or given a dedicated section; security review is often a mandatory part of formal approval workflows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Privacy and PII Concerns
Core_Definition: Documentation of issues involving user privacy, anonymity, and the management of personally identifiable information (PII).
Key_Actors: Project authors; privacy assessment teams; approvers; auditors.
Operational_Workflow: The design document outlines plans for handling PII; this information is then evaluated through a privacy assessment or audit process to ensure the project complies with applicable rules and regulations.
Rules_and_Constraints: PII must be handled in accordance with applicable rules and regulations; privacy assessments may be a mandatory part of the company's audit and control processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: personally identifiable information (PII)
Core_Definition: Information related to user privacy or anonymity that requires specific plans for handling in accordance with applicable rules and regulations.
Key_Actors: Design document authors; privacy assessment reviewers; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: Authors identify privacy or anonymity issues within the design document; the project undergoes a privacy assessment or review of the PII used; approvers sign off on the proposed handling plans as part of the project review.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design documents should include a special mention of issues regarding user privacy; authors must include plans for handling PII in accordance with applicable rules and regulations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Compliance Details
Core_Definition: A section of a design document used to outline compliance and audit plans for meeting regulatory obligations.
Key_Actors: Design document authors; compliance auditors; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The author documents compliance and audit plans within the design document; people who conduct audits of compliance with regulations review these plans; these auditors act as approvers in the design document process.
Rules_and_Constraints: This section is used to meet obligations under laws such as SOX, HIPAA, PCI, or FISMA; compliance details can be lumped into a special constraints section or given their own dedicated section in a template.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SOX
Core_Definition: A regulation that requires organizations to maintain compliance and audit plans which are documented during the design phase of a project.
Key_Actors: Authors; SOX compliance auditors; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: Authors include SOX compliance and audit plans in the design document; auditors of SOX compliance review the document; these auditors serve as approvers who must sign off on the project.
Rules_and_Constraints: SOX is a regulatory obligation that requires specific audit plans; people who conduct SOX audits have a yes or no voice in the approval process and typically provide commentary if they issue a no decision.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: HIPAA
Core_Definition: A regulatory law that necessitates the inclusion of compliance and audit plans in design documents to meet legal obligations.
Key_Actors: Authors; HIPAA compliance auditors; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: Authors detail HIPAA compliance and audit plans within the design document; auditors who conduct HIPAA compliance reviews examine the document; these auditors act as approvers whose sign off is required to move the project forward.
Rules_and_Constraints: HIPAA is listed as a law that may determine the approval workflow of a design document; auditors for HIPAA are categorized as approvers and their feedback is part of the formal or informal review process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: PCI
Core_Definition: A regulatory obligation or law that requires compliance and audit plans to be documented within a project design.
Key_Actors: People who conduct audits of compliance; approvers in the design document process.
Operational_Workflow: Compliance and audit plans for meeting PCI obligations are included in the Compliance Details section of a design document; those who conduct audits of compliance then act as approvers in the design document process to ensure the project meets these regulations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Projects must include compliance and audit plans for meeting regulatory obligations if they fall under PCI; it is listed alongside other similar laws like SOX, HIPAA, and FISMA.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: FISMA
Core_Definition: A specific law or regulatory obligation that requires projects to include compliance and audit plans.
Key_Actors: Project authors; audit and compliance officials.
Operational_Workflow: Documentation regarding FISMA is included in the Compliance Details section of a design document to outline plans for meeting regulatory audit requirements.
Rules_and_Constraints: FISMA is categorized with other regulatory obligations like SOX, HIPAA, and PCI that may necessitate specific sections in a design document template.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Launch Details
Core_Definition: A section in a design document that describes the operational requirements and procedures for the roll-out of a project.
Key_Actors: Project authors; operational staff responsible for launch.
Operational_Workflow: This information is recorded in a specific section of the design document to ensure that roll-out and launch requirements are planned and communicated before the project moves forward.
Rules_and_Constraints: This section is typically considered an optional part of the design document anatomy but should be included in the template to guide the author through roll-out planning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: template
Core_Definition: A standardized tool that guides authors through the process of creating design documents by providing necessary headings, hints, and annotations.
Key_Actors: Authors; managers who require their use; role models who demonstrate their use; team members who provide feedback on template usability.
Operational_Workflow: An organization provides a template in various formats like MS-Word or MarkDown and makes it easily accessible via links on intranets or wikis; authors fill out the sections and delete the instructional annotations; different versions such as short and long templates may be used depending on project complexity; managers may mandate the template for specific approval processes.
Rules_and_Constraints: The template should include all headings even if they are optional; instructional annotations should be in a different font or color; it must be easy to find and available in multiple formats used by the team; content should be prioritized over blind adherence to the format; any failure to use the template should be treated as an opportunity to debug the adoption process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the template is not benefiting the user personally it may need to be updated; when people cannot find the template the organization must update all locations where they looked to include links; negative feedback from respected members of the organization should be used to improve the template.

Concept_Name: short template
Core_Definition: A condensed version of a design document template intended for initial proposals and small projects.
Key_Actors: Authors; team members.
Operational_Workflow: The short template is used to document small changes such as new router configurations, plans to adopt new configuration management features, or new naming schemes for file hierarchies; it serves as a sounding board for team input and to gain consensus before a project moves forward.
Rules_and_Constraints: It should be available in all formats users are comfortable with including MS-Word, HTML, MarkDown, or OpenOffice; it should be easy to find and link to from the table of contents of the document archive; it should follow the same standardized headings and order as larger documents but in a more concise format.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: long template
Core_Definition: A comprehensive version of a design document template used for complex projects or formal proposals.
Key_Actors: Authors; reviewers; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The long template guides a person through the process of creating a full design by providing all necessary headings and annotations for detailed descriptions of algorithms, packaging parameters, and installation locations.
Rules_and_Constraints: The template should include all headings the final document should have, including optional ones; it should be annotated with descriptions of what is expected in each section along with helpful hints in a different font or color which are deleted as the user fills it out; it should be available in multiple formats to ensure easy adoption across different user preferences.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: document archive
Core_Definition: A single repository that serves as a central reference for past decisions and a roadmap for completed or proposed projects.
Key_Actors: Existing team members; new team members; other teams; scripts used for index generation.
Operational_Workflow: The process of adding to or updating the archive is self-service to avoid bottlenecks; documents are linked to from a single page that lists all titles; some archives use scripts to automatically generate an index by parsing document data or reading companion YAML or JSON files.
Rules_and_Constraints: It should be easy to find and search; it should have one page that lists the titles of all documents; it should use a standardized file naming system or unique team prefixes to help with automatic organization; it should be accessible to new team members so they can come up to speed without requiring personal interaction with experts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the process of adding documents involves emailing a person, that person becomes a bottleneck which discourages people from contributing.

Concept_Name: wiki
Core_Definition: A tool used to create a document archive where users add links to their documents on a main page.
Key_Actors: Authors; team members.
Operational_Workflow: Users create documents and then add links to those documents on a central wiki page; the actual documents are either stored directly within the wiki or in another location.
Rules_and_Constraints: The main page must be simple and easy for others to maintain because people will generally add to whatever structure already exists; the wiki should be searchable to ensure documents are easy to find.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: source repository
Core_Definition: A location used to store design documents as part of an organized archive system.
Key_Actors: Authors who add documents; teams who self-select file prefixes; scripts that automatically generate the index.
Operational_Workflow: People add documents to a specific location or subdirectory within the repository; automated scripts parse the document format or associated metadata files to extract necessary data; the scripts then generate an index that lists the filename, title, and most recent revision number.
Rules_and_Constraints: A system for file naming should be created so that document organization happens automatically; each team should be assigned or self-select a unique prefix for their files.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: file server
Core_Definition: A storage system used to host subdirectories for design documents as part of a document archive.
Key_Actors: People who add documents; scripts used for index generation.
Operational_Workflow: Users place their design documents into a designated subdirectory on the server; scripts parse these files or associated data files to automatically extract information; the system generates a searchable index based on the files found in the directory.
Rules_and_Constraints: A file naming system should be implemented to allow for automatic organization; unique prefixes for each team should be used to manage document identification.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: YAML
Core_Definition: A document format used to provide a standardized place for people to list data needed for an automated archive index.
Key_Actors: People who create the files; scripts that parse the files for metadata.
Operational_Workflow: Users include a YAML file along with their design document in a repository or file server subdirectory; scripts parse this file to extract metadata such as title and revision number; the scripts then automatically generate a document index for the archive.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: JSON
Core_Definition: A standardized file format used to store metadata that allows scripts to extract information for a document archive index.
Key_Actors: People who include the file with their documents; scripts that generate the archive index.
Operational_Workflow: A person includes a JSON file containing document details alongside their design document; scripts parse the file to retrieve the title, filename, and most recent revision number; the system uses this data to automatically build and update the index of all documents.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: approval process
Core_Definition: A defined method for authorizing design documents that ranges from low-overhead email replies to multi-stage formal reviews involving specific sign-off requirements.
Key_Actors: Authors who contribute to the document; Reviewers who provide feedback and expertise; Approvers including internal customers, managers, and audit/control staff who must sign off; External review boards; Centralized service groups such as security, privacy, or logging teams.
Operational_Workflow: An author creates a draft design document; the document is circulated to reviewers and approvers via email or a shared repository; reviewers provide meaningful comments and feedback; the author revises the document as necessary while updating version numbers; approvers provide a yes-or-no decision; if a review board is involved they may use a published checklist to evaluate the document; once all necessary stages or individuals have signed off the document status is updated to approved.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process should not entail a huge workload or it will discourage participation; the workflow is often determined by change control or compliance requirements; reviewers have a voice while only approvers have a vote to prevent people on the perimeter from blocking progress; the document must list the specific authors, reviewers, and approvers involved; version numbers and dates must track changes through the process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Approvers usually only provide commentary when giving a "no" decision; individual organizational processes determine if approvals must be unanimous or if negative approvals can be overridden by a majority; some centralized services like Google's logging team may have specialized sign-off requirements for specific sections of a document.

Concept_Name: review workflows
Core_Definition: The various defined styles and stages an organization uses to move a design document from draft status to a final state.
Key_Actors: Document authors; Project approvers; External review boards; Specialized teams for firewall, security, budget, and release schedule reviews.
Operational_Workflow: The author selects a workflow style based on the project's scope or impact; for informational documents a lightweight or nonexistent process is used; for more extensive projects the document moves through one of three main groups which are Simple, Informal, or Formal; each stage of the review is noted with comments; revision numbers or dates are used to track changes throughout the progression.
Rules_and_Constraints: Workflows are typically determined by change control or compliance requirements; the complexity of the workflow should scale with the document's impact; every revision or comment added during the workflow must be tracked with a version number update; the repository index should ideally be updated as revisions occur throughout the workflow.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Different types of documents may require different approval workflows based on their scope; some documents may skip approval stages if they are purely informational.

Concept_Name: Simple
Core_Definition: A lightweight review workflow where a draft is circulated for feedback without requiring a formal stamp of approval.
Key_Actors: The author of the document; Recipient team members or colleagues who provide comments.
Operational_Workflow: An author creates a draft design document; the draft is emailed around to the team or relevant parties for comments; the author revises the document if necessary based on the feedback; the document is finalized without a formal approval stage.
Rules_and_Constraints: This workflow is intended to be a low-overhead activity; it does not involve formal approval or external review boards; it is generally used for informational documents or projects with minimal impact.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Informal
Core_Definition: A review workflow similar to the simple process but involving feedback from project approvers or an external review board acting as a sounding board.
Key_Actors: Document author; Project approvers; External review board.
Operational_Workflow: The author creates a draft document; the document is shared with project approvers or an external review board; the board or approvers provide feedback and warn the author against repeating past mistakes; the author incorporates the feedback into the design; the process concludes without the board providing a formal stamp of approval.
Rules_and_Constraints: The review board exists to provide guidance and warnings rather than mandatory authorization; the process serves as a sounding board for team input; it is more structured than a simple workflow but less rigid than a formal workflow.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Formal Workflow
Core_Definition: A review process consisting of multiple approval stages involving both project approvers and an external review board.
Key_Actors: Project authors; project approvers; external review board; specialized review teams such as security, budget, or release teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process moves through multiple defined approval stages where each stage is documented with comments. Changes made during the process are tracked using revision numbers or dates. Specific examples of these stages include firewall or security reviews, project budget reviews, and release schedule reviews.
Rules_and_Constraints: Revision numbers or dates must be used to track changes throughout the workflow; each review stage must include notation of comments; the workflow is often determined by change control or compliance requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: firewall or security review
Core_Definition: A mandatory process or stage within a formal workflow used to evaluate a project for security concerns and protection of data.
Key_Actors: Project authors; security or firewall review boards; external review board; group managing centralized services.
Operational_Workflow: This review is conducted as a stage in a formal approval workflow or as a requirement for change control. Authors may be required to include specific sections in their design documents, such as a Security Concerns section or a Logging section that details data generation and retention policies for sign-off by centralized teams. Review boards may provide checklists to help authors prepare for the review.
Rules_and_Constraints: Authors should document issues regarding security related to the project such as protection of data; mandatory processes for project review at a company should have their own section on the design document template; content is prioritized over presentation during these approval processes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: project budget review
Core_Definition: A mandatory process or stage within a formal workflow used to evaluate the capital and operational costs associated with a project.
Key_Actors: Project authors; managers who sign off on resources; external review board.
Operational_Workflow: The budget review functions as an approval stage where managers evaluate the resources required for the project. It can be a formal requirement for certain approval processes within an organization.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design documents should include cost projections covering initial capital and operational costs plus a forecast of the costs to keep systems running; managers acting as approvers have a yes-or-no voice and typically only provide commentary for a no decision; content is more important than adherence to template formatting for these approvals.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: release schedule review
Core_Definition: A stage within a formal review workflow that evaluates the proposed timeline and sequence of project events.
Key_Actors: Project authors; external review board; project approvers.
Operational_Workflow: This review occurs as one of the multiple approval stages in a formal workflow to ensure the project timeline is acceptable. The schedule details which project events happen when in relation to each other.
Rules_and_Constraints: The design document must include a schedule section that provides a timeline of project events; revision numbers or dates must track any changes made to the schedule during the review process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: authors
Core_Definition: The individuals who contribute to the creation and content of a design document.
Key_Actors: Project leads; engineers; team members; early adopters; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Authors start by using a template to create a draft that describes a proposed or completed project; they record goals, designs, and alternatives while following a standardized format; the author lists their name in the document header along with the date and revision number; they then share the document for review via email or a central archive and revise the content based on feedback received until sign-off is achieved.
Rules_and_Constraints: Authors should be sincere and brief; they must include specific details such as algorithms and rationale for choices; they are encouraged to use standardized templates and update revision numbers like software releases; they should document both goals and non-goals to manage expectations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an author fails to use a template, they should be asked for feedback in a nonconfrontational way to improve the template’s adoption; authors may need to create different versions of a document such as a short template for initial proposals and a long template for complex projects.

Concept_Name: reviewers
Core_Definition: Individuals whose feedback is requested to improve the design and identify potential issues.
Key_Actors: Team members who may perform the project work; subject-matter experts; individuals from other groups impacted by the project; external review boards.
Operational_Workflow: Reviewers receive a draft design document via email or through a document archive; they examine the proposal for errors or improvements and provide meaningful comments to the author; in some workflows, they participate as part of an informal or formal review board to warn against repeating past mistakes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reviewers are listed in the document header; they are expected to provide constructive feedback that may require the author to make changes; while they have a voice in the process, they do not necessarily have a vote or the power to block a project if they are not directly affected by its success.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Reviewers may provide comments that require significant revisions to the document before it can move toward approval.

Concept_Name: approvers
Core_Definition: The individuals or entities whose formal sign-off is mandatory for a project to proceed.
Key_Actors: Internal customers; managers responsible for resources; audit and control personnel for compliance like SOX or HIPAA; specialized service teams such as security or privacy boards.
Operational_Workflow: Approvers review the design document to ensure it meets business, resource, and compliance requirements; they provide a yes-or-no decision on the proposal; if a project involves centralized services, specific sections like logging or security must be signed off by the relevant specialized teams before the project moves forward.
Rules_and_Constraints: Approvers are listed in the document header; they have the authority to block a project; commentary is typically only provided by an approver when a no decision is made; the approval process should be defined to avoid being a high-overhead activity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual organizational processes determine if approvals must be unanimous or if a majority can override negative approvals.

Concept_Name: subject-matter experts
Core_Definition: Individuals with specialized knowledge whose expertise is utilized to vet the technical or functional aspects of a design.
Key_Actors: Technical experts; experienced team members; specialists in specific systems or protocols.
Operational_Workflow: Subject-matter experts are typically added to the list of reviewers for a design document; they provide specialized feedback based on their area of expertise to ensure the design is sound and to prevent mistakes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Their involvement helps move knowledge from personal interaction into written documentation, making the team more efficient; they are consulted to ensure that the document serves as an effective reference for the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: privacy assessment
Core_Definition: A mandated review process used to evaluate issues regarding user privacy, anonymity, and the handling of personally identifiable information within a project design.
Key_Actors: Project authors; subject-matter experts; specialized groups managing centralized services; approvers responsible for privacy or personally identifiable information reviews.
Operational_Workflow: The author identifies privacy concerns and includes a dedicated section in the design document for privacy and personally identifiable information; the document details specific plans for handling such data in accordance with applicable regulations; the document is submitted for review where specialized approvers evaluate the justifications for data storage and retention before providing sign-off.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must include plans for handling personally identifiable information in accordance with applicable rules and regulations; may be listed under a general special constraints section or have its own dedicated section in the document template; specific details such as bytes of data generated and retention policies must be justified for certain centralized services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: audit and control processes
Core_Definition: Formal mechanisms and review stages mandated by an organization to ensure project designs meet regulatory obligations and follow internal change management standards.
Key_Actors: Project authors; change control boards; review boards; auditors for regulations such as SOX, HIPAA, PCI, or FISMA; internal customers; managers.
Operational_Workflow: A design document is created to capture the specifics of a project or change; the author includes mandatory sections for auditing controls and compliance details; the document is submitted to a formal change control process or an external review board for evaluation; each review stage is noted with comments and tracked using revision numbers or dates.
Rules_and_Constraints: Projects must include compliance and audit plans for meeting regulatory obligations under specific laws like SOX, HIPAA, PCI, or FISMA; approval workflows are determined by the specific change control or compliance requirements of the organization; approvers generally provide a yes-or-no voice rather than detailed commentary unless a negative decision is made.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: People on the perimeter of a project are considered reviewers and should not be able to block a project; individual processes determine whether approvals must be unanimous or if negative approvals can be overridden by a majority of positive approvals.

Concept_Name: review board
Core_Definition: A group of reviewers, approvers, or a mix of both that evaluates design documents to provide feedback, warn against past mistakes, or grant formal approval for a project.
Key_Actors: Project authors; internal or external reviewers; project approvers; specialized teams such as security, firewall, or budget review groups.
Operational_Workflow: The author submits a design document to the board; the board evaluates the document against established criteria or checklists; feedback is provided to the author; in formal workflows, the board notes each review stage with comments and tracks changes via revision numbers or dates until sign-off is achieved.
Rules_and_Constraints: A review board should publish a checklist of items it looks for to help authors prepare; the board may function within informal workflows to give feedback or formal workflows to provide mandatory stamps of approval; the workflow is often determined by change control or compliance requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Approvals might require a unanimous decision or a majority vote depending on the specific organizational process; approvers on a board have the power to block a project whereas reviewers generally provide input without blocking authority.

Concept_Name: review board checklist
Core_Definition: A published set of items and requirements that a review board uses to evaluate a design document.
Key_Actors: Review board members; design document authors.
Operational_Workflow: The review board develops and publishes a list of criteria; the author refers to this checklist while drafting the design document to ensure all requirements are met; the author uses the checklist to prepare for the formal review session.
Rules_and_Constraints: Publishing the checklist is a best practice that respects the time of both the board and the author; it is intended to reduce the number of deficient documents submitted for review.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: cultural change
Core_Definition: The gradual process of shifting organizational behavior and mindset to adopt new standards such as the use of design documents.
Key_Actors: Management; role models; early adopters; respected members of the organization; team members.
Operational_Workflow: Management provides easy-to-use templates and enlists early adopters; role models demonstrate the desired behavior by using templates strictly; successful adoption is highlighted and rewarded in meetings; management may eventually mandate design documents for specific approval processes like budget or security reviews.
Rules_and_Constraints: Adoption should be made easy rather than high-overhead; providing templates in multiple formats is the most important driver of adoption; management should reward early adopters with positive reinforcement; content should be prioritized over perfect adherence to presentation or format; the process should be debugged by asking nonconfrontational questions when people fail to use the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a template does not personally benefit the person introducing it, it is unlikely to be adopted by others; cultural change is noted to be a slow process because people change slowly.

============================================================
Chapter 15. Disaster Preparedness
Section: Chapter 15. Disaster Preparedness
============================================================

Concept_Name: disaster preparedness
Core_Definition: The organizational mindset and strategy of acknowledging that disasters and major outages are inevitable and adopting practices to handle them well while learning from failures. It involves preparing individuals, teams, and the entire organization through training, practice, and the use of effective response procedures.
Key_Actors: Individuals; teams; whole organizations; management; external media; specific roles like the Incident Commander; SRE teams.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations acknowledge that disasters are normal and expected; personnel undergo individual training through documentation and mentoring; teams build cohesion and find procedural problems through fire drills; the organization performs large-scale game day exercises to identify inter-team gaps and dependencies; incident recovery is coordinated using a model like the Incident Command System; each outage is followed by a postmortem report to identify lessons learned and implement preventive checks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must adopt a mindset that accepts outages and learns from them; the goal is to reduce the number of outages while recognizing that total elimination is unrealistic; outages should be treated as opportunities to learn rather than occasions to find someone to blame; systems should be designed to be antifragile by getting stronger when exposed to stress; risky procedures should be practiced in a controlled fashion to reduce risk; failover mechanisms must be exercised regularly to maintain confidence and functionality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When people fear being fired, they may hide mistakes and reduce transparency, which prevents the organization from learning; if a component is too perfect and rarely fails, it is better to artificially cause a failure to reestablish confidence; media pressure may demand someone be fired after a visible outage, but the best approach is to release a public postmortem focused on lessons learned rather than naming individuals.

Concept_Name: training
Core_Definition: The process of building confidence and competence in personnel through formal education and interactive game play to prepare them for handling oncall tasks and troubleshooting. It focuses on sharing best practices and maintaining technical skills within a team.
Key_Actors: Oncall personnel; team members; mentors; Master of Disaster (MoD); contestants; team leader or manager.
Operational_Workflow: Training begins with formal methods such as reading books, documentation, and mentoring or shadowing experienced staff; team members participate in the Wheel of Misfortune game where a Master of Disaster presents a scenario and a contestant explains how they would resolve it; the MoD acts as the system by providing responses or screenshots based on the contestant's actions; team members observe the process to learn and share best practices; managers observe the game to identify who needs private coaching or who has strong skills that can be used to help others.
Rules_and_Constraints: Training should be conducted frequently when adding new people or technology; the goal of training games is to educate workers, not to trick them or provide no-win situations; contestants should be explicit about their thought processes; suggestions for additional coaching should be made in private to avoid embarrassment; basic scenarios are used for new staff while nuanced problems are reserved for advanced participants.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In some versions of training games, a no-laptops rule is enforced, requiring contestants to describe their actions to a teammate who then performs the task on a projected screen to ensure thought processes are exposed.

Concept_Name: fire drills
Core_Definition: Preemptive tests where actual failures are triggered in production to exercise specific disaster recovery processes and build confidence in both the technology and the people. These drills aim to find bugs in procedures and ensure that failover mechanisms work as intended.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; individual team members; engineering teams; management; executives.
Operational_Workflow: A specific failover mechanism or process is identified for testing; a failure is intentionally triggered, such as powering off a machine or killing software; the team observes the response, fixes any discovered issues, and updates documentation; the drill is repeated until the process is perfected; drills may be run by the same people repeatedly to refine the process or by different people to ensure everyone on the team is capable of executing the task.
Rules_and_Constraints: Drills should start small and grow in scale over time; they should be performed in production when the engineering team is ready to respond; testing days must be chosen carefully to avoid major product releases or demonstrations; processes should be exercised whenever they have not been triggered by a real incident for a certain amount of time; management approval is required for tests that span multiple failure domains.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Drills carry the risk of catastrophic failure, which is why they are conducted when the full team is available to respond; if a failover mechanism has not been used for a long time, it may have become obsolete due to environmental changes; some managers may be difficult to convince because they prefer to avoid inducing problems, requiring the value of the drill to be tied to business goals and uptime.

Concept_Name: game day exercises
Core_Definition: Large-scale, multi-day, organization-wide disaster preparedness tests designed to test complex scenarios and rarely used interfaces between various teams and systems. These exercises include both technical and non-technical teams to identify unknown organizational dependencies.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; non-technical teams like communications and finance; executives; coordinators; proctors; tech subteam; coordination subteam; story teller; test controllers.
Operational_Workflow: Planning begins months in advance to set dates and design scripts; the organization is briefed on the upcoming scale of the test; a command center is established to serve as Mission Control; proctors execute specific test scenarios and observe team reactions; the tech team monitors for unforeseen issues and manages global outages; the story teller provides a narrative to keep participants engaged; results are reviewed to track organization-wide improvements; successful execution of team-level fire drills is generally required before participating in these larger exercises.
Rules_and_Constraints: High-level management approval and buy-in are essential; tests should be engineered to avoid user-visible disruptions when first starting the program; all test plans must be reviewed in advance by cross-functional experts to mitigate risk; the command center must be a physical location rather than virtual; identifying problems must be framed as a positive way to improve systems rather than a cause for blame.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems known to be ill-prepared may be pre-failed or whitelisted to be excluded from the event; large-scale network failures may reveal that emergency communication plans are inaccessible or that communication bridges have insufficient capacity; tests may reveal non-technical dependencies, such as remote sites being unable to approve emergency purchases without headquarters' consent.

Concept_Name: disasters
Core_Definition: Disasters are a normal and expected part of business in large distributed systems that requires organizations to adopt a mindset of preparedness and learning.
Key_Actors: Individual personnel; operations teams; entire organizations; management; Incident Command System roles.
Operational_Workflow: Preparation occurs at the individual level through formal training like books and mentoring alongside informal game play; teams use fire drills to build cohesion and fix procedural problems; organizations use game day exercises to find inter-team gaps and ensure readiness; recovery is coordinated through a framework such as the Incident Command System.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must avoid looking for someone to blame or fire when incidents occur; disasters should be treated as opportunities to learn from mistakes and improve the system; people must be trained until they can execute procedures with confidence.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Highly visible outages may cause management to face media pressure to fire individuals to demonstrate seriousness, though the text states this is counterproductive and leads to hidden mistakes.

Concept_Name: major outages
Core_Definition: Major outages are inevitable events in complex systems where the cost of total elimination is often more expensive than the cost of mitigation and preparedness.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; operations organizations; Incident Commanders; Public Information Officers; management.
Operational_Workflow: An outage exposes a weakness in the system which the team uses to identify places to add resilience or preventive checks; the incident is managed by an Incident Commander who delegates roles like operations and logistics; once resolved, the event is followed by a postmortem effort to document lessons learned.
Rules_and_Constraints: Root cause identification must not be used to apportion blame; failover mechanisms must be exercised regularly to maintain confidence in both the technology and the team; the amount of downtime is balanced with preparedness so situations are handled well.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system component is too perfect and does not fail naturally, engineers may intentionally cause an outage to ensure failover mechanisms still work and to prevent teams from writing code that assumes a dependency will never fail.

Concept_Name: antifragile
Core_Definition: Antifragile systems are those that become stronger when they are stressed, exposed to random behavior, or experience adversity.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing systems; technical teams; operations organizations; autonomous agents such as the Netflix Simian Army.
Operational_Workflow: Randomness and malfunctions are actively and frequently introduced into the system to trigger resiliency features; teams repeat risky procedures in controlled environments to remove risk and gain skill; artificial malfunctions are instigated during normal business hours to identify broken processes when the largest number of people are available to respond.
Rules_and_Constraints: Antifragility is not merely the ability to remain unchanged under stress but the ability to improve because of it; resiliency features must be considered expected components rather than extravagances; systems should fail often enough to maintain confidence in failover procedures without being detrimental to the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: postmortem report
Core_Definition: A report that focuses on lessons learned and identifies the additional checks put in place to prevent an incident from recurring.
Key_Actors: Team leads; management; the public; technical teams.
Operational_Workflow: After an incident is declared resolved, a lead individual initiates the postmortem effort; the report documents the root causes found and the specific technical or procedural improvements made; a public version may be released to communicate findings externally.
Rules_and_Constraints: The report must focus on the lessons learned and system improvements rather than naming or blaming individuals; it is used to educate the entire team to prevent the same mistake from being made twice; transparency is required to ensure root causes are not obscured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DevOps culture
Core_Definition: A mindset that accepts outages and failures as normal parts of business to be learned from rather than punished. It focuses on transparency and systematic improvement to build antifragile organizations.
Key_Actors: Individuals; teams; management; operations organizations; media.
Operational_Workflow: An outage occurs and is treated as an opportunity to learn; the team identifies the root cause without seeking to apportion blame; a postmortem report is created to identify weaknesses and add preventive checks; the team is educated on the findings; a public version of the report is released focusing on lessons learned and new safeguards.
Rules_and_Constraints: Management must avoid the urge to fire individuals after an incident; postmortem reports should not name individuals; transparency must be maintained to ensure root causes are not hidden; the focus must remain on improving the system and operational practices.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When highly visible outages occur, management may scramble to fire someone due to media pressure or to demonstrate seriousness; fear of being fired can cause individuals to hide mistakes and reduce transparency.

Concept_Name: antifragile systems
Core_Definition: Distributed computing systems that actually become stronger the more they are stressed or exposed to random behavior. They go beyond simple survival by using adversity to drive improvement.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing systems; operations teams; resiliency features; automated failure agents.
Operational_Workflow: Malfunctions are artificially and frequently triggered; randomness is introduced into the system; teams execute failovers and identify procedural or technical improvements; the system is reengineered to be more robust based on these experiences; the process is repeated to build skill and confidence.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resiliency features must be triggered frequently and actively; malfunctions should be instigated in a controlled way during normal business hours; if a component is too perfect, failure should be artificially caused to maintain confidence in failover mechanisms.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If failover mechanisms are not activated for long periods, they may become incompatible with system changes or the team may lose the skill to execute them.

Concept_Name: Resilient systems
Core_Definition: Systems that are able to survive stress and failure without breaking. They differ from antifragile systems because they remain unchanged rather than getting stronger in response to adversity.
Key_Actors: Technology; computer systems; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system encounters a failure or stressor; resiliency features are activated; the system continues to operate and survives the incident.
Rules_and_Constraints: Building reliable software on top of unreliable components is required; resiliency features should be seen as expected rather than an extravagance or a nice-to-have.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Fragile objects
Core_Definition: Objects or systems that break or change when exposed to stress. They are characterized by their inability to handle the unexpected.
Key_Actors: Computer systems; management; protection systems; traditional policies.
Operational_Workflow: These systems are surrounded by protection systems such as management policies or technical features designed to shield them; when an unexpected event occurs, the protection fails and the object breaks.
Rules_and_Constraints: Traditional thinking dictates that these systems must be protected; they are often associated with the avoidance of risky procedures rather than practicing them.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Fragile systems break specifically when the unexpected happens or when they are not treated gently.

Concept_Name: Hydra
Core_Definition: A mythical creature used as an example of an antifragile object because it reacts to stress by getting stronger.
Key_Actors: The mythical Hydra.
Operational_Workflow: When the Hydra loses one of its heads, it responds to this stress or adversity by growing two new heads in its place.
Rules_and_Constraints: The concept is used to illustrate the principle of antifragility where systems become stronger the more they are stressed or exposed to random behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: failover a database to a replica
Core_Definition: A resiliency procedure where a service is transitioned from a primary database to a backup copy or replica to maintain availability during an outage or maintenance.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; individual team members; database systems; replica systems; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: The team initiates the process by following a procedure book; pre-checks are performed such as verifying available disk space; the database is restored or the replica is updated to be current; the replica begins serving requests; the team verifies the service status is within the Service Level Agreement and monitors latency.
Rules_and_Constraints: The procedure should be documented in a procedure book; failover mechanisms must be exercised regularly whether they are automatic or manual to maintain confidence; if a component is too perfect, a failure should be artificially caused to ensure the mechanism still works; the process should be practiced until the risk is removed and the team is skilled at execution.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Inadequate disk space in the restore area can prevent a successful failover; outdated documentation or commands requiring new parameters can stall the process; a database growing larger than the allocated restore area over time; loss of queries that were in flight during the transition.

Concept_Name: malfunctions
Core_Definition: Failures or random behaviors in a system that stress resiliency features and provide opportunities for the team to gain skill and the system to become stronger.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing systems; operational teams; automated agents such as the Simian Army or Chaos Monkey.
Operational_Workflow: A malfunction occurs naturally or is instigated artificially; the team responds by executing failover or recovery procedures; the team identifies weaknesses exposed by the malfunction; improvements, preventive checks, and documentation updates are added to make the system more resilient or antifragile.
Rules_and_Constraints: Artificial malfunctions should be introduced in a controlled way to accelerate improvement; preferably triggered during normal business hours when the largest number of people are available to respond.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If malfunctions are not triggered in a controlled way, the team will only learn about system problems during real emergencies when staff may be asleep or unavailable.

Concept_Name: risky behavior
Core_Definition: Inherently dangerous actions, such as being careless, that should be avoided because the risk is built-in and cannot be improved.
Key_Actors: Personnel or individual team members.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Risky behavior must be avoided and should not be confused with risky procedures; unlike risky procedures, risky behavior cannot be reengineered or made less risky through practice.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: risky procedures
Core_Definition: Procedures such as a web service failover that can be improved and reengineered to make them less risky through repetition.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; management; personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Identify a procedure that is currently considered risky; perform the procedure repeatedly in a controlled fashion to identify flaws; update documentation and technical checks based on findings; continue repeating the process until the risk is removed and confidence is increased.
Rules_and_Constraints: Risky procedures should not be confused with risky behavior; risky behavior must be avoided while risky procedures should be repeated often until the risk is removed; focus on building antifragile practices rather than moats or protection systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If teams avoid risky procedures rather than practicing them, the risk actually increases because the process is unlikely to work during a real emergency.

Concept_Name: practice
Core_Definition: The technical term for improving a procedure through repetition to remove risk and build confidence.
Key_Actors: Individual team members; operational teams; organizations; Master of Disaster; proctors.
Operational_Workflow: Individuals participate in Wheel of Misfortune games to talk through scenarios; teams conduct fire drills by triggering actual failures in production to test responses; organizations hold Game Day or Disaster Recovery Testing exercises to find inter-team gaps; results from these exercises are used to update documentation and fix bugs in a continuous cycle of improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: Practice should be performed in a controlled way preferably during normal business hours when the largest number of people are available to respond; the goal is to educate and improve the system rather than to blame or trick participants; successful execution and procedural improvements make the system stronger and more antifragile.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a component is too perfect and never fails, it should be artificially failed to maintain confidence in the recovery process; without practice, knowledge can become concentrated among a few people and the team may lose the ability to execute the procedure effectively.

Concept_Name: failover mechanisms
Core_Definition: Technical systems or manual procedures designed to switch a service to a redundant component when a failure occurs to maintain uptime.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; operations teams; autonomous agents like the Netflix Simian Army.
Operational_Workflow: A failure is intentionally or accidentally triggered such as powering off a machine or killing software; the failover mechanism is activated to redirect traffic or service functions; the team monitors the transition to ensure it meets Service Level Agreements; any discovered bugs or configuration problems are fixed to ensure future reliability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Mechanisms must be exercised regularly whether they are automatic or manual; systems should ideally fail often enough to maintain confidence in the procedure but not so often as to be detrimental to the service; the system should aim for N + 2 redundancy to survive two simultaneous outages.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a failover mechanism is not activated for a long time, it may become incompatible with other system changes and fail when needed; manual failovers are prone to human error if the team is not accustomed to dealing with disasters under pressure.

Concept_Name: database failover
Core_Definition: A specific procedure involving switching a service to a database replica or restoring from backups to recover from a malfunction or site outage.
Key_Actors: Oncall responders; proctors; database technicians; database replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The responder identifies a database failure or receives a drill instruction; documentation is consulted to find the correct restore or switchover commands; the responder verifies pre-checks such as available disk space; the database is restored or failed over to a replica; the service is pointed to the new database instance and verified against the SLA.
Rules_and_Constraints: Documentation must be updated as new parameters or required pre-checks are discovered; restore areas must be included in resource estimation and allocation processes; available disk space for restores should be monitored to prevent failover failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failover can be hindered by insufficient disk space or obsolete documentation that does not account for database growth; initial attempts at failover may be rough and require team commentary to identify missing steps or monitoring gaps.

Concept_Name: pre-check
Core_Definition: A documented verification step performed before executing a procedure to ensure the system is in a state where the action can be safely and successfully completed.
Key_Actors: Team members; operations personnel; procedure books.
Operational_Workflow: A team member identifies a specific environmental condition that previously caused a failure during a procedure; the condition is checked manually or automatically before a major action such as a database failover; the check is then documented in the team's procedure book so all members are aware of it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Pre-checks should be explicitly documented in the procedure book; they should be shared with the entire team so that everyone knows to perform them.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a pre-check is not performed or documented, an emergency failover might fail due to environmental issues such as low disk space; if a pre-check is missing from the monitoring system, the team might not realize a condition is failing until a manual procedure is attempted.

Concept_Name: monitoring
Core_Definition: An automated system that tracks the health and status of IT components and issues alerts when specific conditions or thresholds are met.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; oncall person; tech team; technical systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system continuously tracks metrics such as disk space or latency; when a threshold is exceeded, the system sends an alert to the oncall person; the oncall person uses the alert to begin troubleshooting via dashboards and logs; during disaster tests, the tech team uses monitoring to ensure global outages do not affect systems outside the test scope.
Rules_and_Constraints: Monitoring should include rules to detect if critical resources are nearing capacity; it should be used to provide alerts that trigger incident response.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If critical conditions like disk space are not monitored, an emergency failover might be attempted on a system that is unable to handle it; during large-scale tests, monitoring is essential to detect if the test has caused unintended damage to systems that should not be affected.

Concept_Name: Wheel of Misfortune
Core_Definition: A training game played by operational teams to prepare individuals for oncall duties and share best practices through simulated disaster scenarios.
Key_Actors: Team members; contestant; Master of Disaster (MoD); team leader or manager.
Operational_Workflow: The entire team meets in a conference room; one person volunteers as the contestant and another as the Master of Disaster (MoD); the MoD explains an oncall situation or alert; the contestant explains the actions they would take and which logs or dashboards they would check; the MoD acts as the system by describing the results of those actions or showing screenshots; the round ends when the contestant resolves the issue or the MoD explains the solution.
Rules_and_Constraints: The goal is to educate rather than trick the contestant; scenarios should be appropriate for the contestant's experience level; no score or points are tallied as it is purely for training; some teams enforce a no-laptops rule for the contestant to force explicit communication of their thought process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a contestant reaches a dead end or guesses sufficiently, the MoD provides hints; managers use observations of the game to identify members who need private coaching or who have strong skills that can be used to help others.

Concept_Name: oncall
Core_Definition: A designated role where a team member is responsible for responding to system alerts and managing outages during a specific shift.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; Master of Disaster; proctors; Incident Commander; next oncall person.
Operational_Workflow: A team member is assigned an oncall shift; they receive alerts from the monitoring system; they troubleshoot the issue using operational documentation and logs; if a major incident occurs, the oncall person often acts as the initial Incident Commander; at the end of the shift, they hand over responsibilities and relevant information to the next oncall person.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall personnel should be trained through documentation, mentoring, and shadowing; they should be prepared to follow operational documents and bug reporting procedures; they are expected to manage the service within established SLAs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an oncall person is overwhelmed by a major incident, they should hand off the Incident Commander role to another qualified responder; during tests like DiRT, the oncall person may be required to simulate responses or handle artificial outages alongside regular duties.

Concept_Name: Master of Disaster (MoD)
Core_Definition: A person who volunteers during the Wheel of Misfortune game to present a simulated oncall incident and act as the voice of the system.
Key_Actors: The volunteer team member serving as the MoD; the contestant; the rest of the operational team.
Operational_Workflow: The MoD explains an oncall situation to a contestant; the MoD simulates the behavior of the system by responding to any actions the contestant takes; the MoD describes what is seen on dashboards or in logs when requested; the MoD provides hints if the contestant is stuck; the MoD ultimately concludes the round by either confirming the resolution or explaining the correct solution.
Rules_and_Constraints: The MoD must not try to trick the contestant or create impossible situations; the MoD should focus on education rather than defeating the contestant; hints should only be provided after sufficient guessing or when a dead end is reached; the MoD may use real or fake screenshots to add realism to the exercise.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a contestant reaches a dead end and cannot solve the problem, the MoD must explain the intended solution.

Concept_Name: contestant
Core_Definition: A team member who volunteers during the Wheel of Misfortune game to walk through the steps of resolving a simulated oncall alert.
Key_Actors: The contestant; the Master of Disaster (MoD); a teammate who may assist with driving a laptop.
Operational_Workflow: The contestant receives an initial alert message from the MoD; the contestant identifies which dashboards or logs they wish to check; the contestant explains their thought process and the troubleshooting steps they would take; the contestant describes the actions required to fix the identified problem; the contestant works until the issue is resolved or the MoD provides the solution.
Rules_and_Constraints: Basic scenarios are assigned to new contestants while advanced contestants receive nuanced problems to facilitate wider team learning; the contestant may be subject to a no laptops rule where they must describe their actions to a teammate who then displays the results for the audience; contestants must be explicit about their actions to expose their thought processes to the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the contestant is struggling or reaches a dead end, they may receive hints from the MoD; the team leader may identify a contestant as needing additional private coaching based on their performance during the game.

Concept_Name: monitoring system
Core_Definition: An automated technical framework used to track the health of services and alert personnel to outages or performance degradations.
Key_Actors: The monitoring system; technical systems and services; oncall personnel or game contestants.
Operational_Workflow: The system tracks metrics such as available disk space or service latency; it triggers alerts when specified thresholds are met; the system sends messages that serve as the starting point for oncall responses or Wheel of Misfortune scenarios; it identifies when a service is moving outside of its Service Level Agreement (SLA).
Rules_and_Constraints: The system should be configured to monitor critical resources such as free disk space to ensure failover procedures will succeed; rules should be established to detect when growth trends, such as database size, are approaching the limits of restore areas; monitoring should be capable of detecting if latency is growing at a rate that will eventually reach an unacceptable level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a component is too perfect and never triggers the monitoring system, artificial failures should be induced to maintain confidence in failover mechanisms; during large-scale tests like DiRT, monitoring is used to ensure that technical systems not intentionally part of the test remain unaffected.

Concept_Name: dashboard
Core_Definition: A visual interface that aggregates logs and performance data to provide oncall staff with a real-time view of system status.
Key_Actors: Oncall engineers; contestants in training games; technical services.
Operational_Workflow: The user identifies and accesses specific dashboards to verify the nature of an alert; the dashboard displays metrics such as regional outages or latency levels; the user observes the effects of automated transitions or manual interventions; the dashboard serves as a primary source of information for the MoD to describe system states during simulations.
Rules_and_Constraints: In certain training exercises, contestants may be restricted from accessing dashboards directly and must instead describe what they want to see to a teammate who drives the laptop; dashboards should be used to verify that services remain within the SLA during outages or tests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: log
Core_Definition: A technical record or dashboard output used by personnel to diagnose issues and monitor system behavior during an incident.
Key_Actors: Contestants in training exercises; Master of Disaster; oncall responders.
Operational_Workflow: During troubleshooting or training exercises, a responder identifies which log should be checked based on a received alert; the responder or training lead then reviews the information within the log to determine the nature of the problem and identify the necessary steps for resolution.
Rules_and_Constraints: In some training scenarios, contestants are prohibited from using laptops to access logs directly and must instead describe to a teammate what they wish to look at so the results can be projected for the group.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Kobayashi Maru
Core_Definition: A no-win situation designed to trick or defeat an opponent.
Key_Actors: Master of Disaster; contestants.
Operational_Workflow: This concept is identified as a scenario that should be strictly avoided during Wheel of Misfortune training sessions; the person leading the exercise instead focuses on scenarios that educate workers rather than defeating them.
Rules_and_Constraints: The goal of the training lead is to guide the contestant toward resolution or education, and they should never provide a situation that is impossible to win.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: disaster recovery plan
Core_Definition: A documented strategy and set of procedures intended to guide an organization through the response and recovery phases of a system failure or catastrophe.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; operations teams; proctors; executive management; cross-functional review teams.
Operational_Workflow: Proctors document the goals, scenarios, and scripts for a test; these test plans are submitted for review and approval by a cross-functional team of experts to mitigate unreasonable risks; the plan is then executed by preemptively triggering failures in production; the results are observed and used to find bugs in procedures and build team confidence.
Rules_and_Constraints: An untested plan is not considered a valid plan; risky procedures within the plan should be repeated and improved until the risk is removed; testing should be performed in a controlled environment during normal business hours when the largest number of people are available to respond.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Plans can become obsolete if failover mechanisms are not exercised regularly due to environment changes; systems known to be ill-prepared for an outage may be whitelisted or pre-failed so they do not negatively impact the usefulness of a larger drill.

Concept_Name: Service Testing
Core_Definition: A practical drill that involves picking individual, infrequently used failover mechanisms to test the readiness of both technology and personnel.
Key_Actors: Immediate team members; individual team members.
Operational_Workflow: A team identifies a specific failover mechanism and triggers a failure by powering off a machine, shutting down software, or disconnecting a network; the team observes the response to identify broken processes, configuration problems, or documentation gaps; the drill is repeated by the same people to refine the process or by different people to ensure every team member can execute the procedure.
Rules_and_Constraints: These tests should be contained so only the immediate team is required to respond; drills should be performed on days when no major sales demonstrations or product releases are scheduled; tests should not be conducted on the day people return from or leave for vacation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a system component is too reliable and rarely fails on its own, an artificial failure must be induced to maintain confidence in the failover procedure; these tests often reveal that previous environmental changes have made a recovery process obsolete.

Concept_Name: Google Chubby Outage Drills
Core_Definition: Intentional outages of a service created to ensure that dependent teams do not write code that assumes a service is perfect.
Key_Actors: The Google Chubby team; other engineering teams whose code relies on the Chubby service.
Operational_Workflow: The Chubby team monitors the service for downtime; if a month passes without at least a few minutes of downtime, the team intentionally takes the service down for five minutes; the outage schedule is announced well in advance to give teams time to prepare.
Rules_and_Constraints: Outages must occur if the service has been too reliable to maintain healthy coding practices; schedules must be publicized beforehand.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The very first planned outage was cancelled shortly before it began because many critical projects reported they could not survive the test; teams were granted a one-time 30-day delay to fix their code before the drills became a permanent fixture.

Concept_Name: Chubby
Core_Definition: A global lock service used within Google that has an excellent reputation for reliability.
Key_Actors: Google internal engineering teams; the Chubby service itself.
Operational_Workflow: The service provides locking mechanisms for distributed systems; it is used by various teams across the company as a foundational component for their own services.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service must maintain a high level of reliability but should not be assumed to be perfect; it must experience occasional downtime to discourage bad coding practices in dependent systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Because of its reputation for reliability, teams often make the mistake of assuming it cannot fail, leading to big problems for their services during even small Chubby outages.

Concept_Name: Random Testing
Core_Definition: A strategy of selecting random machines or other failure domains and causing them to fail to verify that a system remains operational.
Key_Actors: Management; autonomous agents such as the Netflix Simian Army; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: Tests may be conducted on a scheduled basis or continuously; autonomous agents like Chaos Monkey wake up hourly to terminate random virtual machines; more intensive tests like Chaos Gorilla simulate network partitions or total datacenter failures and are run manually; agents like Latency Monkey induce delays in API calls to simulate degradation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems can be programmed to opt out of certain random tests; large-scale random tests should be approved by management due to the high risk involved; the focus is on improving the ability to respond to inevitable problems in a controlled environment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These tests carry a larger risk at the start because they span many failure domains; recovery from large-scale simulated failures like those caused by Chaos Gorilla requires sophisticated control systems to rebalance load.

Concept_Name: failure domains
Core_Definition: The specific technical boundaries or segments of a system, such as individual machines or entire datacenters, that can experience a malfunction.
Key_Actors: Virtual machines; physical machines; datacenters; network connections.
Operational_Workflow: Engineers identify different levels of failure domains to test system resiliency; random testing agents are assigned to target specific domains, such as Chaos Monkey targeting virtual machines and Chaos Gorilla targeting datacenters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing across these domains should be used to improve the system's ability to handle the unexpected; testing that spans multiple large failure domains requires management approval and careful coordination.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large-scale failures in a major failure domain, such as a datacenter, can cause massive damage and require complex manual or automatic interventions to restore service balance.

Concept_Name: Netflix Simian Army
Core_Definition: A collection of various autonomous agents called monkeys that are each programmed to create different types of outages. This system is designed to improve resilience and maximize availability by inducing malfunctions artificially.
Key_Actors: Netflix; autonomous agents or monkeys; Chaos Monkey; Chaos Gorilla; Latency Monkey.
Operational_Workflow: The army is composed of multiple agents that are added as the system grows; individual agents like Chaos Monkey operate hourly to terminate components; other agents are used for simulated service degradation; larger agents are run manually during scheduled tests.
Rules_and_Constraints: The army is used to actively trigger resiliency features by introducing randomness into the environment; it is intended to help the organization become antifragile by strengthening the system through stress.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain machines within the environment are permitted to opt out of the Simian Army activities entirely.

Concept_Name: Chaos Monkey
Core_Definition: An autonomous agent within the Netflix Simian Army that terminates random virtual machines. Its purpose is to verify that the distributed system remains operational despite the loss of individual virtual instances.
Key_Actors: Chaos Monkey; virtual machines; Netflix Simian Army.
Operational_Workflow: The agent wakes up once every hour; it selects a random virtual machine from the environment based on different probabilities; it terminates the selected machine to simulate a failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The agent is programmed to select different machines based on specific probabilities; it is used as a form of random, continuous testing for individual component failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual machines have the option to opt out of being targeted by Chaos Monkey.

Concept_Name: Chaos Gorilla
Core_Definition: An agent in the Simian Army that simulates a total failure or network partition of an entire datacenter. This test is designed to verify that the system can handle large-scale outages through load rebalancing.
Key_Actors: Chaos Gorilla; entire datacenters; control systems.
Operational_Workflow: The agent simulates a network partition or a total failure affecting a whole datacenter failure domain; this causes massive damage to the system; recovery is managed through sophisticated control systems that rebalance the load to other available infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Because it causes such significant impact, Chaos Gorilla is run manually as part of scheduled tests rather than automatically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: network partition
Core_Definition: A failure scenario where a network connection to a specific failure domain, such as a datacenter, is severed or disabled. This prevents any network traffic from moving in or out of that location.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; network connections; automated redirection systems; Chaos Gorilla; technicians.
Operational_Workflow: A partition may be caused by actual events or simulated using network filtering or disabling physical connections; the system responds by automatically redirecting web requests to other functioning datacenter locations; the service continues to operate while traffic is redistributed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems are often designed with redundancy, such as N plus 2, to survive simultaneous outages including partitions; transitions should occur with minimal loss of in-flight queries and stay within service level agreements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A total network failure can render standard communication tools like chat rooms unusable, requiring separate emergency communication plans; simultaneous partitions can cause latency to increase to levels that barely stay within service level agreements.

Concept_Name: Latency Monkey
Core_Definition: An autonomous agent and member of the Netflix Simian Army that induces artificial delays in API calls.
Key_Actors: The Netflix Simian Army; API calls; distributed systems.
Operational_Workflow: The agent is programmed to create specific kinds of outages by injecting delays into API calls to simulate service degradation; this allows teams to verify how the system handles increased latency and remains running.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is used as part of a random testing strategy to test a wide variety of potential failures across different failure domains.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: service degradation
Core_Definition: A state of reduced system performance or increased latency that is artificially induced for testing purposes.
Key_Actors: Latency Monkey; API calls.
Operational_Workflow: The state is simulated by Latency Monkey inducing artificial delays in API calls; this is done to test the resilience of the system and its ability to handle slow responses rather than total failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: organizational dependencies
Core_Definition: The hidden or rarely exercised reliance that different teams and systems have on one another to function or recover from a disaster.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; non-technical teams such as communications, logistics, and finance; secondary coverage personnel; executive management.
Operational_Workflow: These dependencies are identified during large-scale Game Day or DiRT exercises; the process involves testing complex scenarios and rarely used interfaces between teams to find gaps; for example, verifying if secondary coverage personnel can run a service without primary team assistance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should be proficient at their own individual fire drills before testing for inter-team dependencies; exercises to find these dependencies require significant planning, infrastructure, and high-level management approval.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Examples discovered in the text include remote SRE teams depending on headquarters for assistance; production processes depending on source code repositories not intended to be in the critical path; and emergency purchase approvals requiring consent from people at a specific physical headquarters.

Concept_Name: Google’s Disaster Recovery Testing (DiRT)
Core_Definition: Large-scale, multi-day, organization-wide disaster preparedness exercises that focus on testing complex interactions between teams and rarely tested system interfaces.
Key_Actors: Site Reliability Engineering teams; proctors; test controllers; tech team; coordination team; story teller; event planners; management.
Operational_Workflow: Planning begins months in advance where teams select proctors to design scenarios and scripts; a central command center or Mission Control is established as a physical location; during the event, proctors call oncall responders to walk through or execute tests; test controllers monitor for unforeseen issues and can halt tests if necessary; a story teller narrates a disaster scenario to maintain engagement; results are recorded in post-exercise documents and bugs are filed and tracked.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should be performing well at their own individual fire drills before participating; all test plans must be submitted for review and approval by a cross-functional team of experts; risky or new tests should be performed in a sandbox or via simulation; if a system is known to be ill-prepared, it is whitelisted or pre-failed rather than included in the active test.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems known to be unprepared are excluded to avoid redundant learning; real accidental outages can occur resulting in revenue loss; emergency communication bridges can become unusable due to hold music flooding or caller limits; physical headquarters staff can create a DoS flood in a cafeteria if left with no tasks during a test.

Concept_Name: customer-visible outages
Core_Definition: Major service interruptions that are apparent to the public or users of a system and often attract media attention or management scrutiny.
Key_Actors: Customers; management; the media; CEO or president; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: An incident occurs that impacts the service; management may face pressure from the media to identify someone to blame; the organization should respond by releasing a public postmortem report focusing on lessons learned and preventive measures rather than naming individuals.
Rules_and_Constraints: Accept that eliminating all downtime is unrealistic and more expensive than mitigation; treat outages as opportunities to identify system weaknesses and educate the team; avoid a culture of blame which leads to hidden mistakes and reduced transparency; focus postmortem reports on technical checks and procedural improvements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Large-scale organizational tests like Google's DiRT are more likely to lead to customer-visible outages than smaller team-based exercises; certain systems may be whitelisted or pre-failed if it is known they will not survive a test to avoid unnecessary disruption.

Concept_Name: loss of revenue
Core_Definition: The financial impact resulting from service downtime during a real disaster or as a side effect of large-scale disaster preparedness testing.
Key_Actors: Management; engineering teams; non-technical teams such as finance.
Operational_Workflow: High-level disaster exercises like Game Day are conducted to find organizational gaps; if these tests impact production systems unexpectedly, they can cause a temporary cessation of business transactions or service availability leading to a drop in income.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must weigh the potential for revenue loss against the value realized through learning and preparedness; large-scale testing requires high-level management approval and buy-in due to the risk of financial impact; tests should be planned and coordinated to reduce risk.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: To minimize risk, initial tests should be engineered to be safe and avoid user-visible disruptions until the methodology and tracking systems are proven.

Concept_Name: Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)
Core_Definition: Specialized engineering teams, such as those at Google, responsible for managing the reliability of services through oncall duties and disaster preparedness exercises.
Key_Actors: SRE teams located in different geographical regions; oncall responders; disaster recovery coordinators.
Operational_Workflow: SRE teams handle service operations and alerts; they participate in disaster recovery testing (DiRT) to ensure they can manage services independently during failures; they document procedures in operational docs and update them based on findings from drills.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams should be proficient in their own fire drills before participating in larger company-wide exercises; SRE culture should prioritize identifying root causes over apportioning blame; knowledge should be shared across the team to avoid concentration among a few individuals.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Testing may reveal that remote SRE teams are improperly dependent on headquarters for technical assistance or emergency approvals such as fuel purchases for generators.

Concept_Name: source code repository
Core_Definition: A system for managing software code that can become an unexpected critical dependency for production systems.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; production systems; test coordinators.
Operational_Workflow: As part of disaster testing, an organization may intentionally cut off access to the repository for a period; teams then observe which production processes fail to function without it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Production systems should not rely on systems that are not intended to be in the critical path of operations; active testing is often the only way to identify these hidden dependencies as an organization grows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: production processes
Core_Definition: The operational workflows and methods used to run services which may inadvertently depend on systems not intended for high availability.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service delivery platforms; technical systems such as source code repositories.
Operational_Workflow: Production processes typically function as the daily routine for maintaining services; during disaster testing such as DiRT exercises, these processes are scrutinized to identify hidden dependencies; if a process is found to rely on a non-critical system like a source code repository, bug reports are filed to remove that system from the critical path.
Rules_and_Constraints: Production systems should not rely on systems that were not built to be in the critical path of production; processes must be actively tested to reveal dependencies that are not explicitly known.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: As an organization grows, production processes often develop unknown dependencies that are only discovered during active testing or real failures; an example includes production processes failing when access to a source code repository is cut off.

Concept_Name: continuous delivery system
Core_Definition: A component of a service delivery platform that provides automated testing and deployment for software services.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service delivery platforms.
Operational_Workflow: The system handles the automated pipeline for service delivery and provides a baseline level of testing for new releases; teams may rely on this system for standard reliability checks before considering more complex disaster preparedness exercises.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Operations teams may resist participating in larger disaster exercises like DiRT because they believe the continuous delivery system provides sufficient testing.

Concept_Name: corporate emergency communications plans
Core_Definition: Documented procedures and mechanisms used to facilitate communication across the organization during total network failures or major disasters.
Key_Actors: All employees; emergency responders; management.
Operational_Workflow: When standard communication tools like chat rooms are unavailable, individuals locate the emergency plan; they follow the instructions to join a designated communication channel such as a phone bridge; the plan is periodically tested via spot-checks and fire drills to ensure personnel have the necessary information.
Rules_and_Constraints: The plan must be accessible without the use of the corporate network; employees are required to keep the correct information with them to execute the plan during an emergency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial exercises, it is common for only a small fraction of the staff to be able to find and successfully execute the plan; the plan can fail if technical limitations of the backup communication tools are not understood.

Concept_Name: phone bridge
Core_Definition: A teleconference line used as a backup communication tool when primary network-based communication methods fail.
Key_Actors: Emergency callers; disaster recovery personnel; bridge administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Participants find the bridge details in the corporate emergency communications plan; they dial into the bridge to coordinate disaster response; administrators monitor the bridge to ensure it remains clear for essential communication.
Rules_and_Constraints: The bridge must have a known caller capacity; administrators must have the technical ability to remove callers from the bridge.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The bridge can become unusable if a participant puts the call on hold and floods the line with hold music; the bridge may fail if the number of callers exceeds its technical limit, such as a 40-caller maximum.

Concept_Name: DoS flood
Core_Definition: An event where a resource or communication channel is overwhelmed by an excessive volume of activity or unintended signals.
Key_Actors: Headquarters staff; cafeteria staff; emergency phone bridge callers; hold music systems.
Operational_Workflow: In one instance described in the text, employees at headquarters left idle by disaster tests congregated in the cafeteria simultaneously; in another instance, a caller placed an emergency communication bridge on hold, which filled the channel with hold music and prevented others from using it.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems such as emergency communication bridges should have the capability to kick participants off to prevent the channel from being rendered unusable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hold music can inadvertently flood a conference bridge and cause a total communication failure during a simulated or real disaster.

Concept_Name: Global tests
Core_Definition: Major disaster preparedness events initiated by event planners that involve large-scale disruptions such as taking down a datacenter.
Key_Actors: Event planners; tech team; coordination team; multiple organizational teams.
Operational_Workflow: Event planners initiate a major event like a datacenter shutdown; the tech team is responsible for causing the outages and monitoring them to ensure they do not go awry; the coordination team manages communication and ensures there are no conflicting test plans.
Rules_and_Constraints: All global test plans must be submitted in advance for review and approval by a cross-functional team of experts; global tests should generally be conducted only after individual teams have successfully performed their own fire drills.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Test controllers maintain the authority to halt all global tests immediately if something goes massively wrong or results in an unintended catastrophic situation.

Concept_Name: Team tests
Core_Definition: Disaster preparedness exercises that are initiated by individual teams to evaluate their specific services and systems.
Key_Actors: Individual teams; proctors.
Operational_Workflow: Individual teams start tests to identify gaps in their specific documentation or configurations; proctors design the tests by creating scenarios and scripts; teams use these exercises to build confidence and prepare for larger company-wide events.
Rules_and_Constraints: Test plans must be submitted for expert review to mitigate risk; teams must ensure their individual processes are well-practiced at this level before participating in larger exercises like DiRT.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a team has not sufficiently practiced their individual processes through these tests, larger organization-wide exercises are unlikely to be successful.

Concept_Name: coordinator
Core_Definition: A person with technical and project management experience responsible for the planning and oversight of large-scale disaster preparedness events.
Key_Actors: A designated individual with technical and project management expertise; DiRT test coordinators.
Operational_Workflow: The coordinator manages the many moving parts of a large event over several months of planning; they implement and oversee methodology and tracking systems; following the event, they track organization-wide improvements and review outcomes.
Rules_and_Constraints: The coordinator must be able to dedicate a sufficient amount of time to the project; in very large organizations, this role may require a dedicated full-time position.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: proctors
Core_Definition: Individuals responsible for designing and executing specific tests during disaster preparedness exercises.
Key_Actors: Proctors; oncall persons; test controllers in the command center.
Operational_Workflow: Teams select proctors months before a Game Day or DiRT event; proctors design tests by documenting the goal, scenario, and script to be followed; during the actual event, individuals in the command center call proctors to tell them to start their tests; the proctor then executes the test by calling an oncall person to simulate a situation or by actively taking down a service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Proctors must document the test goal, scenario, and script long before the event day; they are responsible for the execution of the test during the actual exercise.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Proctors may identify documentation gaps or technical bugs during an exercise and are responsible for filing bug reports or providing feedback for improvements.

Concept_Name: test scenarios
Core_Definition: Descriptions of potential failure situations used to train individuals or test organizational readiness.
Key_Actors: Master of Disaster (MoD); contestants; proctors; engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: In individual training like Wheel of Misfortune, the MoD explains an oncall situation to a contestant who then explains how they would work the issue; in larger exercises, proctors construct scenarios months in advance as part of the test design; during the event, these scenarios are narrated or triggered to observe how the team responds to the specific failure described.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scenarios should not be designed to trick the contestant or provide no-win situations; the goal of the scenario is to educate and find gaps rather than defeat the participant; basic scenarios are used for new staff while nuanced problems are for advanced contestants.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If certain systems are known to be ill-prepared and will not survive the scenario, they are pre-failed and marked as failing the test rather than being actively involved.

Concept_Name: script
Core_Definition: A documented sequence of steps or communication prompts used by a proctor to guide the execution of a test.
Key_Actors: Proctors; oncall individuals.
Operational_Workflow: The script is developed during the planning phase months before a major exercise; it outlines exactly how the proctor will interact with the test subject, such as calling an oncall person and having them simulate a database restore; during the actual test, the proctor follows this script to ensure the test is conducted according to the documented goal.
Rules_and_Constraints: The script must be documented along with the goal and scenario during the planning phase; the proctor must follow the script during execution.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: risk mitigation
Core_Definition: Strategies and procedures used to reduce the dangers associated with practicing risky procedures and conducting large-scale failure tests.
Key_Actors: Management; cross-functional team of experts; tech team; coordination team.
Operational_Workflow: Risk is mitigated by practicing procedures in a controlled fashion and repeating risky procedures until the risk is removed; for large events, all test plans must be submitted in advance for review and approval by a cross-functional team of experts who check for unreasonable risks; riskier tests never done before are conducted in sandbox environments or simulations; during live events, a tech team monitors global outages to ensure things do not go awry.
Rules_and_Constraints: Management approval is required for tests that span many failure domains; tests should be performed during normal business hours when the largest number of people are available to respond; failover mechanisms must be exercised regularly to maintain confidence in their performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Test controllers have the authority to halt all tests if something goes massively wrong; systems that are known to be ill-prepared are whitelisted or excluded from filtering to prevent them from causing broader issues during the event.

Concept_Name: sand-box environment
Core_Definition: A restricted and isolated testing environment used for executing disaster recovery tests that are considered high-risk because they have never been performed before.
Key_Actors: Proctors who design the tests; a cross-functional team of experts who review and approve test plans.
Operational_Workflow: Test proctors document the goals and scenarios for new disaster recovery exercises; a cross-functional team of experts reviews these plans to identify unreasonable risks; tests that are identified as high-risk or never-before-attempted are assigned to be conducted within the sand-box environment or through simulation rather than on live production systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: All test plans must be submitted in advance for review; the sand-box is specifically reserved for mitigating the risk of tests that have not been previously validated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: simulation
Core_Definition: A testing method where a disaster scenario is verbally or technically enacted without necessarily disrupting live production services to identify gaps in documentation and knowledge.
Key_Actors: Master of Disaster (MoD) who facilitates the scenario; proctors who design and execute the script; contestants or engineers who respond to the hypothetical failure.
Operational_Workflow: A facilitator or proctor presents a specific alert or failure message to an engineer; the engineer describes the dashboards or logs they would check and the actions they would take to resolve the issue; the proctor provides feedback or screenshots representing how the system would respond to those actions; any discovered documentation errors or resource shortages are recorded as bugs to be fixed.
Rules_and_Constraints: In games like Wheel of Misfortune, simulations may involve a no laptops rule where contestants must describe their actions to teammates who project results; scenarios should be realistic and educational rather than designed to trick the participant.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-risk tests that have never been performed are mandated to be done through simulation or in a sand-box environment to prevent accidental production damage.

Concept_Name: Pre-fail
Core_Definition: The administrative act of marking a system as having failed a disaster test in advance because it is already known to be incapable of surviving the scenario.
Key_Actors: Cross-functional team of experts; event planners; technical teams responsible for the specific systems.
Operational_Workflow: During the planning phase of a large-scale disaster exercise, experts evaluate the preparedness of various systems; systems identified as ill-prepared are designated as pre-failed; these systems are then whitelisted from the actual test activities to ensure they continue to receive service while the event proceeds.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems should only be pre-failed if there is nothing new to be learned by involving them in the live event; pre-failed systems must be excluded from the technical filters or disruptions used during the test.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: tech team
Core_Definition: A specialized subteam within a large-scale disaster exercise responsible for the technical integrity, grading, and execution of global tests.
Key_Actors: Technical experts; coordination team; test controllers; proctors.
Operational_Workflow: The team designs global disaster tests and evaluates individual team test plans based on quality, impact, and risk; during the actual event, they trigger the global outages and monitor the environment to ensure systems do not fail in unexpected ways; they manage any unforeseen technical issues caused by the exercises from a physical command center.
Rules_and_Constraints: The tech team must collaborate with a coordination team to avoid conflicting tests; they are responsible for monitoring the progress of tests and their effects on systems that should remain unaffected.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a test goes massively wrong, the technical controllers within the command center have the authority to halt all tests.

Concept_Name: coordination team
Core_Definition: A subteam responsible for the logistical and administrative planning, scheduling, and execution of large-scale disaster preparedness exercises.
Key_Actors: Coordination team members; tech subteam; management; corporate-wide personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The team plans and schedules the event months in advance; verifies that all preparation work is complete; manages budgets; and handles communication with management and the entire corporation during the event. It works closely with the tech team to prevent conflicting tests and ensure the event runs according to the established timeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: The coordinator should have both technical and project management experience and be able to dedicate significant time to the project; at a large scale, this may require a dedicated full-time position even if the event is annual.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: command center
Core_Definition: A physical location that serves as the central hub for overseeing and managing organization-wide disaster recovery exercises.
Key_Actors: Tech subteam; coordination subteam; proctors; test controllers; story teller.
Operational_Workflow: Participants reside in this location during the event to initiate tests by calling proctors; monitor the progress and technical effects of tests; and manage unexpected issues. The story teller uses this hub to distribute narrative updates to the rest of the organization to maintain engagement.
Rules_and_Constraints: The command center must be a physical location because relying on virtual meeting spaces is considered too risky during major disaster testing; for large organizations, this requirement involves a significant amount of travel for participants.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During specific simulations, such as an earthquake making headquarters unavailable, participants in the command center may be forbidden from communicating with the rest of the company to test the autonomy of remote sites.

Concept_Name: Mission Control
Core_Definition: A functional designation for the command center when it is actively overseeing the complex, moving parts of a large-scale disaster exercise.
Key_Actors: Tech subteam; coordination subteam.
Operational_Workflow: It functions as the primary point of coordination where subteams monitor global outages and team-specific tests; ensuring that the exercise does not cause unintended or unmanageable disruptions to systems that should not be affected.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text treats Mission Control as the operational mode of the physical command center; requiring the physical presence of key subteams to manage the risks associated with large-scale testing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Test controllers
Core_Definition: Individuals located within the command center who are responsible for the real-time monitoring and management of disaster exercises.
Key_Actors: Test controllers; proctors; technical systems.
Operational_Workflow: They monitor the progress of ongoing tests and observe the effects on technical systems; handle unexpected issues as they arise; and provide global communication regarding the status of the event. They also interact with proctors to signal the start or progression of specific test scenarios.
Rules_and_Constraints: Test controllers have the specific authority to halt all tests if something goes massively wrong during the exercise.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Test controllers may introduce secret delays to the start time of an exercise to keep teams alert; they may also coordinate with proctors to introduce specific narrative elements or additional stressors at designated times.

Concept_Name: story teller
Core_Definition: A specific role within the command center during a Game Day or DiRT exercise responsible for creating and narrating a fictional disaster scenario.
Key_Actors: The story teller; individuals in the command center; event participants; proctors.
Operational_Workflow: The story teller concocts a narrative that is just unrealistic enough for people to recognize it as a test; they narrate the disaster as it unfolds; they distribute daily updates that move the story forward via email or old-time radio broadcasts; the last bulletin resolves the story to signal that the test is over.
Rules_and_Constraints: The story should involve unrealistic elements such as zombie attacks or evil geniuses to ensure participants know it is a test; the role is intended to counteract the drudgery of the test and make people look forward to future events.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: bug report
Core_Definition: A formal record filed during or after a disaster exercise to document identified issues such as documentation gaps, outdated parameters, or technical failures.
Key_Actors: Proctors; engineers being tested; test controllers; technical teams responsible for fixes.
Operational_Workflow: A proctor identifies a problem during an exercise such as a command requiring new parameters or insufficient disk space; the proctor files a bug report in the company database; the report is tagged with the specific exercise name for tracking; notifications are sent to relevant parties; the bugs are monitored over several months to ensure they receive attention and are resolved.
Rules_and_Constraints: Bug reports should focus on lessons learned and additional checks rather than naming individuals; tags like DiRT2011 are used to watch the progress of fixes; identifying a root cause is intended for system improvement rather than apportioning blame.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some bugs may take more time to resolve than others, requiring their inclusion in long-term resource planning or the creation of new monitoring rules.

Concept_Name: resource estimation and allocation process
Core_Definition: A quarterly planning process used to identify, request, and assign necessary technical resources such as storage space or capacity for production systems.
Key_Actors: Engineering teams; management; technical staff responsible for system resources.
Operational_Workflow: An engineer or proctor identifies a resource deficiency during an exercise such as a restore area being too small for a grown database; the team identifies the need for a long-term fix; the requirement is added to the quarterly resource estimation and allocation process for official planning and fulfillment.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process is used for improvements that take more time than a simple documentation fix; it is often paired with adding monitoring rules to detect when system sizes approach their allocated limits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: post-exercise document
Core_Definition: A document used to record the events, outcomes, and follow-up actions resulting from a specific disaster recovery test or exercise.
Key_Actors: The engineer or individual being tested; proctors; test controllers.
Operational_Workflow: After an exercise is completed the participant receives an email with a link to the document; the participant updates the document with a summary of what happened during the test; links to any filed bugs or feature requests are added to the document; additional ideas for improving the process are documented within the file.
Rules_and_Executive: The document serves as a record of the test and helps various parties watch tagged bugs to ensure they get attention; it provides a structured way to capture improvements beyond the immediate technical fixes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: feature requests
Core_Definition: Formal documentation and submissions to a bug database used to suggest improvements or new functionality for a system based on lessons learned during disaster recovery exercises.
Key_Actors: Oncall engineers; test participants; bug database systems.
Operational_Workflow: An engineer participating in a disaster recovery exercise like DiRT identifies potential improvements to a process or system; the engineer documents these ideas; the engineer files the requests as specific entries in the organization's bug database; these requests are often tagged with the specific exercise name for tracking and future review.
Rules_and_Constraints: Feature requests are generated as a constructive outcome of an outage or drill to ensure that the system becomes more resilient or easier to manage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SLA
Core_Definition: A service level agreement that defines the acceptable performance boundaries, such as latency and availability, that a service must maintain even during failures or maintenance.
Key_Actors: Oncall engineers; monitoring systems; dashboards; customers; automated redirection systems.
Operational_Workflow: An outage occurs and the system automatically redirects web requests to remaining healthy locations; the oncall engineer monitors the service dashboard to track performance metrics like latency; the engineer verifies if the service is performing within the agreed upon boundaries; if the metrics reach an unacceptable level or grow at a rate that will soon violate the agreement, the monitoring system triggers a page to the engineer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems are often required to stay within SLA even when multiple datacenters are down; the transition during a failure should be flawless and queries lost must stay within the specified limits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service remains within the SLA during a drill but the performance is too close for comfort, engineers may file bugs to improve capacity or efficiency.

Concept_Name: N + 2 redundancy
Core_Definition: A redundancy standard where a service is designed to survive the simultaneous failure or shutdown of two separate components or datacenters.
Key_Actors: Service engineering teams; datacenters; automated load balancing systems.
Operational_Workflow: A service is distributed across multiple datacenters to provide high availability; one datacenter is taken down for planned maintenance; while that maintenance is in progress, a second datacenter experiences an unplanned outage such as a power or network failure; the remaining operational datacenters must have sufficient capacity to handle the redistributed traffic and maintain the service SLA.
Rules_and_Constraints: This standard is implemented because systems must be able to withstand an unplanned emergency during a scheduled maintenance window.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Incident Command System (ICS)
Core_Definition: A standardized and scalable management framework used to coordinate the response to major outages and emergencies by defining clear roles and responsibilities.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; Operations Section Chief; Logistics Section Chief; Planning Section Chief; Admin/Finance Section Chief; Public Information Officer; Liaison Officer; Safety Officer.
Operational_Workflow: The first qualified responder to arrive at an incident automatically assumes the role of Incident Commander; the Incident Commander initially handles all roles but delegates subcommand functions as more people arrive to help; the Incident Commander and Operations lead are separated into different individuals to ensure one person maintains a big-picture view while the other focuses on technical fixes; the team develops and continually refines an Incident Action Plan to set objectives and communication procedures; the Incident Commander provides regular status updates through the Public Information Officer; once the situation is normal, the Incident Commander explicitly terminates the process and initiates a postmortem.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be only one Incident Commander at any time; every person reports to only one supervisor; supervisors should ideally manage three to five direct reports and no more than seven; management is by objective where supervisors tell people what to do but not how to do it; command transfer must be an explicit and thoughtful process; common terminology must be used across all participating teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In small incidents the Incident Commander may handle all roles alone; if the Incident Commander becomes tired they must explicitly hand off the role to a relief person; multi-team incidents can cause confusion if different organizations have adapted ICS differently, requiring joint drills to align procedures.

Concept_Name: Incident Commander (IC)
Core_Definition: The single individual responsible for managing the overall response to an incident, setting objectives, and delegating authority to subcommand systems.
Key_Actors: The first qualified responder; the oncall person; the team lead; specialized officers such as the Public Information Officer and Liaison Officer; subcommand systems including Operations, Logistics, and Planning.
Operational_Workflow: The first qualified responder to arrive automatically assumes the role; the IC assesses the situation and determines which roles to delegate; for IT incidents, the initial responder usually hands off the IC role to the next qualified person to focus on technical operations; the IC establishes clear objectives and priorities; the IC creates and continually refines an Incident Action Plan (IAP); the IC expands the organization by creating subteams as the number of responders grows; control is terminated explicitly once the service returns to normal.
Rules_and_Constraints: There is one and only one Incident Commander; each person on the team reports to only one supervisor; the IC role is transferred only through explicit handoff and is not automatically taken by someone of higher rank; a supervisor should ideally manage three to five direct reports and no more than seven; the IC and the Operations lead should be separate individual roles as quickly as possible to maintain a big-picture view.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Initially, the IC may be alone and assume all roles simultaneously until more people arrive; role handoffs are considered disruptive and are only performed when necessary; if the IC tries to handle technical operations simultaneously, they may fail to perform either role effectively.

Concept_Name: Operations
Core_Definition: The subcommand system responsible for the direct technical aspects of incident recovery and the execution of the actual firefighting tasks.
Key_Actors: Operations lead (Ops lead); technical responders; oncall engineers; specialized technical resources like database or load balancing experts.
Operational_Workflow: The Operations lead receives objectives from the Incident Commander; the team performs troubleshooting and executes recovery procedures such as failing over a database or updating replicas; the lead identifies and requests necessary resources from the Logistics team; the team provides technical status updates to the IC; the team works until the specific technical objectives defined in the Incident Action Plan are met.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations is managed by objective, meaning the IC tells the team what to do but not how to do it; the Operations lead reports directly to the Incident Commander; this team must focus on the technical trenches rather than the global coordination of the incident.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In small incidents, the IC handles the Operations role themselves; for larger incidents, if the same person attempts to be both IC and Ops lead, the lack of a big-picture view can hinder the response.

Concept_Name: Logistics
Core_Definition: The subcommand system responsible for providing the necessary resources, people, and materials required by the Operations team to resolve the incident.
Key_Actors: Logistics Section Chief; technical staff from other departments; third-party vendors.
Operational_Workflow: The Logistics Section Chief receives resource requirements from the Operations lead or the IC; the team sources and coordinates machine resources such as failover servers or network capacity; they arrange for personnel needs, including finding additional experienced staff or specialists from within the company; they handle environmental logistics such as food and supplies for the responders.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logistics reports to the Incident Commander; the team must ensure that the Operations team has the tools and personnel needed to function without the technical responders having to source these items themselves.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Planning
Core_Definition: The subcommand system responsible for forecasting requirements, collecting situation data, and displaying incident information.
Key_Actors: Planning Section Chief; members of the monitoring group; data analysts.
Operational_Workflow: The Planning team gathers detailed information about the outage from monitoring systems; they forecast future situation and resource needs, such as predicting when web traffic demand will peak in different regions; they collect and display information about the current status of the incident for the rest of the ICS team; they help refine the Incident Action Plan by providing data-driven insights.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planning reports to the Incident Commander; the team focuses on the future state and information gathering rather than active technical repair.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Admin/Finance
Core_Definition: A subcommand system within the Incident Command System responsible for general administrative support and managing budgets.
Key_Actors: The Admin/Finance Section Chief; personnel arriving at an incident; administrative support staff.
Operational_Workflow: As new responders arrive at an incident, they check in with the Admin/Finance section for bookkeeping purposes before the Incident Commander assigns them to a specific function; throughout the incident, this team provides general administrative support and manages any budgets associated with the response and recovery efforts.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is defined as one of the standard subcommand systems in the Incident Command System framework; it functions as part of a temporary organizational structure created for a specific event.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Unified Command
Core_Definition: An optional component of the standardized Incident Command System organizational structure used to coordinate efforts.
Key_Actors: The Incident Commander; various subcommand systems.
Operational_Workflow: It serves as a part of the flexible framework that allows people from different organizations to work together effectively; it is established as part of the standardized organizational structure when the Incident Commander determines it is necessary for the incident response.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is listed as an optional subcommand system alongside the Public Information Officer and Liaison Officer within the ICS model.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Public Information Officer
Core_Definition: A role appointed to manage all internal and external communications regarding an incident to parties outside the immediate response team.
Key_Actors: Public Information Officer; Incident Commander; executives; users; customers; internal stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander appoints this officer to handle communications so the leadership is not distracted by status requests; the officer interfaces with the Incident Commander and various section chiefs to gather facts; they provide regular status updates to management and stakeholders; they update internal and customer-facing status pages; each update concludes by specifying when the next update will be provided.
Rules_and_Constraints: Status updates are typically standardized, such as hourly updates, to balance the informational needs of executives with the focus required by technical workers; for multi-day outages, the frequency of updates may be reduced to avoid repetition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Liaison Officer
Core_Definition: The primary point of contact for interacting with outside agencies and third-party entities during an incident.
Key_Actors: Liaison Officer; third-party vendors; outside agencies.
Operational_Workflow: This officer is appointed as part of the subcommand system to facilitate coordination with entities external to the primary organization; they manage the relationship and information exchange with vendors or other outside agencies as required by the Incident Commander.
Rules_and_Constraints: This role is part of the standardized organizational structure of the Incident Command System and is intended to ensure clear lines of communication with external partners.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Management by Objective
Core_Definition: A principle within the Incident Command System where clear objectives and priorities are established for an incident to guide the team.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; subordinates; subcommand systems.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander defines what needs to be accomplished during an incident; these goals and priorities are communicated to the relevant team members; the individuals or teams responsible for the tasks determine the best method for execution based on the current circumstances.
Rules_and_Constraints: Clear objectives and priorities must be established for every incident; supervisors should tell people what to do but not how to do it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Limited Span of Control
Core_Definition: A management principle that restricts the number of direct reports a supervisor oversees to ensure effective management and coordination during an incident.
Key_Actors: Supervisors; direct reports; Incident Commander.
Operational_Workflow: As the number of people involved in an incident response grows, the organization expands by creating new supervisors; responsibilities for each supervisor are kept limited to maintain effective oversight; this temporary organizational structure scales up or down based on the needs of the specific event.
Rules_and_Constraints: A supervisor should ideally have three to five direct reports; the maximum number of direct reports allowed for any supervisor is seven.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a subcommand reaches seven direct reports and an additional person is added, the team must be split in two.

Concept_Name: Common Terminology and Organizational Framework
Core_Definition: A standardized structure of roles, responsibilities, and language that enables diverse teams from different organizations to work together productively.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; subcommand systems including Operations, Logistics, Planning, and Admin/Finance; Public Information Officer; Liaison Officer; Safety Officer.
Operational_Workflow: Responders from different groups adopt standardized ICS roles to create a flexible framework; individuals check in and are assigned to specific functions; this common framework allows participants to understand clearly who is responsible for specific tasks and where those responsibilities lie.
Rules_and_Constraints: ICS training should be part of the onboarding process for new hires; teams should use these procedures for minor incidents and non-emergencies to stay in practice; escalation partners should be informed that these procedures are being used.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Confusion and conflicts can arise if different teams or partner organizations adapt the ICS procedures differently for their specific IT environments.

Concept_Name: Planning/Status
Core_Definition: The subcommand system responsible for forecasting situation and resource needs while collecting and displaying information about an incident.
Key_Actors: Planning Section Chief; Incident Commander; Monitoring group; technical teams.
Operational_Workflow: The Planning team collects and displays data about the incident; they forecast future situational needs and resource requirements; they coordinate with groups like the Monitoring team to gather detailed information for the response.
Rules_and_Constraints: The function must handle both the collection of information and the forecasting of needs; status updates should be regular and standardized to a specific frequency to balance the needs of stakeholders with the focus of technical workers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Outages of long duration or multi-day events may require less frequent status updates to avoid unnecessary repetition.

Concept_Name: Administration/Finance
Core_Definition: A subcommand system within the Incident Command System framework responsible for general administrative support and managing budgets during an incident.
Key_Actors: The Incident Commander; the Admin/Finance team; responders who check in for bookkeeping purposes.
Operational_Workflow: When an incident begins, the Incident Commander assumes this role initially if alone; as more people arrive, the Incident Commander explicitly delegates the function to a subcommand lead; new responders arriving on the scene check in with the Admin/Finance section for bookkeeping and registration before being assigned to specific functions by the Incident Commander; the team then provides ongoing administrative and budgetary oversight throughout the duration of the emergency response.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must follow the standardized organizational structure of the Incident Command System; supervisors within this section should maintain a limited span of control, ideally managing three to five people and never exceeding seven reports; the team functions as a temporary structure created specifically for the event.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In small IT incidents, a separate Admin/Finance section may not be established if the Incident Commander can handle all leadership roles alone.

Concept_Name: Safety Officer
Core_Definition: A specialized role within the Incident Command System responsible for overseeing and monitoring the safety conditions of the incident environment.
Key_Actors: The Safety Officer; the Incident Commander.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander appoints a Safety Officer as part of the expanded organizational structure when the scale of the incident requires dedicated safety monitoring; the officer then monitors conditions and reports safety-related issues within the incident management framework.
Rules_and_Constraints: The role is defined as an optional but standardized part of the organizational structure under the Incident Commander; it is intended to ensure the physical or operational safety of responders during the incident.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Command section
Core_Definition: The management component of the Incident Command System responsible for the overall leadership, decision-making, and coordination of the incident.
Key_Actors: The Incident Commander; supervisors; subcommand systems leads for Operations, Logistics, Planning, and Admin/Finance.
Operational_Workflow: The Command section is established the moment the first qualified responder arrives and assumes the role of Incident Commander; the section sets objectives and priorities for the incident; it directs the various subcommands such as Operations and Logistics through explicit delegations of authority; the management structure functions as a strict hierarchy to ensure efficiency during an emergency.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be an unambiguous definition of who is in charge with only one Incident Commander; each person in the organization reports to only one supervisor for the specific incident; the section must utilize common terminology and a standardized framework; it must maintain a limited span of control to ensure scalability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While the Command section usually expands as people arrive, in IT operations the initial responder may hand off the Incident Commander role to a more qualified person or a team lead to focus on technical operations.

Concept_Name: matrix management
Core_Definition: A management structure characterized by multiple reporting lines or shared authority, which is contrasted against the strict hierarchy of the Incident Command System.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: This style of management is explicitly avoided during emergency responses because it is considered to have vagaries and inefficiencies that are unsuitable for the time-sensitive nature of firefighting or medical emergencies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Operations (Ops) lead
Core_Definition: The individual responsible for managing the technical firefighting and operational aspects of an incident while working directly in the trenches.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; oncall responder; Logistics team; technical personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The role typically begins with the oncall responder who first identifies the alert; this individual often sounds the alarm and hands off the Incident Commander role to another qualified person so they can focus specifically on the technical response; the lead manages by objective, such as executing database failovers or updating replicas; they communicate resource requirements to the Logistics section to obtain necessary machines or additional staff; they report directly to the Incident Commander to maintain alignment with the overall incident strategy.
Rules_and_Constraints: The Operations lead and Incident Commander should be separate individuals as soon as possible to ensure the lead can focus on technical tasks while the commander maintains a big-picture view; the lead must coordinate with Logistics for all resource needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In small IT incidents, a single person may handle all leadership roles, including acting as both the Incident Commander and the Operations lead simultaneously.

Concept_Name: status updates
Core_Definition: Frequent reports provided to management and stakeholders during an outage to communicate progress and manage expectations.
Key_Actors: Public Information Officer; Incident Commander; management; stakeholders; customers; users.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander designates a Public Information Officer to handle communications; status updates are issued to internal management and external stakeholders or customers; updates are typically posted to internal or external status pages; each update follows a standardized frequency to maintain a balance between transparency and technical focus; the process concludes by specifying exactly when the next update will be provided.
Rules_and_Constraints: Updates must be handled by a designated individual to prevent the Incident Commander from being distracted by inquiries; every status update must end by noting when the next status can be expected; many organizations standardize on an hourly frequency for these updates.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During outages of long duration or multi-day incidents, the frequency of status updates is reduced to avoid unnecessary repetition.

Concept_Name: Incident Action Plan (IAP)
Core_Definition: A concise one- to two-page document created and refined during an incident to establish objectives, responsibilities, and communication protocols.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; Operations lead; Logistics section; Planning section; Admin/Finance section.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander initiates the plan by drafting a document based on a standard template; the plan identifies the primary goals of the response and assigns specific responsibilities to team members; it establishes how the team will communicate with one another during the event; it defines procedures for handling the loss of personnel or the detection of additional failures; the document is kept in a standard, accessible location and is continually refined as the situation evolves.
Rules_and_Constraints: The document should be limited to one or two pages; it must address what needs to be done, who is responsible, the communication methods, the procedure for unavailable personnel, and the procedure for additional failures; a standard template must be used to ensure consistency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: onboarding process
Core_Definition: The formal training and integration period where new hires are taught the standardized terminology and frameworks used for disaster response and operations.
Key_Actors: New hires; operation team; Incident Commander.
Operational_Workflow: New hires receive formal training on the Incident Command System as a standard part of their integration; they are introduced to the specific terminology and organizational framework used by the team; they participate in the process during non-emergency situations to gain familiarity; they eventually rotate through roles such as Incident Commander during minor incidents or datacenter moves to build confidence and understanding of the team's capabilities.
Rules_and_Constraints: Training on the Incident Command System must be a mandatory component of the process; everyone on the team must understand the processes and terminology to ensure the system functions effectively during real emergencies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: escalation partners
Core_Definition: Entities or external teams that an operational group coordinates with during an incident.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; Communications Officer; partner teams; technical teams.
Operational_Workflow: The primary team informs their escalation partners about the use of Incident Command System procedures; during an incident these partners interact with specific roles such as the Communications Officer; the teams participate in joint practice sessions using simulated problems or non-emergency issues to identify and resolve coordination problems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Partners must be informed of the specific Incident Command System procedures being used; interactions should be channeled through designated roles; multi-team incidents should be practiced regularly to avoid confusion.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Confusion and conflicts can occur during multi-team incidents if the escalation partners have adapted Incident Command System procedures differently for their own internal use.

Concept_Name: security incidents
Core_Definition: Specialized events involving system security that are managed using the Incident Command System framework to ensure order and practice.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; IT operations teams; security responders.
Operational_Workflow: The team activates Incident Command System procedures for the security event; a qualified responder assumes the role of Incident Commander; the Incident Commander delegates authority to subcommand systems as needed; the team follows the Incident Action Plan to resolve the issue while maintaining standardized terminology.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should use Incident Command System procedures for security incidents even if they are minor to stay in practice for major disasters.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: adversarial terminations
Core_Definition: High-stakes personnel actions involving the firing of an employee that are managed using the Incident Command System as a non-emergency exercise.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; IT operations teams; management; human resources.
Operational_Workflow: The organization applies the standardized Incident Command System organizational structure to the termination process; roles are explicitly delegated to handle various aspects of the event; the team uses the situation as a training opportunity to maintain familiarity with the framework.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process should be used for adversarial terminations to ensure the team remains comfortable with the Incident Command System before a real emergency occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Communications Officer
Core_Definition: An Incident Command System role responsible for managing information flow and status updates to stakeholders and external agencies.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander; management; stakeholders; users; escalation partners; technical workers.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander designates a single person to this role to prevent technical staff from being distracted; the officer interfaces with the Incident Commander and section leads to collect status information; they distribute regular updates to management and stakeholders; they update internal and external status pages; they conclude every update by stating when the next status report can be expected.
Rules_and_Constraints: The role should be used to buffer the Incident Commander and technical teams from executive demands; status updates should follow a standardized frequency such as hourly to balance the needs of stakeholders with the focus of technical workers; status updates should be refined based on the duration of the outage to avoid unnecessary repetition.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In multi-team incidents, confusion can arise if different teams have different expectations or adaptations of how the communications role interacts with escalation partners.

Concept_Name: Logistics Section Chief
Core_Definition: The individual in charge of the Logistics team responsible for managing resources such as people and materials to support the operations during an incident.
Key_Actors: Logistics Section Chief; Logistics team members; Operations team lead; Incident Commander.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander delegates the logistics function to the chief as the incident grows in complexity; the chief receives specific requirements and resource needs from the Operations lead; they direct team members to secure necessary technical resources like machine capacity or additional personnel; they coordinate support resources such as food for the team during long-duration outages.
Rules_and_Constraints: The chief reports directly to the Incident Commander; the role follows a strict hierarchy and should maintain a limited span of control of three to five direct reports with a maximum of seven.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Planning Section Chief
Core_Definition: The leader of the Planning team responsible for collecting incident data, displaying status information, and forecasting future resource and situational needs.
Key_Actors: Planning Section Chief; Planning team members; Monitoring group; Incident Commander.
Operational_Workflow: The Incident Commander appoints a Planning Section Chief to manage the collection of information; the chief works with specialists such as the monitoring group to gather detailed data about the outage; they analyze trends to forecast upcoming demands like shifting regional web traffic; they display this information to maintain situational awareness for the entire response organization.
Rules_and_Constraints: The role reports to the Incident Commander; it is part of the standardized Incident Command System organizational structure; the chief must manage a limited span of control to ensure effective communication.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: postmortem effort
Core_Definition: A process conducted after an outage is resolved to identify root causes, learn from mistakes, and implement improvements to prevent future failures.
Key_Actors: Incident Commander or a designated lead; operations team; management; internal and external stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The effort is initiated after the incident is declared resolved and the Incident Command process is terminated; a lead is assigned to investigate the event and identify the root cause; the team documents weaknesses exposed by the outage and proposes additional preventive checks; a postmortem report is produced to educate the organization; findings are used to make the system more resilient or antifragile.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be blameless and avoid naming individuals or seeking to fire employees; it should focus on lessons learned and technical or procedural improvements; transparency is essential to ensure that the real root causes are fixed rather than obscured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In some organizations, management or the media may demand that someone be blamed or fired, which is described as counterproductive and creates a culture of fear; if employees fear being fired, they may hide their mistakes, which prevents the postmortem effort from identifying and fixing the actual root causes of a failure.

============================================================
Chapter 16. Monitoring Fundamentals
Section: Chapter 16. Monitoring Fundamentals
============================================================

Concept_Name: Monitoring
Core_Definition: Monitoring is the primary way to gain visibility into systems by observing information about the state of things for use in both short-term and long-term decision making. Its operational goal is to detect precursors of outages, collect information for future decision making, and detect actual outages.
Key_Actors: Operations team; monitoring application or system; distributed systems; consumers of monitoring information including capacity planners and product managers; web browsers used in real user monitoring.
Operational_Workflow: Systems and servers are instrumented to expose measurements; a monitoring application collects these measurements from various perspectives at specific frequencies; measurements are converted into metrics by adding names and timestamps; metrics are stored in a storage system with a defined retention policy; the monitoring system processes this data for visualization to find trends or alerting to notify people of exceptional situations; historical data is used for capacity planning and calculating business key performance indicators.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should start with business key performance indicators and collect related measurements; monitoring should be designed to detect precursors of an outage rather than just the outage itself; all systems should use NTP to keep clocks synchronized and record timestamps in UTC; retention policies should generally use two years as a minimum to enable year-over-year comparisons; monitoring systems must be more available and scalable than the services they monitor.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Organizations may monitor the wrong things or ignore important metrics; metrics collection can sometimes overwhelm the systems being monitored or the monitoring system itself; old data may be expired, downsampled, or deleted after the retention period; configuration mistakes can lead to incorrectly normalized logging data; the monitoring system itself can fail, requiring meta-monitoring techniques like a second monitoring system or dividing the network.

Concept_Name: Omniscient
Core_Definition: Omniscient means being all-knowing, where a monitoring system provides the visibility to find out anything required to do a job. It allows an operations team to look up information as needed even if they do not know everything at all times.
Key_Actors: Operations team; monitoring system; distributed systems.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks a vast array of information across complex distributed systems; the operations team accesses this stored knowledge to gain insights into specific system states or events; this process compensates for the fact that distributed systems are too large for any one person to know everything that is happening.
Rules_and_Constraints: The monitoring system must provide the visibility required to find out anything necessary for the team to perform its duties; information must be available for lookup when needed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Omnipresent
Core_Definition: Omnipresent means existing everywhere at the same time, allowing an operations team to keep an eye on all critical components of a system even when they are distributed around the world.
Key_Actors: Operations team; monitoring systems; distributed systems; system administrator.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems are deployed to collect data from multiple perspectives across different datacenters or ISPs; real user monitoring collects performance data directly from user web browsers; this allows a single operations team to maintain a presence across globally distributed systems; the system allows for the observation of all critical components which would be impossible for an individual to watch manually in a distributed environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Monitoring must be distributed globally to match the scale of the system it observes; the system must permit administrators to see what is happening across the entire infrastructure simultaneously.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Monitoring perspective can change the value of certain metrics like page load times; inter-datacenter connectivity failures can interfere with the ability to maintain this omnipresent view.

Concept_Name: Omnipotent
Core_Definition: Omnipotent means being all-powerful, which in the context of system administration is achieved by having the root password to the systems.
Key_Actors: Operations team; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A system administrator gains the root password for the systems they manage; this access provides the power to control or modify the systems as needed to perform operational tasks; combined with omniscience and omnipresence, this makes the operations team omniarchs of the environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Root access is the defining characteristic of this state within the operational context.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Outages
Core_Definition: An outage is a state of system failure or service interruption that monitoring aims to detect, though the preferred operational goal is to identify and fix its precursors before an actual failure occurs.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; oncall persons; monitoring systems; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system observes information about the state of system components to detect precursors of an outage; if precursors are identified, the operations team fixes them to prevent a failure; if an actual outage occurs, the monitoring system detects the failure and generates an alert; the alert is sent to an oncall person who must acknowledge it; if the alert is not acknowledged within a certain timeframe, the system performs an escalation to notify another person.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should not select operational strategies that involve reacting to outages as a primary method because this means outages are effectively baked in to the process; monitoring should be designed to enable the prevention of problems; alerting for an actual outage is used as a last resort.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Monitoring systems can fail themselves, which requires meta-monitoring to ensure that the lack of alerts is due to a healthy system rather than a failed monitoring tool; manual response to outages is considered less reliable than proactive prevention.

Concept_Name: Server
Core_Definition: A server is software running to provide a specific function or API, and it is explicitly defined as not being a piece of hardware.
Key_Actors: Software applications; backend systems; monitoring systems; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A server runs software to provide a function or API; it is instrumented with specific metrics such as request counts, RAM usage, or the build version of the code; these metrics are collected at various frequencies by a monitoring system; the monitoring system uses this data for real-time alerting or historical visualization.
Rules_and_Constraints: A server is categorized as software rather than physical or virtual hardware; backend server stability is a key performance indicator that can be measured by the percentage of queries returning HTTP 5xx Server Errors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Servers may become overloaded or experience bottlenecks such as long queues; they may return errors if they become unstable; in large distributed systems, individual servers are part of a complex environment where no single person can track all activities simultaneously.

Concept_Name: Service
Core_Definition: A service is a user-visible system or product that is composed of many individual servers.
Key_Actors: Users; product managers; capacity planners; operations teams; business managers.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple servers are combined to create a product or system visible to users; the service is measured using key performance indicators like availability, latency, and user satisfaction; monitoring may include real user monitoring which collects performance data directly from web browsers; performance is governed by Service Level Indicators, Service Level Targets, and Service Level Agreements.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services should be managed based on key performance indicators; service management involves defining Service Level Indicators for measurement, Service Level Targets for quality, and Service Level Agreements for contracts and penalties.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services can suffer from abandoned shopping carts or high latency which negatively impact user satisfaction; if a service lacks key performance indicators, the organization is described as a rudderless ship; service level targets may be missed, triggering penalties or refunds defined in the agreement.

Concept_Name: Machine
Core_Definition: A machine refers to either a virtual or physical machine that provides the infrastructure for running software.
Key_Actors: Physical hardware; virtual machines; operating system kernels; devices; monitoring applications.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are collected directly on the machine to ensure consistency; a standard set of system metrics such as CPU utilization, network bandwidth, and disk space is collected from every machine; the operating system kernel and devices generate system logs; all machines synchronize their clocks using NTP or an equivalent; logging and metric data are transmitted to a central storage system for analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a best practice to collect a consistent set of system metrics from all machines rather than hand-crafting unique lists for each; timestamps should be recorded in UTC rather than local time zones to simplify log comparison and consolidation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machines can experience hardware failures, runaway processes, or disk space exhaustion; configuration mistakes can lead to incorrect timestamp normalization during the log consolidation process; machine-level data may be downsampled or deleted based on retention policies to manage storage costs and complexity.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: QPS stands for queries per second and represents the number of web hits or API calls a system receives every second.
Key_Actors: Web servers; API servers; oncall person; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks the number of incoming hits or calls to a server; the data is calculated as a per-second rate; the system monitors this rate for sudden changes; if a sudden drop occurs, the system triggers an alert to notify the oncall person.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A sudden drop in QPS is treated as an exceptional event that requires an alert and potentially an escalation if the oncall person does not acknowledge the notification within a certain timeframe.

Concept_Name: Diurnal Cycles
Core_Definition: Diurnal cycles are recurring patterns in metrics that show high levels of activity during the day and low levels of activity at night.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; services; users.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are collected continuously over time; the monitoring system identifies a repeating daily pattern where values peak during daylight hours and trough during nighttime hours; these cycles are used as a baseline to identify uncharacteristic behavior for specific times of day.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An outage or performance issue may be detected if a metric, such as revenue or traffic, drops in a way that is uncharacteristic for that specific time of the diurnal cycle.

Concept_Name: Measurement
Core_Definition: A measurement is a single point of data that describes a specific aspect of a system, typically represented as a numerical value or a string.
Key_Actors: Monitoring applications; system components; machines.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring application observes information about the state of a system component; data is gathered from a specific monitoring perspective; measurements are taken at a defined measurement frequency; these data points are then processed into metrics or used for diagnostics.
Rules_and_Constraints: Measurements should be taken at a frequency appropriate for the specific metric; the monitoring perspective must be considered for data like page load times; consistency in what is measured across machines is preferred to hand-crafted lists.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Attempts to collect a measurement may fail; measurement data can vary significantly depending on the physical or network location of the monitoring application; collecting too many measurements can overwhelm the monitoring system or the systems being observed.

Concept_Name: Metric
Core_Definition: A metric is a measurement that has been assigned a specific name and a timestamp to provide context and identity.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; operations teams; capacity planners; product managers; servers.
Operational_Workflow: A raw measurement is captured from a system or service; the monitoring system labels the value with a name and a date stamp; metadata such as units are associated with the value; the resulting metric is stored for use in visualization, trend analysis, alerting, or capacity planning.
Rules_and_Constraints: Metrics should include units to allow for automatic conversions and chart labeling; timestamps should be stored in UTC; clocks across all systems should be synchronized via NTP or an equivalent; metrics should be retained for a minimum of two years to allow for year-over-year comparisons.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many monitoring systems fail to track units, leading to manual conversion errors; if clocks are not synchronized or UTC is not used, comparing metrics from different systems during a postmortem is difficult; metric data may be downsampled or deleted after a retention period to manage storage costs and complexity.

Concept_Name: Metadata
Core_Definition: Metadata is information associated with a metric, such as a unit for a numeric value, that provides context for measurements.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; people; metrics.
Operational_Workflow: A metric value is captured; metadata defining the unit is associated with that numeric value; the monitoring system uses this information to perform automatic conversions and provide labeling for charts and graphs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Knowing the unit associated with a metric permits automatic conversions and chart labeling; metadata should be tracked by the monitoring system to avoid requiring people to guess the context of a metric.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many monitoring systems do not track the units of a metric; this requires people to guess units based on the metric name or other context and perform manual conversions, a process that is notably prone to error.

Concept_Name: Measurement frequency
Core_Definition: Measurement frequency is the rate at which new measurements are taken for a particular metric.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; services; machines.
Operational_Workflow: A specific rate of collection is determined for a metric; measurements are captured at that interval, which can range from multiple times per second to once a week; the data is then used for real-time analysis or long-term trend spotting.
Rules_and_Constraints: Different metrics are collected at different frequencies; many pieces of data are collected every 5 minutes; high resolution is defined as many times a second, minute, or hour; low resolution is many times a day.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the conventional wisdom to monitor everything is taken literally, the resulting metrics collection can overwhelm the systems being monitored or the monitoring system itself.

Concept_Name: Monitoring perspective
Core_Definition: Monitoring perspective is the physical or logical location of the monitoring application that is collecting a measurement.
Key_Actors: Monitoring applications; machines; countries; Internet Service Providers.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring application is established at a specific location; it collects data from its vantage point; measurements may be gathered from multiple locations worldwide to detect distance-related latency or directly on a machine when consistency is preferred.
Rules_and_Constraints: The importance of perspective depends on the specific metric; page load times and timing data depend on perspective while packet counts on an interface do not; perspective is used to detect problems with a particular country or Internet Service Provider’s connectivity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Real user monitoring (RUM)
Core_Definition: Real user monitoring is a technique for gathering performance measurements from many perspectives by collecting data directly from the user's web browser.
Key_Actors: Web browsers; application code; original web site; users.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring code is inserted into a web page; the original site sends the web page to the user; the code collects actual performance measurements within the user's web browser; the code transmits the metrics back to the site that sent the page.
Rules_and_Constraints: RUM must collect actual application performance measurements from the browser; it is used as a specific method for gathering information from many different perspectives simultaneously.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Visualization
Core_Definition: Visualization is the assimilation of multiple measurements into a visual representation such as charts and graphs.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; operations team; capacity planners; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system collects multiple measurements; these measurements are assimilated into visual representations like charts and graphs; users then use these visuals to find trends, make comparisons between systems, and compare different time ranges.
Rules_and_Constraints: Visual representations should be used to gain deeper insights into the system to help with design, planning, and communication; metadata such as units should be associated with numeric values to permit automatic chart labeling and avoid manual conversion errors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many monitoring systems do not track units; requiring people to guess based on metric names or context and perform conversions manually; which is a process prone to error.

Concept_Name: Trend
Core_Definition: A trend is the direction of a series of measurements on a metric that indicates whether a value is growing or shrinking over time.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; capacity planners; product managers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are collected over a long period of time to create a series; visualization is used to observe the direction of these measurements; the resulting information is used for capacity planning and predicting future resource needs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Trends should be used to spot changes over long periods; they are used for capacity planning and to see how resource use changes as the product changes; product management uses historical trends to calculate key performance indicators like user retention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Alerting
Core_Definition: Alerting is the process of bringing a situation to the attention of a person or another system; typically to warn of potential or actual outages.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; oncall person; other systems.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system detects a specific condition or state change such as a sudden drop in queries per second; an alert is generated and sent to the oncall person or another system; the recipient must acknowledge the alert within a certain amount of time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Alerting should be designed to detect precursors of an outage in time for the problem to be prevented; it should be used as a last resort to warn when an outage has occurred; alerts must be acknowledged within a specific deadline.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the monitoring system fails or lacks meta-monitoring, alerts may not be sent even when problems exist; which can lead to undetected outages.

Concept_Name: Escalation
Core_Definition: Escalation is the process of alerting a second person or system when an initial alert has not been acknowledged within its specified deadline.
Key_Actors: Monitoring system; primary oncall person; secondary contact or backup personnel.
Operational_Workflow: An alert is sent to an initial person; a predefined amount of time is allowed for acknowledgment; if the deadline is exceeded without acknowledgment; the monitoring system alerts another person.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process is triggered specifically by the expiration of an acknowledgment deadline.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Capacity planning
Core_Definition: The process of predicting resource needs in the future based on the analysis of trends.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; product managers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Historical monitoring data is collected over a long period of time to spot trends; visualization tools assimilate multiple measurements into charts and graphs to identify the direction of service growth or shrinkage; coarse metrics such as the current number of machines, network bandwidth, cost per user, and machine utilization are used to make predictions; alerting is implemented to warn staff when resources are running low.
Rules_and_Constraints: Predictions require the use of coarse metrics rather than high-resolution data; planners must consider how resource use changes as the product changes, such as when a new release requires significantly different resources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service Level Indicator (SLI)
Core_Definition: An agreement that defines how a measurement will be measured, including what is measured, the method of measurement, and the perspective.
Key_Actors: Entities in the business world; parties involved in a Service Level Agreement.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are gathered according to the agreed-upon definitions and locations to provide data on system performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: SLIs are a foundational component of a Service Level Agreement contract.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The term Service Level Agreement is often overused as a synonym for a Service Level Indicator.

Concept_Name: Service Level Target (SLT)
Core_Definition: A target quality of service that represents the expected minimum or maximum value for a Service Level Indicator.
Key_Actors: Entities in the business world; ITIL V3.
Operational_Workflow: Specific targets are established, such as a certain level of availability or a maximum permitted latency, and the system is monitored to ensure these targets are met.
Rules_and_Constraints: This term is used in the ITIL V3 framework; it is stated within a Service Level Agreement along with penalties for when targets are not met.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The term Service Level Agreement is often overused as a synonym for a Service Level Target.

Concept_Name: Service Level Objective (SLO)
Core_Definition: The term used to describe a target quality of service prior to the adoption of the ITIL V3 standard.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: It represents the expected minimum or maximum of a Service Level Indicator; it was the name for what is now called a Service Level Target.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service Level Agreement (SLA)
Core_Definition: A contract that specifies the Service Level Indicators (SLIs), Service Level Targets (SLTs), and the penalties that apply if those targets are not met.
Key_Actors: Business entities; service providers; customers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: An organization identifies its Service Level Indicators to define what is measured and sets Service Level Targets for minimum or maximum quality levels; these elements are incorporated into a formal contract that includes specific penalties such as refunds for performance failures; the monitoring system tracks these metrics to ensure compliance; if a target like an outage duration is exceeded, the predefined penalty is triggered.
Rules_and_Constraints: An SLA must contain SLIs, SLTs, and penalties; the term should be distinguished from SLIs and SLTs; it is primarily used within the business world to manage service expectations and consequences.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A common deviation is the overuse of the term to mean any general service level requirement or simple target; a notable caveat is that penalties are often tied to specific thresholds such as an outage lasting longer than one hour.

Concept_Name: Real-time monitoring
Core_Definition: A monitoring approach focused on detecting state changes immediately to facilitate fast response to operational health issues and exceptional situations.
Key_Actors: Operational health teams; oncall staff; automated alerting systems; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are collected at high resolution and processed with low latency to provide up-to-date visibility; the system uses real-time computation to perform high-speed analysis of the data; if an exceptional situation like a sudden drop in QPS occurs, the system generates an alert; the oncall person must acknowledge the alert within a certain timeframe or it escalates to another person.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach demands high-speed and high-volume storage systems; it requires up-to-date access to all metrics and a reliable alerting mechanism; the resolution and latency must be sufficient to detect and respond to problems within an SLA.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the monitoring system itself fails, meta-monitoring is needed to detect the failure; the strategy aims to detect precursors to outages to prevent downtime rather than simply reacting to an outage that has already occurred.

Concept_Name: Historical monitoring data
Core_Definition: Metric data collected over a long duration used to identify trends, perform capacity planning, and support long-term decision making.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; product managers; business analysts; organizational leadership.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are collected at various frequencies and stored in a retention system; users apply visualization tools to see the direction of measurements over time; planners use this data to predict future resource needs; as data ages, the system may downsample or summarize raw data into averages or percentiles to save storage space before eventually expiring or deleting it.
Rules_and_Constraints: A two-year retention period is the recommended minimum to allow for year-over-year comparisons; retention policies should be based on business goals and requirements; storage systems must manage costs associated with backups, power, and hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Data might be lost or inaccessible if a new monitoring system cannot read the format of an older system; configuration errors such as incorrect time zone normalization can lead to inaccurate historical timelines; legal requirements may force specific limits on how long data containing personal information can be retained.

Concept_Name: Dickson model
Core_Definition: A framework for differentiating consumers of monitoring information based on the three characteristics of resolution, latency, and diversity.
Key_Actors: Operational health teams; quality assurance teams; capacity planners; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring consumers are identified by a 3-tuple representing their specific needs for data; the model evaluates resolution based on how frequently a metric is collected; latency is evaluated based on how quickly the information must be acted upon; diversity is evaluated based on the variety of metrics being collected.
Rules_and_Constraints: High resolution (R+) indicates collection multiple times per second, minute, or hour; low latency (L+) refers to real-time response while high latency (L-) refers to data stored for later analysis; high diversity (D+) means many metrics are collected across various services; the use of the plus sign identifies characteristics that are more difficult to engineer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The model does not include all possible combinations of characteristics; specifically, the combinations of (R-, L+, D-) and (R-, L+, D+) have not yet been observed in practical operational settings.

Concept_Name: Resolution
Core_Definition: Resolution describes how frequently a metric is collected, with high resolution meaning data is gathered many times a second, minute, or hour, and low resolution meaning it is collected many times a day.
Key_Actors: Monitoring applications; operations teams; quality assurance teams; capacity planners; product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are taken at a specific frequency or rate; these measurements are labeled with a name and timestamp to become metrics; the monitoring system stores these metrics for use by various consumers; over time, high-resolution data may be down-sampled or summarized into lower-resolution formats like hourly or daily averages to save storage space.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational health and quality assurance typically require high-resolution data; product management and capacity planning often use lower resolution; organizations should focus on key performance indicators first to avoid overwhelming systems with too many high-resolution metrics.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the collection frequency is too high, it can overwhelm the systems being monitored or the monitoring system itself; data may be expired or deleted after a certain retention period has elapsed.

Concept_Name: Latency
Core_Definition: Latency describes the length of time that passes before monitoring information is acted upon, ranging from low latency for real-time responses to high latency where data is stored for later analysis.
Key_Actors: Oncall persons; automated systems such as load balancers; operations teams; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: A measurement is collected from a specific perspective; for real-time needs, the system processes the metric with low latency to detect state changes immediately; if an exceptional situation is detected, an alert is generated; for high-latency needs, metrics are stored in a database and analyzed later for long-term trends or statistics.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operational health monitoring requires low latency to ensure problems are detected and addressed within a Service Level Agreement; automated traffic management by load balancers requires low latency; capacity planning and product management typically use high-latency data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If an alert is not acknowledged within a specific time, an escalation occurs; the health of the monitoring system itself can be tracked by monitoring the latency trends of key performance indicator calculations.

Concept_Name: Diversity
Core_Definition: Diversity describes the variety and number of metrics being collected across different services and systems.
Key_Actors: Distributed systems; monitoring infrastructure; log processing systems; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are instrumented to provide visibility into their internal state; the monitoring system collects a high diversity of metrics to help administrators find any information needed to perform their jobs; this data is categorized and provided to different consumers who may require a broad set of metrics or a focused subset.
Rules_and_Constraints: High diversity is required for effective operational health monitoring in complex distributed systems; log analysis and load balancing are typically characterized by low diversity because they focus on specific topics or metrics; administrators should focus on key performance indicators and use diagnostic metrics as needed to balance the diversity of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Attempting to monitor everything to achieve maximum diversity can result in metrics collection overwhelming the systems or the monitoring infrastructure.

Concept_Name: Operational Health
Core_Definition: Operational health is a primary use case for monitoring where exceptional situations are detected and alerts are generated for immediate response.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; oncall persons; monitoring infrastructure; high-speed and high-volume storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system observes the state of things to detect precursors of outages; it requires up-to-date access to metrics and real-time computation for high-speed analysis; when an exceptional situation is identified, the system alerts a person or another system; the alert must be acknowledged within a certain time or it is escalated.
Rules_and_Constraints: This use case demands high resolution, low latency, and high diversity; the monitoring infrastructure must be reliable and more available than the services it monitors; the goal is to prevent outages rather than just detecting them after they happen.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the monitoring system itself fails, meta-monitoring is required to detect the failure; if the response to an alert exceeds a deadline, the process of escalation is triggered to notify additional staff.

Concept_Name: Quality Assurance
Core_Definition: A consumer of monitoring information that performs medium-term or long-term analysis of specific quality metrics such as variability.
Key_Actors: Quality assurance teams; developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves analyzing data for unacceptable variations or defects similar to an auto assembly line; data is typically processed in batches after the fact; the information gained is used to find and fix bugs.
Rules_and_Constraints: This function requires high-resolution data; latency is not a critical factor because data is processed after it is collected.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Quality assurance also utilizes specialized information like debug logs; process traces; stack traces; coredumps; and profiler output when identifying issues.

Concept_Name: Product Management (PM)
Core_Definition: A consumer of monitoring information that utilizes data to calculate business-centric metrics and analyze user behavior over time.
Key_Actors: Product managers.
Operational_Workflow: Managers use monitoring systems to calculate specific performance metrics like conversion rates or user counts; they perform analysis on user retention often referred to as 7-day or 30-day actives; they rely on visualizations of historic data to understand long-term trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: Product management requires very low-resolution data; the role benefits significantly from having access to large amounts of historic data for long-term views.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Key performance indicators (KPIs)
Core_Definition: High-level business metrics that define the target goals for a service and guide the selection of what measurements to monitor.
Key_Actors: Product managers; business stakeholders; operations teams; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations start by identifying their business KPIs and then instrument systems to collect related measurements deeper in the pipeline; these measurements are often combined or calculated into statistics like percentiles and averages; meta-monitoring is used to track the freshness and latency of the data used to calculate these indicators.
Rules_and_Constraints: The monitoring strategy should focus on KPIs first and diagnostics second; the text states that an organization without KPIs is like a rudderless ship; measurements must be taken deep in the pipeline to see when things are going to fail before they affect the final KPI.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Revenue can be used as a single proxy KPI to indicate if a site is overloaded or down; the latency or age of the data used to calculate a KPI can be as important to monitor as the KPI itself to understand the health of the monitoring system.

Concept_Name: Availability
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator representing the percentage of time a web site is up, typically measured from outside the datacenter.
Key_Actors: Web site; datacenter; component systems; search trees.
Operational_Workflow: Measurements are taken from a perspective outside the datacenter; the availability of component systems deeper in the pipeline is measured; measurements are analyzed to identify overloaded systems, long queues, or bottlenecks; these metrics are correlated to determine if performance issues like imbalanced search trees are affecting the final availability statistic.
Rules_and_Constraints: A common target is 99.99 percent uptime; availability must be measured for both the end result and component systems to enable better decision making.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Overloaded systems; queues that grow too long; bottlenecks that choke the system; imbalanced search trees that require monitoring to correlate with performance issues.

Concept_Name: Latency (KPI)
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator that measures the time taken for a request to render, such as the 90th percentile latency for a homepage.
Key_Actors: Homepage; monitoring application; web browser; internet service providers.
Operational_Workflow: Timing data is collected based on the monitoring perspective, which may include collecting measurements directly on a machine or from various locations worldwide; real user monitoring may be used to collect performance measurements from the web browser; calculations are performed on the metrics to determine the 90th percentile; the results are used by technical operations to analyze and improve page load time.
Rules_and_Constraints: The 90th percentile latency for the homepage should not exceed 400 ms; latency measurements are dependent on the location of the monitoring application.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Page load times and timing data are affected by distance or problems with a particular country or ISP connectivity; KPI latency can also be used to monitor the health and freshness of the monitoring system itself.

Concept_Name: Urgent Bug Count
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator that defines the maximum number of outstanding high-priority bugs allowed in the system.
Key_Actors: Operations team; development team; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system collects a metric representing the number of open bugs; the specific count of outstanding Severity-0 bugs is identified; this count is compared against a predefined limit to ensure the system remains within the acceptable KPI threshold.
Rules_and_Constraints: There should be no more than n outstanding Severity-0 bugs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Severity-0 bugs
Core_Definition: The highest urgency classification for bugs, used to measure both the count of outstanding issues and the speed of their resolution.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations staff; technical teams responsible for bug resolution.
Operational_Workflow: Bugs are identified and assigned a severity level; Severity-0 bugs are monitored as part of the Urgent Bug Count KPI; the time taken to resolve these bugs is tracked to meet specific resolution targets.
Rules_and_Constraints: There should be no more than n outstanding Severity-0 bugs; all Severity-0 bugs must be closed within 48 hours to satisfy the Urgent Bug Resolution KPI.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Urgent Bug Resolution
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator (KPI) that tracks the timeframe for closing the highest priority software defects.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves selecting metrics to track the number of outstanding Severity-0 bugs and monitoring the time elapsed until they are closed; this measurement allows the organization to see if they are meeting their established performance targets.
Rules_and_Constraints: All Severity-0 bugs should be closed within 48 hours; there should be no more than a specified number of outstanding Severity-0 bugs at any given time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Severity-1 bugs
Core_Definition: A category of software defects associated with the Major Bug Resolution Key Performance Indicator.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: These bugs are identified and tracked within the monitoring system as part of the Major Bug Resolution KPI to ensure they are addressed within the organization's set timeframe.
Rules_and_Constraints: These bugs are required to be closed within a 10-day period.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Major Bug Resolution
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator (KPI) that measures the organization's efficiency in closing mid-to-high priority software defects.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The organization monitors the status of Severity-1 bugs and utilizes metrics to ensure that the resolution process adheres to the defined time limits for this specific class of bug.
Rules_and_Constraints: All Severity-1 bugs must be closed within 10 days.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Backend Server Stability
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator (KPI) that measures the reliability of server-side functions by tracking error response rates.
Key_Actors: Backend servers; monitoring systems that collect HTTP response codes.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks the total number of queries and identifies which ones result in server errors; these measurements are used to calculate the percentage of failed requests to ensure the system stays within stability targets.
Rules_and_Constraints: There should be no more than n percent of queries returning HTTP 5xx Server Error.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: HTTP 5xx Server Error
Core_Definition: A specific metric used as a Key Performance Indicator to evaluate the stability of backend servers by tracking the percentage of queries that result in server-side errors.
Key_Actors: Backend servers; monitoring systems; users or systems issuing queries.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system instruments backend servers to observe the outcome of all queries; it tracks the number of responses that return a 5xx error code; the system then calculates the percentage of these errors relative to total queries to assess server stability.
Rules_and_Constraints: The percentage of queries returning this error must not exceed a predefined threshold, referred to as n percent, to meet stability targets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: User Satisfaction
Core_Definition: A business-focused Key Performance Indicator that uses the percentage of abandoned shopping carts as a metric to gauge user experience quality.
Key_Actors: Users; shopping cart systems; e-commerce web pages; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors user progress through the shopping experience; it tracks when users initiate a cart but do not complete the order; this data is used to calculate the abandonment rate, which serves as a proxy for satisfaction.
Rules_and_Constraints: This KPI is governed by the rule that there should be no more than a specific percentage (n percent) of abandoned carts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: To properly diagnose low satisfaction, the organization must be able to detect if technical problems exist with any individual web pages throughout the shopping experience pipeline.

Concept_Name: Abandoned carts
Core_Definition: A metric representing instances where a user adds items to a shopping cart but fails to complete the purchase, often used to identify friction in the user experience.
Key_Actors: Users; web pages; shopping experience pipeline; monitoring infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system identifies carts that were created but did not reach the final order stage; these measurements are collected and analyzed to determine if the abandonment rate exceeds defined targets; the data is further used to investigate potential failures within the shopping pipeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: Monitoring abandoned carts is a requirement for measuring User Satisfaction KPIs; the goal is to maintain the rate below a specified n percent.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High abandonment rates may be caused by specific technical problems with web pages during the shopping experience rather than user choice, necessitating deeper visibility into the component systems.

Concept_Name: Cart Size
Core_Definition: A Key Performance Indicator defined as the median number of items contained in a shopping cart for each completed order.
Key_Actors: Users; ordering systems; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system records the number of items for every completed order; calculating this value may involve collecting two distinct metrics, such as the total number of items and the total number of orders; the monitoring system then determines the median value across the dataset.
Rules_and_Constraints: The KPI target for cart size is defined as a specific median value, referred to as n.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Finance (KPI)
Core_Definition: Finance is a key performance indicator representing the total revenue generated by a business, often measured over a specific timeframe such as a month.
Key_Actors: Product managers; business organizations; e-commerce systems; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The organization identifies revenue as a top-level business goal; metrics are collected from the system to calculate the total revenue; product management uses this low-resolution data for long-term views and visualizations to understand business trends; the data is used to tie together the health of various technical components.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should have clearly defined KPIs to avoid being a rudderless ship; measurement strategies should start with business KPIs and then collect related deeper metrics to achieve them; product management typically requires high-latency and low-resolution data for these indicators.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If revenue drops in a way that is uncharacteristic for the time of day it may indicate the site is overloaded; if revenue stops completely it indicates the site is down; if revenue ramps up significantly it may indicate the system will soon run out of capacity.

Concept_Name: Diagnostic
Core_Definition: A diagnostic is a metric collected specifically to assist with technical processes such as debugging and performance tuning.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; monitoring systems; software servers.
Operational_Workflow: Technical teams identify specific measurements needed to investigate a problem or tune performance; metrics are collected that may not be directly related to a high-level KPI; these metrics are used to find intermittent or difficult technical issues; technical staff analyze the data to find trends or correlations between system behavior and performance issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should focus on KPIs first and diagnostics as needed; it is not a good use of time to hand-craft bespoke lists of diagnostics for every individual machine; diagnostics should be collected consistently across machines to make management easier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain diagnostic metrics, such as specific CPU-related measurements used for debugging current issues, may be deemed unnecessary or lose their utility after a short period of time such as one year.

Concept_Name: System metrics
Core_Definition: System metrics are a fundamental set of measurements collected from all machines regarding hardware-level resources like CPU, network, and disk.
Key_Actors: Virtual or physical machines; operations teams; monitoring infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: A minimal set of metrics is defined to be collected from every machine in the environment; measurements are taken for CPU utilization, network bandwidth, disk space, and disk access; these measurements are given names and timestamps to become metrics; the monitoring system stores this data for use in visualization, alerting, and capacity planning.
Rules_and_Constraints: Consistency in system metrics across all machines makes management easier; monitoring everything literally can overwhelm the systems being monitored or the monitoring system itself; timestamps for these metrics should be recorded in UTC and clocks should be synchronized via NTP.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-resolution system metrics collected over many years can be used for long-term failure trend analysis, such as studying hard disk reliability; monitoring free disk space specifically can indicate runaway processes or logs filling the disk.

Concept_Name: HTTPS GET
Core_Definition: An HTTPS GET is a monitoring method used to retrieve a web page to verify the availability and performance of a web service.
Key_Actors: Web servers; monitoring systems; SSL certificates; network infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system initiates an HTTPS GET request to a web server; the system measures the time to first byte and the time to full payload using TCP timings; the SSL transaction is analyzed to check the validity and expiration of the SSL certificate; the response is used to determine if the server is up, if the service is overloaded, or if there is network congestion.
Rules_and_Constraints: This single check can be used as a proxy for many possible problems; it is a more comprehensive strategy than a simple ping because it tests the actual application layer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Comparing HTTPS GET results with CPU utilization or disk space metrics helps differentiate between an overloaded system and general network congestion.

Concept_Name: TCP timings
Core_Definition: Metrics that indicate the time to receive the first byte of a response and the time to receive the full payload during a network request.
Key_Actors: Web server; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring system performs an HTTPS GET request; the system measures the duration until the first byte is received; the system then measures the duration until the full payload is delivered.
Rules_and_Constraints: TCP timings are used as indicators to help determine if a server is up, if a service is overloaded, or if there is network congestion.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SSL certificate validity
Core_Definition: A status determined by analyzing an SSL transaction to ensure a security certificate is legitimate and functional.
Key_Actors: Web server; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system initiates an HTTPS GET request; the system analyzes the resulting SSL transaction; the system verifies whether the certificate is currently valid.
Rules_and_Constraints: Monitoring certificate validity is recommended as part of a minimal monitoring strategy where one network transaction proxies for multiple potential problems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SSL certificate expiration
Core_Definition: A metric tracked by analyzing SSL transactions to identify the date when a security certificate will no longer be valid.
Key_Actors: Web server; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system performs an HTTPS GET request; the system extracts expiration data from the SSL transaction; the system monitors this information to prevent service disruptions caused by lapsed certificates.
Rules_and_Constraints: This information should be analyzed during the standard SSL transaction process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: CPU utilization
Core_Definition: A system metric that measures the processing load of a machine to aid in technical processes like debugging and performance tuning.
Key_Actors: Machines; monitoring system; operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system automatically collects CPU metrics from all machines; operations staff analyze these metrics to differentiate between network congestion and an overloaded system; historical CPU data is stored for a period before being deleted or summarized.
Rules_and_Constraints: CPU metrics should be part of a consistent, minimal set of metrics collected from all machines; hand-crafting bespoke lists of metrics for individual machines is discouraged; specific CPU-related metrics may have a limited utility period of approximately one year.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High CPU utilization can be used to distinguish an overloaded system from network-level congestion; CPU-related metrics used for debugging may be deemed unnecessary for long-term retention compared to other metrics.

Concept_Name: Free disk space
Core_Definition: A system metric that measures the available storage on a machine and acts as a proxy for identifying various technical issues.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; operations staff; system administrators; backend servers.
Operational_Workflow: Disk space is collected as part of a minimal set of system metrics from all machines to ensure management consistency; it is also monitored on the monitoring system itself to prevent storage from becoming a limiting factor for the monitoring infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is recommended to collect a consistent set of metrics across all machines rather than hand-crafting lists for each machine; monitoring systems should be monitored for the same capacity issues as the services they watch.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Low free disk space can reveal exceptions such as runaway processes or logs filling the disk; a lack of disk space on the monitoring system itself can lead to its failure.

Concept_Name: Revenue
Core_Definition: A financial Key Performance Indicator that serves as a single metric to tie together the health, status, and capacity of an e-commerce site.
Key_Actors: E-commerce sites; system administrators; business stakeholders; interviewers.
Operational_Workflow: Revenue is measured and compared against characteristic patterns for the specific time of day to detect uncharacteristic drops or stops that indicate system issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: If an organization does not have KPIs like revenue, it is described as a rudderless ship; revenue is suggested as the single most important metric for an e-commerce site because it can proxy for many technical problems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If revenue stops, the site is likely down; if revenue drops in an uncharacteristic way, the site is overloaded; if revenue ramps up, it indicates the system will soon run out of capacity.

Concept_Name: Retention
Core_Definition: The length of time collected metric data is stored in the monitoring system before it is expired, downsampled, or deleted.
Key_Actors: Organizations; storage systems; legal departments; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Retention policies are created by translating business requirements and goals into storage system requirements; data is typically kept for a minimum of two years to facilitate year-over-year comparisons; as data ages, it is consolidated or deleted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Two years is the minimum recommended storage period; retention must balance the desire for historical data against the costs of hardware, backups, power, and complexity; legal issues may regulate how long certain data like logs must be kept.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Storing data forever can lead to excessive complexity and cost; a new monitoring system may be unable to read data collected by a previous system due to lack of conversion processes.

Concept_Name: Downsampling
Core_Definition: A summarization technique used to reduce storage requirements by replacing old, high-resolution data with lower-resolution summaries like averages.
Key_Actors: Monitoring storage systems; business stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Recent data is kept at full fidelity; as the data ages beyond specific thresholds such as 13 months, it is converted into hourly averages, percentiles, maximums, or minimums; once the summary is calculated, the original raw data is deleted.
Rules_and_Constraints: The amount of summarization depends on business needs; raw data is deleted after the summarization has been performed.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Summarization
Core_Definition: A technique also known as down-sampling where recent data is kept at full fidelity while older data is replaced by averages or other condensed forms of information.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; storage systems; business stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are initially collected at frequent intervals such as every 1 or 5 minutes; once data reaches a certain age such as 13 months it is converted into hourly averages, percentiles, maximums, and minimums; the original raw data is then deleted; at further milestones such as 25 to 37 months the data may be further reduced to 4-hour or daily summaries.
Rules_and_Constraints: The degree of summarization must depend on business needs; recent data must be kept at full fidelity; sufficient data should be retained to allow for year-over-year comparisons.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If specific business needs require high-resolution historical data for tasks like approximate bandwidth utilization or detailed debugging, raw data might need to be kept longer than the standard summarization schedule allows.

Concept_Name: Compaction
Core_Definition: A form of data consolidation used to manage storage when infinite facilities are not available.
Key_Actors: Storage systems; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system or administrators identify metrics that have been deemed unnecessary or irrelevant after running a service for a period of time; these specific metrics are then deleted from the storage system to reduce the overall storage footprint.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is better to collect too much data initially during system setup than to lack necessary data later; consolidation is necessary to manage costs related to hardware, backups, power, and complexity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain metrics such as specific CPU-related data might be useful for short-term debugging but lose their utility and become candidates for compaction after a year.

Concept_Name: Time-series databases
Core_Definition: Systems for storing metric data that are becoming increasingly standardized and easier to convert.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; data storage software.
Operational_Workflow: These databases store measurements associated with names and timestamps; they facilitate the retention of data over long periods for historical analysis and trend spotting.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standardization in these databases is expected to eventually remove the upper bounds on how long data can be retained when moving between different monitoring systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: SMART (Self-Monitoring, Analysis and Reporting Technology)
Core_Definition: A self-monitoring facility built into hard drives that provides high-resolution monitoring data.
Key_Actors: Hard drives; monitoring systems; researchers or system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The technology collects internal data from individual disk drives regarding their state and performance; this data can be aggregated over years from large populations of drives to analyze failure trends and reliability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Meta-monitoring
Core_Definition: The practice of monitoring the monitoring system itself to ensure it is functioning correctly and has not failed.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; secondary monitoring systems; datacenters; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: A monitoring system observes the state of various services; a meta-monitoring process or secondary system performs checks to ensure the primary monitoring system is still running; larger systems are monitored for scale and capacity issues such as disk space, CPU, and network usage; the system tracks the accuracy and precision of data, including how often collection attempts fail; the freshness of data and latency trends for key performance indicators are monitored; in multi-datacenter environments, systems may monitor each other in pairs or in a circular arrangement to detect interconnection failures and save bandwidth.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every monitoring system should have some kind of meta-monitoring; the monitoring system must be more available and scalable than the services being monitored; the display of information must be faster than the average person's attention span; secondary monitoring systems should have as few common dependencies as possible to prevent a single bug from disabling both systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a monitoring system fails, an organization may not receive alerts for actual outages; using a second monitoring system adds complexity, necessitates maintenance, and requires additional training.

Concept_Name: Logging data
Core_Definition: The record of system actions and events used to gain visibility into technical and business operations.
Key_Actors: Log processing systems; central storage; machines; services; legal entities; developers; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: Logs are generated by systems, devices, and applications; these logs are collected from various machines and services and transferred to a central place for storage and analysis; automated consolidation processes normalize all logs to a standard time format; software libraries may be used to ensure that generated logs conform to a required format.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is best to establish a single log format for all systems; all machines should use NTP or an equivalent to keep clocks synchronized; all timestamps should be recorded and stored in UTC rather than local time zones; retention periods are often governed by legal requirements because logs may contain personal information; logs are generally categorized as high resolution, high latency, and low diversity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Configuration mistakes can result in logging data being normalized incorrectly; using local time zones like U.S./Pacific can lead to complexity during daylight savings time changes where logs might be missing an hour or contain an extra hour.

Concept_Name: Web "Hit" Logs
Core_Definition: Records generated by web servers that detail each HTTP access, including performance statistics and request origins.
Key_Actors: Web servers; technical operations teams; business teams; users.
Operational_Workflow: The web server logs every HTTP access along with metadata such as whether the request was a success, error, or redirect; technical operations teams use this data to analyze and improve page load times and latency; business teams use the data to track user paths through the system, analyze user origins, and determine page generation times.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Analysis of web hit logs can reveal user interface dead ends that leave users baffled.

Concept_Name: API Logs
Core_Definition: Records of individual API calls that document the identity of the caller, input parameters, and the results of the call.
Key_Actors: API users; developers; billing systems; security forensics teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system records who made each API call along with the specific input parameters; the outcome is stored, often summarized as a simple success or error code; teams use these logs to determine which users will be affected by the elimination of obsolete API calls; the logs provide data for billing, security forensics, and feature usage patterns.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: System Logs
Core_Definition: Records contributed by the operating system kernel, hardware devices, and system services to track state changes and activity.
Key_Actors: Operating system kernel; hardware devices; system services; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: System components generate log entries based on events or changes; these logs are analyzed by operations teams to track hardware problems, monitor system changes, find anomalies, or predict potential hardware failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: All machines contributing to the log must use NTP or an equivalent to keep clocks synchronized; timestamps must be stored in UTC rather than local time zones to ensure consistency during comparison.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Application Logs
Core_Definition: Logs generated by individual services or servers that record specific actions taken by the software.
Key_Actors: Services; servers; developers; operations staff; business analysts.
Operational_Workflow: Applications record actions as they occur; these logs are utilized to study errors, debug technical problems, and provide business insights such as feature usage patterns, sales process flaws, or the identification of high-value customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations should establish a single log format for all systems; the use of a software library is recommended to ensure all logs conform to the desired standard; all timestamps must be synchronized via NTP and recorded in UTC.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Application Debug Logs
Core_Definition: Highly detailed logs generated by applications specifically to provide verbose information for troubleshooting and quality assurance.
Key_Actors: Applications; developers; operations staff; Quality Assurance teams.
Operational_Workflow: Applications produce granular debugging information in a separate log file; these are used by technical staff to find and fix bugs or analyze process traces and stack traces during the quality assurance process.
Rules_and_Constraints: These logs are typically more verbose than standard logs but are retained for a shorter period of time; they must follow standard formatting and use UTC timestamps for synchronization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Log processing system
Core_Definition: An automated architecture used to collect, store, and analyze log data from across a distributed environment in a centralized location.
Key_Actors: Distributed machines; services; central storage systems; log analysis tools; operations staff; legal entities.
Operational_Workflow: Logs are collected from various machines and services and transmitted to a central location for storage; the system automatically normalizes the data to a standard format and UTC time; analysts then use specialized tools to perform batch processing or trend analysis for business and technical purposes.
Rules_and_Constraints: The architecture should be similar to the monitoring architecture; consolidation of data should be automated; all logs must be normalized to UTC; retention policies are often governed by legal requirements because logs may contain regulated personal information.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Configuration mistakes can lead to logs being normalized incorrectly; using local time zones like U.S. Pacific can cause errors during daylight savings time transitions where hours are added or missing; high-resolution data collection may be constrained by the costs of storage, power, and hardware complexity.

Concept_Name: Log format standardization
Core_Definition: The practice of establishing a single, uniform format for all system and application logs to facilitate easier consolidation and analysis.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; systems; services; software libraries; automated consolidation tools.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations provide a software library that generates logs conforming to a specific standard; developers use this library as the path of least resistance for their services; logs are collected from various machines and services and moved to a central place for storage; an automated consolidation process normalizes all logs into the standard format.
Rules_and_Constraints: A single log format should be established for all systems; the software library provided should make the desired behavior the path of least resistance; timestamps must be recorded consistently across all systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Different systems naturally generate logs in different formats which complicates consolidation; configuration mistakes during the automated consolidation process can result in logging data being normalized incorrectly.

Concept_Name: NTP (Network Time Protocol)
Core_Definition: A protocol or equivalent service used to keep the internal clocks of all machines in a network synchronized.
Key_Actors: Machines; services; computer systems.
Operational_Workflow: All machines in the infrastructure run NTP or a similar service to maintain a consistent time; systems use these synchronized clocks to apply timestamps to logs and metrics; operations staff rely on these synchronized timestamps to compare data across different systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: All machines should use NTP or its equivalent to keep clocks synchronized; timestamps generated using these clocks should be stored in UTC rather than local time zones.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)
Core_Definition: A primary time standard used to record timestamps consistently across all systems regardless of their geographic location.
Key_Actors: Machines; services; automated consolidation processes; operations staff.
Operational_Workflow: All systems record timestamps in UTC instead of local time; an automated consolidation process normalizes incoming logs from various sources to UTC; operations staff use these standardized timestamps to build accurate timelines of events during debugging or analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: Timestamps should be stored in UTC rather than local time zones; UTC should be used for everything to avoid errors during normalization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Configuration mistakes can lead to data being normalized incorrectly; organizations using local time zones like U.S. Pacific face complexities during daylight savings time changes where one day may have an extra hour or be missing an hour.

Concept_Name: Postmortem
Core_Definition: A process used to understand the sequence of events during an outage by building a detailed timeline from various data sources.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; services; machines; communication systems such as chat rooms, instant messaging, and email.
Operational_Workflow: Staff collect logs from various machines and services after an event; relevant data is gathered from non-system sources like chat transcripts and email discussions; these various pieces of information are organized into a chronological timeline to determine exactly what happened and in what order.
Rules_and_Constraints: Successful postmortem creation requires that all involved services and communication platforms use consistent timestamping; data must be collected from a wide variety of sources to build a complete picture.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Determining the sequence of events becomes a significant challenge if services record timestamps in local time zones without indicating which time zone is being used.

Concept_Name: Daylight Savings Time
Core_Definition: A timekeeping adjustment where a calendar or time zone dictates that one day each year is missing an hour and another day each year has an extra hour.
Key_Actors: Software; systems administrators; Google; regional entities such as the U.S. and Europe.
Operational_Workflow: Software must be written to understand and account for the missing or extra hour during transitions; log normalization processes must reconcile differences between disparate regional daylight savings calendars to ensure accurate event timelines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Timestamps should be stored in UTC rather than local time zones to avoid complexity; all machines should use NTP or an equivalent to keep clocks synchronized; log normalization should ideally be automated to maintain consistency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Using local time zones like U.S./Pacific creates a situation where the daylight savings calendar differs from other regions like Europe; configuration mistakes can result in logging data being normalized incorrectly; the two weeks each year where regional calendars do not align make log normalization extra complex.

