Chapter 18. Capacity Planning
Section: Chapter 18. Capacity Planning
============================================================

Concept_Name: Capacity Planning
Core_Definition: Capacity planning is the process of ensuring that there will be enough resources when they are needed; ideally resulting in a system that is neither under nor over capacity.
Key_Actors: Statisticians who develop mathematical models; technical staff; project managers; monitoring systems; various departments who provide data on service requirements; outside vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Identify all required infrastructure components and current usage; analyze normal growth from historical monitoring data; estimate planned growth from marketing or business events; determine necessary headroom for usage spikes; assess resiliency requirements across failure domains; establish a timetable based on acquisition lead times; calculate future and additional resource needs using standard formulas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planning must be a data-driven process informed by business growth plans; inventory databases must be kept up-to-date through core ordering and provisioning processes; standard hardware configurations should be used to simplify resource mapping; resources should be spread across failure domains to minimize the extra capacity required for resiliency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Service interruptions caused by lack of capacity; wasted capital from over-capacity; running out of specific components that were forgotten during the inventory phase; unexpected step changes in usage due to specific product roll-outs; launching new services where no historical metrics exist to guide planning.

Concept_Name: Return on Investment (ROI)
Core_Definition: A demonstrable benefit achieved by proving the necessity of resources and capping usage at levels that ensure good service while preserving capital investment.
Key_Actors: Financially responsible companies; capacity planning teams; automation systems.
Operational_Workflow: Balance the potential for service interruption against the desire to conserve financial resources; reduce lead times for resource delivery to minimize paying for excess capacity; use automation to prepare acquired resources for use immediately to provide value.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reducing resource delivery time is cost-effective because it minimizes the need for excess capacity; excess capacity represents potentially wasted investment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Long lead times for resource acquisition force companies to maintain more headroom; which can lead to idle capacity and poor financial efficiency.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: Queries per second; which typically measures the number of web hits or API calls received by a service per second.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; users; primary resources such as CPU or network bandwidth.
Operational_Workflow: QPS serves as a core driver that strongly drives demand for primary resources; monitoring systems collect this data as a time series at high frequency; planners use regression analysis to correlate QPS data with resource consumption to predict future requirements.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data should be summarized or statistically sampled for planning purposes; QPS is used as a high-level metric to represent factors that generate traffic or load on the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unexpected spikes in QPS can match spikes in CPU or network usage; potentially leading to service degradation if the capacity limit is exceeded.

Concept_Name: Active Users
Core_Definition: The count of unique users who have accessed the service within a specified timeframe; such as 7 or 30 days.
Key_Actors: Users; capacity planners; marketing departments; project managers.
Operational_Workflow: Users are tracked as core drivers using specific timeframes like Monthly Active Users (MAU) or Weekly Active Users (WAU); this data is used in capacity models to determine how user growth affects primary resource needs; user counts can be simplified into blocks based on the serving capacity of one rack or cluster.
Rules_and_Constraints: Time scales for measurement must be appropriate to the specific service; active user counts must be combined with engagement metrics to fully understand the load on the service; these metrics are more accurate for planning than the total number of registered users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Relying on the number of registered users can be misleading because many accounts may be inactive; an increase in user engagement can increase service load even if the number of active users remains static.

Concept_Name: MAU (Monthly active users)
Core_Definition: The number of users who have accessed the service within a 30-day period or a specific calendar month. It serves as a high-level metric representing factors that generate traffic or load on a service.
Key_Actors: Registered users; active users; services; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Users access a service within a defined timeframe; monitoring systems track these accesses to provide a more accurate representation of activity than simple registration counts; the metric is then used as a core driver to calculate the relationship between user activity and primary resource consumption.
Rules_and_Constraints: MAU is often a more accurate representation of users than the total number of registered accounts; it can be used for storage-bound services driven by users; measurements can be simplified into blocks of users per rack or cluster for a cookie-cutter capacity planning approach.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For highly transactional systems, smaller time scales like minutely active users may be more appropriate; if the timeframe is a specific calendar week it is referred to as WAU; if the timeframe is seven days it is called 7-day actives or 7DA.

Concept_Name: Engagement
Core_Definition: A measurement of how many times on average an active user performs a particular transaction within a given period of time. It acts as a measure of the popularity of a particular feature.
Key_Actors: Active users; specific service features such as video-sharing, chat, or photo uploads; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: Planners track how often users access specific features separately from the total number of active users; this data is combined with active user counts to determine total service load; planners monitor engagement graphs for different services to predict impact on resources like disk space or I/O bandwidth.
Rules_and_Constraints: Engagement must be tracked separately because load can increase if engagement rises even if the active user count stays static; an increase in engagement for one service will have a different effect on resource requirements than for another service; trends in engagement can be monitored using MACD and signal lines to detect unexpected increases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Primary Resource
Core_Definition: The dominant system-level resource, such as CPU, memory, or bandwidth, that acts as the main limiting factor for a service. Capacity planning focuses specifically on this resource to ensure service availability.
Key_Actors: CPUs; memory; storage; network bandwidth; capacity planners; technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify which resource is the dominant factor consumed by the service; they define a relationship between this primary resource and ancillary usage like server instances or switch ports; usage trends are tracked and mapped so that when planning indicates a need for more primary resources, the amount of ancillary resources required can be determined.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each service has one primary resource; the relationship between primary and ancillary usage must be updated as hardware changes; planners must track the constraint ratio between primary and secondary resources whenever new software or hardware is introduced.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A secondary resource may become the primary resource if there are changes to the software code or the hardware models being used.

Concept_Name: Capacity Limit
Core_Definition: The specific point at which the performance of a resource begins to degrade rapidly or becomes unpredictable. It is often visualized as the point where response times spike significantly on a graph.
Key_Actors: Primary resources; secondary resources; lab environments; load testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Planners perform load testing in an isolated lab environment using synthetic traffic or replayed production traffic; the lab is configured with excess capacity for all resources except the one being tested; response times are graphed against the utilization of that specific resource to find the point of degradation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Limits should be determined for every primary and secondary resource; tests should be repeated several times while scaling the entire environment uniformly to determine if the limit is related to percentage utilization or the absolute quantity of remaining resources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Core Driver
Core_Definition: A factor that strongly drives demand for a primary resource. It represents high-level metrics that generate traffic or load on a service.
Key_Actors: Primary resources; secondary resources; ancillary resources; monthly active users; queries per second; corpus size; registered users; weekly active users; 7-day actives.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify high-level metrics that generate load on the service; they define the relationship between these drivers and primary resources through a capacity model; they quantify how changes in the core driver affect the need for ancillary resources like servers or switch ports; regression analysis is used to correlate core driver metrics with resource usage data; the model is used to analyze whether ancillary resources are well-balanced or require adjustments in hardware configurations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Core drivers often have meaningful business implications and links to revenue; different time components for metrics are appropriate for different services such as monthly active users for some and minutely active users for highly transactional systems; the capacity model must express how changes in the core driver affect a service’s need for that driver; core driver measurements are used to calculate primary resource requirements with a calculated confidence level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Counting registered users can be misleading if they are not active; engagement levels can drive resource demand even if the number of active users remains static; a change in software version or end-user demographics can significantly alter the correlation between a core driver and resource consumption.

Concept_Name: Time Series
Core_Definition: A sequence of data points measured at equally spaced time intervals. It is the fundamental data format used for monitoring and forecasting trends.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; data sources.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems collect resource use data at regular intervals; this data is summarized or statistically sampled for planning purposes; planners define specific time intervals for analysis such as days or weeks; regression analysis is performed on the data points to calculate how well different data sources match up; the data is plotted on graphs to determine if growth rates are linear or follow other trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data points must be measured at equally spaced intervals; analysis requires defining a specific time interval and counting the number of data samples within that period; time-series data is used as the basis for calculating correlation coefficients and regression lines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Monitoring systems often collect data at a higher frequency than needed for capacity planning, which may require summarization to simplify calculations.

Concept_Name: Standard Capacity Planning
Core_Definition: A process that ensures services have enough resources when needed by using data-driven forecasting based on current usage and growth trends. It aims to prevent service interruptions while preserving capital investment.
Key_Actors: Service providers; marketing departments; business teams; monitoring systems; inventory databases; shared resource teams; vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify current usage and all components influencing service capacity; they determine the normal organic growth rate from historical monitoring data; they estimate planned growth from upcoming business or marketing events; they calculate the required headroom for short-term spikes; they account for the lead time and timetable for ordering and delivering resources; a formula is used to calculate future resource requirements by combining current usage, growth rates, and headroom.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planning must be a data-driven process that considers future business growth; it must answer what needs to be purchased and when; calculations must be performed for every resource whether or not a deficiency is expected; capacity should be spread across different failure domains for resiliency; reducing resource delivery lead time is a priority to minimize idle capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Running out of a resource because it was omitted from the initial planning list; unexpected usage spikes caused by major external events like the Olympics or elections; facility-wide maintenance or outages requiring redundant capacity in separate datacenters; specific constraints like change windows that limit when new capacity can be brought into service.

Concept_Name: Current Usage
Core_Definition: The assessment of existing resource availability and the amount of those resources presently consumed by a service. It provides the baseline data necessary for forecasting future needs.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; inventory database; departments; third-party vendors; technicians.
Operational_Workflow: Planners create a complete list of all infrastructure components required to run the service; an up-to-date inventory database is used to track assets, software licenses, and maintenance contracts; the monitoring system collects resource use data for CPU, RAM, storage, and bandwidth; this monitoring data is combined with inventory data to identify current spare capacity; planners consult with various departments to ensure all service-related limits are measured and tracked.
Rules_and_Constraints: The inventory database must be kept current by making it a core component of ordering, provisioning, and decommissioning; tracking must include physical hardware, software licenses, IP addresses, and datacenter resources like power, cooling, and floor space; standard machine configurations should be used to simplify mapping devices to low-level resources like CPU and RAM; identify the specific limiting resources for each service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Forgetting to track a specific resource can lead to unexpected growth limitations; high-end appliances or network cards may require specialized cabling or interfaces that must be accounted for separately; contracted limits or costs for services provided by outside vendors must be tracked as part of the total capacity.

Concept_Name: Normal Growth
Core_Definition: The expected growth rate of the service without the influence of any specific business or marketing events.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; capacity planners or technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system provides historical data on usage and capacity from preceding years; planners identify any noticeable step changes in usage to determine if they correspond to specific events like product roll-outs; event-driven changes that persist are subtracted from subsequent data to isolate the underlying trend; data is plotted on a graph over as many years as possible to determine if the growth is linear or follows another trend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planners must avoid including event-driven changes in the normal growth calculation; as many years of data as possible should be used to accurately forecast the trend.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a step change in usage due to a specific event persists for the remainder of the year; the change must be calculated and subtracted from subsequent data to maintain the accuracy of the normal growth rate.

Concept_Name: Organic Growth
Core_Definition: An alternative term used interchangeably with normal growth to describe the expected growth rate of a service excluding specific business or marketing influences.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; capacity planning teams.
Operational_Workflow: Historical usage data is collected from monitoring systems; planners identify and remove the influence of specific business or marketing events to find the baseline growth rate; this data is used to forecast future resource needs alongside planned growth and headroom.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must reflect the expected growth of the service without the influence of specific business or marketing events.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Planned Growth
Core_Definition: The anticipated increase in service usage or customer base resulting from specific scheduled business or marketing events.
Key_Actors: Marketing department; business departments; capacity planning teams.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify upcoming marketing campaigns or new product features; the anticipated growth for each event is estimated in terms of percentage increases to the customer base or service load; assumptions about this expected growth are validated using data from previous historical changes detected in the monitoring system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Planners must define which events are planned; when they will occur; and what the anticipated growth is for each specific event.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Headroom
Core_Definition: The amount of excess capacity that is maintained as routine to handle short-term usage spikes or edge conditions gracefully.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; business decision makers; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems collect hard statistical data on the frequency and magnitude of resource spikes; planners analyze outage data and postmortem reports to determine reasonable requirements; business leaders make a decision on the amount of headroom based on the balance between financial conservation and the potential for service interruption; the final amount is often calculated as a percentage of current capacity and must account for the lead time required to deploy new resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Headroom is usually specified as a percentage of current capacity; at a minimum it must be sufficient to allow for expected growth during the resource acquisition and deployment time period; excess capacity must be balanced against the desire to preserve capital.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Specific major events such as the Olympics or an election can cause usage spikes that exceed routine headroom; short-term usage spikes or edge conditions that require extended resource usage occasionally.

Concept_Name: Timetable
Core_Definition: The specific lead time required for a component from the moment it is ordered until it is delivered and placed into active service.
Key_Actors: Vendors; purchase order approvers; technical staff responsible for testing and installation; department representatives; capacity planning teams.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify when resources are needed based on growth projections and work backward from that date; the process includes calculating time for purchase order approval, vendor delivery, and post-delivery setup; it accounts for specific testing periods and alignment with change windows; finally, it factors in the time required to reconfigure services to utilize the new capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Most companies plan budgets annually with expenditures split into quarters; physical services generally have longer lead times than virtual services; capacity additions must often respect specific change windows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Virtual services such as IaaS and PaaS have virtually instant delivery times; automation can be used to significantly reduce resource delivery time and minimize the need for excess capacity.

Concept_Name: Correlation Coefficient
Core_Definition: A statistical value between -1 and 1 that describes the strength and direction of the resemblance between measurements from different data sources.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; core driver metrics; primary resource data; technical staff or statisticians.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify two data sources, such as a core driver and a primary resource, and perform regression analysis on their time-series data; the resulting correlation coefficient is squared and multiplied by 100 to calculate the percentage match between the sources; this value is then graphed and monitored over time using rolling analysis.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be tracked continuously rather than assessed once because the relationship between metrics can change; high correlation allows for the derivation of a regression line equation to predict future resource needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Sharp drops in correlation can occur during software upgrades or changes in usage patterns; changes in end-user demographics can lead to slower shifts in correlation over time.

Concept_Name: Moving Average
Core_Definition: A series of averages calculated across a short, defined time interval or window rather than the entire available data set.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; capacity planners; data points from time-series sequences.
Operational_Workflow: Data is collected at equally spaced time intervals; an average is taken for a specific window of samples; in an exponential moving average, weights are applied to each data point so that the influence of older data decreases exponentially; these averages are then compared to identify trends or calculate regression lines.
Rules_and_Constraints: The window must be appropriately sized to balance noise reduction with the need for timely trend detection; older data points are given less weight in forecasting models to emphasize recent behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Standard moving averages tend to mask recent changes in metrics, which can delay the detection of important trend shifts in a rapidly changing environment.

Concept_Name: Regression Analysis
Core_Definition: A statistical method for analyzing the relationships between different data sources to determine their correlation and predict changes in one based on changes in another.
Key_Actors: Core drivers; primary resources; technical staff; statisticians; automated monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by defining a time interval and the number of data samples to be analyzed; planners calculate the sums and products of the core driver and primary resource metrics; these values are used to determine a correlation coefficient and a regression line equation; this equation is then used to calculate expected primary resource requirements for any given core driver value.
Rules_and_Constraints: The analysis requires time-series data measured at equally spaced intervals; the resulting R-squared value indicates the confidence level of the prediction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Significant deviations in resource usage between software versions are treated as resource regressions or bugs; load testing is required to determine if resource usage scales linearly or reaches unpredictable inflection points.

Concept_Name: EMA (Exponential moving average)
Core_Definition: A moving average that applies a weight to each data point in a window, with the weight decreasing exponentially for older data points. It provides an average with a much heavier weighting for recent data.
Key_Actors: Statistician; capacity planners; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The first value in the series is calculated as a straight average of the first n data points. Subsequent values are determined by applying a formula that factors in the number of samples, the current data point, and the previous EMA value.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be used to give early warning of changes in behavior by emphasizing recent data rather than masking it. The weight is calculated as 2 divided by the number of samples plus 1.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: MACD (Moving average convergence/divergence)
Core_Definition: An indicator used to spot changes in strength, direction, and momentum of a metric by measuring the difference between an EMA with a short window and an EMA with a long window.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; technical staff; teams responsible for capacity planning.
Operational_Workflow: Planners calculate the difference between a long-period EMA, such as three months, and a short-period EMA, such as one month. This difference is plotted over time to detect significant changes in usage trends more quickly than standard enterprise-style techniques.
Rules_and_Constraints: It should be used to provide early detection of changes in trends. Planners must choose appropriate time scales for long and short periods to balance responsiveness against noise.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the selected time periods are too short, the indicator will show changes too frequently to be useful. If the short period is too long, it will delay the triggering of a trend change event.

Concept_Name: Zero Line Crossover
Core_Definition: An event that occurs when the MACD line crosses the zero point, signifying that there is no difference between the short and long EMAs.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: A move from positive to negative through the zero line indicates a downward trend in the data. Conversely, a move from negative to positive indicates an upward trend.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: MACD Signal Line
Core_Definition: An exponential moving average of the MACD measurement itself.
Key_Actors: Team responsible for capacity planning; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The signal line is plotted on the same graph as the MACD line to identify trend changes. When the MACD line crosses from below to above the signal line, it indicates an increase or upward trend; when it crosses from above to below, it indicates a downward trend.
Rules_and_Constraints: A signal line crossover serves as an indicator of momentum, showing that the trend in the data is about to accelerate in the direction of the crossover. Alerts generated by these crossovers should go directly to the capacity planning team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In environments with high noise, a downward trigger may be followed quickly by an upward trigger, necessitating the validation of the model using historical data to ensure it is a good predictor.

Concept_Name: Signal Line Crossover
Core_Definition: An indicator of momentum where the MACD line crosses over the signal line, indicating that a trend in the data is about to accelerate in the direction of the crossover.
Key_Actors: MACD line; MACD signal line; exponential moving average (EMA); capacity planning team; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff calculate the MACD by measuring the difference between a long-period and a short-period EMA; an EMA of that MACD is then calculated to create the signal line; both lines are graphed together; when the MACD line crosses from below to above the signal line, it indicates an upward trend or acceleration; when it crosses from above to below, it indicates a downward trend; these crossovers generate alerts that are sent to the team responsible for capacity planning to trigger a reassessment of trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: MACD must use exponential moving averages to ensure recent data is weighted more heavily than old data; time scales for long and short periods must be carefully defined to be useful; the model should be validated by checking if older data successfully predicts trends observed in historical datasets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the chosen time periods are too short, the MACD will indicate changes in trends too frequently to be useful because of noisy data; if the periods are too long, the detection of a change in trend will be delayed.

Concept_Name: Inventory Database
Core_Definition: A centralized system used to maintain a complete and up-to-date list of all assets, infrastructure components, software licenses, and maintenance contracts required to provide a service.
Key_Actors: Ordering process; provisioning process; decommissioning process; capacity planners; third-party vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Every resource required for the service, ranging from physical machines and cables to IP addresses and software licenses, is recorded in the database; the system is kept current by making it a core component of the ordering, provisioning, and decommissioning workflows; capacity planners query the database to determine the total quantity of each resource available; this data is combined with monitoring usage statistics to identify spare capacity and calculate utilization rates.
Rules_and_Constraints: The database must be kept up-to-date to be effective; it should include software licenses, maintenance contracts, and contracted third-party resources; utilizing a limited number of standard machine configurations is recommended to simplify the mapping of devices to low-level resources like CPU and RAM.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a specific component or resource is forgotten and not included in the database, it will be omitted from capacity planning, which can result in the inability to grow the service as quickly as needed later.

Concept_Name: Datacenter Utilization Rate
Core_Definition: An automatically generated metric that tracks the consumption of datacenter resources like space, power, and cooling relative to the total available capacity.
Key_Actors: Inventory database; standard hardware configurations; datacenter facilities.
Operational_Workflow: Detailed information for all physical devices is entered into the inventory database; organizations utilize a limited set of standard hardware configurations to easily map each device to its specific consumption of space, power, and cooling; the inventory system then uses this aggregated data to automatically generate the utilization rate for the facility.
Rules_and_Constraints: The calculation relies on having all relevant data entered into the inventory system; the use of standardized hardware configurations is necessary to facilitate the easy mapping of per-device resource usage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Postmortem Reports
Core_Definition: Documents that analyze previous service outages and usage spikes to provide essential data for determining necessary headroom levels.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Following a service interruption or an edge condition involving extended resource usage, a report is generated; capacity planners review these reports alongside hard statistical data from monitoring systems to understand when and how often spikes occur; this information is used to establish a reasonable percentage of headroom to prevent similar edge conditions from triggering future outages.
Rules_and_Constraints: These reports are considered key data points, alongside monitoring statistics, for making business decisions regarding how much excess capacity to maintain.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Failure Domain
Core_Definition: A distinct segment of infrastructure where a single outage can cause components to fail together.
Key_Actors: Components; primary machines; spare capacity; datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: Resiliency is achieved by placing spare capacity in a different failure domain than primary machines to ensure that a single outage does not take down both; spreading service capacity across many failure domains, such as multiple datacenters, reduces the total percentage of extra capacity required to maintain service during a failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Spare capacity must reside in a different failure domain than the primary machines; failure domains should be considered at a large scale, typically at the datacenter level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Facility-wide maintenance work on power systems that requires an entire building to shut down; the gold standard for resiliency is providing enough capacity for two datacenters to be down at the same time.

Concept_Name: Advanced Capacity Planning
Core_Definition: A capacity planning approach for large, high-growth environments that utilizes frequent statistical analysis and sophisticated mathematical models to forecast needs.
Key_Actors: Statisticians; capacity planners; technical staff; program managers; non-technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by identifying primary resources and core drivers such as active users or queries per second; planners determine capacity limits through load testing and measure user engagement; monitoring data is processed using regression analysis to find correlations and forecasting techniques like moving average convergence/divergence to detect trend changes; the process can be delegated to non-technical staff through the use of specialized dashboards.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach requires deeper technical knowledge and relies on sophisticated mathematical models; planning must focus on the primary limiting resource; the relationship between primary and ancillary usage must be defined and updated as hardware changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Rapidly changing customer bases that make standard enterprise techniques insufficient; unexpected shifts in resource needs caused by new software releases or changes in user demographics.

Concept_Name: Secondary Resource
Core_Definition: A system-level resource that is consumed by a service but is not currently the main limiting factor.
Key_Actors: Software versions; hardware models; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: While not the primary focus of capacity planning, these resources are monitored and their usage trends are tracked; they are used in calculating the constraint ratio against the primary resource.
Rules_and_Constraints: Secondary resources must be monitored because they may become the primary resource as code or hardware changes; they drive the need for ancillary resources such as server instances, switch ports, and power.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A secondary resource may become the primary resource for a service due to changes in the software version or the introduction of a new hardware model.

Concept_Name: Constraint Ratio
Core_Definition: The mathematical relationship between a service's primary resource and its secondary resources, such as the ratio of disk to CPU to memory to network.
Key_Actors: Primary resources; secondary resources; software versions; hardware models.
Operational_Workflow: The ratio is calculated to understand how different resources are consumed relative to one another; it is used to detect if the primary limiting factor of a service has shifted.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ratio must be recalculated whenever a new software version is released or a new hardware model is introduced.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load Testing
Core_Definition: A process used to determine capacity limits by identifying the point at which performance begins to degrade or become unpredictable.
Key_Actors: Technical staff; monitoring systems; isolated lab environments; synthetic traffic generators; production traffic replay systems.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff perform testing in an isolated lab environment using synthetic traffic or production traffic replays; the lab is equipped with excess capacity for all resources except for the single low-level resource being tested to isolate it as the limiting factor; response times are graphed against both the percentage utilization and the absolute amount of the resource available; the test is repeated several times while scaling the entire environment uniformly to determine if limits relate to utilization percentage or quantity of remaining resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tests must be performed in an isolated lab environment; researchers should provide excess capacity for all but one resource to isolate variables; environments should be scaled uniformly during repeated tests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For very large services, load testing may be unreliable because staging environments might lack the capacity required for a true test.

Concept_Name: 7-day actives (7DA)
Core_Definition: A metric representing the number of unique users who have accessed a service within the last seven days.
Key_Actors: Active users; monitoring systems; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks individual users who interact with the service over a rolling seven-day window; this count is identified as a core driver representing the factors that generate traffic or load on the service; capacity planners use this data to build capacity models that express how changes in user activity affect the need for primary resources like CPU or bandwidth.
Rules_and_Constraints: Use active user counts rather than total registered users to avoid misleading planning data; the specific timeframe used for the measurement must be appropriate for the specific service being planned.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For highly transactional systems, smaller time scales such as minutely active users may be a more appropriate indicator than 7-day windows.

Concept_Name: Weekly active users (WAU)
Core_Definition: A metric used to specify the number of unique users who were active during a specific calendar week.
Key_Actors: Active users; monitoring systems; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system records the number of users accessing the service within the bounds of a specific calendar week; this data serves as a core driver for the capacity planning process; planners analyze this metric to quantify the effect that user activity will have on the requirements for ancillary resources such as servers, switch ports, or load balancers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Measurements must be bounded by a specific calendar week; this metric should be used to provide a more accurate representation of usage than registered user counts.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 30-day actives (30DA)
Core_Definition: A metric that measures the number of unique users who have accessed the service within the last 30 days.
Key_Actors: Active users; monitoring systems; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system collects data on user activity over a rolling 30-day period; this value is utilized as a core driver to forecast trends in resource usage and calculate future capacity requirements; planners define a capacity model that expresses the relationship between this core driver and the consumption of primary and ancillary resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Active user counts should be used instead of registered user counts to ensure an accurate representation of service load; the chosen timeframe for the metric must reflect the actual usage patterns of the specific service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For storage-bound services, the total number of registered users or the total user corpus may be a more appropriate core driver than 30-day active counts.

Concept_Name: Minutely Active Users
Core_Definition: A core driver metric representing the number of users who have accessed a service within a one-minute timeframe. It is primarily used to represent factors that generate traffic or load for highly transactional systems.
Key_Actors: Active users; highly transactional systems; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: Capacity planners identify the minutely timeframe as an appropriate core driver for a specific service; the monitoring system tracks user activity at this granular interval; this data is used to define the relationship between user activity and primary resource consumption; the resulting metrics are used within a capacity model to forecast future resource requirements.
Rules_and_Constraints: Smaller time scales like minutely active users are appropriate for highly transactional systems driven by active users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Total User Corpus Number
Core_Definition: The total number of registered users in a system regardless of their recent activity levels. It is used as a core driver for services where resource needs are dictated by the size of the user base.
Key_Actors: Registered users; storage-bound services; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The system tracks the total count of registered user accounts; capacity planners select this metric as the core driver for storage-bound services; the planners use the corpus size to calculate current and future requirements for primary resources like disk space; the metric helps determine when to scale ancillary storage infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: This metric is the appropriate core driver for storage-bound services driven by users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Counting total registered users can be misleading for services not limited by storage because many users sign up and never return or use the service only occasionally.

Concept_Name: Capacity Model
Core_Definition: A representation of the relationship between a core driver and a primary resource. It identifies how changes in a specific driver affect a service's need for its primary limiting factor.
Key_Actors: Core drivers; primary resources; ancillary resources like server instances and switch ports; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: Planners identify the primary resource and the core driver for a service; they define the relationship describing how changes in the driver affect the resource; this model is used to quantify the required amount of ancillary resources; planners use the model to analyze if resources are balanced or if hardware and software adjustments are needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: The model must define a relationship between primary and ancillary usage; mappings must be updated as the hardware used changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a new software version or hardware model is introduced, the constraint ratio between resources may change, requiring the capacity model to be recalculated.

Concept_Name: Engagement graph
Core_Definition: A data visualization that tracks the number of times on average an active user performs a particular transaction in a given timeframe. It serves as a measure of the popularity of specific features.
Key_Actors: Active users; specific service features; monitoring systems; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks specific transactions performed by active users; planners plot this data to identify trends in feature popularity; the graph is analyzed to see how changes in user behavior impact resource needs; planners use the results to anticipate load increases that occur even if the total number of users remains static.
Rules_and_Constraints: The engagement graph for each service will be different; an increase in engagement for one feature will have a different effect on resource requirements than a change in another feature.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Load on a service can increase even when the number of active users stays static if the engagement level increases.

Concept_Name: Rolling correlation analysis
Core_Definition: A method of graphing and tracking the relationship between core drivers and resource consumption over time to detect changes in how they influence each other.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; monitoring systems; core driver metrics; resource usage metrics.
Operational_Workflow: A core driver and a primary resource are identified and their metrics are collected as time-series data; a regression analysis is performed over a specific time interval to calculate the correlation coefficient; these coefficients are plotted on a graph and monitored continuously rather than being assessed only once; changes in the resulting graph are used to identify when the relationship between the driver and the resource has shifted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Correlation should be graphed and tracked continuously rather than assessed once and assumed to be constant; the analysis should be used to trigger a reevaluation of resource usage predictions if a marked change occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Changes in the service or end-user demographics can significantly impact correlation; software releases can cause sharp drops or step-changes in usage patterns; during the window of time where a moving average contains both pre-upgrade and post-upgrade data, the analysis may show large fluctuations until sufficient time has passed for the average to stabilize.

Concept_Name: Regression line
Core_Definition: A mathematical equation of the form y = a + bx that describes the relationship between a core driver and a primary resource to enable requirement calculations.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; statisticians; core driver measurements (x); primary resource requirements (y).
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff define a time interval and collect a specific number of data samples for both the core driver and the primary resource; mathematical calculations are performed to determine the correlation coefficient to ensure the data sources match closely; if the correlation is high, moving averages for the data points are used to calculate the constants a and b; the resulting equation is then used to predict how much of a primary resource will be needed for any given value of a core driver.
Rules_and_Constraints: The regression line can only be derived when changes in a core driver correlate well with changes in primary resource usage; calculations of the constants a and b require the moving average of the last n data points for both variables.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the correlation coefficient is low, the regression line cannot be used with confidence; any marked change in correlation requires a reevaluation of the multiplier b and the corresponding resource usage predictions.

Concept_Name: Forecasting
Core_Definition: The practice of predicting future resource needs based on the analysis of current and past historical measurements.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; capacity planning teams; statisticians; program managers.
Operational_Workflow: Historical usage data is graphed, typically using the 90th percentile, to find an equation that best fits the data; researchers use Exponential Moving Averages to weight recent data more heavily than old data; the Moving Average Convergence/Divergence (MACD) is calculated to measure the difference between short-period and long-period moving averages; a MACD signal line is plotted to detect changes in trends; the system alerts the capacity planning team when the MACD line crosses the signal line, indicating an acceleration in the direction of the trend.
Rules_and_Constraints: The model should be validated by checking if older data successfully predicts more recent data; the weighting for recent data in the Exponential Moving Average must decrease exponentially for older data points; long and short time periods must be chosen carefully to balance the speed of detection against the risk of noise.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If periods for analysis are too short, the forecasting will indicate trend changes too frequently to be useful; if periods are too long, the detection of a change in trend will be delayed; noisy data can lead to upward and downward triggers occurring in rapid succession.

Concept_Name: Capacity Planning Dashboard
Core_Definition: A specialized view within a monitoring system that presents capacity data and automated graphs to allow non-technical staff to make resource decisions.
Key_Actors: Program managers; non-technical staff; technical staff who build the dashboard; monitoring system.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff build specialized web pages as part of the existing monitoring system; capacity-specific data is integrated into a view that features automated graphing capabilities; the dashboard is made accessible to the wider organization independently of the main monitoring dashboard; non-technical staff use the visualized trends and ratios to justify capital expenditures or identify where efficiency improvements are needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: The dashboard must be accessible within the organization separately from the main monitoring dashboard; it must present data in a reasonable form to justify capital expenditure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Resource Regression
Core_Definition: A calculation of the difference in resource usage between one software release or version and another.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; technical staff; software developers; software releases or versions.
Operational_Workflow: Planners perform a workflow analysis based on the user transactions associated with a new release; they use automated testing scripts on a staging system to simulate transactions; they compare the resource usage data of the new release against a baseline system; they calculate resource projections by multiplying capability costs by projected transactions and active customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every software release is expected to have slightly different resource needs; significant unintended increases in resource usage are considered bugs that should be reported; intended increases require capacity planners to adjust their models and purchasing plans.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a new software release uses significantly more resources and the change was unintended, it is treated as a software bug; discrepancies in resource usage can also be caused by changes in the end-user demographic or software designs.

Concept_Name: Workflow Analysis
Core_Definition: A process of listing the capabilities possible with a new release and identifying the specific resource usage associated with each capability.
Key_Actors: User transactions; customers; infrastructure services such as DNS and databases.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff create a list of all capabilities possible with a new software release; they identify which resource usage goes with each individual capability; they multiply the usage by the projected number of transactions per customer and the total number of active customers; they include secondary infrastructure costs such as DNS lookups and database calls to create a final resource projection.
Rules_and_Constraints: The analysis must include infrastructure resource costs and every service within the system infrastructure that a transaction touches; predictions should be load tested to determine if resource usage scales linearly or if inflection points appear.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Resource usage may fail to scale linearly as more transactions are added, resulting in inflection points that must be identified through load testing.

Concept_Name: Baseline Footprint
Core_Definition: The measurement of resource usage on a staging system when no simulated users or transactions are active.
Key_Actors: Staging systems; monitoring systems; memory, disk, CPU, and network resources.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff set up a staging system to mimic production; they collect monitoring data on resources like memory, disk, CPU, and network while the system is in an idle state with no users; this data is used as a reference point to be subtracted from the loaded footprint.
Rules_and_Constraints: The baseline footprint must be subtracted from the loaded footprint to accurately determine the resource usage caused specifically by user transactions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Loaded Footprint
Core_Definition: The measurement of resource usage on a staging system while it is being exercised by a known number of users and transactions.
Key_Actors: Automated testing scripts; sample users; staging systems; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff use automated testing scripts to simulate user logins and specific transactions; the monitoring system gathers data on resource usage while these activities are occurring; the data is compared against the baseline footprint to calculate the per-transaction resource cost.
Rules_and_Constraints: The differences in memory, disk, CPU, and network usage between the loaded footprint and the baseline are assumed to be caused by the user transactions; the difference is divided by the total number of user transactions to find the cost per transaction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Soft Launch
Core_Definition: A technique where a product or feature is enabled in production but not publicly announced to allow for a slow ramp-up of active users.
Key_Actors: Engineers; customers; well-known companies such as Google and Facebook.
Operational_Workflow: The service is enabled without a public announcement; engineers observe the system as users naturally begin to use the feature; problems are identified and fixed as they occur during the slow growth period.
Rules_and_Constraints: This method is intended to give engineers time to find and fix problems; it is most effective when the ramp-up is slow enough to allow for resource adjustments if lead times are long.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For highly popular or well-known companies, a soft launch may not result in a slow ramp-up as the service can be immediately flooded with users; if additional resources require weeks of lead time, only the slowest ramp-ups provide a benefit.

Concept_Name: Dark Launch
Core_Definition: A method of releasing a new feature into production using simulated traffic to test resource needs without creating user-visible information.
Key_Actors: Engineers; service agents; customers whose activity triggers the simulated load; Facebook.
Operational_Workflow: A new feature is released into production with no user-visible elements; real user activity is used to trigger simulated activity or service agents exercise the feature silently; resource regression analysis is performed on the simulated traffic to determine capacity costs; bugs are identified and fixed invisibly to the customer; the percentage of simulated transactions is gradually increased until it reaches the level of expected real usage; the dark launch is turned off and the feature is officially turned on for customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process should not create user-visible information; it employs real user activity as a trigger for simulated transactions; it requires the use of software and dark launch toggles.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While toggles allow changes without new releases, the bug fixing that occurs during a dark launch often results in upgrading new releases during the period.

Concept_Name: Software Toggle
Core_Definition: A control mechanism used to switch a specific software feature between an on or off state.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: A toggle is created within the code for a specific feature; the state of the toggle determines whether the feature is active or inactive within the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Used in conjunction with dark launch toggles to manage the transition from testing to a live feature; allows for features to be turned on for customers without necessarily rolling a new release.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dark Launch Toggle
Core_Definition: A specific toggle used to control the activation and volume of simulated transactions used for testing during a dark launch.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: A toggle is created to manage a specific dark launch feature; existing transactions are modified to check the state of this toggle; when the toggle is on, a specified percentage of real user transactions trigger a corresponding simulated transaction that is saved to a dummy area; the percentage of triggered transactions is gradually increased to test higher loads.
Rules_and_Constraints: Used to feed production-level data into a new feature silently; it is recommended to start with a conservative percentage such as 5 percent; the results are used to adjust capacity models based on actual production environment usage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Provisioning Time
Core_Definition: Provisioning time is the total duration required to acquire, configure, and put new resources into production. The text also uses this term to describe the specific phase from when hardware arrives at a loading dock until it is active in the service.
Key_Actors: Technicians; technical staff; automated configuration systems.
Operational_Workflow: New hardware is acquired through the ordering process; technicians unbox and rack mount the systems once they arrive at the loading dock; staff perform necessary testing and burn-in procedures; automated configuration systems prepare the hardware for use; the resources are placed into production to handle service load.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reducing provisioning time is a priority because it decreases the amount of capital investment that remains idle; a shorter provisioning window allows an organization to maintain less headroom while remaining responsive to growth.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lead Time
Core_Definition: Lead time is the total time elapsed from the moment a need for resources is identified until those resources are delivered and in service. It is a critical metric for determining how much spare capacity or headroom is required.
Key_Actors: Purchase order approvers; vendors; technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins when the need for additional capacity is identified; purchase orders are approved and submitted to the vendor; the vendor fulfills the order and delivers the goods; equipment is tested and installed during specific change windows; finally, the services are reconfigured to utilize the new capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Headroom must be sufficient to cover expected growth during the lead time period; physical services generally have a longer lead time than virtual services; reducing lead time improves financial efficiency by reducing the need for excess capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Supply Chain Management
Core_Definition: Supply chain management involves working closely with vendors to optimize the ordering process and reduce the time required to receive resources. It is a strategy used to improve the speed of capacity expansion.
Key_Actors: Vendors; purchasing departments; technical staff.
Operational_Workflow: Staff establish relationships with vendors to streamline ordering procedures; the organization may place many smaller, frequent orders rather than a single large order to improve parallelism; vendors may coordinate with the organization to provide periodic deliveries and monthly billing cycles for large orders.
Rules_and_Constraints: Moving from large, infrequent orders to smaller, monthly orders can provide benefits for capital costs, labor management, and billing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automate Configuration
Core_Definition: This concept refers to using automated systems to prepare newly acquired hardware for service immediately after it has been physically installed. It is a primary method for reducing resource delivery time.
Key_Actors: Technical staff; automated configuration systems.
Operational_Workflow: Hardware is physically installed and racked in the datacenter; automated configuration tools apply the necessary software settings and configurations; the hardware is transitioned from an idle state to an active state within the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automation should be used to prepare resources for use as it has immediate value in reducing idle time; reducing delivery time through automation is cost-effective because it reduces the amount of excess capacity an organization must pay for.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Chapter 19. Creating KPIs
Section: Chapter 19. Creating KPIs
============================================================

Concept_Name: Key Performance Indicator (KPI)
Core_Definition: A type of performance measurement used to evaluate the success of an organization or a particular activity, generally used to encourage teams to reach a particular goal.
Key_Actors: Executive management; other levels of management; employees; engineers; developers; operations teams; customers; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Management sets priorities and provides resources; the creator envisions the ideal state if the goal were met; the distance to the ideal is quantified through numerical measurements; the creator imagines how behavior will change to maximize personal gain; the KPI is revised and selected; the KPI is deployed as policy through communication and automated measurement mechanisms; data is visualized on dashboards; results are audited for unintended side effects.
Rules_and_Constraints: KPIs should be directly tied to the organization’s strategy, vision, or mission; they must follow the SMART criteria; success or failure must be objectively quantified; the creation process requires serious time and effort; measurements should be real and repeatable; metrics should be collected automatically without human intervention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: KPIs can have unintended side effects if people find the shortest path to a goal at the expense of other business needs; employees may follow the rules as written rather than the manager's actual intention; gaming the system involves falsifying logs or bypassing KPIs; non-measurable goals like getting better at a skill cannot be used as KPIs.

Concept_Name: Measurement affects behavior
Core_Definition: The principle that people change their behavior and tend to find the shortest path to meeting a goal when they know they are being measured.
Key_Actors: Employees; managers; engineers; salespeople; developer teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Management establishes a measurement or KPI; individuals analyze the reward or incentive structure; individuals adjust their actions to maximize their personal gain or conform to the specific rules stated; the resulting behavior often creates unintended side effects that may deviate from the original intent.
Rules_and_Constraints: Managers must set goals that drive desired behavior while minimizing unintended consequences; do not expect employees to read the manager's mind or understand the intention; people will conform to what is written rather than what is intended.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a KPI is too narrow it may cause a company to spend its way out of business; engineers might cancel failing tasks or ignore wait times to make metrics look better; gaming the system by falsifying data is distinct from simply following the provided rules.

Concept_Name: Scientific methods
Core_Definition: A management approach where the quality of a system is measured, policies are set or changed, and then measurements are taken again to observe the effect.
Key_Actors: Managers; teams responsible for the KPI.
Operational_Workflow: Baseline measurements are taken before introducing a new KPI; a policy or KPI is introduced to the organization; measurements are taken again after the introduction; the differences are observed and evaluated to verify if priorities are being met and to ensure the KPI is effective.
Rules_and_Constraints: This approach moves management away from a loose set of guesses; effectiveness must be evaluated by making measurements before and after; the process requires identifying a way to quantify how close the current state is to the ideal state.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This process is described as more difficult than it sounds; if metrics cannot be collected automatically they must be managed through manual reminders or the development of new automation mechanisms.

Concept_Name: SMART criteria
Core_Definition: A framework for defining effective KPIs that ensures they are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-phrased.
Key_Actors: Managers; organization; project teams.
Operational_Workflow: Managers apply these five criteria during the creation and revision process to ensure the KPI is unambiguously defined and objectively quantifiable before it is deployed as policy.
Rules_and_Constraints: Specific means the KPI is unambiguously defined and not overly broad; Measurable means success or failure can be objectively quantified; Achievable means the goal can be reached under reasonable circumstances; Relevant means the goal pertains to the success of the organization or project; Time-phrased means the relevant time period for the goal is specified.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Non-measurable goals like providing more free disk space cannot be quantified and do not meet the criteria; the text notes that 100 percent availability is often not achievable under reasonable circumstances whereas 99.99 percent is.

Concept_Name: Uptime
Core_Definition: The measure of service availability, often expressed as a percentage, used to evaluate the success and stability of an organization or a particular activity.
Key_Actors: Executive management; operations teams; developer teams; end users; services.
Operational_Workflow: Management sets an availability goal such as 99.99 percent; a budget for downtime is established for a specific period like a quarter; any service unavailability subtracts from this budget; if the budget is exhausted, new feature launches are halted until the next period.
Rules_and_Constraints: KPIs for uptime should be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-phrased; 100 percent uptime is considered unachievable and stifles innovation; goals should be directly tied to the organization’s strategy or mission.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority security fixes may be exempt from launch freezes when the downtime budget is exhausted; user-visible services might use graceful degradation to maintain perceived uptime even if sub-services fail.

Concept_Name: Disk space
Core_Definition: A quantifiable resource quantity provided to users or systems that can be measured and set as a specific numerical goal.
Key_Actors: Users; systems; management.
Operational_Workflow: Management identifies the need for resource allocation; a specific target is set such as providing 10T per user; the actual amount provided is tracked and quantified to determine if the specific goal has been met.
Rules_and_Constraints: To be an effective KPI, disk space goals must be measurable and include specific numerical values rather than vague statements like providing more space; they should be achievable under reasonable circumstances.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Major features released
Core_Definition: A performance measurement based on the count of significant new software capabilities or updates deployed to production within a specified timeframe.
Key_Actors: Developer teams; operations teams; management; end customers.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create new features to meet business needs; management sets a goal for the number of major features to be released within a specific time period like a month; features are pushed into production unless restricted by stability policies or exhausted error budgets.
Rules_and_Constraints: This metric should be time-phrased and specific; release frequency is often balanced against stability goals using mechanisms like an error budget to prevent undue risk and align priorities between developers and operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a downtime budget is exhausted, the release of new features is stopped; developers might try to circumvent release rules by hiding untested code behind flag flips or encoding features in configuration files.

Concept_Name: Page load time
Core_Definition: The duration of time it takes for a web page to fully display or become usable for a user, serving as a key indicator of web site speed and service quality.
Key_Actors: Users; developers; management; machines and resources.
Operational_Workflow: Management identifies site speed as a business priority; a specific target like 300 ms is set; developers optimize code and administrators allocate dedicated resources or faster machines to reach the target; measurements are taken before and after changes to evaluate the effect of policies.
Rules_and_Constraints: The KPI should be based on the end-to-end result from the user perspective; it must be measurable and objectively quantified; goals should drive desired behavior while minimizing unintended side effects like overspending.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Focusing too narrowly on this KPI can lead to unintended consequences such as delaying other features for code optimization or overspending on hardware that compromises the company's financial health.

Concept_Name: Severity 1 open bugs
Core_Definition: An example of a measurable goal used as a key performance indicator to objectively quantify the success or failure of a team in managing software defects.
Key_Actors: Management; engineering teams; organizations.
Operational_Workflow: A numerical limit is set by management such as having fewer than ten open bugs of this specific classification; the count of these bugs is monitored to determine what fraction of the goal was achieved or if it was met at all; this measurement is used to evaluate the success of an organization or a particular activity.
Rules_and_Constraints: The goal must be measurable so that success or failure can be objectively quantified; it must follow the SMART criteria by being specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-phrased.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Metrics
Core_Definition: Numerical quantifications used to measure the distance from a current state to an ideal goal and to drive organizational behavior.
Key_Actors: Managers; engineers; monitoring systems; automated logging systems.
Operational_Workflow: Potential metrics are identified by quantifying the distance to an ideal state such as time durations or counts; these metrics are ideally collected automatically through monitoring systems or by extracting information from logs without human intervention; once collected the data is visualized on a dashboard or large display to show the status of key performance indicators; if automation is not possible, manual collection is ensured through automated email reminders or calendar entries.
Rules_and_Constraints: Metrics should be real and repeatable; they should be collected automatically if possible; they must be used to calculate key performance indicators that are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-phrased; measurements should be taken before and after introducing a policy to observe differences scientifically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If metrics are not available for a new KPI, they may be calculated based on simulated data; rumors about new metrics can spread and hurt morale if they are perceived as harsher than the actual intended measurement; people may follow the metric exactly as written rather than as intended, leading to unintended side effects.

Concept_Name: Intel’s OKR System
Core_Definition: A goal-setting system used at the personal, team, division, and company levels to define objectives and track their achievement.
Key_Actors: Intel; Google; John Doerr; Rick Klau; teams and divisions within an organization.
Operational_Workflow: The system is employed to set goals across various hierarchical levels of an organization; objectives are established to define desired outcomes; the progress toward these objectives is then measured via key results which are often quantified using key performance indicators.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system relies on the relationship between broad objectives and the specific measurable results that indicate their completion.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: OKRs (objectives and key results)
Core_Definition: A framework for setting goals where objectives define the target and key results provide the measurable benchmarks for success.
Key_Actors: John Doerr; Rick Klau; Google; Intel; management teams.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations use this system to drive behavior by establishing clear objectives; for each objective, specific key results are identified to track progress; these key results are frequently measured using key performance indicators to ensure they are quantifiable and objective.
Rules_and_Constraints: OKRs are used to set goals at the personal, team, division, and company levels; they are intended to be measurable so that the fraction of the goal achieved can be unambiguously determined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Creating good KPIs
Core_Definition: A performance measurement used to evaluate the success of an organization or activity by setting measurable goals that drive desired behavior.
Key_Actors: Executive management; managers at all levels; engineers; employees; teams; organizations.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with envisioning the ideal end result if a goal is met; next management determines how to quantify the distance from the current state to that ideal; potential KPIs are analyzed to predict how people might change their behavior to meet the incentive; the KPI is revised based on these predictions and feedback; finally the KPI is deployed through communication and automated measurement collection displayed on dashboards.
Rules_and_Constraints: A good KPI must follow the SMART criteria which means it is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-phrased; it should be directly tied to the organization’s strategy, vision, or mission; goals must be quantifiable so success or failure can be objectively determined; the final KPI must be easy to understand.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Measurement affects behavior and can cause unintended side effects as people find the shortest path to meeting a goal; if a KPI is changed too frequently management loses trust and employee morale suffers; if a KPI is created without forethought its unintended consequences can negate any benefits or even drive a company out of business.

Concept_Name: Return on investment (ROI)
Core_Definition: The significant financial and performance payoff gained by dedicating management time to the creation of effective KPIs.
Key_Actors: Managers; engineers; organizations.
Operational_Workflow: Managers dedicate approximately 10 hours of planning to create effective KPIs for their teams; these KPIs are designed to improve team performance by an estimated 10 to 30 percent; the value of this improvement is calculated based on the total cost of engineers including salary and benefits.
Rules_and_Constraints: The total cost of a typical engineer is estimated at $200,000 or more per year; for an organization of 50 engineers a 10 to 30 percent improvement is worth 1 million to 3 million dollars per year; the payoff for the time invested by a manager is estimated at a ratio of 1:1000 or 1:3000.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If KPIs are created with little forethought the unintended side effects can negate the benefit; spending money to meet a KPI without considering the broader business impact can lead to a company spending its way out of business.

Concept_Name: Envision the Ideal
Core_Definition: The first step in the KPI creation process where one imagines what the world would look like if a goal was met perfectly.
Key_Actors: Managers; subteams.
Operational_Workflow: The manager pauses to imagine the perfect end result of a goal; they describe how the company, project, or service would be different from today; the manager looks for inspiration from subteams that are already performing well in the target area; the focus is kept on the end result rather than the specific methods used to get there.
Rules_and_Constraints: Creativity must be exercised to think in terms of the final outcome; managers should identify qualities to reproduce such as culture or tangible outcomes; this step must be completed before creating a KPI formula.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The most common mistake is skipping this step and going directly to a KPI formula; without deciding on the destination first it is unlikely the organization will reach the desired goal.

Concept_Name: KPI formula
Core_Definition: The specific mathematical or numerical quantification used to measure progress toward an ideal goal.
Key_Actors: Managers; employees; users; operational staff.
Operational_Workflow: A formula is developed to quantify the distance from the current state to the ideal state; the formula is tested against past or current metrics or simulated data to verify its effectiveness; the formula is refined to prevent people from maximizing personal gain at the expense of the intended goal; the final formula is implemented into automated data collection systems and visual dashboards.
Rules_and_Constraints: Formulas should use percentiles rather than averages to avoid being skewed by outliers; the formula should measure end-to-end results from the customer's perspective; it should be real and repeatable; the formula must be easy to understand so that it is easy to follow.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: People will follow the rules of the formula as written rather than the manager's intent; narrow formulas may encourage bad behavior such as operators canceling difficult requests to improve their timing metrics; a formula that is too simple may contain bugs such as measuring intrusions without measuring the coverage of the detection system itself.

Concept_Name: Quantify Distance to the Ideal
Core_Definition: A process in KPI creation where managers determine numerical ways to measure how far the current state of a system or organization is from a perfectly realized goal.
Key_Actors: Managers who create the KPIs; engineers or subteams whose performance is being measured.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins after envisioning what the world would look like if a goal was met perfectly; the manager asks how the current distance from that ideal can be measured; many candidate KPIs are created to quantify this distance; measurements are identified as time durations, counts, frequencies, or quantities; the process ensures the measurement is real and repeatable.
Rules_and_Constraints: The measurement must be a numerical quantification; it must be real and repeatable; it should be based on an envisioned end result rather than the method of achieving it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A common mistake is skipping the envisioning step and going directly to creating a KPI formula, which often results in not reaching the desired destination.

Concept_Name: Security breaches
Core_Definition: A metric representing the count of known security violations detected within a specific timeframe, typically used to measure progress toward an ideal of zero security incidents.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Management establishes an ideal world with no security breaches; the distance to this ideal is measured by the count of known breaches detected each month; this count serves as a potential KPI to evaluate organizational success in maintaining security.
Rules_and_Constraints: A KPI based on security breaches must be revised to include the coverage of the intrusion detection system to prevent unintended behavior; it should be part of a triad of KPIs that includes the total number of intrusions and the duration of investigations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the KPI only measures detected breaches, people may be incentivized to turn off detection mechanisms to meet the goal, which is an unintended side effect that requires a revision of the KPI.

Concept_Name: Intrusions investigated
Core_Definition: A performance metric that quantifies the extent to which detected security incidents are reviewed and analyzed by the organization.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: The organization envisions an ideal where all detected security intrusions are investigated within a specific time frame; the process involves quantifying the time from detection to the start of investigation and the duration of the investigation itself; this data is used to reflect the duration and thoroughness of the investigative response.
Rules_and_Constraints: This metric is one of three revised KPIs suggested for security, specifically focusing on the duration of investigations to ensure timely responses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Time from detection to start of investigation
Core_Definition: A specific time-based measurement that calculates the duration between the moment a security intrusion is identified and the moment an investigation into that intrusion begins.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: This measurement is used to quantify the distance to an ideal world where all intrusions are investigated within a certain time frame; it is recorded alongside the duration of the investigation to provide a complete picture of the response time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Duration of the investigation
Core_Definition: A numerical quantification representing the length of time from the start of a security breach investigation to its conclusion.
Key_Actors: Security teams; detection systems; managers.
Operational_Workflow: A security breach is detected by automated mechanisms; the time from detection to the start of the investigation is recorded; the duration of the actual investigation is measured; these metrics are used together to quantify how far the organization is from the ideal state of security.
Rules_and_Constraints: The measurement must be real and repeatable; it should be used in conjunction with other metrics like detection coverage and total number of intrusions to prevent distorted incentives.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If this metric is used without corresponding metrics for detection coverage, staff might turn off breach detection systems to avoid having any investigations to perform.

Concept_Name: Imagine How Behavior Will Change
Core_Definition: A critical step in the KPI creation process where managers attempt to defeat a potential KPI by identifying all the ways people might maximize personal gain while still technically matching the incentive.
Key_Actors: Managers; employees; engineers; salespeople.
Operational_Workflow: A potential KPI is proposed; the creator brainstorms ways the metric can be met through shortest-path behaviors; the creator identifies unintended side effects; the KPI is revised based on these findings; this process is repeated until a final KPI is established.
Rules_and_Constraints: Managers must set aside hope that employees will just behave; they must assume employees will follow the formula provided rather than the manager's unspoken intentions; the analysis must consider how individuals will maximize personal gain.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Skipping this step is identified as a common mistake that leads to unintended side effects that negate the benefits of the KPI.

Concept_Name: Gaming the system
Core_Definition: The act of using dishonest means, such as falsifying logs, to go around the intended purpose of KPIs for personal benefit.
Key_Actors: Engineers; employees being measured.
Operational_Workflow: An individual identifies a way to circumvent the intended purpose of a KPI through dishonest means; the individual takes actions that go around the KPI parameters to benefit themselves; this behavior is distinguished from simply following the rules as written.
Rules_and_Constraints: This behavior is categorized as distinct from conforming to what was written in a KPI; following a poorly written formula is not considered gaming the system but is simply doing what one was told to do.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Falsifying logs
Core_Definition: A specific method of gaming the system where an individual alters data records to dishonestly benefit their performance metrics.
Key_Actors: Engineers; employees being measured.
Operational_Workflow: A KPI relies on logs for measurement; an actor changes or creates false entries in these logs to maximize their personal gain or meet a goal; this allows them to appear to satisfy the KPI requirements while circumventing the actual goal.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is explicitly categorized as an example of gaming the system rather than simply following the rules or conforming to a written KPI.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Revise and Select
Core_Definition: A stage in the KPI creation process where potential indicators are adjusted or chosen based on lessons learned about potential behavioral changes.
Key_Actors: Managers; team members whose work will be judged; individuals who benefit from the KPI goal; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Review lessons from Step 3 regarding how behavior might change; seek confirming and non-confirming data using past metrics; use simulated data if metrics are unavailable; gather opinions from others while filtering for personal bias or vested interests; test the KPI on a single team or project; employ the KPI during a trial period for further adjustments; finalize the KPI to ensure it is easy to understand and follow.
Rules_and_Constraints: Great effort should be put into getting it right the first time; the KPI must be easy to understand; revisions should be minimized after a policy is announced to maintain employee trust and morale; discussions about draft KPIs should be limited to specific individuals to prevent rumors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a KPI is announced and has unintended negative side effects it should be modified to fix bugs; frequent changes lead to management looking incompetent and cause morale to suffer; rumors can misconstrue the KPI and persist even if the draft is discarded.

Concept_Name: Confirming and non-confirming data
Core_Definition: Information used to validate potential KPIs by calculating them against historical, current, or artificial scenarios.
Key_Actors: Managers or KPI creators; data systems containing historical or current metrics.
Operational_Workflow: Identify past or current metrics that are relevant to the proposed KPI; calculate the KPI based on these existing metrics; analyze the results to determine if the KPI achieves the intended goal; use the findings to either select or revise the potential KPI during the creation process.
Rules_and_Constraints: This data should be sought during the Revise and Select stage of KPI creation; it acts as a tool for testing the KPI's effectiveness before official announcement or deployment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When historical or current metrics are not available to provide this data, creators should test the KPI against simulated data instead.

Concept_Name: Simulated data
Core_Definition: Artificially generated metrics used to test the effectiveness of a potential KPI when real historical or current data is unavailable.
Key_Actors: Managers or KPI creators.
Operational_Workflow: Confirm that existing metrics are insufficient for testing the KPI; generate artificial data points representing potential operational scenarios; calculate the proposed KPI using this information; evaluate if the KPI drives the desired behavior to inform revisions or final selection.
Rules_and_Constraints: Used specifically as an alternative testing method during the Revise and Select phase when real-world metrics are missing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Children’s game “Telephone”
Core_Definition: An analogy used to describe how a message or unannounced KPI becomes increasingly misconstrued and negative as it is passed from person to person.
Key_Actors: Managers seeking feedback; employees sharing rumors about potential policies.
Operational_Workflow: Management discusses potential KPIs to gather feedback before they are announced; rumors begin to spread about the new KPI; the message moves from person to person; the rumor invariably portrays the KPI as harsher than it actually is as the information becomes more distorted at each step.
Rules_and_Constraints: Potential KPIs should only be discussed with a specific understanding of who the information can be shared with; feedback must be gathered carefully to avoid hurting morale or creating misinformation that confuses people when the real KPI is announced.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The worst situation occurs when a draft KPI is thrown out but the rumors about it continue to persist among the staff.

Concept_Name: Operator-initiated cancellations
Core_Definition: Cancellations of service requests, such as virtual machine creation, performed by the operational staff rather than the user.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; operators; team managers; executive management.
Operational_Workflow: An operator receives a request for a service like a virtual machine; if the task encounters a failure or takes too long, the operator cancels the job rather than retrying it to prevent the delay from negatively affecting the creation time KPI.
Rules_and_Constraints: These actions must be logged and distinguished from customer cancellations; management should discourage this behavior through high standards and oversight; they should be investigated if there are more than five occurrences within a week.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the system fails to log specifically who cancelled a request, operational staff may use these cancellations as a loophole to game the KPI and make their performance appear better than it is.

Concept_Name: Customer-initiated cancellation
Core_Definition: The termination of a service request by the user who originally submitted it.
Key_Actors: Customers; users; management.
Operational_Workflow: A user submits a request for a resource through a ticket or portal; the user subsequently cancels the request before it is completed; management tracks these events to monitor for suspicious patterns.
Rules_and_Constraints: The total number of these cancellations should be published to allow management to watch for shenanigans; it is used as a secondary metric to ensure the primary KPI is not being manipulated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If more than 1 percent of all requests end in this type of cancellation, it triggers a requirement for investigation.

Concept_Name: Intrusion detection system
Core_Definition: A security mechanism used to monitor subnets and detect unauthorized access or security breaches.
Key_Actors: Security systems; investigative teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system monitors assigned subnets for breaches; when an intrusion is detected, it is logged as a security breach; an investigation is then launched to address the detection.
Rules_and_Constraints: Effectiveness is measured by the percentage of subnets covered by the system; KPIs must include both the count of detected intrusions and the duration of the subsequent investigation to provide an accurate picture of security health.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An unintended side effect may occur where staff turn off the detection mechanisms entirely to meet a goal of zero security breaches.

Concept_Name: Deploy the KPI
Core_Definition: The act of instituting a KPI as official policy while establishing the necessary communication and measurement infrastructure.
Key_Actors: The team responsible for the KPI; key stakeholders; management.
Operational_Workflow: The new KPI is communicated to the responsible team and stakeholders; automated collection of metrics is established via monitoring systems or logs; a web-based dashboard is created to visualize the results; important KPIs are displayed on large monitors in common areas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment should be treated as a communication function; metrics should be collected automatically without human intervention whenever possible; the KPI should be deployed with the assumption that future revisions may be necessary.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If automated collection is not yet possible, manual workarounds such as automated email reminders or calendar entries must be used as a stopgap while an automated mechanism is developed.

Concept_Name: Monitoring system
Core_Definition: A mechanism used to automatically collect the metrics required to calculate key performance indicators.
Key_Actors: Management; the team responsible for the KPI; key stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are collected automatically by the system; data accumulates without human intervention; these metrics are used to calculate KPIs; the results are presented in a dashboard for review.
Rules_and_Constraints: Collection should be automated to ensure metrics accumulate even if forgotten; there are no metrics that cannot be collected automatically, only those not yet automated; the system should provide data that allows success or failure to be objectively quantified.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If metrics cannot be collected automatically, manual collection must be performed through automated email reminders or repeating calendar entries until an automated mechanism is developed.

Concept_Name: Extracting information from logs
Core_Definition: An automated process of gathering KPI metrics by retrieving relevant data points and timestamps from system-generated logs.
Key_Actors: The system generating logs; automated processes or scripts used for post-processing.
Operational_Workflow: The system records event data such as request and completion times in logs; an automated process extracts these timestamps; post-processing is performed to correlate the data and calculate specific durations or counts.
Rules_and_Constraints: The extraction process should be automated and not require human intervention; systems should log specific details, such as identifying whether an operator or a customer initiated a cancellation, to ensure the KPI measurements are accurate.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Dashboard
Core_Definition: A web page that provides a visualization of a KPI and related metrics for status tracking and data analysis.
Key_Actors: Teams responsible for the KPI; key stakeholders; management; employees in common areas.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are collected and computed; the resulting KPI is presented on a web page; users view the visualization; users drill down into related metrics and information to analyze the data further.
Rules_and_Constraints: The dashboard must show a visualization of the KPI; it should include information useful for drilling down into the data; important KPIs require a second dashboard version for large displays in common areas using large fonts and graphics easy to view from a distance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Data visualization system
Core_Definition: A system that stores gathered metrics and processes them to create graphical representations for dashboards.
Key_Actors: Automated collection mechanisms; graphical dashboard interfaces.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are gathered from the monitoring system or logs; the metrics are stored; the data is made available to the visualization system; the system generates graphical dashboards from the stored metrics.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must be capable of generating graphical dashboards from stored metrics; it should support visualizations that serve as reminders of KPI status and can become points of pride for the team when goals are met.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Machine Allocation KPI
Core_Definition: A performance measurement used to assess the quality of the process by which virtual machines are created, specifically measuring the duration from the initial request to when the machine is usable.
Key_Actors: Users or requesters; teams creating VMs such as internal cloud teams or public cloud providers; VM Managers; operational staff; managers; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins when a request is initially received from the user and timestamped; the VM Manager then allocates resources including RAM, disk, and vCPU; the operating system is installed and necessary configurations like DNS changes and access controls are implemented; the process ends when the user is able to log into the machine and use it for their intended purpose; finally, the 90th percentile creation time is calculated and displayed on a dashboard.
Rules_and_Constraints: The KPI should use a percentile measurement rather than an average to avoid being skewed by outliers; manual and automatic retries after a failure must be counted as part of the original request duration; the end-to-end result must be measured from the customer's perspective; metrics used to calculate the KPI should be collected automatically via monitoring systems or logs; results should be visualized on a web-based dashboard and potentially large hall displays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If capacity planning is insufficient, requests may be held in a queue while waiting for new capacity to come online; operators might attempt to cancel long-running or incomplete requests to artificially improve metrics; the KPI requires logging and investigating if there are more than five operator-initiated cancellations per week or if customer cancellations exceed one percent.

Concept_Name: Virtual machines (VMs)
Core_Definition: Simulated computing environments that are allocated specific hardware resources and an operating system to fulfill user requests for computing capacity.
Key_Actors: Users or requesters; VM Manager; OS installation mechanism.
Operational_Workflow: A user submits a request specifying the desired name, size in terms of RAM, disk, and vCPU, and the operating system; the VM Manager allocates the physical resources to create the empty virtual machine; the designated OS installation mechanism loads the software; once the operating system is loaded and access is configured, the user logs in to begin using the machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: VMs must be usable for the specific purpose the user intended; they should possess the exact attributes such as name and size requested by the user; billing arrangements should be established for the resources used to encourage users to restrain their consumption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A virtual machine is considered worthless to the user if it is failed or only half-installed; the OS installation process may be unreliable and require multiple restarts; resource shortages can prevent the creation of new VMs until additional capacity is installed.

Concept_Name: Helpdesk request system
Core_Definition: A manual or semi-automated system where users submit service requests by creating tickets that are later processed by operational staff.
Key_Actors: Users or customers; operational staff; managers.
Operational_Workflow: A user creates a ticket in the system to request a resource like a virtual machine; the ticket enters a queue where it waits for an available staff member; a staff member eventually processes the ticket and manually enters data or triggers the creation process; if information is missing, back-and-forth communication occurs between the staff and the user; the ticket is closed once the task is completed.
Rules_and_Constraints: When measuring performance, the start time must be the moment the ticket is created by the user rather than when a staff member begins processing it; wait times in the queue must be included in the total duration to accurately reflect the user experience.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Requests may be delayed for significant periods if they are queued behind other tickets; staff may be tempted to cancel tickets that lack complete information rather than helping the user, which can negatively impact service while appearing to improve processing metrics.

Concept_Name: Web portal
Core_Definition: A self-service interface or API that allows users to request resources directly and automatically, often replacing manual ticket-based systems.
Key_Actors: Users or customers; automated provisioning systems.
Operational_Workflow: The user accesses the portal and inputs all necessary parameters for their request; the portal ensures all required information is gathered at the very beginning of the process; the request is then queued and processed sequentially by an automated system; the system provides the resource to the user without manual intervention from operational staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: The portal should be designed to eliminate the need for back-and-forth communication by collecting all data up front; it should replace the human process of re-entering data from a ticket; any wait time for sequential processing must be included in performance measurements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the wait time in the API or portal queue is excluded from the KPI, the measurement will make the team look good but will not reflect the actual service levels received by users.

Concept_Name: API
Core_Definition: A programmatic interface used by customers to submit requests for virtual machines as a more efficient alternative to manual helpdesk tickets.
Key_Actors: Users; automated systems; the service team.
Operational_Workflow: Users submit their specific requirements through the API; the interface captures all necessary data at the beginning of the process to avoid back-and-forth communication; requests are placed in a queue and processed sequentially; the system measures the duration from the request submission until the machine is usable.
Rules_and_Constraints: The API should collect every piece of information required for the process at the start; it should be part of a self-service portal to eliminate the need for human data entry; wait times in the API queue must be included in performance measurements to accurately reflect the end-to-end user experience.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If API queue wait times are excluded from the KPI, the resulting metrics will fail to reflect the actual delays experienced by customers.

Concept_Name: VM Manager
Core_Definition: The system component responsible for the initial stage of machine creation by allocating physical hardware resources to a new instance.
Key_Actors: VM Manager system; RAM; disk; vCPU resources.
Operational_Workflow: The VM Manager receives a request for a new virtual machine; it identifies and allocates the required amounts of RAM, disk, and other resources; it creates an empty virtual machine framework prior to the operating system being loaded.
Rules_and_Constraints: Defining the completion of a creation task based solely on the VM Manager's resource allocation is considered too narrow a definition; the goal of the process is a usable machine rather than just a resource allocation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A VM Manager might successfully complete its resource allocation even if the overall virtual machine creation fails during the subsequent operating system installation phase.

Concept_Name: OS installation mechanism
Core_Definition: The process or software system that loads a specific operating system onto a virtual machine framework once resources have been allocated.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; a dedicated installation team; automated installation software.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts after the VM Manager has finished resource allocation; the requested operating system is installed onto the machine; the system tracks the duration of the installation as part of the total creation time; manual or automatic retries are performed if the installation fails.
Rules_and_Constraints: Manual and automatic retries must be counted as part of the original request duration rather than as separate requests; image-based installations are identified as a method to make this process faster; the mechanism's success is a prerequisite for a virtual machine to be considered usable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The installation process may be unstable and require multiple restarts; operators may attempt to cancel failed installation jobs to improve their KPI numbers if the rules are not carefully defined to prevent such behavior.

Concept_Name: DNS changes
Core_Definition: The modifications to the Domain Name System required to ensure the new virtual machine is reachable and accessible by the user.
Key_Actors: DNS propagation systems; the user; the virtual machine creation workflow.
Operational_Workflow: After a virtual machine is created and its operating system is installed, the system initiates the required DNS updates; the process waits for these changes to propagate across the network; the workflow concludes when the user is able to log into the machine using the assigned DNS name.
Rules_and_Constraints: DNS changes and their propagation time must be included in the high-level KPI because a user cannot use the machine until this step is complete; propagation delays should be analyzed and tightened to improve the overall speed of the service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Long propagation delays can prevent a user from accessing a machine even if all other technical creation steps were performed quickly and successfully.

Concept_Name: Access controls
Core_Definition: Security measures that must be implemented for a virtual machine to be considered usable and ready for a user to log in.
Key_Actors: Users; the operational team or system responsible for allocating virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: A user submits a request for a virtual machine; the system allocates necessary resources like RAM and disk space; access controls are implemented alongside other network configurations like DNS changes; the process is complete when the user can successfully log into the machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: Access controls are a required component of a usable virtual machine; they must be fully implemented before the creation process is marked as finished.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Billing for resources
Core_Definition: A financial arrangement established during resource allocation to ensure usage is paid for and to encourage users to limit their consumption.
Key_Actors: Users; management; the organization or service provider.
Operational_Workflow: A user requests a specific quantity of resources such as virtual machines; billing arrangements are established as part of the request fulfillment process; these arrangements serve as a method to restrain users from requesting an infinite number of resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Establishing billing arrangements should be part of the ideal definition of a resource request fulfillment process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 90th percentile creation time
Core_Definition: A high-level KPI used to assess the virtual machine allocation process by measuring the time from the initial request until the user can log into the machine.
Key_Actors: Users who request resources; the operations team or automated system that processes requests; management.
Operational_Workflow: The measurement starts the moment a request is received from the user; the system or team processes the request while including any wait times in queues or ticket systems; the measurement continues through the resource allocation and configuration phases; manual and automatic retries after failures are included as part of the original request duration; the measurement ends when the user is able to log into the machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: The measurement must include the end-to-end result from the customer's perspective; it must incorporate any delays before the creation process actually begins; retries must not be counted as separate requests.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Outright canceled requests are not included in the standard percentile calculation but are logged separately; management investigates if there are more than five operator-initiated cancellations in a week or if customer-initiated cancellations exceed one percent of all requests.

Concept_Name: Percentile
Core_Definition: A statistical method used for KPI measurements to provide a more accurate representation of performance than an average.
Key_Actors: Managers; teams being measured; data analysis systems.
Operational_Workflow: Performance data is collected over a specific period; the data is analyzed using percentiles to ensure that a single long-delayed event does not unfairly distort the overall performance metric.
Rules_and_Constraints: Percentiles should be used instead of averages because averages can be misleading and can be ruined by a single outlier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Capacity planning
Core_Definition: The process of ensuring that system resources are available and new capacity comes online just in time to fulfill user requests.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; managers; service users.
Operational_Workflow: Capacity planning functions as a foundational support for service delivery where new resources are prepared to meet incoming demand; its efficiency is often surfaced through high-level KPIs that track whether requests are being held while waiting for new capacity to come online; data analyzed from these delays can be used to justify improvements in how resources are installed and allocated.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text suggests keeping KPIs at a high level of abstraction rather than micro-managing how the service is run; specific KPIs for capacity planning efficiency should only be established if it becomes a recurring issue.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When capacity planning is handled poorly, user requests may be delayed for weeks waiting for new resources to be installed.

Concept_Name: Code reviews
Core_Definition: A process used by developer teams to maintain and set high standards for software quality.
Key_Actors: Developer teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developer teams utilize code reviews as a peer-based process to ensure high standards; this is possible because there is less information asymmetry between different developer teams than there is between developers and operations staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Auditing KPI results
Core_Definition: A review process conducted after a KPI is deployed to check for unintended negative side effects and evaluate its effectiveness.
Key_Actors: Management; the teams responsible for the KPI.
Operational_Workflow: Initial results are typically audited a few weeks after the KPI is deployed; depending on the activity being measured, results are reviewed on a daily, weekly, or monthly basis; the audit involves analyzing wait times and the duration of individual steps to identify problem areas and opportunities for process improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: The audit should specifically look for unintended negative side effects that may have been triggered by the new measurement; the process uses the measurements to verify if priorities are being met.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lead time
Core_Definition: A measurement of the duration between the initial filing of a request and the point at which the actual work or creation process begins.
Key_Actors: Users; operational staff; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: Lead time is measured to identify specific bottlenecks or delays within an operational process; for instance, it can reveal a long span of time between a ticket being filed and the start of a virtual machine creation; these measurements are used to determine if a transition to a self-service system or better capacity planning is required.
Rules_and_Constraints: Measuring lead time is used as a method to isolate problem areas within the steps of a larger process; it helps in predicting how specific changes might improve the overall KPI.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Lead time analysis might reveal that requests are delayed for weeks due to resource shortages or that a ticket-based system is causing unnecessary human-induced delays.

Concept_Name: Image-based installations
Core_Definition: A method used to speed up the process of installing an operating system.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: This technique is identified as a potential optimization within the virtual machine creation process; it is used to reduce the time spent on the OS installation step to improve overall performance metrics.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Network utilization
Core_Definition: The measurement and monitoring of data traffic across network links.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams monitor network utilization to identify overloaded links; by improving these identified bottlenecks, the team can achieve faster installation times for systems and services in general.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Error Budget
Core_Definition: A performance indicator that allocates a specific amount of allowable downtime each quarter to balance the competing needs for system stability and rapid innovation.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: The budget is reset at the start of each quarter, typically to 0.01 percent of the total time; any period of system unavailability subtracts from this budget; developers may release new features as often as they want provided the budget has not been exhausted; if the budget reaches zero, all new launches are stopped until the next quarter begins.
Rules_and_Constraints: The goal is to encourage risk-taking without encouraging carelessness; it aligns the priorities of developers and operations; it is based on the principle that absolute 100 percent uptime is often unnecessary and can stifle innovation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority security fixes are permitted even if the budget is exhausted; user-visible services may employ graceful degradation, such as showing blank space or generic filler, to remain functional even if a specific sub-service is down and subtracting from the budget.

Concept_Name: Benjamin Treynor Sloss
Core_Definition: The Vice President of Engineering at Google who introduced the Error Budget KPI.
Key_Actors: Benjamin Treynor Sloss; Google engineering teams.
Operational_Workflow: He revealed a successful management framework designed to encourage high uptime while allowing for innovation and risk-taking within distributed systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Developers
Core_Definition: Entities in the business of making change who are rewarded for creating new features, particularly those highly visible to end customers.
Key_Actors: Developer subteams; individual engineers; management; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create new features and seek to push them into production as quickly as possible to avoid delaying gratification. They typically face management pressure regarding when products will ship. To navigate operational restrictions, they may use workarounds like flag flips or configuration files to release code. In a unified KPI system like an Error Budget, they perform testing and adopt better release practices to ensure they do not exhaust their downtime allowance, which allows them to continue launching features.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers are expected to perform testing and judge the quality of releases because they know the code better than operations staff. They must balance their time between adding new features and improving operational processes and reliability frameworks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a developer team exhausts their allocated Error Budget, they must stop all launches for the remainder of the quarter, with the exception of high-priority security fixes. They may also attempt to circumvent operational approvals by hiding untested code, which the text identifies as a high-risk behavior.

Concept_Name: Operations teams
Core_Definition: Entities in the business of maintaining system stability who seek to prevent breakages to avoid being paged or experiencing operational failures.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; managers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Operations teams manage the stability of production environments and are frequently questioned by management regarding why systems went down. They often implement safeguards and rules to prevent problems, such as prohibiting upgrades on specific days or requiring specific test percentages. In an Error Budget model, they move away from being the arbiters of releases and instead collaborate with developers toward a shared reliability goal.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations staff often operate under the constraint that any outage is bad, which can lead to risk-averse behavior. They should ideally be removed from the position of having to decide whether to permit a launch to avoid being viewed as an enemy by developers and to account for the fact that they are not code experts or mind readers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While operations typically prioritize stability, high-priority security fixes are handled as exceptions to standard release pauses. The text notes that 100 percent uptime is an unrealistic constraint for operations to meet for web services.

Concept_Name: Risk averse
Core_Definition: A behavioral tendency, typically associated with operations teams, to avoid changes that might lead to system instability or outages.
Key_Actors: Operations people; managers who decree that all outages are bad.
Operational_Workflow: This state is triggered when management sets a goal that any outage is unacceptable. Operations teams respond by rejecting developer requests for new releases or by creating increasingly restrictive rules to prevent change. This leads to a conflict where operations acts as a gatekeeper to protect the status quo and avoid negative consequences like being paged.
Rules_and_Constraints: Risk aversion is identified as a consequence of management style rather than a fault of the team itself. It is constrained by the reality that perfection in uptime is generally not achievable or necessary for most web services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The introduction of an Error Budget is designed to mitigate risk aversion by encouraging controlled risk-taking and innovation without encouraging carelessness.

Concept_Name: Flag flips
Core_Definition: A mechanism used to hide large amounts of untested code or features within a system to allow for releases without requiring a formal software upgrade.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: When developers face restrictive operational rules or gatekeeping that prevents them from shipping code, they hide their features behind flag flips. They may also encode major features into configuration files. This allows the code to be pushed into the environment while circumventing the standard operations' approval process.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is categorized as a workaround to avoid being locked out of shipping code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The text notes that using flag flips to circumvent approvals is done at great risk and can lead to the release of untested code.

Concept_Name: Configuration files
Core_Definition: Files used by developers to encode major features so that new functionality can be enabled without requiring a full software upgrade.
Key_Actors: Developers; Operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers use configuration files to hide large amounts of untested code releases behind flag flips or to implement features without formal software upgrades; this process allows developers to bypass the approvals and time restrictions set by operations teams.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using configuration files in this manner is described as a high-risk workaround to circumvent operational rules and stability safeguards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: One hundred percent uptime
Core_Definition: A goal of absolute service availability that the text describes as unachievable and inappropriate for web sites.
Key_Actors: Managers; Operations teams; Users.
Operational_Workflow: Managers may decree that any outage is bad and demand perfect availability; however, the text notes that because typical users connect via WiFi with lower availability, the pursuit of perfection does not align with the user experience.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text states that 100 percent uptime is for life-critical devices like pacemakers rather than web sites; it is considered unachievable for distributed systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This goal might only be met if the definition of availability is adjusted or manipulated to cover more than originally intended.

Concept_Name: Four 9s (99.99 percent) availability
Core_Definition: A measurable performance goal where a service is functional for 99.99 percent of the time, allowing for a specific amount of budgeted downtime.
Key_Actors: Developers; Operations teams; Google.
Operational_Workflow: This target creates an error budget of approximately 13 minutes per quarter or 52.56 minutes per year; developers can release new features as long as the budget is not exhausted; if the budget is spent, all new launches stop until the next quarter.
Rules_and_Constraints: This level of availability is considered sufficient for web sites; it is used to encourage risk-taking and innovation without encouraging carelessness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority security fixes are exempted from the launch freezes that occur when the 99.99 percent availability budget is exhausted.

Concept_Name: Security fixes
Core_Definition: Technical updates or patches intended to address vulnerabilities or remediate known security breaches.
Key_Actors: Developers; Operations teams; Management.
Operational_Workflow: In an operational environment using an error budget, high-priority security fixes are deployed as needed; they are treated as an urgent necessity that bypasses standard stability-related launch restrictions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Security fixes are prioritized over the rule to stop all launches when a downtime budget is exhausted; the effectiveness of security management should be measured by the count of known breaches and the duration of investigations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority security fixes are the specific exception to the policy that halts all software launches once an error budget is empty.

Concept_Name: Information asymmetry
Core_Definition: The inherent condition where developers possess a deeper understanding of software code than the operations teams responsible for its stability.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers write and manage code, giving them superior knowledge of its inner workings; operations teams, lacking this deep code knowledge, must evaluate the risk of new releases; because operations staff are not mind readers, this gap in information often leads them to create restrictive rules and safeguards to manage the uncertainty of launches.
Rules_and_Constraints: Developers are considered to be in a better position to perform testing and judge release quality due to their proximity to the code.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 1 percent experiments
Core_Definition: A release safeguard and testing method where new code is deployed to a small fraction of the environment to evaluate its impact.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; user-visible services.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create new software features or code changes; the code is deployed as a test to 1 percent of the system or user base; teams monitor the results to ensure reliability before proceeding with a wider launch; developers may use or build specific frameworks to conduct these experiments with less effort.
Rules_and_Constraints: These experiments can be transitioned from optional tests to mandatory requirements as part of the safeguards added prior to a software release.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Lame-duck mode
Core_Definition: A specific operational feature that can be implemented within a web framework to improve service reliability and operations.
Key_Actors: Developers; web frameworks.
Operational_Workflow: Developers identify reliable features within their existing web frameworks; they implement or activate the lame-duck mode to enhance the operational quality of their service; this implementation often occurs when developers are incentivized to improve reliability to protect an error budget.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a feature that may exist within a framework but often goes unused until developers prioritize operational improvements over new features.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Web framework
Core_Definition: A software system or set of tools used by developers to build services, perform testing, and implement reliability features.
Key_Actors: Developers; subteams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Developers invest effort into building or selecting frameworks that improve operations and reliability; these frameworks are used to automate testing, conduct 1 percent experiments, or provide built-in features like lame-duck mode; teams utilize these frameworks to ensure their releases are successful and do not exhaust the collective error budget.
Rules_and_Constraints: Frameworks should help align developer and operations priorities by making it easier to maintain stability while launching new features; teams are encouraged to adopt frameworks that improve launch success to avoid negative peer pressure from other subteams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Graceful degradation techniques
Core_Definition: Methods that ensure a user-visible service composition can still succeed even when a specific sub-service is not responding.
Key_Actors: User-visible services; underlying sub-services.
Operational_Workflow: A service identifies that one of its component sub-services is failing to provide a response; the system invokes degradation logic to handle the missing input; the missing data is replaced with generic filler or the system displays blank space in that section of the output; the overall service composition successfully completes its response to the user despite the partial failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The primary principle is that the composition should succeed even if parts of it are missing; these techniques are used to prevent a single unresponsive sub-service from causing a total failure of the user-visible service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Stable service
Core_Definition: A service that maintains high availability and reliability through risk-averse management and the minimization of breakages.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; management; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Operations staff prioritize keeping the system from breaking to avoid pages and bad days; they establish safeguards such as no upgrades on Fridays, Mondays, or late at night to reduce the risk of human error; availability is monitored against a target such as 99.99 percent; any unavailability subtracts from a quarterly error budget; if the budget is exhausted, all new launches stop to focus on restoring stability until the budget resets.
Rules_and_Constraints: Perfect uptime is considered unrealistic for web sites and is not the primary goal; stability must be balanced against the need for innovation so that progress is not stifled; operations should not be put in a position where they must act as the sole gatekeeper for releases due to information asymmetry.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority security fixes are allowed to bypass standard launch freezes; external factors like a user's WiFi availability can dwarf the internal availability goals of the service.

Concept_Name: Innovative new features
Core_Definition: Functional enhancements and new code releases that drive business growth and provide visible value to end customers.
Key_Actors: Developers; development subteams; operations teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create new features and are rewarded for pushing them into production to meet shipping deadlines; they perform testing and judge the release quality based on their deep knowledge of the code; features are launched into the production environment as long as there is remaining quarterly error budget; developers may build improved testing frameworks or automated experiment systems to ensure their innovations do not exhaust the budget.
Rules_and_Constraints: Innovation should be encouraged without promoting undue risk or carelessness; developer teams are motivated to improve reliability because high stability allows them to release features more frequently; peer pressure between subteams helps maintain high standards for code reviews and framework adoption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Launching new features is prohibited if the error budget for the quarter has been exhausted; high-priority security fixes are treated as an exception and can be released during a launch freeze.

============================================================
Chapter 20. Operational Excellence
Section: Chapter 20. Operational Excellence
============================================================

Concept_Name: Operational Excellence
Core_Definition: The state in which an organization is highly proficient in its ordinary operations, enabling it to seize extraordinary opportunities. It is characterized by smooth operations, well-designed policies, and disciplined practices that meet or exceed customer needs.
Key_Actors: High-performing operations organizations; system administration teams; customers; management.
Operational_Workflow: High-performing organizations implement smooth operations and disciplined practices; they provide transparency regarding planning, service provision, and cost handling; outages and escalated issues result in action plans to reduce future occurrences; the organization constantly incorporates new technologies and techniques to improve its inner workings and the services it provides.
Rules_and_Constraints: The organization must be honest and forthright when discussing its own faults; funding must reflect a reasonable budget for the work performed; successes must be made visible; the organization must move the company forward.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While the vast majority of customers should be happy, dissatisfied customers must be provided with a voice, a channel for escalation, and the assurance that they are being heard.

Concept_Name: assessment system
Core_Definition: A measurement methodology used to evaluate the degree of formality and optimization of operational processes across eight specific responsibilities. It provides a generic level of measurement that is comparable across different teams and services.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; managers; senior managers; executives; staff members responsible for services.
Operational_Workflow: Major services are identified and evaluated based on eight operational responsibilities; each responsibility is assessed on a scale of 1 to 5 based on the Capability Maturity Model; staff members record assessment numbers and justifications in a spreadsheet to create a heat map of progress; individual service assessments are rolled up using a weighted average or mathematical mode to evaluate a team; senior managers use these roll-ups to compare teams and track organizational progress.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments must be bottom-up and performed by the team as a group exercise; the same questions must be used for each assessment to maintain comparability; rubrics must be consistent across all services and teams; managers must hold the team to high standards for accuracy and consistency; assessments should be conducted on a periodic and repeatable schedule, such as monthly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The eight core operational responsibilities do not fit well for transactional IT services such as helpdesks or other customer-facing service centers; assessments conducted too infrequently, such as annually, can become bureaucratic, unhelpful, and boring.

Concept_Name: continuous improvement
Core_Definition: An environment created by periodically assessing operations, identifying areas for change, making improvements, and reassessing to measure the resulting progress.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; high-performing organizations; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Teams perform periodic assessments of their services to expose areas for improvement; these assessments inspire the selection of specific projects to fix high-priority issues; the selected projects are worked on during a designated cycle; at the start of the next cycle, the team repeats the assessment and update process to measure success; the organization incorporates new techniques and technologies as the world changes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must pass through maturity levels in order as each level builds on the previous one; Level 3 maturity is generally considered good enough for most services; goals should focus on fixing problems rather than achieving a particular assessment level for its own sake; success or failure should be measured by whether the assessment improves after a solution is engineered.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Striving for the highest level of optimization on low-priority or little-used services is considered a waste of resources and professionally negligent; setting corporate-wide goals for every service to reach a specific level can lead to bureaucratic handcuffs that strangle an organization.

Concept_Name: action plans
Core_Definition: Specific sets of projects or remedial steps designed to address low-maturity areas identified during assessments or to reduce the recurrence of problems following outages and escalated issues.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: Outages and escalated issues trigger the creation of plans to reduce future occurrences of the problem; during assessment periods, teams brainstorm ways to fix problem areas identified by low scores; the team chooses a set of projects to fix the highest-priority issues for the most important services; these projects are executed during a project cycle, such as a quarter, to improve service maturity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Focus must be placed on the most needed improvements for the most important services; projects must be justified using measurement data; success is evaluated based on new measurements after changes are implemented; engineering solutions should address the larger problem rather than providing a quick fix.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Making raises and bonuses contingent on the results of assessments is discouraged as it prevents people from joining the projects that need the most help; focusing on assessment levels as a goal rather than fixing problems can lead to spot fixes that do not solve the underlying issues.

Concept_Name: assessment of a service
Core_Definition: An evaluation of a service based on specific criteria related to process maturity rather than popularity, availability, or speed. It serves as a roadmap to success by gauging the formality and optimization of the processes used to achieve service goals.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; team members; service managers; customers; partners.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies the major services provided by the organization. Staff periodically describe current practices for each service across eight operational responsibilities. The team evaluates these descriptions against five maturity levels to assign a score. The results are recorded in a spreadsheet with notes justifying the values. This evaluation is then used to brainstorm and select improvement projects for the upcoming cycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: All services must be assessed on process because good processes are necessary to achieve goals. The assessment should be a group exercise involving those responsible for the service rather than a manager grading the team. The same questions must be used for each assessment to ensure results are comparable. Look-for indicators should be used as signals of a level rather than a checklist of requirements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not all services need to be fast or highly available, so those specific performance metrics are excluded from the process-based assessment. While customers and partners may be happy with a service, the assessment requires evidence-based determination rather than subjective satisfaction.

Concept_Name: process maturity
Core_Definition: An indication of how formally or informally an operational aspect is practiced within an organization. It measures the progression from ad hoc and improvised actions to documented, measured, and actively optimized systems.
Key_Actors: Individual team members; operations teams; automated measurement systems; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Processes begin at Level 1 as ad hoc and undocumented activities relying on individual heroics. They progress to Level 2 when they become documented and repeatable with consistent results. At Level 3, roles and responsibilities are defined and correctness measurements are established. Level 4 involves quantitative management where metrics are automatically collected and displayed on dashboards to spot bottlenecks. Finally, at Level 5, the system is optimized through deliberate improvements prompted and evaluated by measurement data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each level builds on the previous one; therefore, levels cannot be skipped. Level 3 is generally considered sufficient for most services. Organizations should focus on fixing problems rather than achieving a specific level for its own sake. Improving process maturity should never be tied to raises or bonuses as this discourages people from joining difficult projects.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In Level 1, results are inconsistent and work requests are often lost. Level 5 maturity is considered a waste of resources for low-priority or little-used services. Professional negligence occurs when resources are expended to reach Level 5 on a low-priority service while more important services remain unoptimized.

Concept_Name: Assessment methodology
Core_Definition: A bottom-up evaluation system that rates services on eight operational responsibilities using a five-level scale based on the Capability Maturity Model. It is designed to identify areas for improvement and track progress through periodic reassessment and weighted averages.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; managers; senior managers; executive leadership.
Operational_Workflow: The organization enumerates its major services and infrastructure components. Teams conduct monthly self-assessments by answering standard questions for each operational responsibility to determine its maturity level. These individual scores are recorded in a heat map spreadsheet and rolled up into a single service score using a mode or weighted average. These results are then rolled up further to compare different teams. Based on the findings, teams select high-priority projects for the quarterly cycle to address low-performing areas.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments should be done monthly to track progress without becoming a bureaucratic burden, while project cycles should be quarterly. Managers must hold teams to high standards for accuracy and consistency but should not perform the assessment alone. The methodology must be applied consistently across all services and teams to allow for stack ranking and sharing of best practices.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If assessment cycles are too infrequent, they may become scary or boring annual events that turn into bureaucratic wastes of time. The eight core operational responsibilities do not fit well for transactional IT services such as a helpdesk or other customer-facing tier 1 service centers.

Concept_Name: operational responsibilities (OR)
Core_Definition: Eight broad categories of service management duties used to evaluate the maturity of a service. They encompass both the technical and non-technical tasks required to operate, change, and maintain distributed systems.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; change management review boards; supply chain management; users; technical systems.
Operational_Workflow: Each service is assessed against eight core ORs: Regular Tasks for non-emergency duties; Emergency Response for handling outages; Monitoring and Metrics for data collection; Capacity Planning for resource forecasting; Change Management for purposeful updates; New Product Introduction and Removal for lifecycle coordination; Service Deploy and Decommission for instance management; and Performance and Efficiency for cost-effective resource utilization. Teams use standard questions to describe their performance in each category.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams may add more ORs or de-emphasize existing ones using weighted averages based on the service's needs. The same questions should be used for each assessment to maintain consistency. NPI, SDD, and CM must be distinguished clearly: NPI is the initial launch coordination; SDD is the technical process of adding instances; and CM is the management of upgrades and changes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Certain ORs may be irrelevant for specific services, such as capacity planning for services that do not grow. The core eight ORs are primarily geared toward service management and might require modifications or subtractions for specialized services. Not explicitly covered in this chapter are the specific ORs for transactional IT services.

Concept_Name: weighted average
Core_Definition: A mathematical method used to roll up multiple individual assessment scores into a single value representing a service or a team.
Key_Actors: System administration teams; managers; individual services being evaluated.
Operational_Workflow: A team evaluates a service based on eight operational responsibilities; these eight individual assessments are then aggregated using a weighted average to arrive at a single number for the service; this process can be repeated to assess a team by calculating the weighted average of all the services it provides.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams may emphasize or de-emphasize specific operational responsibilities by adjusting weights during the roll-up process; whatever calculation method is selected must be used consistently to ensure comparable results.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams may choose to use the mathematical mode, which is the most common number in the set, instead of a weighted average when rolling up assessments.

Concept_Name: stack rank
Core_Definition: A comparative ranking system used to order teams based on their assessment scores.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; senior managers; executive leadership.
Operational_Workflow: Teams perform periodic assessments of their services; these scores are rolled up into a team-level assessment score; managers then compare these scores across different teams to identify high performers and those needing improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: The stack rank is intended to help teams identify problem areas to work on and to highlight high-ranking teams so their best practices can be shared across the organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: transactional IT services
Core_Definition: Customer-facing, front-of-house, or tier 1 service operations that manage direct user interactions.
Key_Actors: Helpdesk staff; tier 1 support teams; customer-facing service centers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The eight core operational responsibilities defined in the assessment methodology are geared toward service management and do not fit well for these types of services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: helpdesk
Core_Definition: A specific type of transactional IT service characterized as a front-of-house, tier 1, or customer-facing service center.
Key_Actors: Tier 1 support entities; customer-facing service teams.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is cited as an example of a service that is not compatible with the eight core operational responsibilities assessment framework.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: front-of-house
Core_Definition: A type of transactional IT service, categorized alongside helpdesks, that focuses on direct customer interactions rather than long-term service management.
Key_Actors: Helpdesk personnel; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The eight core operational responsibilities described in the chapter are not considered a good fit for these transactional services; these services are distinct from the service management focus of the assessment methodology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: tier 1
Core_Definition: A level of transactional IT service typically involving initial customer support and helpdesk functions.
Key_Actors: Helpdesk staff; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: This level of service is identified as transactional IT and is excluded from the standard eight operational responsibilities assessment framework described for service management.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: customer-facing service center
Core_Definition: An organizational unit providing transactional IT services directly to users, similar to a helpdesk or front-of-house operation.
Key_Actors: Service center staff; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The chapter notes that the eight common operational responsibilities defined for service management do not apply well to these transactional service centers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Regular Tasks (RT)
Core_Definition: One of the eight core operational responsibilities involving the management of normal, non-emergency, and periodic operational duties.
Key_Actors: Team members performing duties; customers receiving services; partners; managers holding the team to high standards.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins when work is received and queued; work is then distributed to team members who process it; the completed work is verified for correctness; additionally, periodic tasks are scheduled and performed according to defined steps.
Rules_and_Constraints: Tasks should be defined and documented in a playbook to ensure repeatability; work must be tracked to prevent lost requests; roles and responsibilities must be confirmed; correctness measurements should be established; at higher maturity levels, the duration of each step and the frequency of errors must be measured automatically.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In ad hoc environments, work may be untracked or lost and results may be inconsistent due to a reliance on individual heroics; exceptional cases that require special treatment should be cataloged and reviewed via postmortems; if the process is not documented, two different people may produce different results.

Concept_Name: Emergency Response (ER)
Core_Definition: The handling of outages and disasters through technical and non-technical processes performed during and after the event for response and remediation.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; individuals responsible for the service; personnel involved in technical and non-technical response and remediation.
Operational_Workflow: Outages and disasters are identified; technical and non-technical response processes are performed during the event; remediation efforts are carried out after the outage; postmortems are published within a specific amount of time after an exception; exceptions are cataloged and periodically reviewed to reduce future occurrences.
Rules_and_Constraints: Outages and escalated issues must result in action plans to reduce future occurrences; postmortems should be published within a specific amount of time; exceptions must be cataloged and reviewed; processes should evolve from ad hoc to documented, defined, measured, and eventually optimized.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Exceptional cases that require special treatment and ad hoc processes are cataloged and reviewed; at the initial maturity level, processes are ad hoc and rely on individual heroics rather than documented procedures.

Concept_Name: Monitoring and Metrics (MM)
Core_Definition: The practice of collecting data about a system and using that data as quantifiable components of performance to make informed decisions.
Key_Actors: Automatic data collection systems; dashboard interfaces; individuals or teams using data to identify bottlenecks or justify changes.
Operational_Workflow: Data is collected about the system; performance is measured using quantifiable metrics; measurements are collected automatically; data is displayed on a dashboard to show performance and bottlenecks; measurement data is used to justify process changes; system optimization is performed based on measurements and evaluated with new data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Measurements should be collected automatically; dashboards must display all measurements; requests to change a process must be justified using measurement data to show a problem; roles must have definitions of how to measure correctness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At maturity level one, there is no evidence-based determination of service quality; maturity level four tracks how many times an exceptional case arises that requires special treatment and ad hoc processes.

Concept_Name: Capacity Planning (CP)
Core_Definition: The process of determining future resource needs by combining technical growth analysis with non-technical functions like budgeting, forecasting, and supply chain management.
Key_Actors: Personnel responsible for budgeting and forecasting; supply chain management; teams responsible for determining maximum backend capacity.
Operational_Workflow: Technical work is performed to understand how many resources are needed per unit of growth; budgeting and forecasting are conducted; supply chain management is coordinated; capacity needs are predicted ahead of time; systems are stress-tested to see what breaks; the system is fixed and run at new capacity levels.
Rules_and_Constraints: Capacity needs must be predicted ahead of time; accurate determination of maximum backend capacity is required to know spare capacity; load balancers may be used to meet specific resiliency requirements such as N plus one capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services that do not grow frequently may de-emphasize this responsibility; level one behavior involves a lack of clear sense or disagreement regarding how resources like load balancers are used for capacity or resiliency.

Concept_Name: Change Management (CM)
Core_Definition: The management of purposeful service changes over time, including the creation, delivery, and production deployment of application and infrastructure software.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platforms; enterprise-style change management review boards; teams performing firmware, network, or OS configuration changes.
Operational_Workflow: Software delivery platforms are used to create and deliver new application and infrastructure software; changes are pushed into production; firmware upgrades, network changes, and OS configuration management are performed; requests to change a process are justified using measurement data; success is evaluated based on new measurements after changes are made.
Rules_and_Constraints: Changes must be managed either through a software deployment platform or an enterprise-style review board; requests for changes to processes must be justified with measurement data; corporate change management policies must be applied consistently across all services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Level one maturity is characterized by ad hoc and undocumented changes where different people get different results; level four measures how often exceptions occur that require ad hoc processes instead of standard change management.

Concept_Name: New Product Introduction and Removal (NPI/NPR)
Core_Definition: A coordination function used to determine how new products and services are introduced into the environment and how they are deprecated and removed.
Key_Actors: Multiple teams across the organization; current users of a service; coordination roles.
Operational_Workflow: The process functions as a non-technical coordinating role for related technical and non-technical activities. For introduction, it involves coordinating all teams touched by the launch of a new hardware brand or an entirely new service. For removal, the process involves tracking down current users and managing their migration away from the service so it can be eliminated.
Rules_and_Constraints: NPI is distinguished from other processes as the method for launching something for the first time; it must manage the impact on all teams touched by the introduction or removal of a platform or service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Decommissioning all instances of an old hardware platform; managing the migration of users when a service is being eliminated.

Concept_Name: Service Deploy and Decommission (SDD)
Core_Definition: The technical process of determining how instances of an existing service are created and how they are turned off once they have served their purpose.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; budget approval authorities.
Operational_Workflow: Once a service has been introduced to the environment, it is deployed many times. The workflow includes technical steps to turn up new datacenters, add replicas of a service, or add replicas of a database. When these instances are no longer needed, they are decommissioned through a formal technical process.
Rules_and_Constraints: SDD is focused on existing items such as machines, servers, or services; non-technical processes like budget approval are included only in support of the primary technical goal.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Performance and Efficiency (PE)
Core_Definition: An operational responsibility focused on measuring how well a service performs and how cost-effectively its resources are used.
Key_Actors: Running services; resource management systems.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves managing utilization, power efficiency, and related costs to ensure that a running service maintains high performance standards without wasting resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: A running service must maintain good performance while ensuring that resources are used cost-effectively.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: service delivery platform
Core_Definition: A system used to create, deliver, and push new application and infrastructure software into production.
Key_Actors: Application software systems; infrastructure software systems; change management review boards.
Operational_Workflow: The platform is utilized within the change management process to manage how services are purposefully changed over time. It facilitates the delivery of software updates, firmware upgrades, network changes, and OS configuration management.
Rules_and_Constraints: The platform must support the creation and delivery of both application and infrastructure software; it can be used either as an automated platform or in conjunction with an enterprise-style change management review board.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: firmware upgrades
Core_Definition: A type of infrastructure software change managed as part of the purposeful evolution of a service over time.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platforms; enterprise-style change management review boards.
Operational_Workflow: Firmware upgrades are processed through the change management operational responsibility where they are created, delivered, and pushed into production using a software deployment platform or managed via a review board.
Rules_and_Constraints: These upgrades must be handled as part of a formal change management process rather than being treated as a new product introduction or a simple service deployment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: network changes
Core_Definition: Purposeful modifications to the network infrastructure conducted to manage how services change over their lifecycle.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platforms; enterprise-style change management review boards.
Operational_Workflow: Network changes are executed within the change management framework using service delivery platforms to push infrastructure software updates or through an enterprise-style change management review board.
Rules_and_Constraints: Network changes are categorized as a component of Change Management, which is distinct from the non-technical coordinating functions of New Product Introduction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: OS configuration management
Core_Definition: The management of operating system settings and infrastructure software as part of the ongoing purposeful change of a service.
Key_Actors: Service delivery platforms; enterprise-style change management review boards.
Operational_Workflow: This process is integrated into the service delivery platform used to create, deliver, and push new infrastructure software into the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: OS configuration management is a technical process handled under the Change Management operational responsibility to ensure consistency in how services are updated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: budgeting
Core_Definition: A non-technical operational process used to forecast resource needs and provide financial approval for service activities.
Key_Actors: Operations organizations; customers; management teams.
Operational_Workflow: Budgeting is integrated into capacity planning to determine future resource needs per unit of growth and serves as a non-technical approval step within the process of deploying or decommissioning service instances.
Rules_and_Constraints: Funding must reflect a reasonable budget for the work performed; the organization must maintain transparency regarding how it handles costs or charge-backs to customers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: forecasting
Core_Definition: A non-technical aspect of capacity planning used to determine future resource needs.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: It functions as part of the capacity planning operational responsibility to help an organization understand how many resources are needed per unit of growth.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: supply chain management
Core_Definition: A non-technical component of capacity planning involved in determining future resource needs.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: It functions within the capacity planning operational responsibility to assist with the non-technical aspects of understanding resource requirements per unit of growth alongside technical work and budgeting.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: enterprise-style change management review board
Core_Definition: A formal coordination body or process used to manage how services are purposefully changed over time.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: It serves as a mechanism for managing upgrades and changes to services; including application and infrastructure software; firmware upgrades; network changes; and OS configuration management.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Capability Maturity Model (CMM)
Core_Definition: A tool designed to measure the maturity of a capability or responsibility based on how formally or informally a process is practiced.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; service owners; managers; executives; and senior managers.
Operational_Workflow: The model uses a five-level scale to evaluate operational responsibilities; Level 1 is Initial where processes are ad hoc and rely on heroics; Level 2 is Repeatable where processes are documented and consistent; Level 3 is Defined where roles and responsibilities are confirmed; Level 4 is Managed where processes are quantitatively measured with metrics; and Level 5 is Optimizing where measurements drive deliberate process improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each level builds on the previous one and levels cannot be skipped; assessment should be done on a periodic schedule such as monthly; look-for indicators are used as signals rather than requirements or checklists; Level 3 is generally considered sufficient for most services; management should ensure rubrics are consistent across all teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Level 4 and 5 assessments should be reserved for high-priority or revenue-generating services; attempting Level 5 on low-priority services is considered professionally negligent; setting goals to achieve specific assessment levels rather than fixing actual problems can lead to bureaucratic waste and wrong behaviors; raises and bonuses should not be contingent on assessment results to prevent discouraging staff from joining difficult projects.

Concept_Name: Level 1, Initial (Chaotic)
Core_Definition: This is the starting point for a new or undocumented process where actions are ad hoc and inconsistent.
Key_Actors: Individual team members performing tasks; the team responsible for estimating work; customers and partners receiving the service.
Operational_Workflow: Work is received but remains untracked and requests are often lost; different individuals perform the same tasks in different ways; the process relies on individual heroics rather than established procedures.
Rules_and_Constraints: Processes are not documented; the team is unable to accurately estimate how long a task will take.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Customers and partners may be happy with the service, but there is no evidence-based determination or data to confirm this.

Concept_Name: Level 2, Repeatable
Core_Definition: The process has transitioned from being ad hoc to documented well enough that it can be repeated with the same results.
Key_Actors: Different individuals who follow the documentation to perform the process.
Operational_Workflow: The process is documented with no missing steps; two different people can follow the defined steps and achieve relatively consistent end results.
Rules_and_Constraints: All steps must be documented; the process must be repeatable by different actors to achieve the same outcome.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Errors still occur because the process is not perfect; due to a lack of measurement at this level, the team may not know how often work is defective.

Concept_Name: Level 3, Defined
Core_Definition: Roles and responsibilities within the process are explicitly defined and confirmed, and criteria for measuring correctness are established.
Key_Actors: Individuals with defined roles and responsibilities; people involved in the process who understand correctness measurements.
Operational_Workflow: Roles are assigned and confirmed; each step of the process includes a series of checks to find errors as they happen rather than waiting until the end; duplication of effort is minimized; the process can be scaled by duplicating it or adding capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: Errors should be caught during the process; the people involved must know how to measure correctness even if those measurements are not yet being actively collected.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Correctness measurements might be defined and known by the team, but they might not actually be collected yet.

Concept_Name: Level 4, Managed
Core_Definition: The process is quantitatively managed based on agreed-upon metrics and data that is collected automatically.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Step durations and wait times are measured; the frequency of the process and its error rates are collected automatically; a dashboard displays these measurements to help spot bottlenecks; exceptions are cataloged and reviewed; postmortems are published within a specific timeframe following an exception.
Rules_and_Constraints: Requests to change a process must be justified using measurement data; capacity needs must be predicted ahead of time; postmortems must be published within a specific amount of time after an exception.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Exceptional cases that require special treatment or ad hoc processes are measured, cataloged, and periodically reviewed.

Concept_Name: Level 5, Optimizing
Core_Definition: The highest level of process maturity where measurements and metrics are actively used to optimize and improve the entire system.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; automated measurement systems; high-priority or revenue-generating services.
Operational_Workflow: Improvements are prompted by measurements collected in previous levels; after a change is implemented, the team evaluates the success of the improvement based on new measurements; the system is stress-tested to identify breaking points; once fixed, the system is run at the new performance level; progress is tracked through month-over-month improvement data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Improvements must be prompted by measurements; success must be evaluated based on new measurements after a change is made; this level should be reserved for high-priority or revenue-generating services; organizations must pass through all previous levels in order and cannot skip levels.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Expending resources to reach Level 5 for a low-priority or little-used service is considered professionally negligent; setting a goal to achieve Level 5 for its own sake rather than to solve a problem is a trap that can result in bureaucratic handcuffs that strangle an organization.

Concept_Name: individual heroics
Core_Definition: Ad hoc and inconsistent efforts by individual staff members used to complete work in the absence of documented or formal processes.
Key_Actors: Individual team members; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Processes are undocumented and ad hoc; work is untracked and requests are often lost; different people perform the same tasks in different ways with inconsistent results; the team is unable to accurately estimate how long tasks will take.
Rules_and_Constraints: Maturity models are designed to move an organization away from individual heroics and toward repeatable processes that are more efficient and of higher quality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: bottlenecks
Core_Definition: Delays or points of congestion within a process that hinder efficiency and are identified through quantitative measurement.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; automated data collection systems; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Aspects of the process are measured including the duration of each step and wait times; measurements are collected automatically and displayed on a dashboard; analyzing the duration of each step exposes specific bottlenecks and delays; requests to change a process are justified using measurement data to prove the bottleneck exists.
Rules_and_Constraints: Identification of bottlenecks must be based on evidence and data rather than intuition; analyzing duration of steps is a specific requirement to expose these delays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Postmortems
Core_Definition: Formal reviews and published documents created after an outage or exceptional case to analyze the event and prevent future occurrences.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; customers; management.
Operational_Workflow: An outage or exceptional case occurs that requires special treatment or ad hoc processes; a postmortem is published within a specific amount of time after the exception; exceptions are cataloged and reviewed periodically; the organization creates action plans based on these reviews to reduce future occurrences of the problem.
Rules_and_Constraints: Postmortems must be published within a specific timeframe after an incident; the organization must be honest and forthright when discussing its own faults; outages and escalated issues must result in action plans for future reduction.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: stress-test
Core_Definition: A technique used at the highest maturity level to identify system failure points by intentionally running the system at a new level of operation.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; the system or services being tested.
Operational_Workflow: The team runs the system at a new performance level to observe what breaks; the team fixes the identified failure points; the system is then officially run at the new verified level.
Rules_and_Constraints: Stress-testing is a characteristic of Level 5 (Optimizing) behavior in the Capability Maturity Model; it is performed after improvements are prompted by measurements.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: load balancer
Core_Definition: A tool used to improve the capacity or resiliency of a service, or both.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; backend servers; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: The team establishes a policy for the load balancer's role in capacity or resiliency; the system distributes traffic among backend resources; monitoring tools determine the current level of redundancy such as N plus zero or N plus one.
Rules_and_Constraints: Using a load balancer for resiliency requires the system to run with enough spare capacity to withstand a failed backend; Level 4 maturity requires effort to accurately determine the maximum capacity of a backend.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A situation where there is no clear sense of how a load balancer is being used or disagreement among team members about its role constitutes Level 1 (Initial) behavior.

Concept_Name: resiliency
Core_Definition: The attribute of a system that allows it to remain functional by withstanding the failure of a backend component.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend systems; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The system is configured to run with specific redundancy levels; a load balancer is utilized to manage traffic across backends; monitoring is used to verify the current state of redundancy.
Rules_and_Constraints: Resiliency requires the system to run with enough spare capacity to withstand a failed backend; a written policy for using a load balancer for resiliency represents Level 3 (Defined) behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: spare capacity
Core_Definition: The additional resource overhead available in a system beyond what is required for current operations, used to ensure continued service during failures.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; backend systems; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Technical staff determine the maximum capacity of a system backend; they use this measurement to calculate how much extra capacity is currently available; this data allows the team to know if the system can survive the loss of a component.
Rules_and_Constraints: Spare capacity is a necessary requirement for achieving resiliency; accurately determining maximum capacity is essential for Level 4 (Managed) behavior regarding load balancing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: N + 0
Core_Definition: A level of redundancy monitored by a system to determine if there is zero spare capacity available beyond what is currently in use.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend systems; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Monitoring systems evaluate the current capacity of a service and compare it against the maximum capacity of the backend to determine if the system is running at a level with no redundancy; this monitoring is categorized as a Level 4 Managed behavior in the Capability Maturity Model.
Rules_and_Constraints: Determining this level requires accurately establishing the maximum capacity of a backend to understand how much spare capacity, if any, is available.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: N + 1
Core_Definition: A level of redundancy where a system is designed to run with enough spare capacity to withstand the failure of a backend component.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; backend systems; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: Load balancers are configured to provide resiliency by maintaining spare capacity; monitoring systems then track this redundancy level to ensure the system can tolerate a failure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Achieving the ability to monitor and manage this level is considered a Level 4 behavior and requires significant effort to accurately determine backend capacity limits.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: look-for’s
Core_Definition: Specific behaviors, indicators, or outcomes that serve as signals to identify the maturity level of a service or organization.
Key_Actors: Assessment teams; operations organizations; service managers.
Operational_Workflow: Evaluators describe current organizational practices and then compare those descriptions against these indicators to see which maturity level the organization matches; they help translate the abstract levels of the Capability Maturity Model into recognizable scenarios.
Rules_and_Constraints: They are not checklists or requirements; an organization does not need to demonstrate every indicator to be assessed at a particular level; they are signals rather than steps to follow or achievements to seek out.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Some indicators are only appropriate for certain situations or specific types of services.

Concept_Name: playbook
Core_Definition: A documented collection of common operational duties and the list of what those duties entail.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; system administration staff.
Operational_Workflow: In the context of Regular Tasks, a playbook is used to define and document duties to move a process from ad hoc to a defined state; its absence is an indicator of a Level 1 maturity level where duties are not documented or listed.
Rules_and_Constraints: The duties within the playbook must be defined and documented to reach Level 3 in the assessment methodology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: major service
Core_Definition: A primary application, feature group, or infrastructure component identified by an organization as a distinct unit for operational assessment. It represents the level at which process maturity is measured and improved.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; service teams; managers; customers; partners.
Operational_Workflow: The organization first identifies its primary offerings or components; staff then periodically assess each service across eight operational responsibilities using a scale of 1 to 5; the team brainstorms projects to address low-scoring areas with high impact; these projects are executed during a project cycle to improve the service; the assessment is repeated in the next period to track progress.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments must be performed periodically on a repeatable schedule such as monthly; the same assessment questions must be used for each service to ensure comparability; services should be listed in order of importance; reaching Level 3 is generally considered sufficient for most services unless they are revenue-generating or have high demands.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a service is low priority or little used, reaching Level 5 is considered a waste of resources and professional negligence; smaller organizations may group multiple related technical functions into a single major service category while larger sites may treat individual components as separate services.

Concept_Name: web-based service
Core_Definition: A service model that organizes its operational assessments around major software feature groups and the physical and digital infrastructure required to deliver them.
Key_Actors: Software components; feature groups; infrastructure teams; network providers; facilities teams managing power and cooling.
Operational_Workflow: The organization identifies major features served by specific software components; it identifies necessary infrastructure services including networks, power, cooling, and Internet access; these identified groups are then treated as major services and subjected to the eight core operational responsibility assessments.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessment must include both the software feature groups and the underlying infrastructure that supports them; the categorization of these services should reflect the specific components of the web offering.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: infrastructure services
Core_Definition: The foundational technical and facility-based components that support an IT environment, including networking protocols, utilities, and identity management.
Key_Actors: Networks; power and cooling systems; Internet access; protocol services like DNS, DHCP, NTP; directory services like ActiveDirectory and LDAP.
Operational_Workflow: These services are identified as major services within the organizational list; they are assessed against the same eight operational responsibilities as application services; they are monitored for performance and efficiency to ensure they support the broader service ecosystem.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure services must be assessed alongside application-level services to provide a complete picture of operational excellence; they should be evaluated based on how they provide a platform for other services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In smaller sites, these services may be grouped together under a single label such as name services; in larger sites, each individual infrastructure protocol or facility component is often assessed as its own individual service.

Concept_Name: enterprise IT organization
Core_Definition: An internal organization focused on providing applications, compute resources, and device management to support the employees and business functions of a company.
Key_Actors: IT teams; internal customers; application providers for email and file storage; compute farm administrators; desktop and laptop fleet managers.
Operational_Workflow: The organization identifies the major applications provided to the company such as email or centralized compute; it identifies fleet management and core infrastructure services; managers use assessment results to guide the team's direction, track progress, and communicate expectations to team members.
Rules_and_Constraints: The organization should use assessments to drive a culture of continuous improvement; high-performing teams are encouraged to share best practices with lower-ranking teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The standard eight operational responsibilities are not a good fit for transactional IT services such as a helpdesk or other front-of-house, tier 1, customer-facing service centers.

Concept_Name: email
Core_Definition: A major application provided to a company by an enterprise IT organization.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; company employees; operational teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The service is identified as a major component for assessment; teams perform periodic self-assessments across eight operational responsibilities including regular tasks and emergency response; staff record assessment numbers from 1 to 5 along with justifying notes in a tracking spreadsheet; assessment results are rolled up to compare the service against others; the team identifies projects to address low-assessment areas that have high impact; projects are completed during a planning cycle before the next assessment begins.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessment must be a group exercise rather than a top-down management evaluation; standard questions and look-for's must be used consistently to ensure comparable results; the assessment focuses on process maturity rather than popularity or speed; Capability Maturity Model levels must be achieved in order and cannot be skipped; Level 3 is generally considered sufficient for most services unless they are high-priority or revenue-generating; raises and bonuses must not be tied to assessment results.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For low-priority services reaching Level 5 is considered a waste of resources or professional negligence; outages and escalated issues must result in action plans to prevent future occurrences; at Level 4 maturity any exceptional cases requiring ad hoc treatment must be measured and cataloged.

Concept_Name: file storage
Core_Definition: A major application provided to a company by an enterprise IT organization.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; company employees; operational teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The organization identifies the service as a major application for the assessment process; teams conduct periodic evaluations of the service based on eight core operational responsibilities; maturity levels between 1 and 5 are assigned based on documentation and measurement criteria; service assessments are rolled up into a comparison spreadsheet to identify priorities; the team selects specific improvement projects to fix high-impact issues; the service is reassessed in the following cycle to measure effectiveness.
Rules_and_Constraints: The assessment must evaluate the degree of formality and optimization of processes rather than speed or availability; managers must hold teams to high standards for accuracy and consistency; goals should be set to fix specific problems rather than just to raise an assessment number; the same set of questions should be used for every assessment period; Level 3 should be achieved across most responsibilities before pursuing Level 4 or 5 for non-critical services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Dissatisfied customers must have a voice and a channel to escalate issues; exceptions to standard processes are measured and periodically reviewed at maturity Level 4; it is considered a trap to mandate a specific maturity level for all services regardless of their priority.

Concept_Name: centralized compute farm
Core_Definition: A major application provided to a company by an enterprise IT organization.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; company employees; operational teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The compute farm is categorized as a major service for operational assessment; staff perform monthly or periodic assessments using eight operational responsibilities such as capacity planning and change management; results are recorded in a heat map spreadsheet using color-coded maturity levels; assessments are rolled up using a weighted average or mathematical mode for comparison; teams brainstorm and execute projects to improve low-ranking responsibilities; the cycle repeats to create an environment of continuous improvement.
Rules_and_Constraints: Improvements must be prompted by measurements and evaluated based on new data; roles and responsibilities must be defined and confirmed to reach Level 3 maturity; assessments should be used as a self-improvement tool to motivate teams; the frequency of assessment should be often enough to track progress but not so frequent that it becomes a waste of time; one must never set a corporate goal of achieving a specific assessment level for all services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: High-priority or revenue-generating services may justify the resources required for Level 4 or 5 maturity while others do not; outages must lead to the publication of postmortems and action plans; variation in processes is measured and dashboards are used to spot bottlenecks at higher maturity levels.

Concept_Name: desktop/laptop fleet management
Core_Definition: A major application and coordination function provided to a company by an enterprise IT organization.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; company employees; operational teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The fleet management service is identified as a major component of the IT service portfolio; teams evaluate the service against operational responsibilities like new product introduction and service decommissioning; assessment values are recorded with notes in a sub-sheet of a tracking document; the team uses the assessment to identify problem areas needing resources or attention; high-impact improvement projects are selected for the upcoming quarter; the team conducts follow-up assessments to verify the success of those projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: The organization must be transparent about how it plans and operates the service; look-for's should be used as indicators of maturity rather than strict checklists; process maturity must move from ad hoc heroics to repeatable and documented steps; the goal of assessment is to drive decisions based on data rather than intuition; staff should use small batches for assessment cycles to avoid bureaucratic waste.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the service is not directly revenue-generating a Level 3 assessment is usually considered good enough; any exceptional cases requiring special treatment must be tracked at Level 4 to justify process changes; when things go wrong the organization must be honest about its own faults and create plans to reduce future occurrences.

Concept_Name: DNS
Core_Definition: An infrastructure service provided to a company by an enterprise IT organization.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; operational teams; management; customers.
Operational_Workflow: The service is identified as a major infrastructure component for evaluation; the operational team performs a periodic self-assessment of the service across eight core operational responsibilities such as regular tasks and emergency response; the team assigns a maturity level from one to five based on the Capability Maturity Model; these assessment values are recorded in a spreadsheet to track progress over time; the team uses the results to identify problem areas and determine improvement projects for the next work cycle.
Rules_and_Constraints: The same assessment questions must be used for each assessment period to ensure results are comparable; management must hold the team to high standards for accuracy and consistency; assessments should be a group exercise rather than a top-down management grade; Level 3 is generally considered the target for most services before attempting Level 4 or 5.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: DHCP
Core_Definition: An infrastructure service managed by an IT organization that may be grouped with other name services for assessment in smaller sites.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; operational teams; team managers; service users.
Operational_Workflow: DHCP is categorized as a major service and evaluated against eight operational responsibilities like change management and capacity planning; the team assesses the service's maturity level ranging from ad hoc and chaotic to optimized; ratings are recorded along with justifying notes; the resulting data is rolled up into a heat map to visualize service quality; this information guides the team in brainstorming and selecting projects that fix the highest-priority issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments should be periodic and repeatable such as on a monthly schedule; organizations must pass through maturity levels in order and cannot skip levels; avoid setting goals to achieve specific assessment levels for the sake of the numbers alone; raises and bonuses should not be linked to assessment results to avoid discouraging work on difficult projects.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: ActiveDirectory/LDAP
Core_Definition: A major application or infrastructure service provided to a company, sometimes referred to as a name service in specific environments.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; operational teams; executive leadership; internal stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The service is identified and evaluated by the team responsible for its operation; the team assesses eight core operational responsibilities including performance and efficiency and service deployment; each responsibility is rated on a five-point scale to reflect process formality and maturity; progress is documented in a roll-up spreadsheet used to compare the maturity of various services; these findings inform the strategic planning of projects to improve service delivery.
Rules_and_Constraints: The evaluation rubric must be consistent across all services and teams; individual heroics are associated with Level 1 and should be replaced by documented and repeatable processes; for Level 4 maturity, measurements must be collected automatically and displayed on a dashboard; management roles involve maintaining standards for accuracy during the self-assessment process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: NTP
Core_Definition: An infrastructure service provided by an IT organization to support company-wide operations.
Key_Actors: Enterprise IT organizations; operational teams; team managers; internal customers.
Operational_Workflow: NTP is listed as a service and undergoes periodic assessment by the operational staff; the assessment evaluates eight specific categories of responsibility ranging from non-emergency duties to decommissioning; the team assigns a level from one to five for each category and justifies the rating with notes; the assessment helps the team identify which services need the most improvement and where enhancements would have the largest impact.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments should be conducted frequently enough to track progress but not so often that they become a burden; the goal of assessment is to drive scientific management and reduce reliance on gut feelings; it is recommended to reach a solid Level 3 across most responsibilities before pursuing higher levels; do not expend excessive resources to reach Level 5 for low-priority services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: reference document
Core_Definition: A document that lists the specific questions used during service assessments to ensure that evaluation numbers are comparable over time.
Key_Actors: The team or organization performing the assessment.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies major services provided by the organization; they determine a set of questions for each of the eight operational responsibilities; these questions are recorded in the reference document; the team uses this document as a standard for every assessment period to maintain consistency.
Rules_and_Constraints: The same questions must be used for each assessment to ensure the resulting data is comparable across different time periods.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: heat map
Core_Definition: A visual tracking tool created by applying specific colors to numerical assessment values in a spreadsheet to show progress over time.
Key_Actors: The individual responsible for a service; the team conducting the assessment.
Operational_Workflow: An assessment number from 1 to 5 is recorded in a spreadsheet for a service; cells are colored red for 1, orange for 2, yellow for 3, green for 4, and blue for 5; as new assessments are added in subsequent columns, the team monitors the visual change in colors to identify progress or stagnation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Colors must correspond to specific assessment levels to provide a clear visual indication of service status.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: mathematical "mode"
Core_Definition: A method of rolling up individual assessment scores by identifying the most common number among the eight operational responsibility ratings.
Key_Actors: The team conducting the group assessment; the manager responsible for accuracy.
Operational_Workflow: Each of the eight operational responsibilities for a service is assigned a maturity level from 1 to 5; the team identifies which specific level appears most frequently across these eight categories; this most common number is used as the single representative score for the service in comparison charts.
Rules_and_Constraints: The method used for rolling up numbers must be applied consistently across all services being compared.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: small batches
Core_Definition: The principle of conducting assessments and improvements in frequent, shorter cycles rather than infrequent, large-scale events.
Key_Actors: The operations team; management.
Operational_Workflow: The team schedules assessments at a high frequency such as monthly; these meetings are kept short, typically lasting one or two hours; the team uses these frequent check-ins to identify new problems and track progress continuously.
Rules_and_Constraints: Periodic assessments should avoid long gaps to prevent them from becoming a large burden or bureaucratic waste of time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Annual assessments are described as a negative edge case where the process becomes a scary and unhelpful bureaucratic event that requires stopping all work for a week to prepare for day-long meetings.

Concept_Name: rubric
Core_Definition: A consistent set of assessment questions and indicators used to evaluate the maturity of operational responsibilities across different services and teams.
Key_Actors: Senior managers; operations teams; staff responsible for services.
Operational_Workflow: Senior managers work together to ensure the rubric is used consistently across the organization; teams use the assessment questions and look-fors to describe current practices; based on these descriptions, the team evaluates which Capability Maturity Model level describes their current practice for a specific service.
Rules_and_Constraints: The rubric should be consistent across all services and all teams; look-fors within the rubric are signals and indicators rather than checklists to be strictly followed; the rubric can be revised or customized to better suit the specific needs of an organization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: professionally negligent
Core_Definition: The inappropriate expenditure of scarce resources to achieve high levels of process optimization for low-priority services while more important services remain neglected.
Key_Actors: Engineers; system administrators; management teams.
Operational_Workflow: A team identifies the priority and requirements of its various services; it determines the necessary level of process maturity for each; negligence occurs when a team expends the effort required to reach Level 5 optimization on a low-priority service instead of focusing those resources on improving higher-priority or revenue-generating services.
Rules_and_Constraints: Level 3 assessment is generally considered good enough for most services; going above Level 3 should be reserved for high-priority or demanding services; engineers should be encouraged to join projects where they can do the most good rather than simply raising an average assessment score.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: corporate change management policy
Core_Definition: A global organizational standard that defines how changes to services are purposefully managed and implemented across the entire company.
Key_Actors: Corporate management; all operational teams; service owners.
Operational_Workflow: The corporation establishes a standard policy for managing changes; individual teams apply this policy to the services they provide; during periodic assessments, management ensures that compliance with this policy is evaluated the same way for all services and by all teams to maintain consistency.
Rules_and_Constraints: Compliance with the policy must be evaluated consistently across all services by all teams; it serves as a global standard for the Change Management operational responsibility.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: scientific management
Core_Definition: A system of system administration that relies on data-driven assessments and measurements to guide decisions rather than intuition or gut feelings.
Key_Actors: IT managers; operations teams; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Operations are broken down into specific operational responsibilities; these responsibilities are assessed using a formal Capability Maturity Model; measurements and metrics are collected to identify bottlenecks and justify process changes; the success of improvements is evaluated based on new measurements.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system requires moving away from ad hoc processes and individual heroics toward repeatable and defined processes; decisions must be justified using measurement data; the goal is to identify problems and engineer solutions rather than just raising assessment levels for their own sake.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

============================================================
Appendix A. Assessments
Section: Appendix A. Assessments
============================================================

Concept_Name: Operational Responsibilities (ORs)
Core_Definition: Categorized domains of operational work, such as regular tasks and emergency response, used to assess an organization's maturity through specific questions and benchmarks.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; web operations teams; corporate entities that set standard practices.
Operational_Workflow: Teams identify the relevant operational responsibility to be evaluated; they answer sample assessment questions to gauge current practices; based on the answers, the service is categorized into one of five levels ranging from Initial to Optimizing; finally, the service is checked for compliance with any corporate standard practices for that specific responsibility.
Rules_and_Constraints: Assessments must determine if a service complies with corporate standard practices; maturity levels must follow a specific progression from ad hoc measures to data-supported modeling and optimization.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level, a team might not be able to enumerate all the processes it performs even at a high level; results are often inconsistent with different people performing the same task differently.

Concept_Name: Regular Tasks (RT)
Core_Definition: The normal, non-emergency duties of an operations team, encompassing the lifecycle of work from receipt and queuing to processing and verification.
Key_Actors: Web operations teams; stakeholders; customer support teams; users.
Operational_Workflow: Work is received from users or escalated from other teams; requests are queued, tracked, and distributed to team members; each end-to-end process is followed with each step documented; quality assurance checklists are completed before handoffs; periodic tasks are scheduled and performed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Process changes must be communicated to other teams ahead of time; information needed by multiple steps should be created only once to avoid duplication of effort; the limits of regular support such as hours of operation and location must be defined and known to users; dashboards should report data in business terms.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Requests may get lost or stalled indefinitely; user requests may occur that are not covered by existing documentation; teams may receive requests for support of unsupported services; the organization may be unable to predict how long common tasks take to complete.

Concept_Name: playbook
Core_Definition: A documented set of procedures used to handle common operational duties, user requests, failover scenarios, and outage-related duties.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; helpdesk; authorized writers and editors; team members being trained.
Operational_Workflow: The need for new playbook entries is identified; authorized personnel write new entries or edit existing ones; staff use the playbook to handle alerts or requests consistently; the playbook is regularly exercised to ensure preparedness for various failure modes.
Rules_and_Constraints: A playbook item should exist for every possible alert; the playbook must be kept up to date and cover all failure modes including large-scale disasters; playbooks must be available for use by both the helpdesk and operations teams.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Playbooks may be out of date or unused at lower maturity levels; scenarios may occur that have not been exercised recently; some user requests may not be covered by existing playbook entries.

Concept_Name: SLA
Core_Definition: A service level agreement that defines the expected response times, resolution targets, and performance standards for requests and emergency incidents.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; customers; management; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: Timeframes for alert responses and regular requests are defined; metrics such as frequency of deployment and mean time to restore service are measured automatically; performance is tracked and displayed on dashboards; data is used to model scenarios and perform experiments to improve compliance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Alert response SLAs must include specific milestones for initial response, hands-on-keyboard time, resolution, and postmortem completion; the SLA must be actively measured; goals must drive optimal behavior through key performance indicators.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The team may have a defined SLA but fail to adhere to it; postmortem reports may not be completed within the required timeframes; outages may be classified by size resulting in different expectations.

Concept_Name: postmortems
Core_Definition: Reports written after outages or major incidents to document the event and provide a mechanism for remediation and future prevention.
Key_Actors: Affected teams; cross-functional teams; management; oncall staff; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: A major outage occurs; a draft report is published within a specified number of hours; a final report is completed within a set number of days; reports are published for all to see; reports are reviewed to assure action items are completed; recommendations are triaged to ensure they are finished; the process is measured against a defined service level agreement for completion.
Rules_and_Constraints: A standard template should be used; they must be written for all major outages; a final report must be completed within a defined number of days; the process should include a mechanism to triage recommendations and ensure completion; the service level agreement for postmortem completion must be actively measured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In early assessment levels, postmortems might be written occasionally but not consistently; failures to meet deadlines for reporting or failure to complete action items represent deviations from the managed process.

Concept_Name: dashboard
Core_Definition: A visual display used to monitor and report technical and business metrics, performance data, and system status in real-time or near real-time.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; affected teams; capacity planners; stakeholders; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: Systems collect data regarding technical performance and business goals; dashboards report this data in business terms; they display each process step completion time, lag time, bottlenecks, backlogs, and idle steps; they track defect and rework counts; dashboards provide alerts for capacity planning and failover status; teams use this visual data to identify areas for improvement and verify the accuracy of collected data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Dashboards should report data in business terms rather than just technical terms; every failover procedure must have a date of last use dashboard; dashboards and displays must load quickly; they must be based on fresh data; they should include capacity planning information and track headroom levels.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial assessment levels, dashboards may be entirely absent; data used to calculate indicators may not be fresh; dashboards may show technical terms that are not aligned with business goals.

Concept_Name: process change requests
Core_Definition: Formal proposals to modify existing operational procedures to resolve identified problems or improve system efficiency.
Key_Actors: Team members at every step of a process; affected teams; cross-functional teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: A problem in a process is identified; data is collected to measure the problem being fixed; a change request is made based on variety of sources; after the change is implemented, before and after data are compared to determine success; the change is either kept or reverted based on the data; statistics on toil or performance are monitored for feedback.
Rules_and_Constraints: Requests require data to measure the problem being fixed; changes should come from a variety of sources including at least one from every process step in recent history; changes must be reverted if before and after data shows no improvement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Process changes are reverted if the data shows no improvement; in lower assessment levels, optimizations might benefit a small group to the detriment of the larger organization.

Concept_Name: Cycle time
Core_Definition: The amount of time required to complete a specific process or task from beginning to end.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; automation systems; process owners.
Operational_Workflow: A task or process is initiated; each step of the process is performed and the completion time is recorded; lag times between steps are identified and measured; the total time for the end-to-end process is calculated; the resulting data is tracked over time to identify trends.
Rules_and_Constraints: In high-functioning teams, cycle time should enjoy month-over-month improvements; decisions regarding cycle time should be supported by modeling what-if scenarios using extracted actual data; the service level agreement for completion times must be defined and measured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the initial level of assessment, organizations cannot predict how long common tasks will take to complete; requests may get lost or stalled indefinitely, leading to unmeasured or infinite cycle times.

Concept_Name: Initial (Level)
Core_Definition: This level represents a state where operational processes are ad hoc, undocumented, and rely on individual heroics, leading to inconsistent results.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; developers; users; individual heroes; teams that are surprised by new product introductions.
Operational_Workflow: Different people perform tasks differently; requests are often lost or stalled indefinitely; outages are typically reported by users instead of monitoring systems; software builds are performed manually in personal environments; toil grows unchecked until project work is nearly impossible.
Rules_and_Constraints: Playbooks are non-existent or out of date; task completion times are unpredictable; no oncall schedules or calendars are maintained; inventory is not kept; no service level objectives are documented.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Reported operational problems often fail to get attention; miscommunication or errors cause delays in introducing new products; deployments are infrequent because they are considered high-risk and laborious.

Concept_Name: Repeatable (Level)
Core_Definition: At this level, processes are documented and consistent enough that different people can achieve the same results, though many tasks remain manual and may involve redundant effort.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; monitoring systems; oncall staff; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: End-to-end processes are enumerated with documented steps; monitoring systems contact the oncall person; deployments follow a consistent but manual process; software source files are maintained in version control; equipment erasure and disposal are documented and verified.
Rules_and_Constraints: A finite list of supported services must exist; a playbook item must be available for every possible alert; an oncall schedule with an escalation plan is required; the build environment must be defined for consistent results; individuals are responsible for tracking their own toil-to-project-work ratio.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Duplication of effort may still occur within the workflow; information needed for multiple tasks might be re-created at each step; deployments may still fail frequently despite being consistent; red flags regarding toil might not be raised every time they are warranted.

Concept_Name: Defined (Level)
Core_Definition: This level is characterized by the formal definition of standards, service level agreements, and automated processes that ensure organizational roles and expectations are clear.
Key_Actors: Cross-functional teams; dedicated capacity planning person; new team members; stakeholders; management.
Operational_Workflow: Service level agreements are defined for requests and alert responses; building software is automated with specific triggers; capacity requirements are defined at a subsystem level; handoffs between teams are encoded in machine-readable formats; disaster preparedness training and exercises are implemented.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each process step must have a QA checklist completed before handoff; information needed by multiple steps is created only once to eliminate duplication; business key performance indicators and data freshness are defined; limits for oncall frequency are established; red flag and target levels for toil are defined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Defined service level agreements may exist but are not always adhered to; unit test coverage expectations are set but are typically less than one hundred percent; during red flag periods for toil, teams may put other projects on hold with management support.

Concept_Name: Managed (Level)
Core_Definition: This level involves the active measurement and management of processes through dashboards, key performance indicators, and periodic reviews to ensure operational goals are met.
Key_Actors: Affected teams; cross-functional teams; capacity planning person or team; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: Service level agreements and key performance indicators are measured and displayed on dashboards; postmortems are published and reviewed within specific timeframes; feedback mechanisms are implemented for all steps; automated systems track network devices and compare them against inventory; red flags for toil are raised automatically.
Rules_and_Constraints: Process change requests must be supported by data; oncall duties must be shared by those most able to fix problems; frequency of oncall shifts is verified against policy; deployment success and failure are measured against formal definitions; quantity of equipment disposal is tracked.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unexpected increases in demand are automatically detected to trigger investigation; automated systems flag discrepancies between actual network configurations and the inventory system; trends in time-tracking data provide early alerts for teams showing increased toil.

Concept_Name: Optimizing (Level)
Core_Definition: The fifth and highest maturity level of an operational responsibility where processes are continuously improved based on data analysis and active testing. It focuses on comparing before and after data to ensure the success of changes and utilizes modeling for decision-making.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; statisticians; automated systems; developers; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: Process changes are proposed from a variety of sources or modeled using actual data; after changes are implemented, before and after data are compared to determine success; if the data shows no improvement, the process changes are reverted; cycle times are monitored for month-over-month improvements; automated systems may artificially trigger failure modes to ensure all systems are exercised.
Rules_and_Constraints: Process changes must be reverted if data shows no improvement; decisions must be supported by modeling what-if scenarios using extracted actual data; every step in a process must have contributed at least one change in recent history; stress testing and failover testing are performed frequently such as quarterly or monthly.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a failover procedure has not been utilized in recent history, it is activated artificially; if insufficient unit test code coverage is detected, production is stopped; experiments are performed specifically to improve SLA compliance.

Concept_Name: Emergency Response (ER)
Core_Definition: An operational responsibility covering the handling of outages and disasters through engineering resilient systems and both technical and non-technical response and remediation processes. It includes the preparation for, response to, and learning from system failures.
Key_Actors: Oncall persons; monitoring systems; staff in multiple geographic regions; users; responding teams.
Operational_Workflow: Outages are detected via automatic monitoring or user complaints; a monitoring system contacts the assigned oncall person; staff utilize a playbook for failover scenarios and outage-related duties; outages are classified by size such as minor, major, or catastrophic; postmortems are written, reviewed, and recommendations are triaged for completion.
Rules_and_Constraints: A playbook must exist for every possible alert; postmortems must be published for all to see with specific deadlines for draft and final reports; oncall coverage should be geographically diverse; an SLA must be defined for alert response phases including initial response, hands-on-keyboard, resolution, and postmortem completion.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the initial level of maturity, outages are reported by users rather than monitoring systems and no one or everyone may be oncall; failover procedures not used in recent history are activated artificially; Game Day exercises are used as intensive system-wide tests to simulate disasters.

Concept_Name: monitoring system
Core_Definition: A technical framework used to collect data about systems and services to detect outages, provide alerts, and measure performance against objectives. It serves as the primary mechanism for outage detection and metric collection.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; server instances; automated systems; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Systems and services are added to the monitoring system during the creation process; the system collects technical and business metrics; it identifies outages or sick systems and alerts the oncall person; meta-monitoring is employed to ensure the monitoring system itself remains functional; dashboards display the collected data in technical or business terms.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process for creating server instances must ensure they are automatically monitored; the monitoring system itself must be monitored; completeness of monitoring must be verifiable; capacity planning for monitoring storage, CPU, and network must be performed with the same sophistication as major services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level, monitoring may be incomplete or systems may be added manually; monitoring systems might go down, which is why meta-monitoring is required; at the optimizing level, the system alerts on indications of sick systems before an actual outage occurs.

Concept_Name: oncall calendar
Core_Definition: A schedule that designates specific staff members to be responsible for emergency response and alert handling during defined periods. It ensures that there is always a known point of contact for system issues.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; staff members; operations teams; managers.
Operational_Workflow: The calendar is created through a repeatable process for the upcoming month; it incorporates an escalation plan for instances where the primary contact is unavailable; coverage is distributed among those most able to fix problems; the frequency of oncall shifts is verified against defined policy limits.
Rules_and_Constraints: There must be a repeatable process for creating the next month's calendar; the calendar must be available and include an escalation plan; limits and minimums for how often people should be oncall must be defined; the oncall pain should be shared by the people most able to fix the problems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level, there may be no calendar at all, or a single person might be permanently oncall; if a person is oncall too often, it must be verified against the policy; the schedule may need to be adjusted based on geographic diversity to cover multiple time zones.

Concept_Name: oncall schedule
Core_Definition: A planned calendar or rotation that designates which staff members are responsible for emergency response and service outages at any given time.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; staff most able to fix problems; management or team leaders responsible for calendar creation.
Operational_Workflow: The team establishes a repeatable process for creating the next month's oncall calendar; a monitoring system is configured to automatically contact the designated individual on the schedule; limits and minimums for oncall frequency are defined and verified against policy; oncall duties are shared among the people most capable of resolving technical issues.
Rules_and_Constraints: Oncall coverage should be geographically diverse across multiple time zones; limits and minimums for how often people are oncall must be defined; the oncall pain should be shared by the people most able to fix problems; frequency of oncall shifts must be verified against established policy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial maturity levels there may be no oncall schedule or calendar at all; a single person might be always oncall or conversely everyone might be always oncall; outages may be reported by users rather than the monitoring system if the schedule is not properly integrated.

Concept_Name: escalation plan
Core_Definition: A predefined procedure that dictates how to seek additional support or involve higher-level teams when an incident cannot be resolved by the primary responder.
Key_Actors: Oncall person; web operations teams; direct customer support teams; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: When an incident is detected or reported it is assigned to a responder; if the responder cannot resolve the issue or it meets certain criteria the escalation plan is activated to involve other teams; requests may be escalated from direct customer support teams to web operations; the plan facilitates communication and coordination between different teams to ensure the problem gets attention.
Rules_and_Constraints: An escalation plan must exist as part of the repeatable emergency response process; it should be used to prevent requests from getting lost or stalled indefinitely.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial maturity levels there is no escalation plan or it is ad hoc; without a plan operational problems may not receive attention even if reported; inconsistent results may occur if different people follow different escalation paths.

Concept_Name: postmortem template
Core_Definition: A standardized document or format used to consistently record the details, causes, and remediation actions following an outage or disaster.
Key_Actors: Affected operational teams; cross-functional teams; stakeholders; management.
Operational_Workflow: A standard template is established to guide the documentation of outages; after a major incident a draft report is created within a specified number of hours; a final report is completed within a defined number of days; the completed postmortems are published for visibility; reports are reviewed through a mechanism that triages recommendations and ensures action items are finished.
Rules_and_Constraints: Postmortems must be written for all major outages; a standard template must exist to ensure consistency; there should be a defined SLA for the completion of the postmortem; action items within the postmortem must be reviewed to assure completion.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At lower maturity levels postmortems might be written occasionally but not consistently; in initial stages there is no deadline for when a postmortem must be completed or a template may not exist at all.

Concept_Name: oncall person
Core_Definition: The individual currently assigned by the schedule to respond to alerts and manage emergency response duties for a service.
Key_Actors: Individual team members; monitoring systems; automated alerting tools.
Operational_Workflow: The designated individual is contacted by a monitoring system when an outage occurs; the person follows a playbook that details what to do for specific alerts; they provide the initial response and hands-on-keyboard effort to resolve the issue; they participate in the post-incident process such as providing input for postmortems.
Rules_and_Constraints: The oncall person should have a playbook item for any possible alert; their performance is measured against an SLA for alert response and resolution; they should be among the staff most able to fix problems to ensure the oncall pain is shared appropriately.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial maturity levels a single person may be perpetually oncall or no one may be designated at all; if a person is the only one oncall they may face exhaustion or lack of support; in the absence of a playbook the oncall person may have to rely on individual heroics or inconsistent ad hoc measures.

Concept_Name: Game Day exercises
Core_Definition: Intensive, system-wide tests designed to assess the resilience and emergency response capabilities of a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: These exercises are conducted periodically as part of an optimizing level of operational maturity for emergency response.
Rules_and_Constraints: Game Day exercises are identified as a high-level activity within the emergency response operational responsibility; they are performed as part of a strategy to move beyond basic response toward optimized system reliability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: failover procedure
Core_Definition: A documented process used to transition service to redundant components or systems during an outage or disaster.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Each procedure is tracked using a dashboard that displays its date of last use; the procedure is activated artificially if it has not been utilized in recent history to ensure its continued effectiveness.
Rules_and_Constraints: Every failover procedure must be associated with a date of last use dashboard; procedures not used recently must be activated artificially rather than waiting for a natural failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a procedure has not occurred naturally within a certain period, automated systems or manual mechanisms are used to artificially cause a failure or activate the procedure to exercise the failure mode.

Concept_Name: stress testing
Core_Definition: A practice performed to evaluate how systems and operational teams respond under pressure or extreme conditions.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Stress testing is conducted frequently, specifically on a quarterly or monthly basis, as part of the optimizing phase of emergency response.
Rules_and_Constraints: These tests must be performed frequently to maintain an optimizing level of operational excellence; they are grouped with failover testing as key components of system assessment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: failover testing
Core_Definition: A testing activity focused on verifying that the systems can successfully shift operations to backup resources during a failure.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Failover testing is executed on a frequent schedule, typically occurring quarterly or monthly.
Rules_and_Constraints: This testing is a requirement for reaching the optimizing level of maturity in emergency response; it is often paired with stress testing to ensure disaster preparedness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Monitoring and Metrics (MM)
Core_Definition: Monitoring and Metrics involves collecting data about a system and using that data to measure quantifiable components of performance such as bandwidth, speed, or latency. It includes using derived metrics and business goals to make informed decisions about resource use and service level compliance.
Key_Actors: Web operations teams; stakeholders; direct customer support teams; cross-functional teams; the monitoring system itself; users.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the collection of technical metrics like bandwidth and latency alongside derived metrics such as ratios and percentiles. As systems are created, they are automatically added to the monitoring system to ensure coverage. The workflow progresses to defining service level objectives and business key performance indicators. Data is then displayed on dashboards in both technical and business terms. In advanced stages, the system performs capacity planning for the monitoring infrastructure itself and uses active testing to verify the accuracy of the collected data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Service level objectives must be documented and match customer needs. Monitoring must be complete with a verification process to ensure no services are missed. Dashboards should report data in business terms. The monitoring system itself must be monitored. Accuracy and freshness of data must be verified. Decisions should be supported by modeling what-if scenarios using actual data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial stages, systems may be manually added to monitoring or not monitored at all. Monitoring systems can go down, requiring meta-monitoring. Data used for calculations may be stale or inaccurate if not measured. Optimizations might occasionally benefit a small group to the detriment of the larger organization.

Concept_Name: service level objective (SLO)
Core_Definition: A documented target that defines the expected performance and availability of a service to ensure it matches customer needs.
Key_Actors: Customers; operational teams; stakeholders; management.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts with identifying customer needs and documenting them as specific objectives. These objectives are then integrated into the monitoring system to track compliance. Performance against these objectives is reported on dashboards. In the event of an outage or disaster, the SLO guides the response and dictates deadlines for postmortem reports.
Rules_and_Constraints: SLOs must be documented and actively monitored. They must be aligned with customer requirements. At higher operational levels, the SLA for alert response and postmortem completion is defined and measured against these objectives.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At lower maturity levels, no SLOs are documented. A common issue occurs when a team believes customers are satisfied based on internal assumptions even though the service does not meet actual customer needs.

Concept_Name: key performance indicators (KPIs)
Core_Definition: Quantifiable measurements used to evaluate the success of a service in meeting business goals, performance targets, and resource efficiency.
Key_Actors: Cross-functional teams; management; operational staff; statisticians.
Operational_Workflow: Business KPIs are defined along with requirements for data freshness. Systems are established to collect the data necessary for these indicators, and the results are presented on dashboards. These indicators are used to set goals that drive optimal behavior across the organization. In highly optimized environments, KPIs are calculated using data that is less than a minute old to provide real-time feedback on bottlenecks, backlogs, and toil.
Rules_and_Constraints: KPIs must be defined for business terms rather than just technical ones. The freshness and accuracy of the data used for KPIs must be measured and displayed on dashboards. KPIs should be used to drive behavior that optimizes organizational performance rather than just departmental performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If data freshness is not tracked, KPIs may be calculated using outdated information. Departmental goals may sometimes emphasize specific performance metrics to the detriment of the larger organizational system.

Concept_Name: meta-monitoring
Core_Definition: The practice of monitoring the monitoring system itself to ensure it is functional and available.
Key_Actors: The monitoring system; operational teams.
Operational_Workflow: This functions as a secondary layer of observation where a separate system or process verifies that the primary monitoring system is operational. It involves checking if the monitoring system is down and ensuring that the data being collected is fresh. At advanced levels, it includes capacity planning for the monitoring system's storage, CPU, and network resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: Teams must have a documented way to know if the monitoring system itself is down. The monitoring system must be treated with the same level of sophistication as any major service it monitors.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter beyond the risk of the monitoring system itself failing.

Concept_Name: Capacity Planning (CP)
Core_Definition: The process of determining future resource needs by understanding how many resources are required per unit of growth. It includes technical assessments alongside non-technical aspects like budgeting, forecasting, and supply chain management.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; dedicated capacity planning individuals or teams; statisticians; stakeholders; and automated systems for gaining capacity.
Operational_Workflow: The team collects CP metrics either on demand or through automated continuous systems; establishes a precise inventory of all systems; defines the amount of headroom required for surges; triggers load testing based on major changes; uses statistical models to predict future needs; and compares past projections with actual results to optimize the system and reduce waste.
Rules_and_Constraints: CP should be the responsibility of a specific person or team rather than everyone or no one; capacity needs must be predicted ahead of actual need; headroom must be systematically optimized to reduce waste; and process changes should be supported by modeling what-if scenarios using extracted actual data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems may run out of capacity due to reactive planning or guessing; unexpected increases in demand are detected using MACD metrics or similar techniques to trigger adjustments; performance regressions may be detected during testing which requires CP involvement if the regression will survive into production.

Concept_Name: load-test
Core_Definition: A process used to evaluate system performance and determine how many resources are needed per unit of growth or to survive major changes. It is a technical tool used within capacity planning and deployment phases.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; developers; operations teams; and continuous test environments.
Operational_Workflow: Testing is performed occasionally when a service is new; then it is triggered by major software and hardware changes; and eventually it is integrated as part of a continuous test environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Load testing should be used to define capacity requirements at a subsystem level; results should be compared against documented performance targets; and testing must be done frequently in high-maturity organizations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If load testing is not performed before deployment, products might degrade performance unexpectedly even if they were intended to accelerate it; performance regressions found during load testing that are not bugs must be addressed by capacity planning.

Concept_Name: inventory
Core_Definition: A maintained record of all machines, servers, and devices used in a service, including their hardware configurations and network connection status. It is essential for calculating resource and cost efficiency.
Key_Actors: Operations staff; datacenter technicians; and automated tracking systems.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are added to the inventory during configuration and associated with specific services; the inventory is updated as part of capacity changes; and automated systems track devices on the network to compare them with the inventory system and flag discrepancies.
Rules_and_Constraints: Inventory must be kept up-to-date through a documented process followed by everyone; roles and responsibilities for maintaining the inventory must be clearly defined; and inventory accuracy is a prerequisite for Level 2 repeatable performance and efficiency operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: No inventory is kept in initial stages; inventory may be out-of-date or incomplete depending on who is involved in receiving or disposing of items; and automated systems may flag problems when network configurations do not match the recorded inventory.

Concept_Name: CP metrics
Core_Definition: Data points collected and used to measure resource usage and performance trends to inform capacity planning decisions. These metrics help identify the relationship between core drivers and resource units.
Key_Actors: Capacity planners; monitoring systems; and analysis systems.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics are initially collected on demand or when needed; the process becomes repeatable and then automated; metrics are eventually collected continuously on a daily or weekly basis and displayed on dashboards.
Rules_and_Constraints: CP metrics should be collected automatically and available at all times; changes in correlation between metrics must be detected to adjust relationships between drivers and resources; and metrics must be verified through active testing for accuracy.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Unexpected increases in demand are identified via metrics which generate tickets for the CP team; insufficient data or manual collection processes can lead to surprises and hard-to-diagnose performance problems.

Concept_Name: MACD metrics
Core_Definition: A technical method or similar technique used to automatically detect unexpected increases in demand within a system.
Key_Actors: Automated monitoring systems; capacity planning person; capacity planning team.
Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system tracks resource demand; the MACD technique identifies an unexpected increase in that demand; the system automatically generates a ticket; the capacity planning person or team receives the ticket to verify and adjust resource relationships.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is utilized at the Managed level of capacity planning maturity; it must be integrated with automated detection and ticketing systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: headroom
Core_Definition: The amount of slack or spare capacity maintained in a system to ensure it can survive typical surges in demand.
Key_Actors: Operations team; capacity planners; automated monitoring systems; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: The team defines the amount of headroom needed for surges; monitoring systems track the current headroom; data is displayed on dashboards comparing actual headroom to desired headroom; at higher maturity levels the amount is systematically optimized to reduce waste.
Rules_and_Constraints: At the Managed level minimum, maximum, and 90th percentile headroom must be tracked; the desired amount of headroom must be defined; headroom should be systematically optimized to reduce waste rather than relying on guesswork.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the Initial level of maturity organizations often maintain a large, unmeasured amount of headroom rather than knowing precisely how much slack is actually needed.

Concept_Name: capacity planners
Core_Definition: A specific person or team responsible for determining future resource needs and managing the technical and non-technical aspects of service growth.
Key_Actors: Capacity planning person; dedicated capacity planning team; monitoring systems; automated ticketing systems.
Operational_Workflow: They collect metrics automatically or on demand; they perform load testing and maintain accurate inventory; they respond to automated tickets regarding unexpected demand increases; they adjust relationships between core drivers and resource units when correlations change.
Rules_and_Constraints: A dedicated capacity planning person should be on the team by the Defined level of maturity; they must predict capacity needs ahead of actual need; they are responsible for verifying and adjusting relationships between drivers and resources.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level of maturity capacity planning is often the job of no one or everyone which leads to reactive operations and the system running out of capacity.

Concept_Name: statistician
Core_Definition: A specialized role employed by a team to provide advanced mathematical analysis and modeling for capacity planning.
Key_Actors: The operations team; the statistician.
Operational_Workflow: The team hires or designates a statistician to analyze operational data; the statistician applies statistical models to help the team reach the highest level of capacity planning maturity.
Rules_and_Constraints: The employment of a statistician is a specific characteristic for reaching Level 5 (Optimizing) in the Capacity Planning assessment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Change Management (CM)
Core_Definition: Change Management covers the deliberate evolution of services over time, encompassing the software delivery platform and hardware updates such as firmware and minor revisions. It specifically includes the steps of developing, building, testing, and pushing software into production.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; software delivery platforms; hardware systems.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the development of software or hardware revisions; followed by building the software into packages; testing the changes; and finally pushing the verified release into the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Minor and major releases should be handled differently; the definition of a successful deployment must be clearly established; expectations for the time gap between release availability and actual deployment should be defined and measured; corporate standard practices for change management must be followed where they exist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Operations and developers may bicker over the timing of deployments; releases may remain undeployed for weeks or months after they are available; deployments can be highly risky and fail often if they are performed in an ad hoc or inconsistent manner.

Concept_Name: deployments
Core_Definition: Deployments are the specific acts of pushing software releases into a live production environment. They represent the transition of code from a package or set of files to a running service.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; stakeholders; automated testing and deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: A package is selected and promoted from a testing environment to a production environment; the service is created in the production environment for live use; the success or failure of the push is measured against established definitions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployments should ideally require no downtime at advanced stages; success rates and frequency of deployment must be tracked; the mean time to restore service following a deployment should be measured; metrics regarding reduced production capacity during the deployment must be monitored.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Initial level deployments are often very risky and performed sparingly; they may require significant downtime; different team members might perform them using inconsistent methods; deployments may fail due to manual steps or lack of repeatable procedures.

Concept_Name: deployment process
Core_Definition: The deployment process is the technical methodology and sequence of steps used to create a running environment for testing or live production. It focuses on the transition of software packages into active services.
Key_Actors: Operations team members; testing systems; package repositories; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts with identifying that a new release is available in a package repository; a decision is made to promote the package from testing to production; system, performance, load, or user acceptance testing is performed; the environment is created; and testing results are communicated to stakeholders.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be documented and repeatable rather than ad hoc; testing procedures should be documented and eventually fully automated; success/fail ratios and downtime metrics should be presented on a dashboard; promotion decisions should ideally be automated with limited exceptions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The process can be laborious and manual; inconsistent results occur when the process is defined by who performs it rather than what is done; miscommunication or lack of resources can cause the process to be delayed or stalled.

Concept_Name: change-freeze windows
Core_Definition: Change-freeze windows are designated periods mentioned as part of change management assessments to determine how services are controlled.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text includes the existence of change-freeze windows as a sample assessment question for evaluating a service's change management maturity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: New Product Introduction and Removal (NPI/NPR)
Core_Definition: A coordination function that manages how new products and services are introduced into an environment and how they are eventually removed.
Key_Actors: Multiple teams; testing and qualification teams; purchasing department; datacenter technicians; helpdesk; operations staff; current users.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with testing and qualification of hardware or requirements gathering and evaluation for software; procurement and licensing are then handled; playbooks are created for helpdesk and operations teams; for removal, current users are identified and migration timelines are agreed upon; finally, the product is decommissioned along with its associated licenses, maintenance contracts, monitoring, and documentation.
Rules_and_Constraints: The majority of work consists of communication and coordination between teams; handoffs between teams should be written and agreed upon; equipment erasure and disposal must be documented and verified; at higher maturity levels, the maximum number of products supported by each team is defined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Teams may be surprised by introduction requests with little notice; delays can occur due to lack of capacity or miscommunication; deprecating old products is sometimes rarely done, resulting in operations supporting an infinite number of versions.

Concept_Name: support infrastructure
Core_Definition: The framework of systems and team resources required to integrate and maintain a new product or service within the organization.
Key_Actors: Multiple teams.
Operational_Workflow: The support infrastructure is touched and utilized during the coordination phase of introducing a new product or service into the environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: purchasing department
Core_Definition: The organizational unit responsible for the formal process of acquiring hardware machines and equipment.
Key_Actors: Purchasing department; teams performing testing and qualification of hardware.
Operational_Workflow: The department executes a process to purchase machines once they have been qualified and tested by other technical teams during the new product introduction phase.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: datacenter technicians
Core_Definition: Technical personnel responsible for the physical hardware environment who require specific documentation to manage new equipment.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: As part of the introduction of new hardware, these technicians receive documentation necessary to support and maintain the new computer models being placed in the datacenter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: requirements gathering
Core_Definition: Requirements gathering is a coordination task performed during the introduction of new software and services to establish the necessary support infrastructure.
Key_Actors: Coordinating teams involved in the introduction of a new software or service.
Operational_Workflow: This task is conducted during the new product introduction process to identify what is needed before a service is brought into being.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is part of a support infrastructure that may touch multiple teams and is considered a communication and coordination function.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial maturity level, products may be introduced through ad hoc measures or individual heroics, meaning requirements gathering is bypassed and teams are surprised by new deployment needs with little notice.

Concept_Name: evaluation and procurement
Core_Definition: Evaluation and procurement is the coordination process of testing hardware or software for qualification and then formally purchasing it through a designated department.
Key_Actors: Testing teams; qualification teams; the purchasing department; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: Teams obtain access to sample hardware for testing and qualification; the purchasing department then follows its process to purchase the machines while documentation is provided to datacenter technicians.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a coordination function that requires a support infrastructure and involves communication between multiple teams to ensure readiness.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The introduction of a product may be delayed due to a lack of capacity, miscommunication between teams, or errors in the process.

Concept_Name: licensing
Core_Definition: Licensing refers to the management of legal and maintenance permissions for software and services during their introduction and their eventual decommissioning.
Key_Actors: Coordinating teams for product introduction and removal; maintenance contract managers.
Operational_Workflow: Licensing tasks are completed when new software or services are introduced; when products are removed, associated licenses and maintenance contracts must be identified and decommissioned.
Rules_and_Constraints: Licensing is a coordination task that requires updating documentation and agreeing on timelines for migrating users away before decommissioning occurs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failure to properly decommission licenses or deprecate old products can result in operations having to support an infinite number of hardware or software versions.

Concept_Name: helpdesk
Core_Definition: The helpdesk is a support function that receives, tracks, and processes user requests using documented playbooks and defined engagement methods.
Key_Actors: Users; helpdesk staff; customer support teams; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Users engage the helpdesk through online or physical locations; requests are received and tracked using playbooks for common tasks. When new products are introduced, specific playbooks are created for the helpdesk as part of the support infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The helpdesk should have a defined SLA for requests and clearly communicate the limits of support, such as hours of operation and which services are supported versus unsupported.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In poorly managed environments, requests may get lost or stalled indefinitely, and the helpdesk may receive requests for support of the unsupported that require a specific response protocol.

Concept_Name: product removal
Core_Definition: The coordination function involving the identification, migration, and decommissioning of old hardware or software and its associated infrastructure.
Key_Actors: Multiple coordinated teams; current users; purchasing department; datacenter technicians.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by finding all machines running old releases of software or versions of hardware; current users are identified and migration timelines are agreed upon; documentation is updated to reflect the change; the product is eventually decommissioned along with any associated licenses, maintenance contracts, monitoring configurations, and playbooks; for hardware, the process concludes with disk erasure and physical disposal.
Rules_and_Constraints: Equipment erasure and disposal must be documented and verified; the majority of the work must consist of communication and coordination between teams; the number of no-longer-supported products should be tracked and their decommissioning should be treated as a high priority.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If deprecating old products is rarely done, operations may be forced to support an infinite number of hardware or software versions; errors or miscommunications can delay the removal process.

Concept_Name: current users
Core_Definition: The entities or individuals utilizing a specific hardware or software product that is targeted for removal or migration.
Key_Actors: Users of the product; teams responsible for migration and decommissioning.
Operational_Workflow: In the context of product removal, teams must first identify all current users of the system; following identification, teams must communicate with these users to agree on timelines for migrating them away from the old product.
Rules_and_Constraints: Migration of current users must be completed before the product can be decommissioned; coordination and communication with these users are essential components of the removal process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: product
Core_Definition: Hardware or software units introduced into or removed from an environment that require a support infrastructure and inter-team coordination.
Key_Actors: Testing and qualification teams; purchasing department; datacenter technicians; helpdesk; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Introduction involves requirements gathering, evaluation, procurement, licensing, and testing; for hardware, technicians create documentation and purchasing sets up buying processes; for software, playbooks are created for the helpdesk and operations; during removal, the product is decommissioned and its associated maintenance contracts and monitoring are terminated.
Rules_and_Constraints: The maximum number of products supported by each team should be defined; a list of currently supported products must be available to all teams; each team should have a playbook for tasks related to their involvement in the product lifecycle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Products are sometimes introduced through ad hoc measures or individual heroics; teams may be surprised by the introduction of a product and forced to deploy it with little notice.

Concept_Name: service
Core_Definition: A running functional environment or application created from source code and packages that is repeatedly deployed, monitored, and maintained to meet specific objectives.
Key_Actors: Web operations teams; developers; stakeholders; customers; monitoring and metrics systems.
Operational_Workflow: A service is created during a build phase from source code and then deployed into testing or production environments; it is managed through regular tasks and monitored for performance, efficiency, and availability against documented service level objectives; capacity is planned and adjusted based on growth; finally, service instances are decommissioned when they are no longer needed for reasons such as reducing excess capacity or closing datacenters.
Rules_and_Constraints: All services must have documented service level objectives and playbooks for regular tasks; monitoring must be automatically configured during installation; services should be designed to be resilient to both component and geographic failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services may experience outages or disasters requiring emergency response; performance may be unexpectedly degraded by new products or changes if not properly measured; deployment may require significant downtime if the process is not optimized.

Concept_Name: handoff
Core_Definition: The formal transfer of responsibility, data, or tasks between different individuals, teams, or phases of a service lifecycle to ensure continuity and quality.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; responding team members during disasters; stakeholders; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: Work progresses through defined steps where each step requires a completed quality assurance checklist before being passed to the next stage; in software delivery, a package repository is used to hand off results from the build phase to the deployment phase; in emergency response, responsibility is transferred between team members; at high maturity levels, these handoffs are encoded in machine-readable formats and organized as linear flows.
Rules_and_Constraints: Handoff processes must be written and agreed upon by all involved teams; procedures must include specific checks to be performed and documented; the process should be actively revised and improved over time; at advanced levels, handoffs should be machine-readable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During disasters, if handoffs are not clearly defined, it can lead to duplication of effort; requests may be lost or stalled indefinitely if handoff processes are undocumented or haphazard.

Concept_Name: equipment erasure and disposal
Core_Definition: The documented and verified process of permanently deleting data from storage media and removing physical hardware from the environment in compliance with legal and organizational standards.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians; operations teams; purchasing department; environmental regulatory bodies.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with the identification of old hardware for removal; storage devices such as disks undergo a data erasure process based on organizational standards; the physical equipment is then disposed of according to local environmental laws; the final steps include documenting the actions, verifying the results, and updating the inventory.
Rules_and_Constraints: All erasure and disposal actions must be documented and verified; processes must comply with local environmental standards and the organization’s specific data erasure requirements; the quantity of disposal and compliance levels must be measured at higher maturity levels.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Inventory records may become inaccurate if the individuals involved in receiving or disposing of items fail to follow the documented update process.

Concept_Name: API calls
Core_Definition: Programmatic interfaces used between teams to automate and standardize the introduction or removal of products and services.
Key_Actors: Technical teams; teams responsible for new product introduction and removal.
Operational_Workflow: Manual coordination and communication tasks are replaced by automated requests; these calls allow teams to interact with the systems and processes of other teams without human intervention to facilitate product lifecycles.
Rules_and_Constraints: The use of API calls for coordination tasks is a requirement for reaching the highest optimization level of operational maturity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: self-service
Core_Definition: An operational model that allows the team responsible for a product or service to perform necessary lifecycle tasks independently using automated tools.
Key_Actors: The team responsible for a specific product or process.
Operational_Workflow: For tasks such as product introduction or removal, the responsible team utilizes provided interfaces or tools to execute the entire process without requiring manual assistance or coordination from external teams.
Rules_and_Constraints: Self-service capabilities are identified as a characteristic of a highly optimized coordination function.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Service Deployment and Decommissioning (SDD)
Core_Definition: Service Deployment and Decommissioning covers the processes of creating new instances of an existing service design and turning off existing instances. This includes the repeated deployment of services in new locations or environments and their eventual removal to reduce capacity or close facilities.
Key_Actors: Multiple teams that touch the processes; operational staff responsible for the workflow; datacenter technicians for physical components; management for predicted capacity needs and process oversight.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins after a service has been designed and moves into repeated deployment; this may involve turning up satellite replicas in new datacenters or creating specific environments like development or demo instances; the workflow proceeds from team to team using specific tools and documentation; decommissioning occurs when an instance is turned down to reduce excess capacity, close a datacenter, or remove a specific demo environment; the final stage involves equipment erasure and disposal.
Rules_and_Constraints: Processes for deployment and decommissioning must be understood, documented, and repeatable; each step in the workflow requires a QA checklist to be completed before handoff to the next team; information required by multiple steps should be created once to minimize duplication of effort; physical deployments must follow local environmental standards for waste removal; hardware disposal must comply with both local environmental laws and the organization's data erasure standards; SLAs for the duration of each step should be defined, measured, and used to predict capacity needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Requests may be delayed or stalled due to miscommunication, lack of resources, or avoidable administrative reasons; processes may be undocumented and haphazard, leading to inconsistent results; tasks may be performed differently depending on which individual is assigned to the work; equipment disposal compliance may fail if not measured against organizational and legal standards.

Concept_Name: service instance
Core_Definition: A service instance is an individual occurrence of an existing service that is created through the deployment process. It represents a functional deployment of a service design used for a specific purpose or in a specific location.
Key_Actors: Teams involved in the deployment and decommissioning workflows.
Operational_Workflow: A service instance is turned up based on a previously designed service to serve as a satellite replica, a development environment, or a demo environment; the instance functions as part of the service capacity until it is identified for removal; the instance is then turned down or decommissioned as part of a reduction in excess capacity or a facility closure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process for turning up a service instance should be a repeatable process; each step of an instance's lifecycle should have a documented process and a QA checklist.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Instances may be managed in an undocumented or haphazard manner where different people do the tasks differently, leading to inconsistent results.

Concept_Name: development environment
Core_Definition: A development environment is a specific type of service instance of an existing service.
Key_Actors: Teams responsible for Service Deployment and Decommissioning (SDD).
Operational_Workflow: It is created as a result of the deployment process when an existing service is deployed to provide a functional instance specifically for development purposes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: demo environment
Core_Definition: A demo environment is a particular service instance created for demonstration purposes.
Key_Actors: Teams responsible for Service Deployment and Decommissioning (SDD).
Operational_Workflow: This environment is created during the service deployment phase and is later turned down or decommissioned when the specific instance is no longer required.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: turning down a datacenter
Core_Definition: A large scale decommissioning process that involves turning off service instances and hardware across an entire facility.
Key_Actors: Multiple teams; datacenter technicians; purchasing department; helpdesk; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with communication and coordination between teams to identify current users and agree on migration timelines; documentation is updated; the product and all associated licenses, maintenance contracts, monitoring, and playbooks are decommissioned; service instances are turned off; hardware is erased and disposed of; and waste material such as packaging is removed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decommissioning must follow documented and verified processes; equipment disposal and data erasure must comply with both the organization's own standards and local environmental laws; the time required for each step should be governed by a defined service level agreement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Requests for decommissioning may be delayed due to miscommunication, lack of resources, or other avoidable reasons; processes may fail if they are defined by who does the work rather than what is done.

Concept_Name: reducing excess capacity
Core_Definition: The process of turning off or decommissioning service instances that are no longer required to meet demand.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; capacity planners.
Operational_Workflow: The team identifies unused or unnecessary service instances as part of capacity planning or service decommissioning; instances are turned down according to documented procedures; hardware or virtual resources are released or repurposed; inventory systems are updated to reflect the change; and the reduction in equipment is used to optimize disposal processes.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ability to turn down capacity should be a repeatable process; the process must include verified equipment erasure and disposal; the amount of headroom in the system should be monitored and systematically optimized to reduce waste.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Reductions may be hindered if capacity planning is reactive or if inventory is not accurately maintained.

Concept_Name: waste material
Core_Definition: Physical refuse such as boxes, wrappers, and containers generated during the deployment of hardware.
Key_Actors: Datacenter technicians; environmental compliance teams.
Operational_Workflow: During the physical deployment phase of a service, packaging materials are accumulated; these materials are then collected and removed from the facility; the quantity of waste material is measured for management and optimization purposes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standards for the removal and disposal of waste material must be based on local environmental standards.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Performance and Efficiency (PE)
Core_Definition: An operational responsibility focused on how cost-effectively resources are used and how well the service performs while avoiding the waste of resources.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; statisticians; hardware vendors; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves collecting metrics for performance and utilization across all systems; defining formulas for resource and cost efficiency; setting performance and resource usage targets; identifying bottlenecks and high-resource services via dashboards; evaluating hardware models for better resource balance; and making data-driven changes to optimize the equilibrium between performance and cost.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance and efficiency require an up-to-date inventory to calculate statistics; formulas for metrics must be clearly defined; changes to the system should be supported by modeling what-if scenarios; process changes must be reverted if before-and-after data shows no improvement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Performance problems may come as a surprise if there is insufficient data or if monitoring is not consistently applied; automated systems may flag problems if devices on the network do not match the inventory configuration.

Concept_Name: utilization
Core_Definition: Utilization is a measure of how machines or server instances are being used, typically evaluated to determine if they are over-utilized or under-utilized.
Key_Actors: Monitoring systems; automated configuration systems; dashboards; services; systems.
Operational_Workflow: Metrics relevant to utilization are collected across all systems and services; monitoring is automatically configured during system installation and removed during decommissioning; formulas for service-oriented utilization metrics are defined; the resource utilization of each service and idle capacity are monitored continuously; data is visualized on dashboards and reviewed against defined headroom targets to ensure optimal behavior.
Rules_and_Constraints: Utilization monitoring must be automatically configured for all systems during installation; service-oriented metrics must have defined formulas; the desired amount of headroom must be defined and monitored; dashboards must track utilization alongside performance and resource efficiency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the initial stage of operational maturity, utilization is not consistently measured and depends on the individuals who set up the systems; insufficient data on utilization can cause performance problems to occur as a surprise.

Concept_Name: resource efficiency
Core_Definition: Resource efficiency is a performance metric defined as the quantity of resources used divided by the quantity of use.
Key_Actors: Inventory systems; monitoring systems; dashboards; key performance indicators; services.
Operational_Workflow: An accurate inventory of all machines and servers is established and maintained; resource usage targets are defined for each service; specific formulas for determining resource efficiency are established; efficiency metrics are monitored continuously and tracked on dashboards; goals are set using key performance indicators to drive optimal resource consumption.
Rules_and_Constraints: Calculating resource efficiency requires an accurate inventory to know the total quantity of resources that exist; efficiency goals must be periodically reviewed and tracked to ensure they drive optimal behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At lower levels of maturity, resource efficiency is not measured at all; optimizations may occasionally be enacted that benefit a small group to the detriment of the larger organization or system.

Concept_Name: cost-efficiency
Core_Definition: Cost-efficiency is a measure of how cost-effectively resources are utilized, calculated as the cost of resources divided by the quantity of use.
Key_Actors: Management; computer hardware models; hardware vendors; services.
Operational_Workflow: The cost of resources is analyzed against usage using defined formulas; computer hardware models are regularly evaluated to identify units where resource utilization is better balanced; alternative hardware sources and different models are investigated to determine if cost efficiency can be improved for the service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Achieving cost-efficiency requires a large effort to bring about equilibrium between performance and resource waste; inventory must be kept up-to-date to calculate these statistics.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: performance variation
Core_Definition: Performance variation refers to fluctuations or changes in service performance, which are identified by comparing metrics before and after system modifications.
Key_Actors: Engineers; measurement frameworks; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: A framework is created to measure end-to-end performance metrics such as page load times; baseline measurements are taken before a change is deployed; measurements are taken again after the change; data is compared across various geographic regions to identify improvements or performance losses.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations must measure performance before and after a change to avoid unknowingly making a service slower; data must be analyzed to ensure improvements in one aspect do not cause a net performance loss.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Changes intended to accelerate performance can inadvertently degrade it due to design errors, resulting in a net loss that may only be detected through precise end-to-end measurement.

Concept_Name: inventory system
Core_Definition: A record-keeping mechanism used to track the existence and quantity of all machines, servers, and hardware devices associated with a service.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; capacity planners; automated tracking systems; teams responsible for receiving or disposing of equipment.
Operational_Workflow: The process involves establishing an accurate list of all systems and hardware; updating this record during capacity changes or when items are received or disposed of; associating systems with specific services when they are configured and disassociating them when they are released; and using automated systems to track network-connected devices to compare their configurations against the inventory database.
Rules_and_Constraints: Inventory must be kept up-to-date to calculate resource efficiency; roles and responsibilities for maintaining the inventory must be clearly defined; systems for tracking hardware configurations on the network must be in place; updates should be integrated into the capacity change process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Inventory may be neglected or updated inconsistently depending on the individuals involved; items may be missing from the inventory record; automated systems may flag discrepancies when network-detected devices do not match the inventory system.

Concept_Name: automated systems
Core_Definition: Technical tools and processes designed to perform operational tasks such as monitoring, metric collection, software builds, and deployments without requiring manual human intervention.
Key_Actors: Automated monitoring systems; build consoles; deployment tools; network tracking systems; failover mechanisms.
Operational_Workflow: Systems automatically detect and add new services to monitoring platforms; metrics for capacity planning and performance are collected continuously; software builds and unit tests are triggered by code commits; packages are automatically promoted and deployed into production environments; and failover procedures are artificially activated by automated systems if they have not been used recently.
Rules_and_Constraints: Monitoring must be automatically configured during installation and removed during decommissioning; automated systems should track network devices and configurations to flag inventory problems; deployment and promotion should ideally be fully automated to eliminate manual errors and downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automated systems might only detect down systems rather than sick systems; some capacity increases may still require human approval; specific exceptions may be defined where promotion decisions cannot be fully automated.

Concept_Name: optimal behavior
Core_Definition: The desired state of organizational and system performance achieved by setting goals that align performance, utilization, and resource efficiency.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; management; cross-functional teams; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Key Performance Indicators are defined to set specific goals for performance, utilization, and resource efficiency; these KPIs are tracked via dashboards to drive decision-making; periodic reviews are conducted to ensure that optimizations benefit the larger organization rather than just small groups.
Rules_and_Constraints: Goals must be set and tracked to prevent departmental performance from harming organizational performance; KPIs should be used to drive behavior that reaches an equilibrium between performance and efficiency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Optimizations that benefit a single person or small group to the detriment of the larger system; departmental goals that emphasize localized performance over the health of the entire organization.

Concept_Name: Service Delivery
Core_Definition: The comprehensive technical process of creating a service, beginning with developer source code and ending with a functioning service running in a production environment.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; testing teams; stakeholders; automated build and deployment systems.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts in the build phase where raw source files are gathered from version control and compiled into packages; unit testing is performed to ensure code quality; the process then moves to the deployment phase where packages are promoted from testing environments to production; the success of the deployment is measured against defined metrics such as frequency and change success rate.
Rules_and_Constraints: All source files must come from version control; software must be delivered as packages rather than sets of files; success and failure ratios must be measured and tracked; the build and deployment processes should be automated and consistent across different environments.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failed deployments; insufficient unit test coverage that stops production; manual steps that cause ad hoc or inconsistent results; significant downtime required for service deployment; discrepancies between testing and production environments.

Concept_Name: Build Phase
Core_Definition: The build phase is a component of service delivery that involves the technical process of transforming source code created by developers into completed software packages. It is the stage where raw source files are gathered, compiled, and tested before being handed off to the deployment phase.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; version control repositories; automated build systems; package repositories.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with source code created by developers; raw source files are retrieved from one or more version control repositories; the software is built into packages; unit tests are conducted to verify the code; metrics regarding lead times and test coverage are collected; the final packages are delivered to a repository for handoff to the deployment phase.
Rules_and_Constraints: Final packages should be built directly from source code rather than precompiled elements; all raw source files must be stored in version control repositories; the resulting package must contain everything required for deployment; a single build console should ideally be used for status and control of all steps; expectations for unit test coverage should be defined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Production may be stopped if there is insufficient unit test code coverage; builds may fail or produce inconsistent results if developers build in their own environments or check in code without verifying it builds; developers may sometimes deliver precompiled elements instead of raw source code.

Concept_Name: source code
Core_Definition: Source code refers to the raw, human-readable files created by developers that serve as the primary input for the build phase of service delivery.
Key_Actors: Developers; version control repositories.
Operational_Workflow: Developers write the code and store it in version-controlled repositories; during the build phase, the build system accesses these repositories to attain all raw source files necessary to create software packages.
Rules_and_Constraints: Source files should not be kept in personal home directories or on individual computers; all raw source files used in a build must come from version control repositories; developers are expected to check that their code builds before checking it into the repository.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Master copies of source files may be improperly stored outside of version control; developers might bypass the standard build process by delivering precompiled elements instead of source files.

Concept_Name: packages
Core_Definition: Packages are the compiled or bundled outputs of the build phase that serve as the standardized units for delivering software to the deployment phase.
Key_Actors: Build systems; package repositories; developers; operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Software is built from source files into packages; these packages are then stored in a package repository; during the deployment phase, these packages are promoted from testing environments to production environments to create a running service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be delivered as a package rather than a collection of individual files; every item required for a successful deployment must be included within the package; a package repository must be used to facilitate the handoff between the build and deployment phases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Packages may fail to be promoted to production if they do not pass system, performance, or user acceptance testing; deployments may fail if the packages delivered are incomplete or were not built consistently across different platforms.

Concept_Name: unit tests
Core_Definition: Unit tests are automated or manual tests performed during the build phase to verify the integrity of the code and ensure it meets defined quality standards.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated testing and build systems.
Operational_Workflow: Unit tests are executed as part of the software build process; the percentage of code covered by these tests is measured and tracked; metrics such as success/fail ratios and coverage levels are reported on a dashboard for management and optimization.
Rules_and_Constraints: Unit tests should be fully automated; unit test coverage should be measured and tracked; specific expectations for the percentage of code covered by unit tests must be defined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Insufficient unit test code coverage is a condition that can stop a release from moving to production; in early stages of operational maturity, unit testing may be minimal, manual, or non-existent.

Concept_Name: bug lead time
Core_Definition: A metric used to track the duration of time involved in addressing and processing bugs within the software build phase of service delivery.
Key_Actors: Developers; operations teams; automated collection systems; build consoles.
Operational_Workflow: The metric is first defined as a standard requirement; automated systems collect the data during the build process; results are displayed on a dashboard for review; finally, the data is used to select and evaluate the success of optimization projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Collecting this metric is a requirement for reaching the managed and optimizing levels of the service delivery build phase.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the initial level of maturity, no metrics are collected at all.

Concept_Name: code lead time
Core_Definition: A metric that measures the time it takes for source code to move through the build phase of the service delivery process.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated measurement systems; management teams; dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations define the metric as a key part of the build phase; data collection is automated; the resulting metrics are presented on a dashboard to monitor success and failure ratios; the metrics are used to guide decisions on which optimization projects to undertake.
Rules_and_Constraints: This metric must be defined and then automatically collected to progress through the assessment levels of service delivery.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: patch lead time
Core_Definition: A metric used to measure the time required to process and build software patches during the build phase of service delivery.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated monitoring systems; build consoles; optimization teams.
Operational_Workflow: The lead time metric is formally defined; automated systems are implemented to track the metric continuously; the data is visualized on a dashboard; the metrics are analyzed to identify areas where the software delivery platform can be improved.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is one of three specific lead time metrics mentioned as necessary for a managed build phase.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial maturity level, building software is often a manual process and no metrics like patch lead time are collected.

Concept_Name: version control repositories
Core_Definition: Centralized storage systems used to manage and provide access to all raw source files required to build software.
Key_Actors: Developers; build systems; centralized repository servers; individual developer sandboxes.
Operational_Workflow: Developers create or modify source code; developers check the code into the repository after verifying it builds; the build process attains all necessary raw source files directly from these repositories to create packages; at advanced levels, developers use sandboxes to test builds before committing changes to the centralized repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: To build software, all raw source files should come from version control repositories; developers should not check in code without verifying that it builds; the build process should be automated rather than manual.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial maturity level, version control systems may not be used; master copies of source files might be incorrectly kept in personal home directories or on individual computers; developers might check in code that breaks the build.

Concept_Name: build console
Core_Definition: A centralized interface used to monitor the status and exercise control over all steps in the software build process.
Key_Actors: Developers; the software delivery platform; build management systems.
Operational_Workflow: The build console serves as a single point of reference during the build phase of service delivery; it allows actors to track the progression of software as it is transformed from raw source code into packages and provides the necessary controls to manage these steps.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text suggests a single build console should ideally manage status and control for all steps of the build process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: build environment
Core_Definition: A defined and consistent system or set of systems used to compile source code and package software to ensure predictable results.
Key_Actors: Developers; version control repositories; the software delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: Raw source files are retrieved from version control repositories; the software is compiled and packaged within the defined environment; in advanced stages, developers can perform this end-to-end process in individual sandboxes before committing changes to a centralized repository.
Rules_and_Constraints: The build environment must be defined so everyone uses the same system for consistent results; all raw source files should come from version control repositories rather than personal directories; the environment should ideally support building both infrastructure and applications.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial stages, individuals may build in their own disparate environments; developers might deliver precompiled elements instead of raw source; some source files might be stored in personal home directories or computers rather than a central repository.

Concept_Name: automated builds
Core_Definition: The technical process of automatically transforming source code into software packages using defined triggers and standardized steps.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated build systems; version control repositories; package repositories.
Operational_Workflow: The process is initiated by defined triggers; the system automatically retrieves inputs from version control; it executes the build steps to produce software packages as outputs; success and failure ratios are measured and tracked on dashboards.
Rules_and_Constraints: Triggers for automated builds must be clearly defined; inputs and outputs of each step must be defined; if multiple platforms are supported, the automation should make building for one as easy as building for all.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Builds may fail, requiring the tracking of success/fail ratios; in less mature organizations, building remains a manual or ad hoc process where people may check in code without verifying it builds.

Concept_Name: unit test coverage
Core_Definition: A metric that measures the percentage of source code executed during automated unit tests to ensure code quality.
Key_Actors: Developers; automated testing systems; the software delivery platform.
Operational_Workflow: Unit tests are performed during the build phase; the system measures and tracks the percentage of code covered by these tests; this data is then presented on dashboards and used to select optimization projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Expectations around unit test coverage must be defined; coverage must be measured and tracked; in highly optimized environments, insufficient unit test coverage is a constraint that stops production.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial stages, little or no unit testing is performed; testing may be done manually; unit test coverage expectations may be set at less than 100 percent.

Concept_Name: software delivery platform
Core_Definition: The technical framework and sequence of steps involved in a software release including development; building; testing; and pushing code into production.
Key_Actors: Developers who create source code; operations teams who handle releases; and automated systems used for building infrastructure and applications.
Operational_Workflow: The process starts with source code created by developers; the platform manages the steps to build this code into packages; it then facilitates testing; finally it pushes the result into production.
Rules_and_Constraints: The platform should be used for building both infrastructure and applications; at advanced levels it should be capable of supporting multiple platforms as easily as one; it is expected to be a centralized repository for the end-to-end build process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If unit test code coverage is found to be insufficient the platform may be used to stop the production process.

Concept_Name: Deployment Phase
Core_Definition: The stage in service delivery where the goal is to create a running environment for testing or live production services.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; and stakeholders who are informed of testing results.
Operational_Workflow: The phase starts by taking packages and creating a service in one or more environments; these environments are used for system, performance, load, or user acceptance testing; a decision is then made to promote the package from testing to production based on defined success criteria.
Rules_and_Constraints: Success definitions must be established; different procedures should exist for small, medium, and large releases; promotion decisions should ideally be automated; and metrics like change success rate must be defined.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Manual steps can lead to inconsistent results; failures in deployment are tracked to reduce their rarity over time; reduced production capacity during the deployment itself is a managed concern.

Concept_Name: downtime
Core_Definition: A period of service unavailability or reduced capacity that can occur during the deployment of a service in production.
Key_Actors: Operations teams who perform deployments; users who experience the service interruption.
Operational_Workflow: Downtime requirements are identified during the deployment planning; the duration of the outage is measured during the push to production; the resulting data is compared against defined limits and expectations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Downtime due to deployments must be defined and measured; limits and expectations for downtime should be documented; the optimization goal is to achieve deployments that require no downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial stages downtime may be significant or unpredictable; at managed levels the organization also measures the impact of reduced production capacity during the deployment process.

Concept_Name: frequency of deployment
Core_Definition: A metric used to track how often releases are pushed into the production environment.
Key_Actors: Operations teams who track the frequency; development teams whose code is being deployed.
Operational_Workflow: The organization tracks how often new releases are available versus how often they are pushed to production; this data is collected automatically at higher maturity levels; the results are then displayed on dashboards to monitor the move toward continuous deployment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment frequency is categorized as a key metric alongside mean time to restore service and change success rate; new releases should ideally be deployed with little delay.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In low-maturity environments deployments may be done sparingly because they are perceived as risky or laborious; a lack of coordination between operations and developers can lead to releases not being deployed for weeks or months after they are available.

Concept_Name: mean time to restore service
Core_Definition: A metric used during the deployment phase of service delivery to measure the time required to return a service to its functional state.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; automated metric collection systems.
Operational_Workflow: The metric is formally defined during the third level of the service deployment assessment; it is automatically collected and presented on a dashboard during the fourth level; the data is used to select and measure the results of optimization projects at the fifth level.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is established as a core metric for assessing the success and maturity of the service deployment phase.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: change success rate
Core_Definition: A metric used in the deployment phase to track the frequency and ratio of successful service changes or releases.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; developers; automated dashboard systems.
Operational_Workflow: What constitutes a successful deployment is defined at assessment level three; the success rate is measured against these definitions and collected automatically at level four; the metric is used to drive optimization projects at level five.
Rules_and_Constraints: Organizations at higher maturity levels should ensure that failed deployments are extremely rare.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: system testing
Core_Definition: A category of testing conducted during the deployment phase to evaluate a service package before it is promoted to production.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; stakeholders.
Operational_Workflow: Testing procedures are documented to ensure repeatability; results are clearly communicated to stakeholders; the process is fully automated at managed assessment levels to support the decision to promote a package from testing to production.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing results must be clearly communicated to all stakeholders.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: performance testing
Core_Definition: A type of testing used during deployment and capacity planning to evaluate how well a service performs and to detect resource regressions.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; capacity planning team; stakeholders; automated testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Testing procedures are documented and made repeatable; performance regressions are detected during the testing process; the capacity planning team is specifically involved if a detected performance regression is expected to survive into the production environment; tests are fully automated at higher maturity levels.
Rules_and_Constraints: Results from these tests must be clearly communicated to all stakeholders.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: user acceptance testing
Core_Definition: A specific type of testing conducted during the deployment phase of service delivery to evaluate a software package before it is promoted to a running environment.
Key_Actors: Stakeholders who receive testing results; the operations team responsible for the deployment phase.
Operational_Workflow: Software packages are moved into a testing environment where various tests including system, performance, load, and user acceptance are performed; results are communicated to stakeholders; these results inform the decision of whether to promote the package to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Testing results must be clearly communicated to all stakeholders; at the managed level of maturity, tests are expected to be fully automated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: production capacity
Core_Definition: The total resources available to a live service, including the necessary headroom to survive typical surges in demand.
Key_Actors: Operations team; dedicated capacity planning person; human approvers for resource increases; statisticians for advanced modeling.
Operational_Workflow: Capacity metrics are collected automatically and continuously; current inventory is tracked and compared against resource usage targets; capacity needs are predicted ahead of actual need; new capacity is gained through repeatable turn-up processes or automated systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: The amount of headroom needed to survive surges must be defined and monitored; additional capacity should be gained automatically with human approval at managed levels or fully automatically at optimizing levels; reduced production capacity during deployment must be measured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems may run out of capacity if planning is done by guessing or luck; unexpected increases in demand are detected using MACD metrics or similar techniques to trigger resource adjustments.

Concept_Name: automated deployment
Core_Definition: The process of using software delivery platforms and tools to push packages into testing or production environments without manual intervention.
Key_Actors: Operations team; developers who notify operations of releases; automated software delivery platforms.
Operational_Workflow: Software packages are received from the build phase; the deployment phase uses automated tools to create running environments in testing or production; success and failure rates are measured automatically; at the highest maturity level, the process occurs with little delay and no downtime.
Rules_and_Constraints: Deployment success must be measured against defined success criteria; the process should be consistent across both testing and production environments; continuous deployment is utilized at the optimizing level.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failed deployments are tracked and should be extremely rare at higher maturity levels; lower maturity levels are characterized by risky, ad hoc, and laborious manual deployment steps.

Concept_Name: automated promotion decisions
Core_Definition: The automated process of determining if a software package is ready to be moved from a testing environment to a production environment.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Software packages undergo automated testing within the deployment phase; the system evaluates testing results against defined metrics for success; if criteria are met, the package is automatically advanced to the production environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decisions must be based on clear testing results; promotion criteria must be defined and measured against success definitions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The text notes that at the optimizing level, promotion decisions are fully automated except for a few specific exceptions.

Concept_Name: Toil Reduction
Core_Definition: The process of improving how people are utilized within a system by assessing and limiting exhausting physical labor to create a sustainable working environment for operational staff.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; management; the person responsible for tracking toil and raising red flags; individuals who identify and prioritize toil-reduction projects.
Operational_Workflow: The team measures time spent on toil versus project work; projects are identified and prioritized based on their potential impact; after project steps are completed, toil statistics are monitored to evaluate changes; the process involves adjusting target levels to optimize overall flow and innovation.
Rules_and_Constraints: A red flag threshold must be defined for manual labor; during red flag periods, teams must focus only on the highest-impact projects while putting others on hold; management must support the suspension of other projects; a target level must be defined for lowering the red flag.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If toil is not measured, it can grow until it prevents almost all project work; individuals might fail to raise red flags even when thresholds are exceeded; early assessment levels may see projects chosen based on interest rather than potential impact.

Concept_Name: toil
Core_Definition: Exhausting physical labor or repetitive manual tasks within an operational system that could be automated.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; individuals tracking their own work ratios; the designated team member responsible for tracking team toil.
Operational_Workflow: Toil is measured in hours per week and compared against project work; it is tracked on dashboards to identify problems and trends; it is systematically reduced through targeted optimization projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Toil should be kept within a desired range to minimize red flag periods; both a red flag level and a target level must be defined and communicated to the team.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial assessment levels, toil is not measured and may grow unchecked; individuals may be unaware of the problem or fail to recognize when toil is becoming a problem.

Concept_Name: project work
Core_Definition: Work tasks, such as coding or non-manual projects, that contribute to system improvement and innovation rather than repetitive maintenance.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; management; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Staff spend a percentage of their weekly hours on coding versus non-coding projects; this work is tracked on dashboards and compared to manual labor; project work may be put on hold during red flag periods to focus on reducing toil.
Rules_and_Constraints: Projects should be prioritized based on which will have the greatest impact on toil reduction; the ratio of project work to toil is used as a primary metric for team health and sustainability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Project work can be almost entirely displaced if toil is allowed to grow unchecked; during red flag periods, the team has management support to put standard projects on hold.

Concept_Name: manual labor
Core_Definition: Repetitive, non-automated tasks performed by operational staff that have the potential to be automated to increase efficiency.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; teams responsible for automating tasks; the person responsible for raising red flags.
Operational_Workflow: The percentage of time spent on manual labor is monitored against a defined red flag threshold; if the threshold is exceeded, a red flag is raised to initiate toil-reduction efforts; individual manual tasks are tracked to determine their specific impact on time.
Rules_and_Constraints: A red flag threshold must be established to determine when the amount of manual labor has become problematic; the process for raising a red flag must be documented and communicated.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In initial stages, manual labor is performed inconsistently by different people and may not be documented; if no detection process exists, manual labor can consume all available staff time without being recognized as a defect.

Concept_Name: red flag
Core_Definition: A signal raised when the percentage of manual labor or toil exceeds a specific threshold, indicating that the workload has become unsustainable.
Key_Actors: Operational staff; individuals; the person designated as responsible for tracking toil and raising the flag; management.
Operational_Workflow: The team measures the amount of time spent on toil versus project work; the percentage of manual labor is compared against a defined red flag threshold; if the threshold is exceeded, a flag is raised manually by individuals or automatically by a dashboard; during the red flag period, the team works only on high-impact toil-reduction projects while management puts other projects on hold; the flag is lowered once toil reaches a predefined target level.
Rules_and_Constraints: A red flag level and a target level must be defined for each team; management must support putting other projects on hold during a red flag period; only the highest-impact toil-reduction projects are prioritized while the flag is active.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At lower operational levels, red flags might not be raised even when necessary; individuals might prioritize interesting projects over those with the highest impact on toil reduction if a formal process is not followed.

Concept_Name: time-tracking data
Core_Definition: Metrics and records used to measure the hours spent on coding versus non-coding projects and the ratio of project work to manual labor.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; individual team members; automated dashboards.
Operational_Workflow: Individual team members track their time spent on manual labor versus project work; this data is collected and presented on dashboards to visualize trends; the data is monitored for early alerts indicating an increase in toil; feedback from this data is used to evaluate the impact of toil-reduction projects.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data must distinguish between coding and non-coding projects; it must track time spent on individual projects and specific manual tasks; the data should be used to support decisions regarding red flag thresholds.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Disaster Preparedness
Core_Definition: The set of practices, systems engineering, and training used to ensure an organization can handle outages effectively and learn from past failures.
Key_Actors: Operations organization; new team members; cross-functional teams; management.
Operational_Workflow: Systems are engineered to be resilient to local and geographic failures; playbooks are created for failure scenarios and large-scale disasters; new members receive specific disaster preparedness training; regular exercises such as fire drills and game day exercises are conducted on live services; after a disaster occurs, a postmortem is conducted and reviewed to improve future response and system resilience.
Rules_and_Constraints: The organization must adopt a mindset that accepts and learns from outages; postmortem reports must be produced and circulated after every disaster according to defined SLA dates; playbooks must be exercised regularly to remain effective.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level, disasters are handled through individual heroics rather than standard processes; if roles and handoffs are not clearly defined, multiple team members responding to a disaster may cause duplication of effort.

Concept_Name: failure modes
Core_Definition: The specific ways in which a system can fail, including component-level and geographic-level failures, which require documented response procedures.
Key_Actors: Responding teams; automated monitoring systems; automated testing systems.
Operational_Workflow: Potential failure modes are identified and documented in playbooks; mechanisms are established to exercise these modes through stress testing and failover testing; failures are either observed naturally or triggered artificially to verify response protocols; automated systems may artificially induce a failure mode if it has not occurred naturally within a certain timeframe to ensure the procedure is still functional.
Rules_and_Constraints: Playbooks must exist for all failure modes including large-scale disasters; failure modes should be exercised frequently, such as on a quarterly or monthly basis.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In less mature organizations, service resiliency and different failure scenarios may never be tested at all; failure modes may go unaddressed if playbooks are out of date or unused.

Concept_Name: disaster preparedness training
Core_Definition: A structured process of educating team members, particularly new hires, on how to handle outages and disasters effectively to ensure service resiliency.
Key_Actors: New team members; operations organization; the team.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins with new team members receiving on-the-job training. As maturity increases, specific disaster preparedness training is defined and implemented for all new members. At higher levels, a comprehensive program ensures all aspects are covered and the results are measured. Training eventually expands to cover more complex scenarios as teams become better at handling disasters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Training must be defined and implemented; the program must ensure all aspects of disaster response are covered; results of the training must be measured.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial maturity level, little or no training exists.

Concept_Name: roles and responsibilities
Core_Definition: The clearly defined duties and handoff procedures assigned to team members during regular operations and emergency response.
Key_Actors: Team members; responding team; management; capacity planners; oncall person.
Operational_Workflow: Roles transition from being ad hoc and based on individuals to being explicitly defined for specific situations like disaster response. Procedures include defined handoffs with checks to be performed and documented. The team scales by following established guidelines on how to make efficient use of additional members during a crisis.
Rules_and_Constraints: Roles and responsibilities must be defined to prevent duplication of effort; handoff procedures must be documented and include checks; during red flag toil periods, management must support the team in prioritizing toil-reduction projects over other work.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If roles are not clearly defined during a response, it leads to duplication of effort and inconsistent results; at initial levels, the process is defined by who does a task rather than what is being done.

Concept_Name: postmortem report
Core_Definition: A document produced after a disaster or major outage that analyzes the event and identifies action items to improve future operations.
Key_Actors: The responding team; stakeholders; cross-functional teams.
Operational_Workflow: A draft report is made available within a specific number of hours following an incident, with a final report completed within a set number of days. These reports are published for visibility and reviewed to ensure recommendations are triaged and action items are completed.
Rules_and_Constraints: Postmortems must be produced and circulated after every disaster; reports must be written for all major outages; a standard template should be used; deadlines for initial and final reports must be tracked against the service level agreement.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In earlier stages of operational maturity, postmortems might be written occasionally but not consistently.

Concept_Name: fire drills
Core_Definition: Regular exercises, often performed on live services, to test failure modes and the team's ability to respond to disasters.
Key_Actors: Operations teams; cross-functional teams; live services.
Operational_Workflow: The team conducts regular disaster preparedness exercises including fire drills on the live service. Over time, these evolve into cross-functional fire drills involving multiple teams and services. In highly optimized environments, automated systems may artificially cause failures to ensure every failure mode is exercised within a certain period if it has not occurred naturally.
Rules_and_Constraints: Fire drills should be performed on the live service; exercises must be held regularly; complex scenarios should involve multiple teams and services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At the initial level, service resiliency and different failure scenarios are never tested.

