Appendix B. The Origins and Future of Distributed Computing and Clouds
Section: Appendix B. The Origins and Future of Distributed Computing and Clouds
============================================================

Concept_Name: pizza box–sized computers
Core_Definition: Commodity hardware devices that serve as the standard building blocks for modern large datacenters and distributed computing environments.
Key_Actors: Hardware vendors; companies such as Google, Yahoo, and Facebook; system administrators; datacenter operators.
Operational_Workflow: These computers are organized into repeated rack structures within a datacenter to reduce complexity; they are often stripped of unnecessary components like video cards and audio cards to reduce costs; they function as individual units of compute and storage that are connected by a network to act like one warehouse-sized computer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hardware and processes must be highly organized to manage enormous datacenters; identical rack structures should be repeated to reduce administrative overhead; components should be standardized to enable automation of operational tasks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual machines are subject to the run run run dead problem where they fail completely; however, the system is designed so that a single machine failure does not interrupt the overall service.

Concept_Name: distributed computing
Core_Definition: A system where many small servers share a workload and employ software techniques to remain reliable even when individual components fail.
Key_Actors: Recovery-Oriented Computing researchers; software developers; load balancers; root and leaf machines; MapReduce and Google File System.
Operational_Workflow: The workload is shared across many machines with enough spare capacity that a percentage can be down without affecting service; datasets are divided into fractions and stored on different leaf machines; root machines receive requests and forward them to the appropriate leaf; software detects failed machines and re-delegates their work to functional ones.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reliability must be handled by the software rather than expensive hardware; systems must be designed to survive outages; scaling and high availability must be closely coupled; data replication is used to ensure resiliency and improve performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Distributed computing techniques are inappropriate for traditional applications like payroll systems that require high consistency and cannot tolerate partial results; simultaneous failures of all leaves holding a specific data fraction can cause service issues.

Concept_Name: cloud computing
Core_Definition: A model where large-scale computing infrastructure is built to exceed internal needs so that the surplus capacity can be rented to external tenants.
Key_Actors: Cloud providers such as Amazon; tenants or customers; virtual machines; software-defined networks.
Operational_Workflow: Providers use virtualization to partition large machines into many smaller ones for different tenants; customers use APIs and self-service control panels to dynamically spin up or release resources; the provider uses the profit from renters to subsidize the cost of their own infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: The infrastructure must be segmented so that one tenant cannot interfere with another; it requires programmable VLANs and software-defined networks; the system must offer elasticity to allow resources to expand and contract based on customer demand.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Cloud computing is sometimes adopted by departments to bypass uncooperative or underfunded internal IT departments; mobile applications create specific demands for lower latency and higher reliability within cloud services.

Concept_Name: economies of scale
Core_Definition: An economic state where additional users or units of capacity become cheaper to support as the overall system grows larger.
Key_Actors: Datacenter owners; hardware manufacturers; automated systems; consumers.
Operational_Workflow: Increasing the scale of operations allows the cost of automated software development to be spread across more machines; large-scale purchasing enables volume discounts for components; higher efficiency in power usage and cooling is achieved at scale to reduce operational costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must move from super-linear cost growth to linear or sub-linear cost growth to be sustainable; standardization is required to ensure that larger systems do not require proportionally more manual labor.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small-scale systems fail to achieve these economies and must rely on expensive hardware and specialized consultants; the first dot-com bubble was unsustainable because scaling became more expensive per user rather than cheaper.

Concept_Name: 24 × 7 basis
Core_Definition: A requirement for services and systems to be available constantly at all times and every day of the week.
Key_Actors: Telephone companies; banks; internal corporate systems; Internet-based businesses; global Internet users.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations providing essential services like ATMs and phone calls ensure systems are always accessible to the public. In the web era, businesses transitioned to this model to support global users across all time zones, moving away from scheduled maintenance windows.
Rules_and_Constraints: Achieving this standard requires highly reliable infrastructure, often utilizing distributed computing and high-availability hardware to ensure constant uptime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the pre-web era, Internet services were not considered business-critical and did not require 24 x 7 operation, allowing downtime to be scheduled for maintenance when the user base was known.

Concept_Name: modem
Core_Definition: A telephony-based hardware device used to facilitate remote access to computer systems over phone lines.
Key_Actors: Employees; remote users.
Operational_Workflow: A user connects a terminal or computer to a modem at home to establish a remote link to internal corporate systems and mainframes.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: dumb terminal
Core_Definition: A hardware device used by employees to remotely access and interact with centralized mainframes or minicomputers.
Key_Actors: Employees; corporate computer systems.
Operational_Workflow: The device is used from a home setting to connect to a business network via a modem, enabling the user to perform work on business-critical applications.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: PC
Core_Definition: A consumer-grade home computer used for personal applications or as a workstation to access corporate systems.
Key_Actors: Home users; employees; hardware manufacturers; Intel.
Operational_Workflow: Users employ these machines to run stand-alone software such as word processors or to access business services remotely. During the dot-bomb era, the components developed for PCs, such as x86 CPUs and RAM, were commoditized to build large, inexpensive server clusters for distributed computing.
Rules_and_Constraints: These machines were historically characterized by consumer-grade components that were cheaper, underpowered, and less reliable than server-class hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Early models were limited by slow processors and fragile 10MB hard drives, but by the cloud era, smartphones began running operating systems as sophisticated as those on PCs.

Concept_Name: Mac
Core_Definition: A type of computer used by employees during the pre-web era to facilitate remote access to internal business operations.
Key_Actors: Employees; businesses.
Operational_Workflow: Employees used the Mac from home to connect to corporate computer systems over a telephony-based modem.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: email
Core_Definition: A text-based Internet service used for communication during the pre-web era.
Key_Actors: Technologists; a small group of early Internet users.
Operational_Workflow: Users accessed the service as part of a largely text-based Internet environment; in the event of an Internet service outage, the system would generate a backlog of messages to be processed once connectivity was restored.
Rules_and_Constraints: During the pre-web era, the service was considered a perk rather than a business-critical tool; it was unclear in the early days if using the Internet for commercial purposes violated acceptable use policies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Outages of the service might cause a backlog of messages, but such interruptions went largely unnoticed by most people because the service was not yet essential for business operations.

Concept_Name: Internet news
Core_Definition: A text-based service available on the Internet during the pre-web era for the exchange of information.
Key_Actors: Technologists; a small group of early Internet users.
Operational_Workflow: The service functioned as one of the primary components of the early text-based Internet; during periods of service failure, news content would accumulate in a backlog.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: System outages would result in a backlog of news updates, though these failures were generally unnoticed by the public as the Internet was not yet business-critical.

Concept_Name: bulletin boards
Core_Definition: Text-based services that were part of the early Internet infrastructure alongside email and file transfer programs.
Key_Actors: Technologists; a small group of early Internet users.
Operational_Workflow: Users interacted with these boards as part of the predominantly text-based Internet environment existing between 1985 and 1994.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: file transfer programs
Core_Definition: A text-based Internet service used for moving data files between systems during the pre-web era.
Key_Actors: Technologists; early Internet users; text-based Internet systems.
Operational_Workflow: Users accessed the text-based Internet to reach various services; files were selected and transferred through the programs; if the service experienced an outage, it would result in a backlog of files that went largely unnoticed by most people.
Rules_and_Constraints: These programs were primarily used by a small group of technologists; the services were not considered business-critical at the time; the general population was mostly unaware of their existence.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Service outages were common but were not considered critical because the Internet was viewed as a perk rather than a business necessity.

Concept_Name: anonymous-ftp drop-boxes
Core_Definition: Internet-based storage locations used by companies to facilitate file sharing with external entities.
Key_Actors: Companies; third parties; customers; support teams.
Operational_Workflow: A company provides a specific Internet-accessible location; third parties or customers use the location to drop off or retrieve files; the system is primarily utilized for providing support to customers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Usage was generally limited to specific needs like customer support; commercial use of the Internet for these purposes was initially of questionable status regarding acceptable use policies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: taping
Core_Definition: A historical term for the process of performing data backups on mainframe systems.
Key_Actors: Mainframe systems; system operators; terminal users.
Operational_Workflow: The system displays a notification message to users upon login regarding the maintenance schedule; at a designated time such as 6 PM, the system is taken offline; the system remains unavailable for several hours while the taping process occurs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Taping required the system to be down every day; the downtime was considered a normal part of daily operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: backups
Core_Definition: The practice of copying system data to a secondary medium to ensure its preservation.
Key_Actors: Computer systems; system administrators; users.
Operational_Workflow: A recurring time is set for system maintenance; the computer system is shut down to prevent user access; the backup process is executed over several hours; once finished, the system is returned to an operational state.
Rules_and_Constraints: In early computing eras, doing backups was a primary reason for daily scheduled downtime; it was accepted as a normal and expected limitation of the technology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: mainframes
Core_Definition: Large, powerful computers used during the pre-web era to run business applications and services.
Key_Actors: Large businesses; schools; students; teachers; internal corporate departments.
Operational_Workflow: Users access the system through connected terminals; the system displays status messages to users upon login; applications such as payroll are processed; the system is taken offline daily for a period of hours to perform backups or taping.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems typically had megabytes of storage and executed less than 1 million instructions per second; outages were scheduled around product release schedules or end-of-quarter periods; the user population was known and easily informed of downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Systems were frequently unavailable for hours every day for maintenance tasks like backups, which was considered normal behavior for the era.

Concept_Name: minicomputers
Core_Definition: Mid-sized computers used in the pre-web era for business applications, often serving as an alternative or supplement to mainframes.
Key_Actors: Businesses; internal customers; specific geographic regions or business units.
Operational_Workflow: Machines are deployed to provide services for a company or specific division; servers are upgraded with additional disks, memory, and CPUs as capacity needs increase; services are sometimes scaled by deploying these machines into different geographic regions.
Rules_and_Constraints: Built with high-quality components and high-speed technologies; designed with expansion capabilities to accommodate growth; purchased with plenty of spare upgrade capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a machine runs out of capacity and cannot be further upgraded, it must be replaced by buying a larger machine.

Concept_Name: server-class machines
Core_Definition: High-end computers built with high-quality components, high-speed technologies, and expansion capabilities designed to handle business-critical workloads.
Key_Actors: System administrators; technology vendors like Sun Microsystems, IBM, and SGI; e-commerce businesses; Internet startups.
Operational_Workflow: Business-critical applications are hosted on a small number of large machines; administrators apply hardware upgrades to disks, memory, and CPUs to meet demand; in high-availability setups, machines are configured in N+1 or N+2 arrangements where one or two machines remain idle to act as spares.
Rules_and_Constraints: Typically use expensive custom hardware and specialized components like RAID subsystems and multiple high-end processors; software and operating system licenses are priced proportionately to the hardware cost; high availability often requires expensive fault-tolerant technologies such as error-correcting RAM.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Scaling becomes super-linearly expensive as machines grow larger; hardware failures require replacement parts through service contracts with four-hour turnarounds; downtime is required for software upgrades unless redundant machines are available for failover.

Concept_Name: consumer grade hardware
Core_Definition: Cheap, mass-produced hardware components originally intended for the home computer market rather than for professional server use.
Key_Actors: Home users; researchers; distributed computing companies; vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Components are manufactured at a massive scale to meet demand for home PCs and gaming; researchers and companies organize these components into pizza box-sized machines and stack them in racks; distributed computing software is used to manage many of these machines simultaneously to provide a single service.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hardware is often underpowered and considered unreliable compared to server-class machines; relies on commoditized components like Intel x86 CPUs, low-end hard drives, and RAM; systems are designed to be stripped down to exact necessary components by removing items like video cards, audio cards, and speakers to reduce costs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual machines are prone to failure but are managed using software techniques that allow the system to remain operational even when a percentage of machines are down; a single machine failure is treated as a signal for eventual repair rather than an immediate crisis.

Concept_Name: 8-bit processors
Core_Definition: Processors used in early home computers during the pre-web era from 1985 to 1994.
Key_Actors: Home computers; consumer-grade hardware components.
Operational_Workflow: These processors powered stand-alone applications including games, word processing, spreadsheets, and tax packages that were delivered on floppy disks.
Rules_and_Constraints: They were categorized as consumer grade hardware; they were characterized as cheap, underpowered, and unreliable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: 16-bit processors
Core_Definition: A generation of processors that followed 8-bit versions in home computers during the pre-web era.
Key_Actors: Home computers; consumer-grade hardware.
Operational_Workflow: These processors were used to run common home applications such as word processing and spreadsheets before the era of internet access.
Rules_and_Constraints: Like their 8-bit predecessors, they were part of hardware systems that were cheap, underpowered, and unreliable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: RAM
Core_Definition: A hardware component used for data storage and processing that evolved from kilobyte capacities to gigabyte scales.
Key_Actors: Home computers; fault-tolerant computers; commodity x86 servers; cloud infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: In the pre-web era, it was measured in kilobytes and megabytes for home use; high-availability systems utilized error-correcting versions to prevent failures; in the cloud era, technologies permit direct memory access between machines to pass information nearly as fast as reading local RAM.
Rules_and_Constraints: RAM in early home computers was consumer grade and unreliable; error-correcting RAM was extremely expensive and restricted to niche fault-tolerant systems used by the military or Wall Street; modern x86 servers often contain significantly more RAM than multiprocessor servers of previous eras to improve cost-efficiency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: disk storage
Core_Definition: Hardware used for storing data, encompassing floppy disks, hard drives, RAID subsystems, and eventually solid-state drives.
Key_Actors: Home computers; server-class machines; RAID subsystems; commodity servers; SSDs; MapReduce systems.
Operational_Workflow: Data was initially stored on slow floppy disks or fragile 10MB hard drives; as systems grew, capacity was added via additional disks or RAID subsystems for reliability; in the cloud era, SSDs replaced traditional disks to reduce latency by removing moving parts like read heads and rotating platters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Early 10MB hard drives were luxury items that were fragile and easily destroyed if dropped; distributed computing allowed the use of cheap consumer-grade disks by handling reliability through software; SSDs are more expensive per gigabyte but offer a lower total cost of ownership by reducing the number of machines needed to provide low-latency service.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hard disks that are starting to fail can cause a machine to take considerably longer to process data; MapReduce manages this by detecting slow performance, directing another machine to process the same data portion, and keeping the results from whichever machine finishes first.

Concept_Name: floppy disks
Core_Definition: A storage medium used during the pre-web era for the distribution and storage of standalone games, word processing, spreadsheets, and tax packages.
Key_Actors: Home computers; consumer-grade hardware.
Operational_Workflow: Applications were provided on these disks to be used on home computers, where they also served as the primary mechanism for storing user data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Storage capacity was limited to kilobytes and megabytes rather than gigabytes and terabytes; data retrieval and storage processes were characterized as slow.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: hard drive
Core_Definition: A data storage device that evolved from a fragile, 10MB luxury item in the pre-web era into a commoditized component used in large-scale distributed computing.
Key_Actors: Home computers; server-class machines; commodity hardware; SSDs.
Operational_Workflow: Hard drives store data such as music, videos, or fractions of a search corpus; they traditionally function using moving parts, including a read head that moves to a disk platter and a platter that rotates into position.
Rules_and_Constraints: In the pre-web era, hard drives were expensive and fragile; in later distributed computing eras, companies used cheap consumer-grade hard drives to achieve cost savings over high-end SCSI drives.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Dropping an early hard drive would destroy it; a hard disk that is starting to fail may be identified by slow performance in a machine processing its share of data during a MapReduce run.

Concept_Name: geographic regions
Core_Definition: Physical locations used as a method for scaling application services by providing users with access to local servers.
Key_Actors: Companies; application servers.
Operational_Workflow: A service was scaled by deploying application servers into multiple distinct areas, allowing each area to utilize its own local server infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: business units
Core_Definition: Organizational divisions within a company used to scale services by dedicating local server resources to specific departments.
Key_Actors: Corporate divisions; payroll processing centers.
Operational_Workflow: Companies scaled business-critical applications by deploying servers to specific internal divisions; for example, AT&T utilized separate payroll processing centers for each division of the company.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: payroll processing center
Core_Definition: A localized facility or server unit used to scale and manage payroll services for specific divisions of a large organization.
Key_Actors: AT&T; company divisions; geographic regions; business units.
Operational_Workflow: A service is scaled by deploying servers for the application into several geographic regions or business units; each division or unit then uses its specific local server to process its specific workload.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scaling was handled by deploying application servers into local geographic regions or business units; compute requirements for these centers scaled in predictable ways bounded by the rate at which new employees were hired.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: fault-tolerant computers
Core_Definition: Specialized machines designed for applications requiring high availability through the use of redundant or advanced hardware components.
Key_Actors: The military; Wall Street; niche technology vendors.
Operational_Workflow: These systems provide high availability for business-critical applications by integrating multiple CPUs, error-correcting RAM, and other specialized technologies into a single unit.
Rules_and_Constraints: These were niche products usually priced out of the reach of typical companies; advanced reliability and scaling needs were addressed with very expensive specialized hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Even the most reliable system fails occasionally; traditional options for failures involved service contracts with four-hour turnarounds for replacement parts or purchasing spare parts to be stored near the machine.

Concept_Name: multiple CPUs
Core_Definition: A hardware configuration involving more than one central processing unit within a single computer to increase capacity or reliability.
Key_Actors: System administrators; high-end hardware vendors; companies requiring high-availability internet presence.
Operational_Workflow: If a computer system ran out of capacity, it could be upgraded with additional CPUs; companies looking to provide a reliable internet presence purchased server-grade machines with multiple high-end processors.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technology was extremely expensive at the time and was a standard feature of fault-tolerant computers; machines used as servers were typically purchased with plenty of spare upgrade capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Growing a web site through bigger and more expensive hardware like multiple CPUs was not economically viable because the cost per unit of capacity grew super-linearly as the system scaled.

Concept_Name: error-correcting RAM
Core_Definition: A high-quality memory technology used in fault-tolerant computers to help ensure high availability for critical applications.
Key_Actors: Military organizations; Wall Street firms; niche computer manufacturers.
Operational_Workflow: This technology is integrated into specialized server-class machines to protect against memory-related failures and support constant system operation.
Rules_and_Constraints: Error-correcting RAM was extremely expensive during the pre-web era; it was a niche product generally priced out of the reach of typical businesses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: risk analysis
Core_Definition: A process used by businesses to calculate the financial impact of system outages and determine the appropriate budget for implementing higher reliability.
Key_Actors: Businesses; decision makers.
Operational_Workflow: A business identifies potential system outages; it calculates the specific costs associated with those outages; it performs a risk analysis to understand the necessary budget for reliability; it makes an informed decision on how to proceed with technology investments.
Rules_and_Constraints: During the pre-web era, these costs were considered under control because the customer base for internal systems was a limited and known set of people.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: web sites
Core_Definition: Repositories of linked documents that initially served as marketing literature and repositories for company information before becoming platforms for business operations.
Key_Actors: Companies; marketing teams; sales and customer support groups; system administrators; external Internet users.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations establish an Internet presence by hosting documents such as press releases and job listings; system administrators manage these sites using server-grade hardware; as a site grows in popularity, administrators upgrade to larger machines or implement load balancers to manage increased traffic.
Rules_and_Constraints: Early web sites were initially treated like internal systems where outages could be scheduled for maintenance; scaling for web sites is not bound by internal employee growth but by the global population of Internet users; traditionally, sites used a model of one machine per web site.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a site becomes popular across all time zones, the window for scheduled nighttime maintenance disappears; sudden and unpredictable fluctuations in service usage can overwhelm scaling strategies.

Concept_Name: e-commerce
Core_Definition: The practice of conducting commercial business and sales transactions entirely over the Internet.
Key_Actors: Startup companies; Internet users; web sales channels.
Operational_Workflow: New companies build business models entirely around a web presence; they establish highly reliable Internet services to facilitate 24 by 7 operations; they utilize expensive hardware and load balancers to ensure the sales channel remains functional for any potential customer.
Rules_and_Constraints: For e-commerce companies, the Internet presence is the entire business; they require 24 by 7 operations with no maintenance windows; they must prepare for success by purchasing the largest and most reliable systems available.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the web sales channel fails, the entire business comes to a standstill; the historical model for scaling e-commerce often led to unsustainable economies where the cost per user increased as the site grew larger.

Concept_Name: static page
Core_Definition: A simple, non-interactive web page used to provide information to users when a primary web service is unavailable.
Key_Actors: System administrators; secondary or backup machines.
Operational_Workflow: During a scheduled maintenance outage, a system administrator configures a secondary machine to respond to incoming traffic; this machine serves a static page to users; the page informs visitors that the site is down for maintenance and suggests they try again at a later time.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While regular users may see the page as a planned event, new or occasional customers may be caught unaware by the service interruption.

Concept_Name: scheduled outage
Core_Definition: A planned period of downtime where a computer system is intentionally taken offline to perform maintenance or upgrades.
Key_Actors: System administrators; internal corporate customers; external Internet users; business units.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators determine the timing for the outage by reviewing product release schedules and avoiding high-activity periods like the end-of-quarter; the known user population is notified through defined contact methods; for web services, administrators may configure a separate machine to display a static page informing visitors that the site is down for maintenance and should be accessed later.
Rules_and_Constraints: Maintenance should be scheduled for times when the user population is known and can be easily informed; web sites should pre-announce downtime to allow regular users to plan around the service interruption; internal systems often avoid end-of-quarter periods to minimize business disruption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: New or occasional web customers may be caught unaware by the outage because there is no way to notify unknown users in advance; hardware failures are unpredictable and cannot be restricted to scheduled windows; advertising an upgrade in advance can lead to negative headlines if the technical process goes poorly.

Concept_Name: maintenance window
Core_Definition: A designated time period during which system maintenance is performed, potentially resulting in service unavailability.
Key_Actors: Startup companies; internal employees; global Internet customers.
Operational_Workflow: In the pre-web era, administrators scheduled windows based on the availability and predictable access patterns of a known user base; in the web era, many companies moved toward 24 by 7 operations and sought to eliminate maintenance windows entirely because their customer base was unknown and global.
Rules_and_Constraints: Maintenance windows are typically used when the user population is defined and can be notified; companies whose business models revolve entirely around the web often do not have the luxury of maintenance windows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Services that gain international popularity may lose the ability to schedule maintenance windows because the site remains busy across every time zone; hardware failures can happen at any time and may fall outside of a planned window.

Concept_Name: xDSL
Core_Definition: A technology that emerged during the first web era to provide faster Internet connections to home computer users.
Key_Actors: Home computer users; telecommunications and Internet service providers.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: VoIP
Core_Definition: Voice over IP technology that emerged for conducting voice communications over the Internet.
Key_Actors: Developers of new products and services; users of networked voice applications.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: RAID subsystems
Core_Definition: A high-availability storage technology used to provide a more reliable Internet presence than standard disks.
Key_Actors: Internet companies; technology vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Companies providing Internet services replaced plain disks with these subsystems in their server-grade hardware to ensure higher reliability for their web presence.
Rules_and_Constraints: These were extremely expensive during the First Web Era; they were categorized as specialized high-availability options.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: high-end processors
Core_Definition: Advanced or custom CPUs, often used in multiples, designed to provide high performance and reliability for server-class computers.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; hardware vendors; Internet startup companies.
Operational_Workflow: Companies purchased machines with multiple processors to enhance reliability; as web sites grew in popularity, custom CPUs and new internal architectures were developed and deployed to meet increasing demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: These components were extremely expensive; they used custom hardware and served a small market; their use resulted in super-linear cost growth where the cost per unit of capacity increased as the system grew larger.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Sun Microsystems’ E4500 server
Core_Definition: A server model that served as the standard and normative hardware during the dot-com era.
Key_Actors: Sun Microsystems; dot-com companies.
Operational_Workflow: This machine was purchased and deployed by a new class of customers who required server hardware that was simultaneously large in capacity and high in reliability.
Rules_and_Constraints: This server was extremely expensive and was part of a class of hardware that utilized custom components and targeted specialized markets.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load balancers
Core_Definition: A device that sits in front of a group of machines providing the same service to monitor availability and distribute traffic.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; primary and secondary server devices; server groups.
Operational_Workflow: The device continually tests each machine to see if the service is available; in a primary/secondary configuration, it sends traffic to the primary until it fails and then automatically switches to the secondary; in load-sharing mode, it stops directing queries to any machine that stops responding correctly until it resumes normal operation.
Rules_and_Constraints: They provide automatic failover for high-availability services; they can be configured for primary/secondary setups or load-sharing; they were considered expensive tools used to minimize downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In an N plus 1 load-sharing configuration, if one machine fails while the other is at high utilization, the remaining machine can become over-utilized and cause the service to become unacceptably slow.

Concept_Name: primary/secondary setup
Core_Definition: A configuration for load balancers where all traffic is directed to a single primary machine while a secondary machine remains idle until the primary one fails.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; primary devices; secondary devices; server machines.
Operational_Workflow: A load balancer is placed in front of a group of machines; the load balancer is configured to recognize one machine as the primary device; all incoming traffic is sent to this primary device during normal operation; the load balancer continuously monitors the status of the primary machine; if the primary machine fails, the load balancer automatically switches the traffic flow to the secondary device.
Rules_and_Constraints: This setup is designed to provide automatic failover for high-availability services; it typically results in a significant portion of hardware investment sitting idle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: load-share
Core_Definition: A configuration where a load balancer distributes incoming service requests across multiple machines that are all active and providing the same service.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; multiple server machines; systems administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A group of machines is set up to provide an identical service; a load balancer is positioned in front of these machines; the load balancer continually tests each machine to see if the service is available and responding correctly; incoming queries are distributed among the machines; if a machine stops responding correctly, the load balancer stops directing queries to it until it resumes normal operation.
Rules_and_Constraints: To allow for software upgrades without impacting service, the combined utilization of the active machines must remain low enough that the remaining machines can handle the extra load if one is taken out of service; for example, in a two-machine setup, neither machine should exceed 50 percent utilization to avoid making the site unacceptably slow if one fails or is removed for maintenance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a site is highly utilized, such as at 80 percent across multiple machines, taking one down for an upgrade can result in the remaining machines being over-utilized at 160 percent, effectively making the service unusable.

Concept_Name: automatic failover
Core_Definition: A functionality provided by load balancers to automatically redirect traffic from a failed machine to a working one to maintain high availability.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; server-class hardware; primary and secondary machines.
Operational_Workflow: The load balancer is integrated into the service architecture to act as a gateway; it performs ongoing health checks on the machines in its pool; the load balancer identifies a failure when a machine stops responding; the system immediately stops sending traffic to the faulty machine; traffic is redirected to a secondary machine or shared among the remaining healthy servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: Automatic failover is used to meet 24 by 7 availability requirements and to handle hardware failures without requiring immediate manual intervention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In configurations without a load balancer, failover typically requires manual intervention by a systems administrator, which generally results in a service outage that can last up to an hour.

Concept_Name: OS license
Core_Definition: A legal and financial requirement to pay for the right to run a specific operating system on a server machine.
Key_Actors: Operating system vendors such as Microsoft or Sun Microsystems; corporations; open source providers like RedHat; server hardware.
Operational_Workflow: A company determines its server requirements and purchases hardware; an OS license is acquired for each machine, with the price typically being proportionate to the hardware capacity; additional licenses for web servers or databases may also be purchased; during the dot-bomb era, many companies began using free open source operating systems like Linux to reduce these costs; corporations eventually accepted commercial versions of Linux because they could purchase support for the software.
Rules_and_Constraints: In the first web era, OS licenses were a significant part of the cost of scaling because they were priced according to the size and power of the server; traditional corporations typically required an OS to be supported by a vendor before it was used for production-level services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The adoption of open source software such as the LAMP stack allowed companies to avoid high traditional OS license fees, even though they might still pay for commercial support.

Concept_Name: web server license
Core_Definition: A type of software license required to operate a web server that was typically priced in proportion to the cost of the hardware it ran on.
Key_Actors: Software vendors; system administrators; dot-com startups and businesses.
Operational_Workflow: A business would dedicate one machine for each web site; they would purchase a hardware server; they would then acquire the necessary software stack which included buying a web server license to legally run the software; the vendor would set the license price based on the scale and cost of the physical hardware being used.
Rules_and_Constraints: Licenses were a significant part of software costs during the first web era; pricing was proportional to hardware costs; the model led to super-linear cost growth where the more a web site grew, the more expensive it became per unit of capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The emergence of open source software like Apache provided a more stable and feature-rich alternative that eventually reduced the reliance on expensive commercial licenses.

Concept_Name: database server license
Core_Definition: A software license required to run database services, which was historically expensive and priced based on the hardware capacity.
Key_Actors: Software vendors such as Oracle or Microsoft; system administrators; corporate customers.
Operational_Workflow: Businesses setting up web-based services would identify their data storage needs; they would purchase server-grade hardware; they would then purchase a database server license from a vendor; the cost was often calculated based on the number of processors or the quality of the hardware in the server.
Rules_and_Constraints: High costs for these licenses were considered acceptable during the dot-com bubble because startups with large losses often received higher stock market valuations; the pricing contributed to a model where providing service became more expensive as more users were added.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The arrival of free open source SQL databases like MySQL allowed companies to avoid the high price tags of traditional database licenses like Oracle.

Concept_Name: custom CPUs
Core_Definition: High-performance processors designed for server-class machines to provide reliability and speed beyond what consumer-grade components could offer.
Key_Actors: Hardware vendors such as Sun Microsystems; researchers like those in the Recovery-Oriented Computing project; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: Vendors designed these CPUs using high-quality components and high-speed technologies; businesses purchased servers containing these chips to handle the scaling needs of their web sites; the CPUs provided built-in reliability at the hardware level to minimize system failures.
Rules_and_Constraints: These chips were expensive because they used custom hardware and served a small, niche market; they were a primary factor in the super-linear cost of scaling during the first web era; they were intended for server-class machines rather than consumer-grade PCs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Research projects such as ROC demonstrated that systems built from many underpowered commodity CPUs could be more reliable and cheaper than those using custom-designed chips when distributed computing techniques were applied.

Concept_Name: internal architectures
Core_Definition: Specialized internal hardware designs and configurations developed for high-end servers to improve performance for web and e-commerce sites.
Key_Actors: Hardware manufacturers; server design teams; corporate IT departments.
Operational_Workflow: Manufacturers created new internal designs to optimize data processing and reliability; businesses purchased these specialized machines, such as the Sun Microsystems E4500, to power their Internet presence; these architectures allowed machines to be upgraded with additional disks and memory to meet growing demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: These architectures were extremely expensive to develop and purchase; they were used in larger systems that served a smaller market, which drove prices up; they resulted in an environment where scaling became more expensive per user rather than creating economies of scale.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The chapter notes that the appearance of these machines was not truly inevitable and they were eventually replaced by massive datacenters full of machines built from commodity hardware.

Concept_Name: N + 1 configuration
Core_Definition: A high-availability best practice involving the use of two machines where one runs the service and the other is a configured spare ready to take over if the first machine fails.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; primary machines; spare machines.
Operational_Workflow: One machine is configured to run the service while a second machine is kept idle but ready for use; if the primary machine fails, the systems administrator performs a failover to the spare machine; software upgrades can be executed by upgrading the spare server and switching the service to it.
Rules_and_Constraints: This configuration requires one more device than is necessary to provide the service; it is characterized by 50 percent of the investment sitting idle at any given time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Failover usually requires manual intervention if the site lacks a load balancer; a systems administrator can typically perform this switch in less than one hour.

Concept_Name: manual intervention
Core_Definition: The human effort and actions required from a systems administrator to restore a service after a hardware failure by switching to a spare machine.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A hardware failure occurs on a machine providing a service; the systems administrator identifies the failure; the administrator manually switches the service to a secondary, configured machine to restore functionality.
Rules_and_Constraints: Manual intervention is the standard requirement for failover in an N + 1 configuration when no load balancer is present.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A good systems administrator may be able to perform the manual switch fast enough that the resulting downtime for a website is less than one hour.

Concept_Name: N + 2 configuration
Core_Definition: A high-availability setup involving three machines where one provides the service and two others are maintained as spares.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; primary machines; spare machines.
Operational_Workflow: One machine handles the active service while two others remain idle; systems administrators perform software upgrades on one spare machine while the other spare remains in a usable state to handle a potential failure of the primary machine; once the upgrade is ready, the service is failed over to the newly upgraded server.
Rules_and_Constraints: This configuration became a best practice to ensure that a usable spare was always available even as software release frequency increased; it results in 66 percent of the total hardware investment being idle at any given time.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: idle capacity
Core_Definition: The portion of hardware and computing resources that are purchased and powered but not currently serving active traffic, typically maintained for redundancy or future growth.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; companies; commodity servers; cloud providers; tenants.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations maintain surplus hardware to ensure service continuity during failures; in early models this involved keeping entire spare servers idle; distributed computing reduced the percentage of idle capacity by spreading redundancy across many commodity machines; cloud providers leverage their surplus idle capacity by renting it to other tenants to offset infrastructure costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Traditional high-availability configurations are expensive because they result in 50 to 66 percent of capacity sitting idle; distributed computing can reduce this waste to as little as 5 percent; the economics of cloud computing allow for a negative infrastructure cost if enough idle capacity is rented to others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If load sharing is used to reduce idle capacity but servers are more than 50 percent utilized, a single machine failure can make the remaining system unacceptably slow for end users.

Concept_Name: utilization
Core_Definition: The amount of a computer system's processing capacity being used to provide a service, often expressed as a percentage of its total capacity.
Key_Actors: System administrators; end users; computer hardware systems.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators monitor the capacity used by services to ensure machines do not become overloaded; in redundant configurations such as N + 1, administrators attempt to keep utilization below 50 percent so a single machine can handle the entire load if the other fails; in more modern distributed computing environments, workload is shared across many machines to reduce the amount of idle capacity and improve the ratio of active use to wasted resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: In an N + 1 configuration, utilization must be kept low enough that the remaining machines can absorb the traffic of a failed unit without becoming unacceptably slow; utilization of resources should be optimized to reduce the costs associated with idle equipment.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If two machines in an N + 1 configuration are both at 80 percent utilization and one is taken out of service, the remaining machine becomes 160 percent utilized and the service becomes unacceptably slow; companies may attempt to perform maintenance when utilization dips late at night, but this is not viable for sites that are popular across all international time zones.

Concept_Name: service contract
Core_Definition: A formal agreement with a hardware vendor to provide assistance or replacement parts for critical systems within a specific timeframe.
Key_Actors: Hardware vendors; companies operating critical computer systems.
Operational_Workflow: A company enters into an agreement with a hardware vendor as a strategy to maintain high availability; when a hardware failure occurs, the company contacts the vendor to initiate a repair or replacement; the vendor provides the necessary components within the timeframe specified in the agreement.
Rules_and_Constraints: The contract typically specifies a specific turnaround time for replacement parts, such as four hours.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Even with a service contract in place, some system downtime is still required to perform the actual replacement of the failed hardware.

Concept_Name: spare parts
Core_Definition: Replacement hardware components that are purchased and kept in reserve to be used in the event of a system failure.
Key_Actors: System administrators; hardware vendors.
Operational_Workflow: A company identifies critical hardware components that may fail; the company purchases these components in advance of any actual need; the parts are stored in a location near the machines they are intended to support; when a failure occurs, a system administrator uses the stored parts to repair the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Spare parts should be stored near the machine to minimize the time needed for repairs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Similar to service contracts, the use of spare parts still results in a period of downtime while the hardware is being replaced.

Concept_Name: software upgrades
Core_Definition: The process of updating or improving the software applications or operating systems running on a computer system.
Key_Actors: System administrators; internal corporate customers; external internet users; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: In early eras, administrators scheduled maintenance outages by looking at product release schedules and avoiding busy periods like the end-of-quarter; for web services, administrators might pre-announce downtime to the user base or use redundant configurations to upgrade a spare machine and then switch traffic to it; in centralized distributed systems like MapReduce, improvements are made to the central system and then automatically benefit all users.
Rules_and_Constraints: Upgrades should be scheduled for periods of low utilization if possible; in N + 1 or N + 2 setups, the spare machine must be in a usable state before traffic is switched to it; administrators of early web sites often pre-announced upgrades to allow regular users to plan around the downtime.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the primary machine fails while a spare is in the middle of being upgraded, the spare may be in an unusable state; advertising upgrades in advance can lead to adverse headlines and negative publicity if the upgrade goes poorly.

Concept_Name: surplus capacity
Core_Definition: Excess computing resources, such as datacenter space or bandwidth, that remain unused after periods of high investment or are intentionally overbuilt to be rented to others.
Key_Actors: Telecommunications providers; colocation facility providers; cloud computing suppliers; tenants or customers.
Operational_Workflow: Companies build large-scale infrastructure to meet predicted needs; during economic downturns, providers with few customers offer these idle resources at slashed prices to remain viable; in the cloud era, companies intentionally build capacity beyond their own needs to rent to others; the resulting rental income offsets the provider's costs and can eventually subsidize the entire infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Providers must develop technology that allows for the secure renting of spare capacity to others; surplus availability is often tied to the ebb and flow of economic prosperity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the profit from renting surplus capacity is large enough, a company may achieve a negative cost for its own computing infrastructure; surpluses are eventually exhausted as they are consumed by new applications.

Concept_Name: commoditization of hardware
Core_Definition: The transition of computer components like CPUs, RAM, and hard drives into mass-produced consumer goods, resulting in significantly lower prices.
Key_Actors: Hardware vendors; home computer users; gamers; researchers; system designers.
Operational_Workflow: High demand for home computers and gaming drives the manufacturing of components at an unprecedented scale; mass production leads to lower unit prices for items like Intel x86 CPUs and consumer-grade disks; researchers and companies use these cheap components to build large clusters of pizza box-sized computers; software techniques are then applied to manage these components as a single reliable system.
Rules_and_Constraints: Commodity hardware requires the use of distributed computing techniques to ensure high availability; machines can be stripped of non-essential components like video cards or speakers to further reduce costs; volume discounts are typically applied when purchasing many units.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual commodity components are less reliable than expensive custom hardware, leading to more frequent hardware failures that the software must be designed to handle.

Concept_Name: open source software
Core_Definition: Community-developed software projects that provide reliable, production-ready alternatives to expensive commercial operating systems and applications.
Key_Actors: Linux developers; Apache project; MySQL project; Perl community; RedHat; corporations.
Operational_Workflow: Projects like Linux and Apache mature over time until they are stable enough for production environments; companies like RedHat offer supported versions of these tools to satisfy corporate requirements for maintenance; businesses adopt the LAMP stack (Linux, Apache, MySQL, and Perl) to replace high-cost proprietary licenses; this enables the use of cheaper commodity hardware running free or low-cost operating systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be mature enough to be considered for production use rather than just as a toy; commercial support is often a prerequisite for corporate adoption; the software must scale alongside distributed computing hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Early versions of open source tools were initially seen as unsuitable for professional use compared to Solaris or Oracle; some traditional applications like payroll may still require specialized features not found in all distributed open source databases.

Concept_Name: fiber
Core_Definition: Physical communication cables laid in the ground and across oceans to provide the bandwidth necessary for global internet traffic.
Key_Actors: Telecommunications providers; internet users.
Operational_Workflow: Millions of miles of fiber are laid to meet the anticipated bandwidth needs of the world during economic booms; when customer demand fails to meet predictions, providers are left with excess capacity; these providers then offer bandwidth deals at low prices to attract users and generate revenue.
Rules_and_Constraints: Infrastructure must be laid in massive quantities to support the predicted bandwidth needs of the global population.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: datacenter “colocation facilities”
Core_Definition: A colocation facility is a highly reliable datacenter facility that rents physical space to other companies to house their computer systems.
Key_Actors: colocation providers; tenant companies; bankrupt providers during the dot-bomb era.
Operational_Workflow: Providers build large-scale datacenter facilities; companies rent space within these facilities to host their hardware; during the dot-bomb era many providers went bankrupt leaving behind surplus capacity that could be rented very inexpensively.
Rules_and_Constraints: These facilities are required to be highly reliable; they were built as some of the world’s largest computing facilities to meet predicted bandwidth needs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Many colocation providers went bankrupt after building large facilities following the bubble burst, which created a temporary surplus of inexpensive space.

Concept_Name: Intel x86 CPUs
Core_Definition: A mass-produced hardware component originally used in home computers that became the standard processor for commodity server clusters in the distributed computing era.
Key_Actors: Intel; home computer users; Linux developers; hardware vendors such as SGI, IBM, and Sun.
Operational_Workflow: CPUs are manufactured at a massive scale for the consumer market which leads to lower prices; the rise of Linux brings server-class operating system capabilities to the platform; companies use these chips in racks of many small computers to provide aggregate processing capacity.
Rules_and_Constraints: High-volume manufacturing is necessary to achieve commoditization and lower prices; components may be stripped down to only essential parts to reduce costs further when buying thousands of units.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Early versions of x86 CPUs could not run server-class UNIX operating systems and instead ran systems like Windows 95; certain generations might be skipped if they use a disproportionate amount of power compared to their speed.

Concept_Name: low-end hard drives
Core_Definition: Cheap consumer-grade hard disks manufactured at high volumes for the home market and used as storage in distributed computing clusters.
Key_Actors: consumer market; research teams such as Recovery-Oriented Computing (ROC) at UC-Berkeley; software developers; Google File System (GFS).
Operational_Workflow: Multiple inexpensive drives are deployed across many small servers; software techniques such as replication and GFS ensure data remains available even when individual drives fail; the system as a whole performs as well or better than individual large servers.
Rules_and_Constraints: These drives are less reliable than high-end server-grade disks; reliability must be addressed through software techniques rather than hardware quality.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Early 10MB versions of hard drives were extremely fragile luxury items that would be destroyed if dropped; a drive that is starting to fail may slow down data processing in parallel systems like MapReduce.

Concept_Name: Linux
Core_Definition: An open-source, UNIX-like operating system that enabled server-class computing on commodity Intel x86 hardware.
Key_Actors: Linux open-source community; commercial vendors like RedHat; corporate IT departments; the LAMP quartet consisting of Linux, Apache, MySQL, and Perl.
Operational_Workflow: Linux is ported to the x86 platform to provide a server-grade operating system on inexpensive hardware; it matures to become stable and feature-rich for production use; commercial vendors offer supported versions to make it acceptable for corporate use; it becomes the foundation for the LAMP stack.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporations typically require commercial support to be available before adopting open-source software; Linux is used to achieve cost savings by replacing expensive proprietary hardware and software licenses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During the first web era Linux was considered a toy and was not mature enough for production use compared to systems like Solaris, Netscape, or Oracle.

Concept_Name: Apache
Core_Definition: An open source web server project that reached maturity during the dot-bomb era as a key component of the LAMP software stack.
Key_Actors: Open source developers; system administrators; corporations transitioning from commercial web servers.
Operational_Workflow: Apache functions as a production-grade web server used to deliver web services on commodity x86 hardware; it is utilized alongside Linux, MySQL, and Perl to create a reliable and cost-effective hosting environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During the first web era, Apache was often dismissed as a toy compared to professional platforms such as the Solaris operating system or Netscape web server before it proved to be faster and more stable than commercial alternatives.

Concept_Name: MySQL
Core_Definition: A free and open source SQL database that serves as the data management component of the LAMP software quartet.
Key_Actors: Database administrators; open source developers; companies seeking alternatives to high-priced databases like Oracle.
Operational_Workflow: MySQL is installed and managed as a production database for web applications; it is noted for being easier to install and manage than traditional enterprise database systems.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Early in its development, MySQL was considered a toy compared to high-end commercial products like Oracle or Microsoft SQL Server.

Concept_Name: Perl
Core_Definition: An open source programming language that matured to include object-oriented features and is a constituent part of the LAMP stack.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; the open source community.
Operational_Workflow: Perl is used within a suite of open source tools to develop web services and handle system administration tasks in a distributed computing environment.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Perl originally occupied a narrow niche as a system administration language before gaining broader acceptance as it matured for production use.

Concept_Name: UNIX-like server operating system
Core_Definition: A category of server-class operating systems, such as Linux and BSD, that were developed to run on commodity Intel x86 hardware.
Key_Actors: Operating system vendors such as RedHat; hardware companies like IBM, Sun, and SGI; corporate IT departments.
Operational_Workflow: These systems provide a stable environment for server-class applications on x86 platforms; corporations typically adopt these systems by purchasing supported versions from vendors like RedHat to ensure administrative reliability.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporations typically require the availability of commercial support before they are willing to accept open source operating systems for production server use.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Prior to the maturation of Linux, Intel x86 computers primarily ran Windows 95, which was not a server operating system; additionally, Windows NT was designed as a server operating system but failed to become a successful web service platform.

Concept_Name: Solaris OS
Core_Definition: A commercial Unix-based operating system used on high-end server hardware during the first web era.
Key_Actors: Sun Microsystems; corporations; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: The OS provided a stable environment for running web and database server licenses on high-end hardware like the Sun E4500.
Rules_and_Constraints: Usage required an OS license that was typically priced proportionately to the hardware; it was not available for the Intel x86 platform during the early eras.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During the dot-bomb era, it began to be viewed as less desirable compared to Linux due to high licensing costs and its inability to run on cheaper commodity x86 hardware.

Concept_Name: Netscape web server
Core_Definition: A commercial web server software platform commonly used by businesses to provide Internet services during the first web era.
Key_Actors: Corporations; system administrators; Netscape (vendor).
Operational_Workflow: The software functioned as the middle tier of a technology stack, requiring a specific license to serve web content to external users.
Rules_and_Constraints: It required a software license that was priced proportionately to the hardware it was installed on.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: It was eventually considered less feature-rich and more difficult to configure than the open-source Apache web server, which proved to be faster and more stable.

Concept_Name: Oracle database
Core_Definition: A high-end commercial SQL database system used for storing and managing data for web-based services.
Key_Actors: Corporations; database administrators; Oracle (vendor).
Operational_Workflow: The database served as the backend of the software stack, handling data requests from web servers to support e-commerce and other applications.
Rules_and_Constraints: It was associated with very high price tags and high support costs; it required a database server license.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The emergence of free SQL databases like MySQL led to a mass migration away from Oracle because MySQL was easier to manage and lacked the expensive license fees.

Concept_Name: Java “stack”
Core_Definition: A commercial application development and execution environment used to build and run complex web services.
Key_Actors: Software developers; corporations; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: It provided the programming and runtime infrastructure for building applications that sat on top of the OS and connected to databases.
Rules_and_Constraints: It was viewed as a production-grade, high-end environment compared to early open-source tools.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the dot-bomb era, the Java stack was often passed over for simpler and cheaper alternatives like Perl, which gained acceptance for production use as part of the LAMP quartet.

Concept_Name: Microsoft NT Server
Core_Definition: A version of the Windows operating system specifically designed to function as a server platform.
Key_Actors: Microsoft; organizations and administrators attempting to deploy web services.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system was specifically designed for use as a server operating system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This operating system did not achieve success as a platform for web services; it was part of a commercial stack once viewed as professional and mature in contrast to early open source software that some technologists considered toys.

Concept_Name: IIS (web server)
Core_Definition: A web server software application that functions as part of the Microsoft technology stack.
Key_Actors: Microsoft.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: It was categorized as a mature commercial platform during an era when early open source alternatives were often regarded as toys.

Concept_Name: SQL Server database
Core_Definition: A database management system provided by Microsoft as a component of its server software suite.
Key_Actors: Microsoft.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: It was part of a group of commercial products that were once seen as superior to open source projects that had not yet reached production maturity.

Concept_Name: .NET environment
Core_Definition: A software framework or environment that serves as a layer within the Microsoft commercial technology platform.
Key_Actors: Microsoft.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: It was considered an established commercial environment in comparison to early open source tools that some technologists viewed as toys.

Concept_Name: LAMP
Core_Definition: An acronym referring to a common quartet of open source software consisting of Linux, Apache, MySQL, and Perl used to build web services.
Key_Actors: Linux operating system; Apache web server; MySQL database; Perl programming language; corporations and support providers such as RedHat.
Operational_Workflow: This software combination provides a production-ready stack where Linux serves as the UNIX-like operating system on Intel x86 hardware; Apache manages web server functions; MySQL handles database requirements; and Perl is used for system administration and application development.
Rules_and_Constraints: Corporations typically required commercial support for these open source tools before adopting them for production use; the stack was required to prove its maturity and stability to compete with expensive commercial alternatives like Solaris or Oracle.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A LAMP system was initially less reliable and slower than high-end hardware; it required many individual servers to equal the aggregate processing capacity of the larger machines it was designed to replace.

Concept_Name: Recovery-Oriented Computing (ROC)
Core_Definition: A research philosophy suggesting that a system composed of many small, low-cost servers can be more reliable and cost-effective than individual high-end computers.
Key_Actors: Researchers at the University of California-Berkeley; Patterson et al.
Operational_Workflow: This approach moves reliability from the hardware level to the software level; it employs distributed computing techniques to share workloads across many machines with enough spare capacity to ensure that service remains unaffected even if a percentage of machines fail.
Rules_and_Constraints: Instead of paying for reliability at every hardware level such as RAID or custom CPUs, the system uses cheaper commodity components and software that is already designed to survive outages; the collection of machines is intended to degrade slowly like an analog system rather than failing completely.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach was inappropriate for traditional applications like payroll systems that require high consistency and cannot handle the return of partial results during machine failures.

Concept_Name: distributed computing techniques
Core_Definition: Software-based methods that allow many individual machines to share a workload to provide high availability; scaling; and resiliency.
Key_Actors: Load balancers; root machines; leaf machines; GFS master; MapReduce system; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: Workloads are shared among many machines; large datasets are divided into fractions and stored on different leaf machines while root machines forward requests to the correct leaf; systems like GFS replicate data chunks across multiple servers to ensure availability; MapReduce delegates data processing tasks to thousands of machines in parallel; and automated systems manage these machines centrally as a single warehouse-scale computer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must be designed so that no single machine failure prevents the system from providing service; standardized rack structures and automated tasks are used to reduce management overhead; reliability is built through software rather than hardware.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These techniques were initially unsuited for applications requiring strict consistency; users might receive only partial results from a query if certain machines failed before replacement algorithms could load missing data onto a spare.

Concept_Name: commodity components
Core_Definition: Inexpensive, mass-produced hardware parts originally designed for the consumer market, such as Intel x86 CPUs, low-end hard drives, and RAM.
Key_Actors: Intel; home computer and gaming consumers; vendors like RedHat; large-scale internet companies like Google, Yahoo, and Facebook.
Operational_Workflow: Components developed for high-volume markets like home PCs and gaming are used to build server-class machines at a lower cost; these components are integrated into pizza box-sized computers and organized into standardized racks; software techniques are then used to manage these components and compensate for the lower reliability of individual parts.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hardware is often stripped of non-essential components like video cards or USB ports to reduce costs; companies focus on achieving the best price per unit of computation; the use of these components allows for sub-linear cost growth as the system scales.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Individual commodity components are more prone to complete failure than custom high-end components; specific generations of hardware might be skipped if they use a disproportionate amount of power; some companies eventually grow large enough to bypass commodity vendors and design their own custom hardware from scratch.

Concept_Name: run run run dead
Core_Definition: A term describing the binary nature of digital electronics where a system functions continuously until it fails completely and stops working.
Key_Actors: Digital electronics; computers; systems.
Operational_Workflow: A computer system is powered on and runs its designated tasks; the system operates at full capacity without any visible signs of component wear or quality loss; a failure occurs suddenly; the system transitions immediately from a running state to a dead state where it can no longer provide any service.
Rules_and_Constraints: The ability to provide service is treated as a binary state; the computer is either on or off; individual digital machines do not provide warning signs before a total failure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Distributed computing techniques are used to ensure that while an individual machine may fail completely the collection of machines as a whole continues to function.

Concept_Name: analog systems degrade slowly
Core_Definition: A characteristic of systems where performance and quality diminish gradually over time providing warning before total failure.
Key_Actors: Analog systems; tube-based radios; users.
Operational_Workflow: A system begins its lifecycle working at peak performance; over time individual components start to wear out; the wear results in a gradual reduction of quality such as a user hearing increased static on a radio; the system remains usable for a period of time despite the degradation; the user eventually receives months of warning before the system becomes completely unusable.
Rules_and_Constraints: Performance drops incrementally as more components fail; the system is not limited to binary on or off states; the degradation serves as a signal that repairs are needed before the system fails entirely.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: While individual digital machines are binary distributed computing researchers found that a collection of digital machines can be made to behave like an analog system by allowing performance to drop as individual machines fail.

Concept_Name: corpus
Core_Definition: A very large dataset such as those used for web search that exceeds the storage capacity of a single commodity server.
Key_Actors: Web search applications; commodity servers; researchers; developers.
Operational_Workflow: A large dataset is identified for a service; the dataset is divided into smaller segments for storage; the segments are distributed across a cluster of servers; the data is utilized for processing requests such as web searches; updates are performed through wholesale replacements when a new version of the dataset is produced.
Rules_and_Constraints: The data is typically mostly static and does not change frequently; the corpus must be divisible into smaller parts; the system must be able to handle request routing across the distributed data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This model is inappropriate for traditional applications like payroll systems where consistency is required and partial results are unacceptable.

Concept_Name: fractions
Core_Definition: The smaller segments created when a large corpus is divided to be stored across multiple distributed machines.
Key_Actors: Leaf machines; root machines; replacement algorithms; corpus.
Operational_Workflow: A large corpus is split into many individual segments; each segment is stored on a specific machine called a leaf; a root machine receives incoming requests and forwards them to the appropriate leaf holding the required segment; to ensure resiliency each segment is typically stored on at least two different leaves; if a segment becomes hot due to high traffic it is placed on more machines to handle the demand.
Rules_and_Constraints: Root machines must manage the mapping of requests to specific leaves; each segment should be replicated to avoid data loss during a machine failure; replacement algorithms must be used to load missing segments onto spare machines when a leaf fails.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If two leaves holding the same segment fail simultaneously the system may return slightly fewer results to the user until the replacement algorithms restore the missing data.

Concept_Name: leaf
Core_Definition: A machine within a distributed computing architecture that stores a specific fraction of a larger dataset or corpus.
Key_Actors: Commodity servers; corpus fractions; root machines.
Operational_Workflow: A large dataset or corpus is divided into multiple fractions. Each fraction is stored on a leaf machine rather than attempting to fit the entire dataset on a single server. When the system receives a request, a root machine identifies which fraction is required and forwards the request to the corresponding leaf machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: To maintain system performance and reliability, fractions can be distributed such that each machine stores multiple fractions, for example ten fractions per machine.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a particular fraction is hot and receiving a large number of hits, it can be placed on more machines, which may involve bumping out less popular fractions. If a leaf fails, the root redirects requests to a different leaf holding the same data fraction.

Concept_Name: root
Core_Definition: A machine in a distributed scaling architecture that receives incoming requests and forwards them to the appropriate leaf machine containing the required data.
Key_Actors: Root machines; leaf machines; user requests; data fractions.
Operational_Workflow: The root acts as a mediator that receives a request for information. It determines which fraction of the divided corpus contains the relevant data and then forwards the request to the specific leaf machine where that fraction is stored.
Rules_and_Constraints: The root is responsible for dividing traffic for a particular data fraction among available leaves as long as those leaves are up and functioning.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a leaf machine fails, the root identifies the failure and sends all requests related to that fraction to the remaining leaf that holds a copy of the data.

Concept_Name: resilient to failures
Core_Definition: The ability of a distributed computing system to remain operational and continue providing service even when individual machines or components fail.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing algorithms; commodity servers; software developers; root and leaf machines.
Operational_Workflow: The system is designed with many machines sharing the workload and maintaining enough spare capacity that a certain percentage of machines can be down without affecting service. Reliability is achieved through software techniques that allow the system to survive outages rather than relying on expensive, fault-tolerant hardware. This creates a system that degrades slowly like an analog radio rather than failing completely when a single digital component stops working.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software must be developed specifically to survive outages so that extra quality and expense are not required at every hardware level. Data must be stored in multiple locations to ensure it remains available if a server goes down.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a simultaneous failure occurs across all machines holding a specific piece of data, users might notice web searches returning slightly fewer results until replacement algorithms can load the missing data onto a spare machine. In systems like MapReduce, the logic detects failed or slow machines and sends their portion of the data to a working machine to ensure the process completes.

Concept_Name: replication
Core_Definition: The practice of storing multiple copies of data or corpus fractions across different machines to increase both reliability and performance.
Key_Actors: GFS master; commodity servers; data chunks or fractions; load balancers.
Operational_Workflow: Data is divided into chunks or fractions and each piece is stored on multiple commodity servers. The system uses these replicas to load balance data access, allowing multiple machines to handle read requests for the same information simultaneously.
Rules_and_Constraints: In the Google File System, each data chunk is typically stored on three or more commodity servers. Increasing the replication level is used to improve aggregate read performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: When a machine fails, a master system uses the remaining copies of the data to populate the missing information onto new machines until the desired replication factor is achieved again. For applications with static data, wholesale replacements occur when a new corpus is produced.

Concept_Name: hot fraction
Core_Definition: A specific portion or segment of a dataset that receives an exceptionally high volume of access requests or hits. It represents a subset of a larger corpus that is currently in high demand by users.
Key_Actors: Root machines that forward requests; leaf machines that store data segments; the data corpus; and distributed computing algorithms that manage data placement.
Operational_Workflow: The system divides a large corpus of data into many small fractions and assigns them to different leaf machines; root machines receive incoming queries and forward them to the specific leaf machine containing the relevant fraction; when a fraction is identified as hot due to high traffic, the system places it on additional machines to distribute the load; this process may involve bumping out less popular fractions to make room for the high-demand data.
Rules_and_Constraints: Each fraction must typically be stored on at least two different leaf machines to ensure resiliency against failure; the root machine must be able to divide traffic for a single fraction among multiple leaves; placement of fractions must be dynamically updated based on current popularity and usage patterns.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If all leaf machines holding a specific fraction fail simultaneously, the system may return partial results to the user until replacement algorithms can load the missing data onto a spare machine.

Concept_Name: cost per unit of capacity
Core_Definition: The financial expenditure required to provide a specific amount of computing performance, storage, or service. It is a metric used to measure the efficiency and scalability of different hardware and software architectures.
Key_Actors: Web-based businesses; hardware vendors; dot-com startups; and distributed computing systems.
Operational_Workflow: In early web environments, businesses scaled by purchasing increasingly larger and more specialized machines, which caused the cost per unit to grow super-linearly as the company expanded; modern distributed computing approaches use clusters of commodity hardware where the cost per unit remains linear or becomes sub-linear; this allows additional users to be supported at a lower cost per user as the scale of the operation increases.
Rules_and_Constraints: Traditional business models expect a lower cost per unit as volume increases due to economies of scale; a system is considered economically unsustainable if the cost per unit of performance increases disproportionately as the system grows larger.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: During the first web era, high costs per unit were temporarily ignored because companies with large losses and high spending were given high valuations by investors, though this model eventually led to the collapse of the dot-com bubble.

Concept_Name: volume discount
Core_Definition: A price reduction offered by hardware suppliers when a customer purchases a large quantity of standardized components or machines. It is a cost-saving mechanism available to organizations building large-scale distributed systems.
Key_Actors: Large-scale technology companies; commodity hardware vendors; and procurement teams.
Operational_Workflow: Instead of buying one or two large multiprocessor servers, a company chooses a distributed computing architecture that requires hundreds or thousands of smaller machines; the company leverages the high quantity of the order to negotiate a lower price per unit from the vendor; this discount contributes to the overall reduction in capital expenditure for the datacenter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Volume discounts are generally only applicable when purchasing many computers; they are not typically available when buying a small number of high-end, custom, or specialized machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: custom computers
Core_Definition: Hardware systems that have been redesigned or stripped of unnecessary features to meet the specific needs of a large-scale datacenter. These machines focus only on essential components to reduce cost and power consumption.
Key_Actors: Technology companies like Yahoo and Google; and hardware vendors.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations identify that standard consumer computers include unnecessary parts such as video cards, audio cards, speakers, and USB ports; companies work with vendors to build machines to their exact specifications or design their own hardware from scratch; components that do not contribute to the specific service, such as plastic bezels or decorative logos, are eliminated; these stripped-down machines are then deployed in massive quantities across datacenters.
Rules_and_Constraints: Custom computers should be stripped down to the exact components needed for their specific task; the savings from eliminating small items are only significant when applied to thousands of machines; custom designs should aim to improve the price per unit of computation and power efficiency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In the first web era, custom computers often referred to machines with custom CPUs and expensive internal architectures, which increased costs rather than decreasing them due to smaller market sizes.

Concept_Name: Moore’s law
Core_Definition: A principle identified as one of the factors reducing the overall cost of hardware in the computing industry.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Moore's law acts as a driver for reducing hardware costs, which alongside open source software and improvements in power efficiency, gives companies a competitive edge.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: warehouse-scale computing
Core_Definition: An approach to designing and operating datacenters where the entire facility is treated and managed as one giant, warehouse-sized computer.
Key_Actors: Google; Facebook; Barroso, Clidaras, and Hölzle.
Operational_Workflow: Large datacenters are constructed using repeating rack structures of commodity computers to reduce management complexity; the datacenter network functions like a communication bus that connects various units of compute and storage; operational tasks are standardized and automated so that software development costs are spread across all machines in the facility.
Rules_and_Constraints: Hardware and processes must be highly organized to manage enormous datacenters; repeating rack structures must be used to reduce administrative overhead; automation is required to ensure that the cost per machine decreases as the number of machines increases.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Google File System (GFS)
Core_Definition: A distributed file system designed to scale to multiple terabytes of data by storing information across many commodity servers.
Key_Actors: Google; GFS master; commodity servers.
Operational_Workflow: Files are divided into 64-megabyte chunks; each chunk is replicated and stored on three or more commodity servers; users access data by communicating directly with the specific machine holding the chunk to prevent bottlenecks; the system performs operations like file copying in parallel by pairing multiple machines together; the GFS master monitors the system and populates data on new machines if a server fails to maintain the required replication factor.
Rules_and_Constraints: Data must be stored on at least three replicas by default; data access should be load balanced among all available replicas; increasing the replication level is a best practice to improve both aggregate read performance and system resiliency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine fails, the GFS master must intervene to restore the replication factor using remaining copies; there is a risk that if the replication factor is too low, all copies of a file could be lost if all hosting machines are down simultaneously.

Concept_Name: MapReduce
Core_Definition: A system and programming model used for processing large batches of data in parallel by distributing the workload across thousands of machines.
Key_Actors: Software developers; MapReduce system; central MapReduce service; Hadoop; HBase.
Operational_Workflow: The system divides a large dataset into portions and delegates tasks to a cluster of machines; machines execute a map function to process their portion of data and create intermediate results; these intermediate results are then summarized by a reduce function to produce a final result; the system automatically manages data division, work delegation, and the detection of failed machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software developers are constrained to writing only the map and reduce functions while the system handles internal logic; a MapReduce run is only as fast as the slowest machine in the cluster; improvements made to the central MapReduce service are automatically applied to all users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Machine failures are common during runs that take several days, requiring the system to detect the failure and re-delegate the work; slow performance from a single machine, potentially caused by a failing hard disk, can delay the entire process; the system may use preemptive redundancy to run the same task on a second machine and accept the results from whichever finishes first.

Concept_Name: 64-megabyte chunks
Core_Definition: The fixed-size units into which files are divided for storage within the Google File System.
Key_Actors: Google File System; commodity servers; data files.
Operational_Workflow: Large files are broken down into these specific segments; each segment is then distributed and stored across three or more different commodity servers to facilitate access and ensure redundancy.
Rules_and_Constraints: Files must be stored as these discrete chunks; each individual chunk must be replicated across at least three servers to maintain system reliability.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: replication factor
Core_Definition: The specific number of copies of a data block that the system is configured to maintain across different machines to ensure availability and performance.
Key_Actors: GFS master; commodity servers; data blocks.
Operational_Workflow: The system is configured to maintain a set number of replicas for every data block; if the GFS master detects that a machine has failed and the number of copies has dropped, it uses the remaining replicas to populate the data onto new machines until the required count is restored.
Rules_and_Constraints: The standard practice is to keep three or more replicas of each block; increasing the replication level is used to improve performance through load balancing and to increase resiliency against simultaneous machine failures.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine fails, the system must perform recovery operations to restore the factor; if all machines holding the replicas for a specific file are down at the same time, the data becomes unavailable.

Concept_Name: GFS master
Core_Definition: The central management component of the Google File System that coordinates data redundancy and machine recovery.
Key_Actors: Google File System; commodity servers; data replicas.
Operational_Workflow: The master monitors the status of the various commodity servers within the system; upon detecting a machine failure, it manages the remaining copies of data to ensure they are repopulated on other functioning machines to maintain the necessary replication factor.
Rules_and_Constraints: The master is responsible for ensuring that the system-wide replication goals are met and maintained.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The master must intervene and initiate data population tasks specifically when a machine goes down.

Concept_Name: parallel processing
Core_Definition: A computing technique where large tasks or data sets are broken down so that multiple operations can be performed simultaneously across many different machines.
Key_Actors: GFS; MapReduce; commodity servers; software developers.
Operational_Workflow: In file systems, machines storing different parts of a file are paired with other machines to transmit all blocks at the same time rather than sequentially; in data processing systems like MapReduce, a large batch of data is divided into portions and delegated to dozens or thousands of machines that process their specific share simultaneously to produce intermediate and then final results.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must be capable of dividing data and delegating work to various machines; a parallel run is only as fast as the slowest machine involved in the process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine fails during a multi-day run, the system must detect it and send its portion of the data to a working machine; if a machine performs slowly due to potential hardware failure, the system may initiate the same task on a second machine in a race to see which finishes first, at which point the slower one is aborted.

Concept_Name: MapReduce run
Core_Definition: A MapReduce run is a process for handling large batches of data in parallel by dividing the workload across a cluster of machines. It automates the delegation of tasks to ensure massive datasets can be processed efficiently.
Key_Actors: The central MapReduce system; software developers who write specific functions; and a cluster consisting of dozens or thousands of machines.
Operational_Workflow: A software developer writes a map function and a reduce function; the centralized MapReduce system divides the large dataset into portions; the system delegates these portions to various machines for processing; machines execute the map function to create intermediate results; these intermediates are processed and summarized by the reduce function to produce a final result.
Rules_and_Constraints: The developer only needs to provide the map and reduce functions while the system handles data division and delegation; the system is designed to manage runs that may take days; any improvements to the centralized service benefit all users.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A run is only as fast as the slowest machine in the cluster; if a machine fails during a run, the system must detect it and reassign its work to another machine.

Concept_Name: detecting a failed machine
Core_Definition: This is the automated logic within the MapReduce system used to identify when a machine in the cluster has stopped functioning or is performing sub-optimally. It ensures that hardware issues do not permanently halt large-scale data processing.
Key_Actors: The central MapReduce system and the individual machines within the processing cluster.
Operational_Workflow: The MapReduce system monitors the machines involved in a data run; it identifies machines that have completely failed; it also identifies machines taking considerably longer than others to process their share of data; slow performance is flagged as a potential sign of a failing hard disk or other underlying problems.
Rules_and_Constraints: The logic for detection is incorporated into the centralized system so that the software developer does not need to manage it.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: sending its portion of data to another working machine
Core_Definition: This is the recovery mechanism in a distributed system where data assigned to a failed or slow machine is reallocated to a functional machine. This ensures the entire batch of data is processed despite individual hardware failures.
Key_Actors: The central MapReduce system; the failed or underperforming machine; and a different working machine.
Operational_Workflow: The system detects that a machine has failed or is performing too slowly; the system identifies the specific portion of data assigned to that machine; the MapReduce system then directs a different working machine to process that same portion of data.
Rules_and_Constraints: This process is an automated feature of the centralized MapReduce system intended to save the overall run from failure or extreme delays.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: preemptive redundancy
Core_Definition: Preemptive redundancy is an optimization technique where a system proactively assigns the same task to an additional machine when it predicts an original machine may fail or cause delays. It prevents the slowest machine in a cluster from bottlenecking the entire process.
Key_Actors: The central MapReduce system; the original slow machine; and a second machine assigned the same data portion.
Operational_Workflow: The system predicts a machine might fail based on it taking considerably longer to process data; the system directs another machine to process the exact same portion of data; the system waits for one of the machines to complete the task; whichever machine finishes first wins and its results are kept; the system aborts the processing on the other machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technique is utilized because a MapReduce run is only as fast as the slowest machine; it is used to save hours of processing time in large-scale operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Hadoop
Core_Definition: The most popular open source implementation of the MapReduce system used for processing large batches of data in parallel.
Key_Actors: Software developers; distributed computing systems; clusters of machines.
Operational_Workflow: Large batches of data are divided into portions; the system delegates work to dozens or thousands of machines to process these portions in parallel; machines execute a map function to create intermediate results; these intermediate results are then summarized by a reduce function to produce a final result.
Rules_and_Constraints: Software developers using the system are responsible for writing the map and reduce functions while the system handles data division and work delegation; the system must incorporate logic for detecting failed machines and reassignment of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If a machine fails during a multi-day run involving thousands of nodes, the system detects the failure and sends its portion of data to a working machine; if a machine performs slowly due to a failing hard disk, the system can preemptively direct another machine to process the same data portion so that the fastest one wins.

Concept_Name: HBase
Core_Definition: An open source data storage system included with Hadoop that is similar in design to the Google File System.
Key_Actors: Hadoop implementation teams; distributed storage systems.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Centralized operations
Core_Definition: A management strategy for large datacenters where hundreds of racks of similarly configured computers are managed as a single unit to achieve economic benefits.
Key_Actors: Operational teams; automated software systems; large-scale companies such as Google or Facebook.
Operational_Workflow: Managers maintain hundreds of racks of similarly configured computers; operational tasks are automated through software; the cost of software development is spread across all machines in the infrastructure.
Rules_and_Constraints: Operations must rely on standardization to make automation possible; the system should be designed to require less labor as it grows by spreading development costs across a larger number of machines; datacenters may be treated as one giant warehouse-scale computer.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Standardization
Core_Definition: The practice of using highly organized and repeating hardware structures and processes to reduce complexity and administrative overhead.
Key_Actors: Hardware designers; datacenter administrators; management teams.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations repeat the same rack structure and computer configurations throughout a datacenter; this uniformity allows for the automation of operational tasks; software is used to manage these standardized units at scale.
Rules_and_Constraints: Highly organized hardware and processes are required to manage enormous datacenters; standardization is a prerequisite for effective automation; the goal of standardization is to reduce management and administrative overhead.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Automate operational tasks
Core_Definition: The use of software to perform management and administrative duties within a datacenter to replace manual human effort.
Key_Actors: Centralized operations teams; software developers; automated systems; commodity servers.
Operational_Workflow: Hardware and processes are first highly organized and standardized to reduce complexity; software is then developed to handle operational tasks centrally; the cost of developing this software is spread across all machines in the infrastructure; as the number of machines increases, the cost of automation per machine decreases.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standardization of rack structures and hardware is a prerequisite for effective automation; the cost of development must be amortized over a large enough number of machines to be economically viable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At a small scale, automation makes less sense because the development costs are not spread across enough units, which can drive up the total operational cost compared to manual labor.

Concept_Name: labor cost
Core_Definition: The financial expense associated with the human work required to manage, maintain, and operate computing systems.
Key_Actors: Systems administrators; specialized teams; generalists; external consultants; IT departments.
Operational_Workflow: In manual operational models, labor requirements increase as the system grows larger; by implementing automation and standardization, companies reduce the amount of manual labor needed per machine; cloud providers further reduce labor costs by providing self-service APIs and control panels that allow customers to support themselves.
Rules_and_Constraints: Labor cost is typically higher in small-scale operations where companies must hire generalists or expensive external consultants; in centralized operations, labor costs should ideally scale sub-linearly with the number of machines.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small companies often cannot afford full-time specialized teams, leading to higher proportional labor costs; recalcitrant or underfunded IT departments can become a bottleneck, leading other departments to bypass them in favor of cloud services.

Concept_Name: datacenter as one giant computer
Core_Definition: A computing model, also known as warehouse-scale computing, where an entire facility of networked machines is designed and operated as a single unified system.
Key_Actors: Google; Facebook; warehouse-scale computing designers.
Operational_Workflow: Thousands of pizza box–sized computers are organized into racks; the datacenter network functions as a communication bus that connects all units of compute and storage; management of memory, disks, and CPUs is handled centrally as if they were components of a single machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: This model requires highly organized hardware and processes to manage the complexity of enormous datacenters; it relies on the network to act as a high-speed bus between compute units.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE)
Core_Definition: A metric representing the ratio of the total power entering a datacenter to the power actually delivered to the IT equipment.
Key_Actors: Datacenter operators; utility providers; cooling systems; power conversion hardware.
Operational_Workflow: Total power is measured at the utility meter as it enters the facility; power is lost through multiple conversions between alternating current and direct current and through various voltage levels; additional power is consumed by systems designed to extract heat generated by the computers; the PUE is calculated to determine how much energy is wasted on non-IT functions.
Rules_and_Constraints: A typical PUE is 2.5; best practices can achieve 1.6; highly efficient providers like Google, Microsoft, and Facebook aim for 1.2 or lower; every decimal improvement in PUE provides a competitive edge in operational costs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: PUE can fluctuate based on environmental factors; for instance, Google reported reaching 1.08 PUE specifically during winter months.

Concept_Name: power conversion
Core_Definition: The process of changing electrical energy between alternating current and direct current or between different voltage levels to meet the requirements of various systems.
Key_Actors: Utility meters; high voltage lines; UPS systems; power outlets; computer components and chips.
Operational_Workflow: Electricity enters the facility at the utility meter and is processed by a UPS where it is converted from alternating current to direct current and back to alternating current; power is then converted from high voltage lines to 110 VAC for use in power outlets; finally the power is converted to 12 V and other voltage levels required by the internal components and chips of the computer.
Rules_and_Constraints: Efficiency is lost every time a power conversion occurs; typical computer power systems undergo four or five conversions between the high voltage lines and the chips.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: UPS
Core_Definition: A system used in datacenters to manage power supply by performing a double conversion of the electrical current.
Key_Actors: Utility meters; power conversion systems; IT equipment.
Operational_Workflow: The system receives alternating current from the utility; it converts this power into direct current; it then converts the power back into alternating current before it is delivered to the computing equipment.
Rules_and_Constraints: The double conversion process from A/C to D/C and back to A/C is noted as a specific source of efficiency loss within the datacenter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Alternating Current (A/C)
Core_Definition: A type of electrical current supplied by utilities and used in standard power outlets for distribution to hardware.
Key_Actors: Utility meters; UPS systems; 110 VAC power outlets.
Operational_Workflow: Alternating current is received from the utility meter; it serves as the input and final output of the UPS conversion process; it is distributed at 110 VAC through power outlets before being converted to direct current for internal computer use.
Rules_and_Constraints: Standard power outlets in datacenters typically provide 110 VAC.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Direct Current (D/C)
Core_Definition: The specific form of electrical power required by computer chips and internal components and used as an intermediate phase in power backup systems.
Key_Actors: UPS systems; computer chips; internal hardware components.
Operational_Workflow: Within a UPS, alternating current is temporarily converted into direct current before being returned to an alternating state; inside a computer, alternating current from the outlet is converted to 12 V and other direct current voltage levels to power the chips and components.
Rules_and_Constraints: Computer components and chips require direct current at specific levels such as 12 V to function.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: cooling
Core_Definition: A critical operational factor in datacenters involving the extraction of heat generated by computer hardware.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; computer hardware; utility meters; cooling systems.
Operational_Workflow: Power enters the datacenter via a utility meter; computer components consume power and generate heat; power is then utilized to extract this heat from the environment rather than simply cooling the air; efficiency is tracked by comparing total power input to power used by IT equipment.
Rules_and_Constraints: It requires at least one watt of power to remove the heat produced by one watt of computer usage; efficiency is measured by Power Usage Effectiveness with typical ratings around 2.5 and efficient goals at 1.6 or lower.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Efficiency ratings can fluctuate based on environmental conditions, such as some datacenters achieving a 1.08 rating during the winter months.

Concept_Name: price per unit of computation
Core_Definition: A measure of efficiency where the goal is to maximize the amount of work performed by a system relative to its total cost.
Key_Actors: Smart companies; IT departments; computer hardware.
Operational_Workflow: Companies evaluate hardware options to determine which provide the best performance for the price; they analyze power usage against computational output; hardware that is faster but uses disproportionate power may be skipped to maintain a better efficiency profile.
Rules_and_Constraints: True efficiency is defined as getting the most capacity, such as queries per second, for the money spent; this model favors large-scale distributed systems where cost growth can be linear or sub-linear.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Higher-end or faster CPUs may be avoided if their electricity consumption makes the cost of computation uncompetitive.

Concept_Name: QPS
Core_Definition: A metric standing for queries per second that measures the service capacity of a computing system.
Key_Actors: Large high-end servers; commodity servers; search engine applications; users.
Operational_Workflow: A system receives requests for service from users; the hardware and software process these requests; the total number of queries handled successfully in a single second is recorded to define the capacity and efficiency of the system.
Rules_and_Constraints: QPS is used as a standard to compare the performance of different hardware architectures like high-end multiprocessor servers versus commodity clusters; achieving the highest QPS for the money spent is a primary goal for internet search services.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: freemium business model
Core_Definition: A business strategy that offers basic services at no cost to build a user base while charging for premium features.
Key_Actors: Customers; web service providers; advertisers.
Operational_Workflow: A provider offers a service with basic features for free to attract large numbers of users; once users become accustomed to using the service, the provider offers them additional premium features for a fee; advertising revenue is often used to ensure the free users do not create a financial burden.
Rules_and_Constraints: The service must have a low barrier to entry to attract a large volume of customers; the business remains viable only if the advertising revenue or the low cost of computing supports the free user tier.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: advertising revenue
Core_Definition: Income generated through automated systems where advertisers bid in online auctions to place ads on websites, providing a business model that can subsidize or pay for computing infrastructure.
Key_Actors: Potential advertisers; advertising systems such as Google AdSense; website owners or bloggers; advertising networks.
Operational_Workflow: A website owner adds JavaScript code to their site; potential advertisers bid in a large online auction for ad placement; the advertising system automates the placement of ads; the advertising network sends checks to the website owners or bloggers as money rolls in.
Rules_and_Constraints: The cost of computing must be significantly cheaper for systems collecting pennies per click to be profitable; the system should reduce or eliminate the need for a dedicated sales force.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: mobile computing
Core_Definition: A period beginning around 2010 characterized by affordable and accessible technology where cell phones evolved into smartphones running sophisticated operating systems.
Key_Actors: Smartphones; mobile applications; supporting infrastructure technology; mobile users.
Operational_Workflow: Mobile devices running sophisticated operating systems access applications that create demand for better latency and reliability; these applications send requests to backend services which must respond in a second or two to be usable; this increased usability leads to higher numbers of users and capacity demands that increase by multiple orders of magnitude.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must respond fast enough to be usable in a real-world context; increased demand requires quantum leaps in support infrastructure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: smartphones
Core_Definition: Handheld mobile devices that run operating systems as sophisticated as those found on personal computers.
Key_Actors: Mobile users; sophisticated operating systems.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Mobile applications
Core_Definition: Software programs running on mobile devices that create specific demands for low-latency services and high levels of reliability.
Key_Actors: Mobile applications; map applications; life-critical applications; backend services.
Operational_Workflow: A user interacts with an application such as a map by dragging points; the application sends dozens of requests to a backend service to recalculate a route in real time; the application relies on backend services for data like real-time traffic reports; map tiles may be cached to improve performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: Applications must respond in a second or two to be functional in a real-world context; reliability of supporting services must increase as applications become more life-critical.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A map application becomes not very useful if the map service it relies on is down when needed; real-time traffic reports are less able to be cached than map tiles.

Concept_Name: lower-latency services
Core_Definition: Services designed to respond faster to user requests to meet the real-world usability requirements of mobile applications.
Key_Actors: Mobile applications; SSD storage technology; internal communication systems; redesigned RPC mechanisms.
Operational_Workflow: Calculations are performed hundreds of times per second to allow for real-time user interactions; SSDs eliminate the mechanical wait times for disk heads to move or platters to rotate; internal communication latency is reduced through direct memory access between machines that often bypasses the CPU; information is passed between machines nearly as fast as reading local RAM.
Rules_and_Constraints: Services must respond within a second or two to be usable in a real-world context.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If calculations take significantly longer than a few seconds, such as an hour for a driving route, the service fails to meet the needs of the mobile application context.

Concept_Name: map tiles can be cached
Core_Definition: The practice of storing map data locally or temporarily so that mobile map applications remain functional even when supporting services are unavailable.
Key_Actors: Mobile map applications; supporting map services; caching systems.
Operational_Workflow: Map data is saved in a cache to ensure the application remains useful during an outage; the mobile application retrieves these tiles from the cache when the primary service is down; this methodology supports the requirement for new levels of reliability in mobile apps.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This specific reliability technique is noted as being less applicable to real-time traffic reports.

Concept_Name: real-time traffic reports
Core_Definition: Live data updates provided to mobile map applications that are difficult to cache and require high service reliability.
Key_Actors: Mobile applications; supporting backend services.
Operational_Workflow: Supporting services deliver live traffic updates to mobile apps to enable real-time route calculations and map interactions; as these applications become more life-critical, the reliability of the services providing these reports becomes more important; the service must maintain constant availability because the data cannot be effectively cached like map tiles.
Rules_and_Constraints: High reliability is mandatory because these reports are life-critical and less amenable to caching.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: negative cost infrastructure
Core_Definition: A computing infrastructure that becomes free for the owner because the profit generated from renting out surplus capacity to others covers all operational costs.
Key_Actors: Cloud computing providers; Amazon; third-party tenants.
Operational_Workflow: A provider builds a computing infrastructure significantly larger than its own internal needs; the provider develops technology to rent the spare capacity to external customers; the profit from these rentals offsets the provider's own infrastructure costs; the provider achieves a scale where its own computing resources are effectively subsidized or become entirely free.
Rules_and_Constraints: The provider must achieve a scale that allows for sub-linear cost growth; the provider must develop technology that allows for renting spare capacity to others; the service must be priced at less than customers could achieve themselves while still delivering profit.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small-scale computing systems cannot take advantage of these economics and must instead rent space on a cloud infrastructure to mitigate high operational costs and lack of specialized knowledge.

Concept_Name: Elasticity
Core_Definition: The ability to increase and decrease the amount of resources consumed dynamically.
Key_Actors: Cloud service providers; customers or tenants; automated systems.
Operational_Workflow: A customer identifies a need for more or fewer resources; the customer uses cloud tools to expand or contract their resource usage; the system adjusts the capacity provided to the customer in a fast and dynamic manner.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be fast enough to be considered elastic; it relies on the provider having spare capacity to rent to others.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: spin up many servers
Core_Definition: The rapid activation of a large quantity of virtual machines for a short duration to handle specific workloads.
Key_Actors: Customers such as advertising agencies; cloud service providers like Amazon EC2.
Operational_Workflow: The user determines a short-term need for high computing capacity; the user initiates the request via a mouse click or a few API calls; the cloud provider's infrastructure creates and allocates the requested virtual machines.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is often used for temporary situations like a week-long advertising campaign; it is made possible by virtualization technology and self-service administration capabilities.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: acquire so many servers in hours
Core_Definition: The rapid procurement of significant computing resources within a very short timeframe compared to traditional hardware setup.
Key_Actors: Business departments; uncooperative or underfunded IT departments; cloud providers.
Operational_Workflow: A user requires hundreds of web servers to handle traffic for a specific event; the user bypasses traditional IT procurement processes which could take months or a year; the user utilizes cloud infrastructure to have all systems ready for use within hours.
Rules_and_Constraints: This capability allows departments to make an end-run around internal IT delays; it relies on the existence of a pre-built, large-scale infrastructure by the provider.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: give back servers
Core_Definition: The act of releasing or disposing of rented virtual computing resources to the provider when they are no longer needed.
Key_Actors: Cloud infrastructure customers; cloud providers.
Operational_Workflow: Once a campaign or project is complete, the user identifies the servers that are no longer required; the user releases or disposes of these machines back to the cloud provider; the provider then makes this spare capacity available for other tenants.
Rules_and_Constraints: Releasing machines should be a simple process; this practice is described as more practical than dealing with physical hardware at the end of a short-term project.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: external consultants
Core_Definition: Specialized professionals hired by small companies to provide specific technical expertise that the internal staff lacks.
Key_Actors: Small companies; generalist IT staff; specialized teams; external consultants.
Operational_Workflow: When a small-scale company encounters complex systems that require specialized knowledge but cannot justify hiring a full-time person or team to oversee that specific area, they hire these outside experts to manage the expertise gap.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text notes that using these consultants is an expensive option for small companies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The need for external consultants is often mitigated when small-scale computing is moved to the cloud, as the cloud provider manages the difficult behind-the-scenes technologies.

Concept_Name: SSDs
Core_Definition: Storage devices with no moving parts that have replaced traditional hard disks to provide lower latency in the cloud computing era.
Key_Actors: Database server replicas; storage systems; cloud infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: SSDs function by providing rapid data access without the mechanical delays associated with moving parts; they allow a service to achieve required performance levels with fewer physical machines than would be necessary using traditional disks.
Rules_and_Constraints: SSDs are more expensive per gigabyte than traditional disks; however, they offer a lower total cost of ownership because they reduce the total number of servers required to maintain specific service latencies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: read head
Core_Definition: A mechanical component found in traditional hard disks that must move to specific locations to retrieve or write data.
Key_Actors: Traditional hard disks; disk platters.
Operational_Workflow: In a traditional disk system, the process of accessing information involves a physical wait while this component relocates to the correct section of the disk platter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The movement of this component is a primary source of latency that modern SSD technology seeks to eliminate.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: disk platter
Core_Definition: A physical, rotating component within a traditional hard drive where data is stored.
Key_Actors: Traditional hard disks; read heads.
Operational_Workflow: For data to be accessed, the system must wait for this component to rotate to the exact position required by the read head.
Rules_and_Constraints: The physical rotation of the platter creates mechanical wait times that increase service latency; these delays are absent in non-mechanical storage like SSDs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: latency of internal communication
Core_Definition: The time delay involved when moving information between different machines within a distributed system or datacenter.
Key_Actors: Source and destination machines; mobile applications; remote procedure call (RPC) mechanisms; storage technologies such as SSDs.
Operational_Workflow: Information is traditionally converted to a wire format, placed into a packet, and passed through the operating system and network layers of both the source and destination machines; modern workflows bypass many of these steps using technologies that permit direct memory access between machines to transfer data nearly as fast as reading local RAM.
Rules_and_Constraints: Low internal latency is required to support mobile applications that need real-time calculations and responses within a second or two; the demand for lower latency increases as applications become more life-critical and usable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: In older systems, information passed through many layers of technology which added significant latency; modern technologies can bypass the CPU of the source or destination machine entirely to reduce delays.

Concept_Name: wire format
Core_Definition: A specific data representation used to prepare information for transmission between machines.
Key_Actors: Information; source machines; destination machines.
Operational_Workflow: Information is converted into a wire format to make it ready for transmission; the formatted data is then placed inside a packet; the process is reversed at the receiving machine to retrieve the original data.
Rules_and_Constraints: The conversion of information to a wire format is identified as a necessary step in traditional network communication that contributes to internal latency.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Technologies developed in the cloud computing era aim to remove the latency associated with wire format conversion by enabling direct memory access between machines.

Concept_Name: packet
Core_Definition: A unit of data containing information that has been formatted for transmission through network layers.
Key_Actors: Information; operating system; device layers; network.
Operational_Workflow: Information is converted to a wire format and placed into a packet; the packet is sent through the operating system’s TCP/IP layer and device layer to the network; it reaches the destination machine where the process is reversed to extract the original information.
Rules_and_Constraints: Packet-based transmission through multiple technology layers is a standard process that adds to the total latency of internal communication.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Modern high-speed communication technologies allow information to be passed between machines while bypassing the traditional packet-processing layers to achieve speeds comparable to local RAM access.

Concept_Name: operating system’s TCP/IP layer
Core_Definition: A software layer within the operating system that handles the processing and routing of data packets for network communication.
Key_Actors: Operating system; packets; device layer; CPU.
Operational_Workflow: A packet travels through the TCP/IP layer and device layer of the source machine before entering the network; upon arrival at the destination machine, the packet must pass through the corresponding device and TCP/IP layers to reach its destination application.
Rules_and_Constraints: This layer acts as a processing stage that information must traverse in traditional networking, which adds to the overall internal latency of the system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Newer distributed computing technologies allow machines to bypass the operating system's TCP/IP layer and sometimes the CPU itself to facilitate faster data transfer between machines.

Concept_Name: device layer
Core_Definition: A component of the operating system that packets must pass through during the transmission of information between computers.
Key_Actors: The operating system; data packets; internal communication systems.
Operational_Workflow: Information is converted to a wire format to prepare for transmission; a copy is made and placed in a packet; the packet travels through the operating system TCP/IP layer and the device layer; the packet reaches the network to be sent to the destination machine.
Rules_and_Constraints: In traditional configurations, this layer acts as one of several steps that add latency to the communication process.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Modern technologies that permit direct memory access between machines can bypass this layer to reduce latency.

Concept_Name: network
Core_Definition: A communication bus that connects units of compute and storage within a datacenter.
Key_Actors: Datacenters; compute units; storage units; tenants; software-defined networks; programmable VLANs.
Operational_Workflow: The network acts as a medium for moving information between machines; in a cloud environment it utilizes programmable VLANs and software-defined networking to segment infrastructure; it allows for the fine-grained partitioning of computers to ensure one tenant cannot interfere with another.
Rules_and_Constraints: Large networks should be designed to treat an entire datacenter as a single warehouse-scale computer; networking must provide segmenting capabilities at a fine granularity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Latency is introduced when information must travel through multiple technology layers like TCP/IP and the device layer before entering the network.

Concept_Name: direct memory access
Core_Definition: A technology used in the cloud computing era to move information between machines nearly as fast as reading local RAM.
Key_Actors: Source machines; destination machines; CPUs; memory systems.
Operational_Workflow: This technology allows information to bypass the operating system's TCP/IP layer and device layer; it frequently bypasses the CPU of the source or destination machine entirely; it enables the transfer of data between machines with minimal latency.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is implemented to meet the demand for lower-latency services required by modern mobile and cloud applications.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: RPC mechanisms
Core_Definition: Underlying communication protocols used to facilitate interaction between machines in a distributed system.
Key_Actors: Software developers; distributed systems; low-latency communication technologies.
Operational_Workflow: These mechanisms manage the exchange of information between different machines; they have been redesigned from scratch in the cloud era to take advantage of low-latency technologies such as direct memory access.
Rules_and_Constraints: Modern versions must be capable of fully utilizing new hardware capabilities that allow data transfer at speeds approaching local RAM access.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: segment infrastructure
Core_Definition: The technological capability to partition a datacenter's physical resources into isolated units that prevent different users from interfering with one another. It is a fundamental technology that drives the functionality of cloud computing.
Key_Actors: Cloud providers; tenants; virtualization technologies; networking systems; self-service control panels and APIs.
Operational_Workflow: The infrastructure is divided by partitioning large machines into many smaller ones using virtualization technology; networking technologies such as software-defined networks provide isolation between these partitioned units; control panels and APIs are implemented to allow customers to perform administration tasks themselves, which removes the need for manual labor by datacenter staff.
Rules_and_Constraints: The primary constraint is that the segmentation must be effective enough to ensure one tenant cannot interfere with another; it requires the ability to segment computers at a very fine level of granularity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: tenant
Core_Definition: A customer or user group that rents computing resources from a larger infrastructure provided by a cloud operator. This model allows the provider to subsidize their own infrastructure costs while giving the user access to scalable resources.
Key_Actors: Individual customers; corporate departments; cloud providers such as Amazon; self-service administration systems.
Operational_Workflow: Tenants access resources by using control panels or making API calls to spin up virtual machines; they utilize elasticity to rapidly increase or decrease their resource consumption based on their current needs; they perform their own administrative tasks using self-service tools to minimize operational costs for the provider.
Rules_and_Constraints: A tenant is prohibited from interfering with the operations of other tenants sharing the same infrastructure; tenants are expected to support themselves through provided APIs and control panels to maintain the low-labor economic model of the cloud.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Tenants sometimes adopt cloud services specifically to perform an end-run around internal IT departments that have become recalcitrant, underfunded, or difficult to deal with.

Concept_Name: networking technologies
Core_Definition: The hardware and software systems that act as a communication bus to connect units of compute and storage within a datacenter. These technologies facilitate the movement of information and the isolation of resources.
Key_Actors: Cloud computing providers; warehouse-scale computers; direct memory access systems; programmable VLANs; software-defined networks.
Operational_Workflow: Networking functions as a bus connecting all units of compute and storage within a warehouse-sized computer; it enables the segmentation of computers at a fine granularity to support multi-tenancy; specialized technologies facilitate direct memory access between machines to bypass traditional processing steps and reduce communication latency.
Rules_and_Constraints: Technologies must support segmenting computers at a very fine level of granularity; networking systems should be designed to minimize latency by reducing the number of technological layers information must pass through.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Traditional networking steps, such as wire format conversion and passing packets through operating system device layers, are bypassed in modern high-performance systems to eliminate latency.

Concept_Name: programmable VLANs
Core_Definition: A specific networking technology used in cloud computing to isolate computers from each other at a very fine level of granularity. It is a key requirement for ensuring that different tenants on a shared infrastructure remain segmented.
Key_Actors: Cloud infrastructure systems; networking software; software-defined networks.
Operational_Workflow: These are implemented as part of the technological stack within a datacenter to provide the granular segmentation necessary for multi-tenancy; they work alongside virtualization and software-defined networks to ensure that one tenant's activities do not impact another.
Rules_and_Constraints: This technology must be capable of providing segmentation at a very fine granularity to prevent interference between isolated systems.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: software-defined networks
Core_Definition: Networking technologies that allow for the segmentation of computers from each other at a very fine granularity.
Key_Actors: Infrastructure systems; tenants; datacenters.
Operational_Workflow: These networks function within the cloud computing era to segment infrastructure such that one tenant cannot interfere with another.
Rules_and_Constraints: They are used alongside programmable VLANs to achieve the fine granularity required for tenant isolation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: partition one large machine into many smaller ones
Core_Definition: A method of hardware segmentation used to divide the resources of a single computer into multiple distinct units.
Key_Actors: One large machine; many smaller machines.
Operational_Workflow: This functions as a foundational capability for cloud computing by allowing a single physical infrastructure to be shared among different tenants.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is identified as a requirement for the cloud computing era and is implemented through virtualization technology.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: virtualization technology
Core_Definition: The specific technology that enables the partitioning of a single large machine into many smaller ones to support tenant systems.
Key_Actors: Cloud providers like Amazon; virtual machines; tenants; customers.
Operational_Workflow: Cloud providers use this technology to rent virtual machines on their infrastructure; it allows customers to spin up new machines via mouse clicks or API calls and dispose of them quickly when they are no longer needed; this provides elasticity by enabling the expansion and contraction of resources.
Rules_and_Constraints: It must be implemented such that one tenant cannot interfere with another; it is used to subsidize the cost of the provider's infrastructure.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: control panels
Core_Definition: Self-service administration capabilities that allow customers to manage their own computing resources.
Key_Actors: Customers; datacenter operations teams; APIs; IT departments.
Operational_Workflow: Customers use these interfaces or APIs to support themselves and acquire resources by clicking a mouse; this workflow bypasses the need for manual labor from datacenter staff or lengthy arguments with internal IT departments.
Rules_and_Constraints: They must be designed so that customers support themselves and do not add considerable labor cost to datacenter operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: APIs
Core_Definition: A technology used to drive cloud computing by allowing customers to manage resources programmatically.
Key_Actors: Customers; datacenter operations systems.
Operational_Workflow: Customers use API calls to perform actions such as spinning up new machines or releasing them when they are no longer needed.
Rules_and_Constraints: APIs are required to enable self-service so that customers do not add considerable labor cost to datacenter operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: self-service administration capabilities
Core_Definition: Features including control panels and APIs that allow customers to manage their own computing resources.
Key_Actors: Customers; datacenter operations teams.
Operational_Workflow: Customers use these tools to support themselves instead of relying on manual labor from the service provider; this helps maintain low operational costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: These capabilities must be designed to ensure customers can support themselves without adding to the labor cost of the datacenter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)
Core_Definition: A system that rents virtual machines on its infrastructure to subsidize the provider's costs and allow for rapid resource scaling.
Key_Actors: Amazon; customers; virtual machines.
Operational_Workflow: Customers spin up machines using a mouse click or API calls to handle sudden spikes in traffic such as advertising campaigns; once the need has passed, the machines are released or given back to the cloud provider.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must allow for the fast expansion and contraction of resources, a quality known as elasticity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: virtual machines
Core_Definition: Smaller units of compute created by partitioning one large physical machine using virtualization technology.
Key_Actors: Large physical machines; tenants; virtualization technology.
Operational_Workflow: Virtualization technology is used to partition a single large machine into many smaller ones; this allows a provider to segment infrastructure so that different tenants can use the same hardware without interfering with each other.
Rules_and_Constraints: Virtual machines must be segmented such that one tenant cannot interfere with another.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: expand and contract the amount of resources
Core_Definition: The ability to dynamically increase and decrease the quantity of computing resources consumed to meet changing demand.
Key_Actors: Cloud computing providers such as Amazon; customers or tenants of cloud services; automated systems and APIs used for resource management.
Operational_Workflow: A customer identifies a need for temporary high capacity such as a week long advertising campaign; the customer uses a mouse click or API calls to spin up hundreds of web servers within hours; the resources are utilized for the duration of the specific project or traffic spike; once the campaign ends the customer releases the machines back to the cloud provider to stop incurring costs.
Rules_and_Constraints: The process must be fast enough to acquire resources in hours rather than the months or years required for physical hardware; the infrastructure must be segmented so that one tenant cannot interfere with another using virtualization and software-defined networks.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Performing this workflow with physical hardware is considered impractical because setting up and disposing of large numbers of physical servers for short term needs is difficult and slow.

Concept_Name: super-linear cost curve
Core_Definition: A cost model where the price per unit of computing capacity grows faster than the actual performance or capacity increases as the system scales.
Key_Actors: Early dot-com era companies; vendors of high-end custom hardware like Sun Microsystems; system administrators managing large-scale websites.
Operational_Workflow: As a website grows in popularity administrators attempt to scale by purchasing larger and more powerful individual machines; these machines use custom hardware and have a smaller market which drives up the price per unit; high availability is achieved through N plus one or N plus two configurations where fifty to sixty-six percent of the expensive hardware investment sits idle.
Rules_and_Constraints: Scaling is handled through hardware upgrades rather than distributed systems; reliability is achieved through expensive built-in hardware features like RAID and custom CPUs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These high costs were initially acceptable during the dot-com bubble because of optimistic company valuations and a lack of understanding of scaling economics but the model eventually became unsustainable and contributed to the economic collapse.

Concept_Name: linear cost curves
Core_Definition: A cost model where the increase in expenditure for computing resources stays proportional to the increase in performance and scale.
Key_Actors: Distributed computing researchers like those at UC-Berkeley; companies like Google and Yahoo; open source software projects; commodity hardware manufacturers.
Operational_Workflow: Organizations replace expensive high-end servers with large clusters of inexpensive commodity PC-based machines; distributed computing algorithms allow these machines to share workloads and provide enough spare capacity to handle failures; software is written to survive machine outages which removes the need for expensive redundant hardware at every level; automation of operational tasks spreads development costs across all machines to prevent labor costs from scaling with the number of devices.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems must move from the run run run dead model of single machines to a graceful degradation model where the system continues working even if individual machines fail; hardware must be standardized and stripped of unnecessary components like video cards or speakers to reduce costs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This approach was initially inappropriate for traditional applications like payroll systems which required strict consistency and could not tolerate the partial results or failure modes of early distributed algorithms.

Concept_Name: sub-linear cost curves
Core_Definition: A cost model achieved at massive scales where the cost per unit of computing decreases as the scale increases often allowing an organization to subsidize its own infrastructure through the sale of surplus capacity.
Key_Actors: Large-scale cloud providers like Amazon; tenants of cloud services; automated datacenter management systems.
Operational_Workflow: A provider builds a computing infrastructure significantly larger than what is required for their own business needs; the provider implements virtualization and self-service APIs to allow external customers to rent the surplus capacity; the efficiencies gained from massive scale and high power usage effectiveness reduce the provider's internal costs; the profit generated from renting the spare capacity offsets or entirely pays for the provider's own computing needs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Providers must achieve extremely high efficiency in power and cooling to maintain a competitive edge; automation must replace labor-intensive operations to ensure costs do not grow with scale; resources must be offered via self-service administration to minimize human intervention.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Small-scale systems cannot achieve sub-linear costs because they cannot amortize the cost of specialized knowledge or automation development over a large enough number of machines.

Concept_Name: replacing labor-intensive operations with more software

Core_Definition: The practice of automating manual operational tasks through software development to reduce management overhead and enable systems to scale without a proportional increase in human labor.

Key_Actors: Centralized operations teams; software developers; cloud service providers such as Amazon, Google, and Microsoft; and customers utilizing self-service interfaces.

Operational_Workflow: Organizations begin by implementing standardization across large numbers of similarly configured racks and computers to reduce complexity. Operational tasks that were previously manual are then identified and replaced by automation software. This software is managed centrally, allowing the cost of its development to be spread across all machines in the infrastructure. In the cloud computing era, this workflow extends to providing APIs and control panels that allow customers to support themselves through self-service administration, such as spinning up or disposing of virtual machines with a mouse click or API call without involving provider staff.

Rules_and_Constraints: Standardization is required to make the automation of operational tasks possible. The economics of this approach dictate that the cost of developing software must be amortized over a large number of machines so that the cost per machine decreases as the number of machines increases. Centralized operations must be utilized to gain these efficiencies of scale.

Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Automation is less economically viable at a small scale because the development costs cannot be amortized over many uses, which drives up the operational cost. Small companies may lack the specialized knowledge required for such systems and may need to hire expensive external consultants. Furthermore, manual or recalcitrant IT departments can become a bottleneck, leading other departments to use cloud services as an end-run to avoid difficult manual processes.

============================================================
Appendix C. Scaling Terminology and Concepts
Section: C.2 Big O Notation
============================================================

Concept_Name: Big O Notation (O())
Core_Definition: A mathematical method for classifying a system based on how it responds to changes in input size; the letter O represents the order of the growth rate of an algorithm's run-time.
Key_Actors: System administrators; computer scientists; software systems; algorithms; research organizations like Google and Facebook.
Operational_Workflow: System administrators use the notation to communicate with specificity about how a system performs as data size grows; it allows professionals to classify behavior into categories like constant, linear, or exponential; it serves as a general guide for describing trends and identifying the dominant factors in a system's performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a gross generalization focusing on the trend as input grows rather than specific run-times; it indicates the biggest factor but not all factors; faster algorithms often require more complex code or additional work ahead of time such as building and maintaining an index; more complex algorithms are riskier and more likely to contain bugs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: At small values systems of different orders may be equivalent; modern CPU complexities such as linear versus random access and parallel execution can make performance non-intuitive; page faults can cause an O(log2 n) algorithm to run slower than an O(n) or O(n2) algorithm; benchmarking is often required because O() is a general guide rather than an absolute.

Concept_Name: O(1)
Core_Definition: Constant Scaling where performance does not change regardless of the scale of the input size.
Key_Actors: Hash-tables in RAM; database systems; authentication systems; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A system performs a task such as a hash-table lookup; the operation is completed in the same amount of time whether the table contains a small number of items or millions of items.
Rules_and_Constraints: Such algorithms are rare; hash-table lookups are limited to situations where the data fits in RAM; this classification is considered a form of sub-linear scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An API call might be classified as O(1) even if it contains an O(n) lookup if that lookup is performed on a tiny list and is inconsequential to the overall run-time; however if the input for that inconsequential part surges significantly it may start to dominate the system performance.

Concept_Name: O(n)
Core_Definition: Linear Scaling where a system slows down in direct proportion to the size of the input.
Key_Actors: Authentication systems; sorting algorithms; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: As the size of the input data grows the system slows down proportionately; for example twice as much data requires twice as much processing time or each unit of growth results in a slowdown of the same size.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is classified as a sub-linear system; sorting algorithms generally cannot be faster than this because they must touch every item at least once; there is only one O(n) sort algorithm and it only works on specific types of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: For very small inputs an O(n) algorithm may be slower than an O(n2) algorithm; a system with linear performance might be acceptable if the input size is not expected to change and the current speed is sufficient but this assumption carries high risk.

Concept_Name: O(nm)
Core_Definition: Exponential Scaling where performance worsens disproportionately as input size grows.
Key_Actors: Authentication systems; system administrators; researchers.
Operational_Workflow: As more data or users are added to a system the response times increase at an accelerating rate; for example response times might jump from 10 ms to 100 ms to 1000 ms as the user base grows.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is classified as super-linear scaling; O(n2) or quadratic scaling is a special case of this where m is 2; such systems can be a disaster if they interact with all parts of a larger system.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: People frequently use the term exponential when they actually mean quadratic; using an exponential algorithm may be fine if the input size is not expected to change and the current performance is fast enough though this carries a high risk.

Concept_Name: O(n2)
Core_Definition: A mathematical classification of a system where performance worsens in relation to the square of the size of the input data.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software developers; computer science researchers such as Poul-Henning Kamp.
Operational_Workflow: As the input size of a system increases, the number of required operations grows at a rate determined by the square of that input, causing the system to slow down significantly compared to linear growth.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a specific case of exponential scaling where the exponent is two; it is classified as a super-linear scaling pattern because its growth rate exceeds linear growth; Big O notation serves as a gross generalization focusing on trends rather than specific run-times.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An O(n2) algorithm may be faster than an O(n) algorithm for very small input values; it is often easier to implement and maintain than more complex algorithms; it can outperform O(log n) algorithms if it is designed to avoid virtual memory page faults by optimizing how it accesses memory pages.

Concept_Name: Quadratic Scaling
Core_Definition: A scaling phenomenon where the performance of a system degrades proportionately to the square of the input size.
Key_Actors: System administrators; computer systems; software applications.
Operational_Workflow: The system processes data such that each additional unit of input results in a performance penalty that follows a quadratic curve, often becoming a disaster if it interacts with all parts of a system.
Rules_and_Constraints: This type of scaling is super-linear; in common conversation, people often incorrectly use the term exponential when they are specifically referring to quadratic scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The term is rarely used except when a practitioner is being very specific or pedantic about a performance curve; using a quadratic algorithm might be a deliberate choice for small inputs where development risk and code complexity need to be minimized.

Concept_Name: O(log n)
Core_Definition: A mathematical classification for systems where performance worsens proportional to the base two logarithm of the input size.
Key_Actors: Binary search algorithms; system administrators; computer science educators.
Operational_Workflow: As the input size or search corpus grows, the system experiences a slowdown that is less than linear and eventually levels off asymptotically as data volume continues to increase.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is categorized as a sub-linear scaling method; practitioners often confuse this mathematical order with O(n log n), which is loglinear scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: These algorithms can be slower than O(n) or O(n2) algorithms if their operations cause virtual memory page faults; a page fault requires the operating system to pause the process and read from disk, a process that can take as long as the time needed to execute 10 million instructions.

Concept_Name: Logarithmic Scaling
Core_Definition: A scaling behavior where performance degradation slows down as input size grows, eventually leveling off over time.
Key_Actors: System administrators; software systems; search algorithms.
Operational_Workflow: The system handles increasing data volumes in a way that the impact on processing time decreases for each additional unit of growth, making it highly efficient for large datasets.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is a sub-linear growth pattern; many people use this term incorrectly when they are actually describing loglinear scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Modern CPU complexities such as memory cache misses and the need for parallel instruction execution can make these algorithms perform differently than theoretical Big O notation suggests; benchmarking is required because an algorithm with better theoretical scaling may be slower if it does not account for how modern operating systems handle disk I/O and RAM.

Concept_Name: O(n log n)
Core_Definition: A mathematical classification where performance worsens more than linearly with the more than component being proportional to log2 of the size of input.
Key_Actors: System administrators; computer scientists; systems processing growing input data.
Operational_Workflow: As the input size grows, the system scales linearly but adds a small and ever-growing performance surcharge for each additional unit of data.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is a type of Big O notation used to describe the growth rate or order of an algorithm's run-time; it is considered a more mathematical way of describing a system's behavior.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: People often incorrectly use the term logarithmic scaling when they actually mean O(n log n); Big O notation is a gross generalization that focuses on trends rather than specific run-times; at small values an O(n log n) algorithm may be slower than an O(n2) algorithm; modern hardware complexities like page faults or CPU cache misses can make the actual performance of same-order algorithms vary significantly.

Concept_Name: Loglinear Scaling
Core_Definition: A scaling terminology describing performance that worsens more than linearly with the extra component being proportional to the log2 of the input size.
Key_Actors: Professional system administrators; systems with increasing data sizes.
Operational_Workflow: The system functions with a performance profile that follows a linear scaling trend with the addition of an ever-increasing performance surcharge as the input grows.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text notes that using this specific term instead of logarithmic scaling is a way to sound like a true expert; it is used to communicate with specificity about how systems perform as data size grows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This term is frequently misused in casual conversation where people incorrectly substitute the term logarithmic scaling; actual performance can be affected by whether data structures are organized to avoid virtual memory page faults regardless of the mathematical order.

Concept_Name: O(n!)
Core_Definition: A mathematical classification where performance worsens proportional to the factorial of the size of the input.
Key_Actors: System administrators; computer science researchers; Google PageRank and Facebook SocialRank developers.
Operational_Workflow: The system's performance worsens so quickly that each additional unit of size increases the slowdown by a leap as large as all previous leaps combined plus an additional amount.
Rules_and_Constraints: This is classified as a super-linear scaling model; it is usually considered a worst-case scenario for algorithm performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Modern breakthroughs in research have allowed for the creation of replacements for these algorithms in systems like Google PageRank; at very small values higher-order algorithms may not be slower than lower-order ones; specific hardware behaviors such as linear memory access being faster than random access can contradict naive Big O comparisons.

Concept_Name: Factorial Scaling
Core_Definition: A term describing performance degradation that is proportional to the factorial of the size of the input data.
Key_Actors: System administrators; large-scale distributed system designers.
Operational_Workflow: Performance reaches a state where every new unit of input data added to the system results in a performance hit as large as all previous performance hits put together plus some more.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is categorized as a super-linear system because it grows faster than linear systems; it is often used as a indicator of a system's biggest performance factor as input grows.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Factorial scaling algorithms are generally avoided in favor of more efficient replacements in production environments; real-world performance is often dominated by other factors like disk I/O or RAM cache misses which O notation does not explicitly account for; a system may appear to scale at a different order if certain operations are inconsequential at small scales but dominate at larger scales.

Concept_Name: Sub-linear
Core_Definition: A term that refers to any system scaling or growth that increases at a rate less than the growth of the input size.
Key_Actors: Constant scaling algorithms; logarithmic scaling algorithms; O(1) systems; O(log n) systems.
Operational_Workflow: The system processes input such that performance either remains unchanged regardless of input size or worsens proportional to the log2 of the size of input, which asymptotically levels off as input size grows.
Rules_and_Constraints: Sub-linear systems are generally preferred over linear or super-linear systems for better performance; they include O(1) constant scaling and O(log n) logarithmic scaling.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Faster sub-linear algorithms often require additional work ahead of time such as building and maintaining an index; an O(n) algorithm may be slower than a super-linear O(n2) algorithm for very small input values.

Concept_Name: Super-linear
Core_Definition: A term used to describe systems where performance worsens at a rate faster than the increase in input size.
Key_Actors: Exponential scaling systems; factorial scaling algorithms; quadratic scaling systems; O(nm), O(n2), and O(n!) algorithms.
Operational_Workflow: As the size of the input grows, the system slows down disproportionately; in extreme cases like factorial scaling, each additional unit of size worsens performance by a leap as big as all previous leaps combined.
Rules_and_Constraints: These systems can be a disaster if they interact with all parts of a system; they are often used when faster algorithms do not exist or when the current performance is sufficient for a static input size.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: An O(n2) algorithm might be the easiest to implement for small inputs even if it is inefficient for larger ones; a super-linear system might be acceptable if the input size is not expected to change, though this carries high risk.

Concept_Name: Order of magnitude
Core_Definition: A term relating to the number of digits in a number describing size, indicating the literal magnitude of a digit such as the 10s, 100s, or 1000s place.
Key_Actors: System administrators; query processing systems; organizations and employee populations.
Operational_Workflow: It serves as a method for comparing the scale of different systems or entities by factors of ten and is used to provide coarse estimates for system performance.
Rules_and_Constraints: It is often used as conversational shorthand for Big O notation although the two are different; it is used in phrases to imply an approximate or coarse estimate of size or capacity.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A system or algorithm may work effectively for one size but break down when the input increases to a larger order of magnitude.

Concept_Name: Varnish HTTP accelerator
Core_Definition: A software package that achieves high performance by leveraging a deep understanding of how modern operating systems handle disk I/O and virtual memory.
Key_Actors: Kamp (2010); modern operating systems; CPU; virtual memory; disk I/O; RAM.
Operational_Workflow: The system organizes data structures and its heap so that parent and child nodes stay on the same 4KB virtual memory page; this avoids page faults which would otherwise cause the OS to pause the process for slow disk reads.
Rules_and_Constraints: Avoiding a page fault has a 1000-to-1 payback because a single fault takes as long as 10 million instructions; the system demonstrates that an O(n) or O(n2) algorithm that avoids page faults can outperform a naive O(log2 n) algorithm.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Performance can be complicated by the effects of threads, multiple CPUs accessing the same memory, locks, and mutexes; specific benchmarking is required to determine true speed as O() notation is only a general guide.

Concept_Name: Disk I/O
Core_Definition: The process of reading from or writing to a physical disk, which is managed by the operating system to handle data that does not fit in RAM.
Key_Actors: Modern operating systems; software programs; computer hardware including disks and RAM; system administrators.
Operational_Workflow: A program attempts to access data; the operating system determines if the data must be retrieved from the disk; the operating system pauses the process while the data is read; the system reads a disk block which may contain multiple records; the data is placed into RAM; the operating system unpauses the process once the read is complete.
Rules_and_Constraints: Disk operations are significantly slower than RAM operations; linear access to data on a disk is much faster than random access; disk I/O performance can be improved by taking advantage of how the operating system handles blocks of data.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Reading two adjacent records may only require one disk read if both fit into the same disk block; theoretically efficient algorithms can be slowed down if they trigger frequent disk operations; page faults act as a bottleneck that can make lower-order algorithms slower than higher-order ones.

Concept_Name: Page fault
Core_Definition: An event that occurs when a program attempts to access a portion of virtual memory that is not currently stored in the computer’s physical RAM.
Key_Actors: Programs; operating systems; computer RAM; physical disks.
Operational_Workflow: A program accesses virtual memory; the operating system identifies that the data is not in RAM; the operating system pauses the process; the system reads the required data from the disk; the operating system unpauses the process once the read is finished.
Rules_and_Constraints: Memory is handled in 4KB blocks; the first access to a 4KB block may cause a page fault but subsequent accesses within the same block do not; a single page fault takes approximately as long as the time it takes to execute 10 million instructions.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Organizing data structures so that parent and child nodes reside on the same memory page can eliminate many page faults; a mathematically superior algorithm like O(log2 n) can be outperformed by O(n) or O(n2) algorithms if the latter avoids page faults.

Concept_Name: Virtual memory
Core_Definition: A memory management scheme that abstracts physical memory and disk storage into a unified address space for programs.
Key_Actors: Operating systems; programs; computer hardware; developers such as Kamp.
Operational_Workflow: Programs store data in virtual memory addresses; the operating system manages the location of this data in 4KB blocks; the system maps these virtual addresses to physical RAM; if the data is not in RAM, the operating system triggers a page fault to retrieve it from disk.
Rules_and_Constraints: Virtual memory is handled in 4KB blocks; performance is highly dependent on whether data access stays within pages currently loaded in RAM; arranging data structures to align with virtual memory pages is a best practice for high performance.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Algorithms that do not account for the mechanics of virtual memory can suffer from extreme performance degradation due to page faults; performance may be non-intuitive because some algorithms use virtual memory in ways that cause the system to spend more time reading from disk than executing instructions.

Concept_Name: RAM
Core_Definition: The high-speed physical memory used by a computer to store data and instructions for immediate access by the CPU.
Key_Actors: Central Processing Units; L1 caches; multiple CPUs; operating systems; programs.
Operational_Workflow: Data is fetched from RAM and moved into the CPU’s L1 cache; the CPU performs operations on the data; the operating system uses RAM to cache blocks of data retrieved from the disk to speed up future accesses.
Rules_and_Constraints: A hash-table lookup in RAM can be done in constant time only if the data fits in RAM; reading every element in an array in row order is faster than column order because it utilizes linear memory reads; reading blocks of memory from RAM to the CPU cache is a slow process that often dominates the run-time of an algorithm.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The performance of the CPU drops when multiple CPUs try to access the same part of memory; cache misses occur when the CPU must wait for data to be retrieved from RAM because it was not in the L1 cache; a loop reading every 16th element takes roughly the same time as reading every element because the number of RAM cache misses is identical.

Concept_Name: CPU
Core_Definition: A hardware component characterized by architectural complexities like memory caching and parallel execution that make its performance characteristics non-intuitive.
Key_Actors: Multiple CPUs; memory cache; L1 (Level 1) cache; RAM.
Operational_Workflow: The CPU executes instructions in parallel when they are provably independent; it transfers blocks of memory from RAM to the L1 cache; it utilizes special instructions to perform sequential memory reads.
Rules_and_Constraints: CPUs are optimized to perform linear memory reads quickly; instructions must be independent to execute in parallel; performance is often dominated by the speed of reading blocks from RAM to the L1 cache.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: CPU performance drops when multiple CPUs attempt to access the same part of memory; performance becomes difficult to predict without benchmarking due to complexities like cache misses.

Concept_Name: Linear access
Core_Definition: A sequential method of reading data, such as accessing every element of an array in row order, which modern CPUs are optimized to process quickly.
Key_Actors: CPU; memory; arrays; RAM; L1 cache.
Operational_Workflow: The CPU reads data elements in a sequential path; it uses specialized instructions for sequential memory reads; it pulls blocks of memory from RAM into the L1 cache to minimize slow data retrieval operations.
Rules_and_Constraints: Linear access is significantly faster than random access; it allows the CPU to process the same number of bytes more efficiently than non-sequential methods.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A loop performing linear access may take the same amount of time as a loop reading every 16th element if the number of RAM cache misses is identical; performance advantages are lost if the data rows exceed the size of the CPU memory cache.

Concept_Name: Random access
Core_Definition: A non-sequential method of accessing data, such as reading array elements in column order rather than row order.
Key_Actors: CPU; memory cache; RAM; arrays.
Operational_Workflow: The system attempts to access data points that are not contiguous in memory; the CPU checks the cache for the required data; the operation often results in a cache miss, requiring a slow read from RAM.
Rules_and_Constraints: Random access is significantly slower than linear access.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the data being accessed in a non-linear fashion is part of a structure where rows are larger than the CPU's memory cache, every read operation can result in a cache miss.

Concept_Name: Threads
Core_Definition: Concurrent units of execution that, along with synchronization mechanisms, impact the scaling and performance of software systems.
Key_Actors: Multiple CPUs; locks; mutexes.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The presence of threads makes it difficult to determine the speed of an algorithm based on Big O notation alone, necessitating the use of benchmarking.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Threads, combined with multiple CPUs accessing the same data and the use of locks or mutexes, cloud performance issues and make Big O notation a general guide rather than an absolute rule.

Concept_Name: Locks
Core_Definition: Locks are mechanisms that coordinate access to data when multiple CPUs or threads are involved in a system.
Key_Actors: Multiple CPUs; threads; data.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple threads or CPUs attempt to access the same data; locks are employed to manage this concurrent access; the interaction between these entities and the locks affects the overall performance and speed of the algorithm.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Locks cloud the issue of performance and can make it non-intuitive; they are cited as a reason why Big O notation is a general guide rather than an absolute indicator of speed.

Concept_Name: Mutexes
Core_Definition: Mutexes are synchronization mechanisms used to manage access to shared data and memory in multi-CPU environments.
Key_Actors: Multiple CPUs; threads; data.
Operational_Workflow: Multiple CPUs or threads try to access the same part of memory; mutexes are used to coordinate this access; this process introduces complexities that impact the actual execution speed of an algorithm.
Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Mutexes contribute to making performance non-intuitive and can cause real-world performance to deviate from theoretical Big O expectations.

Concept_Name: Benchmarking
Core_Definition: Benchmarking is the process of conducting actual tests on an algorithm to determine its real-world speed and performance.
Key_Actors: System administrators; algorithms; computer systems.
Operational_Workflow: A system administrator recognizes that Big O notation is a general guide rather than an absolute; the administrator performs specific tests on the algorithm within its operational environment; the resulting data is used to provide a definitive measurement of speed.
Rules_and_Constraints: You cannot truly tell how fast an algorithm will be without benchmarking; Big O notation serves as a general guide rather than an absolute when benchmarking.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Benchmarking is required because modern CPU complexities and memory access patterns make performance results non-intuitive.

Concept_Name: L1 (Level 1) cache
Core_Definition: The L1 cache is a high-speed memory area within the CPU where blocks of memory retrieved from RAM are stored for faster processing.
Key_Actors: CPU; RAM; memory blocks; algorithms.
Operational_Workflow: The system reads blocks of memory from RAM; these blocks are transferred into the CPU L1 cache; the CPU then performs operations on the data contained in the cache.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reading blocks from RAM to the L1 cache is slow and often dominates the run-time of an algorithm; linear memory reads are optimized by CPUs to be significantly faster than random access; special instructions exist for sequentially reading memory into the cache.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If rows of data are larger than the CPU's memory cache, it can result in a cache miss for every read; a loop reading every 16th element of an array may take the same amount of time as reading every element because both generate the same number of RAM cache misses.

