Concept_Name: Design Document Template
Core_Definition: A standardized framework used to outline the plan and technical details for a project as described in Section 13.2 of the source text.
Key_Actors: Authors; Reviewers; Approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The author fills in administrative details like title and date; provides an executive summary and defines project goals and limits; explains background terminology and history; provides high-level and detailed designs; lists alternatives and security mitigations; the document is then submitted for review and approval while the revision number and status are updated.
Rules_and_Constraints: The template must include specific sections such as revision number, executive summary, goals, non-goals, and security concerns; the executive summary should be two to four sentences; non-goals and goals are presented as bulleted lists; alternatives considered must include pros and cons; security concerns must address privacy ramifications and mitigation strategies.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Reviewer
Core_Definition: A person assigned to evaluate and provide feedback on the technical and operational details of a design document.
Key_Actors: Individual employees or team members such as the people named Joe, Mary, and Jane in the example.
Operational_Workflow: Reviewers are identified in the document header; they analyze the design and participate in the process of evaluating the project details before it reaches the approval stage.
Rules_and_Constraints: Reviewers must be listed by name in the document metadata and should be linked to their email addresses.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Approver
Core_Definition: An individual or entity with the authority to grant final permission for a project design to be implemented.
Key_Actors: Security Operations; Tech Leads; Directors of Operations; other relevant leadership roles.
Operational_Workflow: Approvers evaluate the document after the review phase; they record their approval by adding their name and the date of approval to the document header; the project proceeds to schedule and procurement after approval is granted.
Rules_and_Constraints: Approvers must be listed by name and linked to their email addresses; the specific date of each approval should be recorded in the document header.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Status
Core_Definition: A metadata field in a design document used to track its current phase within the organizational planning and approval workflow.
Key_Actors: Document authors; reviewers; approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The status field is updated as the document progresses through the lifecycle of the project from initial creation to formal sign-off and implementation.
Rules_and_Constraints: The text specifies four allowed status values which are draft, in review, approved, and in progress.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: High-Level Design
Core_Definition: A brief high-level description of the design where diagrams are often included to assist understanding.
Key_Actors: Bosun monitoring system; remote collectors; corporate server hardware; Phoenix and Toronto datacenters; Git sysadmin configuration repository.
Operational_Workflow: The plan utilizes Bosun with remote collectors on standard corporate Linux server hardware; servers are named according to corporate naming conventions; monitoring configurations are stored and maintained in a specific Git repository directory.
Rules_and_Constraints: Descriptions should be kept at a high level; diagrams are helpful; configurations must be stored in the top-level monitor directory of the sysadmin configs repository.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Detailed Design
Core_Definition: A comprehensive top-down description of the design providing specific technical implementation details.
Key_Actors: Dell 720XD hardware; Debian Linux; Bosun server and client packages; corporate backup systems.
Operational_Workflow: Specific hardware is configured with 64G RAM and RAID6 disks; Debian Linux is installed using the standard corporate server configuration; the bosun-server package is installed on central servers while the bosun-client package is installed on all monitored systems; nightly backups are performed using standard corporate mechanisms.
Rules_and_Constraints: Must include specific hardware specifications; must follow standard corporate server configurations; must utilize specific package names for server and client software; should include descriptions of operational tasks like adding collectors or alerting rules.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Security Concerns
Core_Definition: A section of the design document dedicated to identifying security or privacy ramifications and explaining how those concerns are mitigated.
Key_Actors: Security Operations team; password vault systems; Bosun software.
Operational_Workflow: Security and privacy ramifications are identified; mitigation strategies are developed; the storage method for sensitive credentials such as SNMP community strings is reviewed and approved by Security Operations; sensitive data is stored in a secure password vault.
Rules_and_Constraints: Storage methods for passwords and community strings must be reviewed and approved by Security Operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Design Document Example
Core_Definition: A sample document illustrating the practical application of design principles for a new monitoring system project.
Key_Actors: Authors Strata Chalup and Tom Limoncelli; Reviewers Joe, Mary, and Jane; Approvers including Security Operations, the Build System Tech Lead, and the Director of Ops.
Operational_Workflow: The document is drafted by authors; it undergoes a review process by designated individuals; formal approval is obtained from security and technical leadership; hardware procurement occurs over one week; installation and testing take place over two days; the system goes live in the third week after approval.
Rules_and_Constraints: The document must include title, date, authors, reviewers, approvers, revision number, and status; it must define specific goals such as monitoring 10,000 devices and retaining data for 30 years; it must list non-goals to set project limits; it must include cost projections and a project schedule.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Monitoring System
Core_Definition: A system designed to track network devices and collect their attributes to support real-time alerting and long-term data retention.
Key_Actors: Authors Strata Chalup and Tom Limoncelli; Reviewers Joe, Mary, and Jane; Approvers including Security Operations, Chris the Build System Tech Lead, and Sara the Director of Ops; Bosun server and remote collectors.
Operational_Workflow: The system is implemented using Bosun with remote collectors running on Debian Linux servers located in the Phoenix and Toronto datacenters; monitoring configurations are stored in a Git repository; any system being monitored has a client package installed; hardware procurement takes one week followed by two days of installation and testing before the system goes live in the third week; backups are performed nightly using standard corporate mechanisms.
Rules_and_Constraints: The system must monitor at least 10,000 devices with 500 attributes each collected every minute; data must be retained for 30 years; the solution should be a standardized way of monitoring that does not require development time when adding new platforms; software must be open source; SNMP community strings must be stored in a password vault as approved by Security Operations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Monitoring external systems and building chat functionality are explicitly defined as non-goals; historically used home-brew monitoring scripts are rejected in favor of the standardized Bosun system.

