Concept_Name: Postmortem Template
Core_Definition: A structured framework used to document the details of a technical outage and identify recommendations for future prevention.
Key_Actors: People involved in the outage resolution; Affected users; Executive leadership; System logs and communication archives.
Operational_Workflow: The process begins by defining the title and report status followed by an executive summary of the event; technical staff provide a description of the outage and identify affected users; precise start and end times are used to determine duration; a detailed minute by minute timeline is reconstructed from system logs and chat history; the team analyzes contributing causes and evaluates what went well or poorly; finally, actionable recommendations are linked to specific bug or feature request IDs.
Rules_and_Constraints: Recommendations must be actionable and measurable; every recommendation must be associated with a filed bug or feature request ID; timelines should be assembled from objective resources like system logs and emails.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Report Status
Core_Definition: A field in a design or postmortem document that indicates the current stage of the document in its lifecycle.
Key_Actors: Document authors; Reviewers; Approvers.
Operational_Workflow: The status is updated as the document moves through different phases of the organizational workflow; for design documents, the status transitions through specific stages to show progress.
Rules_and_Constraints: For design documents, the status must be one of the following: draft, in review, approved, or in progress.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Duration
Core_Definition: The total elapsed time of a service outage as recorded in a postmortem report.
Key_Actors: Staff involved in timeline assembly; Automated system logs.
Operational_Workflow: The start date and time of the incident are identified; the end date and time are identified; the difference between these two points is calculated and recorded to provide context for the timeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: This value must be derived from the specific start and end times of the outage.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Timeline
Core_Definition: A detailed, minute-by-minute record of events used to document the sequence of an outage within a postmortem report.
Key_Actors: People involved in the resolution of the outage.
Operational_Workflow: An outage occurs and is resolved; investigators gather information from system logs, chat logs, emails, and other available resources; a minute-by-minute sequence of events is reconstructed from these sources; the resulting account is documented in the postmortem report to provide a technical overview of the incident.
Rules_and_Constraints: The timeline must be minute-by-minute; it should be assembled from system logs, chat logs, emails, and any other resources available.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: System Log
Core_Definition: A technical record of system activity used as a source of information for reconstructing the timeline of an outage.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Systems generate logs during operation; following an incident, these logs are accessed to provide data for the postmortem process; the logs are used to verify specific times and events for the minute-by-minute timeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: System logs are identified as one of the primary resources used to assemble an outage timeline.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Chat Log
Core_Definition: A record of electronic communications used as a resource to help establish the sequence of events in a postmortem timeline.
Key_Actors: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Operational_Workflow: Staff communicate via chat during an incident; these records are preserved and later retrieved; they are analyzed alongside other data sources to provide a granular account of the outage timeline.
Rules_and_Constraints: In the context of system design, building chat functionality may be designated as a non-goal for a project.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Email
Core_Definition: A communication method used for identifying document stakeholders and providing data for incident reconstruction.
Key_Actors: Authors; reviewers; approvers; people involved in the resolution of an outage.
Operational_Workflow: Stakeholders link their email addresses in design documents for identification and contact; during an incident, emails are sent and received by the response team; after the incident, these emails are reviewed to provide details for the minute-by-minute timeline in the postmortem report.
Rules_and_Constraints: Design document templates require links to the email addresses of authors, reviewers, and approvers.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Contributing Conditions Analysis
