Rules_and_Constraints: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Disk Space Monitoring
Core_Definition: A specific monitoring task used as an example of an actionable and measurable recommendation to prevent outages by alerting when database storage capacity is low.
Key_Actors: Database servers being monitored; monitoring systems.
Operational_Workflow: As a recommendation in a postmortem report, this involves configuring a system to track available disk space on a database server; the system is set to trigger an alert if the available space falls below a specific threshold such as 20 percent.
Rules_and_Constraints: The monitoring must be defined with clear, measurable parameters such as a specific percentage threshold to ensure it is actionable.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Alerting
Core_Definition: A real-time notification process within a monitoring system used to inform personnel of specific conditions or status changes in devices and attributes.
Key_Actors: Monitoring servers such as Bosun; remote collectors; bosun-client and bosun-server software packages; pagers and SMS delivery systems; system administrators; security operations teams for rule approval.
Operational_Workflow: Monitored systems have client software installed to track specific attributes which are collected every minute; this data is sent to a central monitoring server that evaluates the information against alerting rules stored in a configuration repository; when a rule condition is met, such as disk space falling below a specific percentage, the system triggers a real-time notification; the alert is then transmitted to operators via SMS or pager systems to facilitate a response.
Rules_and_Constraints: Alerting must support real-time delivery via SMS or pagers; alerting rules should be actionable and measurable such as monitoring for disk space being less than twenty percent; configuration for alerts should be kept in a standardized version control repository; the system must be capable of scaling to monitor at least ten thousand devices with five hundred attributes each; security reviews are required for systems that store sensitive data related to monitoring configurations.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Home-brew monitoring scripts often require excessive development and adjustment when platforms change; postmortem analysis may reveal that an outage occurred because specific alerts were missing or monitoring was not granular enough; alerts may be excluded for systems designated as non-goals such as external systems or those requiring internal chat functionality.


Concept_Name: Contract for an On-Premises
Core_Definition: A contract for an on-premises system refers to the implicit or explicit service level agreement and operational expectations between an internal IT team and its users. It defines the responsibilities for maintaining hardware, software, and availability within a company's own data center.
Key_Actors: Internal IT staff; system administrators; internal business units; end-users within the organization.
Operational_Workflow: The internal IT team designs and procures the necessary hardware and software; the team installs and configures the systems within the company's data center; IT staff perform ongoing maintenance including patching and upgrades; users access the services through the internal network; the IT team monitors performance and responds to incidents based on the agreed-upon service levels.
Rules_and_Constraints: The IT organization is responsible for the entire stack from physical facilities to the application layer; services must comply with internal security and governance policies; capacity planning is limited by the physical constraints of the on-premises data center.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Hardware failures require manual intervention and replacement by local staff; power outages or cooling failures in the local data center can impact all hosted services; sudden spikes in demand may lead to performance degradation if the local infrastructure cannot be scaled rapidly.


Concept_Name: centralized log archive
Core_Definition: A centralized log archive is a system that collects, stores, and organizes log data from multiple disparate sources into a single, searchable repository. It serves as a unified platform for monitoring, troubleshooting, and analyzing the behavior of distributed systems.
Key_Actors: System administrators; developers; automated log collection agents; centralized logging servers; storage infrastructure.
Operational_Workflow: Log generation occurs on individual servers and applications; local collection agents or syslog daemons capture these log entries; logs are transmitted over the network to the centralized logging infrastructure; the central server parses, indexes, and stores the incoming data; users access the archive through search interfaces or visualization tools to perform queries and identify patterns.
Rules_and_Constraints: Logs should be transmitted in a standard format to facilitate parsing; the central archive must have sufficient storage capacity and performance to handle the aggregate log volume from all sources; access to sensitive log data must be restricted through appropriate security controls; retention policies should be established to manage the lifecycle of stored logs.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Network congestion or outages can delay or prevent log delivery to the central server; a surge in log volume from a malfunctioning application may overwhelm the central infrastructure's ingestion or storage capabilities; logs may be lost if the local buffer on a source system overflows before data can be transmitted.


Concept_Name: OODA loop
Core_Definition: A decision-making framework consisting of four stages—Observe, Orient, Decide, and Act—that is designed for high-stress situations requiring rapid responses.
Key_Actors: John Boyd; Kyle Brandt; oncall personnel.
Operational_Workflow: The responder begins with the Observe phase by checking logs and reading measurements like I/O; followed by the Orient phase where the responder analyzes and interprets the gathered data; then continues to the Decide and Act phases though the specific procedural details for these final two steps are not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: The framework is intended for fighter jet combat and similarly high-stress operational environments that demand quick responses; oncall situations using this framework prioritize speed over long-term perfection.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Observe (OODA)
Core_Definition: The initial phase of the OODA decision-making framework which involves gathering raw data and signals from the system.
Key_Actors: John Boyd; Kyle Brandt; oncall personnel. Operational_Workflow: When an alert is received, such as a report that a website is slow, the responder begins by checking system logs and reading measurements like I/O to gather raw facts. Rules_and_Constraints: The process is designed for high-stress situations like fighter jet combat that require rapid responses; speed and the prevention of service level agreement violations are prioritized over long-term perfection.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Orient (OODA)
Core_Definition: The second phase of the OODA loop involving the analysis and interpretation of gathered data to establish situational awareness.
Key_Actors: John Boyd; Kyle Brandt; oncall personnel. Operational_Workflow: The responder analyzes observed data fields and uses context and professional experience to turn those raw signals into useful information. Rules_and_Constraints: Orientation is designed for fighter jet combat and similarly high-stress environments where quick responses are mandatory; the process requires active interpretation rather than simple data collection.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Decide (OODA)
Core_Definition: The third stage of the OODA loop framework where a responder selects a course of action based on situational orientation.
Key_Actors: John Boyd; Kyle Brandt; oncall personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Decisions in an oncall context prioritize speed and meeting service level agreement requirements over finding a perfect long-term solution; a quick fix may be the only viable option.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Act (OODA)
Core_Definition: The final stage of the OODA loop framework where the chosen decision is implemented to resolve a system alert or emergency.
Key_Actors: John Boyd; Kyle Brandt; oncall personnel.
Operational_Workflow: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.
Rules_and_Constraints: Actions are focused on resolving immediate alerts to prevent service level agreement violations and may include shortcut solutions rather than permanent fixes.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Load Balancer
Core_Definition: A load balancer is a server or service that receives incoming requests and forwards them to one of many backend replicas configured to service the same URLs.
Key_Actors: Load balancer server; backend replicas; DNS servers; Layer 3 and 4 load balancers; Layer 7 load balancers; end users.
Operational_Workflow: Requests are sent to the load balancer which selects a backend based on an algorithm such as round-robin or least loaded; the request is forwarded to the selected replica; the replica processes the request and sends a response back to the load balancer; the load balancer relays the response to the original requester.
Rules_and_Constraints: Load balancers must continuously perform health checks to ensure backends are ready to accept requests; sophisticated algorithms like least loaded require a slow start mechanism to prevent flooding new machines; Layer 7 balancers can make decisions based on application-level data like cookies or headers; shared state across replicas may require sticky connections or a common data store.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: A naive least loaded algorithm can cause a cascading failure where each machine is overwhelmed and crashes in sequence; if a backend dies and sticky connections are used the user will be forced to log in again on a new replica; load balancers themselves can be a single point of failure requiring redundant pairs for failover.

Concept_Name: Log analysis
Core_Definition: Log analysis is the process of collecting and examining high-level or low-level logs generated by system components to gain visibility into system behavior and internal state.
Key_Actors: System components; web servers; application servers; operating system kernel; central logging infrastructure; operations staff; developers.
Operational_Workflow: Components generate copious logs detailing events like user purchases or function call parameters; these logs are aggregated to a central location for collection and storage; automated tools or staff then analyze the data to determine page generation times or identify high-value customers; timestamps are normalized to a standard like UTC to facilitate cross-system comparisons.
Rules_and_Constraints: A single log format should be established for all systems to simplify consolidation; timestamps should be stored in UTC rather than local time zones to avoid synchronization issues during debugging; logging resolution is typically high with one entry per action while analysis latency is often high due to batch processing.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Configuration mistakes can result in logging data being normalized incorrectly to the wrong time zone; overly verbose debug logs can become overwhelming if not enabled on individual modules; retention of log data may be restricted by legal regulations regarding personal information.


Concept_Name: pager storms
Core_Definition: A pager storm is a situation where a single root cause triggers a massive influx of redundant or related alerts, overwhelming the oncall responder. Key_Actors: Oncall personnel; monitoring systems; alerting and escalation managers. Operational_Workflow: A system failure occurs; multiple monitoring checks detect different symptoms of the same failure; the alerting system sends a separate page for every detected issue; the oncall responder receives a continuous stream of notifications that makes it difficult to focus on the actual repair. Rules_and_Constraints: Alerting systems should be designed to inhibit or silence redundant alerts when a known dependency is down; overactive alerts should be fine-tuned or consolidated rather than ignored; the goal is to maintain a manageable number of alerts per shift to avoid responder fatigue.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the inhibition logic is too aggressive, it may hide unrelated failures that happen to occur at the same time as a major outage.

Concept_Name: two-stage acknowledgment
Core_Definition: Two-stage acknowledgment is a notification process where a responder first acknowledges receipt of an alert to stop the escalation, and then later marks the issue as resolved or enters a second state to indicate active work. Key_Actors: Oncall responder; alerting and escalation manager. Operational_Workflow: The monitoring system triggers an alert; the alerting manager notifies the primary oncall person; the responder performs the first stage by acknowledging the page, which signals to the system to stop paging and not escalate to the secondary person; the responder investigates and works on the issue; once the problem is fixed, the responder performs the second stage by closing the alert or marking it resolved. Rules_and_Constraints: Acknowledging a page must happen within a defined time limit to prevent automatic escalation to a secondary responder; the process ensures that the monitoring system knows a human is actively handling the situation.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: If the primary responder acknowledges the page but then fails to actually fix the issue or falls asleep, the system may not escalate further because the first stage was completed, potentially leading to a prolonged outage.

Concept_Name: Constant Scaling
Core_Definition: A performance characteristic where the system remains unaffected by the scale of the input, maintaining the same performance regardless of data size.
Key_Actors: Professional system administrators; hash table data structures; system RAM.
Operational_Workflow: A lookup or operation is performed on a data set; the system completes the task in an identical amount of time whether the input consists of 100 items or 100 million items.
Rules_and_Constraints: Algorithms that provide constant scaling are rare; hash table lookups only achieve this performance level when the data fits entirely within RAM.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: The constant performance guarantee is lost if the data set grows beyond the capacity of the available RAM.

Concept_Name: Linear Scaling
Core_Definition: A scaling pattern where the system slows down in direct proportion to the growth of the input size.
Key_Actors: Professional system administrators; authentication systems; user databases.
Operational_Workflow: A system handles a set number of users or data; as growth occurs in specific units, such as adding 10,000 users at a time, the processing time increases by a consistent amount, such as 50 ms, for every unit added.
Rules_and_Constraints: This classification requires that each unit of growth results in a slowdown of the same size.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: Not explicitly covered in this chapter.

Concept_Name: Exponential Scaling
Core_Definition: A performance profile where the system slows down disproportionately as the size of the input grows.
Key_Actors: Professional system administrators; authentication systems; integrated system components.
Operational_Workflow: The system processes increasing amounts of data; adding more users or items causes the response time to jump dramatically and non-linearly, for example moving from 10 ms to 100 ms to 1000 ms.
Rules_and_Constraints: Systems exhibiting this behavior are considered a disaster if they interact with all parts of a distributed system; administrators are advised that assuming input size will not change is a high-risk assumption.
Exceptions_and_Edge_Cases: This scaling may be tolerable if the input size is not expected to change and the system is fast enough at its current size.


